/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE English Literature (International GCSE) (4ET1) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-4ET1', KITS: {"unseen-poetry":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"unseen-poetry","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Component 1 is the one question in the whole International GCSE where you have never met the text before. It is a single compulsory essay worth 20 raw marks, every one of them AO2, and the poem is reproduced in the question paper, so there is nothing to revise and nothing to remember. That makes it the most teachable part of the paper, because it tests a method rather than a memory: the specification asks you to apply your knowledge of poetic form, content and meaning to a poem you are meeting for the first time. Nothing you know about the poet’s life or period earns a mark here, and nor does a comparison with an anthology poem, because AO1, AO3 and AO4 carry no marks in this section. What earns marks is reading the poem closely enough to say what it is doing, and then showing how its language, form and structure do it. This room gives you the toolkit of terms, a reading routine that fits the roughly twenty-seven minutes Section A is worth, and the paragraph shape that moves an answer from naming devices to evaluating effects.","idea":[{"code":"Component 1: Content overview","h":"Wide reading of modern poetry is the revision","t":"Component 1 requires learners to ‘Develop skills to analyse unseen poetry’ and to ‘Develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meanings and effects’, while maintaining ‘a critical style and informed personal response’. Alongside the set anthology poems, ‘Additionally, a selection of modern poetry should be studied in order to prepare for the unseen poetry assessment.’ The point of that reading is not content but fluency: if you have met free verse, dramatic monologue, half-rhyme and a poem that turns in its last three lines, none of those will cost you time in the examination. A top-level answer treats the printed poem as one more poem it has already met — it recognises the form quickly and spends its minutes on effect, not on working out what kind of thing it is looking at."},{"code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","h":"Apply what you know about poems to a poem you do not know","t":"The specification is explicit: ‘Learners will apply their knowledge of poetic form, content and meaning to an unseen poem.’ It then lists what they must do: ‘demonstrate understanding of how writers create literary effects using, for example, imagery, descriptive skills, language choice, tone and mood, structure and form; understand and use appropriate literary terminology; identify and use relevant examples from poems.’ Read those three demands as a sequence rather than a menu. Terminology is the cheapest of the three and scores least on its own; the marks live in ‘how writers create literary effects’. A top-level answer never says only that a poem contains a simile — it says what the comparison brings with it, where it is placed, and what the reader is made to feel or understand because of it."},{"code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2","h":"AO2 alone, so analysis is the entire mark","t":"‘Questions will test the following assessment objective: AO2 Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.’ Section A carries 20 raw marks and all of them are AO2, so there is no AO1, AO3 or AO4 credit at all: context, biography, period and any comparison with another poem earn nothing here, however well written. The levels of response show what rises: the language, form and structure strand runs from ‘Minimal identification of language, form and structure’ at the bottom, through ‘Some comment’ and ‘Sound understanding’, to ‘Sustained analysis’ and finally ‘Cohesive evaluation’. A top-level answer is therefore not the one with the most terms in it but the one whose points hold together as a single reading of the poem and weigh the effects it identifies."},{"code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Unseen Poetry","h":"One compulsory 20-mark essay, with the poem in front of you","t":"‘Section A – Unseen Poetry: one 20-mark essay question exploring the meaning and effects created in an unseen poem. The poem will be reproduced in the question paper. This section assesses AO2.’ There is no choice of question, so there is no decision to agonise over and no time to lose making one. Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark, closed book examination worth 60% of the International GCSE, available on paper or onscreen; learners may not bring texts in, but are provided with the relevant poems. Pro rata, Section A is worth about twenty-seven minutes. A top-level answer budgets that time deliberately — a few minutes reading and planning a line of argument, the rest writing it — rather than starting at line one and stopping when the clock does."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a whole short poem before you write a word: Tennyson, ‘The Eagle’","setup":"The printed poem is Alfred, Lord Tennyson, ‘The Eagle’:\n\nHe clasps the crag with crooked hands;\nClose to the sun in lonely lands,\nRing’d with the azure world, he stands.\n\nThe wrinkled sea beneath him crawls;\nHe watches from his mountain walls,\nAnd like a thunderbolt he falls.\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.) Before writing anything, work out the single reading you are going to argue.","steps":["Track the movement first: the opening tercet holds the bird motionless, ending on ‘stands’, and the second releases him, ending on ‘falls’, so the poem is built as stillness broken by one drop.","Fix that as a thesis in one sentence: Tennyson presents the eagle as a creature of lonely, kingly stillness whose power is only proved at the moment it is spent.","Choose the evidence that carries the thesis: ‘clasps the crag with crooked hands’ for the gripping stillness, ‘Ring’d with the azure world’ for the isolating height, ‘like a thunderbolt he falls’ for the release.","Check the form is saying the same thing: two tercets, each rhyming on one sound throughout, so each stanza closes on itself and the poem has exactly two movements, the second arriving as the answer to the first.","Settle the closing judgement before you begin: the effect of the whole is that the watching was always potential energy, and the last line makes the reader feel the drop rather than hear it described."],"cues":["What changes between the first stanza and the second?","How would you say that change in a single sentence?","Which three quotations would carry that sentence?","How many stanzas are there, and how does each one rhyme?","What should your final sentence claim about the poem as a whole?"]},{"title":"Turning a named device into analysis that scores","setup":"A student is answering the 20-mark unseen question on Tennyson’s ‘The Eagle’, whose last line is ‘And like a thunderbolt he falls.’ Their sentence reads: ‘Tennyson uses a simile here which makes it exciting.’ Rewrite that sentence so that it does what AO2 rewards, and say what was wrong with it.","steps":["Diagnose the sentence: it identifies the method and then reports a feeling, which is the bottom of the grid — minimal identification of language with no account of how any effect is made.","Unpack what the comparison actually carries: a thunderbolt is sudden, vertical and belongs to the sky, so the image keeps the bird inside the ‘azure world’ even as he drops out of it.","Add the sound and the shape of the line: it is almost entirely single-syllable words and its last word is the verb ‘falls’, so the line arrives and stops the way the bird does.","Rewrite as one chain: Tennyson closes on the simile ‘like a thunderbolt he falls’, and the comparison carries more than speed, because a thunderbolt belongs to the sky the eagle has been watching from, so the drop reads as the sky discharging itself; the run of single-syllable words and the final verb leave the line nowhere to go, and the poem ends on the fall.","Judge where the marks came from: the rewrite still names the method, but it is paid for what the comparison brings, what the sound does, and what the poem gains by ending there."],"cues":["Which band does the student’s sentence sit in, and why?","What does a thunderbolt bring with it besides speed?","What do you notice about the length of the words in that line?","How would those observations read as one sentence?","Where exactly did the marks come from in the new version?"]},{"title":"Writing about an image that will not settle: Blake, ‘The Sick Rose’","setup":"The printed poem is William Blake, ‘The Sick Rose’:\n\nO Rose thou art sick.\nThe invisible worm,\nThat flies in the night\nIn the howling storm:\n\nHas found out thy bed\nOf crimson joy:\nAnd his dark secret love\nDoes thy life destroy.\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.) Two readings are obviously available — a diseased flower, and something else entirely. Decide how to write about a poem whose central image refuses to settle.","steps":["Take the surface at face value first: a rose has been invaded by a worm, and the poem is spoken straight at the flower in two sentences — a one-line diagnosis, then a seven-line sentence that names the agent — so the reader overhears an accusation rather than a description.","Find the words that will not stay literal: ‘bed’, ‘crimson joy’ and ‘dark secret love’ belong to human desire and concealment, not to gardening, so the image opens outwards the moment it is examined.","Turn that into an argument about effect rather than a decoding: the poem makes the reader feel a corruption whose name it deliberately withholds, and the withholding is itself the effect.","Bring the form in as evidence: two short quatrains of very short lines, and the long second sentence runs across the stanza break to end on the verb ‘destroy’, so its grammar completes only at the moment the damage is named.","Judge without solving: a top-level answer says what the poem does with its ambiguity instead of choosing one meaning and calling it the answer."],"cues":["What happens if you read this simply as being about a flower?","Which words stop that flower reading from working?","How can a reading be an argument rather than a translation?","Where does the poem’s longer sentence finally close, and on what word?","What should the closing judgement refuse to do?"]},{"title":"Letting structure carry the answer: Shelley, ‘Ozymandias’","setup":"The printed poem is Percy Bysshe Shelley, ‘Ozymandias’:\n\nI met a traveller from an antique land,\nWho said—“Two vast and trunkless legs of stone\nStand in the desert. . . . Near them, on the sand,\nHalf sunk a shattered visage lies, whose frown,\nAnd wrinkled lip, and sneer of cold command,\nTell that its sculptor well those passions read\nWhich yet survive, stamped on these lifeless things,\nThe hand that mocked them, and the heart that fed;\nAnd on the pedestal, these words appear:\nMy name is Ozymandias, King of Kings;\nLook on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!\nNothing beside remains. Round the decay\nOf that colossal Wreck, boundless and bare\nThe lone and level sands stretch far away.”\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.) Plan the structural spine of the answer before you choose any quotations.","steps":["Map who is speaking: the reader reaches the king through a speaker quoting a traveller, and inside that a sculptor’s reading of the face, so the boast survives only as the innermost layer of somebody else’s story.","Locate the turn: the flat sentence ‘Nothing beside remains.’ opens the twelfth line and stops inside it, and every grand noun before it has already been broken — ‘trunkless legs’, ‘shattered visage’, ‘colossal Wreck’.","Set the boast against its setting: ‘Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!’ is an order addressed to rivals assumed still to exist, and the desert answers it at once, so the command now works against the king who gave it.","Listen to the closing line: ‘The lone and level sands stretch far away’ is long, flat and unbroken, so the poem ends in the one thing that has outlasted everything the king built.","Judge the source of the effect: the poem’s irony is produced by arrangement — who speaks, and what is placed next to what — rather than by any single image."],"cues":["Who is speaking, and who is quoted inside whom?","Which sentence turns the poem, and where does it fall?","What answers the king’s command, and how quickly?","How does the last line sound beside the broken nouns?","Is the main effect made by an image or by an arrangement?"]},{"title":"Writing the opening paragraph under exam conditions: Thomas, ‘Adlestrop’","setup":"The printed poem is Edward Thomas, ‘Adlestrop’:\n\nYes. I remember Adlestrop —\nThe name, because one afternoon\nOf heat the express-train drew up there\nUnwontedly. It was late June.\n\nThe steam hissed. Someone cleared his throat.\nNo one left and no one came\nOn the bare platform. What I saw\nWas Adlestrop — only the name\n\nAnd willows, willow-herb, and grass,\nAnd meadowsweet, and haycocks dry,\nNo whit less still and lonely fair\nThan the high cloudlets in the sky.\n\nAnd for that minute a blackbird sang\nClose by, and round him, mistier,\nFarther and farther, all the birds\nOf Oxfordshire and Gloucestershire.\n\nThe question is the usual one: explore the meaning and effects created in this poem. Write the first analytical paragraph of that answer, taking it from the opening stanza.","steps":["Begin from the voice rather than a device: the poem opens on an answer, ‘Yes.’, as though a question has already been asked, so the reader arrives mid-conversation and has to catch up.","Show the punctuation working: full stops cut into the first line and the fourth, and the stop after ‘Unwontedly’ breaks the fourth line open, so the memory arrives in pieces rather than as a flow.","Take the odd word seriously: ‘Unwontedly’ is formal and slightly awkward, and its length after three plain lines makes the unscheduled halt the one strange fact the speaker is certain of.","Bind the detail to a reading: memory here is recovered rather than possessed, which is why the plainest facts — the heat, the month, the name — are the ones that have survived.","Close the paragraph with a mini-judgement that reaches forward: the poem earns its later stillness by beginning in ordinary interruption, because the unscheduled halt is what empties the platform — ‘No one left and no one came’ — and clears the silence the blackbird of the last stanza is heard in."],"cues":["What is odd about the poem’s very first word?","Where do the full stops fall, and what do they do?","Which word sounds unlike the others, and why does that matter?","What view of memory do those details add up to?","What must the last sentence of a paragraph do, and what does this opening set up in the stanzas that follow?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many raw marks does Section A carry, and which assessment objective do they all belong to?","a":"20 raw marks, all of them AO2: analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects."},{"q":"You spot a link between the unseen poem and one of your anthology poems. Should you write about it?","a":"No. AO3 is not assessed in Section A, so a comparison earns nothing; spend the time on the unseen poem itself."},{"q":"What is the practical difference between ‘minimal identification’ and ‘cohesive evaluation’?","a":"Identification names the device and stops; evaluation traces how the method makes the meaning and weighs its effect across the whole poem."},{"q":"Roughly how long is Section A worth in a two-hour, 90-mark paper, and is there a choice of question?","a":"About twenty-seven minutes pro rata, and there is no choice: one compulsory question on the poem printed in the paper."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section A is one compulsory 20-mark essay question on an unseen poem; there is no choice of question and the poem is reproduced in the question paper.","All 20 raw marks in Section A are AO2: analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.","No AO1, AO3 or AO4 credit is available in Section A, so context, biography and comparison with another poem earn nothing.","Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark, closed book examination worth 60% of the International GCSE; learners may not bring texts but are provided with the relevant poems.","Section A is worth about twenty-seven minutes of Component 1, pro rata from its marks.","Alongside the set anthology poems, a selection of modern poetry should be studied in order to prepare for the unseen poetry assessment.","Marking is by levels of response on a best-fit basis; the language, form and structure strand runs from minimal identification at the bottom level to cohesive evaluation at the top."],"questions":[{"q":"Which single assessment objective is tested in Section A of Component 1, and how many raw marks does it carry?","a":"AO2 — analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects — carrying all 20 raw marks of Section A.","sol":"Section A tests AO2 only: ‘Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.’ It carries 20 raw marks and every one of them is AO2, so no AO1, AO3 or AO4 credit is available in this section. In practice that means a comparison with an anthology poem, a note on the poet’s life and an account of the period all earn nothing, however accurate; the marks are paid only for showing how the poem’s methods make its meaning.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Section A is the only question in the paper where you are not being tested on anything you learned beforehand.","Look at what the question stem asks you to do with the poem rather than at anything outside it.","Give the objective in the board’s own wording, then the whole-section tariff."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"State what the specification says about the conditions under which the Section A question is answered.","a":"The poem is reproduced in the question paper; there is no choice of question; Component 1 is closed book, so learners may not bring texts but are provided with the relevant poems.","sol":"The specification states that ‘the poem will be reproduced in the question paper’, so no preparation of a particular text is possible or needed. Section A offers one 20-mark essay question with no choice. Component 1 as a whole is a two-hour, 90-mark closed book examination worth 60% of the International GCSE, available as a paper-based or onscreen assessment: learners may not bring texts into the examination, but are provided with the relevant poems.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is physically in front of the candidate when they open the paper.","There are two separate things to state: what the paper gives you, and what you are not allowed to bring.","Say what appears in the question paper, then describe the examination conditions for Component 1."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Unseen Poetry"]},{"q":"Explore the effect of the verb ‘clasps’ in Tennyson’s line ‘He clasps the crag with crooked hands’.","a":"‘Clasps’ is a deliberate, muscular, almost human grip rather than a perch, and with ‘hands’ it makes the eagle a controlled, kingly figure holding his position by choice.","sol":"‘Clasps’ is a verb of deliberate holding, not of resting: it implies effort maintained, so the bird is not simply sitting on the rock but gripping it. Paired with ‘crooked hands’ rather than claws, it half-humanises the eagle and turns him into a figure of controlled, withheld power. The hard c sounds running through ‘clasps’, ‘crag’ and ‘crooked’ clench the line so that the grip is heard as well as seen. The effect matters for the whole poem: a bird that holds on so consciously is storing energy, which is what makes the sudden release of the final line feel earned.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the verb with the ones Tennyson did not use — sits, rests, perches.","Look at the noun it is paired with, and at the consonants repeated across the line.","Say what kind of hold the verb describes, then what that hold suggests about the bird."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry"]},{"q":"Define the term ‘volta’ and say where it is normally found in a Petrarchan sonnet and in a Shakespearean sonnet.","a":"The volta is the turn in a sonnet’s argument: in a Petrarchan sonnet it falls at line nine, between octave and sestet; in a Shakespearean sonnet it usually falls at line nine or in the closing couplet.","sol":"A volta is the turn in the argument of a sonnet — the point at which the poem changes direction, answers itself, qualifies what it has claimed or shifts from problem to resolution. In a Petrarchan sonnet, which divides into an eight-line octave and a six-line sestet, the volta falls at line nine; Rossetti’s ‘Remember’ turns there with ‘Yet if you should forget me for a while’. In a Shakespearean sonnet, built from three quatrains and a couplet, the turn usually comes either at line nine or at the final couplet. In an unseen poem, finding the turn is the quickest way to a structural point worth marks, because it tells you what the poem is arguing rather than merely describing.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The word means a turn, and the turn is in the thinking, not the subject matter.","Think about where the two standard sonnet forms divide their line groups.","Define it first, then attach it to the octave and sestet division."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry"]},{"q":"A student prepares for Section A by learning quotations from the anthology. Explore why this is not the preparation the specification asks for, and state what is.","a":"Section A is unseen and AO2-only, so learned quotations cannot be used and anthology knowledge earns nothing; the specification asks for a selection of modern poetry to be studied so that form and technique are familiar.","sol":"The Section A poem is unseen and is printed in the paper, so quotations learned from the anthology cannot be used, and because Section A assesses AO2 alone, knowledge of another poem earns no credit even where it is relevant. The specification asks instead that learners ‘Develop skills to analyse unseen poetry’, and it states that ‘Additionally, a selection of modern poetry should be studied in order to prepare for the unseen poetry assessment.’ The value of that reading is fluency, not content: a student who has already met free verse, half-rhyme, a dramatic monologue and a poem that turns in its last lines recognises the shape of the printed poem quickly and can spend the twenty-seven minutes on effect rather than on orientation.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the student would actually do with those quotations when the paper is open in front of them.","Two separate things go wrong here: one about the text, one about the assessment objective.","Explain why the stored material is unusable, then quote what the specification asks learners to read instead."],"codes":["Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Read the poem below and then answer the question that follows.\n\nAlfred, Lord Tennyson, ‘The Eagle’\n\nHe clasps the crag with crooked hands;\nClose to the sun in lonely lands,\nRing’d with the azure world, he stands.\n\nThe wrinkled sea beneath him crawls;\nHe watches from his mountain walls,\nAnd like a thunderbolt he falls.\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.)","a":"Stillness held then released: the gripping verbs and height imagery of the first tercet, the diminished world of the second, and the single-rhyme tercets that end the poem on ‘falls’.","sol":"Tennyson makes the eagle a study in withheld power: the poem holds the bird motionless for three lines so that its single movement, when it comes, can end the poem. The opening verb begins that work. ‘He clasps the crag with crooked hands’ gives the bird a grip rather than a perch, and ‘hands’ makes him quietly human, a figure who holds his position deliberately; the hard c sounds of ‘clasps’, ‘crag’ and ‘crooked’ clench the line, so the reader hears the grip as well as seeing it. The height is then made absolute rather than measured: ‘Close to the sun in lonely lands’ sets him beside the sun and above country he does not belong to, and ‘Ring’d with the azure world’ turns the sky into something that encircles him, so he is surrounded and separate at once. The stanza closes on ‘stands’, a verb of pure stasis that the single rhyme has been driving towards.\n\nThe second stanza reverses every one of those effects. The sea, far below, is reduced to a ‘wrinkled’ surface that ‘crawls’, so the poem measures the eagle’s height by shrinking the world rather than by describing a distance, and ‘He watches’ makes the earlier stillness predatory in retrospect. The last line, ‘And like a thunderbolt he falls’, spends everything that has been stored. The comparison carries more than speed: a thunderbolt belongs to the sky the eagle has been watching from, so the fall reads as the sky discharging itself rather than a bird leaving it. The line is almost entirely single-syllable words and ends on the verb, so it stops where the bird does.\n\nForm completes the argument. Two tercets, each locked into one rhyme, give the poem exactly two movements — hold, then release — and because the poem ends on the fall the reader is left in mid-air with it. The effect is not a description of an eagle but the experience of watching stored power become action, with the poem running out at the same instant.","m":20,"d":2,"hints":["Decide what the poem is doing as a whole before you look closely at any single word.","Ask what changes between the first stanza and the second, and what makes the change happen.","Open with one sentence stating your reading, and take the first paragraph from the moment that reading is clearest."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"Read the poem below and then answer the question that follows.\n\nWilliam Blake, ‘The Sick Rose’\n\nO Rose thou art sick.\nThe invisible worm,\nThat flies in the night\nIn the howling storm:\n\nHas found out thy bed\nOf crimson joy:\nAnd his dark secret love\nDoes thy life destroy.\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.)","a":"Two sentences of direct address — a one-line diagnosis, then a seven-line sentence — whose diction (‘bed’, ‘crimson joy’, ‘dark secret love’) lifts the poem out of the literal, with short quatrains and a withheld final verb making the corruption felt but unnamed.","sol":"Blake’s poem is an accusation spoken to the thing being destroyed, and its power comes from naming a corruption precisely while refusing to say what the corruption is. It falls into two sentences: a one-line diagnosis, and then a single seven-line sentence that runs to the end of the poem. ‘O Rose thou art sick.’ is an address, not a description: the apostrophe makes the reader overhear a diagnosis being delivered to the patient, and the full stop closes it before any explanation can arrive. The agent is then withheld twice. ‘The invisible worm’ cannot be seen, and it ‘flies in the night / In the howling storm’, so it is given a time and a weather but no shape; the howling belongs to the storm and to something else at once, and the reader supplies what the poem will not.\n\nThe second quatrain lets the flower reading come apart. ‘Has found out thy bed’ turns the rose’s centre into a bed and, through ‘found out’, into a secret discovered; ‘Of crimson joy’ replaces the colour of the petals with a feeling, so the rose is no longer being described but interpreted. ‘And his dark secret love’ is the point at which the image can no longer be read horticulturally at all: love that is dark and secret is a human property, and the possessive ‘his’ has already made the worm a person. The poem keeps both readings alive — a real blight, and a concealed corruption of desire — and its achievement is that it refuses to settle between them.\n\nThe form makes that refusal feel inevitable. Two short quatrains of very short lines leave the poem almost no room, and the colons closing ‘In the howling storm:’ and ‘Of crimson joy:’ hold that second sentence open across the stanza break, so the reader is carried forward without a resting place. The last word of the whole poem is the verb ‘destroy’: the grammar completes only at the moment the damage is named, and the poem stops there. The effect is a reader made to feel a corruption the poem never identifies.","m":20,"d":2,"hints":["Notice who the poem is speaking to, and where its full stops fall.","Sort the words into those that could describe a flower and those that could not.","Begin with the address, and keep the question of what the worm ‘is’ open rather than answering it."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"Read the poem below and then answer the question that follows.\n\nPercy Bysshe Shelley, ‘Ozymandias’\n\nI met a traveller from an antique land,\nWho said—“Two vast and trunkless legs of stone\nStand in the desert. . . . Near them, on the sand,\nHalf sunk a shattered visage lies, whose frown,\nAnd wrinkled lip, and sneer of cold command,\nTell that its sculptor well those passions read\nWhich yet survive, stamped on these lifeless things,\nThe hand that mocked them, and the heart that fed;\nAnd on the pedestal, these words appear:\nMy name is Ozymandias, King of Kings;\nLook on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!\nNothing beside remains. Round the decay\nOf that colossal Wreck, boundless and bare\nThe lone and level sands stretch far away.”\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.)","a":"Irony produced structurally: nested speakers, ruin described before the boast, the imperative on the pedestal answered by ‘Nothing beside remains.’ and a flat, unbroken closing line.","sol":"Shelley builds the poem so that a tyrant’s own words survive in order to defeat him, and the arrangement of voices does more of that work than any single image. Nothing reaches the reader directly: a speaker reports ‘a traveller from an antique land’, the traveller reports a ruin, the ruin carries a sculptor’s reading of a face, and the king’s inscription sits at the centre of all of it. Ozymandias is audible only inside somebody else’s anecdote, which is already an answer to his claim on the future.\n\nThe ruin is described before the boast is allowed to speak. ‘Two vast and trunkless legs of stone’ gives scale and mutilation in one phrase — ‘vast’ insists on the monument’s ambition, ‘trunkless’ removes the body it belonged to — and ‘Half sunk a shattered visage lies’ puts the face on the ground with the desert taking it back. The features are then read as evidence: the ‘frown, / And wrinkled lip, and sneer of cold command’ are symptoms rather than emotions, and ‘Tell that its sculptor well those passions read’ credits the artist, not the king, with the only lasting act of intelligence in the poem.\n\nThe inscription is placed exactly where it will do most damage to itself. ‘Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!’ is an imperative addressed to rivals assumed still to exist, and the capitalised ‘Works’ claims a scale the reader has already seen broken. The command still works, but on a target the king never intended: the despair now belongs to whoever reads it beside the wreck.\n\nThe turn is structural and audible. ‘Nothing beside remains.’ opens the twelfth line and stops inside it, so the poem’s flattest statement interrupts its grandest, and the fourteen lines never settle into a standard sonnet rhyme scheme, refusing the king the neatness he wanted. The final line, ‘The lone and level sands stretch far away’, is long, level and unbroken, spreading out with nothing in it to catch on. The effect is not an argument against tyranny but a demonstration: Shelley lets the desert answer the boast, and keeps the boast only as a ruin that someone else describes.","m":20,"d":2,"hints":["Work out how many people the king’s words have to pass through before they reach you.","Track where the poem places its shortest sentence and what it interrupts.","Start with the speakers rather than with a device, then show what the arrangement does to the boast."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2","Assessment overview: Section A – Unseen Poetry"]},{"q":"Read the poem below and then answer the question that follows.\n\nWilliam Wordsworth, ‘Composed upon Westminster Bridge, September 3, 1802’\n\nEarth has not any thing to shew more fair:\nDull would he be of soul who could pass by\nA sight so touching in its majesty:\nThis City now doth like a garment wear\nThe beauty of the morning; silent, bare,\nShips, towers, domes, theatres, and temples lie\nOpen unto the fields, and to the sky;\nAll bright and glittering in the smokeless air.\nNever did sun more beautifully steep\nIn his first splendour valley, rock, or hill;\nNe’er saw I, never felt, a calm so deep!\nThe river glideth at his own sweet will:\nDear God! the very houses seem asleep;\nAnd all that mighty heart is lying still!\n\n(‘shew’ is Wordsworth’s spelling of ‘show’.)\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.)","a":"A city given the language of landscape: opening superlative, the garment simile, the released list of buildings, and a sestet that turns inward to make the city one sleeping body.","sol":"Wordsworth takes the subject least expected of him — a city — and makes his effect out of that surprise, arguing by superlative, then by list, then by metaphor that London at dawn deserves the language usually kept for landscape. The opening is a claim rather than a scene: ‘Earth has not any thing to shew more fair’ measures the city against the whole world before a single detail arrives, and ‘Dull would he be of soul who could pass by’ makes disagreement a defect in the reader rather than a matter of taste.\n\nThe city is dressed rather than built. ‘This City now doth like a garment wear / The beauty of the morning’ makes the beauty something worn, and therefore removable; ‘now’ quietly concedes that the sight will not outlast the hour. The list that follows works by accumulation and then release: ‘Ships, towers, domes, theatres, and temples lie / Open unto the fields, and to the sky’ piles up the made things of the city and then lets them out into ‘fields’ and ‘sky’, with the enjambment carrying the eye out of the city in the same movement. ‘All bright and glittering in the smokeless air’ names the reason the vision is possible — the working day has not started — without ever saying so.\n\nThe sestet moves from sight to feeling. ‘Never did sun more beautifully steep / In his first splendour’ borrows its comparison from ‘valley, rock, or hill’, openly admitting the standard the city is being held to, and ‘Ne’er saw I, never felt, a calm so deep!’ turns the poem inward, the repetition and the exclamation making the judgement personal. The last two lines complete the central figure: ‘the very houses seem asleep; / And all that mighty heart is lying still!’ makes the city one sleeping body, so its stillness is rest rather than emptiness — the ‘mighty heart’ is the crowd and the trade, alive and merely paused. The effect of the whole sonnet is a held breath: it persuades the reader to see a city as a living thing at the one moment it is not moving.","m":20,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what kind of subject a reader of this period would expect, and what they are given instead.","Follow the movement from what is seen in the octave to what is felt in the sestet.","Open on the claim the first line makes, then show how the poem earns it."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"Read the poem below and then answer the question that follows.\n\nEmily Dickinson, ‘Hope is the thing with feathers’\n\n‘Hope’ is the thing with feathers –\nThat perches in the soul –\nAnd sings the tune without the words –\nAnd never stops – at all –\n\nAnd sweetest – in the Gale – is heard –\nAnd sore must be the storm –\nThat could abash the little Bird\nThat kept so many warm –\n\nI’ve heard it in the chillest land –\nAnd on the strangest Sea –\nYet – never – in Extremity,\nIt asked a crumb – of me.\n\nExplore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.)","a":"One extended metaphor tested to its limits, with dashes and capitals unsettling the definition; ‘abash’, ‘Extremity’ and ‘a crumb’ carry the argument that hope persists and asks nothing.","sol":"Dickinson’s poem is a definition that behaves like a riddle: it names hope once and then never lets go of the bird it has become, so the meaning is carried by how far a single metaphor can be pushed. The opening line puts ‘Hope’ in quotation marks, holding the word at arm’s length as a term under examination, and the definition refuses the expected abstraction: ‘the thing with feathers’ names the creature by its covering rather than its name, so the reader recognises a bird before being told there is one.\n\nThe metaphor is extended rather than repeated. Hope ‘perches in the soul’, which makes it a lodger rather than a possession and implies it could leave; it ‘sings the tune without the words’, so it is wordless persistence rather than belief in anything specific; and it ‘never stops – at all –’, a stubbornness the dashes refuse to punctuate into a proper close.\n\nThe second stanza tests the metaphor against weather. ‘And sweetest – in the Gale – is heard’ argues that hope is most audible when conditions are worst, and ‘sore must be the storm – / That could abash the little Bird’ concedes that hope can be silenced while insisting on the force it would take. ‘Abash’ is the sharpest choice in the poem: it means to shame rather than to destroy, so the real threat to hope is embarrassment, not violence.\n\nThe last stanza turns definition into debt. The speaker finally appears — ‘I’ve heard it in the chillest land – / And on the strangest Sea –’ — and the superlatives push hope to the edges of the known world before the closing turn: ‘Yet – never – in Extremity, / It asked a crumb – of me.’ The dashes isolate ‘never’ so the refusal lands emphatically, and ‘a crumb’ keeps the bird literal to the final word while measuring hope’s generosity in the smallest unit available. Throughout, the capitals — Gale, Bird, Sea, Extremity — raise ordinary nouns into forces, and the dashes leave almost every line unfinished, so the form itself never settles. That is the point: the poem enacts the unstopping thing it defines.","m":20,"d":2,"hints":["The poem announces what it is doing in its first line; ask how long it keeps doing it.","Treat the punctuation and the capital letters as choices, not as printing errors.","Begin with the definition the first line offers, then follow the bird through each stanza."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"A candidate has written this paragraph in answer to the 20-mark unseen question on Blake’s ‘The Sick Rose’:\n\nBlake uses lots of imagery in ‘The Sick Rose’. He says the worm is ‘invisible’ which shows that it cannot be seen. There is also alliteration and the poem is quite dark and sad, which makes the reader feel sorry for the rose because it is dying.\n\nIdentify the level this paragraph would sit in and explain why, then rewrite it so that it would sit in the top level. (Teaching exercise: Section A itself sets only the one compulsory 20-mark essay.)","a":"Level 1: devices are named, the quotation is glossed rather than analysed, and nothing is said about effect, form or structure. The rewrite must trace how ‘invisible’ makes the threat unlocatable.","sol":"This paragraph sits at Level 1, the bottom of the grid. It shows only minimal identification of language: ‘lots of imagery’ names nothing, ‘alliteration’ is asserted without being located, and the single quotation is glossed with a synonym — ‘invisible’ meaning ‘it cannot be seen’ — rather than analysed. ‘Dark and sad’ reports a mood instead of showing how it is made, and there is no comment at all on form or structure. Rewritten to the top level: ‘Blake withholds the threat rather than describing it. The worm is ‘invisible’, so the reader is given an agent that cannot be located, and the adjective does its work by subtraction — it removes the one thing that would make the danger manageable. Blake then supplies circumstances instead of a shape: the worm ‘flies in the night / In the howling storm’, giving the creature a time and a weather but still no body, and the howling is allowed to belong to the storm and to something else at once. Because the accusation is addressed to the rose, and its second sentence runs unbroken from ‘The invisible worm’ to the poem’s last word, the reader is held inside an accusation whose subject is never made visible, and the effect is a sense of harm that cannot be looked at directly.’","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the paragraph actually proves about the poem that a reader could not see for themselves.","Check the paragraph against the strand that runs from identification to evaluation.","Keep the candidate’s quotation and ask what the chosen adjective takes away from the reader."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"Two readings of Blake’s ‘The Sick Rose’ are offered. Reading A: the poem describes a real flower destroyed by a pest. Reading B: the poem describes a human love corrupted by secrecy. Explore which reading the language of the poem better supports, and explain what a top-level answer should do with the disagreement. (Teaching exercise: Section A itself sets only the one compulsory 20-mark essay.)","a":"Reading B is better supported by ‘bed’, ‘crimson joy’, ‘dark secret love’ and the possessive ‘his’; but a top-level answer keeps both live and argues that the poem’s effect depends on refusing to choose.","sol":"Reading A survives only the first quatrain. ‘O Rose thou art sick’ and ‘The invisible worm’ can both be read horticulturally, and blight is a real thing that arrives in wet, windy nights. The second quatrain makes that reading insufficient. ‘Has found out thy bed’ introduces discovery and concealment, which belong to people, not pests; ‘Of crimson joy’ swaps the colour of the flower for a feeling; ‘his dark secret love’ attributes to the worm a motive, a gender and a secret; and the poem’s last word, ‘destroy’, is placed so that its long second sentence completes only when the harm is named. The weight of the diction therefore supports Reading B. A top-level answer, however, does not simply announce that the poem is ‘really about’ human desire. It argues that the poem’s effect depends on holding both readings at once: because Blake names a corruption without naming its kind, the reader is made to feel something exact and unlocatable at the same time, and any reading that closes the ambiguity destroys the very effect it was meant to explain.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Test each reading against the poem quatrain by quatrain and see where one of them stops working.","Sort the diction of the second quatrain by the world each word belongs to.","Decide which reading the language supports, then ask whether the poem wants you to decide at all."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"AO3 is not assessed in Section A, but the skill of holding two poems together is examined elsewhere in Component 1. As preparation, explore the links and connections between the closing lines of Tennyson’s ‘The Eagle’ — ‘And like a thunderbolt he falls.’ — and Shelley’s ‘Ozymandias’ — ‘The lone and level sands stretch far away.’ Then state what you would do with that material if one of these were the unseen poem. (Teaching exercise: Section A itself sets only the one compulsory 20-mark essay.)","a":"Both end on a single decisive image that empties the poem of its subject, but one ends in motion and the other in stillness; in Section A the comparison itself would earn nothing and must be dropped.","sol":"Both poems place their whole meaning in the last line and let it close the poem absolutely. Tennyson ends in movement: ‘And like a thunderbolt he falls’ is almost all single-syllable words and finishes on the verb, so the poem stops at the instant the bird does, and the reader is left in the fall. Shelley ends in stillness: ‘The lone and level sands stretch far away’ is long, level and unbroken, and its sound spreads outwards with nothing to catch on, so the poem stops because there is nothing left to describe. The connection is in the technique — a final line that removes the subject from the poem — and the difference is in the direction, one emptying into action, the other into absence. In Section A, none of that comparison would score, because all 20 marks are AO2 and AO3 is not assessed there. What transfers is the observation itself: if either poem were printed as the unseen, the mark-bearing point is what that last line does inside its own poem, written without the second poem mentioned at all.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Start from what the two final lines have in common as a technique, not as a subject.","Listen to the length of the words and the direction of movement in each line.","Draw the connection first, then ask what would still be worth writing if only one poem were printed."],"codes":["Component 1: Content overview","Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore why an answer that works through an unseen poem line by line usually scores lower than an answer built on a single reading, referring to the levels of response. (Teaching exercise: Section A itself sets only the one compulsory 20-mark essay.)","a":"A line-by-line answer produces a list of separate identifications, which is the lower half of the grid; a reading produces cohesion, selection and evaluation, which is the top.","sol":"A line-by-line answer is organised by the poem rather than by an argument, so each paragraph begins again: a device is identified, a brief effect is asserted, and the answer moves down the page. That is the shape the grid describes as minimal identification rising at best to some comment on language, form and structure, and it is largely narrative, because retelling the poem in order is a kind of narration. It also forces the candidate to treat every line as equally important, so there is no selection and the answer usually runs out of time before the end of the poem. An answer built on a single reading behaves differently. It states early what the poem is doing, then chooses the moments that carry that reading, which produces the discriminating use of relevant examples the top level asks for; each paragraph adds to the same argument, which is what makes the response cohesive rather than a sequence; and because the reading has to be tested against the whole poem, including its form and its ending, the answer naturally evaluates rather than describes. The practical rule is to spend the first few minutes deciding the sentence your answer will prove, and to let that sentence choose the quotations.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what organises each kind of answer: the poem’s order, or the candidate’s argument.","Look at the words the grid uses for the top level and ask which shape of answer could produce them.","Describe what the weaker structure forces the candidate to do, then what the stronger one allows."],"codes":["Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2","Assessment overview: Section A – Unseen Poetry"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many raw marks does Section A carry, and which assessment objective are they all?","a":"20 raw marks, all AO2."},{"q":"Is the unseen poem printed in the question paper, and is there a choice of question?","a":"Yes, the poem is reproduced in the question paper, and there is no choice of question."},{"q":"How many marks does a comparison with an anthology poem earn in Section A?","a":"None — AO3 is not assessed in Section A."},{"q":"What is the term for the turn in a sonnet’s argument?","a":"The volta."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Volta","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"The turn in a sonnet’s argument. In a Petrarchan sonnet it falls at line nine, after the octave; in a Shakespearean sonnet it usually comes at line nine or in the closing couplet."},{"front":"Enjambment","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"A sentence running over the line break without punctuation. It hurries the reader on, or lets a phrase mean one thing at the line ending and another once the next line completes it."},{"front":"Caesura","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"A pause inside a line, usually made by punctuation. It breaks the movement and often marks a shift in thought, as when ‘Nothing beside remains.’ stops inside the twelfth line of ‘Ozymandias’."},{"front":"Extended metaphor","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"One comparison carried across a whole poem rather than dropped after a line. Dickinson keeps hope a bird from ‘feathers’ in the first line to ‘a crumb’ in the last."},{"front":"Semantic field","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"A group of words drawn from one area of meaning, such as ‘bed’, ‘crimson’, ‘secret’ and ‘love’ in ‘The Sick Rose’, which builds a reading the poem never states outright."},{"front":"Tercet","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"A three-line stanza. Tennyson’s ‘The Eagle’ is two tercets, each rhyming on a single sound, so each stanza closes on itself and the poem falls into exactly two movements."},{"front":"Iambic pentameter","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"Five unstressed-stressed pairs to the line, the standard line of the English sonnet. It is worth writing about mainly where it breaks: a reversed or added stress is where the analysis is."},{"front":"Tone and mood","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"Tone is the speaker’s attitude to the subject; mood is the feeling created in the reader. They need not match, and the point at which tone shifts is usually where the marks are."},{"front":"Section A: tariff and assessment objective","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"One compulsory 20-mark essay on an unseen poem, all 20 raw marks AO2. The poem is reproduced in the question paper and there is no choice of question."},{"front":"What earns no marks in Section A","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2","open":false,"back":"Context, biography, period and comparison with another poem. AO1, AO3 and AO4 carry no credit here, so only analysis of language, form and structure is paid for."},{"front":"AO2 in the board’s wording","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry — AO2","open":false,"back":"Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects."},{"front":"The reading routine for an unseen poem","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":false,"back":"Read it twice; say in one sentence what the poem is doing; find where it turns; pick three or four moments that carry that reading; check the form; then write."},{"front":"The wide-reading requirement for Section A","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false,"back":"Alongside the set anthology poems, a selection of modern poetry should be studied in order to prepare for the unseen poetry assessment."},{"front":"Signals that a poem is changing direction","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":true,"back":"A volta at line nine of a sonnet; a shift of tense, pronoun or speaker; a one-word sentence or heavy caesura; a change in stanza shape or line length."},{"front":"Structural features worth writing about in an unseen poem","code":"Section A: Unseen Poetry","open":true,"back":"Where the poem turns; the shape and length of the stanzas; what the final line does; where the main verb of a long sentence is placed; what changes between the opening and the close."}],"extras":[{"kind":"factfile","title":"Unseen poetry toolkit: the techniques, and what each one is worth","items":["Section A: one compulsory 20-mark essay on an unseen poem, all 20 marks AO2, poem reproduced in the question paper, no choice of question.","Time: about twenty-seven minutes, pro rata from the two-hour, 90-mark Component 1 paper.","Imagery — simile, metaphor, personification: always say what the comparison brings with it, never that it ‘creates a picture’.","Extended metaphor: one comparison carried across the whole poem, as hope stays a bird from ‘feathers’ to ‘crumb’ in Dickinson.","Semantic field: a cluster of words from one area of meaning that builds a reading the poem does not state, as ‘bed’, ‘crimson joy’ and ‘dark secret love’ do in ‘The Sick Rose’.","Diction and register: the kind of word chosen — ‘Unwontedly’ is formal and awkward among the plain words of ‘Adlestrop’, which is why it stands out.","Tone: the speaker’s attitude — reverent, wry, bitter, elegiac. Tone almost always shifts at least once, and the shift is the point worth writing about.","Mood: the feeling created in the reader, which need not match the speaker’s tone.","Sound — alliteration, assonance, sibilance, plosives: name what the repetition does to pace or mood; the device alone earns nothing.","Rhythm and metre: iambic pentameter is the default line of the English sonnet, and the break in the pattern is what to analyse.","Rhyme: full, half and internal. A poem that keeps a rhyme scheme and then abandons it at the end is telling you something.","Form: sonnet (Petrarchan octave and sestet; Shakespearean three quatrains and a couplet), ballad, dramatic monologue, free verse, blank verse.","Stanza shape: couplet, tercet, quatrain. Regular stanzas can enact control, and a broken last stanza can enact its loss.","Line endings: enjambment carries the sense over the break; end-stopping closes it. Say which the poem is doing at the moment you quote.","Caesura: the pause inside the line — ‘Nothing beside remains.’ stops inside the twelfth line of ‘Ozymandias’ and turns the poem.","Volta: the turn — line nine of a Petrarchan sonnet, often the couplet in a Shakespearean one, and somewhere in almost any poem worth analysing.","Structure across the whole poem: what changes between the first stanza and the last is the single most reliable point to make.","Speaker and address: work out who speaks and who is spoken to — Blake’s ‘The Sick Rose’ is addressed to the rose itself.","Title: it is part of the poem, and often supplies the reading the poem then complicates.","Punctuation and typography: Dickinson’s dashes and capitals are choices, and leaving lines unfinished is one of them.","The paragraph that scores: a point that answers the question, a short quotation, an explanation of how the method makes the meaning, and what it adds to the reading of the whole poem.","What scores nothing: feature-spotting lists, paraphrase, guesses about the poet’s life or period, and comparison with an anthology poem."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 (cohesive evaluation, discriminating examples)","items":["The question: explore the meaning and effects created in this poem. The printed poem is Shelley’s ‘Ozymandias’.","Shelley makes the pedestal the centre of the poem’s irony by letting the king speak in his own voice and then giving him nothing to speak over. ‘Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!’ is an imperative addressed to rivals who are assumed still to exist, and the capitalised ‘Works’ claims a scale the poem has already dismantled: by this point the reader has met only ‘trunkless legs’, a ‘shattered visage’ and, two lines later, a ‘colossal Wreck’. The command therefore still works, but on a target the king never intended — the despair the Mighty are ordered to feel becomes the reader’s recognition that nothing has survived, so the boast has turned into the evidence against itself. Shelley secures this structurally: ‘Nothing beside remains.’ opens the twelfth line and stops inside it, so the flattest statement in the poem interrupts the grandest, and the long final line, ‘The lone and level sands stretch far away’, spreads out with nothing left in it to break. The effect is that the poem does not argue against tyranny so much as let the desert answer it.","Why this reaches the top level: the quotation is chosen rather than collected; the analysis traces how the imperative and the capital letter make the meaning, instead of naming them; structure is used as evidence, not as decoration; and the paragraph closes on a judgement about the poem as a whole, which is what ‘cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure’ and ‘discriminating use of relevant examples’ describe."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (some comment, largely narrative)","items":["The same question, the same poem: explore the meaning and effects created in Shelley’s ‘Ozymandias’.","Shelley uses a lot of good description in this poem. He says ‘Look on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!’ which shows that Ozymandias was a very powerful king who wanted everyone to be scared of him. There is also an exclamation mark which makes it sound angry and powerful. Then the statue is broken and there is nothing left except sand, which shows that he is not powerful any more and that everything ends eventually. Shelley uses lots of imagery and adjectives like ‘colossal’ and ‘boundless’ to describe the scene and help the reader picture it.","Why this stays in the lower half: the quotation is present but is glossed rather than analysed — ‘which shows that’ retells the poem; the exclamation mark is named with no account of how it works; ‘lots of imagery and adjectives’ is a list, not analysis; and nothing is said about the frames of narration, the placing of ‘Nothing beside remains.’ or the sonnet form. That is ‘largely narrative’ with only ‘some comment on the language, form and structure’.","How to lift it without changing the quotation: ask who the command is spoken to, notice that the reader has already seen the statue broken before the boast arrives, and end on what the poem gains by finishing in sand."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Explore the meaning and effects created in this poem (20 marks), on Rossetti’s ‘Remember’","items":["The question as the paper sets it: explore the meaning and effects created in this poem. (Total for Question 1 = 20 marks.) The printed poem is Christina Rossetti’s sonnet ‘Remember’.","The printed poem, Christina Rossetti, ‘Remember’:\n\nRemember me when I am gone away,\nGone far away into the silent land;\nWhen you can no more hold me by the hand,\nNor I half turn to go yet turning stay.\nRemember me when no more day by day\nYou tell me of our future that you plann’d:\nOnly remember me; you understand\nIt will be late to counsel then or pray.\nYet if you should forget me for a while\nAnd afterwards remember, do not grieve:\nFor if the darkness and corruption leave\nA vestige of the thoughts that once I had,\nBetter by far you should forget and smile\nThan that you should remember and be sad.","Decoded: all 20 marks are AO2, so the real question is how this poem’s language, form and structure make its meaning. No context, no biography, no comparison — and ‘explore’ means open the effects up rather than retell the poem.","Thesis to state in the first two sentences: the poem asks to be remembered and then, at its turn, releases the listener from the asking, so its effect is of a demand withdrawn — an act of love performed by giving something up.","Paragraph one — the address and its repetition: ‘Remember me when I am gone away’ opens the poem and ‘Remember me’ returns at the fifth line, so the request is pressed twice before it is undone, while ‘the silent land’ keeps death euphemistic and gentle.","Paragraph two — the physical detail of the octave: ‘hold me by the hand’ and ‘half turn to go yet turning stay’ make the parting bodily and hesitant, and the line turns back on its own words as the speaker describes turning back.","Paragraph three — the volta: ‘Yet if you should forget me for a while’ opens the sestet and reverses the poem’s own demand, so ‘Yet’ is the hinge on which the whole argument swings.","Paragraph four — the close: ‘Better by far you should forget and smile / Than that you should remember and be sad’ sets the poem’s two governing verbs against each other and chooses the listener’s happiness over the speaker’s claim.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: the sonnet form is doing the arguing — the octave asks, the sestet gives way — so form here is not a label but the shape of a speaker changing her mind.","What the conclusion must say: name what the whole poem has done, turning an appeal for remembrance into permission to forget, and state the effect on the reader, who is left holding a kindness rather than a claim."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Working the twenty-seven minutes","items":["Read the poem twice before writing: once for what happens, once for how it moves.","Spend roughly five minutes reading and planning, twenty writing and two checking.","Write one sentence of thesis at the top: what the poem is doing, and how.","Four developed paragraphs is enough; each needs a short quotation and a chain of reasoning, not a list of devices.","Quote in phrases rather than whole lines — the poem is in front of you, so precision costs nothing.","Write about structure at least once: the turn, the stanza shape, or the last line.","Close on the effect of the whole poem rather than a summary of your own paragraphs.","Do not write about the poet’s life, the period, or another poem: none of it is assessed here."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T18:05:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"I re-solved all fourteen questions and checked all seven printed texts word by word — Tennyson's 'The Eagle' (with 'Ring'd' restored), Blake's 'The Sick Rose' on the plate punctuation, Shelley's 'Ozymandias' with the traveller's speech marked and the ellipsis after 'Stand in the desert', Wordsworth's Westminster Bridge sonnet with 'shew' and no editorial commas, Dickinson's 'Hope' is the thing with feathers, Rossetti's 'Remember' now printed in full inside the plan, and all sixteen lines of 'Adlestrop' — and every quotation, rhyme-scheme claim, volta location, tariff, AO split and specification quotation checks out against the text and against the Issue 3 specification; the answer keys are internally consistent throughout, so the remaining faults are presentational and this is ready to publish once the 'The Eagle' example setup is made self-contained."}}},"anthology-poetry":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"anthology-poetry","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section B of Component 1 is one 30-mark essay, chosen from two questions, comparing two poems from Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology. Fifteen raw marks are AO2, for analysing the language, form and structure a writer uses to create meanings and effects, and fifteen are AO3, for exploring links and connections between texts. That is a fifth of the whole qualification decided in about forty minutes. The poems are printed in the paper, so nobody is testing your memory of the words; the examiner is testing whether you can hold two poems in one argument. Either question may name any of the sixteen poems, and one of the two will usually leave the second poem to you, so every poem in Part 3 has to be known well enough to be chosen under pressure. This room teaches the sixteen poems as a set of possible pairings, and teaches the one thing that separates a Level 5 answer from a Level 3 one: comparison that runs through every paragraph instead of two essays stapled together.","idea":[{"h":"One question, two poems, two assessment objectives","t":"Section B gives you one 30-mark essay from a choice of two questions, comparing two poems from Part 3 of the anthology. Half the marks are AO2 and half are AO3, which means an answer that analyses beautifully but keeps the poems apart throws away fifteen marks, and an answer that keeps saying both poems are similar without analysing anything throws away the other fifteen. The specification asks you to demonstrate understanding of how writers create literary effects using imagery, descriptive skills, language choice, tone and mood, structure and form; to understand and use appropriate literary terminology; to make comparisons between poems, including use of language, structure and form; and to identify and use relevant examples from poems. A top-level answer builds each paragraph round one point of comparison, analyses a method in both poems inside that paragraph, and states the link or the difference in so many words before moving on.","code":"Section B: Anthology Poetry"},{"h":"If– by Rudyard Kipling: one sentence held open","t":"Kipling addresses a son and sets out the conduct that will earn him the world and his manhood. The poem is four eight-line stanzas of iambic pentameter with alternating rhyme, and almost the whole of it is a single suspended conditional sentence: the anaphora of the repeated conditional opening piles condition on condition, and the main clause is withheld until the last two lines. Antithesis governs the content, pairing opposites such as triumph and disaster, kings and the common crowd, and Triumph and Disaster are personified as two impostors who must be treated exactly alike. In Section B it is most often set against another poem about advice, inheritance or self-command, and Poem at Thirty-Nine is its natural partner. A top-level answer argues that the withheld main clause is the poem's meaning, because the reward cannot arrive until every condition has been met, and then asks whether so much self-control is admirable or inhuman.","code":"Part 3: If–"},{"h":"Prayer Before Birth by Louis MacNeice: a voice from before life","t":"An unborn child speaks, which is the poem's central and impossible conceit. MacNeice writes it as a litany: every stanza opens with the same statement that the speaker is not yet born, and then makes a request to be heard, consoled, provided for, forgiven and rehearsed. The stanzas swell as the fears multiply through a catalogue of threats, from the bloodsucking bat and the club-footed ghoul to the walls, the drugs and the lies of a modern state, and then collapse into a final short stanza that asks to be killed rather than turned into a thing without humanity. The verse is free but packed with alliteration, internal rhyme and cumulative listing, so it sounds incantatory. Written in 1944, it is a poem about totalitarian power and the erasure of the individual. A top-level answer links the sudden shrinking of the last stanza to the shrinking of the child's hope, rather than simply listing the frightening images.","code":"Part 3: Prayer Before Birth"},{"h":"Blessing by Imtiaz Dharker: water as sacrament","t":"A municipal water pipe bursts in a place of drought and the whole neighbourhood runs out with pots and buckets to catch what it can. Dharker begins with almost no water at all: skin compared to a cracking pod, the imagined drip of it into a tin mug and the echo it makes there. Then the pipe bursts, the sudden crash is described as silver, and the short broken lines give way to a long rush of naming as the crowd arrives with brass, copper, aluminium and plastic. The register is religious throughout, with the blessing of the title, a congregation, a kindly god and a flow that is treated as a gift, and the poem closes on the children's small bones and on light flashing off wet skin. A top-level answer shows that the poem's shape imitates a drip becoming a flood, and that the language of worship both celebrates the moment and measures how rare and how fragile it is.","code":"Part 3: Blessing"},{"h":"Search For My Tongue by Sujata Bhatt: two tongues in one mouth","t":"Bhatt takes the word tongue in both its senses, the organ in the mouth and the language it speaks, and runs that extended metaphor through the whole poem. The speaker answers a question put to her about what happens when you carry two tongues and must live in the foreign one: the mother tongue rots, and would die in the mouth. Then the poem does something no summary can do for it. The middle section leaves English altogether, appearing in Gujarati script with a transcription, before an English translation returns. In that section the mother tongue grows back overnight like a plant, in buds, veins and blossom. A top-level answer does not merely note that Gujarati appears; it argues that the poem proves its claim by performing it, since the mother tongue physically takes the page over before English is allowed back, and the form is therefore the evidence for the argument about identity.","code":"Part 3: Search For My Tongue"},{"h":"Half-past Two by U A Fanthorpe: a child outside time","t":"A small boy is kept in after school for doing Something Very Wrong, which the poem capitalises and never explains, and is left alone in a room with a clock he cannot read. Fanthorpe writes his world in compound coinages, running the parts of his day into single words for getting-up time, the time he is told to be off, television time and the time to go home, so the reader is placed inside a mind that measures time by events rather than numbers. The poem is in loose three-line stanzas, and the clock face is read as a creature with little eyes and two long legs rather than as a dial that can be told. The boy slips out of measured time altogether until the teacher comes back and slots him into the school day again. A top-level answer treats the coined words as the poem's argument about how children hold time, and reads the teacher's return as an adult measurement closing over a child's freedom.","code":"Part 3: Half-past Two"},{"h":"Piano by D H Lawrence: ambushed by a song","t":"A man listens to a woman singing to him in the dusk and is carried back to childhood, sitting under the piano and pressing his mother's small poised feet while she played and sang. Lawrence writes three quatrains in rhyming couplets, and that regularity works like the tune that is doing the damage: the form keeps its shape while the speaker loses his. The turn of the poem is a resistance rather than a surrender. He calls the song's mastery insidious and says it betrays him, which admits that he did not want to go back at all. The last stanza gives up the fight, with the black piano, played appassionato, and the remembered glamour of childhood beating on the present until a grown man weeps like a child. A top-level answer argues that the poem is a struggle between an adult's wish not to be moved and music's power to move him, and that the verse form never breaks even when the speaker does.","code":"Part 3: Piano"},{"h":"Hide and Seek by Vernon Scannell: the game that ends badly","t":"A boy hides in a garden shed among sacks that smell of the sea and waits to be found. Scannell writes the poem as one unbroken verse paragraph of unrhymed lines, and addresses the boy as you throughout, so the reader crouches in the dark with him; direct speech breaks in as he calls to the seekers and as they call to one another outside. The detail is cold and physical, all salty sacks, a stiff crouch and a dark that smells of the sea, and the waiting is measured only by the sounds beyond the door. When he finally comes out in triumph the garden is empty, the garden itself is said to watch and the bushes to hold their breath, and the silence is not comfort but abandonment. A top-level answer argues that the second-person address is the poem's method: the trick it plays is to make the reader hide too, so the final emptiness happens to us.","code":"Part 3: Hide and Seek"},{"h":"Sonnet 116 by William Shakespeare: love defined by what it is not","t":"Shakespeare defines constancy by negation, refusing to call it love at all if it changes when it meets change. The form is a Shakespearean sonnet, three quatrains and a closing couplet rhyming ABAB CDCD EFEF GG, in iambic pentameter. The governing metaphors are nautical: love is an ever-fixed mark that faces down tempests, and the star by which a wandering ship steers, whose worth cannot be calculated even though its height can be measured. Time is then personified with a bending sickle, and love is declared no fool of Time, holding out to the edge of doom. The couplet turns the poem into a wager, staking the poet's whole art and every man's experience of love on the truth of the claim. A top-level answer reads the sonnet as an argument with a proof and a bet rather than as a description of feeling, and notices how much of it is built out of what love does not do.","code":"Part 3: Sonnet 116: Let me not to the marriage of true minds"},{"h":"La Belle Dame sans Merci by John Keats: a ballad that will not explain itself","t":"Keats writes a medieval-style ballad in twelve quatrains rhyming ABCB, with three lines of tetrameter and a fourth cut short, so that every stanza falls away beneath the reader. An unnamed questioner finds a knight-at-arms alone and pale beside a lake where the sedge has withered and no birds sing. The knight tells how he met a beautiful woman in the meadows, made her garlands and bracelets, was given strange sweet food, was taken to an elfin grotto where she wept, and dreamt of pale kings and warriors who told him the beautiful lady without mercy had him in thrall. He woke on the cold hillside, and the closing stanza repeats the opening almost word for word, so the poem has no way out of itself. A top-level answer uses the truncated fourth line and the circular structure as its evidence that enchantment, in this poem, is a trap rather than an experience.","code":"Part 3: La Belle Dame sans Merci"},{"h":"Poem at Thirty-Nine by Alice Walker: growing into a father","t":"Walker's speaker, at thirty-nine, misses her father and takes stock of what he gave her. The poem is free verse in short, plain lines and uneven stanzas, and the statement of missing him is repeated so that the poem is organised by grief rather than by argument. The first movement is what he taught: how to write, and how to keep track of money on paper, with a rueful acknowledgement of how tired he already was by the time she was born. The second movement turns to cooking, to the image of him cooking as though he were dancing, and to her belief that he would have admired the woman she became. A top-level answer notices that the poem travels from regret to reconciliation without ever dropping the repeated line of loss, that the plainness of the diction is a choice and not a thinness, and that the daughter ends by finding her father inside her own habits.","code":"Part 3: Poem at Thirty-Nine"},{"h":"War Photographer by Carol Ann Duffy: the darkroom and the supplement","t":"A photographer develops his films alone, and Duffy sets his ordered spools against the disorder they record. Four six-line stanzas of steady rhythm and rhyme give the poem exactly the discipline the man is trying to keep. The red safelight turns the darkroom into a church and the photographer into a priest at a Mass, and the biblical tag from Isaiah, that all flesh is grass, is set beside a list of place names taken from real wars. As one print forms, the features of a dying man come back, and with them the memory of his wife's wordless consent to be photographed. The final stanza measures two gaps: between a hundred agonies and the five or six an editor will print, and between a reader's brief tears and their lunch. A top-level answer argues that the poem's order is the photographer's defence against what he has seen, and that the last stanza takes even that defence away.","code":"Part 3: War Photographer"},{"h":"The Tyger by William Blake: a poem made of questions","t":"Blake's poem is six quatrains of rhyming couplets in a hammering trochaic tetrameter with the final syllable cut, so the rhythm beats like the forge it describes. Almost every sentence in it is a question and not one of them is answered: what immortal hand or eye could frame such fearful symmetry, and what furnace, anvil, hammer and chain were used. The imagery is a blacksmith's, so the creator is imagined as a craftsman working in fire, and the danger of the work is part of the wonder. The fifth stanza asks whether the maker of the lamb also made this creature, which sends the reader to the companion poem in Songs of Innocence. The last stanza then repeats the first with a single word altered, from could to dare. A top-level answer builds on that one alteration, because it moves the poem from awe at the maker's skill to fear about the maker's will.","code":"Part 3: The Tyger"},{"h":"My Last Duchess by Robert Browning: a monologue that convicts its speaker","t":"The Duke of Ferrara draws back a curtain for an envoy, shows him a painted portrait of his previous wife, and in explaining her condemns himself. Browning writes twenty-eight rhyming couplets of iambic pentameter, but heavy enjambment runs the sense across the rhymes so that the verse sounds like a man talking easily while the form quietly holds him in place. The Duke's complaint is that her thanks were given too widely and that she ranked his ancient family name alongside anybody's gift; he says he chose never to stoop to explaining himself, and then that he gave commands and the smiles stopped. He turns immediately to the envoy's master's dowry and, on the stairs, points out a bronze of Neptune taming a sea-horse. A top-level answer treats the art objects as the argument: a wife behind a curtain only he may draw, and a god taming a creature, make the same statement about ownership.","code":"Part 3: My Last Duchess"},{"h":"Half-caste by John Agard: taking an insult literally","t":"Agard answers the word half-caste by pretending to believe it, and the joke is the argument. The speaker demands that the listener explain the term, then offers absurd parallels: a painter who mixes two colours must have made a half-caste canvas, English weather that mixes light and shade must be half-caste, a composer who uses the black keys as well as the white must have written a half-caste symphony. The poem is written in a Caribbean Creole voice with phonetic spelling and almost no punctuation until the very end, so it moves at the speed of live speech, and the demand for an explanation returns as a refrain. At the close the tone changes: the listener is told to come back another day with the whole of their eyes, ears and mind, and then the other half of the story will be told. A top-level answer shows that this shift of register is the moment comedy turns into accusation.","code":"Part 3: Half-caste"},{"h":"Do not go gentle into that good night by Dylan Thomas: a form that will not let go","t":"Thomas writes a villanelle: nineteen lines on two rhymes, five three-line stanzas and a closing quatrain, with two refrains that alternate through the poem and then come together as the final two lines. The middle stanzas take four kinds of men in turn, wise, good, wild and grave, and show each of them, whatever they achieved or failed to achieve, refusing to go quietly. Night and light are the poem's metaphors for death and life, and the compulsory repetition makes the argument sound like something the speaker cannot stop saying. Only in the last quatrain does the poem admit its occasion: the speaker addresses his own dying father on a sad height and asks to be cursed or blessed, so long as there is fierceness in it. A top-level answer uses the villanelle itself as evidence, since a form built on return is the perfect vehicle for a refusal to let a man go.","code":"Part 3: Do not go gentle into that good night"},{"h":"Remember by Christina Rossetti: an instruction withdrawn","t":"Rossetti's Petrarchan sonnet is built on a turn. The octave, rhyming ABBAABBA, repeats the imperative to remember her once she has gone into the silent land, when there can be no more holding of hands and no more daily talk of a planned future. At the ninth line the sestet turns with a concession: if you should forget me for a while and afterwards remember, do not grieve. The closing antithesis prefers that the living forget and smile rather than remember and be sad, so the poem gives away the very thing it opened by demanding. Euphemism keeps death at a polite distance throughout, and the consonants stay soft where a poem of protest would harden them. A top-level answer argues that the volta is itself the act of love, and compares that withdrawal with poems that refuse to release the dying, so that generosity and defiance can be weighed against each other.","code":"Part 3: Remember"}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing the second poem and fixing the comparison thread","setup":"Question: Compare how the poets present memories of childhood in 'Half-past Two' and one other poem from Part 3 of the Anthology. You must consider language, form and structure. (Component 1, Section B; thirty marks, half AO2 and half AO3; the poems are printed in the paper.) The poems in Part 3 that touch childhood are 'Piano', 'Hide and Seek' and 'Poem at Thirty-Nine'. What you have in front of you: Fanthorpe's poem is in loose three-line stanzas without rhyme, told by a narrator standing at the child's shoulder in the third person and using the boy's own compound words for the parts of his day, so the childhood is being lived rather than recalled. Lawrence's poem is three quatrains of rhyming couplets in a regular metre, spoken in the first person by an adult man pulled back into childhood against his will. Scannell's poem is one unbroken verse paragraph of unrhymed lines that addresses the boy as you, putting the reader inside the child's experience as it happens. Walker's poem is in free verse, spoken in the first person by a grown woman looking back at her father and at what he handed on. Before writing a word of the essay you must do two things: choose the second poem, and write the single sentence that will control every paragraph.","steps":["Fanthorpe puts the reader inside a mind as it experiences the day, using the boy's own run-together words for the parts of it, so nothing in her poem is being recalled by an adult at all.","Scannell is also inside the child's experience and pushes it further by calling the boy you, so pairing those two gives agreement about viewpoint and almost nothing to argue about.","Walker's childhood is remembered by a grown woman, but her subject is a father and an inheritance rather than time itself, so the link to Fanthorpe would have to be forced.","Take 'Piano': Lawrence gives an adult dragged back into childhood against his will, the exact opposite of a child simply living inside it, so the pairing offers a real difference of viewpoint and of verse form.","Thread sentence: in both poems the form carries the viewpoint, since Fanthorpe's coined words and loose tercets keep the reader inside an unmeasured childhood while Lawrence's regular rhyming quatrains are the very tune that pulls a resisting adult backwards."],"cues":["Whose mind does the named poem put you inside, and how can you tell?","What is the weakness of pairing it with another poem told from inside childhood?","Why is a poem about a parent a risky choice for this particular question?","Which of the three gives the sharpest contrast of viewpoint?","What one sentence would make language, form and structure the basis of the whole comparison?"]},{"title":"One paragraph comparing form: the sonnet against the ballad","setup":"Question: Compare how the poets present love in 'Sonnet 116' and 'La Belle Dame sans Merci'. You must consider language, form and structure. (Thirty marks.) What you have in front of you: Shakespeare's poem is a sonnet of three quatrains and a couplet in iambic pentameter, which defines love by saying what it is not, calls it a fixed sea-mark that faces storms and the star a wandering ship steers by, personifies Time with a sickle, and ends by staking the poet's whole art on the claim. Keats's poem is a ballad of twelve quatrains rhyming ABCB in which the fourth line of every stanza is cut short; a pale knight is found beside a withered lake, tells of a beautiful woman who fed him strange food and took him to her grotto, dreams of pale kings who name her, wakes on a cold hillside, and the final stanza repeats the opening. Write one comparison paragraph on form.","steps":["Point: the two poets choose forms with opposite ambitions, one built to close an argument and one built to turn back on itself.","Shakespeare: the sonnet gives him a turn and a final couplet, so constancy can be asserted, tested against storms and against Time, and then gambled on in two lines that the form guarantees will land.","Keats: the shortened fourth line drops away under each stanza so that nothing settles, and the last stanza's return to the first means the knight ends exactly where the poem found him.","Link stated: in both poems the shape is the proof of the claim, because a love that endures is argued in a form that resolves and an enchantment with no escape is argued in a form that cannot end.","Mini-judgement: Shakespeare's love survives Time because his form knows how to finish, whereas Keats's love destroys precisely because his never does."],"cues":["What does each form seem designed to do before you look at the content?","Which two features of the sonnet let a claim be settled?","What happens to the reader at the end of each Keats stanza, and at the end of the poem?","How would you state the connection in one sentence, using the word both or whereas?","On this evidence, which poem's form is the more frightening, and why?"]},{"title":"Comparing structure in two poems about dying","setup":"Question: Compare how the poets present attitudes to death in 'Do not go gentle into that good night' and 'Remember'. You must consider language, form and structure. (Thirty marks.) What you have: Thomas's poem is a villanelle of nineteen lines with two refrains that alternate through five tercets and then close the final quatrain together; its middle stanzas take wise, good, wild and grave men in turn, and only at the end does the speaker address his own dying father. Rossetti's poem is a Petrarchan sonnet whose octave repeats an instruction to remember her once she has gone into the silent land, and whose sestet turns at the ninth line to release the listener from that instruction, preferring that they forget and smile rather than remember and be sad. Write the paragraph that compares how each poem's structure carries its attitude to death.","steps":["Point: each poet chooses a form whose characteristic movement is the attitude itself, one that returns and one that turns.","Thomas: a villanelle cannot go forward without bringing its refrains back, so the demand for resistance is repeated whether or not it is heard, and the two refrains landing together at the close make the ending an insistence rather than a resolution.","Rossetti: the sonnet's volta lets her reverse her own imperative in a single word of concession, so the structure enacts a giving-up of the claim she spent eight lines making.","Link stated: both poems are addressed to someone who is being left or leaving, but repetition holds on where the turn lets go, which is the difference between defiance and blessing.","Mini-judgement: Thomas's refusal is the more moving to read and Rossetti's release is the more loving to receive, since hers costs the speaker the one thing she wanted."],"cues":["What is the characteristic movement of each of these two forms?","Why does a refrain suit a speaker who will not accept an outcome?","What does a volta allow a speaker to do that a refrain does not?","How can the difference be put in one sentence that names both forms?","Which attitude costs its speaker more, and what does that let you judge?"]},{"title":"Comparing voice in two poems about language and identity","setup":"Question: Compare how the poets present identity in 'Half-caste' and 'Search For My Tongue'. You must consider language, form and structure. (Thirty marks.) What you have: Agard writes in a Caribbean Creole voice with phonetic spelling and almost no punctuation until the end, repeatedly demanding that the listener explain the word half-caste, and offering absurd parallels from painting, weather and music before changing register at the close to invite the listener back for the other half of his story. Bhatt runs an extended metaphor in which tongue means both the organ and the language, describes the mother tongue rotting in a mouth that must use a foreign one, and then leaves English altogether for a section in Gujarati script with a transcription, in which the mother tongue grows back overnight like a plant. Write the paragraph comparing how each poet uses voice and layout to present identity.","steps":["Point: neither poet describes a divided identity from outside; each makes the page behave like the mouth it is describing.","Agard: the non-standard spelling and missing punctuation put a spoken voice where an examiner expects a written one, so the poem is already refusing the standard it is being judged by before the argument begins.","Bhatt: her middle section removes English from the page entirely, so a reader of English is briefly locked out and experiences the exclusion the poem is about.","Link stated: both poets make form the evidence, but Agard's method is confrontation, addressing the listener directly, while Bhatt's is demonstration, letting the other language take the space and prove it is alive.","Mini-judgement: Agard's ending, which turns comedy into a demand for attention, and Bhatt's, in which the mother tongue simply wins the page back, are two versions of the same refusal to be halved."],"cues":["What do these two poems have in common in how they use the page itself?","What is the effect of spelling speech as it sounds in an examined text?","What happens to an English-only reader in the middle of Bhatt's poem?","How would you name the difference between the two methods in one phrase each?","What judgement can you make about which method is the more persuasive?"]},{"title":"Turning an analysis point into a comparison link","setup":"Question: Compare how the poets present the effects of violence in 'War Photographer' and 'Prayer Before Birth'. You must consider language, form and structure. (Thirty marks.) You have written a correct but unconnected AO2 point on each poem. On Duffy: the four six-line stanzas keep a steady rhythm and rhyme, the darkroom's red light makes a church of the room and a priest of the man, and the last stanza sets a hundred agonies against the handful an editor prints and against a reader's tears between the bath and lunch. On MacNeice: each stanza opens with the same statement that the speaker is not yet born, the catalogue of threats grows longer as the poem goes on, and the final stanza is suddenly very short and asks to be killed rather than made into a thing. The task is to convert these two separate points into one comparative paragraph that would earn AO3.","steps":["Find the shared idea underneath both points: each poet uses an orderly pattern to contain horror, and then shows the pattern failing or being abandoned.","Rebuild the Duffy point so that it serves that idea: the regular stanzas and the priestly calm are a professional defence, and the final stanza withdraws it by turning to the indifference waiting at home.","Rebuild the MacNeice point the same way: the repeated opening is a ritual the speaker clings to, and the collapse of the last stanza is that ritual breaking under the weight of what has been listed.","Write the connective sentence that makes it comparison rather than coincidence: in both poems the shape of the verse is a defence against violence, but Duffy's defence is professional detachment and MacNeice's is prayer.","Close with a judgement that holds both poems: Duffy's speaker survives by being paid to look away, while MacNeice's has not been born yet and can only ask not to begin, so the same formal collapse means exhaustion in one poem and terror in the other."],"cues":["What single idea is hiding underneath these two separate observations?","How should the Duffy point be rephrased so it answers that idea rather than describing the poem?","What is the equivalent move for the MacNeice point?","Which one sentence turns two parallel points into a comparison?","What judgement can be made that is true of both poems at once?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does Section B carry, and how are they divided between the assessment objectives?","a":"Thirty marks: fifteen for AO2, analysing language, form and structure, and fifteen for AO3, exploring links and connections between texts."},{"q":"Why is an answer that writes about one poem and then the other unlikely to pass Level 3, however good the analysis is?","a":"Because half the marks are AO3, which is awarded for links and connections; analysis kept in separate halves cannot show them, so up to fifteen marks are unavailable."},{"q":"The poems are printed in the examination paper. What does that change about how you revise?","a":"Learning quotations by heart matters less than knowing each poem's form, methods and arguable meaning, and knowing which poems pair well with which."},{"q":"What does the instruction to consider language, form and structure require beyond naming devices?","a":"Tracing how the chosen method creates meaning and effect; the grid moves from identification at Level 1 to sustained analysis at Level 4 and cohesive evaluation at Level 5."},{"q":"A question names one poem and leaves the second to you. What makes a good choice?","a":"A poem that shares the question's idea but differs in method or attitude, so that there is a real difference of language, form or structure to analyse rather than a list of similarities."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section B of Component 1 is one 30-mark essay chosen from two questions, comparing two poems from Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology; 15 raw marks are AO2 and 15 are AO3.","Component 1 lasts two hours and is worth 90 marks and 60 per cent of the International GCSE; Section B is worth about forty minutes of that time.","The anthology poems are provided in the examination paper, but no other text may be brought in: Component 1 is otherwise closed book.","Part 3 contains sixteen poems: If–, Prayer Before Birth, Blessing, Search For My Tongue, Half-past Two, Piano, Hide and Seek, Sonnet 116, La Belle Dame sans Merci, Poem at Thirty-Nine, War Photographer, The Tyger, My Last Duchess, Half-caste, Do not go gentle into that good night and Remember.","AO3 is worth only 10 per cent of the whole qualification and every one of those marks is in this section, so comparison must be present in every developed paragraph.","The assessment grid runs from Level 1 to Level 5 and is applied on a best fit basis; Level 4 requires sustained analysis of language, form and structure and Level 5 requires cohesive evaluation with discriminating use of relevant examples."],"questions":[{"q":"Name the form of 'Do not go gentle into that good night', state how many lines it has and how its stanzas are arranged, and explain how its two refrains are used. (short-response practice task)","a":"A villanelle: nineteen lines on two rhymes, five tercets and a closing quatrain, with two refrains alternating as the last line of each tercet and appearing together as the final two lines.","sol":"Dylan Thomas writes a villanelle. It runs to nineteen lines built on only two rhyme sounds, arranged as five three-line stanzas followed by one four-line stanza. Two refrain lines, the one that gives the poem its title and the one that commands rage at the failing light, take turns to end the tercets and are then placed together as the last two lines of the quatrain. The effect matters as much as the label: a form that cannot advance without dragging its own lines back with it is exactly right for a speaker who refuses to let his father go, and the moment the two refrains finally meet is the moment the argument stops being general and becomes a plea to one dying man.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The form is a fixed one built entirely out of repetition, so listen for what keeps coming back.","Count the stanzas, then count how many different rhyme sounds and how many repeated lines there are.","Shape the answer in three parts: the name of the form, its length and stanza pattern, and where the repeated lines fall."],"codes":["Part 3: Do not go gentle into that good night"]},{"q":"Two of the sixteen Part 3 poems are sonnets. Identify both and state which kind of sonnet each one is, with its rhyme pattern. (short-response practice task)","a":"'Sonnet 116' by Shakespeare, a Shakespearean sonnet of three quatrains and a couplet rhyming ABAB CDCD EFEF GG; 'Remember' by Christina Rossetti, a Petrarchan sonnet with an octave rhyming ABBAABBA and a sestet, turning at line nine.","sol":"The first is Shakespeare's 'Sonnet 116', a Shakespearean or English sonnet: three quatrains rhyming ABAB CDCD EFEF and a final couplet rhyming GG, in iambic pentameter. Its shape lets the poet build a definition of constant love across the quatrains and then stake everything on it in two closing lines. The second is Christina Rossetti's 'Remember', a Petrarchan or Italian sonnet: an octave rhyming ABBAABBA followed by a sestet, with the volta at the ninth line. That shape suits her purpose exactly, because the octave can make a demand to be remembered and the sestet can take it back. Both are fourteen lines of iambic pentameter, so the useful comparison is not that they are sonnets but what each uses the sonnet's turn to do.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One is by a Renaissance dramatist and one by a Victorian poet; both are fourteen lines long.","For each, work out where the poem changes direction and how the rhymes are grouped around that point.","Give the title and poet, then the type of sonnet, then the rhyme grouping for each."],"codes":["Part 3: Sonnet 116: Let me not to the marriage of true minds","Part 3: Remember"]},{"q":"Identify the speaker and the listener in 'My Last Duchess', and explain what the Duke reveals about himself that he does not intend to reveal. (short-response practice task)","a":"The speaker is the Duke of Ferrara; the listener is an envoy sent by a count whose daughter the Duke intends to marry. He means to complain about his previous wife but discloses his own jealousy and his part in her death.","sol":"The poem is a dramatic monologue spoken by the Duke of Ferrara to the envoy of a nobleman whose daughter he is negotiating to marry; the listener never speaks, though the Duke's own words show him reacting. The Duke intends to explain that his previous wife was too easily pleased and too free with her thanks, and that she ranked his ancient family name with any other gift. What he actually discloses is his need to control how a woman looks and smiles, his pride in never stooping to speak to her about it, and, in the flat remark that he gave commands and the smiles stopped, his responsibility for her death. Browning then lets him move straight to the question of the dowry and to a bronze of a god taming a creature, so the envoy, and the reader, are shown exactly what kind of marriage is being offered.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who is being shown the portrait, and why that visitor has come to the palace.","Look for the places where the Duke's explanation would sound worse if he stopped to think about it.","Answer in three parts: who speaks, who listens, and what the speech gives away."],"codes":["Part 3: My Last Duchess"]},{"q":"State the form of 'La Belle Dame sans Merci' and explain the effect of its shortened fourth line and of its final stanza. (short-response practice task)","a":"A ballad of twelve quatrains rhyming ABCB, with three tetrameter lines and a curtailed fourth; the short line makes each stanza fall away, and the last stanza repeats the first so the poem closes as a circle.","sol":"Keats imitates the medieval ballad: twelve four-line stanzas rhyming ABCB, the first three lines in tetrameter and the fourth cut short. The truncation is the poem's most useful technical feature, because every stanza drops away beneath the reader just as it gathers momentum, and that sensation of being left suspended matches the knight's condition exactly. The closing stanza then returns almost word for word to the opening description of a pale knight loitering where the sedge has withered and no birds sing. Because the frame shuts on the same image it opened with, the poem offers no escape and no explanation: the reader is returned to the cold hillside along with the knight, which is how the form makes enchantment feel like a trap rather than an adventure.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["It imitates an old story-telling form, so listen for what a story-teller's stanza usually does.","Read the last line of any stanza aloud and notice what your ear expects and does not get.","Answer in two halves: the form and rhyme, then the effect of the short line and of the ending."],"codes":["Part 3: La Belle Dame sans Merci"]},{"q":"Explain what the two tongues are in 'Search For My Tongue', and how the poem's three-part layout carries that meaning. (short-response practice task)","a":"The mother tongue, Gujarati, and the foreign tongue, English, held in one mouth; the poem moves from English, to a section in Gujarati script with a transcription, and back to English, so the form performs the struggle it describes.","sol":"Bhatt uses tongue in both of its senses at once: the physical organ in the mouth and the language it speaks. The speaker's mother tongue is Gujarati and the foreign tongue is English, and the poem's claim is that living in the second makes the first rot and threaten to die. The layout is the proof. The opening section answers a question in English and describes that decay; the middle section abandons English altogether, appearing in Gujarati script with a transcription so that an English-only reader is shut out; the closing English lines then describe the mother tongue growing back overnight like a plant, in buds and blossom. Because the suppressed language physically takes the page before English is allowed to return, the form demonstrates the survival that the words only assert.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The title's key word has two everyday meanings; the poem needs both of them at the same time.","Look at what happens to the page in the middle of the poem, not only at what the words say.","Answer in two parts: what each tongue is, and what the three sections do in turn."],"codes":["Part 3: Search For My Tongue"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present childhood in 'Half-past Two' and 'Piano'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Fanthorpe writes from inside a child's consciousness using his own compound words and loose tercets; Lawrence writes an adult dragged backwards by music, in regular rhyming quatrains. Compare viewpoint, the handling of time and what each form does to the reader.","sol":"Both poets present childhood as a place adults cannot simply visit, but they approach it from opposite sides. Fanthorpe stays inside the child. Her boy is kept in for Something Very Wrong, a capitalised phrase left deliberately unexplained because he does not understand it either, and his day is measured in run-together coinages for getting up, being told to be off, television and going home. That invented vocabulary is the poem's argument: a child holds time as a sequence of events, so a clock face he cannot read is just an object with hands and a face. The loose tercets drift as he drifts, and the ending, in which the teacher slots him back into the school day, is the closing of adult measurement over a child's timeless hour. Lawrence writes from the far side. His speaker is an adult listening to a woman singing, and the memory of sitting under the piano pressing his mother's feet arrives uninvited; he calls the song's mastery insidious and says it betrays him, which is the language of someone under attack. The form is the ambush: three quatrains of rhyming couplets, a regularity as inescapable as the tune, holding their shape while the man loses his and weeps. The comparison, then, is one of resistance. Fanthorpe's child cannot leave childhood and Lawrence's adult cannot stay out of it; her form loosens to match a mind without measurement, his stays rigid to show a man being overpowered by pattern. Fanthorpe mourns for a child who will be slotted back into time, while Lawrence mourns for a man who cannot be.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask first who is doing the remembering in each poem, and whether they want to be there.","Set the two verse forms side by side before you look at any image: one loosens, one refuses to.","Build each paragraph on one shared idea, taking both poems inside it, and end every paragraph by naming the difference."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Half-past Two","Part 3: Piano"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present attitudes to death in 'Do not go gentle into that good night' and 'Remember'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Thomas's villanelle repeats a demand for resistance and only names his father at the end; Rossetti's Petrarchan sonnet demands remembrance in the octave and releases the listener at the volta. Compare defiance against acceptance through form, address and diction.","sol":"Each poet chooses a form whose characteristic movement is the attitude itself. Thomas writes a villanelle, and a villanelle cannot move forward without bringing its refrains back with it; the demand for resistance and the command to rage at the failing light return whether or not anyone is listening, so the poem behaves like a man who will not stop saying the same thing. Its middle stanzas generalise, taking wise, good, wild and grave men in turn and finding that none of them will go quietly, and this delay matters: only in the final quatrain does the speaker admit that he is addressing his own dying father, and the two refrains land together there, so the ending insists rather than resolves. Rossetti's Petrarchan sonnet turns instead of returning. Her octave repeats the imperative to remember her once she has gone into the silent land, when there is no more holding of hands and no more talk of a planned future; then the volta at the ninth line concedes that if he forgets her for a while and afterwards remembers, he should not grieve. The closing antithesis prefers forgetting and smiling to remembering and sadness, so the structure enacts a surrender of the very claim the octave made. The link is that both poems are spoken to someone about to be parted from the speaker, and both make the shape of the verse carry the feeling; the difference is that repetition holds on where the turn lets go. Thomas's refusal is the more thrilling to read, but Rossetti's release is the more generous, because hers costs the speaker the one thing she wanted, and his costs him nothing but breath.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Both poems are spoken to a particular person; work out when each poem lets you know that.","Describe what each form does as a movement, one that returns and one that turns, before you discuss any image.","Let one paragraph handle form, one the way each speaker addresses the listener, and one the diction of night and of silence, judging as you go."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Do not go gentle into that good night","Part 3: Remember"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present love in 'Sonnet 116' and 'La Belle Dame sans Merci'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Shakespeare defines constant love by negation in a sonnet that resolves in a wager; Keats narrates destructive enchantment in a ballad that circles back to its opening. Compare definition against narrative, and forms that close against forms that trap.","sol":"Shakespeare argues and Keats tells a story, and the difference of method is the difference of belief. Sonnet 116 works by negation: love is refused the name if it alters when it meets alteration, and the positives that follow are navigational, an ever-fixed mark that faces down tempests and a star by which a wandering ship steers, whose worth cannot be calculated even when its height can be measured. Time is then personified with a sickle and dismissed, love being no fool of his, and the closing couplet gambles the poet's entire art on the truth of the claim. The sonnet form permits all of this because it has a turn and an ending; constancy can be defined, tested and settled. Keats denies his lovers any such settlement. His ballad of twelve quatrains rhymes ABCB and cuts the fourth line short, so each stanza falls away as it gathers speed, and the knight's story of garlands, strange sweet food, an elfin grotto and a dream of pale kings who name the lady is delivered in the same falling motion. The final stanza then repeats the first, returning him to the withered sedge where no birds sing, so the poem shuts like a circle with the knight still inside it. In both poems form is the evidence for the claim about love: Shakespeare's love survives Time because his verse knows how to finish an argument, while Keats's love destroys because his verse can only come back to where it began. Read together, they put a definition against a case study, and the case study is the harder to refute.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["One poem makes a claim about love and the other shows what love did to somebody; keep that distinction in view.","Take the forms first, asking what each is built to do, then find the imagery that serves it.","Make each paragraph carry both poems, and finish by weighing which poem's account of love is the more convincing."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Sonnet 116: Let me not to the marriage of true minds","Part 3: La Belle Dame sans Merci"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present identity in 'Half-caste' and 'Search For My Tongue'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Agard confronts a listener in a Creole voice with phonetic spelling, repetition and absurd analogies; Bhatt runs an extended metaphor and surrenders the page to Gujarati script. Compare confrontation against demonstration, and how each makes the form the evidence.","sol":"Neither poet describes a divided identity from outside; each makes the page behave like the mouth it is describing. Agard's method is confrontation. He writes in a Caribbean Creole voice with phonetic spelling and almost no punctuation until the end, so a poem being read inside an examination is already refusing the standard by which it is judged. He takes the insult literally and turns it into comedy: a painter mixing two colours must have made a half-caste canvas, English weather mixing light and shade must be half-caste, a composer using both the black and the white keys must have written a half-caste symphony. The repeated demand that the listener explain the term works as a refrain and keeps the listener in the dock. Then the register shifts at the close, where the listener is told to return with the whole of their eyes, ears and mind, and the comedy resolves into an accusation. Bhatt's method is demonstration. Her extended metaphor holds the word tongue to both of its meanings, so a language dying is described as an organ rotting in the mouth, and the middle of the poem abandons English for Gujarati script with a transcription, briefly locking an English reader out of a poem about being locked out. When English returns it reports that the mother tongue has grown back overnight like a plant, in buds and blossom, but the reader has already watched it happen. The link is that in both poems form is the argument rather than its decoration; the difference is that Agard demands recognition from the listener while Bhatt simply takes the ground back, and the quieter method may be the more complete refusal to be halved.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["In both poems the way the words appear on the page is doing work; start there rather than with the theme.","Ask who each poem is speaking to, and what that listener is made to feel.","Give a paragraph to voice and spelling, one to structure and the endings, and judge which method is the more persuasive."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Half-caste","Part 3: Search For My Tongue"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present power over another person in 'My Last Duchess' and 'La Belle Dame sans Merci'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Browning's Duke controls a woman by turning her into an object he alone uncovers; Keats's knight is emptied by a woman he cannot hold. Compare the dramatic monologue with the ballad, and the direction in which power runs.","sol":"The two poems place power in opposite hands and use their forms to prove it. Browning writes a dramatic monologue in twenty-eight rhyming couplets of iambic pentameter, but enjambment carries the sense across the rhymes so that the Duke sounds like a man chatting while the form quietly holds him to a pattern he does not notice. His power is exercised through possession and display: a portrait behind a curtain that only he may draw, a complaint that his wife's thanks were spread too widely and that she ranked a nine-hundred-year-old name with anybody's gift, a refusal to stoop to explaining himself, and the smooth remark that he gave commands and the smiles stopped. He then turns to the dowry and points out a bronze of a god taming a creature, so the whole speech is a warning delivered as connoisseurship. Keats reverses the direction. His knight is not the possessor but the possessed, and the ballad's shortened fourth line keeps pulling the ground from under him as he tells how the lady fed him, sang to him, wept in her grotto and left him to a dream of pale kings who told him he was in her thrall. He ends where the poem began, pale and loitering by the withered lake. The connection is that both poems present love as a transfer of power rather than a meeting, and that both use art or enchantment to fix a person in place; the difference is that Browning's speaker survives to admire his own collection, while Keats's is left inside the circle the form has drawn round him.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Work out who has the power in each poem, and whether the speaker knows it.","Compare what each form does to its speaker: one is held to a pattern he cannot hear, one is dropped by his own stanzas.","Organise by shared ideas such as possession, display and thrall, taking both poems inside each, and judge at the end."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: My Last Duchess","Part 3: La Belle Dame sans Merci"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present suffering in 'War Photographer' and one other poem from Part 3 of the Anthology. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Duffy's four ordered sestets and priestly imagery hold suffering at a professional distance until the last stanza gives way to indifference. A strong partner is 'Prayer Before Birth', where a ritual opening is repeated until the final stanza collapses.","sol":"Choosing the second poem is half the work: 'Prayer Before Birth' pairs with Duffy because both poets build an orderly pattern to contain horror and then show the pattern failing. Duffy gives her photographer four six-line stanzas of steady rhythm and rhyme, and the discipline of the verse is the discipline he is trying to keep. His spools are set out in ordered rows, the red safelight makes a church of the darkroom and a priest of the man, and the biblical tag from Isaiah about all flesh being grass is set against a list of place names from real wars, so ritual is being used to make the unbearable handleable. The defence holds until a print begins to form and a dying man's features return, bringing with them the memory of his wife's wordless consent; by the final stanza the poem has moved from the darkroom to the reader, whose tears last between the bath and lunch, and to an editor who will print five or six of a hundred agonies. MacNeice's unborn speaker also works by ritual. Every stanza opens with the same statement that he is not yet born, a repetition that functions as a prayer, and the requests lengthen as the catalogue of threats grows, from the bloodsucking bat to the walls and lies of a state that would make him a thing. The last stanza is suddenly very short and asks to be killed rather than emptied out. In both poems the shape of the verse is a defence against violence; Duffy's defence is professional detachment, worn away by indifference, while MacNeice's is prayer, broken by fear. The suffering is the same; what differs is whether anyone is left able to look at it.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Before choosing the second poem, decide what Duffy's poem is really about: the suffering, or what people do with it.","Look for a partner whose form also sets an order against a horror, so that the comparison is about method and not subject alone.","Make each paragraph hold both poems, and use the last one to judge what each poem says about who is left to care."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: War Photographer","Part 3: Prayer Before Birth"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present the relationship between a parent and a child in 'Poem at Thirty-Nine' and one other poem from Part 3 of the Anthology. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Walker's free verse, repeated line of loss and plain diction turn grief into inheritance. 'If–' is a strong partner: a father's advice in four tight stanzas held open by one suspended sentence, so the comparison is between a child's retrospect and a parent's instruction.","sol":"Walker and Kipling both write the relationship as something transmitted, but one looks back at what was received and the other looks forward at what must be earned. Walker's speaker, at thirty-nine, repeats that she misses her father, and that repeated statement rather than any argument is what organises the poem. Her free verse runs in short plain lines and uneven stanzas, so the reader moves at the speed of recollection: he taught her to write and to keep track of money on paper, and she notes how tired he already was by the time she was born. The second movement turns to cooking, to the image of him working as though he were dancing, and to her belief that he would have admired the woman she became; the inheritance turns out to be a manner rather than a lesson, and the poem ends in reconciliation without ever dropping the line of loss. Kipling's father speaks before the fact. His four eight-line stanzas of alternating rhyme are almost one suspended sentence, the anaphora of the conditional piling condition on condition and the main clause withheld until the last two lines, where the world and manhood are granted. Antithesis runs through it, pairing triumph with disaster as two impostors to be met alike. The connection is that both poems define a child by what a father gives, and both make structure carry the idea; the difference is direction and cost. Walker's repetition admits that the father is gone and finds him in her own hands, while Kipling's withheld main clause makes love conditional on endurance. Walker's child has arrived; Kipling's has not yet been allowed to.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first whether Walker's poem is about grief, inheritance or both, because that choice picks your partner poem.","Look for a second poem in which a parent's voice or a parent's teaching shapes the form itself.","Run the comparison through structure as well as content, and finish by weighing what each child is given."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Poem at Thirty-Nine","Part 3: If–"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets use questions in 'The Tyger' and one other poem from Part 3 of the Anthology. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Blake's poem is almost entirely unanswered questions in hammering trochaic tetrameter, with one word changed in the final stanza. 'Half-caste' pairs well: Agard's repeated demand for an explanation is a question used as attack rather than as wonder.","sol":"Both poets build a poem out of questions that are never going to be answered, but they aim them at opposite targets. Blake asks about a maker. Six quatrains of rhyming couplets in a hammering trochaic tetrameter, with the last syllable cut so the line lands like a blow, carry a sequence of questions about what hand or eye could frame such fearful symmetry, and about the furnace, anvil, hammer and chain that made it. The vocabulary is a blacksmith's, so creation is imagined as dangerous manual work, and the fifth stanza's question about whether the maker of the lamb made this creature too sets the poem against its companion in Songs of Innocence. The final stanza then repeats the first with one word altered, from could to dare, and that single change turns admiration of skill into fear of intention. Agard aims his questions at a person. His repeated demand that the listener explain the term half-caste works as a refrain, and each repetition drags the listener further into a trap built out of absurd parallels from painting, weather and music. The Creole spelling and the near absence of punctuation keep the questions moving at the speed of speech, so they accumulate rather than wait for replies. Then the ending changes register and the questioning stops, replaced by an invitation to come back with the whole of one's attention. The link is that in both poems an unanswered question is a way of making the reader do the work; the difference is that Blake's questions open on to a universe he cannot interrogate, while Agard's have a defendant standing in front of them, and his poem can therefore end with a demand rather than a mystery.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what a question does to a reader when nobody in the poem is going to answer it.","Choose a partner poem where questions are aimed at someone, so there is a difference of target to analyse.","Track rhythm and repetition in both, and end by judging what each poet gains by refusing an answer."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: The Tyger","Part 3: Half-caste"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present being alone in 'Hide and Seek' and one other poem from Part 3 of the Anthology. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Scannell's single verse paragraph and second-person address make the reader hide with the boy until the garden turns hostile. 'Half-past Two' is a strong partner: a child alone in a room outside time, presented through his own coined words.","sol":"Both poets isolate a child and then use form to put the reader in that isolation with him. Scannell writes one unbroken verse paragraph of unrhymed lines, so there is no stanza break for the boy or the reader to rest in, and he addresses the child as you throughout. The detail is physical and cold, the salty sacks, the stiff legs, the dark that smells of the sea, and time passes only through what can be heard outside the shed door; snatches of direct speech break in as the seekers call and then go. The turn is saved for the end: when the boy comes out in triumph the garden is empty, the garden is said to watch and the bushes to hold their breath, and the silence that should be victory becomes abandonment. Because of the second person, that abandonment happens to the reader. Fanthorpe isolates her boy indoors. He is kept in for Something Very Wrong, capitalised and never explained because he does not understand it, and left with a clock he cannot read; his day exists only as the run-together coinages for getting up, being told to be off, television and going home, so without those markers he falls out of time altogether into a hushed, dusty hour. The loose tercets drift with him until the teacher returns and slots him back. The connection is that both poets make a child's aloneness out of what is missing, the seekers in one poem and measured time in the other; the difference is that Scannell's silence is a cruelty and Fanthorpe's is an escape, which is why his poem ends in exposure and hers in retrieval.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Notice who the poem is speaking to, and what that does to the reader's position.","Pick a partner in which a child is also left without something, so the comparison is about method rather than plot.","Take the endings seriously: judge whether each silence is presented as a loss or as a freedom."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Hide and Seek","Part 3: Half-past Two"]},{"q":"Compare how the poets present hope in 'Blessing' and 'Prayer Before Birth'. You must consider language, form and structure.","a":"Dharker's lines lengthen from a drip to a roar and her religious diction treats water as grace; MacNeice's litany swells with fears and collapses into a plea. Compare hope granted in a moment with hope requested in advance.","sol":"These two poems are worth pairing because their shapes run in opposite directions, and the shapes are where the hope is. Dharker begins with scarcity: skin compared to a cracking pod, the merely imagined drip of water into a tin mug and the echo it makes there, all carried in short broken lines that ration the sound as the place rations the water. Then the municipal pipe bursts, the crash is described as silver, and the lines open out into a long rush of naming as the crowd arrives with brass, copper, aluminium and plastic. Her religious register does the rest of the work: the blessing of the title, a congregation, a kindly god and a flow treated as a gift, so an accident is received as grace. The poem closes on small bones and on light off wet skin, which keeps the joy honest, because the children are thin and the pipe will be mended. MacNeice moves the other way. His unborn speaker opens every stanza with the same statement that he is not yet born, a ritual that is itself a kind of hope, and each stanza asks for something reasonable, to be heard, consoled, provided for, forgiven. But the catalogue of threats grows longer than the requests, and the final stanza shrinks to a plea to be killed rather than made into a thing. The link is that both poets use the length of the stanza as a measure of hope; the difference is direction, since Dharker's expands towards a gift that actually arrives and MacNeice's contracts towards a request that expects refusal. Hope in Dharker is momentary and real; in MacNeice it is a formality performed against the evidence.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the length of the lines and stanzas in each poem before you look at anything else.","One poem is about something that happens and one is about something requested; let that difference drive the comparison.","Keep both poems inside every paragraph, and use the endings to judge what kind of hope each poet allows."],"codes":["Section B: Anthology Poetry","Part 3: Blessing","Part 3: Prayer Before Birth"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does Section B carry and how are they split between AO2 and AO3?","a":"Thirty marks: fifteen AO2 and fifteen AO3."},{"q":"How many poems are in Part 3 of the anthology, and how many must you write about in Section B?","a":"Sixteen poems in Part 3; you write about two of them."},{"q":"Which Part 3 poem is a villanelle, and which two are sonnets?","a":"'Do not go gentle into that good night' is the villanelle; 'Sonnet 116' and 'Remember' are the sonnets."},{"q":"What must appear in every developed paragraph of a Section B answer for the AO3 marks to be available?","a":"An explicit link or difference between the two poems, drawn out in the same paragraph as the analysis."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Section B mark split","back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two, comparing two Part 3 poems: 15 marks AO2 for language, form and structure, 15 marks AO3 for links and connections between texts.","code":"Section B: Anthology Poetry","open":false},{"front":"Villanelle","back":"Nineteen lines on two rhymes: five tercets and a closing quatrain, with two refrains that alternate and then land together at the end. Dylan Thomas uses it in 'Do not go gentle into that good night'.","code":"Part 3: Do not go gentle into that good night","open":false},{"front":"Petrarchan sonnet and volta in 'Remember'","back":"Octave rhyming ABBAABBA demanding remembrance, then a sestet turning at line nine to release the listener. The turn lets Rossetti withdraw the instruction she began with.","code":"Part 3: Remember","open":false},{"front":"Dramatic monologue","back":"One speaker, a silent listener, and a speech that reveals more than the speaker intends. Browning's Duke of Ferrara in 'My Last Duchess' is the anthology's example, in enjambed rhyming couplets.","code":"Part 3: My Last Duchess","open":false},{"front":"The truncated fourth line in 'La Belle Dame sans Merci'","back":"Keats's ballad quatrains rhyme ABCB with three tetrameter lines and a shortened fourth, so each stanza drops away; the final stanza repeats the first, closing the poem as a circle.","code":"Part 3: La Belle Dame sans Merci","open":false},{"front":"Trochaic tetrameter in 'The Tyger'","back":"A stressed-then-unstressed four-beat line with the last syllable cut, so it strikes like the forge Blake describes. The poem is almost all unanswered questions, and could becomes dare in the final stanza.","code":"Part 3: The Tyger","open":false},{"front":"Anaphora in 'Prayer Before Birth'","back":"Every stanza opens with the unborn speaker's same statement that he is not yet born, turning the poem into a litany; the stanzas swell with threats before the last one shrinks to a plea.","code":"Part 3: Prayer Before Birth","open":false},{"front":"Extended metaphor in 'Search For My Tongue'","back":"Tongue means both the organ and the language, so a dying mother tongue rots in the mouth and grows back like a plant. The middle section appears in Gujarati script with a transcription.","code":"Part 3: Search For My Tongue","open":false},{"front":"Compound coinages in 'Half-past Two'","back":"Fanthorpe runs the parts of the boy's day into single invented words for getting up, being told to be off, television and going home, so time is shown as a child holds it, by events and not by numbers.","code":"Part 3: Half-past Two","open":false},{"front":"Second-person address in 'Hide and Seek'","back":"Scannell calls the hiding boy you and writes one unbroken verse paragraph, so the reader waits in the shed too and is present when the garden turns watchful and the friends have gone.","code":"Part 3: Hide and Seek","open":false},{"front":"Religious register in 'Blessing'","back":"Dharker treats a burst water pipe as grace, with a congregation, a kindly god and a gift, while the lines lengthen from a rationed drip to a rush; the close returns to the children's small bones.","code":"Part 3: Blessing","open":false},{"front":"Creole voice in 'Half-caste'","back":"Agard uses phonetic spelling and almost no punctuation until the end, repeating a demand that the listener explain the word, before the register changes and comedy becomes accusation.","code":"Part 3: Half-caste","open":false},{"front":"The suspended sentence in 'If–'","back":"Kipling holds back the main clause through four eight-line stanzas of conditionals in alternating rhyme, so the reward of the world and of manhood only arrives in the final two lines.","code":"Part 3: If–","open":false},{"front":"A Part 3 pairing for a question on attitudes to death","back":"'Do not go gentle into that good night' with 'Remember', defiance against release; 'Remember' with 'Poem at Thirty-Nine', two kinds of living with loss; 'War Photographer' with 'Prayer Before Birth', death witnessed and death feared.","code":"Section B: Anthology Poetry","open":true},{"front":"A Part 3 pairing for a question on memory and the past","back":"'Piano' with 'Half-past Two', an adult pulled back against a child still inside it; 'Piano' with 'Poem at Thirty-Nine', two adults remembering a parent; 'Half-past Two' with 'Poem at Thirty-Nine', a remembered childhood hour and an adult's memory of a father.","code":"Part 3: Piano","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: Compare how the poets present attitudes to death in 'Do not go gentle into that good night' and 'Remember'. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","Point: each poet chooses a form whose characteristic movement is the attitude itself, so that Thomas's refusal to release his father and Rossetti's decision to release her listener are enacted before either is stated.","Evidence: Thomas writes a villanelle, whose two refrains, the title line and the command to rage at the failing light, alternate through five tercets and then arrive together in the closing quatrain, where the speaker finally names his dying father; Rossetti writes a Petrarchan sonnet whose octave repeats an instruction to remember her once she has gone into the silent land, and whose sestet turns at the ninth line with a concession that begins with the word yet.","Analysis of form: a villanelle cannot move forward without dragging its own lines back, so the demand for resistance keeps returning whether or not anyone can act on it, and the refrains meeting at the end makes the ending an insistence rather than a resolution; a Petrarchan sonnet, by contrast, is built round a single hinge, and Rossetti uses that hinge to reverse an imperative she has spent eight lines repeating, so the structure performs a surrender.","The comparison drawn out: both poems are spoken to a person who is being parted from the speaker, and both make the movement of the verse carry the feeling, but repetition holds on where the turn lets go; Thomas keeps his father inside the poem's machinery, while Rossetti deliberately opens her hand.","Mini-judgement: Thomas's poem is the more thrilling to read because its form never concedes, but Rossetti's is the more loving, since her volta costs her the one thing she asked for and his refrain costs him nothing but breath.","Why this is Level 5: the paragraph evaluates rather than describes, both poems are analysed inside a single argument, the technical terms are used to explain effects rather than to label features, and the judgement is qualified rather than decorative."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: Compare how the poets present attitudes to death in 'Do not go gentle into that good night' and 'Remember'. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","Point: both poems are about death, but Thomas is angry about it and Rossetti is calm about it.","Evidence: Thomas repeats the same two lines all the way through his poem, and at the end he is talking to his father. Rossetti keeps telling her listener to remember her, and then at the end she says it would be better if he forgot and was happy.","Analysis: the repetition in Thomas's poem shows how strongly he feels, because he keeps saying it again and again, which makes the reader feel his anger. Rossetti's poem is a sonnet, which is a fourteen-line poem often used for love, and this makes it sound gentle and sad.","Comparison: this is different from Thomas because Rossetti accepts death and he does not.","Mini-judgement: overall both poems show that people react to death in different ways.","Why this is only Level 3, and what would lift it: the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant, so it is not a weak answer, but the technical terms are named rather than used. Repetition is said to show strong feeling instead of being explained as a villanelle's refrain returning whether or not it is heard; the sonnet is defined instead of being read for its volta, which is the exact moment Rossetti changes her mind. The comparison arrives in one bolted-on sentence rather than running through the analysis, and the judgement restates the question instead of weighing the two attitudes. Naming a device earns little; tracing what it does to the reader is what moves an answer up the grid."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the sixteen Part 3 poems: poet, form, subject, methods and a comparison partner","items":["If– by Rudyard Kipling. Form: four eight-line stanzas, iambic pentameter, alternating rhyme, almost a single suspended conditional sentence. Subject: the conduct that will earn a son the world and his manhood. Methods: anaphora of the conditional opening, antithesis of paired opposites, personification of Triumph and Disaster as impostors, the main clause withheld until the final two lines. Compare with: 'Poem at Thirty-Nine', for what a father passes on.","Prayer Before Birth by Louis MacNeice, 1944. Form: free verse litany, each stanza opening with the same statement, stanzas lengthening and then collapsing into a very short last one. Subject: an unborn child asking to be protected from the world it is about to enter. Methods: dramatic conceit of a voice before life, catalogue of threats, heavy alliteration and internal rhyme, structural collapse at the close. Compare with: 'War Photographer', for order set against violence.","Blessing by Imtiaz Dharker. Form: free verse in stanzas that lengthen as the water arrives, short broken lines at the start. Subject: a municipal pipe bursts in a place of drought and a crowd runs to catch the water. Methods: religious register of blessing, congregation and a kindly god, the simile of skin cracking like a pod, sound imagery from drip to roar, the closing image of children's small bones. Compare with: 'Prayer Before Birth', for hope granted against hope requested.","Search For My Tongue by Sujata Bhatt. Form: free verse in three movements, English, then Gujarati script with a transcription, then English again. Subject: living with a mother tongue and a foreign tongue in one mouth. Methods: extended metaphor on the two senses of tongue, direct address to a questioner, the imagery of rotting and of a plant growing back in buds and blossom, and a layout that performs the argument. Compare with: 'Half-caste', for language and identity.","Half-past Two by U A Fanthorpe. Form: loose three-line stanzas, free verse. Subject: a small boy kept in after school who cannot tell the time and falls out of time altogether. Methods: compound coinages for the parts of his day, the capitalised and unexplained offence, the clock misread as a face with hands, the teacher's return slotting him back into the school day. Compare with: 'Piano', for childhood seen from inside and from outside.","Piano by D H Lawrence. Form: three quatrains in rhyming couplets, regular metre. Subject: a man hearing a woman sing is carried back to sitting under the piano at his mother's feet. Methods: the song's mastery called insidious and accused of betraying him, sibilance and assonance, the contrast of the black piano played appassionato with a child's memory, and a form that holds firm while the speaker weeps. Compare with: 'Poem at Thirty-Nine', for an adult remembering a parent.","Hide and Seek by Vernon Scannell. Form: one unbroken verse paragraph of unrhymed lines with snatches of direct speech. Subject: a boy hides in a garden shed, waits too long, and emerges to find everyone gone. Methods: second-person address placing the reader in the shed, cold sensory detail of salty sacks and cramped legs, time measured by sounds outside, and the personified garden watching at the end. Compare with: 'Half-past Two', for a child left alone.","Sonnet 116: Let me not to the marriage of true minds by William Shakespeare. Form: Shakespearean sonnet, three quatrains and a couplet, ABAB CDCD EFEF GG, iambic pentameter. Subject: love that does not alter is the only love worth the name. Methods: definition by negation, nautical metaphors of the fixed sea-mark and the guiding star, personification of Time with a sickle, and a closing couplet that stakes the poet's art on the claim. Compare with: 'La Belle Dame sans Merci', for constancy against enchantment.","La Belle Dame sans Merci by John Keats, 1819. Form: ballad, twelve quatrains rhyming ABCB, three tetrameter lines and a curtailed fourth, with a framing questioner. Subject: a knight left pale and loitering after meeting a beautiful woman in the meadows. Methods: medieval ballad revival, the femme fatale, the falling short line, the dream of pale kings, and a final stanza that echoes the first. Compare with: 'My Last Duchess', for power inside a relationship.","Poem at Thirty-Nine by Alice Walker. Form: free verse, short plain lines, uneven stanzas. Subject: a woman of thirty-nine missing her father and recognising him in herself. Methods: the repeated statement of missing him, simple chosen diction, the memory of being taught to write and to handle money, the image of him cooking as though dancing, and a movement from regret to reconciliation. Compare with: 'If–', for what a parent hands on.","War Photographer by Carol Ann Duffy. Form: four six-line stanzas with a regular rhythm and rhyme scheme. Subject: a photographer developing his films at home and the indifference that meets them. Methods: the darkroom as a church and the man as a priest, the biblical tag from Isaiah that all flesh is grass, a list of place names from real wars, the half-formed print recalling a dying man and his wife's wordless consent, and a final contrast between a hundred agonies and a Sunday supplement. Compare with: 'Prayer Before Birth', for order used as a defence against horror.","The Tyger by William Blake, from Songs of Experience, 1794. Form: six quatrains of rhyming couplets in catalectic trochaic tetrameter. Subject: what kind of creator could make a creature of such beauty and terror. Methods: a poem built almost entirely of unanswered questions, the blacksmith's imagery of furnace, hammer, anvil and chain, the reference to the lamb and its companion poem in Songs of Innocence, and the final stanza repeating the first with could altered to dare. Compare with: 'Half-caste', for questions used as a method.","My Last Duchess by Robert Browning, 1842. Form: dramatic monologue, twenty-eight rhyming couplets of iambic pentameter, heavily enjambed. Subject: a Renaissance duke shows an envoy the portrait of his previous wife while negotiating his next marriage. Methods: self-incriminating speech, the curtain only he may draw, the complaint that she ranked his ancient name with any gift, the flat admission that he gave commands, and the closing bronze of Neptune taming a sea-horse. Compare with: 'La Belle Dame sans Merci', for possession and thrall.","Half-caste by John Agard. Form: free verse in a Caribbean Creole voice, phonetic spelling, almost no punctuation until the end, three uneven movements. Subject: a challenge to the person who uses the phrase half-caste. Methods: taking an insult literally, absurd parallels from painting, weather and music, the repeated demand for an explanation used as a refrain, humour, and a change of register at the close. Compare with: 'Search For My Tongue', for language, identity and the page itself.","Do not go gentle into that good night by Dylan Thomas, 1951. Form: villanelle, nineteen lines on two rhymes, five tercets and a quatrain, two alternating refrains that meet at the end. Subject: a son urging his dying father to resist death. Methods: the refrains as compulsion, the metaphors of night and light, four kinds of men taken in turn, and the late revelation of the father on a sad height. Compare with: 'Remember', for defiance against acceptance.","Remember by Christina Rossetti, published 1862. Form: Petrarchan sonnet, octave ABBAABBA and a sestet, volta at line nine. Subject: a dying woman asks to be remembered and then frees the listener from the obligation. Methods: repeated imperative, euphemism for death including the silent land, soft consonants, and a closing antithesis preferring forgetting and smiling to remembering and sadness. Compare with: 'Do not go gentle into that good night', for two ways of facing a parting."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — how Section B is set and marked","items":["The section is Section B of Component 1, Poetry and Modern Prose (4ET1/01), a two-hour paper worth 90 raw marks and 60 per cent of the International GCSE.","You answer one 30-mark essay question, chosen from two, comparing two poems from Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology.","Fifteen raw marks are AO2, analysing the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects; fifteen are AO3, exploring links and connections between texts.","AO3 is worth 10 per cent of the whole qualification and all of it sits in this section, so an answer with no comparison in it cannot reach half marks however good the analysis is.","Learners must demonstrate understanding of how writers create literary effects, use appropriate literary terminology, make comparisons between poems including use of language, structure and form, and identify and use relevant examples from poems.","The anthology poems are printed in the question paper; the rest of Component 1 is closed book, and no text may be brought in.","The specification's assessment grid runs over five levels on a best-fit basis: Level 3 is sound understanding with clearly relevant examples, Level 4 sustained analysis with fully relevant examples, and Level 5 cohesive evaluation with discriminating use of relevant examples.","Allow about forty minutes, which is this section's pro-rata share of the two-hour paper; the specification itself sets no per-question time."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Compare how the poets present childhood in 'Half-past Two' and 'Piano'. You must consider language, form and structure (30 marks)","items":["Decoded question: 'Compare how the poets present' is AO3 and AO2 together, so every paragraph must analyse a method in both poems and state the link or difference; 'childhood' is the thread, and the examiner will expect it tested rather than described; 'You must consider language, form and structure' carries the AO2 marks and cannot be met by naming devices, since the grid rises from identification at Level 1 to cohesive evaluation at Level 5. There are no AO1 or AO4 marks here, so no biography and no plot summary.","Introduction: state the argument in one sentence, that both poets present childhood as a place adults cannot simply visit, Fanthorpe by staying inside it and Lawrence by showing a man dragged back into it against his will, and that in each poem the verse form is what makes the difference felt.","Paragraph 1, viewpoint and language: Fanthorpe's compound coinages for the parts of the boy's day and the capitalised, unexplained offence keep the reader inside a mind that measures time by events; Lawrence's adult speaker calls the song's mastery insidious and says it betrays him, the vocabulary of a man under attack. Link: one poem has no adult narrator at all, the other is written by the adult who is losing the argument.","Paragraph 2, form: Fanthorpe's loose tercets drift as the boy drifts out of measured time; Lawrence's three quatrains of rhyming couplets keep perfect order while the speaker breaks down, so the regularity is the tune that is doing the damage. Link: both use form as pressure, but one loosens to release a child and the other tightens to trap an adult.","Paragraph 3, structure and endings: Fanthorpe ends with the teacher slotting the boy back into the school day, adult measurement closing over a timeless hour; Lawrence ends with a grown man weeping like a child, the resistance abandoned. Link: both endings are defeats, but the child is returned to time while the adult is returned to childhood.","Paragraph 4, what childhood is worth in each poem: for Fanthorpe it is a way of being in the world that adults have lost the vocabulary for; for Lawrence it is a security that can only be felt as loss. Use this paragraph to evaluate rather than to add evidence.","Comparison thread to keep running: in both poems the form carries the viewpoint, so ask of every quotation whether it is showing childhood from inside or from outside, and say so.","Conclusion must say: that the two poems present childhood as the same country seen from opposite borders, that Fanthorpe's method is immersion and Lawrence's is ambush, and that the sharper loss is Lawrence's, because his speaker knows what he has lost while Fanthorpe's boy does not yet know he is losing it."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"What earns nothing in Section B, and what to write instead","items":["Two separate essays with a joining sentence at the end: the AO3 marks are for links and connections, and an examiner cannot award them to a comparison that appears once. Instead, build every paragraph on one point of comparison and put both poems inside it.","Feature-spotting: naming alliteration, enjambment or a simile without saying what it does sits at the bottom of the grid, which asks for minimal identification at Level 1 and cohesive evaluation at Level 5. Instead, write the effect first and let the term follow.","Similarity-only comparison: saying both poems are about death, both use imagery and both have a sad tone is true and worthless. Instead, find the difference inside the similarity, which is where the analysis lives.","Biography and background: there are no AO4 marks in Section B, so a paragraph on a poet's life earns nothing. Instead, spend that time on form, and mention context only where it explains a choice in the poem.","Retelling the poem: paraphrase is narrative, and the grid calls a largely narrative response Level 2. Instead, assume the examiner has the poem in front of them, because the candidate does too.","Choosing a second poem by theme alone when the question leaves it open: a partner with nothing to differ about produces a list. Instead, choose the poem that shares the idea but handles it by a different method, so there is something to argue."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:11:59.320Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Nothing anywhere in this kit is presented as a quotation — not one line of any poem is reproduced, so there is no misquotation to find — and every form, date, rhyme scheme, speaker, event and publication fact I re-checked across the seventeen lesson sections, fifteen questions, sixteen factfile entries and fifteen cards is true to the text; with no wrong answer key the kit is above the publish threshold, but the worked-example step that now merely repeats its own setup, the over-length card back and the mislabelled stretch question are worth a last pass."}}},"prose-mockingbird":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-mockingbird","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"To Kill a Mockingbird is your modern prose set text for Component 1, Section C. In the examination you answer one 40-mark essay question, chosen from two, with the book shut: no text, no notes, nothing to flick back to. Twenty of those marks are AO1 (close knowledge and understanding of the novel, a critical style, an informed personal engagement) and twenty are AO4 (the relationship between the novel and the context in which it was written), so context is half the mark on every question you could be asked. That is why this room drills remembered evidence and integrated context together. An answer that retells the story, or that parks the Depression and the Scottsboro trials in a paragraph of their own, throws away half the marks it could have had. Section C carries 40 of the 90 raw marks on a paper worth 60% of the whole International GCSE, and pro rata you have about 53 minutes for it.","idea":[{"h":"One novel, studied whole","code":"Component 1: Content overview","t":"Component 1 has two set texts: all the poems in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one modern prose text chosen from the list of seven — for you, To Kill a Mockingbird by Harper Lee. The specification asks for the study of whole texts, developing comprehension, critical reading and clear, accurate writing in Standard English, and it encourages wider reading around the text. The examiner therefore expects an answer that moves across the whole novel: Scout's first day at school, the gifts in the knothole, the fire, the mad dog, Mrs Dubose, the mob at the jail, the trial, Tom's death, the attack under the oaks, the walk home to the Radley porch. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it never lives in one favourite chapter, and it shows the novel changing between Part One and Part Two rather than treating it as one flat block of evidence."},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, a personal position","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","t":"AO1 is 20 of the 40 marks. The specification tells you to demonstrate a close understanding of your prose text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from it. Because Component 1 is a closed book examination, every example must come out of your memory: the precise moment, the right names, and a short phrase you are certain of — Atticus telling Scout that you never really understand a person until you climb into his skin and walk around in it, or Miss Maudie saying that Atticus Finch is the same in his house as he is on the public streets. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it argues a case about the novel in its own critical voice, proving each step with short remembered evidence, instead of telling the examiner what happens next."},{"h":"AO4: the novel and the world that wrote it","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","t":"AO4 is the other 20 marks, an equal share, so context is half the mark on every To Kill a Mockingbird question. The specification says learners should understand the relationship between their prose text and the context in which it was written, and should understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. That second phrase is the important one: context is not background information, it is a way of reading. Lee published in 1960 a novel set in the Depression South, so the segregation her readers were then fighting is written into a story about their parents' generation. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it puts the context inside the sentence that explains the moment, so that removing the context would take the explanation away, not just a decoration."},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","t":"The specification lists the categories that affect a writer's work and the reader's response. One: the writer's own life and situation, where it relates to the text — Harper Lee, born in Monroeville, Alabama in 1926, daughter of a lawyer and newspaper editor, writing her childhood friend Truman Capote into Dill. Two: the historical setting — Maycomb in the Depression years of the 1930s. Three: social and cultural contexts — Jim Crow segregation, the class ladder from the Finches to the Cunninghams to the Ewells, and what a lady was expected to be. Four: the literary context — a coming-of-age novel with Southern Gothic furniture in the Radley house. Five: reception — how the book read in 1960 and how it reads now. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it picks the one or two categories that actually explain the question, rather than emptying all five onto the page."},{"h":"The question, the tariff and the clock","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","t":"Section C — Modern Prose gives one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, and assesses AO1 and AO4; in the breakdown of raw marks these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4. The component is closed book and you may not take the novel in, although the anthology poems are provided for Section B. Component 1 is two hours, 90 raw marks and 60% of the International GCSE, so Section C is worth roughly 53 minutes of it. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it chooses between the two questions by asking which one it has remembered evidence for, spends about five minutes planning four or five paragraphs and a judgement, and leaves time to finish the conclusion, because an unfinished answer cannot show a sustained argument. One caution on wording: the specification prints model question stems only for the literary heritage questions (You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel), so the Section C instruction line used throughout this room is reconstructed from those rather than quoted, with the AO2 clause dropped because Section C carries no AO2 marks."}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a 40-mark Section C question before you write","setup":"The paper offers you this question on your set text: How does Lee portray prejudice in Maycomb in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (A caution on wording: the specification prints model stems only for the literary heritage questions, which end 'You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel'. The Section C instruction line used here is reconstructed from those, with the language, form and structure clause dropped because Section C carries no AO2 marks.) It is worth 40 marks, split 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4, the examination is closed book, and pro rata you have about 53 minutes. Turn the question into a plan before you write a word.","steps":["Split the tariff first: half the marks want proof that you know the novel closely, half want the world it was written in and about, so every paragraph has to carry both.","Underline the two load-bearing words: portray asks by what means Lee builds the picture, and prejudice in this novel is wider than race — it takes in class, gender and the town's fear of Boo Radley.","Choose four moments you can reach from memory: Walter Cunningham at the Finch dinner table; Aunt Alexandra ruling on who is fit company; the mob at the jail turned back by Scout; the guilty verdict and Reverend Sykes telling Scout to stand up.","Attach one context to each moment: Depression poverty and the Maycomb class ladder; Southern expectations of a lady; Jim Crow segregation and the all-white Alabama juries of the Scottsboro trials; the civil rights readership Lee was writing for.","Write the thesis the paragraphs will prove: Lee portrays prejudice as ordinary and inherited, learned at the dinner table long before it is voted on in a jury room, so the verdict shocks nobody in Maycomb and everybody reading it."],"cues":["What do the marks divide into, and what does that force every paragraph to do?","Which word tells you the answer is about method, and how wide is prejudice here?","Which four moments can you actually reach without the book in front of you?","Which context belongs beside each of those four moments?","What single claim about prejudice will the whole answer defend?"]},{"title":"Turning narrative into an AO1 argument","setup":"A student's paragraph on courage reads: Jem has to read to Mrs Dubose every afternoon because he cut the tops off her camellias. She is horrible to him and shouts at him. Later she dies and Atticus tells the children she was a morphine addict who wanted to die free of it. Everything in that is true, but it is narrative and sits at the bottom of the AO1 range. Rebuild it as a paragraph that argues.","steps":["Name the claim the material actually supports: Lee uses her least likeable character to redefine courage before the trial makes the reader need that definition.","Put the claim in the first sentence and demote the story to evidence behind it, so the paragraph opens with an idea rather than with an afternoon.","Keep the detail that proves the claim and cut the rest: the enforced reading, the white camellia left in a candy box, Atticus's verdict that she was the bravest person he ever knew.","Quote short and exactly, in the words you are sure of: it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway.","Close with a mini-judgement that points forward in the novel's own order: Atticus has already told Scout, back in Part One, that they were licked a hundred years before they started; Mrs Dubose's death and the definition of courage then close Part One; and the trial that tests that definition comes several chapters later, in Part Two."],"cues":["What claim does this material prove, beyond the fact that Jem was punished?","Which sentence should come first, the claim or the afternoon?","Which details survive once the claim is leading the paragraph?","How much of Atticus's definition do you actually need?","Which comes first in the novel — the line about being licked a hundred years before we started, or the definition of courage, or the trial?"]},{"title":"Making a context point earn its AO4 marks","setup":"Two versions of the same point. Version A reads: The novel was written in 1960, when the civil rights movement was growing, and Rosa Parks had refused to give up her seat in Montgomery. This shows the time Lee was writing in. Version B is yours to write. The evidence both versions are handling is the conviction of Tom Robinson although Mayella's bruises are on the right side of her face and Tom's left arm was crippled in a cotton gin. The question is on injustice.","steps":["Diagnose version A: the 1960 date, the growing civil rights movement and Rosa Parks refusing her seat are all true, but they stand alone, attached to no moment in the novel and doing no work on injustice.","Start from the textual fact instead: the physical evidence points to a left-handed attacker and Tom cannot use his left hand, so the jury convicts against proof rather than in the absence of it.","Bring the context to that exact fact: all-white Alabama juries had convicted Black defendants on white testimony within living memory of Lee's first readers, as the Scottsboro cases had shown.","Weld them into one sentence: Lee makes the evidence unanswerable so that the verdict can only be explained by the colour of the defendant, which is the charge those trials had already laid against Southern justice.","Test the join by deleting the context clause — if the sentence loses its explanation and not merely a fact, the context is integrated."],"cues":["Why does version A score so little, when everything in it is true?","Which fact from the trial should the sentence start from?","Which context explains that fact rather than sitting beside it?","How do you get both halves into a single sentence?","What test tells you the context is integrated rather than bolted on?"]},{"title":"Choosing evidence you can trust under closed-book conditions","setup":"Closed book, forty minutes gone, and you are on the Boo Radley paragraph of this question: To what extent is To Kill a Mockingbird a novel about innocence? You must refer to the context of the novel. In your memory you have the gifts left in the knothole of the oak, Nathan Radley filling the knothole with cement, the blanket put round Scout's shoulders during the fire at Miss Maudie's, Boo carrying Jem home, Scout's answer to Atticus about Heck Tate's decision, and Scout on the Radley porch at the end. Decide what to use and how.","steps":["Rank the moments by what they prove about innocence rather than by how much you like them: the blanket and the carrying home prove that Boo protects, and the porch proves that Scout finally sees.","Take one from each half of the novel so the paragraph shows development: Boo unseen and imagined in Part One, Boo present and silent at the end.","Quote only the words you are certain of and keep them to a phrase: Scout tells Atticus it would be sort of like shootin' a mockingbird.","Refer to, rather than quote, anything you are less sure of — the gifts in the knothole and the cement are still relevant examples when described in your own words.","Land the judgement: Lee gives the novel's clearest moral act to a child applying an adult's rule better than the court managed to."],"cues":["Which of these moments actually bear on innocence, and which are just memorable?","Why take one moment from each part of the novel?","How much of Scout's line do you need, and why no more?","What do you do with the knothole material if the wording escapes you?","What judgement should the paragraph end on?"]},{"title":"Writing the conclusion of a to what extent answer","setup":"You have written four paragraphs on this question: Atticus Finch is the moral centre of To Kill a Mockingbird. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. It carries 40 marks. Your paragraphs argued that he defends Tom knowing the case is lost, that he teaches Scout to climb into another person's skin, that he cannot save Tom, who is shot at the prison farm, and that it is Heck Tate, not Atticus, who decides what happens after Bob Ewell's death. Five minutes are left. Write the conclusion.","steps":["Answer the proposition in the opening clause, with the qualification built in: he is the novel's moral voice but not its moral engine, since nothing he does alters the verdict or saves Tom.","Turn one paragraph into the counterweight: Miss Maudie's praise that he is the same in his house as he is on the public streets makes him consistent, which is not the same as effective.","Add the contextual turn that only a conclusion can make: a white lawyer hero reassured many readers in 1960, and it is the part of the book later readers have most often questioned.","Give the last word to the novel rather than to yourself: Scout performs its final moral act when she stands on the Radley porch and sees her own street from Boo's side.","Read it back against the wording — to what extent has to be answered by a measured position, not by agreement or by a summary of your paragraphs."],"cues":["What must the first clause of the conclusion do?","Which of your own paragraphs can be turned against the proposition?","What can a conclusion say about context that a body paragraph cannot?","Who, in the novel, should have the last word here?","How do you check the conclusion against the command words?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How are the 40 marks for a Section C question divided, and which assessment objective does not appear?","a":"20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4; there is no AO2 credit in Section C, so analysis of language, form and structure is not what is being rewarded here."},{"q":"Why must your quotations from To Kill a Mockingbird be short and certain?","a":"Component 1 is closed book, so everything is written from memory; a phrase you are sure of proves the point, while a long or shaky quotation wastes time and can misrepresent the text."},{"q":"What is the test for a context point that is integrated rather than bolted on?","a":"Delete the context clause: if the sentence loses its explanation of the moment, the context was doing work; if it only loses a fact, it was decoration."},{"q":"Name the five categories of context the specification lists.","a":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section C — Modern Prose: one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1 (20 raw marks) and AO4 (20 raw marks). No AO2 credit is available in this section.","Component 1 is a two-hour written examination worth 90 raw marks and 60% of the International GCSE; pro rata, Section C is worth about 53 minutes. It is closed book: learners may not take texts into the examination, although the anthology poems are provided.","AO1: demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.","AO4: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.","The five categories of context the specification names: the writer's own life and individual situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context, such as movements or genres; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","To Kill a Mockingbird was published in 1960 and won the Pulitzer Prize in 1961. It is set in the fictional town of Maycomb, Alabama, in the Depression years of the 1930s.","The novel is in two parts and 31 chapters, narrated in the first person by the adult Jean Louise 'Scout' Finch, looking back on the three years that end with her brother Jem getting his arm badly broken at the elbow when he was nearly thirteen.","Part One closes with the death of Mrs Dubose; Part Two opens with Calpurnia taking Jem and Scout to First Purchase African M.E. Church.","Tom Robinson's left arm was crippled in a cotton-gin accident as a boy; the bruising on Mayella Ewell's face was on the right side, which points to a left-handed attacker.","Harper Lee was born in Monroeville, Alabama, in 1926; her father was a lawyer and newspaper editor, and her childhood friend Truman Capote is the model for Dill.","A caution about wording: the 40-mark tariff, the command words and the assessment objectives below are the board's. The instruction line used on the Section C stems in this room — You must refer to the context of the novel — is reconstructed, not quoted: the specification prints model stems only for the literary heritage questions, which read You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. The language, form and structure clause is dropped here because Section C carries no AO2 marks, but expect the real paper's wording to differ."],"questions":[{"q":"State how the 40 marks for the To Kill a Mockingbird question are divided between assessment objectives, and say what each objective requires. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"20 marks AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, a critical style, informed personal engagement) and 20 marks AO4 (the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written); no AO2 marks.","sol":"Section C, Modern Prose, is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two, and the breakdown of raw marks gives Questions 4 to 17 as 20 marks AO1 plus 20 marks AO4. AO1 asks the candidate to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. AO4 asks the candidate to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Analysis of language, form and structure (AO2) carries no marks in this section, so an answer built on device-spotting cannot reach the top of the range, while an answer with no context loses half the tariff.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This one is answered from the assessment overview, not from the novel.","Work from the tariff for the section and the objectives it names, then define each objective in the board's own words.","Two sentences, each naming one objective, its share of the tariff, and what it asks the candidate to demonstrate."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Identify four moments in To Kill a Mockingbird that an examiner would accept as relevant examples of racial prejudice, and say where each sits in the novel. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"Any four, correctly located: Lula's objection at First Purchase church; the mob at the jail; the children watching from the coloured balcony with Reverend Sykes; Mr Gilmer's cross-examination of Tom; the guilty verdict; Mrs Merriweather's missionary tea talk.","sol":"In Part Two, Calpurnia takes Jem and Scout to First Purchase African M.E. Church, where Lula challenges her for bringing white children — prejudice shown as a two-way wound rather than a one-way fault. Shortly after, a mob gathers at the jail to take Tom Robinson, and disperses only when Scout speaks to Mr Cunningham by name. At the trial the children can find seats only in the coloured balcony, where Reverend Sykes makes room for them, so the courtroom's own architecture is segregated. Mr Gilmer cross-examines Tom with contempt, fastening on his admission that he felt right sorry for her. Finally, the jury returns a guilty verdict against unanswerable physical evidence, and the balcony stands as Atticus leaves. Each of these is a moment, not a summary, which is what makes it usable as a relevant example.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Moments, not themes: an examiner rewards a place in the novel you can point to.","Take them from different stages of Part Two so they show the novel developing rather than one scene repeated.","One line each: who is involved, what happens, and roughly where in the novel it falls."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"State the five categories of context the specification names, giving one example of each from To Kill a Mockingbird. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"Writer's life (Lee's Monroeville childhood and lawyer father); historical setting (Depression Alabama); social and cultural (Jim Crow segregation, Maycomb's class ladder); literary (coming-of-age novel with Southern Gothic elements); reception (1960 readers against readers now).","sol":"One, the writer's own life and individual situation: Harper Lee grew up in Monroeville, Alabama, the daughter of a lawyer and newspaper editor, and wrote her childhood friend Truman Capote into Dill. Two, the historical setting, time and location of the text: Maycomb in the Depression years of the 1930s, where Walter Cunningham's family pays its debts in hickory nuts and stovewood. Three, social and cultural contexts: Jim Crow segregation, which puts the children in the coloured balcony and an all-white jury in the box, and the code of Southern womanhood that Aunt Alexandra presses on Scout. Four, the literary context: a first-person coming-of-age novel that borrows Southern Gothic furniture in the shuttered Radley house and its shut-in. Five, reception: published in 1960 into the civil rights years, it has since been challenged both for its language and for centring a white lawyer's heroism.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The list is the specification's, not one you invent for the novel.","Take the categories in the order the document gives them and match each with a single moment you can name.","Five lines: category, then the example, with nothing longer than a sentence."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Write down three short quotations from To Kill a Mockingbird that you could use in an answer on courage, and say what each one proves. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"Mrs Dubose definition — it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway; Atticus on the case — simply because we were licked a hundred years before we started is no reason for us not to try to win; Atticus on Mrs Dubose — she was the bravest person I ever knew.","sol":"First, Atticus's definition after Mrs Dubose's death: it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway — this proves that the novel's idea of courage is moral endurance rather than a man with a gun, and it is set up before the trial so that the trial can test it. Second, Atticus to Scout on why he is taking the case: simply because we were licked a hundred years before we started is no reason for us not to try to win — this proves he acts knowing the outcome, which is the definition applied to himself. Third, his verdict on his neighbour: she was the bravest person I ever knew — this proves that Lee gives the highest praise in the book to a dying, bad-tempered morphine addict rather than to a hero, which keeps courage unglamorous. Each is short enough to be written accurately from memory under closed-book conditions.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Courage in this novel is defined for the reader once, explicitly, before the trial.","Pick lines short enough to write out exactly from memory, then say what each one proves rather than where it comes from.","Three pairs: the quotation, then one clause on the claim it can support."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Explain why the closed-book condition of Component 1 changes how you prepare evidence for To Kill a Mockingbird. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"No text is allowed in the examination, so quotations must be memorised, short and exact; longer evidence has to be held as precisely located moments described accurately in your own words.","sol":"The specification states that Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into it, so every piece of support for a 40-mark essay is produced from memory. Preparation therefore has two layers. The first is a small bank of short, certain quotations, a phrase each, spread across the novel so that whichever of the two questions appears there is evidence for it. The second is a set of precisely located moments — the blanket round Scout's shoulders at the fire, the mob at the jail, the balcony standing as Atticus leaves — that can be referred to accurately without quotation, because the specification asks learners to identify and use relevant examples, and a moment described correctly is a relevant example. The risk the condition creates is misquotation, which damages the close knowledge AO1 rewards, so an uncertain line should be turned into a reference instead.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from the condition the assessment overview sets for this component.","Think about what a candidate can still use as support when no exact wording can be checked.","Two halves: what you memorise, and what you do with evidence you cannot recall word for word."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"How does Lee portray prejudice in Maycomb in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Prejudice shown as ordinary and inherited — class (Cunninghams, Ewells), gender (what a lady may be), fear of Boo, and race at the trial — built from household manners upward, and read against Jim Crow Alabama and Lee's 1960 readership.","sol":"Lee portrays prejudice in Maycomb as ordinary, inherited and domestic: it is learned at a dinner table long before it is voted on in a jury room. The novel begins with class rather than race. Walter Cunningham cannot pay for his lunch and pours syrup over his dinner, and when Scout shames him Calpurnia corrects her in the kitchen: anyone who sets foot in the house is company. Later Aunt Alexandra refuses to have him in it at all. In a town where a family pays a lawyer in hickory nuts and stovewood, Depression poverty has hardened into a ranking of families that everyone can recite. Gender prejudice runs alongside it: Scout is told what a lady wears and does, and Mayella Ewell's word is believed in court partly because the code of Southern womanhood requires it. The fear of Boo Radley shows the same habit working on a single harmless man: the children invent a monster out of gossip. Against this background the trial is not an aberration but the town's normal business conducted under oath. The physical evidence points to a left-handed attacker; Bob Ewell writes his name with his left hand; Tom's left arm was crippled in a cotton gin; and the all-white jury convicts anyway. Lee's first readers in 1960 knew such juries, and knew the Scottsboro cases of 1931, in which nine Black youths were accused in Alabama on the word of two white women and all but the youngest were convicted by all-white juries, the convictions later being overturned on appeal; writing about their parents' generation let her charge her own. The child narrator is the method that holds all this together, because Scout reports the town's attitudes before she understands them, so the reader watches prejudice being transmitted rather than being told it exists. Judgement: Lee portrays prejudice less as villainy than as inheritance, which is why the novel's hopeful moments are small — a jury that takes hours instead of minutes, a child on a porch — and why its charge against Maycomb is so hard to answer.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Prejudice in Maycomb is not only about race — the question says Maycomb, not the courtroom.","Build the answer from the ordinary scenes towards the trial, attaching one context to each stage.","Open with a claim about what kind of thing prejudice is in this novel, then let each paragraph test that claim on a different group."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Lee present courage in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Courage defined by Atticus through Mrs Dubose as beginning when you know you are licked; tested by Atticus's defence of Tom, by Scout at the jail, by Tom's testimony, and redefined by Boo at the end; read against Depression Alabama and 1960.","sol":"Lee presents courage as unglamorous moral endurance, and she defines it before she tests it. The definition is given over the least likeable character in the novel: Jem is sentenced to read to Mrs Dubose after destroying her camellias, and only after her death does Atticus explain that she was fighting a morphine addiction and meant to die free of it — it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway. That she was the bravest person he ever knew is deliberately awkward praise, because it separates courage from likeability. The definition is then applied to Atticus himself, who tells Scout that simply because we were licked a hundred years before we started is no reason for us not to try to win. Lee is careful to make his courage moral rather than physical: he shoots the mad dog only because he must, and Miss Maudie's praise is that he is the same in his house as he is on the public streets. Courage is also given to those with least protection. Tom Robinson admits in court that he felt right sorry for her, a sentence that a Black man in 1930s Alabama could not safely say of a white woman, and it ruins him. Scout, in ignorance, disperses a lynch mob by talking to Mr Cunningham about his entailment. Finally Boo Radley, who has not left his house in years, crosses the street in the dark for two children. Context sharpens all of this: in a segregated South where the Scottsboro convictions had shown what an Alabama jury would do, and for readers in 1960 who were watching people sit at lunch counters knowing they would be dragged off them, a definition of courage that begins with certain defeat was not an abstraction. Judgement: Lee's most convincing courage is the kind that changes nothing — Mrs Dubose dies, Tom is shot, Atticus loses — and the novel asks to be judged on whether beginning anyway is enough.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The novel defines courage for you, explicitly, before the trial begins.","Take the definition first and then test it on characters with very different amounts of power.","Let each paragraph ask what the courage costs and what it actually achieves, so the conclusion has something to weigh."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Lee use Scout as the narrator of To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Retrospective first-person narration: the child sees and the adult judges; innocence exposes adult prejudice, controls what is understood, allows comedy and the Radley myth, and lets Lee address 1960 readers through a 1930s child.","sol":"Lee narrates through Jean Louise Finch twice over: a child sees the events and a grown woman tells them, and the gap between the two is where the novel does its work. The opening sentence looks back from adulthood at Jem's arm being broken when he was nearly thirteen, so the reader knows from the first page that the story is being reconstructed and judged. The child's eye is used first to expose prejudice that adults have learned to disguise. Scout reports Maycomb's ranking of families, her own rudeness to Walter Cunningham and the town's talk about the Radleys without editing it, so the reader sees attitudes being passed on. The same innocence dissolves a lynch mob: she cannot see a mob, only Mr Cunningham, and asking after his entailment turns the men back into individuals. Narrative innocence also controls information. The children invent Boo as a monster out of neighbourhood gossip, which lets Lee build a Southern Gothic myth and then dismantle it, and Scout does not grasp the sexual charge of the trial, so the courtroom is reported as evidence rather than sensation and the injustice is left plain. The retrospective voice supplies what the child cannot: the adult narrator can tell us that Maycomb was a tired old town, can weigh Atticus, and can place the story in the Depression South. That double vision suited Lee's position in 1960, writing about her own Alabama childhood for readers in the middle of the civil rights years: a child's puzzlement at segregation is harder to argue with than an adult's accusation. Judgement: the narrator is the novel's central technique, and its limits are also the novel's — what Scout can reach, we reach, which is why Tom Robinson is seen mainly from the white side of the balcony rail, a limitation later readers have pressed hardest.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["There are two Scouts in the book, not one.","Group the uses: what the child's eye exposes, what it cannot see, and what the adult voice adds.","End by asking what the choice of narrator costs the novel as well as what it gains."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Lee make the trial of Tom Robinson the centre of To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"The trial gathers the novel's themes and tests its lessons: evidence against verdict, the balcony standing, Tom's death, and the children's loss of faith; Part One prepares it and Part Two lives with it, read against Jim Crow and Scottsboro.","sol":"Lee builds her novel so that the trial is both its structural centre and its moral test. Part One teaches the reader everything the courtroom will need: that Maycomb ranks its families, that courage means beginning when you are already beaten, that you never really understand a person until you climb into his skin and walk around in it. Part Two puts those lessons in front of a jury. The evidence is made deliberately one-sided. No doctor is called; the bruising is on the right side of Mayella's face; Bob Ewell writes his name with his left hand; Tom's left arm has been useless since a cotton gin took it. Lee removes every excuse the jury could have, so that when the verdict comes the only remaining explanation is race. The scene's most telling moments are wordless: the children watching from the coloured balcony, and Reverend Sykes telling Scout to stand up because her father is passing. The trial also breaks the children. Jem, who has believed in the law, cries at the verdict, and Lee refuses him consolation: Tom is shot dead attempting to escape the prison farm, and Mr Underwood writes that his death was like the senseless slaughter of songbirds, tying the trial to the novel's title symbol. Context is what gives the section its weight. In Jim Crow Alabama an all-white jury was the law's ordinary instrument, and the Scottsboro trials of 1931 had already shown the nation what such a jury would do with a Black defendant and a white accuser; Lee's 1960 readers, six years after Brown v. Board of Education, could not read the verdict as history. Judgement: the trial is central not because it is the most exciting section but because it converts the novel's private lessons into public failure, and everything after it — Bob Ewell's grudge, the attack, Boo's rescue — is the cost of that failure being paid.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the trial gathers up from Part One and what it releases into Part Two.","Handle the evidence precisely before you handle the verdict; the two are the same point.","Use the title symbol and the aftermath to show that the trial does not end when the jury speaks."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"To Kill a Mockingbird is a novel about childhood, not about justice. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"A qualified position: the childhood material is the vehicle, the justice material the cargo; the two cannot be separated because the children's education is what the injustice is measured by. Must weigh both and reach a judgement.","sol":"The proposition is half right, and the half it gets wrong is the more interesting one. The childhood claim is easy to support: the novel's shape is a summer-by-summer chronicle of games, dares and school, framed by Jem's broken elbow; its comedy belongs to children; and its deepest lessons are delivered as parenting, from Atticus's advice about climbing into another person's skin to the enforced afternoons with Mrs Dubose. Read this way the trial is one episode in an education. But Lee makes the education inseparable from the justice. The lessons of Part One are the criteria by which Part Two is judged: a reader who has learned what courage and point of view mean cannot watch an all-white jury convict a man whose left arm has been useless since childhood without measuring the verdict against those lessons. The novel is about justice precisely because it refuses to argue about it, letting a child report what she sees. Context strengthens the second reading. Lee published in 1960, into the years of Brown v. Board of Education and the bus boycott, a book set in the Depression South; a purely nostalgic novel about Alabama childhood would have been a strange thing to send into that moment, and its early readers did not take it as one. It is fair, though, to grant the proposition one real point: what the novel can show of injustice is limited by its narrator, since Tom Robinson is seen from the balcony and never from inside, and Helen Robinson's grief is reported rather than entered. Judgement: I agree only that the novel uses childhood as its method. Its subject is what a just society would have to be for those children to keep believing what they were taught, which is why it ends not in the courtroom but on the Radley porch, with Scout finally doing what Atticus asked.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["A to what extent question wants the proposition weighed, so find the strongest case for it first.","Ask what the childhood material is actually doing in the book rather than how much of it there is.","Let the concession be a real one — there is something the narration genuinely cannot reach."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Atticus Finch is the moral centre of To Kill a Mockingbird. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Agree that he is the moral voice, qualify heavily: he changes nothing, others act decisively (Scout at the jail, Boo, Heck Tate), and later readings, including after Go Set a Watchman, question the white-lawyer hero.","sol":"Atticus is the novel's moral voice, but calling him its centre overstates what he actually moves. The case for the proposition is strong. He gives the book its rules — you never really understand a person until you climb into his skin and walk around in it; it's a sin to kill a mockingbird — and he lives by them consistently, which is exactly what Miss Maudie praises when she says he is the same in his house as he is on the public streets. He takes a case he has already called lost, telling Scout that before he can live with other folks he has to live with himself. Yet the novel is careful to show that his integrity is not effective. He cannot save Tom, who is shot at the prison farm. He does not disperse the mob at the jail; his daughter does, by accident, because she sees Mr Cunningham as a neighbour rather than a mob. He does not resolve the final crisis either: Heck Tate overrules him about Bob Ewell's death, and it is Scout, not Atticus, who translates that decision into the novel's own moral language when she says it would be like shooting a mockingbird. Boo Radley, silent and shut in, performs the decisive act of the book. Context matters here too. In 1960 a principled white lawyer was a reassuring figure for a largely white readership, and it is exactly that figure which later readers have questioned, arguing that the novel centres a white man's conscience in a story about a Black man's death; the publication of Go Set a Watchman in 2015, with an older Atticus attending a citizens' council meeting, sharpened the argument. Judgement: he is the moral centre in the sense that he supplies the standard, but the novel deliberately gives the moral action to a child and a recluse, and that displacement is one of Lee's shrewdest decisions.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what Atticus says from what he changes.","Test the proposition against the three crises of the plot and ask who actually acts in each.","Reception is a category of context the specification names, and this proposition has a reception history."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"To what extent does To Kill a Mockingbird read differently now from the way it read on publication in 1960? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Substantially: 1960 readers met a hopeful, reforming novel about their own South; later readers question the white saviour framing, the limited interiority of Black characters and the racial language, while its account of inherited prejudice still holds.","sol":"The specification asks how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from the way it was read when it was written, and this novel is an unusually clear case. For readers in 1960 the book arrived in the middle of the argument it describes: six years after Brown v. Board of Education, four years after the Montgomery bus boycott ended, in the year of the lunch-counter sit-ins. Its setting in the Depression thirties let readers examine segregation at one generation's distance, and a lawyer who defends a Black man against a white accusation in an Alabama courtroom was, for that audience, a demanding and hopeful figure rather than a comfortable one; the Pulitzer Prize followed in 1961. Later readers have asked harder questions of the same pages. Tom Robinson is seen from the coloured balcony and never from inside his own mind; Calpurnia is defined by her service to the Finches; Black suffering is largely the occasion of a white child's education, and the novel's optimism rests on the decency of individual white people rather than on any Black character's agency. The racial language, historically accurate to the setting, has led to the book being challenged and removed from reading lists in some American schools. Go Set a Watchman, published in 2015, showed an older Atticus at a citizens' council meeting and forced many readers to reconsider the figure they had learned to admire. What survives the change of audience is the diagnosis: prejudice as ordinary, inherited household habit rather than monstrous exception, and the trial's demonstration that a court can be given unanswerable evidence and still do as the town expects. Judgement: the novel's account of how prejudice is learned reads as strongly now as in 1960, but its account of who fixes it does not, and a modern answer should say both.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["One of the five context categories is about audiences at different times — this question lives entirely inside it.","Set out what the first readership was living through before you set out what later readers object to.","Decide what in the novel has changed its meaning and what has not, so the judgement is a division rather than a verdict."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Both Section C questions on To Kill a Mockingbird look possible: one on the presentation of Boo Radley and one on the presentation of the town of Maycomb. Explain how you would choose between them, and set out the plan for the one you choose. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"Choose on remembered evidence and available context, not on interest; then a five-point plan with a thesis, four moments each carrying a context, and a conclusion that answers the question.","sol":"The choice is made on evidence, not on preference, because the examination is closed book. Test each question by listing, in ninety seconds, the moments you could locate accurately and the short quotations you are certain of. For Boo Radley that might be the gifts in the knothole, the cement, the mended trousers, the blanket at the fire, the rescue and the porch, with the line about shootin' a mockingbird. For Maycomb it might be the tired old town of the opening, the Cunninghams' entailment, the Ewells behind the dump, the missionary tea and the segregated balcony. Then test the context: a Boo question is served by the literary context of Southern Gothic and by the way the town creates its own monster, while a Maycomb question is served directly by the Depression setting and Jim Crow, which is the richer AO4 seam. Choosing Maycomb, the plan runs: thesis, that Lee presents Maycomb as a community whose manners, money and law all enforce the same ranking; paragraph one, the opening portrait of a tired old town in the Depression; paragraph two, the class ladder from the Cunninghams to the Ewells and Aunt Alexandra's rules about company; paragraph three, the town's treatment of Boo, showing the same instinct applied to a harmless man; paragraph four, the trial and the balcony, where the ranking is given the force of law; conclusion, that Maycomb is presented as ordinary rather than wicked, which is the charge Lee's 1960 readers were meant to feel. Each paragraph carries one context, so AO4 is earned throughout rather than in a block.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Under closed-book conditions the deciding factor is not which question you find more interesting.","Audit both questions for remembered evidence and then for the context each would let you use.","Give the plan as a thesis plus ordered paragraphs, each with its moment and its context attached."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Write one paragraph on Mayella Ewell that carries AO1 and AO4 marks at the same time, and then explain what a weaker version of the same paragraph would do instead. (Practice task, not an exam tariff: Component 1 offers only 20-, 30- and 40-mark questions.)","a":"A paragraph leading with a claim about Mayella as both victim and instrument of prejudice, proved by located evidence and explained through class and gender context in the same sentences; the weaker version narrates her testimony and adds a separate sentence about the 1930s.","sol":"Paragraph: Lee makes Mayella Ewell the point where class and race prejudice meet, and the novel's hardest test of its own lesson about climbing into another person's skin. She is the eldest Ewell child, motherless, keeping a houseful of children behind the town dump, and the only beautiful thing in her life is a row of red geraniums in slop jars; she is so unused to courtesy that Atticus calling her ma'am makes her think she is being mocked. That poverty is Depression poverty, and it puts her family at the bottom of every ranking in Maycomb except one: she is white, and Tom Robinson is not. Her accusation is therefore the use of the one privilege she has, and the code of Southern womanhood that the jury enforces requires them to believe her rather than him. Lee lets Scout pity her and still hold her responsible, which is why her cross-examination is uncomfortable rather than triumphant. Judgement: Mayella shows that prejudice in Maycomb is not simply the strong oppressing the weak but the weak being handed a weapon. Weaker version: it would retell her testimony — she says Tom attacked her, Atticus asks about her father, she gets angry and refuses to answer — and then add a separate sentence explaining that in the 1930s there was a lot of racism in the American South and women were expected to behave in certain ways. Every fact in it could be true, but the evidence would be narrated rather than used, the context would sit in its own sentence where it could be moved to any essay on the novel, and there would be no claim for the paragraph to prove.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["Look at two places in the novel and let them argue with each other: where Mayella stands inside the Ewell household behind the dump, and what her word is worth once she is in the witness box.","Write the claim first, then let each piece of evidence be explained by the context in the same sentence.","For the second half, describe the moves the weaker paragraph makes rather than its content: what it does with the story and where it puts the context."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section C question carry, and from a choice of how many questions on your set text?","a":"40 marks, from a choice of two."},{"q":"Which two assessment objectives are assessed in Section C, and with how many raw marks each?","a":"AO1 with 20 raw marks and AO4 with 20 raw marks."},{"q":"Whose left arm is crippled, and why does that matter at the trial?","a":"Tom Robinson's; the bruising on the right side of Mayella's face points to a left-handed attacker, so he could not have inflicted it."},{"q":"In what year was To Kill a Mockingbird published, and in which decade is it set?","a":"Published in 1960; set in the 1930s, the Depression years."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Section C tariff and objectives","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false,"back":"One 40-mark essay on the prose text, chosen from two. AO1 20 raw marks, AO4 20 raw marks, no AO2. Questions 4 to 17 in the breakdown of raw marks."},{"front":"Component 1: the paper","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false,"back":"Two-hour written examination, 90 raw marks, 60% of the International GCSE. Closed book: no texts allowed in, although the anthology poems are provided. Section C is worth about 53 minutes."},{"front":"AO1 in the board's words","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement."},{"front":"AO4 in the board's words","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false,"back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts."},{"front":"The five categories of context","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context, such as genre; and reception by different audiences at different times."},{"front":"The mockingbird symbol","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Atticus gives the rule: it's a sin to kill a mockingbird. Miss Maudie gives Scout the reason — mockingbirds do nothing but sing for us. Tom Robinson and Boo Radley are the novel's mockingbirds."},{"front":"Atticus on point of view","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"You never really understand a person until you consider things from his point of view, until you climb into his skin and walk around in it. Scout finally does it on the Radley porch."},{"front":"Mrs Dubose and the definition of courage","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Atticus on courage: it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway. She dies free of morphine; Atticus calls her the bravest person he ever knew. Part One ends with her death."},{"front":"The evidence at Tom Robinson's trial","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"No doctor was called; Mayella's bruising is on the right side of her face; Bob Ewell writes with his left hand; Tom's left arm was crippled in a cotton gin. The all-white jury convicts anyway."},{"front":"Harper Lee: the writer's own situation","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Born 1926 in Monroeville, Alabama; her father was a lawyer and newspaper editor; her childhood friend Truman Capote is the model for Dill. Published 1960, Pulitzer Prize 1961."},{"front":"The Scottsboro trials, 1931","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Nine Black youths, the youngest aged twelve, were accused of raping two white women in Alabama in 1931; all but the youngest were convicted by all-white juries, and the convictions were later overturned on appeal."},{"front":"Jim Crow segregation","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Southern state laws enforcing racial separation, upheld as separate but equal from 1896. They explain First Purchase church, the coloured balcony and the all-white jury in the novel."},{"front":"Reception since 1960","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"A prize-winning bestseller for readers living through Brown v. Board of Education and the bus boycott; later challenged for its racial language and its white saviour framing. Go Set a Watchman, 2015, prompted rereadings of Atticus."},{"front":"Form and structure of the novel","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false,"back":"Two parts, 31 chapters, first-person retrospective narration by the adult Scout. A coming-of-age novel with Southern Gothic elements, opening and closing on Jem's broken elbow."},{"front":"Three moments that show Maycomb's class prejudice","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":true,"back":"Walter Cunningham shamed at the Finch dinner table and corrected by Calpurnia; Aunt Alexandra ruling that a Cunningham is not fit company for Scout; Miss Caroline offering Walter Cunningham a quarter for his lunch, and Scout explaining that he is a Cunningham."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["The question: How does Lee portray prejudice in Maycomb in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","The paragraph: Lee makes prejudice domestic before she makes it judicial: it is learned at a dinner table long before it is voted on in a jury room. When Walter Cunningham drowns his dinner in syrup and Scout shames him for it, Calpurnia hauls her out to the kitchen and rules that anybody who sets foot in the house is company — the first of the novel's corrections, and the one that presents prejudice as a habit of manners rather than a matter of law. The choice of a child narrator is what makes the moment work: Scout reports her own snobbery without excusing it, so the reader watches the attitude being handed on instead of being told that it exists. For a readership in 1960, living through the sit-ins and six years past Brown v. Board of Education, the suggestion that segregation was sustained by ordinary inherited household manners, rather than by monsters, was the sharpest charge the novel could bring; it is also why the guilty verdict, when it comes, surprises nobody in Maycomb. Lee builds her courtroom, in other words, out of kitchens.","Why it reaches Level 5: assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style, and discriminating use of relevant examples — one located moment carries the whole point instead of three half-remembered ones.","Why it reaches Level 5: the context is integrated convincingly — delete the clause about 1960 and the paragraph loses its explanation of why the scene matters, not merely a fact.","Why it reaches Level 5: it takes a position (prejudice as inheritance rather than villainy) and ends on a mini-judgement that the rest of the essay can build on.","A note on the level label: the specification prints assessment grids only for the non-examined component (Component 3), where five levels are marked out of 30. There is no published grid for the 40-mark Section C essay, so the level named in this title is that grid borrowed as the nearest available standard, and the wording quoted in the justifications is the Component 3 wording, not a printed Section C level."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["The same question: How does Lee portray prejudice in Maycomb in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","The paragraph: Lee shows prejudice in Maycomb in lots of different ways. When Walter Cunningham comes to dinner he puts syrup all over his food and Scout is rude about it, so Calpurnia takes her into the kitchen and tells her off. This shows that the Finch children have to learn to treat poorer people properly. There was a lot of prejudice in America in the 1930s because of segregation, and Harper Lee was writing in 1960 when the civil rights movement was happening. This shows that prejudice was a big problem at the time and that Lee wanted things to change.","Why it stops at Level 3: the knowledge is sound and the example is clearly relevant, but the scene is retold rather than used — the paragraph narrates the dinner instead of proving a claim about it.","Why it stops at Level 3: the context is true but bolted on. Both context sentences could be lifted into any paragraph of any essay on this novel without changing anything, which is what the grid means by context that is not integrated.","Why it stops at Level 3: this shows replaces a critical voice, there is no personal position, and the paragraph ends with a general wish rather than a judgement about how Lee portrays prejudice.","How to move it up: lead with the claim (prejudice is taught in households before it is enforced in courts), keep the syrup and Calpurnia's rule as evidence behind it, and put the 1960 readership inside the sentence that explains why the moment matters.","A note on the level label: the specification prints assessment grids only for the non-examined component (Component 3), where five levels are marked out of 30. There is no published grid for the 40-mark Section C essay, so the level named in this title is that grid borrowed as the nearest available standard, and the wording quoted in the justifications is the Component 3 wording, not a printed Section C level."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"To Kill a Mockingbird, Harper Lee — the facts an examiner rewards","items":["Published 1960; awarded the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961; filmed in 1962 with Gregory Peck as Atticus.","Setting: the fictional town of Maycomb, Maycomb County, Alabama, in the Depression years of the 1930s.","Narrator: Jean Louise 'Scout' Finch, telling the story as an adult about her own childhood; the novel opens and closes with Jem's arm being broken at the elbow when he was nearly thirteen.","Structure: two parts, 31 chapters. Part One ends with the death of Mrs Dubose; Part Two opens with Calpurnia taking the children to First Purchase African M.E. Church.","Form: first-person retrospective coming-of-age novel, with Southern Gothic elements in the shuttered Radley house and its recluse.","Character — Atticus Finch: the children's widowed father, a Maycomb lawyer who also sits in the state legislature; Scout calls him feeble because he was nearly fifty.","Character — Jem Finch: Scout's older brother, whose faith in the law is broken by the verdict.","Character — Calpurnia: the Finches' cook, who raises the children, corrects Scout's manners and takes them to her own church.","Character — Charles Baker 'Dill' Harris: the children's summer friend, who stays next door with his aunt Miss Rachel; he weeps at Mr Gilmer's treatment of Tom.","Character — Arthur 'Boo' Radley: the shut-in next door who leaves gifts in the knothole, mends Jem's trousers, puts a blanket round Scout at the fire and finally saves both children.","Character — Nathan Radley: Boo's brother, who fills the knothole with cement.","Character — Tom Robinson: a Black field hand, married to Helen and employed by Link Deas; his left arm was crippled in a cotton gin; he is convicted of raping Mayella Ewell and shot dead trying to escape the prison farm.","Character — Mayella Ewell: the eldest Ewell child, who keeps red geraniums in slop jars behind the town dump and accuses Tom.","Character — Bob Ewell: her father, who spits in Atticus's face after the trial, threatens Helen Robinson and attacks the children on Halloween night.","Character — Aunt Alexandra: Atticus's sister, who moves in to give Scout a feminine influence and hosts the missionary circle tea.","Character — Miss Maudie Atkinson: the neighbour whose house burns down; she explains why it is a sin to kill a mockingbird and says Atticus Finch is the same in his house as he is on the public streets.","Character — Mrs Henry Lafayette Dubose: the neighbour whose camellias Jem destroys; Jem reads to her while she breaks a morphine addiction before her death.","Character — Heck Tate: the sheriff, who testifies at the trial and insists at the end that Bob Ewell fell on his knife.","Character — Judge Taylor, who appoints Atticus, and Mr Gilmer, who prosecutes.","Character — Reverend Sykes, who seats the children in the coloured balcony and tells Scout to stand up because her father is passing.","Character — Mr Dolphus Raymond, who pretends to be drunk so that the town has an explanation for his life among Black neighbours.","Character — Mr Underwood of The Maycomb Tribune, who writes that Tom's death was like the senseless slaughter of songbirds.","Plot 1: summer games with Dill, and the children's attempts to make Boo Radley come out.","Plot 2: Scout's first day at school with Miss Caroline; Walter Cunningham's lunch; Burris Ewell; Atticus teaches her to climb into another person's skin.","Plot 3: gifts appear in the knothole of the oak — chewing gum, Indian-head pennies, soap carvings of the children, a spelling medal, a broken pocket watch — until Nathan Radley fills the hole with cement.","Plot 4: Jem's torn trousers are returned to him mended and folded on the fence.","Plot 5: Miss Maudie's house burns down and a blanket is put round Scout's shoulders in the dark without her noticing.","Plot 6: Atticus shoots the mad dog, Tim Johnson, and the children learn he was once the best shot in the county.","Plot 7: Jem destroys Mrs Dubose's camellias and reads to her as punishment; she dies free of morphine and leaves him a white camellia in a candy box.","Plot 8: Calpurnia takes Jem and Scout to First Purchase, where Lula objects; Aunt Alexandra arrives to stay.","Plot 9: a mob comes to the jail for Tom, and disperses when Scout speaks to Mr Cunningham about his entailment and his son.","Plot 10: the trial — Heck Tate, Bob Ewell, Mayella and Tom all testify; the children watch from the coloured balcony; the jury is out for hours and returns a guilty verdict.","Plot 11: Bob Ewell spits in Atticus's face; Tom is shot at the prison farm; the news reaches the Finch house during Aunt Alexandra's missionary tea.","Plot 12: on Halloween night Bob Ewell attacks Jem and Scout on the way home from the school pageant; Boo Radley saves them and carries Jem home; Bob Ewell is found dead under the oaks.","Plot 13: Heck Tate insists Bob Ewell fell on his own knife; Scout tells Atticus that dragging Boo into the light would be sort of like shootin' a mockingbird, and walks him home.","Theme: prejudice as inherited habit — racial, but also class (Finches, Cunninghams, Ewells) and gender (what a lady may be).","Theme: courage as moral endurance, defined through Mrs Dubose and tested on Atticus, Tom, Scout and Boo.","Theme: innocence and its protection — the mockingbird rule, applied to Tom by Mr Underwood and to Boo by Scout.","Theme: education and point of view — the novel is a sequence of lessons, ending when Scout stands on the Radley porch.","Theme: the law and the community — a court given unanswerable evidence that still does what the town expects.","Context, writer's life: Harper Lee, born 1926 in Monroeville, Alabama, daughter of a lawyer and newspaper editor; Truman Capote was her childhood neighbour and the model for Dill.","Context, historical setting: the Great Depression; Walter Cunningham's family pays its debts in hickory nuts and stovewood because there is no money in Maycomb County.","Context, social and cultural: Jim Crow segregation laws in the Southern states, separate but equal since 1896; all-white juries; the code of Southern womanhood that Aunt Alexandra presses on Scout and the jury enforces for Mayella.","Context, historical parallel: the Scottsboro cases of 1931, in which nine Black youths, the youngest aged twelve, were accused of raping two white women in Alabama; eight were convicted by all-white juries and the youngest's trial ended in a mistrial, and the convictions were later overturned on appeal (Powell v. Alabama, 1932; Norris v. Alabama, 1935).","Context, the moment of publication: 1960 — six years after Brown v. Board of Education, four years after the Montgomery bus boycott ended, the year lunch-counter sit-ins spread across the South.","Context, literary: a coming-of-age novel in the Southern tradition, using Gothic materials (the closed house, the rumoured monster) which the plot then dismantles.","Context, reception: hugely popular and widely taught, but challenged for its racial language, for the limited interiority of its Black characters and for centring a white lawyer's conscience; Go Set a Watchman (2015) showed an older Atticus at a citizens' council meeting.","Key moment an examiner rewards: Reverend Sykes telling Scout to stand up because her father is passing, as the balcony rises after the verdict.","Key moment an examiner rewards: Scout dispersing the mob by talking to Mr Cunningham as a neighbour rather than as part of a crowd.","Key moment an examiner rewards: the final exchange — Atticus, he was real nice; most people are, Scout, when you finally see them."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Examination conditions and the edition","items":["Component 1 is a closed book examination: learners may not take texts into it, so all To Kill a Mockingbird evidence is written from memory.","Because the component is closed book there are no prescribed editions of the set texts.","The recommended edition is the Arrow 50th Anniversary Edition, June 2010, ISBN 9780099549482.","Only the anthology poems are provided in the paper; nothing from the prose text is reproduced.","Component 2, by contrast, is open book with a clean unmarked copy allowed — do not carry that habit into Section C revision.","Practical consequence: revise a bank of short, certain quotations plus a longer list of precisely located moments that can be referred to accurately in your own words.","Wording caution: the Section C stems in this room end You must refer to the context of the novel. That line is inferred, not printed by the board — the specification models stems only for the literary heritage questions, which also ask for language, form and structure. The tariff, the command words and the objectives are the board's; this instruction line is a reconstruction."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How does Lee portray prejudice in Maycomb in To Kill a Mockingbird? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","items":["Decoded: portray asks by what means Lee builds the picture, not what happens; prejudice covers race, class, gender and the town's treatment of Boo; Maycomb means the whole town and therefore the whole novel, not only the trial.","A note on the stem: the question above is written in the board's command words and at the board's 40-mark tariff, but the instruction line You must refer to the context of the novel is reconstructed — the specification prints model stems only for the literary heritage questions, where the line also asks for language, form and structure.","Tariff: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4, so every paragraph needs a located moment from memory and a context that explains it. No AO2 marks, so device-spotting earns nothing here.","Thesis to defend: Lee portrays prejudice as ordinary and inherited — taught in households before it is enforced in courtrooms — so that the verdict is the town's normal business rather than an aberration.","Paragraph 1 — class: Walter Cunningham at the dinner table and Calpurnia's rule about company; Aunt Alexandra later refusing him the house. Context: Depression poverty, debts paid in hickory nuts and stovewood, and a ranking of families everyone can recite.","Paragraph 2 — gender: Aunt Alexandra on what a lady is, and Mayella's word carrying weight in court because she is a white woman. Context: the code of Southern womanhood, which protects and confines at once.","Paragraph 3 — fear of difference: the children's invented monster at the Radley place, built out of Miss Stephanie Crawford's gossip. Context: the literary Gothic materials Lee borrows and then dismantles when Boo turns out to be the rescuer.","Paragraph 4 — race and the law: the coloured balcony, the unanswerable physical evidence, the all-white jury, the guilty verdict, and the balcony standing as Atticus leaves. Context: Jim Crow segregation and the Scottsboro trials of 1931.","Paragraph 5 — the aftermath: Tom shot at the prison farm and Mr Underwood's editorial on the senseless slaughter of songbirds. Context: a 1960 readership in the middle of Brown v. Board of Education and the sit-ins, being shown their parents' South.","Evaluation thread running through: is Maycomb's prejudice the fault of the town's manners or of its law? Each paragraph pushes the answer further from individual wickedness towards inherited, institutional habit — which is what makes the child narrator the right method.","The conclusion must say: that Lee portrays prejudice as inheritance rather than villainy, that this is why the novel's hopeful signs are so small (a jury out for hours, a child on a porch), and that a reader in 1960 was being shown their own inheritance rather than a historical curiosity. It must answer how, not summarise what.","Timing: about 53 minutes — five to plan, forty-three to write five paragraphs and a conclusion, five to check names, quotations and that every paragraph carries a context."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Closed-book quotation bank: short lines you can hold exactly","items":["Narration, opening: Maycomb was an old town, but it was a tired old town when I first knew it. Use for setting and the adult voice.","Atticus to Scout, after her first day at school: you never really understand a person until you consider things from his point of view; until you climb into his skin and walk around in it. Use for education, point of view, the ending.","Atticus on air rifles: shoot all the bluejays you want, if you can hit 'em, but remember it's a sin to kill a mockingbird. Use for the title symbol, innocence, Tom and Boo.","Atticus on the case: simply because we were licked a hundred years before we started is no reason for us not to try to win. Use for courage, integrity, futility.","Atticus on conscience: before I can live with other folks I've got to live with myself. The one thing that doesn't abide by majority rule is a person's conscience. Use for the individual against the town.","Atticus on Mrs Dubose: it's when you know you're licked before you begin but you begin anyway. She was the bravest person I ever knew. Use for the definition of courage.","Miss Maudie on Atticus: Atticus Finch is the same in his house as he is on the public streets. Use for integrity and for the counter-argument that consistency is not effectiveness.","Reverend Sykes to Scout after the verdict: Miss Jean Louise, stand up. Your father's passin'. Use for respect, the balcony, dignity in defeat.","Tom Robinson in the witness box: I felt right sorry for her. Use for the moment pity from a Black man to a white woman becomes unforgivable in that courtroom.","Heck Tate at the end: let the dead bury the dead. Use for the law bending to protect Boo.","Scout to Atticus about Boo: well, it'd be sort of like shootin' a mockingbird, wouldn't it? Use for the child applying the novel's rule better than the court did.","The closing exchange: Atticus, he was real nice. Most people are, Scout, when you finally see them. Use for the ending, point of view, qualified hope.","Rule for everything else: if you are not certain of the wording, describe the moment instead — a precisely located reference is still a relevant example, a misquotation is not."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:43:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"I re-solved all fourteen questions and checked every line in the quotation bank word for word against the novel — all twelve are Lee's actual wording, correctly attributed and correctly used, and the answer keys and model answers are sound, so this publishes; the remaining faults are a worked example whose steps prune details its setup never supplied, and three small looseness points."}}},"prose-of-mice-and-men":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-of-mice-and-men","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Of Mice and Men is your Section C text on Component 1, and Section C is the largest single question on the paper: one 40-mark essay chosen from two, closed book, inside a two-hour, 90-mark examination worth 60% of the International GCSE. Twenty of those marks are AO1, close knowledge and understanding of the novel held in memory and used in a critical style, and twenty are AO4, understanding of the relationship between the novel and the context in which it was written. Context is therefore half the mark on every question you can be asked about this text, and nothing may be carried into the room with you. This room drills both halves together: the remembered moments and short quotations, and the argument that makes the history of the 1930s explain them.","idea":[{"code":"Component 1: Content overview","h":"Of Mice and Men as your one modern prose set text","t":"Component 1 asks you to study two set texts: every poem in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one modern prose text, which for you is Of Mice and Men by John Steinbeck, chosen from a list of seven. The specification wants whole texts studied, developing comprehension and critical reading, so you need the shape of all six chapters, the characters, the themes and the ending in memory, not a handful of favourite extracts. A top-level answer shows that whole-text knowledge by ranging deliberately across the novella: a point drawn from the clearing by the pool, a point from the bunkhouse or Crooks's room, and a point from the barn or the final chapter, rather than three points from one remembered scene."},{"code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style and informed personal engagement","t":"Learners must demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the text. That is twenty of the forty marks. Because Section C is closed book, every example comes out of your memory: short exact quotations where you are certain of the words, and precisely described moments where you are not, because a misremembered line does more damage than an accurate description. A critical style means writing as a reader making an argument rather than as a narrator retelling the plot. A top-level answer states a personal reading in its opening lines, then supports it with examples chosen for that argument, and never spends a paragraph telling the examiner what happens."},{"code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","h":"AO4: the novel and the context in which it was written","t":"The other twenty marks are AO4: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. Context is half the mark on every Of Mice and Men question, so a paragraph that only discusses the characters is competing for half of what it could earn. The useful contexts here are the Great Depression that followed the Wall Street Crash, itinerant ranch labour in California, segregation, the position of women, and attitudes to disability. A top-level answer never bolts a history lesson on at the end: it uses context to explain why a character behaves as he does, so that deleting the context would leave the argument broken."},{"code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","t":"The specification lists five categories that affect writers' work and readers' responses: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from when it was written. Each opens a different paragraph on this novel: Steinbeck's own ranch work in the Salinas Valley; California in the 1930s; attitudes to race, sex, age and disability; the play-novelette form; and how readers now judge Curley's wife. A top-level answer draws on more than one category across the essay instead of repeating the Depression in every paragraph."},{"code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","h":"What the Of Mice and Men question looks like on the day","t":"Section C – Modern Prose sets one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and it assesses AO1 and AO4 only; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4, with no AO2 credit available. Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark, closed book examination worth 60% of the International GCSE: the anthology poems are provided for Sections A and B, but the novel is not. Pro rata, Section C is worth about 53 of your 120 minutes. A top-level answer spends two or three of those minutes choosing between the two questions and planning four paragraphs that each carry evidence and context, then writes straight through without pausing to summarise the plot."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a Section C question into a plan that carries both objectives","setup":"The paper offers: 'How does Steinbeck present loneliness in Of Mice and Men?' It is worth 40 marks, split 20 AO1 and 20 AO4, you have about 53 minutes, and you have no copy of the novel. Produce a plan of four paragraphs in which each paragraph carries both objectives.","steps":["Decode the command first: 'How does Steinbeck present' asks for the writer's handling of loneliness, so every paragraph must name something Steinbeck does to the reader, not something that merely happens to a character.","Choose four figures whose loneliness has a different social cause: George and Lennie as the exception, Crooks shut in the harness room, Curley's wife shut out of the bunkhouse, and Candy after his dog is shot.","Attach one remembered example to each: George's line that guys who work on ranches are the loneliest guys in the world; Crooks telling Lennie a guy goes nuts if he ain't got nobody; Curley's wife saying she gets awful lonely; Candy's regret that he let a stranger shoot his dog.","Attach one context to each so that AO4 is earned inside the paragraph: itinerant ranch work in the Depression, segregation in California, the narrow role open to a married woman on a ranch, and the fear of being discarded once you can no longer work.","Fix the thread that makes it an argument rather than a list: loneliness here is manufactured by the ranch and the decade, not by any character's temperament, so the conclusion can judge the society instead of the people."],"cues":["What does the phrase 'How does Steinbeck present' require in every single paragraph?","Which four figures let you show four different causes of the same thing?","What must each of those four paragraphs be able to quote or point to from memory?","Where does the context have to sit if it is going to earn AO4 marks?","What one idea would turn four separate paragraphs into a single argument?"]},{"title":"Integrating context instead of bolting it on","setup":"A student ends a paragraph about Crooks like this: 'Crooks is treated badly. In the 1930s there was a lot of racism in America and black people were segregated.' The task is to rewrite that ending so the context earns AO4 marks inside the argument about Steinbeck's presentation of Crooks.","steps":["Apply the deletion test: cross the history sentence out and the paragraph reads exactly as before, which is the definition of detachable context and sits at the bottom of the AO4 range however accurate it is.","Find the textual detail the history could explain: Crooks has his bunk in the harness room itself, a shed leaning off the wall of the barn, rather than in the bunkhouse with the white hands, and he keeps a battered copy of the California civil code among his things.","Join them so the history explains the detail: segregation is why the one man on the ranch with a door of his own is also the one man with nobody to let through it, and why he needs a law book to check rights he has no way of enforcing.","Turn the join into a judgement about the writer: Steinbeck gives Crooks privacy and reading only to show that both are consolation prizes handed out in place of company."],"cues":["What quick test shows you that the context is bolted on?","Which remembered details of Crooks's room could the history explain?","How do you word the link so the history does the explaining?","What must the closing sentence say about Steinbeck to reach the top band?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions in Section C","setup":"Section C offers two questions on Of Mice and Men: 'In what ways does Steinbeck present the dream of owning land?' and 'To what extent do you agree that Curley's wife is presented sympathetically?' Both are worth 40 marks. You have four minutes to choose and rough out a plan. You are certain of the words of Lennie's \"An' live off the fatta the lan'\" and of Curley's wife's \"I get awful lonely\".","steps":["Count the evidence from memory before you read either wording again: the land question can call on the recitation of the farm by the pool in the first chapter, Candy's savings in the third, Crooks asking whether there would be room for him in the fourth and George telling the story once more in the last, which is four different chapters; the question on Curley's wife can call on the bunkhouse doorway in the second chapter, her threat to Crooks in the fourth and the barn in the fifth, which is three.","Place the two quotations you are certain of: Lennie's \"An' live off the fatta the lan'\" belongs to the land question and can anchor two of its paragraphs, the recitation and the retelling at the end, while Curley's wife's \"I get awful lonely\" anchors only the barn paragraph of the other, leaving the rest to be described rather than quoted.","Test each against context: the land question opens the Depression, monthly wages, the families driven west and the American Dream, which is a different context for every paragraph; the second opens the position of women and how differently readers judge her now, which is strong material but two ideas asked to cover four paragraphs.","Weigh the command phrases: 'In what ways' rewards range of method, which suits paragraphs with four different causes, while 'To what extent' obliges you to build in a concession and come down on one side, which is more planning than the time allows here.","Make the choice and commit: take the land question, because it carries the most recallable evidence, uses the quotation you are sure of twice over and gives each paragraph its own context; then write the thesis sentence before starting paragraph one, that Steinbeck presents the farm as a story holding two men together which the decade had already made impossible."],"cues":["What should you count before you read either wording a second time?","Where does each of the two quotations you are certain of actually fit?","Which contexts does each question open, and how far do they stretch?","What does each of the two command phrases oblige you to do?","Which question wins on that evidence, and what is the last thing to write before paragraph one?"]},{"title":"Building one paragraph that carries AO1 and AO4 together","setup":"Question: 'How does Steinbeck present the treatment of those who are no longer useful in Of Mice and Men?' (40 marks). Work on one moment only: Carlson insists that Candy's old dog be shot, Candy gives way once Slim agrees, and afterwards Candy tells George that he ought to have shot the dog himself and should never have let a stranger do it. Build a single paragraph from it.","steps":["Open with a claim, not a retelling: Steinbeck uses the dog to show that usefulness, not loyalty or long service, decides who is allowed to stay on the ranch.","Set out the evidence as an argument nobody answers: Carlson's case is simply that the dog is old, smells and can do nothing for Candy any more, and not one man in the bunkhouse contradicts it.","Analyse Candy's regret: he blames himself for letting a stranger do it rather than blaming the killing, which shows he has accepted the ranch's reasoning, and that is how the weak are made complicit in their own disposal.","Place the context inside the analysis: Candy is a swamper kept on with one hand after an accident, so in a decade with far more fit men than jobs the shooting of the dog is a rehearsal of his own dismissal.","Close on a mini-judgement: the moment is less about cruelty than about a system that persuades its victims to agree with it."],"cues":["What kind of sentence must the paragraph open with?","What is the argument that nobody on the ranch contradicts?","What does Candy's particular regret reveal about him?","Which context explains why Candy could not resist?","What judgement should close the paragraph?"]},{"title":"Writing a conclusion to a 'To what extent' question","setup":"Question: 'Of Mice and Men shows that dreams are worth having even when they fail. To what extent do you agree?' (40 marks). Your four paragraphs have argued that the dream gives George and Lennie a future the other hands lack; that it briefly draws in Candy and even Crooks; that Curley's wife has a failed dream of her own; and that George shoots Lennie at the end. Write the conclusion.","steps":["Refuse to summarise: the opening sentence must land on the side you come down on, because a conclusion that begins by restating the paragraphs wastes the place where a marker looks for judgement.","Concede the strongest point against you: nobody in the novel reaches the farm, and it is the dream itself that makes the last chapter bearable enough for George to go through with the shooting.","State the qualified agreement: the dream is worth having for what it does while it is alive, since it gives two men something to say to each other that the hands who blow their pay in a night have never had.","Close on the writer and his time: Steinbeck lets the dream fail because in Depression California men on monthly wages had no way of buying land, so the novel passes judgement on the country rather than on the dreamers."],"cues":["How must the first sentence of a conclusion behave?","What is the strongest objection you have to admit?","On what grounds can you still agree with the proposition?","What must the final sentence say about Steinbeck and his decade?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does Section C carry on Of Mice and Men, and how are they divided?","a":"40 marks, divided equally: 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4, with no AO2 credit in this section."},{"q":"Why must the quotations you use be short and certain?","a":"Component 1 is closed book, so every example is recalled from memory; an accurately described moment scores, a misquoted line undermines the whole answer."},{"q":"Where must context appear in a Section C paragraph to reach the top band?","a":"Inside the argument, explaining a detail of the text, so that deleting it would break the point; a block of history at the end scores at the bottom of the AO4 range."},{"q":"What are the five categories of context the specification names?","a":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times."},{"q":"What does a critical style mean in practice for AO1?","a":"Writing as a reader arguing a personal reading of the novel, not as a narrator retelling the plot."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section C of Component 1 is one 40-mark essay on Of Mice and Men chosen from two questions: 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4, closed book, Questions 4 to 17 in the breakdown.","Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark examination worth 60% of the International GCSE; the anthology poems are provided but the novel is not.","Of Mice and Men was published in 1937 and is set on a ranch a few miles south of Soledad, in the Salinas Valley of California, during the Great Depression.","The title comes from Robert Burns's poem 'To a Mouse' (1785): 'The best laid schemes o' Mice an' Men / Gang aft agley.'","The novella has six chapters, each set in one place and built largely from dialogue: Steinbeck wrote it as a play-novelette and it was staged in the year it was published.","The five categories of context named by the specification: the writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; reception by different audiences at different times.","John Steinbeck was born in Salinas, California in 1902, worked on ranches as a young man, published The Grapes of Wrath in 1939 and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1962."],"questions":[{"q":"State how many marks the Of Mice and Men question carries in Section C of Component 1, how those marks are divided between the assessment objectives, and whether the novel may be taken into the examination. (Practice recall task: Section C itself sets only the 40-mark essay, so this tariff is for practice, not an examination tariff.)","a":"40 marks, divided 20 AO1 and 20 AO4 with no AO2; the examination is closed book, so the text may not be taken in.","sol":"Section C – Modern Prose sets one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 4 to 17. The 40 raw marks divide equally: 20 for AO1, which is close knowledge and understanding of the text maintained in a critical style with informed personal engagement, and 20 for AO4, understanding of the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written. No AO2 marks are available in this section. Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts in; only the anthology poems needed for Sections A and B are provided. Section C is the largest single question on a 90-mark, two-hour paper worth 60% of the International GCSE.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Work out where Section C sits inside the 90 marks of Component 1.","Only two of the four assessment objectives are named for this section, and they share the marks equally.","Finish by stating the rule this component applies to texts in the examination room."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"State three contextual facts about 1930s America that an examiner would reward in an answer on Of Mice and Men, and say in each case which character they would help you explain. (Practice recall task: Section C itself sets only the 40-mark essay, so this tariff is for practice, not an examination tariff.)","a":"For example: the Depression after the 1929 Crash (George and Lennie's itinerant work); segregation in California (Crooks); the narrow role allowed to women (Curley's wife).","sol":"Any three of the following, each tied to a character. The Wall Street Crash of 1929 and the Depression that followed left far more men looking for work than there was work to be had, which is why George and Lennie arrive on foot with their bindles and take what the ranch offers. Itinerant ranch hands, hired by the month, carried their bedding from place to place and had no home, which is exactly the life George describes when he calls men like them the loneliest in the world. The Dust Bowl drove farming families west to California in search of seasonal farm work, so the ranch is surrounded by surplus labour. Racial segregation kept a black stable buck such as Crooks out of the white bunkhouse by custom rather than by law. Women of the period had little independent work or money and were expected to marry, which is the whole of Curley's wife's situation. The American Dream of owning a smallholding had, by the 1930s, become unreachable for men on monthly wages, which is why the farm never arrives. What earns the marks is not the fact but the use: each one must explain a detail of Steinbeck's novel.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about why so many men were on the road in the years after the Crash.","The specification asks for historical, social and cultural contexts, so take your three from more than one of those categories.","Each fact you pick should be one you could immediately attach to a named character."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Identify three moments in Of Mice and Men in which Lennie's strength causes harm, and say where each falls in the novel. (Practice recall task: Section C itself sets only the 40-mark essay, so this tariff is for practice, not an examination tariff.)","a":"Weed, before the novel opens; the bunkhouse in Chapter 3, where he crushes Curley's hand; the barn in Chapter 5, where he kills the puppy and then Curley's wife.","sol":"Steinbeck spaces the incidents so that each is worse than the last. Before the novel opens, at Weed, Lennie takes hold of a girl's red dress because he likes the feel of it and will not let go when she screams, and George has to hide with him in an irrigation ditch until dark; George reports this to Slim in the bunkhouse. In the third chapter Curley picks a fight with Lennie in the bunkhouse and Lennie, once George tells him to fight back, catches and crushes Curley's hand; Slim then forces Curley to say the hand was caught in a machine. In the fifth chapter, in the barn, Lennie has already killed his puppy by handling it too roughly, and then, stroking Curley's wife's hair, panics at her cries and breaks her neck. The pattern matters for AO1: mouse, puppy, woman, and in every case the harm comes from wanting to hold something soft rather than from anger, which is why the ending is a tragedy and not a punishment.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look for the pattern of soft things Lennie wants to touch.","One of the incidents happens before the novel starts and reaches the reader as a story told to Slim.","The last two both happen indoors, in two different buildings on the ranch."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explain why Crooks does not sleep in the bunkhouse, and state what a reader learns from this about the society Steinbeck is writing about. (Practice recall task: Section C itself sets only the 40-mark essay, so this tariff is for practice, not an examination tariff.)","a":"He is the black stable buck and is segregated into the harness room itself, a shed leaning off the barn wall, admitted to the bunkhouse only at Christmas; this shows the everyday segregation of 1930s California.","sol":"Crooks is the ranch's stable buck and the only black man on it, and he is not allowed to live with the white hands. Steinbeck gives him his bunk in the harness room itself, a shed leaning off the wall of the barn, among the harness, the tools and the medicine bottles, and Crooks tells Lennie plainly that because he is not wanted in the bunkhouse, Lennie is not wanted in his room. The men let him in only at Christmas, when there is drink and a fight. A reader learns that segregation in 1930s America was not only a matter of law in the South but of daily custom in California too, and that it was enforced by men who were themselves poor and powerless. It also explains Crooks's behaviour without excusing it: the loneliness the other hands complain about between jobs is, for him, permanent, so his cruelty to Lennie about George not coming back is a man practising on someone weaker the only power he has ever been given.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["His job and the place he sleeps are both clues.","Think about the single day of the year on which the men's behaviour towards him changes.","Link the arrangement to the social attitudes of the decade in which the novel was written."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall one short quotation in which George describes the loneliness of ranch workers, and explain what it contributes to the argument of the novel. (Practice recall task: Section C itself sets only the 40-mark essay, so this tariff is for practice, not an examination tariff.)","a":"'Guys like us, that work on ranches, are the loneliest guys in the world' - George makes loneliness a condition of the job rather than a fault in any individual.","sol":"George says it by the pool in the first chapter, and the speech is repeated at that same pool in the last: 'Guys like us, that work on ranches, are the loneliest guys in the world.' What he says to Slim in the bunkhouse in the third chapter is a different remark, that he has seen the men who go round the ranches on their own and that it does them no good, so do not place the famous line there. The line is the novel's thesis, given to a character rather than to the narrator: it defines a whole class of men rather than one unhappy person, so the loneliness the reader later meets in Candy, in Crooks and in Curley's wife arrives as the ordinary result of itinerant work and social exclusion rather than as bad luck. George then sets himself and Lennie against it, insisting that they have a future and somebody to talk to who gives a damn about them. A top-level answer therefore uses the quotation as the standard against which every later character is measured, and notices that Steinbeck has the exception state the rule.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The line comes early, when George is talking about the class of men he belongs to.","It begins by naming a group rather than an individual.","Say what it lets a reader expect about every character met after it."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"How does Steinbeck present loneliness in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"Loneliness is presented as structural: produced by itinerant ranch work, by segregation, by the position of women and by age, with George and Lennie's friendship as the exception that measures it and the ending that removes it.","sol":"Steinbeck presents loneliness not as a mood but as the normal condition of ranch life, and he builds the novel so that each character suffers a version of it produced by his or her place in 1930s society. George states the case early: guys like them, who work on ranches, are the loneliest guys in the world. Because the line names a class rather than a person, everything that follows reads as evidence for it. The itinerant hands of Depression California were hired by the month, moved on, and owned nothing but what they carried; Steinbeck's bunkhouse is full of men whose only relief is a Saturday night in town on which the month's pay disappears.\n\nCrooks shows the same loneliness sharpened by race. Segregated into the harness room, he tells Lennie that since he is not wanted in the bunkhouse, Lennie is not wanted in his room, and he keeps a battered law book to remind himself of rights nobody will enforce. His remark that a guy goes nuts if he ain't got nobody is the plainest statement of the novel's subject, and Steinbeck puts it in the mouth of the man his society has isolated most completely. Curley's wife suffers the version available to a woman: married onto a ranch with no work, no wage and no company, never given a name, she drifts from doorway to doorway looking for conversation and admits that she gets awful lonely. The men read her need as danger, which is precisely the expectation of the period that Steinbeck is examining. Candy's loneliness is the loneliness of age and usefulness: once his dog is shot he has nothing living that belongs to him.\n\nAgainst all this, George and Lennie's companionship is presented as remarkable, and the ranch treats it as such; Slim observes that it is unusual for men to travel around together. Yet Steinbeck takes it away himself. George shoots Lennie, Candy is left with his savings and no farm, and Crooks has already withdrawn his name from the plan.\n\nSteinbeck's judgement is therefore social rather than sentimental. Loneliness in Of Mice and Men is manufactured by an economy that moves men about singly and by attitudes that shut out the black, the female, the old and the disabled; friendship is shown as the only thing capable of answering it, and as the first thing such a world destroys.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Begin from the idea that the loneliness on this ranch has causes outside the characters themselves.","Group the lonely figures by what excludes each of them, and give one paragraph to each cause.","Decide what the ending does to the one relationship that resists it, and let that decide your final judgement."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"In what ways does Steinbeck present the dream of owning land in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"As a recited ritual that binds George and Lennie, as recruitment that briefly reaches Candy and Crooks, as a test of who is allowed to hope, and finally as anaesthetic; Depression economics make it unreachable.","sol":"Steinbeck presents the dream of the little place as a story before it is ever a plan: George recites it, Lennie demands it, and it closes always on the rabbits and on living off the fatta the lan'. That it is a ritual matters, because its first function is companionship rather than property. The two men own nothing, but they own something to say to one another, which is exactly what the other hands lack.\n\nThe dream becomes briefly credible when Candy offers the compensation money he was given after losing his hand, and the three of them find they are only a few hundred dollars short of a real farm. Steinbeck times this to the chapter in which Candy's dog is shot, so the farm arrives as an answer to the fear of being thrown away once you can no longer work. Crooks, who has watched men carry land in their heads all his life and seen none of them get it, dismisses the plan as a fairy tale and then, for one moment, asks to come too, before Curley's wife reminds him what he is and he takes it back. Steinbeck thus lets the dream spread exactly as far as the ranch's hierarchy permits, and no further.\n\nThe context makes the outcome inevitable, and Steinbeck, who had worked on such ranches in the Salinas Valley himself, does not disguise it: wages were monthly and small, work was seasonal, and land was held by families who had owned it for generations. By the 1930s the American Dream of a self-sufficient smallholding had become, for men like these, a story rather than a possibility, and the title borrowed from Burns, whose mouse's careful plans are destroyed by a plough that means it no harm, announces that from the first page.\n\nIn what ways, then? As ritual, as recruitment, as a test of who may hope, and at the last as anaesthetic: in the final chapter George tells the story once more, to steady Lennie, while he raises the Luger. The dream is presented sympathetically and without illusion. Steinbeck honours the need for it and denies its possibility, and that double movement is his argument about his country.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Notice how often the farm is described aloud rather than worked towards.","Trace who is allowed to join the dream and who is pushed back out of it.","Set the economics of Depression ranch work against what the men believe is only a month away."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"How does Steinbeck present Curley's wife in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"First through the men's words as a danger, then in her own voice as a lonely woman with a dead ambition, then in death as young and harmless; her namelessness and confinement reflect the position of women in the 1930s, though her threat to Crooks refuses simple victimhood.","sol":"Steinbeck introduces Curley's wife before she appears, and he does it in the men's voices: Candy tells George that she got the eye and that Curley has married a tart, and in the second chapter, once she has gone from the bunkhouse doorway, George shakes Lennie and calls her a piece of jail bait he must keep away from. The reader therefore meets a reputation rather than a person, which is itself the point, because the ranch has no category for a young woman except trouble; and the phrase that fixes her comes from George, so the man who builds the reputation is the same man who issues the warning. The heavy make-up and the red mules are noticed before a word of hers is heard. She is never given a name at all: belonging to Curley is her whole identity in the novel, and that is a social fact of the 1930s as much as an authorial choice, since a woman married onto a ranch had no work, no wage and no company of her own.\n\nSteinbeck then lets her speak. In the barn she tells Lennie that she never gets to talk to anybody and that she gets awful lonely, and she describes the man who told her he would put her in the movies and the letter she believes her mother took. Her ambition is as unreachable as George and Lennie's farm and fails for the same kind of reason, but Steinbeck gives her no partner at all, so she has no one to tell it to except a man who is not listening. He also refuses to make her merely a victim: she uses the one power her race gives her when she threatens Crooks with what she could have done to him, and the cruelty of that moment measures how little else she has ever been given.\n\nHer death completes the presentation. Steinbeck stops the narrative to tell the reader that she was very pretty and simple, that her face was sweet and young, and that the meanness and the ache for attention had gone out of it. Only when she is dead is she allowed to be seen as she is, and Candy's furious accusation over her body shows that the men will not allow even that for long.\n\nSteinbeck therefore presents Curley's wife as the novel's clearest case of a person turned into a problem by her position. A reader in the twenty-first century, less willing than a reader in 1937 to take the men's account of her on trust, is likely to find her the character the ranch treats most unjustly, and Steinbeck's own corrections show he expected that reading.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Notice who describes her before she is allowed to speak for herself.","Track how the presentation changes across the barn conversation and again after her death.","Ask what a woman in her position in the 1930s could actually have done differently."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"In what ways does Steinbeck present Crooks in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"As the ranch's permanently excluded man: his bunk in the harness room, proud and bookish, cruel to Lennie because cruelty is the only power he is given, briefly admitted to the dream and then silenced by Curley's wife's threat; segregation by custom in 1930s California explains every detail.","sol":"Steinbeck presents Crooks as the character on whom the arrangements of the ranch can be read most plainly, because what is temporary for the other men is permanent for him. He is the stable buck, kept with the horses: his bunk is in the harness room, a shed leaning off the wall of the barn, among the harness, the tools and the medicine bottles. Steinbeck makes privacy and exclusion the same thing, since the only reason Crooks has a door of his own is that the white hands will not have him behind theirs, and he is let into the bunkhouse only at Christmas, when there is drink and a fight.\n\nSteinbeck then shows what permanent exclusion does to a man. When Lennie comes to the door Crooks turns him away, telling him that because he is not wanted in the bunkhouse Lennie is not wanted in his room; then, once Lennie stays, he cannot stop talking. He taunts Lennie with the idea that George will not come back and watches the fear he has produced, which is cruelty practised on the only person on the ranch weaker than himself and the single kind of power he has ever been handed. His observation that a guy goes nuts if he ain't got nobody is the plainest statement of the novel's subject, and Steinbeck gives it to the man his society has isolated most completely.\n\nThe context does the explaining rather than decorating it. Segregation in 1930s California worked by custom where the South worked by law, so no rule has to be quoted for Crooks to be shut out; the men simply do it, and they are themselves poor, landless and hired by the month. The battered copy of the California civil code he keeps among his things sharpens the point: he owns the letter of his rights and has no means whatever of enforcing them, which is proved within minutes when Curley's wife, the least powerful white person on the ranch, reminds him what she could have done to him and he goes quiet and reduces himself to nothing.\n\nThe dream measures him too. Crooks has watched men carry land in their heads all his life and has seen none of them get any, yet for one moment in his own room he asks whether there would be room for him and offers to work for nothing, before the threat makes him take it back. Steinbeck therefore presents Crooks in a way that indicts an arrangement rather than a character: the pride, the reading and the cruelty are all responses to a position he did not choose, and the reader is left judging the ranch and the decade that built it.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Begin from where Steinbeck makes him sleep, and ask what that arrangement is for.","Follow what happens in his room when Lennie, then Candy, then Curley's wife come in, and what each visit costs him.","Let the segregation of the period explain the details you have chosen instead of sitting beside them."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"In what ways does Steinbeck present strength and weakness on the ranch in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"Physical strength is shown to be powerless in Lennie, while real power comes from position (Curley, the boss) or earned authority (Slim); weakness means uselessness, and the Depression's surplus of labour is what makes usefulness the measure.","sol":"Steinbeck separates strength from power, and the gap between them is where the novel does its thinking. Lennie is the strongest man on the ranch and the least able to protect himself: he crushes Curley's hand without meaning to and is terrified while he is doing it, and the hands that can buck barley all day and put up more grain alone than most pairs can kill a puppy by stroking it. Curley, a small former lightweight boxer, has power without strength, and Steinbeck makes the reason explicit: he is the boss's son, so the men have to take what he gives them. Slim is the single figure whose authority is earned rather than inherited, and Steinbeck shows it in the way the bunkhouse falls quiet when he speaks and in his ruling that Curley will tell everyone his hand was caught in a machine.\n\nWeakness on this ranch is not physical either; it is uselessness. Candy is a swamper with one hand, kept on after an accident and certain he will be let go when he can no longer push a broom, and his dog is shot, over his objection, on the grounds that it can do nothing for him any more. Crooks's weakness is his race and his damaged back, Curley's wife's is her sex and her marriage, and each of them is excluded from a different room with no one to appeal to.\n\nThe context explains the standard being applied. In Depression California there were far more men than jobs, so a worker's value was exactly what he could do that week; a man who could not work was surplus, and the ranch disposed of its surplus as the economy around it did. Carlson's Luger, used first on the dog and then, in George's hand, on Lennie, is the instrument of that logic twice over.\n\nSteinbeck therefore presents strength as very nearly irrelevant and power as a matter of position, and he asks the reader to notice the sharpest irony of all: Carlson, who argues the dog away and whose Luger performs both killings, is himself a hired hand who will one day be as old and as surplus as Candy, while Curley is exempt from the rule only because the ranch belongs to his father.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the man with the most muscle from the men with the most say.","Ask what each excluded character is actually being judged on.","Bring in the labour market of the decade to explain why usefulness decides everything."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"'Steinbeck shows a world in which the weak are always destroyed.' To what extent do you agree? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"Largely agree on the plot - the dog, Curley's wife, Lennie and the farm all go - but qualify 'always' and the implied agent: the destroying is done by a particular society, and Slim, Candy and George's last act allow real solidarity.","sol":"The proposition is very nearly true of the plot and not quite true of the novel. Everything weak in Of Mice and Men is removed: Candy's dog is shot because it is old and useless, Curley's wife is killed in the barn, Lennie is shot by the one person who loved him, and the farm that would have sheltered all three men is abandoned in the same conversation. Even the mice of the first chapter are dead before the book begins, and the title, taken from Burns's poem about a mouse whose home is destroyed by a plough that means it no harm, announces the pattern before a word of the story is told.\n\nWhere the proposition needs qualifying is in the word 'always' and in the agent it hides. Steinbeck does not present destruction as natural or inevitable; it is administered by people for reasons the novel names. Carlson wants the smell out of the bunkhouse, Curley wants a target, the boss wants labour that works. In Depression California, with more men than work, a human being's value had shrunk to what he could still do, and Steinbeck is arguing about that arrangement rather than about the human condition. A different arrangement would have left Candy his dog and Lennie a life.\n\nThe novel also allows real, if small, resistance. Slim uses his authority to protect Lennie from Curley and to steady George afterwards. Candy's savings very nearly buy the farm. Crooks, for one moment in his own room, asks to come. And George's last act, however it is judged, is protection rather than abandonment: he spares Lennie the death Curley intends and tells him the story one more time while he does it.\n\nI agree, then, to a considerable extent, but with the emphasis moved. The weak are destroyed in this novel because a particular society had no use for them, and Steinbeck makes that destruction so complete in order to make the arrangement, rather than the weakness, look intolerable.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Test the proposition against every figure the novel disposes of before you agree with it.","Ask who or what does the destroying, because that decides how far you can agree.","Find the moments of protection or solidarity that force you to qualify the claim."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"How might a reader today respond differently to Steinbeck's presentation of Curley's wife from a reader in 1937, and to what extent does the novel itself invite that difference? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"A modern reader reads her as trapped by the position of women rather than as a temptress; Steinbeck's ordering - reputation first, her own voice second, sympathy at her death - invites the shift, though her namelessness, her threat to Crooks and her role in the plot qualify it.","sol":"The specification asks for understanding of the way texts are received by different audiences at different times, and Curley's wife is the clearest case in this novel. A reader in 1937, meeting her first through George's warning in the bunkhouse that she is a piece of jail bait to be kept away from, would have found the men's judgement unremarkable: she is a newly married woman who will not stay in the house, and the plot appears to confirm the men's fear when the hair she offers Lennie to stroke destroys them both. A reader in the twenty-first century, trained to ask what a character is allowed to want, is far likelier to see a young woman with no name, no work, no money and no company, married to a man she does not like on a ranch she cannot leave.\n\nThe novel does invite the change, and this is what stops the answer being merely about modern attitudes. Steinbeck orders his material so that the reputation comes first and the person second: only in the barn is she allowed to explain that she never gets to talk to anybody, that she gets awful lonely, and that the offer of the movies never reached her. He then tells the reader over her body that she was very pretty and simple and that her face was sweet and young, stripping away the meanness the men had seen in her. That is an authorial correction, not a modern reinterpretation.\n\nIt must be qualified, though. Steinbeck also gives her the threat against Crooks, keeps her nameless from first page to last, and builds the plot so that she is the occasion of the catastrophe; a reader who makes her purely a victim has to ignore all three. The sympathy, moreover, is delivered almost entirely after she is dead, which a modern reader may well judge a limited kind of justice.\n\nTo a considerable extent, then, the difference is invited rather than imposed: Steinbeck built the correction into the novel. What has changed is that a reader today no longer needs the correction to be supplied.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the specification's category about audiences at different times.","Look closely at the order in which Steinbeck lets the reader learn things about her.","Find the details that stop you making her purely a victim, and let them qualify your answer."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"To what extent does Steinbeck present the American Dream as an illusion in Of Mice and Men? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"An illusion in its object - nobody gets land, and Crooks says he has watched hundreds fail - but real and necessary in its function while it lasts; the failure is located in Depression economics, not in the dreamers.","sol":"Judged by results, the dream is an illusion, and Steinbeck says so through the character best placed to know. Crooks, who has watched men arrive with land in their heads all his life and seen none of them get any, tells Lennie that nobody ever gets to heaven and nobody gets any land, and the novel proves him right: George and Lennie's farm dies with Lennie, Candy's savings buy nothing, and Curley's wife's future in the movies never existed outside a stranger's promise. The Depression is the reason and Steinbeck does not hide it: monthly wages, seasonal work, land long since owned, and in the background whole families moving west to discover that California had no room for them either.\n\nBut illusion is too dismissive of what the dream is shown doing. For most of the novel it is the one thing keeping two men out of the life the others lead, in which the month's pay goes in a night and nobody has anyone to tell it to. The recitation of the little place is a ritual of belonging, and when Candy joins it the effect is immediate: an old man with one hand suddenly has a future that is not the county road. Even Crooks, who has just called the plan a fairy tale, asks whether there is room for him. Steinbeck presents the dream as false in its object and true in its function.\n\nThe ending holds both truths at once. George tells the story one last time, to steady Lennie, and shoots him while he is looking across the river at it. The dream is used, at the last, as a kindness, because it has no other power left.\n\nI agree to a large extent, therefore, but with Steinbeck's own emphasis: the American Dream is shown as an illusion because the country of the 1930s had made it one, and the novel's grief is for the men who needed it and were not wrong to.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Find the character who has watched the most men fail and let him open the case against the dream.","Weigh what the dream achieves while it is alive against what it finally delivers.","Decide whether the failure belongs to the dreamers or to the decade, and answer accordingly."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"'Of Mice and Men is a novel about 1930s America rather than about two men.' To what extent do you agree? (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","a":"Agree substantially, since every private misfortune in the novel is produced by the decade's economics and attitudes, but qualify the wording: the two men are Steinbeck's method, and the friendship and the killing carry weight a social argument alone could not.","sol":"There is a great deal in the proposition, and the design of the novel supports it. Almost nothing that happens on this ranch happens only to the person it happens to. George and Lennie arrive on foot carrying their bedding because itinerant hands in Depression California were hired by the month and moved on. Candy expects to be let go once he can no longer push a broom because a decade with far more men than jobs valued a worker at what he could still do that week. Crooks sleeps with the horses because segregation in California was enforced by custom. Curley's wife has no work, no money and no company because marriage was the whole of the career available to her. Change the decade and the book has no plot.\n\nSteinbeck reinforces the reading by refusing to make anyone exceptional. The ranch is anonymous, a few miles south of Soledad; Curley's wife is never given a name at all; several of the men are known by their work, the swamper, the stable buck, the jerkline skinner. The title, taken from Burns's poem about a mouse whose winter home is destroyed by a plough that means it no harm, announces before the story begins that the schemes of ordinary creatures are broken by forces indifferent to them.\n\nBut the proposition understates what the two men are for. Steinbeck does not write an essay about the Depression; he writes six chapters in which one man keeps another man's work card and recites a story about a smallholding and rabbits, and the whole argument is carried by that. The recitation is exactly what the other hands have not got, which is why the reader feels the loss of a farm that never existed. When George shoots Lennie at the pool the novel is not making an economic point; it is asking what love can still do when there is nothing left for it to do, and no amount of social history answers that question.\n\nI agree, then, to a considerable extent, but with the emphasis moved from what the novel is about to how it works. Steinbeck's subject is the America of the 1930s, and his method is two men, because a reader who has been made to want the farm for George and Lennie will not afterwards accept the arrangement that made it impossible. In this novel the particular is how the general gets in.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Take each character's misfortune in turn and ask whether it could have happened in a different decade.","Notice how much of the novel declines to name its people and places, and what that does to the argument.","Then ask what the friendship and the last chapter achieve that a social argument on its own could not, and let that qualify your agreement."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section C essay on Of Mice and Men carry, and how are they split?","a":"40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4, with no AO2 credit."},{"q":"Is Component 1 open book or closed book?","a":"Closed book; only the anthology poems are provided, never the novel."},{"q":"Where must context appear if it is to reach the top of the AO4 range?","a":"Integrated into the argument, explaining a detail of the text, rather than in a separate block of history."},{"q":"From which poem and poet does Steinbeck take the title Of Mice and Men?","a":"Robert Burns's poem 'To a Mouse' (1785)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Section C – Modern Prose: the question and its marks","back":"One 40-mark essay from a choice of two on each set text, closed book. Twenty marks AO1, twenty AO4, no AO2. Questions 4 to 17 of a 90-mark, two-hour Component 1.","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in Section C","back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. Worth 20 of the 40 marks, and recalled from memory.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO4 in Section C","back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Worth 20 of the 40 marks, exactly the same share as AO1.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The five categories of context in the specification","back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Soledad","back":"The California town the novel opens a few miles south of, by the Salinas River. The name means solitude in Spanish, which suits a book about isolated men.","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"Crooks","back":"The black stable buck, segregated from the bunkhouse. His bunk is in the harness room, a shed leaning off the barn wall; his back is bent by a horse's kick and he keeps a battered California civil code.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"Candy","back":"The old swamper who lost a hand in a ranch accident, owner of the ancient dog Carlson shoots, and the savings that almost buy the farm. He expects to be let go when useless.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Curley's wife","back":"Never named. Married to the boss's son, with no work and no company, she says she gets awful lonely, threatens Crooks, and is killed by Lennie in the barn.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Slim","back":"The jerkline skinner whose word settles the ranch: he approves the shooting of Candy's dog, makes Curley lie about his crushed hand, and steadies George at the end.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Lennie's line \"An' live off the fatta the lan'\"","back":"His part of the recited dream of the small farm. The dialect and the ritual repetition show the dream works first as a story shared between friends, long before it is a plan.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The source of the title Of Mice and Men","back":"Robert Burns's poem 'To a Mouse' (1785): 'The best laid schemes o' Mice an' Men / Gang aft agley.' It announces that careful plans will come to nothing.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"The play-novelette form","back":"Six chapters, each in a single place, built from dialogue with description that works like a stage direction. Steinbeck wrote it to be performed, and it was staged in 1937, the year of publication.","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"Weed","back":"The town where, before the novel opens, Lennie held on to a girl's red dress and was accused of attacking her, so that he and George had to hide and run. George reports it to Slim.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"One example of Depression context an examiner rewards on Of Mice and Men","back":"The Wall Street Crash of 1929 and mass unemployment; itinerant ranch hands hired by the month with no home of their own; farming families driven west to California for seasonal work.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":true},{"front":"One way to integrate context rather than bolt it on","back":"Let the history explain a detail you have quoted; place it inside the paragraph's analysis rather than at the end; test it by deleting it and seeing whether the argument still stands.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: How does Steinbeck present loneliness in Of Mice and Men? (Section C, 40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","Point: Steinbeck makes loneliness a condition of employment rather than a flaw in a personality, and he uses Crooks to show what happens when that condition is made permanent by race.","Evidence: Crooks has his bunk in the harness room itself, a shed leaning off the wall of the barn, among the harness, the tools and the medicine bottles, and he tells Lennie that because he is not wanted in the bunkhouse, Lennie is not wanted in his room.","Analysis: Steinbeck gives him the one door on the ranch and then empties the room behind it, so that privacy and isolation become the same thing; the battered law book he keeps there completes the effect, since a man who has to own a copy of his rights is a man with nobody to enforce them for him.","Context, integrated: the arrangement is not Crooks's choice but the everyday segregation of 1930s California, where custom did the work the South's laws did, and it is enforced by white hands who are themselves poor and landless, which is why Curley's wife, the least powerful white person on the ranch, can still silence him with a threat.","Judgement: Crooks is therefore the measure of the novel's argument, because the loneliness the other men complain about between jobs is for him structural and permanent, and Steinbeck uses him to show that the ranch does not merely fail to prevent isolation but manufactures it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: How does Steinbeck present loneliness in Of Mice and Men? (Section C, 40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4)","Point: Crooks is a lonely character in the novel.","Evidence: He is not allowed in the bunkhouse and has to live on his own in a room near the stable.","Analysis: This makes him unhappy, and he is quite mean to Lennie when he says George might not come back, which shows how lonely he is.","Context, bolted on: In the 1930s there was a lot of racism in America and black people were segregated, which was very unfair.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 5: on this board's grid Level 2 is 'some knowledge and understanding' of a text that is 'largely narrative', and that is what this is. The point states a fact instead of arguing one, the analysis reports Crooks's behaviour instead of showing what Steinbeck does with it, not one word of the text is used, and the context is a detachable sentence of history that could be deleted without changing anything. To reach Level 3 the same material would need relevant personal engagement, an appropriate critical style and clearly relevant examples: a remembered detail of the harness room quoted or precisely described, and a reading argued rather than reported. Level 5 then adds a perceptive critical style, discriminating use of relevant examples and context integrated convincingly into the response."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Of Mice and Men: characters and the plot in order","items":["George Milton: small, quick and sharp-featured, the responsible one, who keeps Lennie's work card and recites the story of the farm.","Lennie Small: huge and immensely strong, with the mind of a child and a compulsion to stroke soft things; Steinbeck introduces him dragging his feet the way a bear drags his paws.","Candy: the old one-handed swamper, kept on after a ranch accident, owner of the ancient dog and of the savings that almost buy the farm.","Crooks: the black stable buck, segregated into the harness room, his back bent by a horse's kick.","Curley: the boss's son, a small former lightweight boxer who picks fights with big men; Lennie crushes his hand.","Curley's wife: unnamed throughout, newly married, heavily made up, wandering the ranch for someone to talk to; killed in the barn.","Slim: the jerkline skinner whose authority on the ranch is complete and unofficial; he gives Lennie a puppy and tells George at the end that he had to do it.","Carlson: the ranch hand who insists on shooting Candy's dog, and whose Luger George later uses on Lennie.","Chapter 1: by a pool on the Salinas River a few miles south of Soledad; George and Lennie camp, Lennie has a dead mouse, the farm is described, and George names the pool as the place to hide if there is trouble.","Chapter 2: the bunkhouse; the boss questions them, Curley and then Curley's wife appear, Slim and Carlson are introduced, and Slim's dog has had a litter of puppies.","Chapter 3: the bunkhouse in the evening; George tells Slim what happened at Weed, Carlson shoots Candy's dog, Candy joins the farm plan with his savings, and Lennie crushes Curley's hand.","Chapter 4: Crooks's room on a Saturday night; Lennie, then Candy, then Curley's wife come in, the farm briefly includes Crooks, and her threat drives him back out of it.","Chapter 5: the barn on Sunday afternoon; Lennie has killed his puppy, Curley's wife talks to him about the movies, he breaks her neck, Candy and George find her, and Curley sets out with the men to kill him.","Chapter 6: back at the pool of the first chapter; Lennie sees Aunt Clara and a giant rabbit, George tells the story of the farm one more time and shoots him with Carlson's Luger, Slim leads George away, and Carlson asks what is eating those two."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Of Mice and Men: themes, form and structure","items":["Loneliness and companionship: the novel's central argument, stated by George in the first chapter and then tested against Candy, Crooks and Curley's wife.","The dream of land: recited as a ritual, nearly funded by Candy, briefly joined by Crooks, and destroyed in the barn.","Power, usefulness and disposal: the dog, Candy, Crooks and Lennie are all judged by what they can still do.","Prejudice: race in Crooks, sex in Curley's wife, age and injury in Candy, and intellectual disability in Lennie.","Form: a play-novelette in six chapters, each confined to one place and built from dialogue, with description that reads like a stage direction, so it can be performed as written.","Structure: circular, beginning and ending at the same pool on the Salinas River, so the refuge named in the first chapter becomes the place of the killing in the last.","Foreshadowing: the dead mouse, the shooting of Candy's dog with Carlson's Luger, the dead puppy and the history of Weed each prepare the ending.","Title: from Robert Burns's 'To a Mouse' (1785), where the best laid schemes of mice and men gang aft agley."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Of Mice and Men: the context an examiner rewards (AO4)","items":["Published in 1937, during the Great Depression that followed the Wall Street Crash of 1929.","Set in the Salinas Valley of California, where Steinbeck was born in 1902 and where he worked on ranches as a young man, so the detail of ranch life is first-hand.","Itinerant ranch hands were hired by the month, carried their bedding from place to place and had no home; this is the life George describes as the loneliest in the world.","The Dust Bowl and the collapse of farming drove families west to California in the 1930s to look for seasonal work; Steinbeck wrote about them again in The Grapes of Wrath (1939).","Racial segregation: black workers in 1930s California were kept out of white living quarters by custom, which is why Crooks sleeps beside the stable and is admitted to the bunkhouse only at Christmas.","The position of women: a young woman married onto a ranch had no paid work, no money of her own and no company, which is the whole of Curley's wife's situation.","Attitudes to disability: in 1930s America people with learning disabilities had little legal protection and were commonly shut away in institutions, which sharpens the question of what else George could have done for Lennie.","The American Dream of owning a smallholding, put out of reach of wage labourers by the Depression.","Literary context: a short novel written to be staged, and produced as a play in the year it was published; Steinbeck went on to win the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1962.","Reception: the novel is read differently today, with more sympathy for Curley's wife and more attention to the language used about Crooks, and it has often been challenged in American schools."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the set text, its edition and the closed book rule","items":["Of Mice and Men by John Steinbeck is the modern prose set text chosen for Component 1, from a list of seven; learners study it alongside all the poems in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology.","Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into the examination: the anthology poems are provided in the paper, the novel is not.","Because the examination is closed book, the specification sets no prescribed editions of the set texts, so any edition may be used for study.","The edition the specification recommends for Of Mice and Men is the Penguin Red Classics New Ed Edition, January 2006, ISBN 9780141023571.","What follows for the exam: page references and edition-specific details earn nothing, and everything you use must be carried in memory — short quotations you are certain of, and precisely described moments where you are not."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How does Steinbeck present Curley's wife in Of Mice and Men? (Section C, 40 marks)","items":["Decoded question: 'How does Steinbeck present' asks for the writer's handling of her, so every paragraph must name a choice Steinbeck makes; the 40 marks split 20 AO1 and 20 AO4, so every paragraph needs a remembered example and a context that explains it; the paper is closed book, so quote only what you are certain of and describe the rest.","Thesis for the opening five lines: Steinbeck presents her first as the men see her and then as she is, so that the reader is made to correct a judgement the ranch itself never corrects.","Paragraph 1 - the reputation before the person: Candy's remark that she got the eye and that Curley has married a tart, George's warning in the second chapter that she is a piece of jail bait to be kept away from, the heavy make-up and the red mules all reach the reader before she speaks; note that the warning and the phrase both come from George. Context: the ranch has no category for a young woman except danger, and none of these men is used to living beside one.","Paragraph 2 - namelessness and ownership: she is Curley's wife from the first page to the last, defined by a husband she does not like. Context: marriage was the career available to her, and on a ranch it came with neither work nor wages nor company.","Paragraph 3 - her own voice in the barn: she never gets to talk to anybody, she gets awful lonely, and the promise of the movies never reached her. Context: her dream fails as George and Lennie's does, but she has no partner to share it with, which is the sharper deprivation.","Paragraph 4 - the limits of sympathy: her threat to Crooks shows her using the one power her race gives her, and the plot makes her the occasion of the catastrophe. Context: powerlessness does not make a person harmless, and Steinbeck refuses the simple victim.","Evaluation thread to run through all four paragraphs: how far does Steinbeck endorse the men's view of her, and how far does he correct it? Return to that question in every paragraph so the essay argues rather than describes.","The conclusion must say: Steinbeck presents her as a person turned into a problem by her position, corrects the men's account of her only once she is dead, and leaves a reader today readier to make that correction than a reader in 1937 would have been. It must judge, not summarise."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T18:02:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Judged independently and then against the first examiner's list: every fault it named is gone — 'jail bait' is George's in all three places (questions[7].sol, questions[11].sol, extras[6]) with an explicit note to that effect, the loneliest-guys line is placed at the pool in Chapters 1 and 6 with the different Chapter 3 remark to Slim distinguished in questions[4].sol, Crooks is now in the harness room itself, 'a shed leaning off the wall of the barn', consistently in all eight places, examples[2] now makes and commits to the choice its setup asks for, the weaker model paragraph is honestly relabelled Level 2 with a justification tied to this board's grid, questions[9] no longer calls Curley a hired hand and now has Lennie bucking barley rather than pitching bales, the invented 10-mark tariffs are gone (the five d=1 tasks carry 3-4 marks and each says in its stem that the tariff is for practice, which is an honest fix), and the specification's second case study is covered by the new extras[5] on the closed-book rule, the absence of prescribed editions and the recommended Penguin Red Classics edition. I re-checked all four quoted lines (nine occurrences) and roughly seventy unquoted attributions, chapter placements, character details and context claims — including the six-chapter map, Candy's savings, the Christmas admission, Crooks reducing himself to nothing, Carlson's Luger doing both killings, the Burns title, Steinbeck's 1902 Salinas birth, 1937 publication and staging, The Grapes of Wrath 1939 and the 1962 Nobel — and found no misquotation, invented line or wrong attribution; what remains is a chronological slip about Chapter 2 repeated twice, one over-long quotation and two vaguenesses, all fixable without touching the argument."}}},"prose-whale-rider":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-whale-rider","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section C of Component 1 is the only place The Whale Rider is examined: one 40-mark essay, chosen from two, inside a two-hour, 90-mark paper, with no copy of the novel in front of you. Twenty of those marks are AO1 and twenty are AO4, and Section C carries no AO2 marks at all, so a beautiful piece of device-spotting scores nothing on its own, while a moment recalled accurately and used as evidence, and a context that explains why the novel is written as it is, score everything. This room builds the two halves together: the people, the story and the key moments you must carry in your head because the examination is closed book, and the New Zealand of the 1980s that Witi Ihimaera wrote the novel into. It also drills the one habit that separates the top level from the middle — context argued inside the paragraph rather than parked in one of its own.","idea":[{"h":"One modern prose text, studied whole","code":"Component 1: Content overview","t":"The specification says learners study two set texts for Component 1: all the poems from Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one of the modern prose set texts. Here that text is The Whale Rider by Witi Ihimaera, and teaching “should focus on the study of whole texts”. Whole-text study matters in practice because the novel runs on two strands at once: Rawiri’s narration of his family at Whangara, and the italicised passages that follow the whale herd and its ancient leader. A question about Kahu, or about Koro Apirana, or about the sea, can only be answered well by someone who can move between both strands and across the whole arc, from the prologue’s account of the first whale rider to the stranding and what follows it. A top-level answer therefore ranges over the whole novel, choosing moments from the beginning, middle and end rather than circling three favourite scenes, and shows that the human story and the whale story are one argument."},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","t":"AO1 is twenty of the forty marks: “Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.” The specification spells out what learners must do: “demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text; maintain a critical style; present an informed personal engagement; identify and use relevant examples from their prose text.” Because the examination is closed book, those examples have to be held in memory, which changes how you revise: an exactly remembered moment — who did what, to whom, and when in the story — is safer and scores better than a half-remembered quotation, and a misquotation costs you the credit the reference was meant to earn. Critical style means writing about a novel that someone made, not about people who exist; personal engagement means a reading of your own, argued. A top-level answer reads as one continuous argument built on precise, varied references, not as a retelling with opinions attached."},{"h":"AO4: the novel and the moment it was written into","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","t":"AO4 is the other twenty marks, an equal share, so context is half of every Whale Rider question: “Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written”, and learners must “understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts”. The word that does the work is relationships. Credit comes from showing how knowing something about 1980s New Zealand changes what a moment in the novel means — why Koro Apirana’s school for the village boys is urgent rather than quaint, why Nanny Flowers’s claim of descent from the ancestress Muriwai counts as evidence rather than opinion, why a novel published in 1987 puts its argument about renewal inside a tradition rather than against it. A top-level answer never devotes a paragraph to history: every contextual sentence is fastened to a moment in the text and ends by saying what that moment now means."},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","t":"The specification lists the categories teaching should include: the writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, for example attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times. For this novel that means five different supplies of AO4: Ihimaera as a Māori novelist writing in New York and prompted by his daughters’ complaint that the hero was always a boy; Whangara and the East Coast, and the mid-century movement of Māori families into the cities; Māori custom, whakapapa and the argument over whether leadership may pass to a girl; myth retold as modern fiction; and a book now read worldwide, and filmed. A top-level answer draws on more than one category and picks the category that actually explains the moment in hand."},{"h":"What the question looks like, and what it does not ask for","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","t":"“Section C – Modern Prose: one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts. This section assesses AO1 and AO4.” In the breakdown of raw marks these are Questions 4 to 17, twenty marks AO1 and twenty AO4; the component is closed book and learners may not take texts in, although the anthology poems are provided. Component 1 lasts two hours for 90 marks, so Section C is worth roughly 53 minutes — about ten planning and the rest writing. Two consequences follow. First, read both options and choose the one you have four strong moments for, not the one with the attractive wording. Second, because there are no AO2 marks in this section, the instruction about language, form and structure that the specification prints on its Component 2 examples does not belong here: naming a technique earns nothing by itself. A top-level answer spends the time it saves on evidence and on context instead."}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a Section C question before you write","setup":"The question in front of you reads: In what ways does Ihimaera present Koro Apirana’s attitude to leadership in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. It is worth 40 marks, 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4, the examination is closed book, and about 53 minutes are available for the section. Task: work out what the question is actually asking for, and fix the shape of the answer, before writing a word.","steps":["Underline ‘in what ways’ and ‘present’: the question asks how Ihimaera builds Koro’s attitude for the reader, so a retelling of what Koro does cannot score, however accurate.","Turn the topic into one arguable line: Koro’s attitude is presented as a genuine devotion to descent that has hardened into a rule about gender, so that the novel can correct a custom from the inside rather than reject it.","Choose four remembered moments that each show a different face of that line: his refusal to come when Kahu’s birth cord is buried at Whangara; the school he sets up to teach the village boys the old knowledge; the carved stone he throws into the sea for them to bring back; his despair when not one of them can find it.","Attach a context to each moment so the AO4 marks are earned inside the argument: chiefly descent traced through the male line against Nanny Flowers’s descent from the ancestress Muriwai; the loss of te reo Māori in the decades before the novel, and the fight to revive it, behind Koro’s urgency in the schoolroom.","Fix the judgement the conclusion will reach, so every paragraph can point at it: Koro is presented as a faithful guardian whose mistake is one of reading, which is why the novel can restore him at the end instead of discarding him."],"cues":["Which two words in the stem tell you what kind of answer is wanted?","Before you list any evidence, what single claim about Koro could the whole essay argue?","Which remembered moments would show different faces of that claim?","Half the marks here are AO4 — where must the context sit if it is not to become a bolted-on paragraph?","What does the conclusion still have to do that the paragraphs will not have done?"]},{"title":"Turning a context fact into AO4 credit","setup":"You have revised this fact: te reo Māori had declined steeply by the 1980s, after generations in which Māori families moved into the cities and children were schooled in English; kōhanga reo language-nest preschools began in 1982 and the Māori Language Act made te reo an official language of New Zealand in 1987, the year The Whale Rider was published. The question is: How does Ihimaera present the importance of ancestors in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. Task: convert the fact into a sentence that earns AO4 inside the argument instead of sitting in a paragraph of its own.","steps":["Reject the bolted-on version first: a sentence that simply reports the Act and the preschools tells the examiner something about New Zealand and nothing about the novel, so it earns almost nothing.","Find the moment the fact actually explains: Koro Apirana’s school for the village boys, where he drills them in the language, the chants and the genealogies he is afraid will die with him.","Join them in one sentence that ends on the text: Koro’s near-desperation to hand the old language and the genealogies to a generation that does not want them reads, in a book published while te reo was being argued for in law and taught again to toddlers, as a portrait of an elder watching inheritance thin out in his own lifetime.","Test the direction of the link: the history must explain the writing, so if the sentence could be deleted and the point about Koro would survive unchanged, it has not yet been integrated."],"cues":["What is wrong with stating the fact and moving straight on?","Which episode in the novel does this particular fact illuminate?","How would you join fact and moment inside a single sentence?","How can you test whether the link is genuinely integrated rather than decorative?"]},{"title":"Choosing evidence you can hold in your memory","setup":"Closed book, you must support this point: Kahu’s right to lead is established by what she does, not by what anyone says about her. Three candidates are in your memory: (a) Kahu in tears after Koro Apirana turns away from her; (b) Kahu diving from the boat to bring up the carved stone that not one of Koro’s boys could find, and surfacing with a crayfish for her great-grandfather as well; (c) Rawiri’s years working in Sydney and then in Papua New Guinea. Task: choose the evidence that proves the point and say exactly how you would use it.","steps":["Measure each candidate against the wording of the point: (a) shows Kahu suffering exclusion rather than acting, and (c) belongs to Rawiri’s story and would need a paragraph of explaining before it reached Kahu at all.","Take (b): the dive is an action of Kahu’s own, performed in the very test Koro had designed for the boys, so it proves the point without any help.","Make the reference exact rather than approximate: say what Koro threw overboard, who was sent for it, who failed, what Kahu brought back, and the detail that she came up with the crayfish for him too.","Convert reference into argument: Kahu never contests the rule that the test was built on, she simply completes the test, so the novel establishes her fitness by performance and lets the reader reach the conclusion before Koro is able to."],"cues":["Which candidate does the least work for this exact point, and why?","Which one proves the point on its own?","Closed book, how do you keep a reference precise without risking a misquotation?","What does this evidence let you say about how Ihimaera makes his case?"]},{"title":"Rebuilding a middle-level paragraph","setup":"A student has written this for: In what ways does Ihimaera present Nanny Flowers in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. ‘Nanny Flowers is funny and she is always arguing with Koro Apirana and threatening to divorce him. She comes from a different ancestor to him. This shows that women were important in Māori culture as well.’ Task: rebuild the paragraph to the top level using the same three ingredients — her comedy, her quarrels and her descent.","steps":["Name the authorial choice the student only reported: Ihimaera gives the novel’s counter-argument to its funniest character, so the challenge to Koro arrives disarmingly and is never resented by the reader.","Make the descent precise and let it carry weight: Nanny Flowers claims descent from the ancestress Muriwai, so her objection is not modern opinion set against custom but one line of descent set against another.","Fasten the context at that point: in a culture where authority is argued through whakapapa, a claim of descent is evidence, which is why she can win exchanges that Koro has no authority simply to end.","Finish with the mini-judgement the student never reached: Nanny Flowers is less comic relief than the novel’s method of arguing, because through her the case for Kahu is made in Koro’s own terms long before Kahu proves it in the water."],"cues":["The student reports Nanny Flowers’s behaviour — what has been left unsaid about the writer?","Which detail of her descent turns a running joke into an argument?","Where exactly should the AO4 credit attach in this paragraph?","What is missing from the end of the paragraph?"]},{"title":"Writing the conclusion a ‘to what extent’ question demands","setup":"You have written three paragraphs on: The Whale Rider is a novel about change rather than tradition. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. Paragraph one argued that Koro’s rule about male succession is overturned. Paragraph two argued that Kahu’s authority is confirmed by the oldest thing in the book, the ancient whale itself. Paragraph three argued that Rawiri comes home from his years abroad valuing a culture he had taken for granted. Eight minutes remain. Task: write the conclusion the question requires.","steps":["Refuse both extremes the proposition offers: nothing in those paragraphs showed tradition replaced by change, and nothing showed tradition simply preserved intact.","State the position the three paragraphs jointly support: the novel presents change as the means by which tradition survives, since what breaks is the rule about gender while what it was protecting, the line back to the whale rider, is confirmed.","Fold the context in once more, briefly: written at the height of a Māori cultural revival, the novel argues for renewal from inside an inheritance rather than a break with it.","Close on the question’s own terms so the judgement cannot be missed: change, in this novel, is the form tradition takes when it is meant to last."],"cues":["What must a conclusion to a ‘to what extent’ question avoid doing?","What position do those three paragraphs actually support together?","Half the marks are AO4 — what should appear once more, and how briefly?","How should the final sentence be worded?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many of the 40 marks in Section C are AO4, and what does AO4 ask you to show?","a":"Twenty of the forty. AO4 asks you to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written."},{"q":"Why does identifying a simile in The Whale Rider earn you nothing by itself in Section C?","a":"Section C assesses only AO1 and AO4; there are no AO2 marks for language, form and structure here. The detail can still serve AO1 as a relevant example, but naming the device adds nothing."},{"q":"Where should context appear in a Section C answer?","a":"Inside the paragraphs, fastened to the moment it explains, so that it changes what the moment means. A separate history paragraph sits at the bottom of the AO4 range."},{"q":"What does closed book change about the way you revise your examples?","a":"You must carry the moments themselves — who did what, to whom, and where in the story — because a precise reference scores and a half-remembered quotation risks losing the credit it was meant to earn."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark paper worth 60% of the International GCSE, and it is closed book: no copy of The Whale Rider may be taken in, although the anthology poems are provided.","Section C is one 40-mark essay from a choice of two on each set text, Questions 4 to 17, marked 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4, with no AO2 marks in the section.","AO1: demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. AO4: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.","The five kinds of context the specification names: the writer’s life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","The Whale Rider was published in 1987 by Witi Ihimaera, who was the first Māori writer to publish a book of short stories and the first to publish a novel; he wrote it while working for New Zealand in New York.","The novel is set at Whangara, a real village on the East Coast of New Zealand’s North Island, whose meeting house carries a carved figure of the ancestor riding a whale.","Rawiri, Kahu’s uncle, narrates the human story; italicised passages between the chapters follow the whale herd and its ancient leader, and a prologue and an epilogue frame the whole novel.","At about 53 minutes pro rata, plan for roughly ten minutes of planning and forty of writing, with four developed paragraphs and a conclusion that answers the question."],"questions":[{"q":"State how the 40 marks for the Section C essay on The Whale Rider are divided between assessment objectives, and name what each of those objectives asks for. (4 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"20 AO1 and 20 AO4.","sol":"The essay carries 20 marks for AO1 and 20 for AO4. AO1 asks you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of the text, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, using relevant examples from the prose text. AO4 asks you to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. There are no AO2 marks in Section C, so analysis of language, form and structure is not credited here in its own right.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Section C assesses two objectives only, and they carry the same weight.","Recall the breakdown of raw marks for Questions 4 to 17, then set the wording of each objective beside its share.","Answer in two sentences: the split first, then one sentence saying what each objective demands."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Name the ancestor whose arrival at Whangara on the back of a whale opens the novel, and state the name Kahu is given at birth. (2 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"Kahutia Te Rangi, later known as Paikea; Kahu is named Kahutia Te Rangi after him.","sol":"The prologue tells of Kahutia Te Rangi, known afterwards as Paikea, who came from Hawaiki riding a whale and reached Whangara. Kahu is given that same name, Kahutia Te Rangi, at her birth, which is precisely what offends Koro Apirana: the name of the founding male ancestor has been given to a girl. The naming plants the novel’s argument in its first pages, because Kahu is claimed for the line before she has done anything to earn it.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The prologue and the child’s naming carry the same name for a reason.","Work from the moment Koro Apirana takes offence and ask what exactly it was that offended him.","Give the ancestor’s name, the name he is also known by, and then the child’s birth name."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Describe the test Koro Apirana sets his school of village boys at sea, and state what Kahu later does in connection with it. (3 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"He drops a carved stone into the sea and asks the boys to bring it up; none can. Kahu later dives and retrieves it, surfacing with a crayfish for him as well.","sol":"Koro Apirana takes the boys out in the boat and throws a carved stone overboard, telling them to bring it back; not one of them can find it, and he is left in despair about the next generation. Later Kahu dives for it herself and brings it up, returning with a crayfish for her great-grandfather too. The episode matters because Kahu passes, without argument, the very test that had been designed to exclude her.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The test is set for the boys, and its outcome is what drives Koro to despair.","Recall who goes into the water on each occasion, and what each of them comes back with.","Give the test first, then the failure, then what Kahu does."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"State three of the five kinds of context the specification says teaching should include for AO4. (3 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"Any three of: the writer’s own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","sol":"Three acceptable answers, from the five: the writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way in which texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, such as how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from when it was written.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The specification groups context into categories rather than listing facts.","Run through them by asking whose context each one is: the writer’s, the story’s, the society’s, literature’s, the reader’s.","Write them as a short list in the specification’s own terms."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Explain how the closed-book nature of Component 1 should change the way you prepare examples from The Whale Rider. (4 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"Examples must be carried in memory, so revise precise moments rather than long quotations, and never risk an approximate quotation.","sol":"No copy of the novel may be taken into the examination, so every example has to be recalled. That means revising moments in detail — who acted, on whom, and where in the story it falls — rather than trying to memorise long passages. A precisely referenced moment satisfies the requirement to identify and use relevant examples from the prose text just as well as a quotation, whereas a quotation recalled inaccurately undermines the very knowledge it was meant to display. It also means revising by theme, so that four moments can be produced for any of the likely questions.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The requirement is for relevant examples, not necessarily for quotations.","Think about what the examiner can actually verify in an answer written without the book.","Say what you would revise instead, and why that choice is safer under these conditions."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ihimaera present Koro Apirana’s attitude to leadership in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Koro is presented as a devoted guardian of descent whose reading of the tradition, not his faith in it, is at fault; the novel corrects him from inside the tradition rather than against it.","sol":"Ihimaera presents Koro Apirana’s attitude to leadership as devotion hardened into a rule. His conviction that authority must pass to a firstborn male is not vanity: he is the keeper of a line that runs back to the whale rider, and the novel shows him working at it — setting up a school for the village boys, teaching the language, the chants and the genealogies that his own generation is watching thin out. Read against the New Zealand of the novel’s making, where Māori families had moved into the cities in enormous numbers and te reo had declined so far that it was being fought for in law and taught again to toddlers in the year the novel appeared, Koro’s urgency is not quaintness but alarm. That is why his refusal to come when Kahu’s birth cord is buried at Whangara is presented as painful rather than merely cruel: he cannot see how the line survives through her. Ihimaera then dismantles the rule with the tradition’s own instruments. Nanny Flowers answers descent with descent, claiming the ancestress Muriwai, so the counter-argument is made in Koro’s own language rather than in the language of modern rights. The carved stone he throws into the sea is a test the boys fail and Kahu passes; the ancient whale, older than any rule of his, accepts her. Koro is left in despair before he is left corrected. The presentation is finally generous: because his error is one of reading and not of loyalty, the novel can restore him at the end rather than discard him, and his acceptance of Kahu is the sign that a tradition able to correct itself is a tradition that will last.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about an attitude, not a character sketch: find the single claim about that attitude which your whole answer can argue.","Trace the attitude through moments that show different faces of it — what he builds, what he refuses, what he tests — and let the context explain why he is in such a hurry.","Open with the position you will argue, then take one moment per paragraph, each closing on what it shows about how Ihimaera wants Koro to be understood."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"How does Ihimaera present the importance of ancestors in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Ancestors are presented as living authority rather than history: descent is the ground of the argument, and the ancient whale makes the inheritance physically present.","sol":"In The Whale Rider ancestors are not background. They are the evidence people argue with. The novel opens with Kahutia Te Rangi, remembered as Paikea, arriving at Whangara on a whale, and it hands that name to a newborn girl, so the inheritance is placed on her before she can do anything to deserve it. Koro Apirana and Nanny Flowers then conduct their whole quarrel in the language of descent: he traces the chiefly line through its men, she answers by claiming the ancestress Muriwai, and the reader watches whakapapa function as argument, not decoration. Ihimaera reinforces this by keeping a second narrative running in italics, in which the ancient bull whale remembers the man who once rode him; the ancestor is not only recalled by the village, he is still swimming. The context of writing sharpens all of this. The novel appeared in 1987, at the height of a Māori cultural revival, after decades in which families had moved to the cities and the language had fallen away; a book which insists that descent is present, usable and binding is doing cultural work as well as telling a story, and it is set in a real village whose meeting house carries the whale rider carved on it. The importance of ancestors is finally proved rather than asserted: Kahu is accepted not because she is told she belongs to the line but because the oldest member of it accepts her in the water. The novel therefore presents ancestry as something that must be answered to, and as something that can be answered by anyone the ancestors themselves acknowledge — which is exactly how a tradition renews itself instead of ending.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the ancestors actually do in the novel rather than what the characters say about them.","Use both strands — the family at Whangara and the italicised passages at sea — and let 1987 explain why the claim is pressed so hard.","Begin with the position that ancestry functions as authority in this novel, then take one demonstration of it per paragraph."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ihimaera present Nanny Flowers in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Nanny Flowers carries the novel’s counter-argument: comic and combative, she answers descent with descent and protects Kahu until Kahu can act for herself.","sol":"Ihimaera gives his novel’s argument to its funniest character. Nanny Flowers quarrels with Koro Apirana constantly and threatens to leave him at the smallest provocation, and the comedy is doing serious work: it lets the challenge to a chief be made repeatedly without the novel becoming a lecture. Her authority is not simply force of personality. She claims descent from the ancestress Muriwai, and in a culture where standing is argued through whakapapa that claim is evidence, which is why Koro cannot simply overrule her; the counter-argument is made in his own terms, from inside the tradition. Ihimaera also uses her as Kahu’s protector during the years when the child can do nothing for herself: it is Nanny Flowers who sees that the birth cord is buried at Whangara when Koro will not come, Nanny Flowers who keeps the child close, and Nanny Flowers who knows what Kahu has done in the water before Koro is ready to be told. Read in context, she embodies the argument the novel was written into: Ihimaera has said he wrote the book after his daughters asked why the hero was always a boy, and 1980s New Zealand was arguing about women’s place in public life as well as about Māori culture. The presentation is therefore double: she is the household’s comedy and the book’s case. By the end she has been proved right, and the fact that she is proved right by a child’s actions rather than by winning an argument is the novel’s point — her role was to keep the possibility alive until it could demonstrate itself.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Comedy is a method here, not a personality trait: ask what the laughter makes possible.","Pair each comic moment with the serious function it performs, and let the argument over descent supply the context.","State first what Nanny Flowers is for in the novel, then devote each paragraph to one of the jobs she does."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"How does Ihimaera portray the relationship between the people of Whangara and the sea in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"The sea is presented as kin and as origin rather than as setting: it brought the founding ancestor, it is the arena of every test, and its damage measures the damage done to the people.","sol":"Ihimaera portrays the sea as a relative rather than a landscape. The village exists because the sea carried Kahutia Te Rangi to it, and the novel keeps that origin in view by running a second, italicised narrative from the whales’ point of view, so that the reader is never allowed to treat the ocean as mere scenery. Every test of authority in the book happens in the water: Koro Apirana throws the carved stone overboard for his boys, Kahu goes down for it, and the final claim to leadership is settled far out at sea rather than in the meeting house. The relationship is also shown to be damaged. The strandings, which the villagers work desperately and largely vainly to reverse, are written as a wound to the people themselves, not as a natural curiosity; Rawiri, who has been away in Sydney and in Papua New Guinea, returns able to see both the damage and the value of what he had grown up taking for granted. The context of writing is inseparable from this. A novel published in 1987, at the height of a Māori cultural revival and in a decade of rising alarm about what was being done to the oceans, portrays a people whose standing with the sea is a measure of their health as a community. That is why the ending works: when the ancient whale accepts Kahu and returns her, the sea is not rescuing an individual, it is answering the village — and the relationship, which the novel has shown to be strained, is restored.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the sea is treated as in this novel, and the answer is not a setting.","Gather the moments that happen in or because of the water, then ask what the strandings are made to mean.","Argue a single description of the relationship in your first sentences, then let each paragraph test it against a different moment."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explain how Rawiri’s years away from Whangara contribute to the novel’s presentation of Māori culture. (12 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"His time in Sydney and Papua New Guinea shows him his own culture from outside and gives the reader a narrator who has chosen it rather than merely inherited it.","sol":"Rawiri leaves Whangara for Sydney and later goes to Papua New Guinea with his friend Jeff’s family, and what he meets there — including casual assumptions about people like himself — teaches him to see his own inheritance from the outside. Ihimaera gains two things. First, the narrator who tells us about Koro Apirana, Nanny Flowers and Kahu is not a man who never left: his admiration for what Whangara holds has been tested against the world and chosen, which makes the novel’s valuation of Māori culture argued rather than assumed. Second, his journey mirrors a real movement: in the decades before the novel the great majority of Māori had moved away from rural communities into the cities, and the question of what could be carried with them, and what would be lost, is exactly the question Koro is trying to answer in his schoolroom. Rawiri comes home having decided the inheritance is worth having, which is why he is the right person to narrate a story about someone claiming it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why the story is told by this particular narrator rather than by someone who never left.","Set his journey beside what was happening to Māori communities in the decades before the novel was written.","Say first what his absence gives the narration, then what his return allows the novel to argue."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"The Whale Rider is a novel about change rather than tradition. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Partly: the rule about male succession changes, but the novel presents change as the means by which the tradition survives, so the two cannot be opposed.","sol":"The proposition is half right, and the novel is built to show why. Change is undeniably at its centre: the rule that leadership passes to a firstborn male is broken, and it is broken by a girl who is claimed for the line at birth and proves herself in the water. Rawiri’s narration adds a second kind of change, the movement of a generation out to the cities and beyond, which the novel places against the real history of Māori urban migration in the decades before 1987. But to call the book a novel about change rather than tradition is to miss what Ihimaera does with the two words. Every instrument used to overturn Koro Apirana’s rule is itself traditional. Nanny Flowers answers his descent with her own, claiming the ancestress Muriwai. Kahu is authorised not by a modern argument about fairness but by the carved stone, the old knowledge she picks up by listening at the edge of Koro’s school, and finally by the ancient whale, which is the oldest authority the novel has. Context supports this reading: the book appeared at the height of a Māori cultural revival, in the year te reo became an official language of New Zealand, a movement whose whole logic was renewal from inside an inheritance rather than a break with the past. My judgement, therefore, is that the novel is about change only in the sense that it shows a tradition correcting an error in order to continue: what breaks is a rule about gender, what survives — confirmed, not weakened — is the line back to the whale rider.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["The proposition asks you to separate two things the novel deliberately entangles.","Give the strongest case for change first, then show which instruments actually achieve it and where they come from.","Your conclusion must state how far you agree in the question’s own terms rather than announcing a compromise."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"A reader in the twenty-first century is bound to read The Whale Rider differently from its first readers in 1987. To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Largely: reception has shifted the novel from a local intervention to a world text, and the film changed who meets the story first, though its argument about inheritance reads as it always did.","sol":"The specification asks us to consider the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, and few set texts reward the question better. To its first readers in 1987 the novel was an intervention in a live argument: te reo had only just become an official language of New Zealand, Māori communities were recovering ground after decades of migration into the cities, and a Māori novelist insisting that a girl could inherit a chiefly line was taking a position in two disputes at once. A reader now meets the book differently. Its argument about who may lead is far less startling, so the pressure of the novel shifts from the claim itself to the delicacy with which Ihimaera makes it — the fact that Koro Apirana is written with sympathy rather than contempt, and that the counter-argument is made in his own terms by Nanny Flowers, who claims descent from Muriwai. A reader now also arrives, very often, by way of the 2002 film, and may know the girl by a different name and the story without Rawiri’s narration at all, which quietly removes the perspective that tested Whangara against the world. Yet the difference can be overstated: the emotional centre — a child seeking the recognition of an elder who will not look at her, and being acknowledged at last by the oldest authority in the book — does not depend on 1987 at all. To a large extent, then, the novel is read differently, but what has changed is its temperature rather than its meaning: it has moved from argument to classic, and the reading it invites is now admiring where it was once contentious.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["The specification names reception by different audiences at different times as one of the kinds of context.","Ask what a reader in 1987 had at stake that a reader now does not, and what has since come between new readers and the book.","Concede what has genuinely not changed before you fix how far you agree."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"To what extent is The Whale Rider best understood as a retelling of the Paikea tradition rather than as a realistic novel about a modern family? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"It is both by design: the mythic frame and the realist family narrative are made to authenticate each other, so choosing between them misreads the form.","sol":"The question poses a choice the novel refuses to allow. The case for retelling is strong. It opens with Kahutia Te Rangi coming to Whangara on the back of a whale, it gives that name to a newborn girl, it runs a whole narrative in italics from the whales’ point of view, and it ends with a child riding a whale out to sea — an event no realist novel could accommodate. The case for the realist family novel is just as strong. Rawiri narrates in a wry, ordinary voice; the family bickers; a great-grandmother threatens divorce over breakfast; a young man goes to Sydney for work and comes back changed; a child cries because an old man will not come to her prize-giving. What Ihimaera builds is one form out of both, in the manner of much postcolonial fiction that re-tells inherited story in a contemporary setting, and the context explains the choice: a novel written during a Māori cultural revival, at a moment when ancestry was being reasserted as a living claim, needs its myth to be true in the present tense, and it is set in a real village whose meeting house carries the whale rider. The two registers therefore authenticate each other: the domestic detail makes the mythic ending credible, and the myth gives the domestic quarrel its stakes. To the extent that we must choose, the retelling is the deeper structure and the family novel is the means by which it is made believable — but the novel’s achievement lies precisely in refusing the separation the question proposes.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["The literary context is one of the kinds of context the specification names — ask what kind of book this is.","Build the best case for each side from real moments, then ask what each does for the other.","Decide, in the conclusion, not which side wins but what the combination achieves."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Take one piece of evidence — Kahu retrieving the carved stone that Koro Apirana’s boys could not find — and show how it would be used differently in an answer on Koro Apirana’s attitude to leadership and in an answer on the importance of ancestors. (10 marks — practice task, not an exam tariff)","a":"Same moment, different work: for Koro it is the failure of his test and the first crack in his rule; for ancestors it is the old knowledge recognising the child the line had already claimed.","sol":"For a question on Koro Apirana’s attitude to leadership, the stone is evidence about him: he designs a test that assumes the next leader is among the boys, the boys fail it, his despair is the measure of how much he has invested in the rule, and Kahu’s success is therefore an answer he did not ask for and cannot yet accept. The emphasis falls on what the episode does to his position. For a question on the importance of ancestors, the same moment is evidence about the line: the stone is an object of the old knowledge, the test is inherited rather than invented, and Kahu — who already carries the ancestor’s name and has been picking up the teaching from outside Koro’s schoolroom — satisfies it, which suggests that the inheritance recognises her even while its custodian does not. The emphasis falls on what the episode shows about the tradition. The lesson for the examination is that evidence is not owned by a topic: the moment stays the same and the sentence of analysis after it changes, which is why four well-known moments can serve whichever of the two questions you are offered.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["The same evidence can prove different things depending on the sentence you write after it.","For each question, ask what the episode is evidence about — a person, or a tradition.","Set the two uses side by side and finish with what this shows about revising a small number of moments thoroughly."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Component 1: Content overview"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks is the Section C essay on The Whale Rider worth, and how are they split between assessment objectives?","a":"40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4."},{"q":"Who narrates the human story of The Whale Rider?","a":"Rawiri, Kahu’s uncle and Koro Apirana’s grandson."},{"q":"Which ancestor does Nanny Flowers claim descent from when she argues with Koro Apirana?","a":"Muriwai."},{"q":"Name one of the five kinds of context the specification says teaching should include.","a":"Any one of: the writer’s own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; how different audiences receive the text at different times."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Kahutia Te Rangi","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The ancestor, remembered as Paikea, who came from Hawaiki to Whangara riding a whale. Kahu is given the same name at birth, which is what offends Koro Apirana."},{"front":"Koro Apirana","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Kahu’s great-grandfather and the chief at Whangara. He teaches the village boys the old knowledge and insists leadership must pass to a firstborn male, until Kahu’s actions change his mind."},{"front":"Nanny Flowers","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Koro’s wife and Kahu’s great-grandmother: comic, combative, forever threatening divorce. She claims descent from the ancestress Muriwai and uses that descent to argue that a girl may lead."},{"front":"Rawiri","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The narrator, Kahu’s uncle and Koro’s grandson. His years in Sydney and Papua New Guinea send him home valuing the culture he had grown up taking for granted."},{"front":"Porourangi","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Kahu’s father and Rawiri’s elder brother. As the firstborn son of his generation he is the heir Koro’s rule depends on, which is why Kahu’s birth matters so much to the old man."},{"front":"The carved stone","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Koro drops it into the sea and tells his school of boys to bring it up; none can, and he despairs. Kahu later dives and retrieves it, surfacing with a crayfish for him too."},{"front":"The stranding at Whangara","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Whales beach themselves by the village, led by the ancient bull that once carried the whale rider. The villagers’ efforts fail, and Kahu goes out to the great whale alone."},{"front":"Whangara","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false,"back":"The novel’s setting: a real village on the East Coast of New Zealand’s North Island, whose meeting house carries a carved figure of the ancestor riding a whale."},{"front":"Whakapapa","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Genealogy: the line of descent through which belonging and authority are traced and argued. Both Koro’s rule and Nanny Flowers’s objection to it are arguments from whakapapa."},{"front":"Witi Ihimaera","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Māori writer, the first to publish a book of short stories and the first to publish a novel. He wrote The Whale Rider in New York after his daughters asked why the hero was always a boy."},{"front":"Te reo Māori in 1987","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"The year the novel was published, te reo became an official language of New Zealand, after decades of decline and the founding of the kōhanga reo language-nest preschools in 1982."},{"front":"The 2002 film of The Whale Rider","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"Niki Caro’s adaptation took the story to a worldwide audience and brought its young lead an Academy Award nomination — a clear case of a text received differently at a different time."},{"front":"The marks for the Section C essay","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false,"back":"Forty marks: twenty AO1 for close knowledge, critical style, personal engagement and relevant examples, and twenty AO4 for the relationship between the text and the context it was written in."},{"front":"Closed book","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false,"back":"No copy of The Whale Rider may be taken into Component 1, so plot, characters, moments and any quotation must be held in memory. Only the anthology poems are provided."},{"front":"One kind of context the specification names for AO4","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true,"back":"The writer’s own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context, such as genre; the way different audiences receive the text at different times."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: How does Ihimaera present the importance of ancestors in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel.","Point: Ihimaera presents ancestry not as something remembered but as something argued with, so that descent operates in the novel as evidence rather than sentiment.","Evidence: the quarrel between Koro Apirana and Nanny Flowers, in which he traces the chiefly line through its men and she answers by claiming descent from the ancestress Muriwai.","Analysis: because she meets a claim of descent with a claim of descent, the challenge to Koro is mounted inside his own system of authority rather than against it; he can be angered but he cannot dismiss her, and the reader is shown that the tradition already contains the resources to correct itself.","Context integrated: in a novel published in 1987, at the height of a Māori cultural revival and in the year te reo became an official language of New Zealand, this insistence that whakapapa is live and usable is itself part of the argument the book was written into — inheritance presented as a present-tense claim, not a museum piece.","Judgement: ancestors therefore matter in this novel chiefly as authority, which is why the question of Kahu’s leadership can only be settled by the oldest ancestor of all, and why the settlement feels like a confirmation of the line rather than a break with it.","Why this is Level 5: the point answers the question rather than the topic; the evidence is precise and used, not merely mentioned; the reasoning runs in a chain; the context is fastened to the evidence and changes what it means; and the paragraph closes with a judgement in the question’s own terms."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: How does Ihimaera present the importance of ancestors in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel.","Point: Ancestors are very important in The Whale Rider because the characters talk about them all the time.","Evidence: Koro Apirana and Nanny Flowers argue about their ancestors, and Kahu is named after the ancestor who rode the whale.","Analysis: this shows that the family cares about where they come from and that their ancestors are a big part of their identity.","Context: Māori culture values ancestors, and Witi Ihimaera is a Māori writer, so he included a lot about ancestors in the novel.","Why this is only Level 3: the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant, so it is clearly not weaker than that — but the point restates the question instead of arguing a position; the evidence is named rather than used, with no detail of what either character actually claims; the analysis stops at what the moment shows about the characters rather than what Ihimaera achieves by it; the context is a general statement about Māori culture and the author that could sit beside any moment in any of his books; and there is no judgement at the end. Compare it with the Level 5 paragraph: the same two pieces of evidence are there, but only one of the two paragraphs makes them do any work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The Whale Rider: people, story, form and key moments","items":["Author and date: The Whale Rider by Witi Ihimaera, published in 1987. The specification sets no prescribed edition because Component 1 is closed book; the recommended edition is Heinemann, 1st edition, February 2005, ISBN 9780435131081.","Setting: Whangara, a coastal village on the East Coast of New Zealand’s North Island, north of Gisborne — a real place whose meeting house carries a carved figure of the ancestor riding a whale.","Narrator: Rawiri, Kahu’s uncle and Koro Apirana’s grandson, who tells the family story in a wry, conversational voice.","Form: a prologue and an epilogue frame the novel, and the chapters of family life alternate with italicised passages that follow the whale herd and its ancient leader, so the human and the mythic narratives run in parallel until they meet.","Kahu: the great-granddaughter of Koro Apirana, named Kahutia Te Rangi after the founding ancestor, excluded from the teaching because she is a girl, and finally acknowledged as the one who will lead.","Koro Apirana: the chief, keeper of the genealogies and the old knowledge, convinced that authority must pass to a firstborn male, and increasingly desperate that no boy of the next generation can carry it.","Nanny Flowers: Koro’s wife, comic and formidable, forever threatening to divorce him, who claims descent from the ancestress Muriwai and makes the case for Kahu in the language of descent.","Porourangi: Kahu’s father, Rawiri’s elder brother and the firstborn son of his generation, on whom Koro’s expectations for the succession rest.","Key moment — the prologue: Kahutia Te Rangi, remembered as Paikea, comes from Hawaiki to Whangara on the back of a whale, and the novel’s later events answer back to that arrival.","Key moment — the naming and the burial of the birth cord: Kahu is given the ancestor’s name after her mother’s death and her birth cord is buried at Whangara; Koro Apirana will not come, and Nanny Flowers stands in his place.","Key moment — the school: Koro teaches the village boys the language, the chants and the genealogies; Kahu, shut out because she is a girl, picks the teaching up from the edges.","Key moment — the carved stone: Koro drops it into the sea for the boys to retrieve, not one of them can find it, and he despairs; Kahu later dives for it and brings it up, with a crayfish for him as well.","Key moment — the prize-giving: Kahu speaks in Māori at her school ceremony and dedicates her prize to her great-grandfather, who does not come.","Key moment — Rawiri away: years in Sydney and then in Papua New Guinea with his friend Jeff’s family teach him how his own people are seen from outside, and he returns valuing what he had taken for granted.","Key moment — the stranding and the ride: whales beach themselves near the village, the effort to save them largely fails, and Kahu goes out alone to the ancient bull, mounts it, and is carried out to sea; she is recovered alive, and Koro Apirana at last acknowledges her as the one who will lead.","Themes an examiner rewards: inheritance and whakapapa; gender and who may lead; tradition renewed rather than abandoned; the sea and kinship with the whales; belonging, leaving and returning; the survival of a language and a culture."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — context for AO4, by the specification’s five categories","items":["The writer’s life and situation: Witi Ihimaera is a Māori writer and was the first Māori to publish a book of short stories and the first to publish a novel; he wrote The Whale Rider while living and working for New Zealand in New York, prompted, as he has said, by his daughters asking why the hero was always a boy.","The historical setting: Whangara and the East Coast in the later twentieth century, a rural Māori community from which the young leave for work — as Rawiri does for Sydney and Papua New Guinea — and to which they may or may not return.","The historical setting: in the decades after the Second World War the great majority of Māori moved from rural communities into towns and cities, which is the pressure behind Koro Apirana’s fear that no one is left to carry the knowledge.","Social and cultural context: authority, identity and belonging are argued through whakapapa, so both Koro’s rule about male succession and Nanny Flowers’s objection to it are made as claims of descent.","Social and cultural context: te reo Māori had declined severely; kōhanga reo language-nest preschools began in 1982 and the Māori Language Act made te reo an official language of New Zealand in 1987, the year the novel was published.","Social and cultural context: the Waitangi Tribunal, established in 1975 and empowered in 1985 to hear historical claims, is part of the same revival of Māori standing that the novel belongs to.","Literary context: the novel retells an inherited tradition — the coming of the whale rider to Whangara — inside a contemporary, realist family story, in the manner of much postcolonial fiction, and it weaves te reo Māori into English prose rather than translating everything for the reader.","Reception: the novel is now read internationally and taught worldwide; the 2002 film directed by Niki Caro carried the story far beyond New Zealand and brought its young lead an Academy Award nomination, so many readers now meet the book after the film.","Reception: what was contentious in 1987 — a girl inheriting a chiefly line — is far less startling to a reader now, so a modern reading tends to attend instead to the sympathy with which Koro Apirana is written and to the novel’s insistence on renewal from inside a tradition."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How does Ihimaera present the importance of ancestors in The Whale Rider? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","items":["Decoded question: ‘How does Ihimaera present’ asks for the writer’s handling, not a list of ancestors; ‘the importance of ancestors’ asks you to define what kind of importance they have; and the instruction to refer to context means the twenty AO4 marks must be earned inside the paragraphs, not in a paragraph of their own.","Position to argue from the first sentences: ancestors function in this novel as living authority — as the evidence people argue with and are finally judged by — rather than as history or sentiment.","Paragraph 1 — the inheritance is placed on Kahu before she can earn it: the prologue’s arrival of Kahutia Te Rangi at Whangara, and the naming of the newborn girl after him. Context: a real village whose meeting house carries the whale rider carved on it, so the story is rooted in a living tradition rather than a legend at a distance.","Paragraph 2 — descent is how authority is argued: Koro Apirana traces the line through its men, Nanny Flowers answers by claiming Muriwai. Context: whakapapa as the ground of standing in Māori custom, which is why her claim is evidence rather than opinion.","Paragraph 3 — the ancestors are present, not past: the italicised narrative in which the ancient bull whale remembers the man who once rode him, running alongside the family story until the two meet. Context: a novel published in 1987, during a Māori cultural revival and in the year te reo became an official language, insisting that inheritance is present tense.","Paragraph 4 — the inheritance recognises Kahu before its custodian does: the carved stone she brings up from the sea after Koro’s boys have failed, and the ancient whale that accepts her. Context: a generation’s knowledge slipping away, which is what makes Koro’s blindness tragic rather than merely stubborn.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: how far does the novel present ancestry as a constraint on the living, and how far as their resource? Track the answer moving from constraint, in Koro’s rule, to resource, in Nanny Flowers’s counter-claim and in Kahu’s acceptance by the whale.","Conclusion must say: that ancestors matter in this novel as authority that can be appealed to, so the tradition proves able to correct one of its own rules without being abandoned — which is exactly why Koro Apirana is restored at the end rather than defeated, and why the novel reads as an argument for renewal rather than for rupture.","Timing: about ten minutes to plan and choose four moments, about forty to write, and do not leave the conclusion to chance — decide its judgement during the planning."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Exam-day notes for Section C","items":["Read both options on The Whale Rider before choosing: pick the one for which you can already name four distinct moments, not the one whose wording sounds most attractive.","There are no AO2 marks in Section C, so do not spend sentences naming techniques; spend them on evidence and on context instead.","Every paragraph should contain a moment from the novel and a sentence of context fastened to it. If a contextual sentence could be deleted without the point changing, it was decoration.","Range across the whole novel and across both strands — the family at Whangara and the whales at sea — rather than returning three times to the same episode.","Refer precisely rather than quoting loosely: an exactly recalled moment earns the credit a shaky quotation risks losing.","Leave time for a conclusion that answers the question in its own words; on a ‘to what extent’ question, say how far, and why that far."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:32:06Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Textually sound: the kit quotes nothing from the novel (its six quotations are all from the specification and all check out word for word), and every described moment, name and relationship I could test - Kahutia Te Rangi/Paikea and the prologue, Kahu's naming and the birth cord buried at Whangara with Koro absent, Koro's school for the boys, the carved stone and the crayfish, Nanny Flowers's claim to Muriwai, Rawiri's Sydney and Papua New Guinea years with Jeff, the Whangara stranding and the ride, plus 1987 publication, the Maori Language Act 1987, kohanga reo 1982, the Waitangi Tribunal 1975/1985, New York composition and the daughters' prompt, and Niki Caro's 2002 film and its Oscar-nominated lead - is true to the novel and to the record; publish after fixing the card 14 mismatch and the two hints that hand over their answers."}}},"prose-joy-luck-club":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-joy-luck-club","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room is Section C of Component 1: one 40-mark essay on The Joy Luck Club, chosen from two, written closed book in roughly fifty-three minutes of the two-hour paper. Twenty of those marks are AO1 — a close knowledge and understanding of the novel, held in a critical style with an informed personal engagement — and the other twenty are AO4, the relationship between the novel and the contexts in which it was written. Because you may not take the text into the examination, every example has to be carried in memory; because context carries exactly as much credit as knowledge, an answer that only retells Tan's sixteen stories throws away half the marks before it begins. This room builds both halves at once.","idea":[{"code":"Component 1: Content overview","h":"The Joy Luck Club as your one modern prose set text, studied whole","t":"Component 1 asks you to study two set texts: all the poems in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one modern prose set text chosen from a list of seven — here Amy Tan's The Joy Luck Club, published in 1989. Teaching focuses on the study of whole texts, so you are responsible for the entire novel: sixteen linked first-person stories arranged in four parts of four, each part opening with a short parable, covering four mother-and-daughter pairs across pre-war China and late twentieth-century San Francisco. Nothing is extracted for you and no part of the book is off the syllabus. A top-level answer shows this whole-text reach by pairing a moment from a mother's Chinese chapter with a moment from a daughter's American chapter in the same paragraph, rather than working through the novel story by story."},{"code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style and an informed personal engagement","t":"AO1 is worth twenty of the forty marks. The specification is precise about what you must do: demonstrate a close understanding of your prose text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement, and identify and use relevant examples from the text. Close understanding means remembering the detail — that An-mei's scar comes from a pot of boiling soup, that Lena's husband charges her for half of everything, that the piano piece June fails with at the talent show turns out to be one half of a pair. Critical style means writing about what Tan does and why, not about what happens next. Personal engagement means a reading of your own, argued and qualified, not an opinion asserted. The grid the specification prints rises from largely narrative work to discriminating use of relevant examples, so a top-level answer selects a small, exact example and makes it do argumentative work, instead of summarising a whole story to prove it has been read."},{"code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","h":"AO4: context is half of every Section C mark","t":"AO4 is the other twenty marks: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. Because AO4 and AO1 are equal here, context cannot be a paragraph near the end; it has to sit inside the argument. The test is simple: your context sentence must change how the reader understands the moment you have just described. Saying that women had few rights in old China earns little. Saying that a girl promised by a matchmaker at the age of two, sent to her husband's family at twelve to serve her mother-in-law and married at sixteen had no legal person of her own, so that Lindo's only available weapon is the belief system itself, explains why she escapes by inventing an ancestral sign rather than by running away. A top-level answer makes context explain motive, so that removing the context sentence would leave the paragraph's claim unsupported."},{"code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","h":"The five kinds of context the specification names, anchored in the novel","t":"The specification lists five categories. The writer's own life and individual situation, where it relates to the text: Tan was born in Oakland, California in 1952 to Chinese immigrant parents, her mother had left daughters behind in China, and Tan travelled to China in 1987 and met her half-sisters — which stands behind Suyuan's lost twins. The historical setting: the Japanese invasion of China from 1937 that drives Suyuan out of Kweilin, and the Communist victory of 1949 that closed the mothers' route home. Social and cultural contexts: arranged betrothal, concubinage and filial duty in China; assimilation, Chinatown and immigrant ambition in America. The literary context: a novel-in-stories that borrows the shape of folk tale and parable, written in a decade of Chinese-American women's writing. Reception: the novel was a bestseller and was filmed in 1993, and some critics have argued its China is written for Western readers. A top-level answer chooses the one category that actually explains the moment in hand rather than listing all five."},{"code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","h":"What the question looks like, and how to spend the fifty-three minutes","t":"Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying twenty marks AO1 and twenty marks AO4. The component is closed book and you may not take the novel in, although the anthology poems are provided. Component 1 lasts two hours for ninety marks and is sixty per cent of the International GCSE, so this essay is worth about fifty-three minutes. The question stems the specification models are In what ways does…, How does… and a proposition followed by To what extent do you agree? Choose between the two questions by asking which one you hold five strong examples for, not which sounds easier. Spend about five minutes planning. A top-level answer settles its judgement before the first sentence is written, so that every paragraph argues towards it and the conclusion answers the question rather than repeating the essay."}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing between the two Section C questions and decoding the one you pick","setup":"Section C offers a choice of two questions on The Joy Luck Club. Question A: In what ways does Tan present the difficulties of communication between mothers and daughters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks). Question B: How does Tan present the influence of the past on the present in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks). You are closed book, the marks are twenty AO1 and twenty AO4, and you have about fifty-three minutes. Decide which to answer and set the essay up.","steps":["Circle what each stem actually names: difficulties of communication in A, influence of the past in B — both ask how Tan presents something, so neither rewards retelling.","Audit your memory for each: A gives you Lena mistranslating her mother to her father, June's fight over the piano, and Lindo's two faces at the hairdresser's; B gives you Kweilin and the abandoned twins, An-mei's mother's sacrifice, and Ying-ying's first husband.","Weigh the AO4 half: B reaches into China in every paragraph, so the Japanese invasion, concubinage and the closing of China after the revolution can do argumentative work rather than being bolted on; A risks confining context to America.","Choose B, fix the judgement — that the past reaches the daughters as inheritance rather than as memory, because it was never told to them — and plan five paragraphs, each pairing one Chinese moment with its American consequence."],"cues":["Which words in each stem name Tan rather than the story?","Which examples could you write out accurately with no book in front of you?","Which question lets context sit inside every paragraph?","What has to be decided before the first sentence is written?"]},{"title":"Building one paragraph that earns AO1 and AO4 together","setup":"Question: In what ways does Tan present marriage in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks). You have chosen Lindo Jong's first marriage in the story The Red Candle: promised by a matchmaker to Tyan-yu of the Huang family at the age of two, sent to live with the Huangs at twelve after the Fen River flood destroys her family's home and set to serve her mother-in-law, married to Tyan-yu at sixteen, and freed at last by inventing an ancestral warning that the marriage is cursed. Write the paragraph.","steps":["Point first: Tan presents marriage in the mothers' generation as a contract between families in which the bride is the item exchanged.","Evidence, recalled exactly: Lindo is promised at the age of two, sent to the Huangs at twelve to serve her mother-in-law and married to Tyan-yu at sixteen, and the household's anxiety is fixed on the wedding candle burning at both ends through the night as a sign the union cannot be broken.","Context woven in, not appended: childhood betrothal and a daughter's absorption into her husband's family were ordinary in pre-revolutionary rural China, so Lindo's obedience is not timidity but the only conduct available to her.","Develop the claim: her escape works because she turns that belief system against itself, using an ancestral sign the Huangs dare not defy, so she leaves with her reputation and their consent intact.","Mini-judgement: Tan therefore presents marriage less as romance than as a test of what she calls, through Waverly, the art of invisible strength — the quality Lindo will pass to a daughter who uses it on her mother."],"cues":["What claim about marriage will this paragraph argue?","Which details of the betrothal can you produce without the book?","Which kind of context explains why nobody in the story objects?","How does Lindo defeat the arrangement without openly breaking it?","What should the final sentence of the paragraph judge?"]},{"title":"Repairing a paragraph that only retells the story","setup":"A student answering How does Tan present the effects of the past on her characters? (40 marks) has written: An-mei's mother came back to the house when Popo was dying. She cut a piece of flesh from her own arm and put it into the soup to try to cure her. This shows she loved her mother. Then An-mei went away with her to Tientsin. The sentences are accurate but the paragraph scores low. Repair it.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: every sentence reports an event, so the writing touches AO1 only weakly and touches AO4 not at all.","Insert the claim the events are evidence for: Tan uses the sacrifice to show filial duty as a debt paid in the body rather than as an emotion expressed in words.","Insert the context that makes the act legible: it belongs to an extreme tradition of filial piety, and it is performed for a mother who had already cast her daughter out for becoming a rich man's concubine.","Reach forward into the American half of the novel: An-mei grows into a woman who believes a daughter is shaped by what her mother suffered, which is why she tells Rose she has been bent by other people's opinions.","End on judgement: Tan presents the past as an inheritance the daughters receive whether or not the story is ever told to them."],"cues":["What is this paragraph doing in every single sentence?","What claim about Tan is missing above the events?","Which cultural belief makes the sacrifice readable rather than shocking?","Where does this Chinese moment surface in a daughter's story?","What work is left for the last sentence?"]},{"title":"Deciding which of the five contexts earns the marks","setup":"Question: Tan presents America as a land of disappointment for the mothers. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks). Twenty of the forty marks are AO4, and the specification names five kinds of context: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times. Decide which of them belong in this essay and where.","steps":["Refuse the biography-first reflex: Tan's own life earns credit only where it explains the text — that her mother left daughters in China and that Tan met her half-sisters there in 1987 lies directly behind Suyuan's twins, and nothing else from her biography is needed.","Place the historical context where it does argumentative work: the mothers leave a country at war and then find it closed behind them after the revolution, so disappointment in America cannot be cured by going home, and the Joy Luck Club in San Francisco is a rebuilt version of the one founded under bombardment in Kweilin.","Give the daughters the social and cultural context: the mothers were raised where obedience and achievement were the same virtue, while their daughters are raised in a country that teaches them to separate the two, which is the ground of every quarrel in the novel.","Use reception at the point of judgement: readers now, accustomed to second-generation immigrant writing, may take the daughters' side more readily than the novel's first readers did, and some critics have argued that the Chinese chapters are shaped for a Western audience.","Judge with a qualification: America gives the mothers safety and gives their daughters success, but takes from them the language in which either could be explained, so the proposition is largely true of the mothers' inner lives and largely false of their circumstances."],"cues":["When is a fact about the writer worth a mark?","Why can the mothers not simply return to China?","Which context explains the daughters' side of the quarrel?","What does the audience category ask you to notice?","What shape must a judgement take for this stem?"]},{"title":"Planning the whole essay in the first five minutes","setup":"Question: In what ways does Tan present mothers and daughters coming to understand one another in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks). Component 1 runs for two hours and ninety marks, so this essay is worth about fifty-three minutes, and the examination is closed book. Produce the plan you would write before starting.","steps":["Fix one line of argument the whole essay serves: understanding arrives late and partially, and it arrives through objects and actions rather than through conversation.","Paragraph one: June at the mah jong table, taking the East seat her mother left and being handed the Kweilin story by the aunties who expect her to carry it to her sisters.","Paragraph two: Lindo and Waverly at the hairdresser's before the wedding, mother and daughter looking into the same mirror and reading the face they share.","Paragraph three: Ying-ying and Lena and the wobbling table, where a mother who cannot say that the marriage is unbalanced knocks over the thing that is.","Paragraph four: An-mei and Rose, where the mother's blunt judgement that her daughter bends to everyone is what finally lets Rose refuse to give up the house.","Conclusion: state the limit of the understanding reached — it is transmitted, not negotiated — and answer the question in a single sentence rather than summarising the paragraphs."],"cues":["What single claim will every paragraph have to serve?","Which moment opens the novel by handing over a story?","Which pair recognise each other in a mirror?","Where does a mother act because she cannot speak?","Which daughter is told she bends too easily?","What must the conclusion do beyond repeating the essay?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Section C is worth 40 marks. How are they divided, and between which assessment objectives?","a":"Twenty marks AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, critical style, informed personal engagement, relevant examples) and twenty marks AO4 (the relationship between the text and the contexts in which it was written)."},{"q":"Why does the closed-book rule change how you revise The Joy Luck Club?","a":"Nothing can be looked up, so names, moments and any short quotation must be memorised; examiners reward exact, relevant examples produced from memory, not long retold plot."},{"q":"What are the five kinds of context the specification lists?","a":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and how texts are received by different audiences at different times."},{"q":"A paragraph ends by explaining what happens next in the story. Why does that lose marks here?","a":"It is narrative, not argument: it shows no critical style, no personal engagement and no judgement, and it uses up space that an AO4 context sentence should occupy."}]},"room":{"facts":["The Joy Luck Club, Amy Tan's first book, was published in 1989: sixteen linked first-person stories in four parts of four, each part introduced by a short parable.","The four part titles, in order: Feathers from a Thousand Li Away; The Twenty-Six Malignant Gates; American Translation; Queen Mother of the Western Skies.","The four pairs: Suyuan Woo and Jing-mei (June) Woo; An-mei Hsu and Rose Hsu Jordan; Lindo Jong and Waverly Jong; Ying-ying St. Clair and Lena St. Clair.","Suyuan Woo has died before the novel opens and never narrates; the mothers narrate Parts One and Four, the daughters Parts Two and Three, and June narrates one story in each of the four parts, so there are seven narrators in all.","Suyuan founded the first Joy Luck Club in Kweilin during the Japanese invasion, and abandoned her twin baby daughters on the road when she fled the city; the San Francisco club is her second.","Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, carrying twenty marks AO1 and twenty marks AO4; Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark, closed-book paper worth sixty per cent of the International GCSE.","The five kinds of context: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times.","Context anchors for AO4: Amy Tan was born in Oakland, California in 1952 to Chinese immigrant parents and travelled to China in 1987 to meet the half-sisters her mother had left behind; Japan invaded China in 1937; Chinese immigration to the United States was restricted by the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, not repealed until 1943; the novel was filmed in 1993.","Component 1 is closed book: no texts may be taken into the examination, and because of that the specification prescribes no editions of the set texts; the recommended edition of The Joy Luck Club is Vintage, New Edition, June 1991, ISBN 9780749399573."],"questions":[{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: set out how the 40 marks for a Section C question on The Joy Luck Club are divided between the assessment objectives, and say what each of those objectives requires of your writing. (4 marks)","a":"Twenty marks AO1 and twenty marks AO4 — close knowledge and understanding in a critical style with informed personal engagement and relevant examples, and understanding of the relationship between the novel and the contexts in which it was written.","sol":"Section C carries forty marks in total, divided evenly: twenty for AO1 and twenty for AO4. AO1 requires you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of the novel, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the text. AO4 requires you to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and to understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. Because the two are equal, an answer that knows the novel thoroughly but never places it in its contexts can reach only half the available marks, and the reverse is equally true. No marks in this section are given for AO2 analysis of language, form and structure, so naming devices is not, by itself, rewarded here.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"],"hints":["How many assessment objectives does the specification list for Section C, and where would you find them named?","Recall the specification wording for each objective rather than paraphrasing loosely.","Give the mark split first, then take each objective in turn in the specification's own terms."]},{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: describe the structure of The Joy Luck Club — how many stories it contains, how they are grouped, and who narrates each group. (4 marks)","a":"Sixteen first-person stories in four parts of four; mothers narrate Parts One and Four, daughters Parts Two and Three, June narrates once in each part, and every part opens with a short parable.","sol":"The novel is made of sixteen first-person stories arranged in four parts of four, each part opening with a short parable that sets its theme. The parts are Feathers from a Thousand Li Away, The Twenty-Six Malignant Gates, American Translation and Queen Mother of the Western Skies. The mothers narrate Parts One and Four, looking back to China; the daughters narrate Parts Two and Three, set in America. Jing-mei Woo narrates one story in each of the four parts, because she speaks in her dead mother's place as well as her own, which is why there are seven narrators rather than eight: Suyuan Woo never narrates. The result is a frame: the novel opens with June taking her mother's seat at the mah jong table and closes with her meeting her mother's lost twin daughters in China.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["Component 1: Content overview","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"],"hints":["Count the stories in each part before counting the parts.","Ask which generation is speaking in the first half of the novel and which in the middle.","Answer in three moves: the number of stories, the grouping, then who speaks where."]},{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: state the five kinds of context the specification requires you to understand, and give one example of each from The Joy Luck Club. (5 marks)","a":"Writer's life (Tan met the half-sisters her mother left in China, in 1987); historical setting (the Japanese invasion of 1937 drives Suyuan from Kweilin); social and cultural (childhood betrothal and concubinage); literary (a novel-in-stories built on parable); reception (the 1993 film and later criticism).","sol":"The five categories are the writer's own life and individual situation, the historical setting, time and location of the text, social and cultural contexts, the literary context, and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times. For The Joy Luck Club: Tan was born in Oakland, California in 1952 to Chinese immigrant parents and travelled to China in 1987 to meet the half-sisters her mother had left behind, which stands behind Suyuan's abandoned twins. The historical setting includes the Japanese invasion of China from 1937, which drives Suyuan out of Kweilin, and the revolution of 1949, which closed the mothers' route home. Social and cultural contexts include betrothal arranged by a matchmaker in early childhood — Lindo is promised at two, sent to the Huangs at twelve and married at sixteen — together with concubinage and filial duty in China, and assimilation and immigrant ambition in San Francisco. The literary context is the novel-in-stories, built out of folk tale and parable and written within a growing body of Chinese-American women's writing. Reception includes the novel's success and its 1993 film, and the argument of some critics that its China is shaped for Western readers.","m":5,"d":1,"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"],"hints":["Two of the five are about the world of the text and one is about the world of the reader.","Work outwards: the writer, then the period, then the society, then the books around it, then the audiences.","List the categories in the specification's order and attach one example to each."]},{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: who sits at the mah jong table when the novel opens, and why is Jing-mei Woo there? (3 marks)","a":"An-mei Hsu, Lindo Jong and Ying-ying St. Clair, the three aunties of the San Francisco Joy Luck Club; June has been asked to take the East seat left empty by her mother Suyuan, who has died.","sol":"The San Francisco Joy Luck Club is made up of Suyuan Woo and the three women the novel calls the aunties: An-mei Hsu, Lindo Jong and Ying-ying St. Clair. Suyuan has died shortly before the novel opens, and June is asked to take her mother's place at the table, in the East corner — the direction her mother had told her is where things begin. She discovers at that table that her mother's twin daughters, abandoned on the road out of Kweilin, have been found alive in China, and that the aunties expect her to go and tell them about the mother they never knew. The scene therefore does structural work as well as narrative work: it hands June both the seat and the story, and the novel ends with her completing the errand.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["Component 1: Content overview","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"],"hints":["Ask who founded the club and how many seats its table has.","Think about what the aunties want from June before she has agreed to anything.","Name the three women first, then explain the empty place."]},{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: explain what \"maintain a critical style\" and \"present an informed personal engagement\" require in an answer on The Joy Luck Club, and what each looks like when it goes wrong. (5 marks)","a":"Critical style: writing about what Tan does and why, not what happens; it fails as retelling. Informed personal engagement: an argued, qualified reading of your own supported by textual knowledge; it fails as unsupported opinion.","sol":"A critical style means that the grammar of your sentences is about the writer and the reader rather than about the characters: Tan presents, Tan withholds, the reader is placed. It goes wrong when it becomes narrative — when a paragraph reports that An-mei's mother returned, then cut her arm, then took An-mei to Tientsin, which shows that the novel has been read but argues nothing. An informed personal engagement means a reading that is yours and that you are prepared to qualify: for instance, that the daughters' rebellion is less brave than it looks because each of them uses a weapon her mother gave her. It goes wrong in two directions — as bare opinion with no textual support, and as a summary of other people's readings with no position of your own. The specification's grid moves from a simple response with little evidence of personal engagement to an assured, perceptive critical style, so the difference between a middle and a top answer is usually whether the examples are being used to argue or merely displayed.","m":5,"d":1,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"],"hints":["Look at what the subject of each of your sentences is: a character, or the writer?","Consider what a reading has to do before it counts as informed rather than merely personal.","Define each phrase, then show the failure mode of each."]},{"q":"Practice, not an exam tariff: explain what the closed book rule means for a Section C answer on The Joy Luck Club, and what the specification says about editions of the novel. (3 marks)","a":"Learners may not take texts into Component 1, so every name, moment and quotation must come from memory; because it is closed book there are no prescribed editions, and the recommended edition is Vintage, New Edition, June 1991 (ISBN 9780749399573).","sol":"Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into it, although the anthology poems needed for Sections A and B are printed in the paper. For Section C this means that the whole of The Joy Luck Club has to be carried in memory: the names of the eight women, the order of events, the moments you intend to use and any short phrase you intend to quote. Because the component is closed book, the specification sets no prescribed editions of the set texts, so any edition may be used for study; the edition it recommends for this novel is Vintage, New Edition, June 1991, ISBN 9780749399573. Two practical consequences follow. Page references earn nothing, so revise moments and short phrases rather than page numbers. And a short phrase remembered exactly is worth more than a long one remembered loosely — where you are unsure of the wording, refer to the moment instead. Component 2 works the other way, being open book with a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition allowed, so habits formed there do not transfer.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"],"hints":["Ask first what you are allowed to carry into the examination room for this paper.","If no one can bring a book in, what follows for the wording of the set text?","Answer in two parts: what the rule is, then what the specification says about which edition to buy."]},{"q":"In what ways does Tan present the relationship between mothers and daughters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"A quarrel conducted in two languages about what a daughter owes; the mothers transmit skills and expectations formed by China, the daughters inherit and misuse them; understanding, when it comes, arrives through objects and acts rather than conversation.","sol":"Tan presents the relationship as a quarrel carried on in two languages about a single question: what a daughter owes her mother. I would argue that the novel treats the quarrel as inevitable rather than tragic, because the mothers' authority was formed by a China their daughters cannot imagine. Lindo teaches Waverly what the novel calls the art of invisible strength, and Waverly turns it into chess and then into a career, the lesson inherited perfectly and used against its teacher; in 'Four Directions', though, she wants Lindo's approval of Rich and fears her disapproval, manoeuvring for it rather than against it. Suyuan's demand that June be a prodigy comes from a woman who has already lost two daughters and who believes America is a place where anything lost can be remade; June hears only that she is not enough, and the talent-show humiliation and the fight at the piano follow. Ying-ying, who has been making herself invisible since she fell from the boat at the Moon Festival, watches her daughter being quietly erased by a husband who charges her half of everything, and can act only by knocking over the table. Tan's structure carries the argument: the mothers speak in Parts One and Four and the daughters in Parts Two and Three, so the reader hears each side separately and knows more than either. Context does real work here. The mothers' expectations belong to a culture of filial duty, arranged betrothal and concubinage that Tan lays out in An-mei's and Lindo's Chinese chapters, and their silences belong to a war and a revolution that made their histories unspeakable in San Francisco; the daughters' resistance belongs to post-war California, where self-expression is itself a virtue. A reader today, after decades of second-generation writing, may side with the daughters more quickly than the novel's first readers did, but Tan gives neither side an outright victory. My judgement is that understanding, where it is reached at all, is transmitted through objects and acts — a jade pendant pressed on a daughter, a broken table, a plane ticket — because the one thing these mothers and daughters do not share is a language.","m":40,"d":2,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"],"hints":["Ask what each mother is actually trying to hand on, and what her daughter receives instead.","Build each paragraph on one pair, and pair a Chinese cause with an American consequence.","Decide before you begin whether the novel thinks the gap can be closed, and argue towards that."]},{"q":"How does Tan present the influence of the past on her characters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"The past is inheritance, not memory: the daughters are shaped by events never told to them — the abandoned twins, the flesh-cutting and suicide, the first marriages — and the novel withholds and restores those stories to make the reader share their position.","sol":"Tan presents the past not as something the characters remember but as something they inherit without being told. The clearest case is June, who grows up hearing fragments of the Kweilin story, told differently each time and always stopping before the ending, and who learns the truth about the twin sisters abandoned on the road only from her father on the journey to meet them, after her mother is dead. Because the reader receives the story in the same broken order, Tan makes the reader occupy a daughter's position, which is the novel's central formal choice. The same pattern governs the other pairs. An-mei watches her mother cut flesh from her own arm for a dying grandmother who had disowned her, and later watches her kill herself with opium hidden in sweet rice cakes so that her spirit will return at the new year and force Wu Tsing to honour her son; the adult An-mei therefore believes absolutely that a daughter is shaped by what her mother suffered, which is why she tells Rose she bends to everyone. Ying-ying's first husband abandoned her, and the self she lost then is the self Lena never meets. Context is inseparable from this. These pasts are unspeakable partly because of what they are and partly because of where they happened: a China at war from 1937, then closed by revolution, and reachable only in memory by women whose English could not carry it. Tan's own mother left daughters in China and Tan met her half-sisters there in 1987, which is why the novel treats a recovered family as the natural ending rather than a sentimental one. My judgement is that the influence of the past in this novel is measured by silence: the daughters are most shaped by exactly those events their mothers never described, and the book's structure — sixteen stories that keep circling back — is itself the argument that the past is not over.","m":40,"d":2,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"],"hints":["Notice which of the past events the daughters have actually been told about.","Use the order in which the reader is given the Kweilin story as evidence, not just the story itself.","Choose a judgement about whether the past helps or harms, and hold it across every paragraph."]},{"q":"Tan presents her mothers as women who never stop fighting. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks)","a":"Largely agree — Lindo engineers her own release, An-mei's mother turns her death into a weapon, Suyuan rebuilds the club in America — but Ying-ying's long passivity and the mothers' powerlessness in English qualify it.","sol":"I largely agree, but the proposition needs qualifying twice. The evidence for it is strong. Lindo, sent to the Huang family at twelve to serve her mother-in-law and married to Tyan-yu at sixteen, does not run away but studies the household until she can invent an ancestral sign the family dare not disobey, and leaves with their consent and their money; she fights inside the rules because they are the only weapon a girl with no legal person of her own possesses. An-mei's mother, made a fourth wife after her rape by Wu Tsing, cannot win while alive and so times her suicide to fall just before the new year, so that her spirit returns to be feared and her son is raised as a proper heir; her last act is strategy. Suyuan, having lost her first husband, her twins and her city, founds a second Joy Luck Club in San Francisco and treats each week as another attempt at luck. The first qualification is Ying-ying, who is the novel's counter-example: after the Moon Festival she becomes, in her own account, a woman who waits, and the fight she finally makes on Lena's behalf is a single act of destruction rather than a campaign. The second is contextual. In China the mothers fight expertly because they understand the system exactly; in America they are disarmed by language, and their fights with their daughters are lost in translation rather than in substance — Lindo can outmanoeuvre a matchmaker but cannot explain herself to Waverly. My judgement is therefore that Tan presents the mothers as formidable rather than simply combative: their fighting is the accumulated skill of survivors of a specific history, and the novel's sadness lies in watching that skill fail in the one place it matters most to them, their own kitchens.","m":40,"d":2,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"],"hints":["A To what extent question needs the strongest case against your position, written properly.","Look for one mother who does not fit the proposition and ask why Tan includes her.","Separate the fights the mothers win in China from the ones they lose in America."]},{"q":"In what ways does Tan present marriage in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"Marriage in China is a contract between families that erases the bride (Lindo and Tyan-yu; An-mei's mother as fourth wife); in America it repeats the imbalance in modern dress (Lena and Harold's accounts, Rose and Ted's decisions).","sol":"Tan presents marriage twice over, and the force of the novel comes from the rhyme between the two versions. In the Chinese chapters marriage is a contract between families in which the bride is the item exchanged: Lindo is promised by a matchmaker at the age of two, sent to the Huangs at twelve to serve her mother-in-law and married to Tyan-yu at sixteen, and An-mei's mother, having been raped by Wu Tsing, becomes his fourth wife and loses both her standing and her son to Second Wife. Contextually this is accurate rather than exaggerated: betrothal arranged in childhood, concubinage and the absorption of a daughter into her husband's family were ordinary in the pre-revolutionary China Tan is writing about, so these are not villains' marriages but normal ones, which is the point. The American marriages then repeat the same imbalance in a form the daughters cannot name, because it arrives disguised as equality. Lena and Harold, who work at the same firm, split every cost precisely, and the arithmetic conceals the fact that he is her employer, earning about seven times what she does as a designer in his firm; the marriage is unbalanced in exactly the way the wobbling marble table in the guest bedroom of their Woodside house is. Rose lets Ted make every decision for years and is then divorced for having no opinions. Waverly, having survived one failed marriage, brings Rich to her mother's table and watches him pour soy sauce over the steamed pork with preserved vegetables Lindo has just disparaged in order to be contradicted. My judgement is that Tan presents marriage as the institution in which the mothers' history most exactly predicts the daughters' futures: the Chinese wives are silenced by law and custom and know it, while the American wives are silenced by habit and do not, so the daughters' freedom to choose has not, by itself, made them free.","m":40,"d":2,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"],"hints":["Set the Chinese marriages and the American marriages side by side rather than in two blocks.","Ask what each wife is permitted to say, and to whom.","Fix the connecting claim between the generations before you choose your examples."]},{"q":"How does Tan present the experience of living between two cultures in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"Through language above all — broken English and broken Chinese, mistranslation, two faces — and through food, names and rituals; the daughters are fluent in America and illiterate in their mothers, until June's journey reverses it.","sol":"Tan presents living between two cultures chiefly as a problem of language, and only secondly as a problem of belonging. The mothers speak an English that their daughters hear as broken, and the daughters speak a Chinese too thin to carry anything; the result is that the most important things in the novel are said in the wrong language or not at all. Lena, as a child, translates between her Chinese mother and her English-speaking father and quietly edits what she passes on, so the family's understanding of itself is her invention. Lindo, at the hairdresser's before Waverly's wedding, thinks about the two faces she has learned to wear and about which of them she has passed on; she knows how to be Chinese in America and American in China, and knows that the skill has cost her something. Waverly's terror of her mother at the dinner with Rich is not about culture in the abstract but about a code Rich cannot read and Waverly can no longer explain. Tan sets this in a real history: the mothers arrived from a country at war and then sealed off, into a San Francisco whose Chinese community had been shaped by decades of exclusion laws, so assimilation was both a demand and a bargain. The daughters, born American, were raised to believe that the bargain had already been made for them. The novel's ending reverses the movement: June flies to China, is recognised by her sisters, and finds that what she inherits is not a culture she can study but a resemblance she cannot help. My judgement is that Tan presents being between cultures as a condition that cannot be resolved by choosing a side — the daughters who reject China are as defined by it as the mothers who left it.","m":40,"d":2,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"],"hints":["Follow the language in the novel — who translates, who edits, who cannot say what they mean.","Find a moment where a cultural code is misread at a table.","Let the ending of the novel test the claim your earlier paragraphs have built."]},{"q":"The Joy Luck Club is a novel about storytelling rather than a novel about mothers and daughters. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks)","a":"Partly agree: the book is built of told, retold and withheld stories, framed by parables, and its subject is transmission — but storytelling matters because of what it carries between the generations, so the two cannot be separated.","sol":"I agree in part, and the qualification is the whole argument. The novel is unmistakably about storytelling. Its title names a club whose purpose was to eat, play mah jong and tell stories under Japanese bombardment; each of its four parts opens with a parable rather than a scene; and its plot is set in motion by a story June has never been allowed to finish. Tan makes the telling itself the event: Suyuan's Kweilin story changes with each retelling and always stops short, so that June only learns the ending from her father, and the aunties' demand at the mah jong table is not that June grieve but that she carry a story to her sisters. Even the mothers' Chinese chapters read as stories being told to daughters who are not in the room. But storytelling here is never an end in itself. What is at stake in every withheld story is inheritance: An-mei's mother teaches by sacrifice and by a suicide timed for the new year, and the lesson reaches Rose two generations later as a blunt remark about bending to other people. The parables work the same way — each sets a condition the daughters will live out. Contextually, this matters because the mothers' histories are unspeakable for historical reasons, not merely private ones: a war, a revolution and a migration stand between the event and the telling, and the mothers' English cannot cross that distance. Tan's own recovery of her mother's story, and her meeting with her half-sisters in China in 1987, sits directly behind the ending. My judgement is that the proposition is a false alternative: the novel is about mothers and daughters, and storytelling is the mechanism by which that relationship exists at all, so to call it a novel about storytelling is to name the method and mistake it for the subject.","m":40,"d":3,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"],"hints":["Test the proposition by asking what the stories in the novel are for.","Use the parables at the head of each part as evidence, not decoration.","A proposition offering two options can be answered by rejecting the choice, provided you argue it."]},{"q":"How does Tan present the difference between what the reader knows and what the daughters know in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"The reader assembles a history no character holds whole: the daughters' stories are read against Chinese stories they have never been told, Suyuan reaches the reader only through June, and the movement between China and San Francisco means every misunderstanding on the page is one the reader can already explain.","sol":"Tan arranges the novel so that the reader always knows more than the daughters do, and the distance between the two is where the meaning of the book sits. Because there is no single narrator, no one inside the book holds the whole story, and the reader comes to know more than any character does; the daughters' chapters are read against Chinese chapters the daughters have never heard, so every misunderstanding on the page is one the reader can already explain. The arrangement is deliberate rather than loose. The mothers speak in Parts One and Four and the daughters in Parts Two and Three, so the novel opens and closes in the older generation and encloses the younger; the daughters are literally surrounded by a history they think they have escaped. Jing-mei Woo narrates one story in each of the four parts because she speaks in her dead mother's place as well as her own, so Suyuan — the only one of the eight who never narrates — is present throughout by proxy. The four parables that head the parts give each section its condition before any character enacts it. The effect on chronology is equally deliberate: the book moves between pre-war China and late twentieth-century San Francisco without transition, which enacts the claim that the past is not finished. Contextually, the form belongs to a tradition of the novel-in-stories and draws on folk tale and parable, and it also does immigrant work, since a family split by war, revolution and migration cannot be told as a single continuous line. My judgement is that the structure converts a set of family quarrels into a single inheritance: the last chapter, in which June meets the twins and sees her mother in their faces, works only because the shape of the book has been quietly promising that return since the first page.","m":40,"d":3,"codes":["Component 1: Content overview","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"],"hints":["Ask who knows what, and when, including the reader.","Track where each generation is allowed to speak across the four parts.","Connect the arrangement of the parts to the novel's final scene before you judge."]},{"q":"In what ways does Tan present different kinds of inheritance in the mother-and-daughter relationships in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","a":"Each daughter inherits something different and unchosen — Waverly a strategy, Lena a silence, Rose a belief that she is at fault, June an unfinished story — and Tan shows the inheritance passing whatever the mother's temperament.","sol":"I will argue that what passes from these mothers to these daughters is unchosen: a habit of survival is handed on whether or not it is ever explained. The Jongs and the St. Clairs set the range, because they are Tan's two extremes and taking them together exposes what every pair in the novel shares. Lindo and Waverly fight in the open. Lindo teaches her daughter to win by indirection, and Waverly, having learned it at the chessboard, uses it first on her opponents and then on her mother, breaking with her in the market over being paraded as a trophy and later dreading the moment she must present Rich. Their relationship is conducted as strategy on both sides, and Tan gives them the novel's most direct recognition scene, at the hairdresser's before the wedding, where the two women look into one mirror and each sees the other's face in her own. Ying-ying and Lena are the reverse. Ying-ying has been diminishing herself since she fell into the lake at the Moon Festival as a child, and after the loss of her son she is barely present to her daughter at all; Lena grows up watching and translating rather than arguing. Where Lindo instructs, Ying-ying withholds, and where Waverly rebels, Lena simply accommodates — to a mother who cannot speak, then to a husband who charges her half of everything. Ying-ying's one intervention is not a sentence but an act: she brings down the unbalanced table. The context is the same for both pairs and produces opposite results, which is the point: both mothers are survivors of marriages and losses in a China that gave women no standing, and both daughters are Americans who have been told nothing. My judgement is that Tan uses the contrast to show that the inheritance is transmitted whatever the temperament of the mother — Waverly inherits a weapon and Lena inherits a silence, and neither daughter chose hers.","m":40,"d":3,"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"],"hints":["Choose the two pairs that differ most, then look for what they nevertheless share.","Take both pairs inside the same paragraph rather than writing one pair and then the other.","Let the shared context carry the judgement about why the two outcomes differ."]},{"q":"How far might a reader today respond to The Joy Luck Club differently from its first readers in 1989? (40 marks)","a":"First readers met an unfamiliar immigrant history and a rare Chinese-American woman's voice; later readers meet it inside a large body of diaspora writing, are readier to side with the daughters, and meet the charge that its China is written for Western readers.","sol":"This question turns on the fifth kind of context the specification names: how texts are received by different audiences at different times. In 1989 the novel arrived as a first book by a Chinese-American woman into a market where such voices were rare, and much of its early reception treated the Chinese chapters as revelation — the Kweilin club under bombardment, the household of Wu Tsing and its wives, the wedding candle burnt at both ends. A reader today meets the same material inside a large and established body of diaspora writing, so the Chinese chapters are likely to read less as anthropology and more as plot, and the daughters' chapters gain in weight: their difficulty is now legible as a generational condition many readers share rather than as an exotic one. Two further shifts matter. First, the 1993 film and the novel's long life as a set text mean many readers arrive already knowing the ending, which changes the effect of a structure built on withheld stories. Second, a critical argument has grown up that Tan's China is shaped for a Western readership and that the mothers' English is written to be overheard rather than understood; a reader today is likely to be aware of that argument and to test the novel against it. Against all this, the constants should be conceded: the mothers' historical situation has not changed, and a reader in any decade can see that a girl promised at two and sent at twelve to serve her mother-in-law, or a woman made a fourth wife, had no legal person of her own. My judgement is that the novel's reception has moved from discovery to recognition — the same book, read in 1989 as a window and now largely as a mirror — and that Tan's decision to give the daughters half the narration is what allows it to survive the change.","m":40,"d":3,"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"],"hints":["One of the five context categories is about audiences rather than about the text.","Ask what a reader in 1989 had not yet read, and what a reader now has.","Concede what has not changed before you reach the judgement."]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks is the Section C question on The Joy Luck Club worth, and how is it split between assessment objectives?","a":"40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4."},{"q":"Who narrates Parts Two and Three of the novel, and who narrates Parts One and Four?","a":"The daughters narrate Parts Two and Three; the mothers narrate Parts One and Four, with June narrating one story in every part."},{"q":"Where and in what circumstances did Suyuan Woo found the first Joy Luck Club?","a":"In Kweilin, during the Japanese invasion of China, with three other women who met to play mah jong, eat and tell stories."},{"q":"Name one of the five kinds of context the specification lists for AO4.","a":"Any one of: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; how texts are received by different audiences at different times."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Suyuan Woo","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"June's mother, dead before the novel opens. Founded the first Joy Luck Club in Kweilin during the Japanese invasion and abandoned her twin baby daughters on the road out of the city."},{"front":"Jing-mei (June) Woo","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Takes her dead mother's seat at the mah jong table and narrates one story in each of the four parts. Travels to China with her father and meets her half-sisters."},{"front":"The first Joy Luck Club, Kweilin","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false,"back":"Four women meeting under Japanese bombardment to play mah jong, eat and tell stories — luck made deliberately, against the odds. The San Francisco club is Suyuan's second attempt."},{"front":"An-mei Hsu and her mother","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"An-mei is scarred by boiling soup as a small child. Her mother, disowned for becoming a rich man's concubine, cuts flesh from her own arm for a dying Popo, and later kills herself with opium at new year."},{"front":"Lindo Jong","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Promised to Tyan-yu of the Huang family at two, sent to live with them at twelve and married at sixteen. Frees herself by inventing an ancestral warning; later teaches Waverly to win indirectly."},{"front":"Ying-ying St. Clair","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Falls from the boat at the Moon Festival as a child and spends her life becoming invisible. Abandoned by her first husband; renamed by her second. Breaks the wobbling table for Lena."},{"front":"Waverly Jong","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Chinatown chess prodigy who turns her mother's lesson into strategy, then into a career. Dreads presenting her fiance Rich, and meets her mother's eyes in the hairdresser's mirror."},{"front":"Rose Hsu Jordan","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Blames herself for her brother Bing's drowning. Lets Ted make every decision, is divorced for it, and finds her voice only after her mother says she bends to everyone."},{"front":"Lena St. Clair","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Translates for her mother as a child, editing what she passes on. A designer at her husband Harold's firm, she splits every household cost with him although he earns about seven times what she does."},{"front":"One object that stands in for speech between a mother and a daughter","back":"The jade pendant Suyuan gives June after the crab dinner; the unbalanced table Ying-ying knocks over in the guest bedroom of Lena and Harold's house in Woodside; the piano Suyuan offers June years after their fight over it.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":true},{"front":"One way the form of the novel changes what the reader knows","back":"There is no single narrator, so the reader assembles a history no character holds whole; the mothers' parts enclose the daughters' parts; the book moves between China and San Francisco without transition, so the past is never finished.","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":true},{"front":"'The art of invisible strength'","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Waverly's name for the lesson her mother teaches her at the start of 'Rules of the Game': winning by indirection and silence rather than open argument. She applies it to chess, then to Lindo."},{"front":"'Pleading Child' and 'Perfectly Contented'","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The two piano pieces in 'Two Kinds'. June plays the first badly at the talent show; years later she finds they are two halves of one piece."},{"front":"One way a context sentence earns its AO4 marks in Section C","back":"It must change how the moment is read: that a girl promised at two had no legal person of her own; that China closed after 1949, so the mothers could not go home; that exclusion laws shaped Chinatown.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":true},{"front":"Context for the mothers' generation in China","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true,"back":"Betrothal arranged in early childhood and marriage into the husband's family; concubinage, with wives ranked by order; Confucian filial duty, in which obedience is a debt; the Japanese invasion from 1937 and the revolution of 1949."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Note on the level labels: the only assessment grid the specification prints is the five-level grid for the non-examined component, and these labels use those levels — Level 5 is assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation and discriminating use of relevant examples.","Question: How does Tan present the influence of the past on her characters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","Point: Tan presents the past not as memory but as inheritance — something that reaches the daughters precisely because it was never told to them.","Evidence: June grows up with a Kweilin story that changes each time it is told and always stops before its ending, and learns what actually happened on the road — the twins left behind, the possessions left beside them, her mother's own collapse — only from her father, on the journey to meet the sisters, after Suyuan is dead.","Development: because the reader receives the story in the same broken order, and from the same second-hand source, Tan puts the reader in a daughter's position rather than a historian's; we are made to inherit the ending rather than to be told it, which is the effect the novel is built to produce.","Context, integrated: this silence is historical as well as temperamental. Suyuan flees a city under Japanese bombardment, and the country she leaves is closed behind her after 1949, so the story has no living audience in San Francisco and no language that can carry it; Tan's own mother left daughters in China, and Tan met her half-sisters there in 1987, which is why the novel treats the recovery of a family as an ending rather than a fantasy.","Mini-judgement: the past therefore operates in this novel as a debt that is transferred without being explained, and June's flight to China is less a discovery than a repayment she did not know had been assigned to her."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: How does Tan present the influence of the past on her characters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","Point: The past affects the characters a lot in the novel, especially June and her mother.","Evidence: Suyuan had to leave her twin babies behind in China when she escaped from Kweilin, and she never told June the full story. June only finds out from her father later, and at the end she goes to China and meets her sisters.","Development: this shows that the past is important because it is still affecting June even though she was not born yet, and it makes her feel closer to her mother at the end.","Context: Amy Tan is Chinese-American and her mother also came from China, and there was a war in China at that time which made life very hard for women.","Judgement: so Tan shows that the past has a big influence on the characters in the novel."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Why the Level 3 paragraph is weaker — and exactly what to change","items":["Knowledge: the Level 3 paragraph is accurate but general — \"she never told June the full story\" — while the Level 5 paragraph names what was withheld and where June finally hears it. Close knowledge means detail that could only come from having read the book.","Critical style: the Level 3 paragraph's sentences are about characters (\"June only finds out\"), so it narrates. The Level 5 paragraph's sentences are about the writer and the reader (\"Tan puts the reader in a daughter's position\"), so it argues.","Development: \"this shows that the past is important\" restates the point instead of developing it. Development means explaining the effect of Tan's choice — here, the order in which the reader is given the information.","Context: the Level 3 context is a separate sentence of biography and background that could be attached to any question on any novel, and it would score at the bottom of the AO4 grid. The Level 5 context explains why the silence exists, so removing it would leave the claim unsupported — which is what \"integrated convincingly\" means.","Judgement: \"the past has a big influence\" repeats the question. A judgement has to say something the question did not: that the past is transferred as a debt rather than as a memory.","Quick test before you leave a paragraph: could this sentence be true of a different novel? If yes, it is not yet close knowledge or integrated context."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The Joy Luck Club: the eight women and the four pairs","items":["Suyuan Woo: fled Kweilin during the Japanese invasion, abandoning her twin baby daughters on the road; founded both Joy Luck Clubs; dies shortly before the novel opens and is the only one of the eight who never narrates.","Jing-mei \"June\" Woo: Suyuan's American daughter, who takes her mother's seat at the mah jong table, narrates one story in each of the four parts, and travels to China with her father Canning Woo to meet her half-sisters.","An-mei Hsu: scarred as a small child when a pot of boiling soup fell on her; raised by her grandmother Popo after her mother was disowned for becoming the concubine of the rich Wu Tsing; taken to his house in Tientsin as a girl.","Rose Hsu Jordan: An-mei's daughter; as a teenager she was watching her younger brother Bing when he drowned; married to Ted Jordan, who divorces her after years in which she let him decide everything.","Lindo Jong: promised by a matchmaker to Tyan-yu of the Huang family at the age of two; after the Fen River flood she is sent to live with the Huangs at twelve to serve her mother-in-law, and is married to Tyan-yu at sixteen; frees herself by inventing an ancestral warning; reaches America and later marries Tin Jong.","Waverly Jong: Lindo's daughter, a Chinatown chess prodigy; has a young daughter, Shoshana, from a first marriage, and a fiance, Rich Schields, whom she is terrified to present to her mother.","Ying-ying St. Clair: born in a wealthy family, she fell into the lake at the Moon Festival as a small child; her first husband abandoned her; she married Clifford St. Clair, who renamed her, and lost a baby son after coming to America.","Lena St. Clair: Ying-ying's daughter; a designer at the firm her husband Harold Livotny runs, so he is her employer and earns about seven times what she does, yet she splits every household expense with him exactly."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — plot in order: the four parts and the sixteen stories","items":["Part One, Feathers from a Thousand Li Away: June takes her mother's place at the mah jong table and learns the twins are alive; An-mei tells of her scar and her mother's return; Lindo tells of her betrothal at two, her years serving the Huangs from the age of twelve, the wedding candle and her escape; Ying-ying tells of falling into the lake at the Moon Festival.","Part Two, The Twenty-Six Malignant Gates: Waverly learns chess and quarrels with her mother over being paraded through Chinatown; Lena hears the family next door fighting through the wall while her own mother withdraws; Rose recalls Bing's drowning and her mother's faith; June fails at the piano in the talent show and fights her mother afterwards.","Part Three, American Translation: Lena watches her marriage to Harold measured out in shared costs until her mother brings down the unbalanced table; Waverly brings Rich to dinner and watches him misread every rule at the table; Rose, in therapy, at last refuses to give up the house; June is given the jade pendant at the crab dinner at new year.","Part Four, Queen Mother of the Western Skies: An-mei tells of Wu Tsing's household, Second Wife's false suicide and the necklace whose pearls prove to be glass, and of her mother's death by opium just before the new year; Ying-ying tells of her first marriage and the child she would not bear; Lindo tells of her two faces at the hairdresser's before Waverly's wedding; June flies to China with her father and meets her sisters.","The frame: the novel opens with June sitting down in her mother's empty seat and closes with her standing in front of the twin sisters her mother lost, so the errand set in the first chapter is completed in the last."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — themes, form and structure","items":["Form: a novel-in-stories — sixteen first-person narratives, seven narrators, four parts of four, each part headed by a short parable that states its condition before any character lives it out.","Structure: the mothers narrate Parts One and Four and the daughters Parts Two and Three, so the younger generation is enclosed by the older; June narrates once in every part, speaking in her dead mother's place.","Chronology: the novel moves between pre-war China and late twentieth-century San Francisco without transition, which enacts its claim that the past is not finished.","Theme — inheritance: what passes from mother to daughter is transmitted whether or not it is explained; Waverly inherits a strategy, Lena a silence, Rose a belief that she is at fault, June an unfinished story.","Theme — language and translation: the mothers' English and the daughters' Chinese are both too thin to carry what matters, so the novel's decisive moments are misread, edited or unspoken; Lena translating for her mother is the model case.","Theme — two faces: Lindo names the double identity directly, and the novel's American chapters repeat in modern form the imbalances its Chinese chapters set out.","Theme — luck, fate and will: the club is founded to manufacture luck under bombardment, and the mothers' superstitions and warnings are attempts to control outcomes their daughters believe they choose freely.","Motifs an examiner recognises: the mah jong table and its four corners; food, and who is served what; mirrors and faces; birds and feathers; water and drowning; objects that stand in for speech — a jade pendant, a broken table, a candle, a piano."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — context the examiner rewards (AO4)","items":["The writer: Amy Tan was born in Oakland, California in 1952 to Chinese immigrant parents; The Joy Luck Club, published in 1989, was her first book.","The writer, where it bears on the text: Tan's mother had left daughters behind in China from an earlier marriage, and Tan travelled to China in 1987 and met her half-sisters — the situation Suyuan's twins and June's journey are built from.","Historical setting, China: Japan invaded China in 1937 and the war drove civilians inland to cities such as Kweilin, where Suyuan founds the first club; the Communist victory of 1949 then closed the mothers' route home for decades, which is why their histories become unreachable rather than merely distant.","Social and cultural context, China: marriages arranged by matchmakers and betrothals settled in early childhood — Lindo is promised at two, sent to the Huangs at twelve to serve her mother-in-law and married at sixteen; concubinage, with wives ranked by order of arrival, as in Wu Tsing's household; Confucian filial duty, under which a daughter's obedience is a debt owed to her parents and then to her husband's family.","Historical and social context, America: Chinese immigration was restricted by the Chinese Exclusion Act of 1882, not repealed until 1943, which shaped the San Francisco Chinatown the daughters grow up in and the pressure on immigrant families to prove themselves.","Literary context: the novel-in-stories, drawing on folk tale, parable and oral storytelling, written within a body of Chinese-American women's writing that had grown since the 1970s.","Reception: the novel was a bestseller and was filmed in 1993; it has since become a widely taught set text, and some critics have argued that its China is shaped for a Western readership — an argument a twenty-first-century reader is likely to be aware of and can test against the text.","Using context well: the sentence must change how the moment is understood. \"Women had few rights in China\" scores at the bottom; \"a girl promised at two and sent at twelve to serve her mother-in-law had no legal person of her own, so the only weapon available to Lindo is the belief system itself\" scores at the top."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — key moments to hold in memory for a closed-book essay","items":["June takes the East seat at the mah jong table and the aunties tell her that her mother's twin daughters have been found, and press money on her so that she can go to them.","An-mei's mother cuts flesh from her own arm into a soup for the dying Popo, the grandmother who had cast her out.","An-mei's mother crushes a single bead of the pearl necklace Second Wife has given An-mei under her shoe, to show her daughter that it is only glass.","An-mei's mother eats opium hidden in sweet rice cakes and dies two days before the new year, timing her death so that her spirit will return and her son will be raised as an heir.","Lindo watches the red wedding candle that must burn at both ends through the night, and later frees herself by reporting a dream in which the ancestors declare the marriage cursed.","Ying-ying, aged four, falls from the family boat at the Moon Festival, is pulled from the water by fishermen, and discovers that the Moon Lady of the shadow play is a man.","Lindo teaches Waverly to win by indirection — what Waverly calls the art of invisible strength — and Waverly turns it on her mother after being paraded through the Chinatown market.","June plays 'Pleading Child' badly at the talent show, fights her mother afterwards, and years later finds that the piece has a second half called 'Perfectly Contented'.","Rose is watching her younger brother Bing at the beach when he drowns; her mother's Bible afterwards props up the short leg of a table.","Ying-ying pushes over the unsteady table in the guest bedroom of Lena and Harold's house in Woodside so that the vase falls; when Lena says she knew it would happen, her mother asks why she did nothing to stop it.","Rich pours soy sauce over Lindo's steamed pork with preserved vegetables, the dish she is proudest of, after she disparages her own cooking, missing the fact that her complaint was an invitation to praise it.","Suyuan gives June the jade pendant after the crab dinner and tells her that her nature is different from Waverly's.","Lindo sits with Waverly in the hairdresser's mirror before the wedding and thinks about which of her two faces she has passed on.","June lands in China with her father, hears the true end of the Kweilin story from him, and is photographed with the twin sisters, in whom the three of them can see their mother."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the closed book rule and the recommended edition","items":["Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into it; only the anthology poems needed for Sections A and B are provided in the paper, so the whole of The Joy Luck Club must be held in memory.","Because the component is closed book, the specification prescribes no editions of the set texts: any edition may be used for study, and nothing in the marking depends on which one you own.","The edition recommended for this novel is Vintage, New Edition, June 1991, ISBN 9780749399573.","What this changes in practice: page and chapter references earn nothing, so revise named moments, the order of the sixteen stories and a small stock of short phrases you are certain of.","A phrase of a few words remembered exactly is worth more than a long one remembered loosely; where the wording has gone, refer to the moment instead — an examiner rewards the relevant example, not the length of the quotation.","Component 2 is the opposite case — open book, with a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition allowed — so revision habits formed for the drama and literary heritage texts will not serve this room."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Tan present the relationship between mothers and daughters in The Joy Luck Club? (40 marks)","items":["Decode the question: \"In what ways does Tan present\" asks for the writer's methods and choices, not for what happens; \"relationship\" is plural in practice, so the answer must cover more than one pair; the marks are twenty AO1 and twenty AO4, so context belongs in every paragraph and there is no credit here for naming devices.","Judgement to argue from the first sentence: the quarrel between these mothers and daughters is not a failure of love but a failure of translation, and where understanding is reached it is transmitted through objects and acts rather than negotiated in words.","Paragraph 1 — Lindo and Waverly: the lesson in winning indirectly is inherited exactly and used against its teacher; context — a woman promised at two and sent at twelve to serve her mother-in-law teaches survival, not affection.","Paragraph 2 — Suyuan and June: the demand that June be a prodigy comes from a mother who has already lost two daughters; the talent show and the fight at the piano; context — the Kweilin history June has never been told, and an immigrant belief that America remakes what was lost.","Paragraph 3 — Ying-ying and Lena: a mother who has spent her life disappearing cannot warn her daughter in words, so she breaks the table; context — a first marriage and a lost son in a China that gave women no standing.","Paragraph 4 — An-mei and Rose: An-mei transmits by blunt statement what she learned by watching her own mother's sacrifice and suicide; Rose finds her voice only after it; context — filial duty as a debt paid in the body.","Paragraph 5 — the structure as evidence: mothers speak in Parts One and Four, daughters in Parts Two and Three, and June speaks in every part, so the reader is given both sides and knows what neither generation knows.","Evaluation thread to carry through every paragraph: each daughter is fighting with a weapon her mother gave her, so rebellion is itself a form of inheritance — test this against Lena, the daughter who does not fight, and concede that it works differently there.","What the conclusion must do: answer the question in a sentence rather than summarise; name the limit of the understanding the novel allows — it is reached at the end, through a photograph and a resemblance rather than a conversation — and say what that implies about Tan's presentation of the relationship overall.","Timing: about five minutes planning, about forty writing, about eight checking that every paragraph contains one exact example and one context sentence that earns its place."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:03:16.180033Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"All five faults from the previous verdict are fixed and nothing new has replaced them: room.questions[9].sol now places the wobbling marble table in the guest bedroom of Lena and Harold's Woodside house, cards[9].back and extras[7].items[9] both name the Woodside house rather than a flat, cards[13].back and cards[14].back are down to 38 and 36 words, room.questions[3].hints[0] now only asks who founded the club and how many seats the table has, and room.questions[6].sol has Waverly seeking Lindo's approval of Rich in 'Four Directions' rather than defying her; re-solving all fifteen questions independently produced no disagreement with any answer key, the sixteen stories are listed in their correct order in extras[4] and every name, date and moment I checked (the Fen River flood, the crushed glass bead, the opium in the rice cakes before the new year, Bing's drowning and the Bible under the table leg, Harold's seven-times salary, the Exclusion Act of 1882 repealed in 1943, Tan's 1987 visit and the 1993 film) is true to the novel and to the specification; on copyright the kit is unusually careful — the only phrase quoted from the novel anywhere is the five-word 'The art of invisible strength' in cards[11].front, every other quotation is of the specification or of the kit's own model paragraphs, and nothing exceeds seven words, so this is publishable as it stands."}}},"prose-things-fall-apart":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-things-fall-apart","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Things Fall Apart is your modern prose set text for Component 1, Section C. On the day you will meet one 40-mark essay question, chosen from two, on Achebe's novel, and that single essay carries 40 of the paper's 90 raw marks. Section C is assessed by AO1 and AO4 only, twenty raw marks each, so half of everything you earn comes from close knowledge of the novel argued in a critical style and the other half from understanding the contexts in which it was written. Component 1 is closed book: no copy of the novel, no notes, nothing in the room but what you carry in your head. That is why this room is built around remembered moments, a small bank of quotations you can trust, and a method for making context do argumentative work instead of sitting in a paragraph of its own.","idea":[{"h":"One modern prose text, studied whole","code":"Component 1: Content overview","t":"For Component 1 you study two set texts: every poem in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one of the modern prose set texts. Here that choice is Things Fall Apart by Chinua Achebe, chosen from the list of seven. The specification asks for the study of whole texts, developing comprehension, critical reading and, where relevant, comparison, so the examiner expects you to move freely across all three parts of the novel: Umuofia before the missionaries, the seven years of exile in Mbanta, and the return to a clan that has changed without Okonkwo. A top-level answer proves that whole-text reach inside single paragraphs, setting a moment from Part One beside a moment from Part Three, rather than working through the book in order and running out of time at the exile."},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","t":"The specification says learners must demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from their prose text. That is twenty of the forty marks. Close understanding means precise moments, named: the fine Ezeani takes from Okonkwo after the Week of Peace, the night Chielo carries Ezinma to the caves, the gun that explodes at Ezeudu's funeral. A critical style means you write about what Achebe does, not about what Okonkwo does next. An informed personal engagement means you hold a position and defend it. Because the paper is closed book, every example has to come out of memory, so a top-level answer uses short, certain quotations and confident references to moments, and never pads with retold plot."},{"h":"AO4: context is half the mark on every question","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","t":"The specification requires that learners understand the relationship between their prose text and the context in which it was written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. AO4 is worth twenty of the forty marks in Section C, an equal share with AO1, so context is half the mark on every Things Fall Apart question you could be set. That equality is the single most useful fact in this room: an essay that is brilliant on Okonkwo and silent on 1958, on colonial administration or on how the novel is read now has thrown away half the paper. The top level asks for understanding of the relationship between text and context to be integrated convincingly, so a top-level answer never has a context paragraph; it has context inside the reason every point gives."},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","t":"The specification divides context into five kinds that affect a writer's work and a reader's response: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times. For Things Fall Apart each one is live: Achebe born in Ogidi in 1930 into a Christian convert household; the Igbo world of the late nineteenth century as British administration arrives; a society governed without kings; an English novel built on Igbo proverb and folk tale; and a book read very differently in Lagos now and in London in 1958. A top-level answer chooses the category that answers the question instead of reciting all five."},{"h":"What the question looks like, and the time you have","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","t":"Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and the section assesses AO1 and AO4. In the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying twenty marks AO1 and twenty marks AO4. Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark paper worth 60% of the International GCSE, taken on paper or onscreen, and it is a closed book examination: learners may not take texts in, although the anthology poems are reproduced for Section B. Forty of ninety marks in two hours works out at about fifty-three minutes for this essay. A top-level answer spends about five of those minutes deciding which of the two questions its memory can actually feed, then writes to a fixed plan, because there is no text to fall back on if the argument stalls."}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a Section C question before you write","setup":"A Section C question of the kind set on your prose text reads: \"In what ways does Achebe present the power of the clan in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.\" It is worth 40 marks, twenty AO1 and twenty AO4, and the paper allows roughly 53 minutes for it. Work out what the question is actually asking for, and the order you will answer it in, before writing a sentence.","steps":["Mark the focus, \"the power of the clan\", and register that \"in what ways\" wants Achebe's methods of presenting it, so every paragraph must name a way, not an event.","Break the focus into the forms that power actually takes in the novel: the egwugwu who hear disputes, the Oracle of the Hills and Caves whose word ends argument, the Week of Peace and the fine Ezeani takes, the titles a man buys, and exile for a killing committed inadvertently.","Set a context strand beside each form, using the social and cultural category: a society without kings that governs by elders, titled men, masked ancestors and an oracle, and an author writing that society down for readers who had been told it had no institutions.","Write the position you will defend in one sentence: Achebe presents the clan's power as real and binding, but as resting on shared belief, which is why it dissolves when belief divides rather than when guns arrive.","Order the paragraphs so the last one reaches Part Three, where the clan will not act and Okonkwo stands alone, because that is where the argument is decided."],"cues":["Which words are the focus, and what does the stem want?","Where does the clan's power actually show itself?","Which of the five context categories fits a society governed this way?","What single position will you defend all the way through?","Which part of the novel must the final paragraph reach?"]},{"title":"Turning remembered material into an AO1 paragraph","setup":"Closed book, a student has this much in memory for the question \"How does Achebe portray Okonkwo's fear in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.\": Okonkwo made his name as a young man by throwing the wrestler Amalinze the Cat; his father Unoka died a debtor who loved his flute; the narrator says “his whole life was dominated by fear”, naming failure and weakness as what he fears; and when Ikemefuna runs to him calling him father, Okonkwo cuts him down because, the narrator says, he was afraid of being thought weak. Build the opening claim and the evidence of one AO1 paragraph out of that material.","steps":["Open on Achebe rather than on Okonkwo: Achebe portrays fear as the engine of Okonkwo's strength, so that what looks like courage is avoidance.","Take the evidence that proves the claim first, not the evidence that is easiest to retell: the narrator's statement that “his whole life was dominated by fear”, with failure and weakness named as its objects.","Add the moment where that fear becomes action, quoting only what you are sure of: he was afraid of being thought weak.","Make the chain explicit so the paragraph reasons rather than lists: the same dread of resembling Unoka that built the barns and bought the titles also lifts the machete.","Finish with a judgement in your own voice: Achebe makes fear, not pride, the cause of the fall, which is why the fall reads as inevitable rather than unlucky."],"cues":["Should the first sentence be about the character or the writer?","Which remembered line proves the claim most directly?","Which moment shows the fear turning into an act?","What links the wrestling ring and the machete?","What must the last sentence of an AO1 paragraph do?"]},{"title":"Making context do the work instead of sitting beside it","setup":"The question is \"In what ways does Achebe present the coming of the missionaries in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.\" A student writes: \"Chinua Achebe was born in Nigeria in 1930 and Things Fall Apart was published in 1958. Anyway, the missionaries are given land in the Evil Forest and build their church there.\" Twenty of the forty marks are AO4, and the top of the context strand asks for understanding integrated convincingly into the response. Decide why this scores at the bottom of that strand, and rewrite it.","steps":["Name the fault precisely: the dates occupy their own sentence and alter nothing in the reading, which is the little comment on the relationship between text and context that the lowest level describes.","Choose the context category that actually explains the moment, the writer's own life and individual situation where it relates to the text: Achebe grew up in a household his father had moved from Igbo religion into the Church Missionary Society.","Attach the context to the evidence rather than to the paragraph: the clan grants the Evil Forest expecting the land to kill the newcomers, and when nothing kills them the young and the excluded begin to follow.","Rewrite it as one sentence in which the context is the reason: Achebe, writing from inside a family the mission had already divided, presents conversion not as trickery but as a door the clan's own rules held open for the people it had pushed out.","Apply the examiner's test: delete the context and see whether the argument changes; if it does not, the context is bolted on and must be rewritten."],"cues":["What exactly is wrong with a sentence of dates?","Which context category best explains missionary arrival?","Which moment in the novel should that context explain?","How would the sentence read with context as the reason?","What quick test shows whether context is integrated?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions you are offered","setup":"Section C offers a choice of two questions on your set text. Suppose they are: (a) \"In what ways does Achebe present Okonkwo as a tragic figure in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.\" and (b) \"To what extent do you agree that Things Fall Apart is a novel about the destruction of a way of life? You must refer to the context of the novel.\" Each carries 40 marks, twenty AO1 and twenty AO4, you may answer only one, and the paper is closed book. Choose, and justify the choice in the terms an examiner would use.","steps":["Test each stem against the evidence you can actually reach from memory, because closed book means an idea you cannot support with a named moment is worth nothing.","Read the two shapes: (a) asks for ways, so paragraphs are methods of presentation; (b) puts a proposition to you, so paragraphs must weigh it and the essay must end in a qualified judgement.","Ask which stem feeds the AO4 half more easily: (b) opens directly onto social and cultural context, the historical setting of the late nineteenth century, and how the novel is received now, so the context marks are simpler to reach.","Spot the trap in (b): total agreement forfeits the evaluation, since Achebe shows Umuofia arguing with itself, over twins and over the killing of Ikemefuna, before any missionary arrives.","Take (b), and write the position down before the first paragraph: the way of life is destroyed, but Achebe shows the fractures that let the destruction in."],"cues":["What limits your choice before anything else does?","What different shape does each stem demand?","Which stem feeds the context half more easily?","What is the danger of simply agreeing with (b)?","What goes on the page first once you have chosen?"]},{"title":"Writing a conclusion that earns the judgement","setup":"A student has written four paragraphs on \"To what extent do you agree that Okonkwo destroys himself in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.\" The paragraphs argue, in order: his dread of resembling Unoka drives everything he does; he takes part in killing Ikemefuna despite Obierika's reproach; exile removes him from Umuofia for seven years while the clan changes without him; the clan will not go to war and he beheads the court messenger alone. About ten minutes remain. Decide what the conclusion must do and what judgement it must reach.","steps":["Refuse the summary: a conclusion to a proposition question must agree, disagree or qualify in its first clause, not restate the paragraphs.","Weigh the two causes the essay has actually put in play, the character Achebe built and the history that arrived while Okonkwo was in Mbanta.","Give the qualified judgement: Okonkwo destroys himself, but only because colonial administration removes the audience his self was built for.","Carry the context into the judgement in a single clause: Achebe, writing as Nigeria moved towards independence, makes the personal collapse inseparable from the political one.","Land the final sentence on the novel itself, on the District Commissioner deciding that a whole life will make a reasonable paragraph in The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger."],"cues":["What must the first clause of this conclusion do?","Which two causes has the essay put in play?","How do you qualify rather than simply agree?","Where does context belong in a conclusion?","Which moment should the last sentence rest on?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How are the 40 marks for a Section C question on Things Fall Apart divided?","a":"Twenty for AO1 and twenty for AO4. Nothing is awarded separately for analysis of language, form and structure in this section."},{"q":"Why does closed book change the way you revise the novel?","a":"Every example, moment and quotation has to come out of memory, so revision means building a small bank of certain quotations and precisely remembered moments rather than relying on finding evidence on the day."},{"q":"A paragraph ends with two sentences of biography about Achebe. What is wrong with it?","a":"The context is bolted on. Delete those sentences and the argument is unchanged, so it sits at the bottom of the context strand. Context must be the reason a point is true."},{"q":"Which of the five context categories would you use to discuss how Things Fall Apart reads today compared with 1958?","a":"The way in which texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 1, Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on the modern prose set text, assessing AO1 (20 raw marks) and AO4 (20 raw marks).","Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark paper worth 60% of the International GCSE, and it is a closed book examination: no texts may be taken in, though the anthology poems are provided.","AO1: Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.","AO4: Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.","The five categories of context: the writer's own life and individual situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","Things Fall Apart was published in 1958; its title comes from W. B. Yeats's poem The Second Coming, which contains the line “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”.","Chinua Achebe was born in 1930 in Ogidi, in Igboland in eastern Nigeria, and died in 2013; Nigeria became independent in 1960, two years after the novel appeared.","The novel has twenty-five chapters in three parts: Umuofia before the missionaries, the seven years of exile in Mbanta, and the return to a changed clan.","About fifty-three minutes are available for this essay if the ninety marks of Component 1 are divided across the two hours."],"questions":[{"q":"State the assessment objectives assessed in Component 1, Section C, and the share of the marks each carries.","a":"AO1 and AO4, twenty raw marks each out of forty; no marks in this section are awarded for AO2 or AO3.","sol":"Section C – Modern Prose is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and the specification states that this section assesses AO1 and AO4. In the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks, Questions 4 to 17 carry 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4. AO1 is “Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement” and AO4 is “Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written”. The practical consequence is that the two halves are equal: a paragraph naming similes in Achebe's prose earns nothing on its own here, while the same evidence used to prove a point about the novel and to read it against the world it came from earns from both halves.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only two of the four assessment objectives are examined in this section.","Find where the specification breaks Questions 4 to 17 down into raw marks.","One sentence naming the objectives, one giving the share of the total each takes."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"The specification names the categories of context that teaching for AO4 should include. List them.","a":"The writer's own life and individual situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","sol":"There are different kinds, or categories, of context which affect writers' work and the reader's response to it, and the specification lists five. First, the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text: Achebe was born in Ogidi in 1930 into a household his father had moved into the Church Missionary Society, and wrote in 1958. Second, the historical setting, time and location of the text: Igboland in the late nineteenth century as British administration and missionaries arrive. Third, social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups: a clan governed without kings, by elders, titled men, the egwugwu and the Oracle. Fourth, the literary context, for example literary movements or genres: an English-language novel that carries Igbo proverbs, songs and folk tales, answering European fictions of Africa. Fifth, the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times: the novel reads differently in the twenty-first century from the way it read in 1958.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["AO4 is not one thing: the specification divides context into distinct kinds that shape both the writing and the reading.","Sort them by where they come from: some belong to the writer, some to the world of the story, some to whoever is reading.","Five items, each a short phrase rather than a sentence."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Demonstrate a close knowledge of the novel: state what happens at Ogbuefi Ezeudu's funeral and what follows from it for Okonkwo.","a":"Okonkwo's gun explodes and kills the dead man's son; because the killing is inadvertent it is a female crime, so Okonkwo must leave Umuofia for seven years and goes to Mbanta, his mother's village.","sol":"At the funeral of Ogbuefi Ezeudu, one of the greatest men of the clan, the warriors fire their guns and dance. Okonkwo's gun explodes and a piece of iron kills Ezeudu's son. The clan distinguishes between a killing done deliberately and one done inadvertently, and this is the second kind, the female sort, so the penalty is exile rather than death: Okonkwo must leave Umuofia for seven years. He leaves that same night, before cockcrow, taking his family and what could be carried to Mbanta, his mother's village; only after he has gone, as soon as the day breaks, do the men of Ezeudu's quarter come to destroy his compound, bearing him no personal ill will and acting to cleanse the land the offence has polluted. Examiners reward this moment because Obierika, sitting afterwards in his own hut, asks why a man should “suffer so grievously” for what he did by accident, which is the novel putting its own culture under question long before any missionary does.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The event is an accident at a funeral, and the clan's law separates two kinds of killing.","Say what happens, then what category the clan places it in, then the penalty that category carries.","Three short sentences: the accident, the classification, the consequence and where he goes."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Show understanding of context: give the year Things Fall Apart was published and the poem from which Achebe takes his title.","a":"1958; W. B. Yeats's The Second Coming, which contains the line “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”.","sol":"Things Fall Apart was published in 1958, two years before Nigerian independence in 1960, and it later became the first book in the Heinemann African Writers Series. The title is taken from W. B. Yeats's poem The Second Coming, whose opening lines describe a falcon that cannot hear the falconer and then state that “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”. Achebe prints the lines as an epigraph. This is useful AO4 material rather than decoration, because it sets the novel inside the literary context of European modernism at the same moment as it tells an Igbo story: the borrowed English line announces that the centre which cannot hold is Umuofia's, and it warns the 1958 reader that a coherent world is about to be broken rather than that a primitive one is about to be improved.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two facts are wanted: a year in the 1950s, and a poem by an Irish poet.","The title is a quotation, so the source is a poem printed at the front of the novel.","One line: the year, then the poet and poem."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"State, in the specification's own terms, what learners must do to satisfy AO1 in Section C.","a":"Demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text; maintain a critical style; present an informed personal engagement; identify and use relevant examples from their prose text.","sol":"The specification prints five “learners must” bullets for Section C: demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text; maintain a critical style; present an informed personal engagement; identify and use relevant examples from their prose text; and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. The first four are the AO1 demands and are the answer to this question; the fifth is the AO4 bullet, which is why it is set aside here. Each phrase is a separate demand. Close understanding is precision about moments, not coverage of plot: the fine Ezeani takes after the Week of Peace, Chielo carrying Ezinma through the night, the gun at Ezeudu's funeral. A critical style means writing about Achebe's choices rather than narrating Okonkwo's life. An informed personal engagement means holding and defending a position, not balancing two views politely. Relevant examples means evidence chosen because it proves the point, which in a closed book examination means a small bank of quotations you are certain of and a larger set of moments you can describe accurately.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The specification gives a short list of “learners must” requirements for this section.","One of them is about knowledge, one about the manner of the writing, one about the position you take and one about evidence.","Four items, each a phrase, in the specification's wording."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Achebe present Okonkwo's relationship with his father in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Okonkwo is built entirely in negation of Unoka; Achebe presents the relationship as the source of his wealth, his violence and his cruelty to Nwoye, and uses it to show a value system that rewards achievement over age and so manufactures the man who destroys himself.","sol":"Achebe presents the relationship as the whole architecture of Okonkwo's character. The novel opens by making Okonkwo a man “well known throughout the nine villages”, with his fame reaching further still, and then immediately supplies the reason he needed to be: Unoka, the debtor with the flute, who owed every neighbour and died of a swelling in the belly, unburied in the ordinary way. Achebe makes the relationship causal rather than incidental, telling us that “his whole life was dominated by fear”, and naming failure and weakness as what he fears. The clan supplies the terms of that fear: age earns respect among his people, but “achievement was revered”, so a man could rise above the father he was born to. This is where context does real work. Achebe, writing in 1958 for readers who had learned Africa from Conrad and from Joyce Cary, is careful to show a society with a scale of value, a system of titles and an argued ethics, rather than an undifferentiated mass. Okonkwo's cruelty is therefore not exotic but legible: he rules his household with a heavy hand, drives Nwoye by fear, and reads gentleness as the return of Unoka. Brooding alone in Mbanta after Nwoye has gone to the missionaries, Okonkwo thinks of himself as a flaming fire and cannot understand how such a fire came to father such a son, and the chapter closes on his own conclusion that living fire begets cold, impotent ash. The image is a private reflection rather than a taunt spoken to Nwoye, and it convicts the father: the ash is what the fire itself has made. The tragedy Achebe builds is that Okonkwo's over-obedience to his clan's values, not his rejection of them, makes him unfit for the clan's survival. My judgement is that Achebe presents the relationship as an indictment of a value system rather than of a man: Unoka is the price Umuofia charges for its own definition of strength.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The question asks for ways of presenting, so each paragraph should be a method Achebe uses rather than an episode in sequence.","Look for the places where the narrator states the cause directly, and for the places where Okonkwo repeats his father's treatment of him on his own son.","Open with a position on what the relationship is for in the novel, then build paragraphs that each set a moment beside the value system that produced it."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"How does Achebe portray the position of women in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Achebe portrays a patriarchal society in which women are beaten and ranked below men, yet which invests its highest spiritual authority in women, and uses that contradiction to complicate both Okonkwo's idea of strength and any simple colonial reading of Igbo life.","sol":"Achebe portrays the position of women as double, and the doubleness is the point. Socially, women are subordinate: Okonkwo rules his household with a heavy hand, beats Ojiugo during the Week of Peace and is fined by Ezeani for it, and one of the sharpest insults the clan has for a man is agbala, a word meaning woman and also a man who has taken no title, which is what Unoka was called. Spiritually, however, the clan's authority is female. Ani, the earth goddess, is the clan's most important deity, the source of its law and its harvests, and it is her priest who punishes Okonkwo. Chielo, an ordinary widow by day, becomes the priestess of Agbala, and when she carries Ezinma on her back through the night to the caves it is Ekwefi who follows her, alone and unbidden, while Okonkwo comes to the mouth of the cave later with his machete and can do nothing there that counts. Uchendu presses the same point in Mbanta, teaching the exiled Okonkwo that a man lives in his fatherland in good times but seeks refuge with his mother's people in sorrow, which is why Nneka, mother is supreme, is such a common name. Context sharpens this. Achebe, writing in 1958 against a colonial literature that described African societies as brute patriarchies without structure, shows a society whose gendering is systematic, self-aware and internally contested; and a reader in the twenty-first century, who will not accept the beating of Ojiugo as the novel's narrator partly does, reads the same material as an indictment. My judgement is that Achebe neither defends nor merely reports the position of women: he makes Okonkwo's contempt for the feminine the exact flaw that destroys him.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Do not settle for listing the ways women are treated badly; the question is about how Achebe portrays their position, and his portrayal has two sides.","Set the social treatment of wives and daughters against the religious authority the clan gives to women, and let the contradiction generate the argument.","Begin with the doubleness as your position, give each paragraph one social moment and one moment of female authority, and let context explain why Achebe wanted the reader to see both."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Achebe present the coming of the missionaries to Umuofia and Mbanta in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Achebe presents the coming of the missionaries as quiet, patient and lawful rather than violent, entering through the gaps the clan has already made by excluding twins, outcasts and doubters, so that division precedes conquest.","sol":"Achebe presents the missionaries as arriving softly, and he presents the softness as the danger. In Mbanta the clan grants them land in the Evil Forest, expecting the ground to kill them within days; when nothing kills them, the clan's own reasoning has been publicly falsified, and the church begins to fill. Achebe is exact about who fills it: the efulefu, the men of no title, then the mothers of twins the clan has thrown away, then the osu, the outcasts Mr Kiaga admits. Conversion is presented as a door the clan's own rules held open for the people it had already excluded. Nwoye's case is the intimate version of the same movement: Achebe places his turning not in argument but in relief, in the hymn that answers the questions he cannot ask, about the twins left crying in the bush and about Ikemefuna. The presentation is deliberately graduated. Mr Brown learns the clan's language of belief, argues with Akunna, builds a school and a hospital, and restrains his converts; only after he goes does the Reverend James Smith arrive, seeing things in black and white, and only then does Enoch unmask an egwugwu and the church burn. Context is decisive here. Achebe, whose own father left Igbo religion for the Church Missionary Society, is writing in 1958 about a generation that chose, and he refuses both the missionary's account of rescue and a nostalgic account of seduction. My judgement is that Achebe presents the coming of the missionaries as an occupation of a vacancy: as Obierika says, the white man has “put a knife on the things” which bound the clan together, so that it has fallen apart, and the knife found the seams the clan had already cut.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about presentation, so ask what Achebe chooses to show first, and who he chooses to show joining.","Track the order of events in Mbanta and then in Umuofia, and notice how different the two missionaries are from one another.","Take a position on why the arrival is written as gentle, then use each paragraph to show a group the clan had excluded and the reason context gives for their choice."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"To what extent do you agree that Okonkwo is responsible for his own downfall in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"A qualified agreement: his fear of resembling Unoka drives the killing of Ikemefuna, the beating in the Week of Peace and the final act of violence, but Achebe makes the value system and the arrival of colonial administration the conditions that turn those traits into a downfall.","sol":"I agree that Okonkwo is responsible, but only in the sense that a man is responsible for a character his society designed for him. The case for responsibility is strong and Achebe builds it carefully. Okonkwo beats Ojiugo in the Week of Peace and has to be fined by Ezeani; he goes with the men to kill Ikemefuna, who has called him father for three years, and cuts the boy down because he was afraid of being thought weak, ignoring Ezeudu's warning to have no hand in it; at the end he beheads the court messenger without waiting to see whether the clan is with him. Each is a choice, and Obierika is placed in the novel precisely to show that a thoughtful member of the same culture would have chosen otherwise. Yet Achebe also removes the ground from under him. The seven years of exile take him out of Umuofia exactly while Umuofia changes, so that he returns with a plan for a world that no longer exists. The court, the messengers and the District Commissioner make his kind of strength not merely ineffective but illegal. The final act, though, is his own: Okonkwo hangs himself from a tree behind his compound, and because suicide is an offence against the Earth the body is an abomination, so that only strangers may cut him down and his own clansmen may not bury him. The context of writing matters here: Achebe, writing in 1958 as Nigeria moved towards independence, had no interest in a colonial story in which Africans destroy themselves unaided, nor in a nationalist one in which they are blameless. My judgement is that responsibility is genuinely Okonkwo's, and that Achebe's point is how little that responsibility explains: the last word on him is not his own but the Commissioner's, planning The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["A proposition question wants the proposition weighed, so plan the case for and the case against before choosing your position.","Gather the moments where Okonkwo clearly chooses, then the circumstances he did not choose, and ask which the novel gives more weight to.","State a qualified position in the opening sentence and make every paragraph test it, so the conclusion refines the position rather than repeating it."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Explore the difference in emphasis between an answer to a Section C question on Things Fall Apart and an answer to a Component 2, Section B question on a literary heritage text, and explain why the difference exists. (Practice tariff, not a board tariff: Section C sets a single 40-mark essay.)","a":"Section C is AO1 and AO4 only, twenty marks each, closed book; Component 2 Section B is AO1, AO2 and AO4 at ten marks each and is open book, so analysis of language, form and structure must be present in every paragraph there but earns no separate credit in Section C.","sol":"The difference is created by the assessment objectives each section carries. Section C of Component 1 is one 40-mark essay assessing AO1 and AO4, twenty raw marks each, so an answer on Things Fall Apart is rewarded for close knowledge of the text argued in a critical style with an informed personal engagement, and for understanding of the relationship between the novel and the contexts in which it was written. Nothing is separately credited for analysis of method, which is why Section C questions are not accompanied by the instruction to consider language, form and structure. Component 2, Section B is a 30-mark essay carrying ten marks each of AO1, AO2 and AO4, so all three strands must appear in every developed paragraph and the question carries both the instruction to consider language, form and structure and the instruction to refer to the context. The practical consequence for writing is that in Section C a quotation earns its place by proving a point about the novel, whereas in Component 2 the same quotation must additionally be taken apart. Conditions differ too: Component 1 is closed book, so evidence comes from memory, while Component 2 is open book and a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in, so quotation there should be exact and chosen rather than approximate.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the assessment objectives each section carries rather than from the texts themselves.","Compare the mark splits of the two sections, then ask what each instruction printed under a question is there to pay for.","Two or three sentences on the objectives and mark splits, then a sentence on what that changes in the way you use a quotation, then one on open and closed book."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"To what extent do you agree that Things Fall Apart is the tragedy of a community rather than the tragedy of one man? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Largely agree: Achebe gives the clan its own institutions, law and internal argument and lets the last two parts turn on what happens to Umuofia, but Okonkwo's individual fall is the instrument through which the communal one is made visible.","sol":"I agree substantially, though the novel needs the individual to show the communal. The structure supports the communal reading. Achebe spends the whole of Part One on Umuofia rather than on Okonkwo alone: the Week of Peace and the fine, the marriage negotiation at Obierika's, the egwugwu hearing the dispute between Uzowulu and his wife's family, the night journey of Chielo and Ezinma. These are the institutions of a working society, and Achebe includes them because in 1958 the reader he most needed to reach did not believe such institutions existed. The title, taken from Yeats, names a centre that cannot hold, not a man who cannot cope, and Obierika supplies the novel's thesis when he says the white man has “put a knife on the things” which bound the clan together, so that it has fallen apart. Against that, Achebe organises the reader's attention around one life, and the crises that matter are Okonkwo's: Ikemefuna, the exploding gun, the exile, the machete in the marketplace. The reconciliation is that Achebe uses the individual as the measuring instrument. Okonkwo can be destroyed in a page; a culture cannot, and the novel therefore shows the culture's destruction through the one member least able to bend. The final paragraph confirms the communal claim by method as well as content, since the narration abandons Okonkwo for the District Commissioner, who will reduce him to a paragraph in The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger. My judgement is that the tragedy is the community's, and Okonkwo is how Achebe makes it legible.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The proposition sets two readings against each other, so find the evidence in the novel's structure, not only in its events.","Ask what Part One is actually spent on, and what the title and the closing paragraph are about.","Take a position that says which reading dominates and why the other is still necessary, and make the conclusion explain the relationship between the two rather than choosing blindly."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Show understanding of the way texts are received by different audiences at different times: explain how the closing paragraph of Things Fall Apart might be read differently by a British reader in 1958 and by a Nigerian reader today. (The 8-mark tariff here is a practice tariff for a short synoptic exercise, not a board tariff: Section C sets one 40-mark essay.)","a":"In 1958 the shift into the District Commissioner's point of view exposed a coloniser's account of Africa to a readership that had largely trusted it; today the same paragraph is read as a settled demonstration of how colonial history was written, and the irony lands as familiar rather than shocking.","sol":"The novel ends by leaving Okonkwo's body to the District Commissioner, who decides that Okonkwo, a man who killed a messenger and then took his own life, is worth no more than “a reasonable paragraph” in the book he is planning, and who has already chosen his title: The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger. A British reader in 1958, two years before Nigerian independence and still inside an imperial common sense, meets the shock structurally: two hundred pages have made Okonkwo a person with a father, a daughter, a temper and a history, and the final page hands him to an official who can see only a specimen and a paragraph. The irony works by demoting the reader's own habitual viewpoint. A Nigerian reader today comes to the paragraph already convinced of the point and reads it differently: less as a revelation than as an exhibit, evidence of how the archive of colonial administration was actually composed, and often with attention to what Achebe implies about who gets to write history rather than about whether the Commissioner is wrong. This is exactly the category of context the specification names, the way in which texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, and it is worth AO4 marks precisely because the words on the page have not changed while their force has.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The final paragraph changes whose point of view the reader is inside; start there.","Ask what an imperial readership in the year of publication would have had to give up in order to feel the irony, and what a reader who already agrees with Achebe notices instead.","One paragraph on the 1958 reading, one on the present-day reading, and a closing sentence naming the context category this belongs to."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Compare what the killing of Ikemefuna and the killing of the court messenger reveal about Okonkwo, judge which does him the greater damage, and use the difference between the clan's response to the two to say what has happened to Umuofia itself between Part One and Part Three. (The 10-mark tariff here is a practice tariff for a synoptic exercise, not a board tariff: Section C sets one 40-mark essay.)","a":"Both are acts of fear disguised as strength, but the first destroys him privately, costing him Nwoye and his own peace while the clan still stands behind its customs, and the second destroys him publicly, because this time the clan does not follow; the change in the response measures the whole distance the novel travels, from a Umuofia whose Oracle and elders still hold to one whose authority now sits in a colonial court.","sol":"The two killings rhyme deliberately. In the first, Ikemefuna has lived three years in Okonkwo's compound and calls him father; Ezeudu has warned Okonkwo to have no hand in the boy's death; and when the boy runs to him Okonkwo cuts him down because he was afraid of being thought weak. In the second, Okonkwo beheads a court messenger in front of the assembled clan, again acting before thought and again to avoid the appearance of weakness. The difference is in the response. After Ikemefuna the clan's order remains intact: Obierika reproaches him, Okonkwo cannot sleep, Nwoye turns away from him permanently, but Umuofia is still a society whose Oracle has spoken and whose rules hold. After the messenger, Achebe removes the response entirely, and the silence is the point: the other messengers escape, the crowd breaks into tumult and asks why he did it, and Okonkwo understands that Umuofia will not go to war. My judgement is that the killing of Ikemefuna does the greater damage to Okonkwo as a man, because it costs him his son and his own conviction, but the killing of the messenger is the more damaging to him as a member of the clan, because it proves that the community which made his values no longer shares them, and the hanging follows directly from that proof. Taken together the two killings measure the novel's whole arc: the same act meets a clan that still judges by its own law in Part One and a clan that will not act at all in Part Three, so the comparison is finally about Umuofia rather than about Okonkwo, and it is the context of writing, Achebe in 1958 refusing a story in which Africans destroy themselves unaided, that decides which reading the ending supports.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Look for what the two acts have in common in Okonkwo's motive before you look for what separates them.","Compare the reaction each killing receives, from the clan and from Okonkwo himself, rather than the acts alone.","Build the answer as a comparison with a judgement at the end that distinguishes personal damage from damage to his standing in the clan, then widen it: say what the change in the clan's response between Part One and Part Three shows about Umuofia, and let the context of writing decide the reading."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Explore how Achebe's use of Igbo proverbs, songs and folk tales inside an English-language novel contributes to a reader's understanding of its literary context. (Practice tariff, not a board tariff: Section C sets a single 40-mark essay.)","a":"It places the novel in two literary traditions at once: an English form carrying an Igbo oral culture, which makes Igbo speech a system of thought rather than local colour and answers European fiction that had denied African societies any intellectual life.","sol":"The novel tells us that the Igbo place a high value on the art of conversation, and that proverbs are described as “the palm-oil with which words are eaten”, and Achebe then writes a narrator who eats his own words the same way. Negotiations, quarrels and consolations proceed through proverb; Ekwefi and the children exchange folk tales, including the story of the tortoise and the birds; and Nwoye, who prefers his mother's stories to his father's tales of violence, is the character whose inner life is shaped by that oral inheritance. The literary context this creates is double. Formally the book is a European novel, in English, with the Yeats epigraph from The Second Coming at its head, so it stands inside a tradition that in 1958 largely belonged to writers like Conrad and Joyce Cary, whose Africa had scenery but no philosophy. Materially it is built out of Igbo oral forms, and untranslated Igbo words are left standing. The effect is that Igbo thought is presented as thought, complex, argued and quotable, rather than as custom to be described from outside. That is why the technique is AO4 material and not merely stylistic: it shows a writer using the coloniser's form to carry the colonised culture's intelligence, and it makes the novel's later reception, as the first title in the Heinemann African Writers Series, intelligible.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The question is about literary context, which the specification defines in terms of literary movements or genres, so think about what kind of book this is and whose tradition it belongs to.","Find the places where speech in the novel is conducted through proverb or story, then ask what a reader in 1958 would have expected an African setting in an English novel to sound like.","Explain the two traditions the novel sits in, give examples of the oral forms inside it, and finish on what the combination proves about the culture being written."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section C essay carry, and how are they split between assessment objectives?","a":"40 marks: 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4."},{"q":"Is Component 1 open book or closed book?","a":"Closed book. No texts may be taken into the examination, though the anthology poems are reproduced in the paper."},{"q":"In what year was Things Fall Apart published, and from whose poem does its title come?","a":"1958; from W. B. Yeats's poem The Second Coming."},{"q":"How long is Okonkwo's exile, and what causes it?","a":"Seven years in Mbanta, his mother's village, because his gun exploded at Ogbuefi Ezeudu's funeral and killed the dead man's son, an inadvertent killing."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Component 1, Section C: marks and assessment objectives","back":"One 40-mark essay on the modern prose set text, chosen from two. Twenty raw marks AO1, twenty AO4, and no separate credit for AO2. The section is examined closed book.","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in this specification","back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. Worth twenty of the forty marks in Section C.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO4 in this specification","back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Worth twenty of the forty marks in Section C, an equal share with AO1.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The five categories of context","back":"The writer's own life and individual situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Publication and title of Things Fall Apart","back":"Published 1958, two years before Nigerian independence. The title comes from W. B. Yeats's The Second Coming: “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”. It became the first book in the Heinemann African Writers Series.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Chinua Achebe","back":"Nigerian writer, 1930 to 2013, born in Ogidi in Igboland, eastern Nigeria, and raised in a household his father had moved into the Church Missionary Society. He wrote the novel in English.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Umuofia","back":"The fictional group of nine villages where Okonkwo lives, set in Igboland in the late nineteenth century. It is governed without kings, by elders, titled men, the masked egwugwu and the Oracle of the Hills and Caves.","code":"Component 1: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"Egwugwu","back":"Masked ancestral spirits, embodied by titled men, who hear disputes and give judgement. Enoch's unmasking of one in Part Three is the sacrilege that leads to the burning of the church.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Ikemefuna","back":"The boy given to Umuofia by Mbaino as compensation for a killing. He lives three years in Okonkwo's household and calls him father; when the Oracle decrees his death, Okonkwo takes part in killing him.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Nwoye","back":"Okonkwo's eldest son, whom his father thinks weak. Disturbed by Ikemefuna's death and by the twins left in the forest, he joins the missionaries and is disowned.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Obierika","back":"Okonkwo's friend and the novel's questioning voice, who doubts customs he still obeys. He says the white man has “put a knife on the things” which bound the clan, so that it has fallen apart.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Okonkwo's exile","back":"At Ogbuefi Ezeudu's funeral Okonkwo's gun explodes and kills the dead man's son. The killing is inadvertent, a female crime, so he is exiled to Mbanta, his mother's village, for seven years.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The District Commissioner's book","back":"The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger. He decides Okonkwo's whole life would make a reasonable paragraph, and the novel closes inside the coloniser's point of view.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"Social and cultural context in Things Fall Apart (any one)","back":"A clan governed without kings, by elders, titled men and the egwugwu; the Week of Peace and the fine Ezeani takes for breaking it; the twins left in the Evil Forest.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true},{"front":"Changes the arrival of the British brings to Umuofia (any two)","back":"A church and a body of converts that divides the clan; a District Commissioner's court and court messengers that take judgement away from the egwugwu; a trading store that gives palm-oil and kernel a new value.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question answered: To what extent do you agree that Okonkwo is responsible for his own downfall in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.","Okonkwo's downfall begins in a fear that Achebe locates long before any missionary lands: the narrator tells us that “his whole life was dominated by fear”, names failure and weakness as its objects, and points to the father whose memory he spends his life outrunning. That fear is not private neurosis but an accurate reading of what Umuofia rewards, since his people respect age but, as the narrator puts it, “achievement was revered”; the clan itself teaches him that a man is worth what he has taken. So when Ikemefuna, who has called him father for three years, runs to him for protection, the machete is not a departure from his society's values but an over-obedience to them, and Achebe gives the motive without ornament: he was afraid of being thought weak. Writing in 1958, for a readership that had learned Africa from Conrad and from Joyce Cary, Achebe is careful to place Obierika's reproach immediately afterwards, so the reader sees a culture that has a conscience Okonkwo has outrun rather than a culture that lacks one. Responsibility is therefore genuinely his, but it is a responsibility manufactured by a value system that made terror look like strength, which is why the collapse reads less as punishment than as demonstration.","Why it is Level 5: the knowledge is assured and discriminating, evidence is chosen to prove the claim rather than to show coverage, the personal engagement is assured and the critical style perceptive across the whole paragraph, and the context of writing is integrated convincingly, since removing the sentence about 1958 would break the argument rather than shorten it.","Transferable moves: open on Achebe rather than on Okonkwo; quote only what you are certain of and keep it short; let the context supply the reason a point is true; finish on a mini-judgement that refines the essay's position instead of repeating it.","A note on the label: the specification prints no level grid for Section C. The five levels used here are borrowed from the five-level grid it does print for the non-examined component, which is marked out of 30, because that is the nearest published description of quality for a 40-mark essay. “Level 5” therefore means the top band of that borrowed grid — assured knowledge and understanding, assured personal engagement and a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation, discriminating use of relevant examples, and, on the context strand of the Assignment B grid, understanding of the relationship between text and context integrated convincingly into the response — and not an official Section C band."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question answered: the same question, To what extent do you agree that Okonkwo is responsible for his own downfall in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.","Okonkwo is partly responsible for his own downfall because he is a violent and stubborn man. He beats his wife Ojiugo during the Week of Peace, which shows that he does not respect the customs of the clan, and he takes part in killing Ikemefuna even though Obierika tells him afterwards that he should not have done it. Achebe shows that Okonkwo is afraid of being weak like his father Unoka, who was lazy and owed money to everyone in the village. Chinua Achebe wrote the novel in 1958 and Nigeria became independent in 1960, and there were missionaries in Nigeria at that time. At the end Okonkwo kills the court messenger and then hangs himself, so his own temper is what destroys him in the end.","Why it is only Level 3: the knowledge is sound and the examples are clearly relevant, but the writing drifts into narrative, the evidence is retold rather than used, and the proposition is never really weighed, so there is no qualified judgement.","What holds it back most: the context is bolted on. The sentence about 1958 and independence sits in its own space and changes nothing in the reading, which is the little comment on the relationship between text and context that the bottom of the grid describes. Delete it and the paragraph loses nothing, which is the test to apply to your own writing.","How to lift it: replace the biography sentence with a reason, for example that Achebe, writing as Nigeria moved towards independence, refuses a story in which Africans destroy themselves unaided; cut the retelling of the ending; and end with a judgement that says how far you agree and why.","The same note on the label: no level grid is printed for Section C, so these levels are borrowed from the specification's five-level grid for the non-examined component, marked out of 30. “Level 3” means the middle band of that borrowed grid — sound knowledge and understanding, relevant personal engagement and an appropriate critical style, clearly relevant examples, and, on the context strand of the Assignment B grid, relevant comment on the relationship between text and context — and not an official Section C band. Note that the paragraph above does not sit in the middle band on every strand: its AO4 strand is at the bottom of the grid, on little comment on the relationship between text and context, and Level 3 is the best-fit judgement the specification asks for across the paragraph as a whole."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Things Fall Apart — Chinua Achebe","items":["Author: Chinua Achebe, Nigerian novelist, born 1930 in Ogidi in Igboland, eastern Nigeria; died 2013.","Publication: 1958, two years before Nigerian independence in 1960; later the first title in the Heinemann African Writers Series.","Title: taken from W. B. Yeats's poem The Second Coming, quoted as the epigraph: “Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold”.","Setting: Umuofia, a fictional group of nine Igbo villages in south-eastern Nigeria, in the late nineteenth century, as British missionaries and administration arrive.","Form and structure: twenty-five chapters in three parts — Umuofia before the missionaries; the seven years of exile in Mbanta; the return to a changed clan.","Narration: a third-person narrator who speaks in the idiom of the clan, carrying Igbo proverbs, songs and folk tales, and leaving Igbo words untranslated; the final page switches into the District Commissioner's point of view.","Okonkwo: a wealthy, titled farmer of Umuofia, famous as a young man for throwing the wrestler Amalinze the Cat; he rules his household with a heavy hand and has three wives.","Unoka: Okonkwo's father, a gentle, improvident man who owed money to his neighbours and loved his flute; the failure Okonkwo defines himself against.","Nwoye: Okonkwo's eldest son, drawn to his mother's stories, troubled by Ikemefuna's death and by the abandoning of twins; he joins the Christians and is disowned.","Ekwefi: Okonkwo's second wife, who left her first husband to come to him; mother of Ezinma, her only surviving child.","Ezinma: Ekwefi's daughter, an ogbanje child, the one Okonkwo loves best and of whom he thinks that she should have been a boy.","Ikemefuna: the boy handed over by Mbaino as compensation for the killing of a woman of Umuofia; he lives three years with Okonkwo and calls him father before the Oracle decrees his death.","Obierika: Okonkwo's friend, the novel's questioning intelligence, who asks why a man should “suffer so grievously” for what was an accident.","Uchendu: Okonkwo's maternal uncle in Mbanta, who teaches the exiled Okonkwo why a man belongs to his fatherland in prosperity but is taken in by his motherland in sorrow.","Chielo: an ordinary widow by day and the priestess of Agbala, the Oracle of the Hills and Caves; she carries Ezinma through the night to the caves.","Mr Brown: the first missionary at Umuofia, who learns the clan's beliefs, argues with Akunna, builds a school and a hospital and restrains his converts; the Reverend James Smith, who replaces him, sees things in black and white.","Enoch: the zealous convert whose unmasking of an egwugwu provokes the burning of the church.","The District Commissioner: the colonial officer whose court and messengers replace the clan's justice, and who closes the novel planning The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger.","Plot, Part One in order: Okonkwo's rise from Unoka's debts; Ikemefuna handed over by Mbaino and placed in Okonkwo's household; the beating of Ojiugo in the Week of Peace and the fine paid to Ezeani, priest of Ani; three years after that arrival, the killing of Ikemefuna; Ezinma's illness and Chielo's night journey to the caves; the betrothal and feast at Obierika's compound; Ogbuefi Ezeudu's funeral, the exploding gun and exile.","Plot, Part Two in order: arrival at Mbanta, his mother's village; Uchendu's teaching on the motherland; Obierika's visit in the second year of the exile with the news that Abame has been wiped out after a white man was killed there; the missionaries' arrival in Mbanta and the grant of land in the Evil Forest; Nwoye's conversion and his father's disowning of him; the farewell feast at the end of the seven years.","Plot, Part Three in order: return to a Umuofia that now has a church, a court and a trading store; Enoch unmasks an egwugwu; the church is burnt; six leaders, Okonkwo among them, are seized at a meeting and held until a fine is paid; at the marketplace meeting Okonkwo beheads a court messenger and the others escape; Okonkwo hangs himself; Obierika rebukes the Commissioner; the Commissioner plans his paragraph.","Themes an examiner rewards: masculinity built on the fear of weakness; the individual against the clan; change and the limits of tradition; language, proverb and who tells the story; religion and the appeal of a new one; justice, crime and punishment.","Context, the writer's situation: Achebe grew up between Igbo tradition and a Christian convert household, and wrote in English while arguing that an African writer could make the language carry African experience.","Context, historical setting: British missionary and administrative expansion in south-eastern Nigeria in the late nineteenth century, with courts, messengers and trade replacing the authority of elders and oracles.","Context, social and cultural: a decentralised society with no king, governed by elders, titled men, the egwugwu and the Oracle; the Week of Peace; the exposure of twins; the osu, outcasts the church later admits.","Context, literary: an English-language novel built from Igbo oral forms, written against European fictions of Africa such as Conrad's Heart of Darkness and Joyce Cary's Mister Johnson.","Context, reception: read in 1958 by a largely European readership as a corrective, and read now as a founding African novel, so the closing paragraph's irony lands differently in each case.","Key moments to hold in memory, in chapter order: the fine after the Week of Peace; the killing of Ikemefuna; the egwugwu hearing Uzowulu's case; Chielo carrying Ezinma; the gun at Ezeudu's funeral; the destruction of Abame; Nwoye hearing the hymn; the unmasking of the egwugwu; the burning of the church; the seizure of the six leaders; the beheading of the messenger; Okonkwo's body in the tree; the Commissioner's title."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: exam conditions and edition for Section C","items":["Component 1 is a written examination of two hours, 90 raw marks, 60% of the International GCSE, available on paper or onscreen.","Section C – Modern Prose is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts; in the breakdown of marks these are Questions 4 to 17.","The section assesses AO1 and AO4 only, at twenty raw marks each; there is no AO2 or AO3 credit in Section C.","Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into the examination; only the anthology poems for Section B are provided in the paper.","Because it is closed book there are no prescribed editions of the set texts; the recommended edition of Things Fall Apart is Penguin Red Classics, January 2006, ISBN 9780141023380.","Timing: forty of the paper's ninety marks means about fifty-three minutes, of which roughly five should go on choosing between the two questions and planning.","For this component learners study two set texts: all the poems in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one modern prose set text, here Things Fall Apart.","The qualification is graded on a nine-grade scale from 9 to 1; individual components are not graded.","A note on the question stems printed in this kit: the specification prints model stems only under Component 3, in the “Setting the question” guidance for Assignment A and Assignment B of the non-examined assessment; Component 2 prints no stems at all. The Assignment B stems carry AO2 and read “You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel”. Section C carries no AO2, so the stems used here drop that clause and read “You must refer to the context of the novel”. That wording is inferred from the assessment objectives, not quoted from the specification, so practise the skills the instruction pays for rather than memorising the sentence."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — “How does Achebe portray the coming of the white man to Umuofia in Things Fall Apart? You must refer to the context of the novel.” (40 marks)","items":["Decoded: “How does Achebe portray” asks for the writer's choices and methods, not the sequence of events, so every paragraph names a way of portraying. “The coming of the white man” covers the missionaries, the court and the trading store together, not the missionaries alone. “You must refer to the context of the novel” is the instruction that pays the twenty AO4 marks.","Position to argue, written down before paragraph one: Achebe portrays the coming of the white man as quiet, patient and lawful rather than violent, so that the clan is divided before it is conquered.","Paragraph 1 — before anything happens in Umuofia: the clan's first knowledge of what the white man can do is the destruction of Abame in Chapter 15, the first violent encounter with white men that the novel reports, and it reaches Okonkwo in exile secondhand through Obierika. White men themselves were known of earlier, joked about among the men at Obierika's compound in Chapter 8, so what is new here is not their existence but what they do. Achebe therefore introduces colonial power as rumour and as massacre at a distance. Say plainly that Abame and Mbanta lie outside Umuofia and are being used as the parallel case the novel supplies; the point about Umuofia is that this is the reputation the quiet arrival there has to be read against. Context: the historical setting of punitive expeditions of the period.","Paragraph 2 — the arrival Umuofia does not see happen: while Okonkwo is away Obierika reports that the missionaries have come to Umuofia, built their church, won a handful of converts and begun sending evangelists to the villages round about, so Achebe portrays the change as accomplished quietly, off the page, in the gap the exile opens. The long Mbanta sequence — the Evil Forest granted, Mr Kiaga admitting the osu, Nwoye's conversion — is the same process shown at length, and should be cited as the parallel case, not as Umuofia. Context: social and cultural, a religion whose authority depends on shared belief.","Paragraph 3 — the two missionaries at Umuofia: Mr Brown learns the clan's language of belief, argues with Akunna, restrains his converts and builds a school and a hospital, and the clan honours him; the Reverend James Smith, who replaces him, sees things in black and white, and the change of man changes the outcome, since it is under Smith that Enoch unmasks an egwugwu and the church is burnt. Context: the writer's own situation, a childhood between the clan and a Christian convert household, so conversion is presented from inside rather than judged from outside.","Paragraph 4 — law replaces judgement in Umuofia: the District Commissioner's court, his court messengers, and the seizure, shaving and humiliation of the six leaders take justice away from the egwugwu, so that portrayal shifts from persuasion to force. Context: historical setting, colonial administration and the native court system.","Paragraph 5 — trade and the last portrayal: the trading store makes palm-oil and kernel things of great price and money flows into Umuofia, which buys the clan's acquiescence; then at the marketplace meeting Okonkwo beheads a court messenger and the others let the rest escape, and the final page hands the portrayal to the Commissioner himself. Context: historical setting and reception, since the Commissioner's view of the scene is the European account Achebe is writing against.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: is the clan destroyed from outside or opened from within? Return to it each time by asking who chose, and what the clan had already excluded.","Conclusion must: answer the question rather than summarise, stating that Achebe portrays the coming of the white man as an occupation of a vacancy the clan had made; cite Obierika's judgement that the white man has “put a knife on the things” which bound the clan together, so that it has fallen apart; and close on the District Commissioner's planned paragraph, so that the last portrayal of the white man in the essay is the novel's own.","Timing: about five minutes to choose and plan, about forty-three to write five paragraphs and a conclusion, about five to check names and quotations."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Quotation bank you can use with confidence","items":["“Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold” — W. B. Yeats, The Second Coming, the novel's epigraph and the source of its title.","“well known throughout the nine villages” — from the novel's opening sentence on Okonkwo's fame, which reaches beyond them; use for reputation and for the size of the world the novel starts in.","“no patience with unsuccessful men” — on Okonkwo's values and his contempt for his father.","“achievement was revered” — the narrator sets this against the mere respect owed to age: the clan's scale of value, and the reason a son can outgrow a father.","“his whole life was dominated by fear” — the narrator's direct statement of cause, and he names the fear as one of failure and of weakness; the single most useful line for any question about Okonkwo.","“ruled his household with a heavy hand” — for the treatment of his wives and of Nwoye.","“afraid of being thought weak” — the motive given at the killing of Ikemefuna.","The image of living fire and “cold, impotent ash” — Okonkwo's own thought about Nwoye in Chapter 17, during the exile; the fire is Okonkwo and the ash is what such a fire begets, and it is narrated, not said to Nwoye.","“the palm-oil with which words are eaten” — the narrator's words in Chapter 1 about proverbs; the clause sits inside a sentence on the high value the Igbo place on the art of conversation; it is not spoken by any character, so attribute it to the narrator. Useful for literary context.","“put a knife on the things” — Obierika's image for what the white man has done to the ties that held the clan together, so that it has fallen apart; the novel's own thesis and the sentence the title answers.","“one of the greatest men in Umuofia” — Obierika to the District Commissioner over Okonkwo's body.","The District Commissioner's chosen title, The Pacification of the Primitive Tribes of the Lower Niger — the last words of the novel.","Rule for the examination: if you are not certain of a line, do not quote it. Describe the moment instead — “when Ikemefuna runs to Okonkwo calling him father” — which scores for close knowledge just as well and cannot be wrong."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:12:34Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Fourth examination, read whole and fresh against the novel and the specification map. The three substantive fixes from the third verdict have landed and landed correctly: extras[4].items[2] now reads 'the clan's first knowledge of what the white man can do', names Abame (Chapter 15) as the first violent encounter the novel reports, says it reaches Okonkwo in exile through Obierika, and adds that white men were already joked about at Obierika's compound in Chapter 8 - all accurate, including the chalk and the leper Amadi behind that joke; room.questions[8].sol now names the agent, with Okonkwo hanging himself from a tree behind his compound and the suicide, as an offence against the Earth, making the body an abomination that only strangers may touch; and room.questions[11] and [12] now say in the stem that their tariffs are practice tariffs, not board tariffs. On copyright I ran my own scan rather than trusting the reported one: every quoted run in all 340 strings is seven words or fewer (the longer quoted strings are exam stems, AO wording and the Yeats epigraph, none of them Achebe's prose), and I read every sol, card, model paragraph, fact-file item and worked example for unquoted verbatim runs. All eight locations the third verdict named are now clean - the fear sentence, Obierika's knife sentence, the opening sentence, the age/achievement line, the palm-oil clause, 'suffer so grievously' and the Commissioner's reflection are each down to a bank-licensed phrase of seven words or fewer, and 'he was afraid of being thought weak' sits at exactly seven. One run the trim pass never looked at survives, Uchendu's fatherland/motherland teaching at about ten words in questions[6].sol, which is the only over-length reproduction left in the kit. I checked roughly thirty-five quotations and reproductions and found no misquotation and no invented fact: Achebe 1930-2013, Ogidi, the CMS household, 1958, independence 1960, the Heinemann African Writers Series, twenty-five chapters in three parts, Amalinze the Cat, Ezeani and the Week of Peace fine, Chielo and Ezinma, the exploding gun and the female crime, three years of Ikemefuna, Ezeudu's warning, the Evil Forest grant, the efulefu, the mothers of twins, Mr Kiaga and the osu, Mr Brown and Akunna, James Smith, Enoch, the six leaders, the messenger, the trading store, and Obierika's 'one of the greatest men in Umuofia' over the body - every one correct, correctly attributed and correctly placed. The three 'in order' plot lists and the chapter-order list in extras[2] items 18 to 20 and 27 are right end to end, the borrowed five-level grid is quoted accurately and honestly flagged as borrowed from the non-examined component, questions[9]'s comparison with Component 2 Section B matches the specification's mark splits and open-book conditions, and every answer key agrees with its sol, so 'wrong' is empty. This is above the publish line: what remains is one clause of Achebe to paraphrase and two stems to label."}}},"prose-klara-and-the-sun":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-klara-and-the-sun","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Klara and the Sun is your modern prose text, and it is examined in one place only: Section C of Component 1, where you write one 40-mark essay chosen from two. That single essay is 40 of the paper’s 90 raw marks, so it is worth a little over a quarter of the whole International GCSE. The marks split exactly in half — 20 for AO1, close knowledge and a critical style with informed personal engagement, and 20 for AO4, the relationship between the novel and the context in which it was written. There is no AO2 credit here at all, and the examination is closed book, so everything you use must be carried in your memory. This room builds the two things that are actually rewarded: an accurate store of moments from the novel that you can reach for under pressure, and the habit of arguing with context rather than bolting it on at the end of a paragraph.","idea":[{"h":"Your one modern prose text, studied whole","t":"The specification says learners study two set texts for this component: all the poems from Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one of the modern prose set texts. Klara and the Sun is one of seven, and it is new — Klara and the Sun and Western Lane are for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026, so there is very little past-paper material and the examiner will be reading a great many first attempts. Teaching must focus on the study of whole texts, which is why Section C questions always reach across the book rather than sitting on one scene. A top-level answer therefore shows the whole shape of the novel in miniature: it draws moments from the store, from the house, from the city and from the ending, and makes them argue for one position rather than retelling the plot in order.","code":"Component 1: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, a personal position","t":"AO1 asks you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, and the section’s own list is blunt: demonstrate a close understanding of your prose text; maintain a critical style; present an informed personal engagement; identify and use relevant examples from your prose text. That is 20 of the 40 marks. Because the examination is closed book, an example may be a precisely described moment rather than a quotation — a half-remembered line that turns out to be wrong helps nobody, while the Mother asking Klara to walk as Josie walks at Morgan’s Falls is exact, checkable and yours. A top-level answer uses short, precise references, never narrates, and keeps a first-person critical voice running through the essay, so the examiner hears a reader thinking rather than a summary being delivered.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"},{"h":"AO4: context is half the marks, not the last sentence","t":"The specification says learners should also understand the relationship between their prose text and the context in which it was written, and must understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. AO4 carries 20 of the 40 marks — an equal share with AO1 — so context is half the mark on every Klara and the Sun question. That is why a paragraph that ends with a bolted-on sentence about genetic engineering throws away marks: the grid’s top band asks for understanding of the relationship between text and context to be integrated convincingly into the response. A top-level answer uses context as the reason a moment means what you say it means: Rick’s exclusion from almost every college is not merely sad, it is what a society that sells advantage to parents does to the children of parents who refused or could not pay.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"},{"h":"The five kinds of context — and which ones this novel pays back","t":"The specification names five categories: the writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, for example attitudes in society and expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times. Klara and the Sun pays back the last four richly — a near-future society of genetic lifting and automation, a society sorted by paid-for advantage, a literary line of unreliable and self-effacing Ishiguro narrators, and a novel published in March 2021 that reads differently now that talking machines are ordinary. The biography pays back least, and the specification’s own phrase, only where these relate to the text, is your warning. A top-level answer names the category it is using and then never mentions it again, because the argument carries it.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"The question, the clock and the choice","t":"Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, assessing AO1 and AO4; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4. Component 1 is two hours for 90 marks, so Section C is worth about 53 minutes of it, and the component is a closed book examination in which learners may not take texts in, although the anthology poems are provided. Expect the board’s own stems: In what ways does…, How does…, and a proposition followed by To what extent do you agree? Spend two or three minutes choosing between the two questions and planning four paragraphs, and choose the question you have moments for, not the one with the nicer theme. A top-level answer states its position in the opening sentence, keeps four paragraphs of roughly equal weight, and ends with a judgement that answers the question rather than a summary of what it has said.","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"}],"examples":[{"title":"From a moment to an AO1 point: the request at Morgan’s Falls","setup":"The question is: In what ways does Ishiguro present the Mother in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. It carries 40 marks, split 20 AO1 and 20 AO4. The moment you have chosen is the outing to Morgan’s Falls: Josie is too unwell to come, so the Mother takes Klara instead, and there asks Klara to walk as Josie walks and then to speak in Josie’s voice, watching rather than joining in. Build one paragraph from this moment.","steps":["Lead with a claim about the woman, not the trip: she is trying to keep her daughter by rehearsing a replacement for her, while refusing to call it that.","Anchor it in the precise detail of the request — first the walk, then the voice, and the Mother standing back to watch the performance she has asked for.","Draw out what the detail proves: an outing meant as a treat has become an audition, so her love and her plan to substitute cannot be separated, and the reader understands this long before Klara does.","Fold the context into the same argument: a woman who has already lost an elder daughter, in a society where machines are sold as companions and people are replaced at work, has been taught that a substitute is a reasonable answer to grief.","Finish with a mini-judgement that answers the question: Ishiguro presents the Mother not as a villain but as a grief that her society has given the wrong tools to."],"cues":["Is this paragraph about the Mother or about an outing?","Which two things does she ask for, and in which order?","Why does asking for the walk and the voice matter more than asking for company?","Which fact about her past, and which fact about her society, make the request comprehensible?","What is your one-sentence verdict on how she is presented?"]},{"title":"Weaving AO4 in instead of bolting it on","setup":"A student has ended a paragraph about Rick like this: “This links to context because people worried about genetic engineering, and Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki and later moved to Britain.” The question is: How does Ishiguro present inequality in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. It carries 40 marks, split 20 AO1 and 20 AO4, and the top band wants context integrated convincingly. Rewrite the contextual material so that it earns AO4 marks.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: the sentence is a bolt-on, and the biography has no connection to Rick, which the specification’s own phrase about the writer’s life — only where these relate to the text — rules out here.","Choose the category the moment actually needs: social and cultural contexts, meaning attitudes in society and expectations of different groups.","Name the textual fact that context has to explain: Rick has not been lifted, so almost every college is closed to him, and Atlas Brookings keeps only a small number of places for students like him.","Fuse the two into one sentence of argument: in a society where parents buy their children’s futures by editing them, a boy whose parent did not is not unlucky but sorted, and Ishiguro makes the sorting look like ordinary administration.","Test the rewrite: cross out the contextual clause and see whether the sentence still says the same thing — if it does, the context is decoration and must be rewritten again."],"cues":["What exactly is wrong with the student’s sentence?","Which of the five categories does this moment call for?","What is the hard fact about Rick that the context has to explain?","How would you say both things in one sentence rather than two?","How can you check that the context is doing work?"]},{"title":"Decoding a 40-mark question and choosing four moments","setup":"You open the paper and choose this question: In what ways does Ishiguro present faith in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. Component 1 is two hours for 90 marks, of which this essay is 40, so you have about 53 minutes and should plan for four paragraphs. Decide the line of argument and the four moments before you write a word.","steps":["Read the stem: in what ways asks how the presentation is achieved, so every paragraph must be about a method of presenting belief, not a stage of the plot.","Fix one arguable position: Klara’s sun-worship is built exactly like a religion — observation, then doctrine, then a bargain, then a sacrifice, then an apparent miracle — and Ishiguro keeps the miracle unprovable.","Choose four moments that follow that spine: Beggar Man and his dog seeming to be revived by morning light outside the store; Klara’s first approach to the barn to make her offer; the destruction of the Cootings Machine with fluid taken from her own head; the light that fills Josie’s room at the crisis.","Set the evaluation thread you will carry through all four: nothing in the novel settles whether the Sun heals Josie or whether Klara is a believer finding patterns, and Ishiguro withholds the answer deliberately.","Decide what the conclusion must do: answer the question with a qualified judgement — faith is presented as a machine’s reasoning taken to its logical end, which is why it is not ridiculous."],"cues":["What does the phrase in what ways demand that a theme-list would miss?","What single claim about faith could you defend for four paragraphs?","Which four moments fall in the right order to prove that claim?","What is the question the novel refuses to settle?","What must your last paragraph do that your first did not?"]},{"title":"Turning narrative into a critical style","setup":"A student has written this in answer to: In what ways does Ishiguro present Josie in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. The paragraph reads: “At the interaction meeting the lifted children arrive and they talk about Klara. One of the boys says she should be thrown across the room to see whether she lands on her feet. Josie does not stop him straight away. Then the meeting ends and everyone goes home.” Rewrite it so it shows a critical style and an informed personal engagement.","steps":["Find the claim buried in the retelling: Josie’s silence, not the boy’s cruelty, is the thing worth arguing about.","Put that claim in the first sentence, so the paragraph opens with a position rather than an event.","Keep only the detail that proves it — the proposal to throw Klara, and the pause before Josie speaks — and cut the arrivals and the departures.","Develop the reasoning: Josie is bought by her own social world for a moment, and because Klara reports it without complaint the reader has to supply the anger the narrator does not feel.","Add the mini-judgement and the context in one move: Ishiguro presents Josie as a girl whose kindness survives but is taxed by the group of lifted children her mother’s choice has placed her among."],"cues":["Which sentence in the student’s paragraph hides an argument?","Where does that argument have to go?","Which two details survive the cut, and which go?","Why does Klara’s calm reporting change the reader’s response?","What judgement about Josie does the moment support?"]},{"title":"Using reception context without writing about technology","setup":"The specification’s fifth category of context is the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the 21st century from when it was written. Your question is: To what extent does Ishiguro make the reader sympathise with Klara? You must refer to the context of the novel, for 40 marks. The moment you will use is the ending, in which Klara sits among discarded machines in the Yard, still able to order her memories, and does not complain. Write one paragraph that uses reception context without drifting into an essay about modern technology.","steps":["Pick the single detail that will carry the sympathy: her final effort goes into arranging what she holds of a girl who is gone, and nobody is asked to pity her for it.","Say what the novel’s first readers brought to that page: it was published in March 2021, when a machine with an inner life was still a thought experiment for most readers.","Say what a reader brings to the same page now, when talking to machines is an ordinary daily act, so Klara’s contentment feels like a claim the reader must accept or refuse.","Make the sympathy argument out of that gap: Ishiguro gives Klara no self-pity at all, so the reader’s pity is volunteered, and what the reader feels has changed while the page has not.","Guard the paragraph: every sentence still names something in the novel, and the word used for the present day appears once."],"cues":["Which detail of the ending will you build on?","What did a reader in the year of publication not yet have?","What does a reader now bring that changes the same scene?","Where does the sympathy actually come from, Klara or the reader?","How do you stop this becoming an essay about artificial intelligence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Klara and the Sun essay carry, and how are they divided?","a":"40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4. There is no AO2 credit in Section C."},{"q":"Why is a half-remembered quotation a poor risk in this section?","a":"The examination is closed book and the section gives no AO2 marks, so a precisely described moment scores as an example just as well and cannot be got wrong."},{"q":"Which of the five categories of context does the specification most restrict?","a":"The writer’s own life and individual situation, which is to be used only where it relates to the text."},{"q":"What makes a context sentence AO4-worthy rather than decorative?","a":"It has to be the reason a moment in the novel means what you claim; if the sentence can be deleted without changing the point, it is decoration."},{"q":"What should the opening sentence of a Section C essay do?","a":"State the position the whole essay will defend, in answer to the question asked, rather than introduce the novel."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section C of Component 1 is one 40-mark essay from a choice of two on Klara and the Sun: 20 marks AO1, 20 marks AO4, no AO2 credit.","Component 1 is a two-hour, 90-mark closed book paper worth 60% of the International GCSE; texts may not be taken in, though the anthology poems are provided.","The five categories of context: the writer’s life and situation only where it relates to the text; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times.","Klara is a solar-powered Artificial Friend, a B2 model, and she narrates the whole novel in the first person.","Lifted means genetically edited for advantage: Josie is lifted and becomes seriously ill; Rick is not lifted, and Atlas Brookings is almost the only college that keeps places for students like him.","Klara’s bargain with the Sun: the Cootings Machine, the source of the pollution she watched from the store window, is to be destroyed if Josie is healed — and on the city trip the Father, at Klara’s request, draws P-E-G Nine solution from her head and puts it into the machine.","Mr Capaldi’s portrait of Josie is in fact an Artificial Friend body, which the Mother intends Klara to inhabit and animate should Josie die.","Kazuo Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki in 1954 and moved to Britain in 1960; he won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2017, and Klara and the Sun, published in 2021, was his first novel after it.","The novel ends with Klara in the Yard among discarded machines, concluding that she could never have continued Josie, because what made Josie special lay in the hearts of those who loved her."],"questions":[{"q":"Recall drill, not a board tariff — Section C sets one 40-mark essay from a choice of two, and nothing shorter. Demonstrate a close knowledge of Klara and the Sun: what does AF stand for, and how is Klara powered? (2 practice marks)","a":"Artificial Friend; Klara runs on the Sun’s light, that is, she is solar-powered.","sol":"AF stands for Artificial Friend, the name given to the machine companions sold to children in the novel. Klara is solar-powered, which is why she watches the Sun’s progress across the sky so closely and why she treats the Sun as the giver of nourishment — the fact that keeps her alive becomes the fact she builds a religion on.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The abbreviation is used on the very first pages, in the store.","Think about what Klara needs in order to keep working, and why the window matters to her.","Give the two words behind the letters, then the source of her energy."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall drill, not a board tariff — Section C sets one 40-mark essay from a choice of two, and nothing shorter. Show understanding of how that essay is marked: how many marks does it carry, and how are they divided between the assessment objectives? (3 practice marks)","a":"40 marks in total: 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4; no marks for AO2 or AO3.","sol":"Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and it assesses AO1 and AO4 only. The 40 raw marks are divided equally: 20 for AO1, close knowledge and understanding with a critical style and informed personal engagement, and 20 for AO4, understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Nothing here is awarded for AO2 analysis of language, form and structure, and nothing for AO3 comparison.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at which objectives the specification says this section assesses.","Go to the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks, and find the row for Questions 4 to 17 — that row is where this section’s split is set out.","Give the total first, then the split, then say which objectives are absent."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Recall drill, not a board tariff — Section C sets one 40-mark essay from a choice of two, and nothing shorter. Demonstrate a close knowledge of Klara and the Sun: what does it mean to say that Josie has been lifted, and what has it cost her? (3 practice marks)","a":"Lifting is elective genetic editing that gives a child academic advantage and access to university; Josie has been lifted and has become seriously ill as a result.","sol":"To be lifted is to have been genetically edited as a child so as to be academically advantaged, and in the society of the novel it is effectively the entry ticket to higher education. Josie has been lifted, and the price is her illness: she weakens by stages through the book and her mother has already lost an elder daughter, Sal. The cost is therefore double — the physical danger to Josie herself, and the knowledge, never stated openly by the Mother, that the risk was taken deliberately for her sake.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The word is used as though it were ordinary, which is part of the point.","Ask what lifting buys a child, and what it seems to have done to Josie’s body.","Define the term first, then name the consequence the novel shows."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Recall drill, not a board tariff — Section C sets one 40-mark essay from a choice of two, and nothing shorter. Demonstrate a close knowledge of Klara and the Sun: what two things does the Mother ask Klara to do during the outing to Morgan’s Falls, and what does the request reveal? (3 practice marks)","a":"To walk as Josie walks and to speak in Josie’s voice; it reveals that she is already testing Klara as a replacement for her daughter.","sol":"At Morgan’s Falls, with Josie too unwell to come, the Mother asks Klara first to walk as Josie walks and then to speak in Josie’s voice, and she watches rather than taking part. The request turns an outing into an audition. It reveals that the Mother is already preparing for Josie’s death and has decided that a copy is a tolerable answer to it, and it plants the idea long before Mr Capaldi’s studio explains it, so that the reader understands the plan before Klara does.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Josie is not with them, and that absence is the reason for the request.","Notice what the Mother does while Klara performs — she watches rather than joins in.","Answer in two parts: the two requests in the order they come, then one sentence on what they add up to."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall drill, not a board tariff — Section C sets one 40-mark essay from a choice of two, and nothing shorter. Demonstrate a close knowledge of Klara and the Sun: what is the Cootings Machine, and why does Klara want it destroyed? (4 practice marks)","a":"A machine whose work fills the street with pollution, blocking the Sun; it is destroyed with P-E-G Nine from Klara’s head as her side of a bargain for Josie’s healing.","sol":"The Cootings Machine is the road machine Klara watches from the store window, whose smoke darkens the street and blots out the Sun. Because Klara believes the Sun gives a special nourishment, and because she has seen Beggar Man and his dog appear lifeless and then seem to be restored by morning light, she reasons that the Sun withholds its help where the air is poisoned. She therefore offers a bargain: the machine is to be destroyed if the Sun will heal Josie. Klara plans the act and asks the Father for help, and it is carried out on the city trip — the Father, an engineer, draws the P-E-G Nine solution from inside her head and puts it into the machine, with Klara consenting although she is told the loss may impair her. The two of them agree to keep it from Josie and the Mother. Her despair when another such machine appears shows how completely she had believed in the exchange.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["She first sees it from the store, long before she can do anything about it.","Connect it to what she believes the Sun does, and to what she thinks stops the Sun helping.","Say what the machine does, then what Klara offers to give up to stop it."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ishiguro present Klara in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Klara is presented through the gap between her observation and her understanding: a machine built to watch, whose goodness is total, whose reasoning is faulty, and whose sacrifice costs her more than anyone notices.","sol":"Ishiguro presents Klara as a narrator whose limits are the point of her: she sees more than anyone and understands less, and the reader is placed in the space between. From the store window she learns the world in pieces — the street, Beggar Man and his dog, the Sun going down behind a tall building — and from that small sample she builds a doctrine about the Sun’s nourishment. Her attention is scrupulous and her inference is wrong, and Ishiguro never lets the two come apart. In the house she is presented through service: she measures rooms by where the Sun’s light falls rather than by her own comfort, she records Melania Housekeeper’s hostility without resentment, and she consents to the Mother’s request at Morgan’s Falls without asking what it is for. The sacrifice is the moment her goodness becomes costly rather than decorative: she asks the Father to draw the P-E-G Nine solution from inside her head and put it into the Cootings Machine, consenting to a loss she is warned may impair her, and the two of them then keep it from Josie, the Mother and Mr Capaldi — so the one person she serves most never learns what she paid, and the reader is left holding the knowledge alone. Context sharpens this. Ishiguro’s narrators have always served systems they cannot see, from the butler of The Remains of the Day to the donor of Never Let Me Go, and Klara belongs to that line; readers in 2021 met her as a thought experiment, where readers now, familiar with machines that talk back, must decide what her inner life amounts to. The ending completes the presentation: left in the Yard among discarded machines, she orders her memories of Josie and does not complain. Ishiguro presents Klara, finally, as better than the people who bought her — and the discomfort of the novel is that her goodness costs them nothing at all.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what Klara can see and what she cannot work out.","Take four moments spread across the book — store, house, city, Yard — and let each prove a different part of one claim.","Open with the claim you intend to defend in a single sentence, and let the first moment begin the argument rather than the plot."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Ishiguro present the friendship between Josie and Rick in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"As a childhood contract their society will not let them keep: the two are equals in private and unequal in every public arrangement, and the novel lets the inequality, not a quarrel, part them.","sol":"Ishiguro presents Josie and Rick as two children who have made a private agreement about their future in a society that has already decided it for them. In private they are equals: they have grown up over the same fields, they plan a life together, and Rick’s practical brilliance with machines is treated by both as ordinary. In public the arrangement collapses. At the interaction meeting, among the lifted children her mother’s choice has placed her among, Josie hesitates before defending him, and Ishiguro makes that pause, rather than any cruelty of hers, the wound. The social context does the work here: Josie has been lifted and Rick has not, so almost every college is closed to him and Atlas Brookings, which keeps a small number of places for unlifted students, becomes his one narrow door — a society that sells advantage to parents sorts children before they can consent. What keeps the friendship morally alive is what Rick does rather than says: he takes Klara out towards the barn when she cannot get there alone, an act of love performed for Josie through a machine, and asking nothing. Ishiguro withholds the romantic ending that the childhood plan promises; as Josie grows well and leaves for college, their paths separate without bitterness. The presentation is therefore elegiac rather than tragic: the friendship is real, and it is defeated not by feeling but by arrangement, which is a harsher judgement on the world of the novel than a betrayal would have been.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what separates them, and notice that it is not a quarrel.","Contrast what they are to each other in private with what the arrangements around them permit in public.","Decide first whether the friendship fails or is defeated, and build every paragraph towards that word."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ishiguro present the Mother in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"As love and calculation fused: a woman already bereaved of one daughter who commissions a replacement for the other, presented with sympathy because her society taught her that substitution is reasonable.","sol":"Ishiguro presents the Mother as a woman whose love for Josie cannot be separated from her plan to replace her, and he refuses to let the reader dismiss her. The first sign is at Morgan’s Falls, where, with Josie too ill to come, she asks Klara to walk as Josie walks and then to speak in Josie’s voice — a treat turned into an audition, and the reader grasps its purpose long before Klara does. Mr Capaldi’s studio completes the picture: the portrait is an Artificial Friend body, and Klara is to inhabit it and continue Josie. That the Mother has commissioned this while her daughter still lives is the novel’s coldest fact, and Ishiguro places it beside her exhaustion, her hard driving, her long hours and the elder daughter, Sal, she has already lost. The context is what makes her comprehensible rather than monstrous. She lives in a society that sells machine companions to lonely children, that replaces workers with machines — her own husband among them — and that invites parents to buy their children’s prospects by lifting them, at a known risk she accepted twice. In such a world, a substitute looks like a solution rather than a blasphemy. Ishiguro’s method is to give her no self-justifying speech; we see her decisions and are left to judge them. The judgement the novel earns is a hard one: the Mother is presented not as a woman who loves her daughter too little but as one who has been taught to believe that love can be transferred, and the final rebuke to her comes from Klara, who concludes that what was special in Josie was never inside her to be copied.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Notice where her grief and her planning are doing the same thing at the same time.","Use Morgan’s Falls and the studio as two stages of one decision, and set the loss of Sal beside them.","Take a position on whether she is to be blamed, and keep testing it against what her society offers her."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ishiguro present faith in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Faith is presented as a machine’s empirical reasoning carried to its logical end — observation, doctrine, bargain, sacrifice, apparent miracle — with the miracle left unprovable.","sol":"Ishiguro presents faith not as foolishness but as reasoning that has run out of evidence and kept going. Klara’s religion has a perfectly respectable empirical beginning: she is solar-powered, so the Sun is demonstrably her source of life, and from the store window she watches it cross the sky each day. From that she draws a doctrine — that the Sun gives a special nourishment — and she confirms it with the one piece of evidence she has, Beggar Man and his dog appearing lifeless one evening and seeming restored by the morning light. Every step is a reasonable inference from an unreasonably small sample, which is exactly how belief is built. Then come the religious forms in order: a place of worship in the barn where she goes to make her request, a bargain offered to the Sun, and a sacrifice paid in advance when she asks the Father to draw the P-E-G Nine solution from inside her head and put it into the Cootings Machine, accepting damage to herself as the price of the exchange. The despair she feels when another such machine appears is the despair of the believer whose offering seems not to have been accepted. At the crisis, light floods Josie’s room and Josie recovers; Ishiguro leaves the causation permanently open. Contextually, the novel sits in a literary line of narrators whose limited understanding creates the reader’s irony, and it was published in 2021 into a culture arguing about what machines can be said to believe. The judgement is that faith here is presented with respect: Klara’s reasoning is better than most human reasoning in the novel, and the fact that we cannot prove her wrong is the point Ishiguro is making about our own convictions.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Start from why the Sun matters to Klara physically before it matters to her spiritually.","Follow the shape of a religion through the book — evidence, doctrine, place, bargain, sacrifice, answer.","Decide what the novel does with the healing, and let that decision govern your conclusion."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Ishiguro present loneliness in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Loneliness is presented as manufactured by the society and then sold back to it as a product: children taught at home need arranged meetings and bought friends, and every adult is isolated too.","sol":"Ishiguro presents loneliness as something the society of the novel produces and then sells a remedy for. Children are taught at home on their oblongs, so that friendship has to be arranged: the interaction meeting is a social occasion organised by adults, and it goes badly precisely because it is organised. Into that gap the Artificial Friend is sold — a companion manufactured to solve a problem manufactured elsewhere — and Klara’s own account of the store, waiting to be chosen and watching children pass, quietly establishes that the loneliness runs in both directions. The adults are no better placed. The Mother works long hours and drives hard and has lost one daughter already; the Father has been substituted from his work and lives among others like him, keeping to themselves; Rick and his mother are shut out of the world their neighbours move in. Ishiguro’s method is to show isolation as ordinary arrangement rather than as tragedy, which is why the novel feels cold before it feels sad. The social context is inseparable from this: a society that automates work, educates children privately at screens, and sorts them by whether they have been lifted, will produce exactly these separations, and it has a market ready for each of them. The ending seals the argument. Klara is left in the Yard among discarded machines, having solved someone else’s loneliness for a while and been kept no longer than she was useful. My judgement is that loneliness in this novel is not a feeling characters have but a condition their world manufactures, and the machines sold to cure it are the clearest proof of the diagnosis.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask who in this novel is not alone, and notice how short the list is.","Look at how children are educated and how their friendships have to be arranged.","Decide whether loneliness here is a private feeling or a social product, and argue the harder of the two."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Stretch — whole-text evaluation, and the literary context of Ishiguro’s other novels. “In Klara and the Sun, Ishiguro is more interested in what makes a person unique than in what machines can do.” To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Largely agree: the machinery is the instrument of the question, not its subject, and the novel’s argument is settled by Klara’s closing conclusion about where a person’s uniqueness lies.","sol":"I largely agree, though the two interests cannot be separated as cleanly as the proposition suggests. The novel’s central question is put by Mr Capaldi, who holds that there is nothing inside Josie that cannot be learned and continued, and the Mother’s commission — a portrait that is in fact an Artificial Friend body for Klara to inhabit — is that belief put into practice. Everything technical in the book exists to make this question answerable: lifting, the AF, the studio. The Father’s question to Klara about whether she believes in the human heart, in the sense of something particular to a person, frames the same argument in different words. Against the proposition, Ishiguro clearly is interested in what machines can do, and the novel is exact about it: Klara’s observation, her fragmented perception under stress, her solar dependence and her finite working life are all carefully imagined, and the ending in the Yard treats obsolescence with real attention. But these are the conditions of the experiment rather than its object. The decisive evidence is Klara’s own conclusion. Having come closer than anyone to acquiring Josie, she decides that she could never have continued her, because what was special lay not inside Josie but in the hearts of those who loved her. A machine built to imitate reaches, by observation, an answer about persons. Contextually the novel belongs with Never Let Me Go, where the science exists to ask what is owed to a person, and it arrived in 2021 into a debate that has since grown louder. I therefore agree with the proposition substantially: the machines are how the question is asked, and the uniqueness of a person is what is asked about.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Find the character who states the opposite of Klara’s final view, and start there.","Test the proposition by asking what every piece of technology in the novel is used for.","A proposition question needs a qualified verdict, so decide early how far you will go and hold that line."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Stretch — whole-text evaluation of the novel’s form and narration. “Klara’s limited understanding makes her the wrong narrator for this story.” To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Disagree: the gap between what Klara records and what she understands is what produces the novel’s irony, its restraint and its pathos; a fully understanding narrator would destroy all three.","sol":"I disagree firmly, because the limitation the proposition objects to is the source of almost every effect the novel achieves. Klara reports accurately and interprets badly, and that gap gives the reader work to do. At Morgan’s Falls she obeys the Mother’s request to walk and speak as Josie without grasping its purpose, and the reader assembles the plan before she does; at Mr Capaldi’s studio the portrait is explained to her, and the horror lands on us rather than on her. A narrator who understood would have to editorialise, and the restraint would be gone. Her limitation also makes the faith plot possible. Because she reasons from too little evidence, the Sun becomes a deity and the Cootings Machine a sacrifice worth self-damage; a better-informed narrator would simply be wrong in an uninteresting way. The same limitation governs how feeling is delivered: Klara records the interaction meeting and the Yard without self-pity, so the reader supplies the anger and the grief, which is why the ending is affecting rather than sentimental. There is a real cost, and I concede it: the interior lives of Josie, the Mother and Rick reach us only in fragments, and readers who want psychological fullness are refused it. But this is Ishiguro’s settled method — the butler of The Remains of the Day and the donor of Never Let Me Go both narrate what they cannot interpret — and it is the literary context in which the choice makes sense. My judgement is that Klara is not merely an acceptable narrator but the necessary one: the novel is about the limits of understanding, and it is told by those limits.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the reader works out that Klara does not, and when.","Test the proposition by imagining the same scenes told by a narrator who understands everything.","Concede the strongest point against you somewhere in the essay, then explain why it does not overturn your verdict."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Stretch — whole-text evaluation using the specification’s fifth category of context, reception by different audiences at different times. “A reader today cannot read Klara and the Sun as its first readers did.” To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Agree in part: reception has changed sharply since 2021 because machines that talk are now ordinary, but the novel’s questions about substitution and worth are not time-locked, so the change is in the reader.","sol":"I agree in part. The specification asks us to consider how texts are received by different audiences at different times, and few recent novels illustrate the point so quickly. Klara and the Sun was published in March 2021, when a machine with a plausible inner voice was for most readers a thought experiment; the first readers met Klara as a hypothesis. Within a few years conversational machines became an everyday tool, and the same pages now read as a claim about something familiar rather than something imagined, so her politeness, her patience and her contentment in the Yard prompt a different and more uncomfortable question: not could such a thing exist, but what do we owe the things that already do. The first readers also met the novel in a period of enforced isolation and schooling at home, which made the children’s oblongs and the arranged interaction meeting startlingly recognisable; a reader coming to it later loses that shock. Against the proposition, the novel’s central matters have not aged at all. Whether a person can be copied, whether love is transferable, whether a parent may gamble a child’s health for a child’s prospects, and whether the useful are kept only while they are useful — these were not new in 2021 and are not resolved now. Ishiguro also insures the book against dating by keeping the technology vague: lifting is never explained scientifically, and the AFs are described by what they do rather than how. My judgement is that the proposition is right about the reader and wrong about the book: what has changed is the equipment we bring to the page, not the questions waiting on it.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["Fix the year of publication and ask what a reader then did not yet have at home.","Separate what has changed in the world from what the novel itself argues about.","A part-agreement needs its two halves named clearly, so decide which half you will end on."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Stretch — whole-text evaluation across the whole arc of the novel, ending included. “Klara and the Sun is a novel about class, not about machines.” To what extent do you agree? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","a":"Agree substantially: lifting, colleges, substituted work and the disposal of the Yard are a class system in new clothing, though the novel ends on love and worth rather than on privilege.","sol":"I agree substantially, because almost every mechanism in the novel turns out to sort people. Lifting is the clearest case: an expensive, elective advantage that parents buy for their children, with a risk attached that only some are willing or able to run, and it determines what follows. Rick is not lifted, so college doors close, and Atlas Brookings, which keeps a small number of places for unlifted students, is a token exception that proves the rule. The interaction meeting shows the sorting socially rather than administratively: a room of lifted children in which Rick is the outsider, and in which Josie’s hesitation before defending him measures the pressure the system puts on private loyalty. The adult world repeats the pattern, since the Father has been substituted from his engineering work and now lives among others like him, apart and self-guarding. Even the AFs are placed: bought, ranked by model, and, when superseded, left in the Yard. Contextually this is a society that has converted money into biology, which makes class heritable in a newly literal way, and the novel’s coolness about it — nobody protests, everybody adapts — is its sharpest social observation. Against the proposition, the ending is not about class at all. Klara’s conclusion concerns love and irreplaceability, and Mr Capaldi’s challenge is philosophical rather than political. My judgement is that class is the engine of the plot but not its destination: Ishiguro uses a stratified society to create the pressures that force the question of what a person is worth, and then answers that question in terms class cannot reach.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["List the ways people in this novel are sorted, and see how many are paid for.","Test the proposition against the ending as well as against the middle of the book.","Distinguish what drives the plot from what the novel finally wants to say, and make that distinction your argument."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How long is Component 1, and how many raw marks does it carry?","a":"Two hours, 90 raw marks — 60% of the International GCSE."},{"q":"Which two assessment objectives are assessed in Section C, and with how many marks each?","a":"AO1 with 20 marks and AO4 with 20 marks, out of 40."},{"q":"What does Klara give up to disable the Cootings Machine, and who puts it into the machine?","a":"P-E-G Nine solution from inside her own head; the Father draws it off and puts it in, at her request."},{"q":"Where is Klara at the end of the novel, and what does she conclude about Josie?","a":"In the Yard among discarded machines; that she could never have continued Josie, because what was special lay in the hearts of those who loved her."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Artificial Friend (AF)","back":"A manufactured companion sold to children in the novel. Klara is one: a solar-powered B2 model who narrates the book. AFs are chosen in a store, ranked by model, and discarded when superseded.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Lifted","back":"Genetically edited as a child for academic advantage, and effectively the entry ticket to university. Josie is lifted and becomes seriously ill; Rick is not, and is shut out of almost every college.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The Cootings Machine","back":"The road machine whose smoke blots out the Sun, first seen from the store window. It is disabled with P-E-G Nine from Klara’s head, her side of a bargain for Josie’s healing; she despairs when another appears.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"P-E-G Nine solution","back":"Fluid from inside Klara’s head, offered to the Sun as her side of the bargain. On the city trip the Father draws it off and puts it into the Cootings Machine; Josie, the Mother and Capaldi never know.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Beggar Man and his dog","back":"The pair Klara watches from the store window, seemingly lifeless one evening and apparently restored by the morning light. This is the evidence on which she builds her belief in the Sun’s special nourishment.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Morgan’s Falls","back":"The outing Josie is too ill to join. The Mother takes Klara instead and asks her to walk as Josie walks and to speak in Josie’s voice — an audition for a replacement, understood by the reader before Klara.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Mr Capaldi’s portrait","back":"Not a painting but an Artificial Friend body of Josie. The Mother intends Klara to inhabit and continue Josie should she die; Capaldi holds that nothing inside Josie is beyond copying.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Atlas Brookings","back":"The college that keeps a small number of places for students who have not been lifted, and therefore Rick’s one narrow route into higher education. Proof that the exception confirms the sorting.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The Yard","back":"The place of discarded machines where Klara ends the novel, still able to order her memories and making no complaint. Manager visits her there, and Klara states her conclusion about Josie.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Section C: the shape of the question","back":"One 40-mark essay from a choice of two on the set text, assessing AO1 (20) and AO4 (20). Closed book, about 53 minutes of the two-hour, 90-mark paper. No AO2 or AO3 credit.","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","open":false},{"front":"The five categories of context (AO4)","back":"The writer’s life and situation, only where it relates to the text; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Kazuo Ishiguro: the facts worth knowing","back":"Born in Nagasaki in 1954, moved to Britain in 1960; Nobel Prize in Literature 2017; Klara and the Sun published 2021, his first novel after the Nobel. Also wrote Never Let Me Go (2005).","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false},{"front":"One way Klara’s narration limits what the reader is told","back":"She reports precisely but interprets wrongly, so the Mother’s plan is clear to us first; her perception fragments into boxes when a scene overwhelms her; she records other characters only from outside, never their thoughts.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":true},{"front":"One social or cultural context the novel invites","back":"Paid-for genetic advantage sorting children before they can consent; automation replacing skilled workers, as with the Father; children schooled at home on oblongs, so friendship must be arranged and companionship sold.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true},{"front":"Klara’s closing judgement","back":"That she could never have continued Josie: what made Josie special was not inside her, to be observed and copied, but in the hearts of the people who loved her. It answers Capaldi directly.","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["The question: How does Ishiguro present inequality in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4.)","The paragraph: Ishiguro’s harshest point about inequality is that nobody in the novel has to enforce it — the sorting has already been done in the body, and the rest is administration. Rick’s exclusion is never announced as a punishment: he is simply a boy who has not been lifted, and so almost every college is closed to him, with Atlas Brookings keeping a small number of places for students like him, an exception quoted as though it were generosity. That single arrangement tells us what kind of society this is. Where advantage is bought by parents and written into a child before that child can consent, class stops being a matter of income and becomes heritable in a newly literal way, and the injustice can be maintained without a single cruel decision by anyone in the room. Ishiguro then shows the same sorting operating socially at the interaction meeting, where Rick is the outsider among lifted children and Josie hesitates before defending him; her pause measures exactly how much private loyalty costs in public. What makes the presentation so uncomfortable is the tone — mild, procedural, accepted by everyone including its victims — so that the reader, and not the novel, has to supply the outrage. My judgement is that inequality here is presented as infrastructure rather than villainy, and that is a more damning account of a society than any single act of cruelty could be.","Why this reaches Level 5: it opens with a position and argues it rather than describing a theme; its examples are discriminating rather than retold, one administrative fact and one social moment, each held to a clause; its context is integrated convincingly, because the claim about heritable, paid-for advantage is the only reason the Atlas Brookings detail means anything; and the critical style is sustained and personal to the end, closing on a judgement that answers the question in terms the question did not supply."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["The same question: How does Ishiguro present inequality in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks: 20 AO1, 20 AO4.)","The paragraph: Ishiguro shows inequality through Rick. Rick has not been lifted, unlike Josie, and this means he cannot go to a good college. He goes to the interaction meeting at Josie’s house and the other children are unkind to him and talk about Klara, and Josie does not say anything for a while. Rick is very clever with machines and drones, and he and Josie have known each other a long time and want to be together in the future. This shows that Rick is treated unfairly because of something that is not his fault. This links to context because in the modern world people worry about genetic engineering, and Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki in 1954 before moving to Britain.","Why this sits at Level 2: the knowledge is broadly sound but the paragraph is largely narrative, telling the events of the meeting instead of arguing from them; the response is simple rather than critical, and the personal engagement amounts to one flat sentence about unfairness; and the context is a bolt-on final sentence whose biography has no bearing on Rick, which the specification’s rule that the writer’s life is used only where it relates to the text rules out.","The single change that lifts it: delete the last sentence and the plot summary, put the claim about heritable, paid-for advantage first, and keep only the Atlas Brookings fact and Josie’s pause as evidence."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Klara and the Sun — Kazuo Ishiguro","items":["Characters — Klara: the narrator; a solar-powered Artificial Friend, a B2 model, unusually observant, who tells the whole novel in the first person.","Characters — Rosa: the AF who shares the store and the window with Klara, and who notices far less than she does.","Characters — Manager: runs the store, arranges the AFs in the window, warns them not to rely too much on the promises children make, and meets Klara again at the end.","Characters — Josie: the teenage girl who chooses Klara; she has been lifted, becomes seriously ill, recovers, and leaves for college.","Characters — the Mother (Chrissie): Josie’s mother; works long hours, has already lost an elder daughter, commissions Mr Capaldi’s portrait and asks Klara to imitate Josie.","Characters — the Father (Paul): an engineer who has been substituted from his work and now lives among others in the same position; he helps Klara take the P-E-G Nine solution from her own head.","Characters — Rick: Josie’s neighbour and closest friend; not lifted, gifted with machines, and shut out of almost every college.","Characters — Helen: Rick’s mother, who tries to use an old connection to help him win a place.","Characters — Melania Housekeeper: the housekeeper at Josie’s house, fiercely protective of Josie and openly suspicious of Klara.","Characters — Mr Capaldi: makes the portrait of Josie, and argues that there is nothing inside her that cannot be learned, copied and continued.","Characters — Sal: Josie’s elder sister, who died before the novel begins.","Plot in order — Klara and Rosa wait in the store; from the window Klara studies the street, Beggar Man and his dog, the polluting Cootings Machine and the Sun’s daily path.","Plot in order — Josie visits the store and promises to return; Manager cautions Klara about such promises; after a long wait Josie comes back and the Mother buys Klara.","Plot in order — at the house Klara meets Melania Housekeeper’s hostility, watches Josie weaken by stages, and chooses her positions in the house by where the Sun’s light falls.","Plot in order — the outing to Morgan’s Falls: Josie is too ill to come, and the Mother asks Klara to walk as Josie walks and to speak in Josie’s voice.","Plot in order — the interaction meeting: lifted children test Klara, one proposing that she be thrown to see whether she lands on her feet, and Josie hesitates before standing by Rick.","Plot in order — Klara goes out to the barn at sunset to put her request to the Sun, offering to destroy the Cootings Machine if Josie is healed.","Plot in order — the trip to the city: Josie sits for Mr Capaldi, and the portrait is revealed as an AF body that Klara is meant to inhabit and continue; at Klara’s request the Father draws the P-E-G Nine solution from her head and puts it into the Cootings Machine, and they agree to keep it from Josie and the Mother; later another such machine appears and she despairs.","Plot in order — as Josie worsens, Rick helps Klara get out to the barn again so that she can plead a second time.","Plot in order — at the crisis the cloud breaks and the Sun’s light floods Josie’s room; Josie recovers, grows strong, and in time leaves for college, her path and Rick’s separating without bitterness.","Plot in order — Klara ends in the Yard among discarded machines, ordering her memories; Manager visits, and Klara says she could never have continued Josie because what made her special lay in the hearts of those who loved her.","Theme — substitution: whether a person can be copied, argued by Mr Capaldi, practised by the Mother, and answered by Klara.","Theme — faith: Klara’s belief in the Sun, built from observation into doctrine, bargain, sacrifice and an apparent miracle the novel never explains.","Theme — inequality: lifting as paid-for advantage, Rick’s exclusion, Atlas Brookings as the token exception, and the Father’s substitution from his work.","Theme — loneliness: children taught at home on their oblongs, friendships arranged at interaction meetings, and companionship manufactured and sold.","Theme — sacrifice and disposability: Klara accepts lasting damage for Josie’s sake, with only the Father knowing what it cost, and she is kept no longer than she is useful.","Form and structure — first-person retrospective narration by a non-human narrator, in numbered parts, moving chronologically but governed entirely by Klara’s selective attention.","Form and structure — dramatic irony throughout: Klara reports accurately and interprets wrongly, so the reader understands the Mother’s plan before she does.","Form and structure — Klara’s vision fragments into boxes when a scene overwhelms her, so the prose registers feeling as a change in perception rather than as emotion.","Form and structure — the flat, courteous diction of Klara’s voice, which names people by function — the Mother, the Father, Manager, Melania Housekeeper — and the fable-like shape of bargain, sacrifice and apparent miracle.","Context — the writer: Kazuo Ishiguro was born in Nagasaki in 1954 and moved to Britain in 1960; he won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2017, and Klara and the Sun, published in 2021, was his first novel after it. He has said the story began as an idea for a children’s book.","Context — the setting: an unnamed near-future society in which children are genetically lifted, taught at home on screens, and given manufactured companions, and in which skilled adults are substituted at work by machines.","Context — social and cultural: advantage is bought by parents and written into the child, so class becomes heritable in a literal sense; the unlifted are sorted out of higher education without anyone having to be cruel.","Context — literary: the novel belongs to Ishiguro’s line of self-effacing narrators who serve systems they cannot see, from the butler of The Remains of the Day (1989) to the donor of Never Let Me Go (2005), and it borrows the fable and the science-fiction premise without explaining its science.","Context — reception: published in March 2021, when isolation and schooling at home were everyday experience and a machine with an inner voice was still a thought experiment; since conversational machines became ordinary, the same pages ask readers what is owed to something that already exists.","Key moment an examiner rewards — Beggar Man and his dog seeming to be restored by morning light: the whole of Klara’s theology rests on this one observation.","Key moment an examiner rewards — the request at Morgan’s Falls: the Mother’s grief and her plan revealed in the same gesture.","Key moment an examiner rewards — the interaction meeting: inequality enacted socially, and Josie’s loyalty measured by a pause.","Key moment an examiner rewards — the studio, where the portrait is explained: substitution stated as a philosophy by Mr Capaldi.","Key moment an examiner rewards — the destruction of the Cootings Machine with P-E-G Nine: a sacrifice hidden from Josie, the Mother and Mr Capaldi, and known only to the Father, who draws the solution from Klara’s head at her request and puts it into the machine.","Key moment an examiner rewards — the light filling Josie’s room at the crisis: the apparent miracle the novel refuses to explain.","Key moment an examiner rewards — Klara in the Yard: obsolescence without complaint, and the novel’s answer to Capaldi."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: closed book, and the recommended edition","items":["Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into the examination, so there are no prescribed editions of the set texts.","The recommended edition of Klara and the Sun is Faber & Faber, March 2022, ISBN 9780571364909.","The anthology poems for Section B are provided in the question paper; the prose text is not, so plot, characters and moments must be held in memory.","Klara and the Sun and Western Lane are the new modern prose set texts, for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026.","Because Section C awards no AO2 marks, an accurately described moment counts as a relevant example just as a quotation does — and cannot be misquoted."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Ishiguro present the Mother in Klara and the Sun? You must refer to the context of the novel. (40 marks)","items":["Decoded question — in what ways asks for the methods of presentation, not a character description: every paragraph must be about how Ishiguro makes us see her, and half the marks (20 of 40, AO4) depend on the society that formed her.","Decoded question — the word Mother is doing work: Klara names her by function throughout, so the essay can argue that the novel presents her as a role under pressure as much as a woman.","Timing — about 53 minutes: three minutes planning, four paragraphs of roughly equal weight, four minutes for the conclusion and a check.","Opening sentence — state the position: Ishiguro presents the Mother as a woman whose love for Josie is inseparable from her plan to replace her, and refuses to let us dismiss her for it.","Paragraph 1 — Morgan’s Falls: Josie too ill to come, the Mother asks Klara to walk as Josie walks and then to speak in Josie’s voice, and watches. The treat is an audition; the reader understands before Klara. Context: a society that sells manufactured companions has taught her that a substitute is a reasonable answer to loss.","Paragraph 2 — Mr Capaldi’s studio: the portrait is an AF body, and Klara is to continue Josie. The commission was made while Josie still lives. Context: Capaldi’s claim that nothing inside Josie is beyond copying is the belief of a culture that has already converted persons into specifications.","Paragraph 3 — what lies behind the plan: Sal, the elder daughter already lost, the long hours, the hard driving, and the decision to have Josie lifted at a known risk. Context: parents in this world buy their children’s futures by editing them, so her worst decisions are the ordinary decisions of her class.","Paragraph 4 — how Ishiguro controls our judgement: she is given no self-justifying speech, and Klara reports her from outside without resentment, so the reader must judge from behaviour alone; the reckoning arrives indirectly, through Klara’s closing conclusion.","Evaluation thread to carry through all four paragraphs — is she culpable or formed? Keep asking, at the end of each paragraph, whether the society explains her or excuses her, and let the answer shift slightly as the evidence hardens.","Conclusion — must answer the question, not summarise: she is presented not as a mother who loves too little but as one taught to believe love can be transferred; the novel’s verdict is delivered by Klara, who decides that what was special in Josie was never inside her to be copied — and that is a judgement on the Mother’s whole world, not only on her."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:44:11Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"A textually accurate, well-argued kit that is now publishable: the kit quotes nothing from the novel — the only quoted strings are the four essay propositions it writes itself and two invented student sentences — which is the right decision for a closed-book section with no AO2, so there is nothing to misquote, and every described moment I checked (Klara as a solar-powered B2 AF and Rosa beside her, Manager's warning about children's promises, Beggar Man and his dog, lifting and Josie's illness, Sal, Melania Housekeeper, Morgan's Falls, the interaction meeting and the proposal to throw Klara, Rick and Atlas Brookings, Helen's old connection, the two barn visits, Capaldi's portrait as an AF body, the Father's substitution, the P-E-G Nine act on the city trip, the second machine, the light in Josie's room, the Yard and Klara's closing conclusion, plus Ishiguro's 1954 Nagasaki birth, 1960 move, 2017 Nobel, March 2021 publication, the children's-book origin, the Faber 2022 edition and ISBN, and the 40-mark, 20 AO1 / 20 AO4 closed-book shape of Section C) is true to the text or to the specification; the three places where the earlier draft made the Cootings Machine sacrifice Klara's own act, hidden from everyone, are all corrected and now agree with one another, and the giveaway hints, the short-answer tariffs and the repeated Level 5 headings are fixed — what is left is one open card whose first answer does not match its front, a repeated mislabel of the narrator of Never Let Me Go, and two phrases (\"a girl who is gone\", \"twice\") that state more than the novel does."}}},"prose-western-lane":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"prose-western-lane","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Western Lane is one of the seven modern prose texts you may be set for Component 1, Section C, and it is new: first teaching 2024, first assessment May 2026, so there is no bank of past papers to copy answers from. Your question is a single essay worth 40 marks, half of them for close knowledge and understanding of the novel (AO1) and half for its relationship with the contexts in which it was written (AO4). The paper is closed book, so every character, moment and detail has to come out of your memory, and nothing you cannot remember accurately can be made to earn a mark. This room teaches you how to hold a short, quiet novel in your head, how to turn a remembered moment into an argued paragraph, and how to spend twenty marks on context without bolting a biography on to the front of the essay.","idea":[{"h":"Western Lane as your one modern prose set text, studied whole","code":"Component 1: Content overview","t":"Component 1 asks you to study two set texts: every poem in Part 3 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology, and one modern prose text chosen from a list of seven. Where that choice is Western Lane by Chetna Maroo, the specification expects the study of whole texts, developing comprehension, critical reading and, where relevant, comparison, alongside clear and coherent writing in accurate Standard English. Western Lane and Klara and the Sun are the newest texts on the list, for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026. Because Western Lane is short, the temptation is to know one favourite stretch of it very well and the rest vaguely. A top-level answer treats the novel as one whole shape: it reaches for moments from the opening, the middle and the close of the book inside a single essay, so the examiner can see the whole text being used, not one scene being defended."},{"h":"Close understanding, a critical style, and an informed personal engagement","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","t":"AO1 asks you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, and the specification spells out what learners must do: demonstrate a close understanding of their prose text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement, and identify and use relevant examples from their prose text. That is 20 of the 40 marks. Two things sink answers here. The first is narrative: retelling what Pa does at Western Lane instead of arguing about it. The second is reaction dressed as engagement: saying a moment is sad is not a critical style. A top-level answer names a precise moment in half a sentence and spends the rest of the sentence saying what that moment does to the question it has been asked."},{"h":"Context is half the marks, not a paragraph at the end","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","t":"The specification says learners should also understand the relationship between their prose text and the context in which it was written, and must understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. AO4 carries 20 of the 40 marks in Section C, an equal share with AO1, so context is half the mark on every Western Lane question, whatever the wording of the question you choose. The specification prints no assessment grid for this 40-mark question. The only grids it prints are those for the non-examined coursework component, five levels across 30 marks, and this kit uses them as the nearest published description of what the levels reward. The word that matters in them is integrated: understanding of the relationship between text and context has to be woven convincingly into the response, not announced. A block of publication facts in the introduction is inert; it could be pasted into an essay on any text and it changes no reading. A top-level answer uses context to alter how a particular moment is understood, so that removing the context would weaken the point being made."},{"h":"The five categories of context the specification names","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","t":"The specification lists the kinds of context that affect a writer's work and the reader's response, and teaching should include all five: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, for example attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the 21st century from when it was written. For Western Lane three of the five pay: the social and cultural contexts of a British family of Gujarati Indian heritage in which the extended family has a recognised claim on how children are raised; the literary context of the short, reticent, first-person contemporary novel; and reception, since the book is being read by its first audience. The other two are thin here. Maroo dates almost nothing on the page, so the historical setting gives you very little to argue from, and the writer's own life and situation is admitted by the specification only where it genuinely relates to the text. A top-level answer does not tour all five: it picks the two or three the question actually needs and proves them from the text."},{"h":"What the Western Lane question looks like on the day","code":"Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","t":"Section C, Modern Prose, is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and in the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks these are Questions 4 to 17, carrying 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4. Component 1 is a two-hour paper worth 90 raw marks and 60% of the International GCSE, and it is a closed book examination: learners may not take texts into the examination, although the anthology poems are provided. Pro rata, a 40-mark question is worth about 53 minutes. The choice of two matters more than students expect: with no book in front of you, the better question is the one you can evidence from memory across four paragraphs, not the one that sounds more interesting. A top-level answer spends about five minutes deciding and planning, then writes four or five developed paragraphs and a conclusion that answers the question it was actually asked."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning one remembered moment into an AO1 paragraph","setup":"Exam question: In what ways does Maroo present Pa in Western Lane? (40 marks, Section C, closed book.) The moment you can remember clearly: after the death of their mother, Pa starts taking Gopi, Mona and Khush to the sports centre at Western Lane and keeps them training on the squash court session after session, including the ghosting drills he sets, in which they move to the corners of the court with no ball to hit, rather than talking to them about what has happened. Your task is to build one paragraph out of that single moment that earns AO1 credit.","steps":["Point: Maroo presents a father who addresses his daughters through coaching because the loss itself is something he cannot put into words.","Evidence, kept to half a sentence: the ghosting drills he sets them at the sports centre in the months after the death.","Analysis: a drill with no ball in it is pure obedience and pure company, so the court gives him something he can actually hand over, which is attention, instruction and routine, while asking nothing of him that would have to be spoken.","Personal engagement, argued not felt: the discipline reads as devotion rather than harshness precisely because it is the only currency he has.","Mini-judgement: so the training is less an ambition for the girls than a way of holding the family in one room, and that is how Maroo keeps Pa sympathetic while he is being hard on his children."],"cues":["What claim about Pa does this moment allow you to make?","How much room should the evidence itself be given in the sentence?","What does the court give Pa that a conversation would not?","Is this severity or is it care, and how would you argue it?","What is the training really for, and what does that gain Maroo?"]},{"title":"Making context earn the AO4 marks instead of decorating the essay","setup":"Exam question: How does Maroo present the pressures on the family in Western Lane? (40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4.) A student has written this sentence to collect the context marks: Western Lane was published in 2023 and was shortlisted for the Booker Prize. The moment their paragraph is really about is Aunt Ranjan taking a critical view of how the household is being run after the mother's death. Rewrite the context so that it works inside the argument.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: the publication sentence is true but inert, because it could sit in an essay on any question and it changes nothing about how Aunt Ranjan is read.","Choose the category that fits: of the five the specification lists, an interfering relative belongs to social and cultural contexts, which the document defines as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups.","Turn the category into a claim: in the extended family Maroo draws, an aunt has standing to judge how a widowed father is raising three daughters, and to propose doing something about it.","Attach the claim to the evidence: Aunt Ranjan's criticism therefore carries more than private disapproval, since she speaks with the family's authority behind her and the household knows it.","Mini-judgement: the pressure on Pa is not only grief, then, but the watchfulness of relatives who believe the girls are partly their business, and the novel makes that pressure felt without anyone stating it."],"cues":["Why will the publication sentence earn nothing, given what AO4 rewards?","Which of the five categories does a relative's disapproval belong to?","What does that category let you claim about an aunt's standing here?","How does that claim change the weight of the criticism itself?","So what exactly is pressing on Pa, beyond his own grief?"]},{"title":"Planning a forty-mark answer in five minutes","setup":"Exam question: To what extent do you agree that Western Lane is a novel about silence? (40 marks, Section C, closed book, about 53 minutes.) You have five minutes to plan and you can remember these clearly: the household after the mother's death, the training at the Western Lane sports centre and the ghosting drills Pa sets, the videos of the squash champion Jahangir Khan the family watch, Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan becoming involved in the family and wanting Gopi to come and live with them, Gopi's friendship with Ged, and the tournament Gopi is training towards. Produce the plan.","steps":["Decode the command: to what extent means the proposition has to be weighed, so the plan needs a position and a genuine challenge to it, not four paragraphs of agreement.","Paragraph one, the household: the family barely speak of the mother, and Gopi registers that from inside it, so the silence the question names is real and is where the novel starts.","Paragraph two, the court: at Western Lane the silence becomes usable, because ghosting drills and rallies let them be together with nothing that has to be said, and even the champion they watch on video is admired without discussion.","Paragraph three, the challenge: Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan show that the family is not silent at all about the girls' future, only about the loss, so the proposition needs narrowing.","Paragraph four, Ged and the tournament: away from the family Gopi can be easy with someone, and the tournament gives her something to say yes to, which suggests the silence is a habit of this household rather than of the girl.","Conclusion: agree with a qualification, since the novel is about what a family does instead of speaking, so silence names its method rather than its subject."],"cues":["What does the command word oblige the plan to contain?","Which remembered material gives you the silence at its plainest?","How does the sports centre change what the silence is worth?","Which of your four items can be turned against the proposition?","What do Gopi outside the family, and the goal she is training for, tell you about the silence inside it?","What position has the essay earned the right to take?"]},{"title":"Lifting a Level 2 paragraph towards Level 5","setup":"A student's paragraph on the question In what ways does Maroo present Gopi's relationship with her father? (40 marks) reads: Gopi's dad makes her play squash a lot at Western Lane. This shows he is sad about her mum dying. It made me feel sorry for Gopi. The only assessment grids the specification prints are those for the non-examined coursework component, five levels across 30 marks; no grid is printed for the 40-mark Section C question, so those coursework levels are used here as the nearest published description of the standard. They run from largely narrative at Level 2 to assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style and discriminating use of relevant examples at Level 5. The paragraph above is a best-fit Level 2: sound as far as it goes, but narrative, and with the context half untouched. Say what is missing, then rebuild the paragraph.","steps":["Diagnose the AO1: this retells rather than argues, the example is general rather than chosen, and a statement of sympathy is a reaction where the grid wants an informed personal engagement.","Diagnose the AO4: nothing here reads the relationship against the world the novel places it in, so half of the available marks are untouched.","Rebuild the point: Maroo presents a father and daughter whose closeness is expressed as instruction, so that being coached is the shape Pa's care has to take.","Rebuild the evidence and the reasoning: the ghosting drills Pa sets, where Gopi moves to the corners of the court with no ball to hit, show her being given the one thing her father can give, and show her learning to read his mood from how long he keeps her doing it.","Rebuild the context and judge: in a household where the loss goes unspoken, the court is the one place where care can be shown openly, which is why Gopi holds on to it harder than she holds on to winning."],"cues":["Which words in the student's paragraph are narrative rather than argument?","Which assessment objective has this paragraph ignored completely?","What claim about the relationship could replace the retelling?","How could the same example be made precise enough to be discriminating?","What does reading the moment against the silent household add to the point?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions on the paper","setup":"Section C offers a choice of two 40-mark questions on Western Lane. Question A: In what ways does Maroo present the relationship between the three sisters? Question B: How does Maroo present the influence of the wider family in Western Lane? You can remember the sisters and the training at the sports centre in detail; you can remember Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan only in outline. The paper is closed book. Choose, and justify the choice as an examiner would.","steps":["Test each question against memory before taste, because in a closed book paper a question you cannot evidence is a question you cannot answer.","Weigh Question B: the wider family offers obvious purchase on context, but material remembered in outline will not supply the precise moments AO1 needs in four separate paragraphs.","Weigh Question A: Mona, Khush and Gopi run through everything you remember well, so each paragraph can be built on a different moment.","Check the context half before committing, since the sisters can be read against the expectations placed on girls in this family, so choosing A does not forfeit the AO4 marks.","Decide and protect the decision: take Question A, and plan one paragraph that brings the wider family in as context, so the AO4 strand is present without depending on detail you do not have."],"cues":["What should be tested first when the paper is closed book?","What will outline knowledge fail to supply across four paragraphs?","Which question do your strongest memories actually support?","Before committing, what must you check about the other half of the marks?","How can the weaker material still be made to serve the essay?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section C question on Western Lane carry, and how are they divided?","a":"40 marks: 20 for AO1 and 20 for AO4."},{"q":"Why can you not copy quotations out of the novel during Component 1?","a":"Component 1 is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts in; only the anthology poems are provided."},{"q":"What is wrong with an introduction made of publication facts and prize shortlistings?","a":"It is not integrated. The grid rewards context woven convincingly into the response, so facts that change no reading of the text sit at the bottom levels."},{"q":"Which five categories of context does the specification say teaching should include?","a":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and how texts are received by different audiences at different times."},{"q":"What does an informed personal engagement look like, as opposed to a reaction?","a":"A position you argue from the text, such as reading Pa's coaching as devotion rather than harshness, rather than a statement that a moment made you feel sad."},{"q":"When this kit talks about Level 2 or Level 5, which grid is it using, and why?","a":"The five-level, 30-mark grids the specification prints for the non-examined coursework component. They are the only grids it prints, so they are used here as the nearest published description of the levels; the 40-mark Section C question has no printed grid."}]},"room":{"facts":["Western Lane by Chetna Maroo is one of the modern prose set texts for Component 1, Section C; there are no prescribed editions because the paper is closed book, and the recommended edition is Pan Macmillan, May 2023, ISBN 9781529094640/9781529094626.","Section C is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, Questions 4 to 17, carrying 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4.","Component 1 is a two-hour written examination worth 90 raw marks and 60% of the International GCSE, and it is closed book: learners may not take texts in, although the anthology poems are provided.","Western Lane and Klara and the Sun are the new modern prose set texts, for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026.","AO1: demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.","AO4: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.","The five categories of context: the writer's own life and individual situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","The people of the novel: Gopi, the youngest of three sisters and the narrator; her older sisters Mona and Khush; their father, Pa; their mother, who has died; Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan, who live at a distance from the family; and Ged, a boy Gopi meets at the Western Lane sports centre, whose mother is also seen there.","The only assessment grids the specification prints are those for the non-examined coursework component: five levels across 30 marks, awarded on a best-fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses. No grid is printed for the 40-mark Section C question, so this kit uses the coursework grid as the nearest published description of the levels, in which Level 5 requires assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style and discriminating use of relevant examples.","Pro rata, a 40-mark question in a 90-mark, two-hour paper is worth about 53 minutes: roughly five minutes to plan, forty-five to write and a few to check.","The family are Gujarati Indians living in England, and the novel carries that background without explaining it, which is where the social and cultural context of every Western Lane essay starts.","Moments to have ready for evidence: the ghosting drills Pa sets on court; the family watching video of the squash champion Jahangir Khan, whom Pa reveres; Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan wanting to take Gopi to live with them; Ged and his mother at Western Lane; and the tournament Gopi trains towards."],"questions":[{"q":"State the two assessment objectives assessed in Component 1, Section C, and the raw marks each carries. (2 marks)","a":"AO1, 20 marks, and AO4, 20 marks, out of a 40-mark question.","sol":"Section C, Modern Prose, is one 40-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks gives Questions 4 to 17 as 20 marks AO1 and 20 marks AO4. AO1 is demonstrating a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. AO4 is showing understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. No AO2 or AO3 credit is available in this section, so analysis of language for its own sake, and comparison with another text, earn nothing here.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what the section itself is called and which two things the specification says it assesses.","One of the two objectives is about knowing the novel; the other is about knowing the world the novel came out of.","Set the answer out as two lines, each naming an objective with its marks beside it, and make the two lines account for the whole question."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Identify the three sisters in Western Lane, say which of them narrates the novel, and state what has happened to the family before the events the novel centres on. (3 marks)","a":"Mona, Khush and Gopi; Gopi, the youngest, narrates in the first person; their mother has died.","sol":"The three sisters are Mona, Khush and Gopi. Gopi is the youngest, and the novel is told in her voice, looking back. Before the events the novel centres on, their mother has died, leaving the four of them, Pa and his three daughters, as the family. This is the fact from which everything else in the book follows: the training, the silence in the house, and the involvement of Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan in how the girls are being raised. In a closed book examination this roster of names has to be exact, because misnaming a character costs an examiner's confidence in everything else the answer claims to know.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Start from the household: how many children are there, and whose voice are you hearing the house through?","Think about how much the narrating voice is allowed to know, which is what makes so much of the novel overheard rather than explained.","Three names first, then the narrator, then the single fact about the family that the rest of the book follows from."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"List the five categories of context the specification says teaching should include for a set text. (5 marks)","a":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times.","sol":"The specification says there are different kinds of context which affect writers' work and the reader's response to it, and that teaching should include: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, for example attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context of the text, for example literary movements or genres; and the way in which texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the 21st century from when it was written. For Western Lane the productive three are the third, the fourth and the fifth: the social and cultural contexts of a Gujarati Indian family in England, the literary context of the short, reticent contemporary novel, and reception, since the book is being read by its first audience. The second is thin here, because Maroo dates almost nothing on the page and there is little historical setting to argue from, and the first should be used sparingly, only where it genuinely relates to the text.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The specification groups context into kinds and says teaching should include all five.","Recall them in the order the specification prints them, working outwards from the writer towards the reader.","Write five separate lines and use the specification's own wording for each rather than a paraphrase."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4"]},{"q":"Explain why the characters, key moments and any quotations used in a Western Lane answer must be held in memory. (3 marks)","a":"Component 1 is a closed book examination; learners may not take texts in, and only the anthology poems are provided.","sol":"The specification states that this component is a closed book examination and learners may not take texts into the examination, although the relevant poems are provided for the poetry sections. Nothing of the novel is in front of you. Everything you use in Section C, the names, the order of events, the moments you build paragraphs on and any quotation, has to come from memory, and AO1 explicitly requires learners to identify and use relevant examples from their prose text. The practical consequence is that a small number of short phrases learned exactly is worth more than a long half-remembered passage, and that a precisely recalled moment described in your own words scores where an approximate quotation does not.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the sentence in the assessment overview about what learners may and may not take into the room.","Consider which of the three sections of this paper has its material reprinted for you, and which does not.","Say what the examination conditions are, then say what that forces the candidate to have prepared."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Outline the situation Western Lane begins from and the routine Pa establishes for his daughters. (4 marks)","a":"The girls' mother has died, leaving Pa with Gopi, Mona and Khush; he begins taking them to the Western Lane sports centre, where squash training becomes their routine.","sol":"The novel begins from the death of the girls' mother, which leaves Pa alone with his three daughters, Mona, Khush and Gopi. Rather than talk to them about the loss, Pa begins taking them to the sports centre at Western Lane, where squash training becomes their routine rather than a hobby: they are on court regularly and seriously, drilled by Pa in ghosting, moving to the corners with no ball to hit, and Gopi takes to the game most of the three. The family also watch video of the champion Jahangir Khan, whom Pa reveres, and Gopi's training gathers towards a tournament. The wider family becomes involved in how the household is being run, in the persons of Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan. Stated this plainly, the shape of the book is already visible: a loss that is not discussed and a discipline that grows up in the space where the discussion should be.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The situation the novel begins from is a fact about the family, not an event on the court.","Then say what the father does with his daughters' time instead of talking to them.","Two sentences will do it: one for the situation, one for the routine and where it happens."],"codes":["Component 1: Content overview","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Maroo present grief in Western Lane? (40 marks)","a":"Grief shown as something the family does rather than says: the near-wordlessness of the house about the mother, the training at Western Lane as a substitute for speech, the wider family's intervention, and their wish to take Gopi in, turning loss into a practical question, and Ged as relief; context of an extended British Indian family carried through every paragraph.","sol":"Maroo presents grief not as a feeling her characters describe but as a shape their lives take, and the novel's power comes from how little of it is ever said aloud. The first way is the silence of the house. The mother has died before the events the novel centres on, and the family very rarely speak of her directly; Gopi, narrating as the youngest, registers that absence from inside it, noticing what is not happening rather than explaining what has been lost. Because the reader is held inside a child's attention, the loss reaches us at the edges of what she reports, and that indirectness makes it heavier than any statement could. The second way is the court. Pa takes his three daughters to the Western Lane sports centre and keeps them training there, setting them ghosting drills in which they move to the corners of the court with no ball to hit, and the routine grows into the family's substitute for conversation. Even the family's admiration is conducted this way: they watch video of the champion Jahangir Khan together, and what Pa reveres is shown rather than said. Read against the social and cultural world Maroo draws, a household of Indian heritage in England in which grief is carried rather than declared, this is not evasion so much as the available form of care: Pa gives what he can give, which is attention, instruction and hours in one another's company. The court lets four people be together with nothing that has to be said. The third way is the wider family. When Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan involve themselves, grief stops being private and becomes a practical question about who these girls belong to and whether a father alone can raise them; their wish to take Gopi to live with them makes the loss material, since it is pressed on Pa as a solution rather than floated as a kindness. The aunt's standing to judge is itself contextual: in an extended family with recognised claims over the raising of children, her criticism carries the family's authority behind it, which is why it presses on Pa as hard as it does. The fourth way is contrast. With Ged, a boy she meets at Western Lane, Gopi can be at ease in a way she cannot be at home, and that lightness measures, by difference, how much weight the house is carrying. Maroo therefore presents grief chiefly through proportion: the novel is busiest where the game is, and quietest where the mother is, and the reader feels the loss most in that imbalance. The most powerful of these ways is the training itself, because it lets the family keep going and keeps them from speaking at the same time, so that survival and silence become the same act.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["In what ways asks for the means by which grief is made present to the reader, not for the story of the death.","Sort your material by where the grief is being handled: in the house, on the court, among the relatives, and away from all three.","Set out four paragraphs, each taking one of those places, and decide before you write which of them you will call the most telling."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"How does Maroo present Pa as a father in Western Lane? (40 marks)","a":"Pa presented through action rather than speech: coaching as care, discipline standing in for comfort, his authority questioned by Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan, and his limits seen through Gopi's partial understanding; context of a widowed father in an extended family that watches him.","sol":"Maroo presents Pa almost entirely through what he does, and the reader is asked to interpret a man who never explains himself, which is why he remains sympathetic while being hard on his children. First, coaching is how his care is expressed. Left alone with Mona, Khush and Gopi after their mother's death, Pa does not talk to them about it; he takes them to Western Lane and keeps them on court, setting ghosting drills and putting on video of the champion Jahangir Khan for them to watch. What looks like severity is better read as the only currency he has: routine, instruction and his own presence, given in quantity. The reader sees the daughters learn to read his mood from how long he keeps them training, which tells us that in this house feeling is transmitted through the game rather than through speech. Second, his authority is shown under pressure. The arrival of Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan in the household's affairs puts Pa's competence on trial, and their wish to have Gopi live with them is a judgement on him as much as an offer to her. Here the social and cultural context does real work: in an extended family with a recognised claim on how children are raised, a widowed father is not left to get on with it, and the watchfulness of relatives is a legitimate force he has to absorb without answering back. Third, Maroo limits what we can know of him. Because Gopi narrates, and narrates as a child, Pa is given to us in fragments: what he sets them to do, what he does not say, what the sisters guess. That narrative restriction is the presentation. A more explanatory narrator would make him either a tyrant or a hero; the partial view keeps him human and keeps the reader working. Maroo therefore presents Pa as a father whose love is legible only in the form it has had to take. The reading I find most persuasive is that the training is not ambition for his daughters at all but an attempt to hold a family together in the one activity he can still organise, and the novel's sympathy for him depends on the reader seeing that before the characters do.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about presentation: ask how the reader is given Pa, not what sort of man you think he is.","Notice who is telling you about him and how much that narrator can know or explain.","Plan three strands: what he does with his daughters, what the wider family thinks of it, and what the narration withholds."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Squash is the only thing holding Gopi's family together after the mother's death. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks)","a":"Largely agree for the middle of the novel but qualify: the training supplies routine and shared purpose, yet the sisters' own bond, and the pressure of the wider family, also hold the household in place, and squash also drives Gopi apart from the others.","sol":"The proposition is strong but too absolute, and a good answer agrees with it in part while showing what else is at work. There is much to agree with. After their mother's death Pa takes the three girls to Western Lane and the training becomes the household's timetable: it decides where they go, what they do together and what the days are for. In a family that does not talk about its loss, an activity that requires no talking is exactly what can be sustained, and the court is where the four of them are most obviously a family. To that extent squash is doing the work that conversation would do in another household. But the sisters hold each other too. Mona, Khush and Gopi share the loss and the house, and the ordinary closeness between them, the alliances and the separations of three girls of different ages, exists apart from the game. If squash disappeared, that would remain. The wider family also holds the household together, and not gently. Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan involve themselves in how the girls are being raised, and their wish to have Gopi live with them threatens the family while simultaneously proving that it is being held: an extended family with recognised claims over children does not let a widowed father's household simply drift. Read in that social and cultural context, the pressure that feels like intrusion is also a form of keeping. Finally, squash is not only a binding force. Gopi takes to the game most seriously of the three, and what binds the family also singles her out and sends her away from her sisters into her own ambition, towards the tournament she is training for and towards a friendship with Ged that belongs to her alone. I therefore agree substantially but not wholly: squash is the form the family's life takes after the mother's death, and without it there is very little they do together, but it is the visible container rather than the only bond, and Maroo is careful to show it dividing the sisters at the same time as it gathers them.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["To what extent requires a weighed judgement, so the essay needs a real challenge to the proposition as well as support for it.","The word to test is only: find what else is holding the household in place besides the game.","Give at least one paragraph to something squash does that pulls the family apart rather than together."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"In what ways does Maroo present the relationship between the three sisters in Western Lane? (40 marks)","a":"Sisterhood presented through shared routine and shared silence rather than declared affection: the three of them trained together, Gopi's narration from the youngest position, the differences in how far each takes the game, and the threat of separation carried by Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan's wish to take Gopi to live with them.","sol":"Maroo presents the bond between Mona, Khush and Gopi as something practised rather than announced, and the novel measures it by what the three girls do alongside one another. The first way is shared routine. The sisters are taken to Western Lane together and trained together, so the reader often sees them as a group in motion, drilled side by side at the sports centre. Their closeness is therefore shown as proximity and habit: they share the court, the hours and the loss, and nothing about the affection between them needs to be stated for the reader to feel it. The second way is the narrating position. Gopi is the youngest, and the novel is told from underneath, which means the reader gets her sisters partly through what she watches and half-understands about them. That angle makes the older two slightly out of reach, exactly as older sisters are, and it lets Maroo present intimacy and distance in the same sentence. The third way is difference. The three do not take the game equally: Gopi goes furthest into it, and as she does the shared activity begins to separate them, so that the thing they hold in common becomes the thing that marks her out. Maroo is unsentimental about this; growing up inside a family is partly a process of drawing apart. The fourth way is threat. When Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan press their wish to have Gopi come and live with them, at a distance from the house and the court, the sisters' relationship is suddenly something that can be broken by adults. Read against the social and cultural context of an extended family with claims on the raising of children, the girls' bond is shown to be less self-governing than they assume, which is why the prospect of separation carries such weight. Maroo therefore presents sisterhood as an unspoken competence: three girls who know how to be in a room together in a house where the adults do not know what to say. The novel's most telling move is to let that bond be endangered by kindness rather than by conflict, since the plan for Gopi is meant well and would still take her away.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["The relationship is shown rather than described, so look for what the three girls are doing while nothing is being said.","Remember who is narrating and what that position lets her see and not see about the other two.","Plan paragraphs that move from what the sisters share, to what divides them, to what threatens them."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"How does Maroo present the part the wider family plays in Gopi's life in Western Lane? (40 marks)","a":"The wider family presented as legitimate authority rather than mere interference: Aunt Ranjan's scrutiny of the household, Uncle Pavan's gentler presence, their wish that Gopi come and live with them, and the pressure this puts on Pa; grounded in the expectations of an extended family with claims over children.","sol":"Maroo presents the wider family as a force with real standing in the household, not as background, and Gopi's life is shaped by people who believe they have a right to shape it. First, scrutiny. After the mother's death Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan involve themselves in how the girls are being raised, and Aunt Ranjan takes a critical view of it. Maroo lets the reader feel the temperature of this through Gopi's watchfulness rather than through argument: the household adjusts itself when the relatives are present, which tells us more about their authority than any confrontation would. Second, the difference between the two. Uncle Pavan is the gentler presence, and that contrast keeps the aunt and uncle from becoming a single hostile block; the wider family is shown as a mixture of judgement and genuine care, which is why it cannot simply be resisted. Third, the proposal. Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan want Gopi to leave home and live with them, at a distance from the house, her sisters and the court, and that wish turns the family's concern into a decision about a child's life, and it does so without malice. This is where the social and cultural context is indispensable: in an extended family of this kind, relatives have a recognised claim on the raising of children, so an offer to take a motherless girl in is both an act of duty and a verdict on her father. Without that context the proposal reads as interference; with it, it reads as the family system working as it is supposed to, at Gopi's expense. Fourth, the effect on Pa. The relatives' involvement measures him, and the reader watches him absorb a judgement he cannot answer, which deepens our sense of how alone he is. Maroo therefore presents the wider family as the novel's other kind of pressure, distinct from grief and sometimes stronger: Gopi's future is discussed by adults who love her and who do not ask her, and the book's quiet fear is not that she will be treated badly but that she will be arranged.","m":40,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what gives the aunt and uncle the right to intervene at all, and where that right comes from.","Keep the two relatives distinct from one another rather than treating them as a single presence.","Build towards the proposal about where Gopi should live, and make the context explain why it carries such weight."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO1"]},{"q":"Western Lane is a novel about learning to live with an absence, not about learning a game. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks)","a":"Agree in the main, but argue that the two are inseparable: the game is the form the mourning takes, and the novel's competitive strand carries the emotional one, so the proposition's opposition is false.","sol":"The proposition invites agreement, and I do largely agree, but the essay worth writing takes issue with the word not, because in Western Lane learning the game is how the absence is lived with. On the side of absence, the evidence is plain. The mother's death precedes the events the novel centres on, and the household barely speaks of her; Gopi narrates a house organised around a person who is not in it. Everything the family does afterwards is legible as a response to that gap, including the training itself. But the novel is also seriously about the game. Maroo gives close physical attention to movement, to the court, to practice and improvement, and the book gathers its forward motion from Gopi's progress towards competitive play. A novel that treated squash merely as a symbol would not spend this much of its attention on it. The reader's own experience of the book is of watching someone get good at something. The two strands are therefore not alternatives. The court is where grief is held rather than where it is escaped: a rally lets the family be together with nothing that has to be said, so mastery of the game is mastery of a way of enduring. Read against the literary context of the short, reticent contemporary novel, which prefers to show an interior state through an exterior discipline rather than analyse it, this is a formal choice and not an accident. Contextually, too, the proposition can be pressed. The absence is not only the mother's. Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan want to take Gopi away from her home to live with them, so the novel is also about the threatened absence of the girl herself from the family she has left, and about the social expectations that make such a proposal reasonable to the adults. I agree, then, that the game is not the subject, but disagree that the subject can be separated from it. Western Lane is about learning to live with an absence by learning a game, and its achievement is that the reader never has to be told so.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["The sharpest answers to a proposition of this shape attack its joint rather than choosing one of its halves.","Ask what the novel would lose if the squash were replaced by any other shared activity.","Bring in the kind of novel this is, and what that kind of novel typically does with an interior state."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"A student plans to open their 40-mark Western Lane essay with a paragraph on Chetna Maroo's career and the novel's Booker Prize shortlisting in 2023. Explain why this will not secure the AO4 marks, and set out what that opening should do instead. (12 marks)","a":"The facts are true but inert and change no reading, so they are not integrated; AO4 needs a context category attached to a moment in the text, altering how that moment is understood.","sol":"AO4 asks for understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and the top level of the five-level grid the specification prints for its coursework component, the nearest published description of the standard, requires that understanding to be integrated convincingly into the response. A paragraph of career and prize information fails on three counts. It is detachable: it could be lifted into an essay on any question about this novel, so it cannot be argued to belong to this one. It is inert: it changes nothing about how any moment in the text is read, and AO4 credit follows from influence on interpretation, not from possession of facts. And it uses the weakest of the five categories, the writer's own life and situation, which the specification admits only where it relates to the text. What the opening should do instead is take a position on the question and plant the contextual frame the essay will use. For a question on the family, that means naming the social and cultural context that will do the work throughout, an extended family of Indian heritage in England in which relatives have a recognised claim on how children are raised, and stating in a line how that frame will change the reading of Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan's involvement. The test to apply to every contextual sentence is simple: if deleting it leaves the surrounding point exactly as strong, it was decoration.","m":12,"d":3,"hints":["The issue is not whether the facts are true; ask what the grid means by integrated.","Work out which of the specification's five categories these facts belong to, and read what the specification allows that category to be used for.","Structure the answer as a diagnosis of the fault followed by the contextual frame the opening should establish instead."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Assessment overview: Section C – Modern Prose"]},{"q":"Explain how you would use the same moment, Pa keeping his daughters training at Western Lane, in an answer on grief and in an answer on the influence of the wider family. (10 marks)","a":"Same moment, different point and different context strand: for grief it evidences action replacing speech; for the wider family it evidences a father's chosen regime being the thing relatives judge him by.","sol":"A moment has no fixed meaning in an essay; it means whatever the point it is attached to makes it mean, and learning to re-aim remembered material is what makes a closed book paper survivable. In an answer on grief, the training evidences a family converting an unbearable subject into a routine. The point would be that Maroo shows mourning as something done rather than said; the reasoning would dwell on what the court offers, which is company without conversation; and the context strand would be the reticence of a household that carries loss rather than declaring it. In an answer on the wider family, the same training becomes evidence of a decision that other adults feel entitled to assess. The point would be that Pa's regime is the visible thing relatives can measure him by; the reasoning would show how a father's private coping becomes public conduct once Aunt Ranjan is watching; and the context strand would be the expectations of an extended family with recognised claims over children. The differences are therefore the claim it supports, the part of the moment that is emphasised, and which of the five categories of context is brought alongside it. The similarity is that in both essays the moment itself takes no more than half a sentence, because the marks live in what is said about it.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["A moment is evidence for a claim, so start by writing the two different claims it would have to serve.","Ask which part of the same moment each question would make you emphasise.","Set the answer out as two short readings of the one moment, then name what changed between them."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"The specification's fifth category of context is the way in which texts are received and engaged with by different audiences, at different times. Explain how this category can be used responsibly for a novel published as recently as 2023. (8 marks)","a":"Use present reception rather than invented historical reception: the novel is read by its first audience, so discuss how a 2020s readership meets this family's grief as contemporary rather than historical, and avoid claiming responses you cannot argue from the page.","sol":"The category is usually taught with older texts, where a candidate can contrast how a Victorian and a 21st-century reader might respond. Western Lane offers no such distance: published in 2023 and shortlisted for the Booker Prize that year, it is being read by its first audience, and a student who invents a historical reception for it will be writing fiction. Used responsibly, the category still pays. First, you can discuss the position of the reader you can actually account for, yourself and your contemporaries, and argue what this audience brings to the novel: a readership for which a British family of Indian heritage is not an object of explanation, so that Maroo can leave the family's world unglossed and spend her attention elsewhere. Second, you can argue about what the novel asks of a reader, since its reticence requires an audience willing to infer, and a readership used to more explanatory fiction might find it withholding. Third, you can point forwards rather than backwards: a novel this quiet about its loss may well read differently to an audience that has not lived through the period of its publication. The discipline is the same as for every other category. Make a claim about reading, attach it to a moment in the text, and never assert a response you cannot argue from what is on the page.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what makes this category awkward when the book has only one generation of readers so far.","Work out which readerships you could honestly describe for a book this new, then take every claim only from those.","Frame the answer around what can be argued from the page, and say plainly what must not be invented."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Component 1: Content overview"]},{"q":"Western Lane is shaped more by the people outside Gopi's household than by those inside it. To what extent do you agree? (40 marks)","a":"Disagree in the main while conceding real force to the outsiders: Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan's wish to take Gopi in, Ged and his mother at the sports centre and the champion the family watch on video all press on the household, but Pa's court and the sisters' shared routine remain what shapes it.","sol":"The proposition is worth taking seriously, because Western Lane is unusually open to the outside for a novel about one grieving family, but I disagree with it in the end, and the interest of the essay lies in showing what the outsiders actually do. There is a case for the proposition. Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan arrive with a view about how three girls should be raised and with a wish to take Gopi to live with them, away from the house, her sisters and the court, and that wish changes the temperature of the household long before anything is settled. Read against the social and cultural context of an extended Gujarati Indian family in England, in which relatives hold a recognised claim on the raising of children, their standing is not intrusion but entitlement, which is exactly why Pa cannot simply refuse them. The outside presses in other ways too. Ged, and his mother, meet Gopi at Western Lane and belong to no part of her family's grief, and the champion the family watch on video, Jahangir Khan, gives Pa a standard from outside the house to measure his daughters against. Even the tournament Gopi trains towards is an outside authority: it will judge her in public. Against the proposition, however, stands everything that decides how the girls actually spend their days. It is Pa who takes them to the sports centre and Pa who sets the ghosting drills; it is the three sisters who share the house, the loss and the hours on court. The outsiders supply pressure and a measure, but the household supplies the form of life the novel describes, and Maroo keeps the reader inside it, since Gopi narrates and can report the adults only from underneath. That narrative choice, which belongs to the literary context of the short, reticent contemporary novel, is the strongest argument against the proposition: a book shaped by outsiders would let us hear them. I agree, then, that the outside world is what threatens and what measures this family, and that the novel would be much smaller without it, but the shaping is done at home, and the outsiders matter chiefly because of what they can take from a household that has already lost one person.","m":40,"d":3,"hints":["To what extent obliges you to weigh the claim, so decide first who in this novel counts as outside the household at all.","Sort your evidence by what each person or force actually changes: the family's daily shape, or the pressure the family is under.","Plan paragraphs that put the outsiders' case first and the household's case second, and settle in advance which side your conclusion will come down on."],"codes":["Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Component 1: Content overview"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks is the Section C essay on Western Lane, and which assessment objectives does it assess?","a":"40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4."},{"q":"Who narrates Western Lane?","a":"Gopi, the youngest of the three sisters."},{"q":"Is Component 1 open book or closed book?","a":"Closed book; learners may not take texts in, although the anthology poems are provided."},{"q":"What is the name of the sports centre where Pa takes his daughters to train?","a":"Western Lane."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Gopi","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The youngest of the three sisters and the first-person narrator of Western Lane; after her mother's death she trains at the Western Lane sports centre and takes to squash more seriously than her sisters do."},{"front":"Mona and Khush","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Gopi's two older sisters, Mona the eldest and Khush in the middle; they share the loss, the house and the training, and are seen through their younger sister's partial understanding."},{"front":"Pa","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The girls' father, left to raise Mona, Khush and Gopi after their mother's death; he takes them to Western Lane and expresses care as coaching rather than conversation."},{"front":"The mother in Western Lane","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"She has died before the events the novel centres on and is rarely spoken of directly in the household; her absence organises the whole book."},{"front":"Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false,"back":"Relatives who involve themselves in the household after the death and live at a distance from it; Aunt Ranjan is the more severe, Uncle Pavan the gentler, and they want Gopi to come and live with them."},{"front":"Ged","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"A boy Gopi meets at the Western Lane sports centre and plays squash with; his mother is seen there too, and the friendship is one of the few parts of Gopi's life that belongs to her alone."},{"front":"Ghosting at Western Lane","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The drill Pa sets his daughters on court: moving to the corners as if playing, with no ball to hit. It is the novel's clearest image of care given as instruction."},{"front":"Jahangir Khan","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The squash champion Pa reveres; the family watch video of him play. Admiration in this house is shown by watching together rather than said."},{"front":"The tournament Gopi trains towards","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The competition Gopi's training gathers towards, which gives the novel its forward movement and turns private practice into something that will be judged in public."},{"front":"The family's Gujarati Indian background","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false,"back":"Gopi's family are Gujarati Indians living in England. Maroo carries this without explaining it, and it is what gives the extended family its claim on how the girls are raised."},{"front":"AO1 in Section C","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. Worth 20 of the 40 marks."},{"front":"AO4 in Section C","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO4","open":false,"back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Worth 20 of the 40 marks, an equal share with AO1."},{"front":"The five categories of context","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times."},{"front":"One way to make context earn AO4 marks on Western Lane","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true,"back":"Read Aunt Ranjan's standing through an extended family's recognised claim over the raising of children; read the novel's reticence through the literary context of short contemporary fiction; read the family's world through a 2020s audience that needs no explanation of it."},{"front":"The five-level grid this kit uses, and where it comes from","code":"Section C: Modern Prose — AO1","open":false,"back":"The coursework grids, the only ones the specification prints: five levels over 30 marks, best fit. No grid is printed for the 40-mark Section C question, so these are used as the nearest published description of the levels."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Which levels these are: the specification prints assessment grids only for its coursework component, five levels over 30 marks. No grid is printed for the 40-mark Section C question, so these labels use the coursework grid as the nearest published description of the standard.","Question: In what ways does Maroo present grief in Western Lane? (40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4.)","Point: Maroo presents grief as something the family does rather than something it says, and the squash court is where the doing happens.","Evidence: after their mother's death Pa takes Gopi, Mona and Khush to the Western Lane sports centre and keeps them training there session after session, setting ghosting drills in which the girls move to the corners of the court with no ball to hit, while the mother herself goes almost unmentioned in the house.","Developed analysis: because the novel is narrated by its youngest member from inside that household, the reader is given the hours on court in close detail and the loss only at the edges of what Gopi notices, and the proportion is itself the argument. A drill with no ball in it is the purest form of this: nothing is being achieved except being there, together, doing as he says. The family's energy has been redirected into a routine that can be sustained where conversation cannot, so that training becomes a way of being in one room together without anyone having to begin.","Context, integrated: the specification's social and cultural category earns its place here, because the reticence is not simply a personal failing. In the extended family Maroo draws, grief is carried between relatives and discharged through duty rather than declared, which is exactly why Aunt Ranjan's later scrutiny lands as an intrusion into an arrangement the household had almost settled into.","Mini-judgement: grief in Western Lane is therefore not an emotion the novel describes but a shape the novel takes, and the reader feels it most sharply where the prose is busiest with the game.","Why this is Level 5: the knowledge is assured and specific, the critical style is perceptive rather than descriptive, since the claim about proportion is an argument about the writing and not about the characters' feelings; the examples are discriminating rather than general; and the context changes how the moment reads, so it is integrated convincingly rather than announced."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Set this beside the Level 5 paragraph above: same question, same moment, and the difference is what is done with them.","Question: In what ways does Maroo present grief in Western Lane? (40 marks: 20 AO1 and 20 AO4.)","Point: Maroo shows that the family are very sad after the mother dies.","Evidence: Pa takes the three girls to the Western Lane sports centre to train nearly every day after their mother's death.","Analysis: this shows that they are trying to keep busy so that they do not have to think about their mother, which is a normal reaction to grief, and it means they do not talk about her much.","Context: the novel was published in 2023 and was shortlisted for the Booker Prize, and it is about an Indian family living in Britain.","Mini-judgement: so grief is an important theme in Western Lane.","Why this is a best-fit Level 2: the knowledge is sound enough to be recognisable and the example is relevant, but the writing is largely narrative, which is where Level 2 sits; the reasoning explains the characters' feelings instead of the writing; the personal engagement is an assertion rather than a position; and the context is a detachable fact that would sit equally well in an essay on any question, so the AO4 half of the marks is barely touched and pulls the best-fit judgement down. Level 3 would need the example made precise and the context attached to it; Level 4 would need sustained analysis and fully relevant examples; Level 5 would need the context doing argumentative work, as the paragraph above it does."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Western Lane: the people","items":["The family: Gujarati Indians living in England, a father and three daughters after the mother's death, with the wider family at a distance but closely involved.","Gopi: the youngest of the three sisters and the novel's first-person narrator; the book is told in her voice.","Mona: the eldest of the three sisters.","Khush: the middle sister, between Mona and Gopi.","Pa: the girls' father, left to raise the three of them after their mother's death; it is he who takes them to train at Western Lane.","The mother: dead before the events the novel centres on, and rarely spoken of directly in the household; her absence organises the novel.","Aunt Ranjan: a relative who involves herself in the family after the death and is critical of how the household is being run.","Uncle Pavan: the gentler of the aunt and uncle; the two of them live at a distance from Gopi's home and want her to come and live with them.","Ged: a boy Gopi meets at the Western Lane sports centre; they play squash together and he becomes her closest connection outside the family.","Jahangir Khan: the squash champion Pa reveres, watched by the family on video; a real player, and the one figure from outside the family whom Pa openly admires.","Ged's mother: also seen at Western Lane, and one of the very few adults outside the family to enter Gopi's life at all.","Names must be used and spelled exactly in a closed book paper: Gopi, Mona, Khush, Pa, Aunt Ranjan, Uncle Pavan, Ged."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Western Lane: the shape of the story and the moments to have ready","items":["The family are Gujarati Indians living in England: Pa, his three daughters and, until shortly before the novel opens, their mother. Maroo carries the background without explaining it.","The novel proceeds from the death of the girls' mother: what is left is Pa and his three daughters, and Gopi narrates from inside that.","Pa begins taking Mona, Khush and Gopi to the sports centre at Western Lane, and the training becomes a routine rather than a pastime.","The ghosting drills: Pa has the girls move to the corners of the court as though playing, with no ball to hit. A scene to have ready, because it shows discipline, attention and wordless company in one image.","Watching squash on video: the family watch the champion Jahangir Khan, whom Pa reveres. A scene to have ready, because it shows admiration and ambition being handed on without a word of instruction about either.","Squash takes over the girls' time, and Gopi takes to the game most seriously of the three; the court becomes the place where the family most resembles a family.","Ged at Western Lane: the boy Gopi plays and talks with at the sports centre, and his mother, who is there too. A scene to have ready, because it is where Gopi is least like the girl the house has made her.","Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan become involved in the household, and the way a widowed father is raising three girls comes under scrutiny.","The wish to take Gopi in: Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan want Gopi to come and live with them, at a distance from the house, her sisters and the court. A moment to have ready, because a question on the wider family turns on it.","The tournament: Gopi's training gathers towards competitive play, so the practice acquires a destination and the book's tension collects around what she is really playing for.","How to use these: a closed book answer needs four different moments, not one situation described four times. Take the ghosting, the video, the relatives' wish and Ged as four separate paragraphs of evidence and the essay will not repeat itself."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Western Lane: themes, form and structure","items":["Form: a short novel, told in the first person by Gopi, looking back on the time after her mother's death.","Style: spare and understated, giving close physical attention to the game and leaving emotion to be inferred, so that what is not said carries the weight.","Structure: the training provides the forward movement, so the book advances through practice, improvement and the approach of competition while the grief stays where it was.","Narrative viewpoint: because the youngest sister narrates, adults are seen from underneath and their motives are reported rather than explained, which is the main source of the novel's reticence.","Theme, grief and silence: the loss is managed rather than discussed, and the family's habits form around it.","Theme, a father and his daughters: care expressed as coaching, and discipline standing in for comfort.","Theme, sisters: three girls of different ages sharing one house and one loss, close and separate at the same time.","Theme, belonging: a family of Indian heritage in England, an extended family with claims on the household, and a girl's first attachment outside both.","Theme, mastery: what can be learned and controlled, which is movement, a racket and a court, set against what cannot be, which is the absence at home."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Western Lane: context for AO4, and the facts the specification gives","items":["The writer: Chetna Maroo; Western Lane (2023) is her debut novel and was shortlisted for the Booker Prize in 2023.","The text on the specification: one of the modern prose set texts for Component 1, Section C, chosen from a list of seven; teaching should focus on the study of whole texts.","Edition: Component 1 is closed book and there are therefore no prescribed editions; the recommended edition is Pan Macmillan, May 2023, ISBN 9781529094640/9781529094626.","First teaching and first assessment: Western Lane and Klara and the Sun are the new modern prose set texts, for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026.","Social and cultural context to use: the novel is set in a British family of Gujarati Indian heritage in which the extended family has a recognised claim on how children are raised, which is what gives Aunt Ranjan standing to judge Pa and to want Gopi in her own house.","Social and cultural context to use: squash itself carries the point, since the champion the family watch on video, Jahangir Khan, is an Asian player at the top of a game played in British sports centres, so Pa's ambition for his daughters has a model behind it.","Social and cultural context to use: expectations about what girls should be doing with their time run underneath the novel, so a father who trains his daughters hard at a sport is making a choice others feel entitled to assess.","Literary context to use: contemporary British literary fiction of the short, reticent kind, closer to the novella than the family saga, and a coming-of-age story told through a sport rather than through confession.","Historical setting is the thinnest of the five categories here: Maroo dates almost nothing on the page and signals the period only obliquely, so a student who builds on it has very little to argue from. Prefer the social and cultural, literary and reception categories.","Reception context to use: published in 2023 and read by its first audience in the 2020s, the novel can assume a readership that needs no explanation of the family's world, which is part of why Maroo can leave so much unglossed.","The writer's own life and situation is the weakest category here, and the specification admits it only where it relates to the text, so use it sparingly and never as an opening paragraph.","How to use any of these: attach the context to a moment in the novel and make it change how that moment reads, because the top level requires context integrated convincingly into the response."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Maroo present grief in Western Lane? (40 marks)","items":["Decoded question: in what ways asks for the means by which grief is made present to the reader, not for the story of the mother's death; 40 marks means 20 for close knowledge and informed personal engagement (AO1) and 20 for the relationship between the novel and its contexts (AO4), so context belongs inside every paragraph rather than in one of them.","Introduction, about four lines: state the position, that grief in this novel is shown through what the family does rather than what it says, and name the three or four places the essay will take its evidence from.","Paragraph 1, the household: the mother is seldom spoken of and Gopi registers the absence indirectly, which establishes the novel's method; context strand, the reticence of a family that carries loss rather than declaring it.","Paragraph 2, the court at Western Lane: Pa's training regime, the ghosting drills and the videos of Jahangir Khan they watch together, is the family's substitute for talking, and the reader watches grief being converted into routine; context strand, care expressed through duty and provision.","Paragraph 3, the wider family: Aunt Ranjan and Uncle Pavan bring the household under scrutiny and their wish to take Gopi to live with them turns grief into a practical question of belonging; context strand, the extended family's recognised claim over the raising of children.","Paragraph 4, Gopi and Ged: away from the family Gopi can be light, which measures by contrast how heavy the house is; context strand, the literary habit of showing an interior state through an exterior scene.","Evaluation thread to run through all four: is the family's silence a failure or its way of surviving? Take a side in the introduction and let each paragraph test it, so the essay argues rather than lists.","Conclusion: answer in what ways with the ranked list the essay has earned, name the way you find most powerful, which is the training, because it lets the family continue and keeps them from speaking in the same act, and say why the novel's reticence makes the grief more present rather than less.","Timing: about 53 minutes pro rata for a 40-mark question in a two-hour, 90-mark paper, so roughly five minutes to plan, forty-five to write and three to check names and spellings."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Notes — writing about a very recent novel in a closed book paper","items":["Western Lane is new to the specification, for first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026, so there is no bank of past papers; prepare for the stems the specification models, which are In what ways does..., How does... and To what extent do you agree?","Closed book means no quotation is available except the words you have deliberately learned. Learn a small number of short phrases you are certain of, and refer precisely to moments for everything else; a precisely described moment earns AO1, an approximate quotation does not.","An examiner cannot reward an invented detail, and a wrong one costs credibility across the whole answer. If you cannot remember whether something happened, write about something you can remember.","Do not pad AO4 with biography or prize facts. Apply the test: if deleting the contextual sentence leaves the point exactly as strong, it was decoration.","No AO2 credit is available in Section C, so do not spend paragraphs naming techniques for their own sake; use the writing as evidence for what the novel is doing, which is AO1, and keep half your effort for context, which is AO4.","Plan the choice of question against memory, not against interest: in a closed book paper the answerable question is the one you can evidence in four separate paragraphs.","When this kit names a level, it is using the five-level, 30-mark grid the specification prints for its coursework component. That is the only grid the document prints; the 40-mark Section C question has none, so treat the levels as a description of the standard rather than as a mark scheme for your paper.","Four different moments beat one situation repeated. Prepare the ghosting drills, the squash watched on video, the relatives' wish to take Gopi in, Ged at the sports centre and the tournament, so that each paragraph has evidence of its own."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:50:34Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"A careful, honest kit that is above the publish line: it contains no quotation marks at all, so there is nothing to misquote, every specification figure I checked against the map and spec.txt is exact (40 marks, 20 AO1 + 20 AO4, Questions 4 to 17, closed book with the anthology poems provided, two hours, 90 marks, 60%, the list of seven prose texts, the five context categories, first teaching 2024 and first assessment May 2026, Pan Macmillan May 2023 and the ISBNs), and all nine of the first examiner's faults are answered - the context-category lists in lesson.idea[3], room.questions[2].sol and extras[5] now agree on the third, fourth and fifth; the grid is named as the coursework grid everywhere it is used; extras[1] is relabelled Level 2 with commentary that matches; the two revealing hints on room.questions[11] and [13] are replaced with method nudges; room.questions[8].sol now says 'often' rather than 'almost always'; extras[2] and [3] have gained the ghosting drills, the Jahangir Khan video, Ged's mother and the tournament; room.questions[12] is stemmed 'Explain' rather than the AO3-reserved 'Compare'; and a second 40-mark 'To what extent do you agree?' essay (room.questions[14]) now balances the d=3 tier against the three technique questions. What is left is coverage and two flags I cannot settle without the novel: whether Gopi actually goes to stay with the aunt and uncle, and whether the Jahangir Khan video is really watched in silence."}}},"drama-view-from-the-bridge":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"drama-view-from-the-bridge","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Your A View from the Bridge answer is Section A of Component 2: one 30-mark essay chosen from two, on a paper of 1 hour 30 minutes that is worth 40 per cent of the International GCSE. Fifteen of those marks are AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, a critical style, an informed personal engagement) and fifteen are AO2 (analysis of the language, form and structure Miller uses to create meanings and effects). AO4 is not assessed in this section, so paragraphs about Miller's life or 1950s America earn nothing on their own here. The paper is open book with a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition, so quotation is expected to be exact, short and chosen for the point it proves. This room drills the play itself and the paragraph shape that carries both objectives in the same breath.","idea":[{"h":"The whole play, studied whole","t":"Component 2 asks you to study two set texts: one modern drama text, here A View from the Bridge, and one literary heritage text. The content overview requires you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. The word that matters is whole: the examiner sets one question on the entire play, so you must be able to reach from Alfieri's first speech to Eddie's death inside a single paragraph. A top-level answer treats the play as one designed object, tracking a method across both acts rather than retelling a favourite scene.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, critical style, informed personal engagement","t":"AO1 carries 15 of the 30 marks in Section A. You must demonstrate a close understanding of your drama text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement, and identify and use relevant examples from the play. The board's own model wordings show what such a question fastens on: a character or group of characters, as in 'the ways in which Priestley presents women', or a theme, as in 'In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge?'. So the characters, relationships, themes and key moments must be known closely enough to be argued about, not merely recalled. A top-level answer selects discriminatingly: three exact, well-chosen references beat twelve half-remembered ones, and each is chosen because it advances a position the student actually holds.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: language, form and structure in a play","t":"AO2 carries the other 15 marks. You must show how writers use language, form and structure to create effects, understand how writers create literary effects, and understand and use appropriate literary terminology. For a play, form and structure are not extras: Alfieri's direct address to the audience, the two-act shape, the act curtain, the stage directions, the silences and the physical business on stage are all Miller's method. The grid rises from minimal identification of language, form and structure at Level 1, through sustained analysis at Level 4, to cohesive evaluation at Level 5, so naming a device scores almost nothing. A top-level answer never stops at the label: it traces how the chosen method produces a particular effect on this audience at this point in the play, and weighs how well it works.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"},{"h":"The question, the tariff and the open book","t":"Section A is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, and these are Questions 1 to 10 on the paper, carrying 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2. AO4 is not assessed in this section at all. Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and 40 per cent of the qualification, so roughly forty-five minutes belongs to this essay. It is an open book examination: you may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition into the room, and the copy must be completely unmarked, so you cannot rely on annotations or flags. A top-level answer uses the book the way an examiner hopes: to check that a short quotation is exact, not to copy long passages that eat time and prove nothing.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment A","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same modern drama list. Assignment A is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2, and the assignment must allow learners to access both objectives. The board's model wording for this text is exactly the stem you should practise on: 'In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.' The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, marking is on the same five-level grid on a best fit basis, and the work is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. A top-level assignment answer uses the tight word count as a discipline: every paragraph must earn AO1 and AO2 together, because there is no room for a paragraph that only narrates.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"}],"examples":[{"title":"Writing the conflict paragraph on the Act One curtain","setup":"Question: 'In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.' (Component 2 Section A, 30 marks, AO1 and AO2.) The moment you are working on is the close of Act One. Eddie has just given Rodolpho a boxing lesson in the apartment and struck him under cover of teaching. Marco then asks Eddie whether he can lift a chair by one leg. Eddie tries and cannot. Marco lifts the chair and raises it over Eddie's head like a weapon, smiling, as the curtain falls. Write one developed paragraph of the essay on this moment.","steps":["Point: Miller stages the rivalry between Eddie and Marco as a wordless contest of strength, so that the quarrel is settled by gesture long before either man will put it into speech.","Evidence: the boxing lesson lets Eddie hurt Rodolpho while pretending to teach him, and Marco replies not with an accusation but by raising the chair Eddie could not lift and holding it above Eddie's head.","Analysis of form: because the challenge is silent and held at the curtain, the audience is left looking at a raised weapon with no dialogue to soften it, and Marco's smile converts a parlour trick into a warning that he, not Eddie, now commands the room.","Analysis of structure: placing the image at the act break makes it the hinge of the play, so every scene after the interval is watched as the working out of a threat the audience has already seen delivered.","Judgement: conflict here is presented as a struggle for authority rather than for Catherine, which is why it cannot be argued out and can only be finished in the street, over a name, with a knife."],"cues":["What kind of conflict is being staged, and between whom?","Which two actions in the setup carry that conflict?","What does Miller gain by giving Marco no words at all here?","Why does it matter that this is the last thing before the interval?","On this evidence, what is the conflict actually about?"]},{"title":"From naming a device to evaluating an effect","setup":"Question: 'Explore the ways in which Miller presents Eddie Carbone. You must consider language, form and structure.' A student has written this sentence: 'Miller uses repetition because Eddie keeps saying that Rodolpho ain't right. This shows Eddie does not like him.' The material available is Eddie's repeated judgement of Rodolpho, 'the guy ain't right', and his remark to Catherine that Rodolpho is 'only bowin' to his passport'. Rewrite that sentence as a chain of reasoning that would sit at the top level.","steps":["Identify precisely: what recurs is an unfinished judgement, since 'the guy ain't right' never becomes a completed accusation with a named charge behind it.","Analyse the language: the vagueness is the point, because an insinuation spreads doubt without needing evidence, which is exactly what a man building a case he cannot prove requires.","Bring in the second reference: 'only bowin' to his passport' reinterprets ordinary courtesy as fraud, so Eddie's method is to turn innocent behaviour into motive.","Link to the question: Miller therefore presents Eddie as a man betrayed by his own idiom, since each attack reveals more about Eddie's need than about Rodolpho's character.","Judge: the repetition is not decoration but the engine of the plot, because a suspicion Eddie can never articulate is one he can only settle outside language, by the telephone call and finally the knife."],"cues":["What exactly is being repeated, and what is missing from it?","Why would a vague phrase suit Eddie better than a precise one?","What does the passport remark do to Rodolpho's politeness?","What does this tell the audience about Eddie rather than Rodolpho?","Where does a suspicion that cannot be spoken have to end?"]},{"title":"Alfieri: writing the paragraph on dramatic form","setup":"Question: 'In what ways does Miller use the character of Alfieri in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.' What you have to work with: Alfieri is a lawyer who appears in the action and also steps out of it to speak directly to the audience; he tells the audience at the start that he could not stop what happened; in his office he tells Eddie that there is no law for what Eddie wants; in the prison reception room in Act Two he tells Marco that this is not God and that 'Only God makes justice', and will stand bail only once Marco has promised not to touch Eddie; and he ends the play saying that we 'settle for half', and that he mourns Eddie 'with a certain... alarm'. Write the paragraph on Alfieri's dramatic function.","steps":["Point: Alfieri is Miller's chorus, a figure who stands inside the neighbourhood and outside the story at once, and that double position is what makes the play a tragedy rather than a crime report.","Evidence: he narrates in retrospect and admits from the beginning that he was powerless to prevent the ending, and the two scenes where he intervenes — the office and the prison — both end in the law being unable to give either man what he came for, so the audience watches events he has already judged and already failed to alter.","Analysis of language: his formal, measured register — the flat certainty of 'Only God makes justice', spoken to Marco across a prison table — is set against the clipped Brooklyn idiom of the longshoremen, and the gap between the two registers is the gap between the law and the neighbourhood.","Analysis of structure: by opening and closing the play he frames the action, and 'settle for half' becomes the ordinary compromise that every other character makes and Eddie alone refuses.","Judgement: Miller's final stroke is that Alfieri mourns 'with a certain... alarm', which denies the audience the comfort of simply condemning Eddie and forces them to admire the very wholeness that destroyed him."],"cues":["Where does Alfieri stand in relation to the story he is telling?","What has Alfieri already told the audience before the action starts?","How does his way of speaking differ from Eddie's and Marco's, and why?","What job does the phrase about settling for half do across the play?","Why does Miller refuse the audience a clean verdict at the end?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions and planning in forty-five minutes","setup":"You are in Component 2 Section A and must answer one question on A View from the Bridge from a choice of two. Question A: 'In what ways does Miller present the relationship between Eddie and Catherine? You must consider language, form and structure.' Question B: 'Explore the ways in which Miller presents ideas about the law in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure.' You have about forty-five minutes and a clean, unmarked copy of the play. Decide which question to answer and sketch the plan.","steps":["Test each stem against material you can locate quickly and quote exactly, not against which topic sounds easier to talk about.","Choose Question B, because the law material is spread deliberately across the whole play and gives you Alfieri's office scenes, the telephone call, the arrest and the final street confrontation.","Sketch the paragraphs in order: the law as Alfieri describes it and cannot use; the neighbourhood law of name and honour that Eddie lives by; the telephone call as the point where the two codes collide; the knife as the verdict the neighbourhood law delivers.","Set the evaluation thread: keep asking in every paragraph which code the audience is being invited to accept, and let the answer shift as the play goes on.","Fix the conclusion before writing: the play refuses to hand victory to either code, since Alfieri's compromise keeps everyone alive and Eddie's refusal of it is what makes him worth mourning."],"cues":["What should decide between two stems you could both attempt?","Which stem gives you evidence from both acts rather than one scene?","What order would let the argument build instead of listing scenes?","What single question can run through every paragraph?","What must the last paragraph settle that the others left open?"]},{"title":"Closing the essay on justice with a judgement","setup":"Question: 'In what ways does Miller present ideas about justice in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.' The moments you are working on: after Eddie has telephoned the Immigration Bureau the officers take Marco and Rodolpho from the apartment, and Marco breaks from the group, dashes back into the room and spits in Eddie's face, with Beatrice, Catherine, Rodolpho and the officers there; from the street a moment later, the neighbours having by then crowded round the stoop outside, Marco points Eddie out and accuses him of killing his children. In the prison reception room Alfieri will stand bail only when Marco promises not to touch Eddie, telling him that this is not God and that 'Only God makes justice'. On the wedding day Marco comes to the street; Eddie demands his name back and lunges at him unarmed; Marco strikes him beside the neck, crying 'Animal!' as the blow falls and demanding that Eddie go on his knees to him; Eddie goes down with that blow, and only then does he spring a knife into his hand, and Marco turns the blade back into him. Write the closing paragraph of the essay.","steps":["Point: Miller closes the play by letting the neighbourhood's idea of justice carry out a sentence that the state's law can neither pass nor prevent.","Evidence: the spitting is a sentence executed inside the home Eddie has made himself the head of, while the accusation shouted from the street is the charge read out in public, so the community receives the verdict twice and in two different registers.","Analysis of language and form: 'Animal!' strips Eddie of the standing in the community he has spent the play defending, and because Marco calls it out with the blow that fells him, the demand that Eddie go on his knees is for submission rather than for a death — which is the one thing Eddie cannot give and still have a name.","Analysis of structure: Miller makes Eddie supply the weapon himself, after the fight is already lost, so the punishment is self-inflicted and the audience is denied the comfort of an outside executioner.","Judgement: justice in the play is therefore presented as real, immediate and savage, and Miller's refusal to let either Alfieri's law or Marco's code look wholly right is what keeps the ending tragic instead of merely just."],"cues":["Whose idea of justice actually operates at the end?","What is the difference between what Marco does in the apartment and what he does in the street?","What does the single word 'Animal!' take away from Eddie, and why does it matter that the cry comes with the blow rather than after it?","Who supplies the weapon, and at what point in the fight?","What stops this ending from feeling like simple punishment?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which assessment objectives carry the marks in Section A of Component 2, and how are the 30 marks split?","a":"AO1 carries 15 marks and AO2 carries 15 marks. AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in this section."},{"q":"What may you take into the Component 2 examination room, and in what condition?","a":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition of each set text, which must be completely unmarked."},{"q":"Alfieri speaks directly to the audience and stands outside the action as well as inside it. What is the term for this kind of figure?","a":"A chorus, or chorus figure, as in Greek tragedy; his speeches are direct address to the audience."},{"q":"Why does copying a long passage out of your open book usually lose you marks rather than gain them?","a":"Length is not credited; AO2 rewards analysis of how a method creates an effect, and long copying spends the forty-five minutes you need for that analysis."},{"q":"A candidate writes a paragraph on American immigration law in the 1950s in a Section A answer. What happens to it?","a":"It earns nothing by itself, because AO4 is not assessed in Section A; it only helps if it is turned into analysis of how Miller presents the play's ideas."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section A of Component 2 is one 30-mark essay on the modern drama text, chosen from two questions, carrying 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2, with no AO3 or AO4 credit.","Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes, is worth 60 marks and 40 per cent of the International GCSE, and is open book with a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition.","The assessment grid runs from Level 1 (1 to 6 marks) to Level 5 (25 to 30 marks) and is applied on a best fit basis; Level 5 requires cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure and discriminating use of relevant examples.","A View from the Bridge is set in Red Hook, Brooklyn, among Italian-American longshoremen, and is narrated by Alfieri, a lawyer who speaks directly to the audience.","Marco and Rodolpho are Beatrice's cousins, brought into the country illegally as 'submarines'; Marco has a wife and three children in Italy, one of them sick, and has come to send money home.","Act One ends with Marco lifting a chair by one leg above Eddie's head; the play ends with Eddie dying on the knife he drew himself, in Beatrice's arms, followed by Alfieri's closing speech.","Component 3 Assignment A is the coursework alternative: one teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the modern drama text, 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, with an advisory word count of 650 to 800 words.","In Act One Eddie raises the name of Vinny Bolzano at the table and Beatrice tells the story; in Act Two Marco spits in Eddie's face inside the apartment as the officers take him away, and Alfieri tells Marco in the prison reception room that only God makes justice."],"questions":[{"q":"Who is Alfieri, and what part does he play in the telling of A View from the Bridge? (short-response practice task)","a":"A Brooklyn lawyer who both appears in the action and narrates it, addressing the audience directly as a chorus figure and telling the story in retrospect as one he could not prevent.","sol":"Alfieri is an Italian-American lawyer working in Red Hook. He belongs to the story, since Eddie visits his office twice and he later bails Marco and Rodolpho out, but he also steps outside it to speak directly to the audience. He opens and closes the play, tells the audience from the start that he was powerless to change what happened, and comments on the action as it goes. This makes him a chorus in the Greek sense: the device that turns a neighbourhood killing into a tragedy the audience watches with foreknowledge rather than suspense.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which character is in two places at once: inside the story and in front of the audience.","Consider what his job is, and what his speeches do that no other character's speeches do.","Answer in two parts: who he is in Red Hook, and what he does for the play's form."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explain how Marco and Rodolpho come to be living in Eddie and Beatrice's apartment, and what they intend to do in America. (short-response practice task)","a":"They are Beatrice's cousins from Italy, landed illegally as 'submarines' and delivered by Tony; Marco means to work and send money to his wife and three children, one of them sick, while Rodolpho wants to stay.","sol":"Marco and Rodolpho are cousins of Beatrice who have come from Italy illegally, put ashore from a ship and brought to the apartment by Tony. The neighbourhood word for such arrivals is 'submarines', because they must stay hidden. The family shelters them, which is expected in this community, and they work the docks. Their intentions differ, and Miller sets up the plot on that difference: Marco has a wife and three children in Italy, one of them sick, and plans to earn, send money home and return; Rodolpho wants to remain in America, which is what allows Eddie to accuse him of courting Catherine only for a passport.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from who they are related to, and which character in the play is not related to them at all.","Think about the word the neighbourhood uses for men who arrive this way, and why it is used.","Give the two brothers separate plans, because the difference between them drives the plot."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"State two things Miller does at the end of Act One to make it a turning point, and say briefly what each achieves. (short-response practice task)","a":"The boxing lesson, in which Eddie strikes Rodolpho under cover of teaching him; and Marco's chair lift, raising the chair over Eddie's head as the curtain falls, which reverses the power in the household.","sol":"First, Eddie offers to teach Rodolpho to box and then hits him. Because it is framed as teaching, Eddie can commit violence while denying it, which shows the audience how his hostility will always travel under a respectable cover. Second, Marco challenges Eddie to lift a chair by one leg; Eddie cannot, Marco can, and he raises it over Eddie's head as the curtain falls. This is wordless, so it cannot be argued with, and it transfers authority in the apartment from Eddie to Marco. Placing both at the act break means the audience goes into the interval holding the image of a raised weapon, and reads everything after it as the consequence.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both answers are physical actions rather than speeches, which is itself worth noticing.","Ask what each action lets a character do that words would not allow him to do.","Say for each one what the audience is left knowing that it did not know before."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Which assessment objectives are assessed in Section A of Component 2, what is each worth, and what does this mean for how you write? (short-response practice task)","a":"AO1 15 marks and AO2 15 marks, out of 30; AO3 and AO4 are not assessed, so every paragraph must combine close textual knowledge with analysis of language, form and structure, and context earns nothing on its own.","sol":"Section A assesses AO1 and AO2 only, at 15 marks each within a 30-mark question. AO1 asks for a close knowledge and understanding of the text, a critical style and an informed personal engagement; AO2 asks for analysis of the language, form and structure used to create meanings and effects. Because the split is even, a paragraph that narrates the plot accurately can only ever reach half the marks, and so can a paragraph that lists devices without knowing the play. AO4 is not assessed here, so a section on Miller's life or on 1950s immigration earns nothing unless it has been turned into argument about the play itself.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Two objectives are in play, and they are worth exactly the same.","Work out what a paragraph would be missing if it satisfied only one of them.","Finish by saying what happens to a paragraph of background information in this section."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"What is the story of Vinny Bolzano, who tells it, and why does Miller place it where he does in Act One? (short-response practice task)","a":"Eddie raises the name at the supper table, before the cousins arrive, and Beatrice tells it: a boy who informed on a relative hidden in the family home was dragged down the stairs and spat on in the street by his own father and brothers. Eddie supplies the moral, so the neighbourhood's verdict on informing is on record before Eddie informs.","sol":"The story belongs to the scene at the table in Act One, while the family is still waiting for Marco and Rodolpho to be delivered that night. Eddie is warning Catherine to say nothing about the cousins, and it is he who produces the name, asking whether she remembers Vinny and telling Beatrice to tell her about him. Beatrice then narrates it: the boy informed on a relative hidden in his own family's home, and his own father and brothers seized him indoors, hauled him down three flights of stairs and spat on him in the street. Asked what became of him, Beatrice says only that she thinks he went away. Eddie caps the story with the moral, that a stolen million dollars is easier to get back than a word that you gave away. Two things follow from the placing. First, the sentence on informers is passed by the household, and loudest by Eddie, while Eddie is still certain no decent man could ever earn it, so when he lifts the telephone in Act Two he is condemned out of his own mouth. Second, Miller puts the story before the brothers have even walked in, so the audience is taught the neighbourhood's law before it meets the men that law will be used against.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from what Eddie is warning Catherine about at the moment the name comes up, and ask why he wants that warning on record now.","Separate the three jobs in the scene: who produces the name, who actually narrates the story, and who supplies the moral at the end.","Finish on the placing, by saying what it does to the audience when Eddie later reaches for the telephone."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Conflict operates on three levels: the household quarrel with Rodolpho, the clash between the state's law and the neighbourhood's code, and the conflict inside Eddie that only Beatrice names; Miller gives the external conflicts physical rather than verbal form.","sol":"Miller presents conflict on three levels at once, and the play's power comes from the fact that only one of them can be spoken aloud. On the surface is the household quarrel: Eddie against Rodolpho, conducted by insinuation rather than accusation, since Eddie's charge never gets beyond the unfinished 'the guy ain't right' and the sneer that Rodolpho is 'only bowin' to his passport'. The vagueness is deliberate, because an insinuation needs no evidence. Beneath it lies the conflict of two codes of justice, which Miller gives to Alfieri: the law of the state, which as Alfieri tells Eddie in his office has nothing to say about what Eddie wants, and the older neighbourhood law of name and honour under which Eddie's telephone call is unforgivable and Marco's revenge is legitimate. Beneath both is the conflict inside Eddie, which only Beatrice will name when she tells him 'You want somethin' else, Eddie', and that he can never have her. Miller's form matches the layering: the external conflicts are given a physical vocabulary rather than dialogue, running from the boxing lesson to the chair raised above Eddie's head at the Act One curtain and finally to the knife in the street, while Alfieri's direct address keeps reminding the audience that the outcome is already fixed. The conflict is therefore tragic because it is misdirected: Eddie fights everyone except the antagonist he cannot admit to, so the play can only end with a weapon he brought himself.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Sort the conflicts by how openly each one can be spoken about in this neighbourhood.","For each level, find the method Miller uses to show it, and notice that the loudest conflicts are the least verbal.","Build the argument so that each paragraph moves one layer deeper, and make the last layer the one that explains the ending."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Miller presents Eddie Carbone in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Eddie is presented through an idiom that betrays him, through a refusal to compromise that Alfieri frames as both his fault and his distinction, and through a structure that turns his own earlier judgements against him.","sol":"Miller presents Eddie as a man whose language gives him away. His case against Rodolpho never becomes an argument, only the repeated, unfinished 'the guy ain't right' and the reduction of courtesy to calculation in 'only bowin' to his passport'. Because the charge is never completed, the audience hears what Eddie cannot say, and the gap between his words and his motive is where Miller places his characterisation. Second, Miller builds Eddie out of a refusal to compromise. Alfieri's framing phrase for ordinary life is that we 'settle for half'; every other character does, and Eddie will not, which is why the lawyer's warnings slide off him. Third, the structure convicts him from his own mouth: he calls up the Vinny Bolzano story at the table, has Beatrice tell it and caps it with his own moral about a word that you gave away, so the neighbourhood's verdict on informers is on record long before he lifts the telephone and his fall is presented as self-authored rather than accidental. Beatrice is given the sentence he cannot bear — 'You want somethin' else, Eddie', she tells him, and he can never have Catherine — and the play's final action lets Eddie supply the knife himself. What lifts the presentation above condemnation is Alfieri's closing admission that he mourns Eddie 'with a certain... alarm'. Miller makes the audience feel the pull of a man who would not settle, so Eddie is presented as destructive and, uncomfortably, as the only character in the play who is wholly himself.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Begin from how Eddie speaks rather than from what he does; the method is in the idiom.","Find the standard other characters accept and Eddie will not, and use it to explain his choices.","Make room in the argument for the way the play refuses to let the audience simply condemn him."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"In what ways does Miller present the relationship between Eddie and Catherine? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Miller presents the relationship through Eddie's controlling affection and pet name for her, through Catherine's movement towards independence, and through a structure that lets the audience understand the relationship before either character will.","sol":"Miller presents the relationship first through the texture of Eddie's affection, which is warm and possessive in the same breath. He calls her Katie, and his objections to her job and to Rodolpho are always dressed as protection, so the audience is made to hear care and control in the same sentences. Second, the relationship is presented through change: Catherine moves through the play from asking Eddie's permission to choosing a job, then a man, then a wedding, and Miller measures Eddie's decline against that movement, so that the more she claims, the less reasonable he becomes. Third, Miller controls what the audience knows and when. Alfieri's narration tells us early that the ending cannot be avoided, so the audience watches with a knowledge neither Eddie nor Catherine has; Beatrice then supplies the plain statement both of them avoid, telling Eddie to his face that he wants 'somethin' else' and can never have Catherine, and Eddie's drunken kiss makes explicit what the language had only leaked. The relationship is therefore presented as a love that has curdled into ownership without ever admitting its own nature, and Miller's method is to let the audience reach that conclusion several scenes before the characters do, which is what makes the final scenes painful rather than shocking.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Listen to how Eddie addresses Catherine and how he justifies each refusal.","Track the relationship as a change across the play rather than as a fixed state.","Ask what the audience knows, and when, compared with what the two characters admit."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Miller uses the character of Alfieri in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Alfieri works as chorus, as the voice of the law and of compromise, and as the device that frames the action as remembered tragedy; his register contrasts with the longshoremen's idiom, his office and prison scenes mark the limits of the law, and his final speech denies the audience a clean verdict.","sol":"Miller uses Alfieri as a chorus in the Greek sense: he stands inside the neighbourhood, since Eddie consults him and he later bails the brothers out, and outside the play, since he addresses the audience directly. That double position is the play's most important formal decision, because it replaces suspense with foreknowledge. He tells the audience from the start that he could not stop what happened, so every scene is watched as the working out of a settled outcome, and the play becomes a tragedy rather than a thriller. Second, Alfieri is the voice of the law and its limits, and Miller splits that work between two scenes. In his office he can only tell Eddie that there is no law for what he wants, so legality is shown to have nothing to say about the one thing that matters to Eddie. In the prison reception room in Act Two he takes the other side of the same argument, warning Marco that this is not God and that 'Only God makes justice' before he will stand bail. The two scenes together measure the distance between what is legal and what the neighbourhood believes is right: the law can refuse a man his revenge but cannot supply the justice he came for. Third, his register does structural work. His formal, considered English is set against the clipped Brooklyn idiom of the longshoremen, and the gap between the two ways of speaking is the gap between the state and the community. Finally, his closing speech refuses comfort: having offered the standard that we 'settle for half', he admits that he mourns Eddie 'with a certain... alarm'. Miller therefore uses Alfieri not to explain the play but to complicate it, leaving the audience with admiration and unease at once.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Start from where he stands in relation to the audience, not from what he believes.","Ask what is gained and what is lost by telling the audience the ending in advance.","Look closely at how he speaks compared with everyone else, and at what his last words refuse to do."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Miller present ideas about justice and the law in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Two systems of justice run side by side: the state's law, which Alfieri represents and cannot use, and the neighbourhood's law of name and honour, which delivers the verdict; Miller structures the play so that the second one wins and the audience is not allowed to celebrate it.","sol":"Miller sets two systems of justice against each other and refuses to let either win cleanly. The first is the law of the state, embodied in Alfieri, a lawyer whose office Eddie visits twice and who can offer nothing, because there is no law against what Eddie feels and none that will stop a marriage. Miller gives Alfieri the other half of the case in the prison reception room in Act Two, where he will stand bail for Marco only on a promise, telling him that this is not God and that 'Only God makes justice': the law can forbid a revenge without being able to supply the justice behind it. The second system is the neighbourhood's code of name, loyalty and honour, and Miller teaches the audience its rules early through the Vinny Bolzano story that Eddie calls for and Beatrice tells, so that the penalty for informing is understood long before Eddie earns it. The collision is engineered: Eddie's telephone call uses the state's law, and by doing so he forfeits his standing under the other. Miller then stages the forfeiture twice over. As the officers take him out, Marco breaks from the group, dashes back into the apartment and spits in Eddie's face, in front of Beatrice, Catherine, Rodolpho and the officers themselves, so the sentence is executed in the home Eddie has made himself the head of; then, with the neighbours now crowding round the stoop outside, he turns the disgrace into a public charge from the street, pointing Eddie out and accusing him of killing his children. The last scene reads the verdict out. Marco strikes an unarmed Eddie beside the neck, crying 'Animal!' with the blow that fells him and demanding that he go on his knees — a public submission rather than a death — and only then does Eddie spring the knife he dies on, in the street, with the community for a court. Miller therefore presents justice as real, immediate and savage, and by having Alfieri mourn Eddie at the end he prevents the audience from treating a killing as a solution.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["There is more than one system of justice at work; name both before you judge between them.","Find where the play teaches the audience the rules of the second system, and how early that happens.","Decide what the final scene settles and what Miller deliberately leaves unsettled."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"'It is Marco, not Rodolpho, who is the real threat to Eddie.' Explore the ways in which Miller presents the two brothers in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Rodolpho threatens Eddie's authority through Catherine and through a masculinity Eddie cannot categorise; Marco threatens his standing and his life; a top answer argues that the threats are of different kinds and that Marco only becomes lethal because of what Rodolpho exposes.","sol":"Miller builds the brothers as a deliberate pair, and the strongest answer argues that they threaten different things. Rodolpho is given singing, cooking, sewing and jokes, and it is this set of qualities, rather than any action, that Eddie cannot place, which is why his attack takes the form of the unfinished insinuation 'the guy ain't right' and the accusation that Rodolpho is 'only bowin' to his passport'. Rodolpho's threat is therefore to Eddie's authority and to his sense of what a man is, and it is expressed in language because language is where Eddie tries to fight it. Marco threatens something else entirely. Miller gives him few words and great physical presence: the chair lifted by one leg and held over Eddie's head ends Act One without a line of dialogue, and when the officers take him he breaks back into the apartment to spit in Eddie's face, in front of Beatrice, Catherine, Rodolpho and the officers, which is a sentence passed rather than an argument made — a sentence he repeats minutes later as an open accusation shouted from the street, where the neighbours have by then crowded round the stoop. In the last scene he strikes Eddie beside the neck and cries 'Animal!' with the blow that fells him, asking for submission on Eddie's knees in front of the community, and Eddie then springs the knife he dies on. The judgement worth making is that the proposition is half right: Marco delivers the destruction, but only because Rodolpho has already exposed the desire Eddie cannot name, so the brothers function as two stages of a single mechanism rather than as rival dangers.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The two brothers are drawn in deliberately opposite ways; start from how differently Miller lets each of them speak.","Separate the kinds of threat before ranking them: one is aimed at Eddie's authority, the other at his standing and his body.","A top-level answer qualifies the statement rather than simply agreeing, so decide how the two threats depend on each other."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Miller uses the structure of A View from the Bridge to make Eddie's death feel inevitable. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Retrospective narration by Alfieri, the planting of the Vinny Bolzano story, the escalating physical confrontations across the two acts, and the act curtain all remove suspense and replace it with foreknowledge.","sol":"Miller removes suspense on purpose and replaces it with dread. The first structural decision is Alfieri's retrospective narration: he addresses the audience before the action begins and admits that he could not prevent what followed, so the audience never asks whether Eddie will be destroyed, only how. The second is the planting of the Vinny Bolzano story at the table in Act One, before the cousins have even arrived. Eddie produces the name and asks for the story, Beatrice tells how the boy was dragged down the stairs and spat on by his own family, and Eddie supplies the moral while he is still certain he could never be such a man, so the telephone call in Act Two is heard as a sentence he has already passed on himself. The third is escalation through physical confrontation rather than argument: the boxing lesson, then the chair raised over Eddie's head at the Act One curtain, then the kisses, then the knife, each one a step further from anything that words could undo. The act break is used precisely, leaving the audience through the interval with the image of a raised weapon. Finally, Miller makes the instrument Eddie's own, so the ending arrives as completion rather than accident. The effect an examiner rewards is the tragic one: because the audience knows more than the characters throughout, each of Eddie's choices is watched with pity, and Alfieri's closing admission that he mourns him 'with a certain... alarm' confirms that the inevitability was designed to produce mourning rather than satisfaction.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Ask what the audience is told before the action begins, and what that does to suspense.","Find the moment where Eddie states the rule that will condemn him, and work out why it is placed so early.","Trace the confrontations in order and notice that each one moves further away from anything words can settle."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"For Component 3 Assignment A you must answer a teacher-devised assignment of about 650 to 800 words that gives access to both AO1 and AO2. Write a suitable title on Beatrice, then write the opening paragraph of your response and explain in two sentences why your title reaches both objectives. (planning task, 10 marks; the full assignment it prepares for is the one marked out of 30)","a":"A title in the board's model form, for example 'Explore the ways in which Miller presents Beatrice in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure.', plus an opening paragraph that takes a position, and a justification showing where each objective is earned.","sol":"Title: 'Explore the ways in which Miller presents Beatrice in A View from the Bridge. You must consider language, form and structure.' Opening paragraph: Miller presents Beatrice as the only character who will say aloud what everybody in the apartment already knows, and he uses her plainness of speech as a measure of everyone else's evasion. Where Eddie's hostility hides inside the unfinished 'the guy ain't right', Beatrice's decisive line to him, 'You want somethin' else, Eddie', is a flat declarative with no euphemism in it, and it goes on to tell him he can never have Catherine; the contrast of registers is Miller's method, and it makes her the play's moment of diagnosis. Structurally she is placed between Eddie and Catherine, so her attempts to send Catherine into adulthood and to reclaim her own marriage read as the one available alternative to the disaster, which is why her failure to be heard is what dates the tragedy. Why the title reaches both objectives: naming a character forces close knowledge of the whole play and an argued personal view, which is AO1, while the instruction to consider language, form and structure obliges the writer to analyse how Miller creates those effects, which is AO2. The 650 to 800 word guidance then means each paragraph must carry both at once, since there is no room for a paragraph that only narrates.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Use the board's own model wording for this text as the template for your title.","Check your title against the two objectives separately: what forces close knowledge, and what forces analysis of method?","Write the opening paragraph as a position with evidence, not as an introduction that announces what you will do."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"A candidate answering the Section A question on A View from the Bridge writes three paragraphs on 1950s American immigration policy and on Arthur Miller's own life. Explain what will happen to those paragraphs, and rewrite one of them so that it earns credit. (short-response practice task)","a":"They earn nothing, because Section A assesses AO1 and AO2 only and AO4 is not assessed; the rewrite must convert the background into analysis of how Miller presents the play's ideas, using exact textual reference.","sol":"Section A of Component 2 assesses AO1 and AO2, 15 marks each, and AO4 is not assessed, so three paragraphs of historical and biographical background are not merely thin, they are outside the objectives and score nothing. The same material would be creditable in Section B, where the literary heritage question splits 10 marks AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4 and requires understanding of context to be integrated convincingly. The rewrite keeps the knowledge but makes the play the subject. Instead of 'Illegal immigrants in the 1950s were called submarines and could be deported', write: Miller uses the neighbourhood's own word, 'submarines', to make concealment a condition of the brothers' existence, so the audience understands before any quarrel begins that any quarrel can be ended by a telephone call; the power Eddie eventually uses is built into the situation from their first entrance, which is why the informing feels less like a sudden betrayal than like a weapon that has been lying on the table since Act One.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Check which objectives this section actually assesses before deciding what the paragraphs are worth.","Compare the objective weightings for Section A and Section B to see where the same material would pay.","To rewrite, keep the fact but change the subject of the sentence from history to Miller and his effect on the audience."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks is the Section A modern drama question worth, and how many of those are AO2?","a":"30 marks in total, of which 15 are AO2."},{"q":"What does Marco lift above Eddie's head at the end of Act One?","a":"A chair, held by one leg."},{"q":"Who narrates A View from the Bridge and speaks directly to the audience?","a":"Alfieri, a lawyer in Red Hook."},{"q":"Which assessment objective about context earns no marks in Section A of Component 2?","a":"AO4."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Alfieri","back":"The Red Hook lawyer who both takes part in the action and narrates it, speaking directly to the audience as a chorus figure and telling the story in retrospect.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Submarines","back":"The neighbourhood word for illegal immigrants landed from ships, such as Marco and Rodolpho, who must stay hidden and can be removed by a single telephone call.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Red Hook, Brooklyn","back":"The Italian-American longshoremen's district near the Brooklyn Bridge where Miller sets the whole play, and the community whose code of honour judges Eddie.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"The Vinny Bolzano story","back":"Act One, at the table before the cousins arrive: Eddie produces the name, Beatrice tells how a boy who informed on a hidden relative was dragged downstairs and spat on by his own family, and Eddie gives the moral.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"'the guy ain't right'","back":"Eddie's repeated, never completed judgement of Rodolpho. Its vagueness lets him spread suspicion without evidence, because he cannot say what he actually means.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"'only bowin' to his passport'","back":"Eddie's remark to Catherine, recasting Rodolpho's courtesy as fraud and his love as a route to citizenship.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The chair lift","back":"Marco lifts by one leg the chair Eddie could not raise, and holds it over Eddie's head as the Act One curtain falls: a wordless transfer of authority and a warning.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Marco's cry of 'Animal!' in the final street fight","back":"Marco cries it as the blow that fells Eddie lands, demanding that he go on his knees — a public submission rather than a death — and stripping him of standing in the community.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"'I want my name!'","back":"Eddie's cry in the last scene. Reputation, not Catherine, is what he finally fights for, which is why the quarrel has to be settled publicly in the street.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"'settle for half'","back":"Alfieri's phrase for the compromise ordinary life requires. Every character accepts it except Eddie, and his refusal is what makes him both destructive and tragic.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"'You want somethin' else, Eddie'","back":"Beatrice's line to Eddie in Act Two, which goes on to tell him he can never have Catherine. It names plainly what nobody else in the play will say, and her directness contrasts with Eddie's evasive idiom.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Section A tariff and objectives","back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two, Questions 1 to 10: 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2. AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in this section.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"The open book rule for Component 2","back":"You may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of each set text into the examination, and it must be completely unmarked, so quotation should be short, exact and chosen.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment A","back":"The coursework alternative: one teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the modern drama text, 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, advisory 650 to 800 words, internally assessed and externally moderated.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"One way Miller makes Eddie's death feel inevitable","back":"Any one of: Alfieri narrates in retrospect, telling the audience at the start that he could not stop it; the Vinny Bolzano story fixes the penalty for informing before Eddie informs; the confrontations escalate physically, from the boxing lesson to the raised chair to the knife Eddie supplies himself.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Miller presents the deepest conflict in the play as one Eddie is having with himself, and stages it as a conflict with Rodolpho because that is the only form in which Eddie can express it.","Evidence: Eddie's charge against Rodolpho never becomes an accusation, only the repeated, unfinished 'the guy ain't right' and the sneer that Rodolpho is 'only bowin' to his passport'; it is Beatrice, not Eddie, who names the real trouble when she tells him 'You want somethin' else, Eddie', and that he can never have Catherine.","Analysis of language: 'ain't right' is an insinuation rather than a claim, and its vagueness is precisely what makes it usable in a neighbourhood where Eddie cannot say what he means; Beatrice's line, by contrast, is a flat declarative with no euphemism in it, and the distance between her directness and his evasion is where Miller locates the conflict.","Analysis of form and structure: Miller gives the external conflict a physical vocabulary instead of words, running from the boxing lesson, through the chair raised over Eddie's head at the Act One curtain, to the knife in the street, so the play's structure is a sequence of escalating substitutes for a confession that never comes.","Judgement: the conflict is therefore tragic rather than merely violent, because every blow is aimed at the wrong target; Eddie can fight Rodolpho, fight Marco and finally fight the whole neighbourhood for his name, but he cannot fight the desire Beatrice has already named, and that is why only his own knife can settle it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Miller presents the conflict between Eddie and Rodolpho as something Eddie cannot argue out in the open, so it comes out first as suspicion and then as violence.","Evidence: Eddie repeats that 'the guy ain't right' and tells Catherine that Rodolpho is 'only bowin' to his passport'; at the end of Act One he offers to teach Rodolpho to box and hits him, and Marco answers by lifting the chair over Eddie's head as the curtain falls.","Analysis: the phrase 'ain't right' is vague, and Miller uses that vagueness to show Eddie attacking without evidence, because he has no evidence to give. The boxing lesson works in a similar way, since teaching gives Eddie a cover for hitting Rodolpho, and Marco's chair is a warning made without words, which is harder for Eddie to answer than an insult would be.","Judgement: Miller therefore presents this conflict as one that cannot be settled by talking, which prepares the audience for the violence of the ending.","Why this sits at Level 3 and not Level 5: the knowledge is sound, the examples are clearly relevant, and there is genuine comment on Miller's methods rather than plain retelling, which is what Level 3 rewards. What keeps it below Level 4 is that every comment stops after one move. The vagueness of 'ain't right' is noticed but never traced to the thing Eddie cannot say; the chair is called wordless but nothing is made of its position at the act curtain; the three references sit side by side instead of being built into one line of argument; and the judgement names an effect without weighing what kind of conflict this is. Level 4 wants that chain sustained across the whole paragraph, and Level 5 wants it evaluated — which is what the Level 5 model above does when it argues that every blow is aimed at the wrong target."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — A View from the Bridge: characters, plot, themes and key moments","items":["Text and form: A View from the Bridge by Arthur Miller, the two-act version, set in Red Hook, Brooklyn, among Italian-American longshoremen and narrated throughout by a lawyer who addresses the audience directly.","Eddie Carbone: a longshoreman, the play's tragic centre, who shelters his wife's cousins and destroys himself over his niece.","Beatrice: Eddie's wife, cousin of the two brothers, the only character who will say plainly what Eddie wants and cannot have.","Catherine: Beatrice's orphaned niece, brought up by Eddie and Beatrice, who takes a job as a stenographer and falls in love with Rodolpho.","Alfieri: a lawyer, both a character in the story and its narrator, who advises Eddie, later bails the brothers out, and opens and closes the play.","Marco: Beatrice's cousin, physically powerful and sparing with words, who has come to work and send money to a wife and three children in Italy, one of them sick.","Rodolpho: Marco's younger brother, blond, who sings, cooks and sews, and who wants to remain in America.","Minor figures: Tony, who delivers the brothers from the ship; the longshoremen Louis and Mike; the Lipari family, whose relatives are also taken in the arrest; and the Immigration Officers.","Plot, Act One in order: Alfieri introduces the story to the audience; Catherine is offered a job as a stenographer and Eddie resists until Beatrice presses him; at the table, with the cousins still to come that night, Eddie warns Catherine to say nothing, produces the name of Vinny Bolzano and has Beatrice tell the story of the boy who informed, then supplies its moral; Marco and Rodolpho arrive as 'submarines' and are taken in; Rodolpho sings 'Paper Doll' and Eddie stops him for fear of attention; as Catherine and Rodolpho begin going about together Eddie's hostility grows and he complains about Rodolpho to Louis and Mike; Eddie visits Alfieri's office and is told there is no law to help him; back in the apartment Catherine puts the 'Paper Doll' record on and dances with Rodolpho, Eddie gives Rodolpho a boxing lesson and strikes him, and the act ends with Marco's chair lift.","Plot, Act Two in order: Alfieri dates what follows to the twenty-third of December; Catherine and Rodolpho are alone in the apartment and go into the bedroom; Eddie comes home drunk, orders Rodolpho out and kisses first Catherine and then Rodolpho; Eddie visits Alfieri again and is warned to let it go, then telephones the Immigration Bureau; the officers come for the brothers and also take two other submarines lodging upstairs with relatives of Lipari the butcher; as Marco is taken out he breaks from the group, dashes back into the apartment and spits in Eddie's face in front of Beatrice, Catherine, Rodolpho and the officers, and then, from the street, with the neighbours now gathering round the stoop, he points Eddie out and accuses him of killing his children; in the prison reception room Alfieri bails the brothers out once Marco promises not to touch Eddie, telling him that this is not God and that only God makes justice; on the wedding day Marco comes to the street, Eddie demands his name back and lunges at him unarmed, Marco strikes him down and calls him an animal, and Eddie springs a knife that Marco turns back into him; Eddie dies in Beatrice's arms and Alfieri closes the play.","Theme, justice and the law: the state's law that Alfieri represents and cannot use, set against the neighbourhood's older code of honour that finally delivers the verdict.","Theme, name and reputation: standing in the community is what Eddie ultimately fights for, which is why the last confrontation must happen publicly in the street.","Theme, loyalty and betrayal: the family duty to shelter relatives, and informing as the one unforgivable act, established through the Vinny Bolzano story that Beatrice tells at Eddie's prompting in Act One.","Theme, masculinity: Rodolpho's singing, cooking and sewing sit outside Eddie's idea of a man, and Eddie attacks what he cannot categorise.","Theme, desire that cannot be named: Eddie's feeling for Catherine is never admitted by him and is spoken aloud only by Beatrice.","Key moment: Alfieri's opening address, which tells the audience the ending cannot be prevented and replaces suspense with foreknowledge.","Key moment: the Vinny Bolzano story at the table in Act One — Eddie produces the name, Beatrice tells it and Eddie supplies the moral — which puts the neighbourhood's sentence on informers on record before the cousins have even arrived.","Key moment: Eddie's first visit to Alfieri's office, where the law is shown to have nothing to offer him; its counterpart is the prison reception room in Act Two, where Alfieri will bail Marco out only on a promise and tells him that only God makes justice.","Key moment: the boxing lesson, where Eddie strikes Rodolpho under cover of teaching him.","Key moment: Marco's chair lift at the Act One curtain, a wordless reversal of authority held in front of the audience through the interval.","Key moment: the two kisses in Act Two, which make explicit what Eddie's language has only leaked.","Key moment: the telephone call to the Immigration Bureau, the act Eddie has already condemned in his own words.","Key moment: Marco spitting in Eddie's face inside the apartment as the officers take him away, before Beatrice, Catherine, Rodolpho and the officers, followed by the accusation he shouts from the street, where the neighbours have by then gathered round the stoop.","Key moment: the final confrontation in the street, in which Eddie demands his name and lunges unarmed, Marco strikes him down and calls him an animal, and Eddie springs the knife he then dies on.","Key moment: Alfieri's closing speech, which offers the standard of settling for half and then admits he mourns Eddie with alarm, denying the audience a clean verdict."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the prescribed editions and what the examination allows","items":["Component 2 is an open book examination: learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room, and the copy must be completely unmarked.","Prescribed edition: Penguin Modern Classics, March 2010, ISBN 9780141189963.","Prescribed edition: Bloomsbury, June 2022, ISBN 9781350245785.","Appendix 3 requires learners or centres to purchase a prescribed edition of each set text at the start of the course.","Because the book is in front of you, examiners expect quotation to be exact and short, chosen for the point it proves rather than copied at length.","Learners study two set texts for Component 2: one modern drama text, here A View from the Bridge, and one literary heritage text."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Miller present conflict in A View from the Bridge? You must consider language, form and structure (30 marks)","items":["Decoded question: 'In what ways does Miller present' asks for the writer's methods, not the story, so every paragraph must be about a choice Miller made; 'You must consider language, form and structure' carries the 15 AO2 marks and cannot be answered by naming devices alone; the other 15 marks are AO1, which means close knowledge, an argued position and exact, chosen references; AO4 is not assessed, so no paragraph of background.","Paragraph 1, the conflict that can be spoken: Eddie against Rodolpho, argued through the unfinished insinuation 'the guy ain't right' and the reduction of courtesy to calculation in 'only bowin' to his passport'; analyse why vagueness serves Eddie better than an accusation would.","Paragraph 2, the conflict of codes: the state's law that Alfieri represents and cannot use, against the neighbourhood's law of name and honour taught to the audience through the Vinny Bolzano story that Eddie calls for and Beatrice tells; use the office scene, where the law has nothing to offer Eddie, and Alfieri's warning to Marco in the prison that 'Only God makes justice', to measure the distance between what is legal and what this community calls right.","Paragraph 3, the conflict that has no words: Beatrice alone names it, telling Eddie that he wants 'somethin' else' and can never have Catherine; contrast her plain declarative with Eddie's evasions and show that the play's violence is a substitute for this admission.","Paragraph 4, how the form carries all three: the escalation from the boxing lesson to the chair raised at the Act One curtain to the knife in the street; Alfieri's retrospective narration removing suspense so that conflict is watched with dread rather than curiosity.","Evaluation thread: in every paragraph keep asking which conflict the audience is actually watching, and show that the visible fight is never the real one, so the essay tightens rather than lists.","Conclusion must say: the conflict is presented as tragic because it is misdirected. Eddie can fight Rodolpho, then Marco, then the whole neighbourhood for his name, but not the desire Beatrice has already named, which is why Miller gives him a death by his own weapon rather than a defeat by an enemy."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"What earns nothing in Section A, and what to write instead","items":["Retelling the plot: accurate narrative can reach only the lower levels, because AO1 asks for understanding and a critical style, not recall. Replace 'Eddie rings Immigration and the brothers are arrested' with what the placing of that call does to an audience that has already heard the Vinny Bolzano story.","Naming devices: 'Miller uses repetition' scores almost nothing, since the grid moves from identification at Level 1 to cohesive evaluation at Level 5. Say what the repeated phrase cannot finish, and why that suits the speaker.","Context: AO4 is not assessed in Section A, so paragraphs on Miller's life or on 1950s immigration policy earn no marks by themselves. If a fact is genuinely useful, turn it into analysis of the play, and keep it to a clause.","Long quotation: the paper is open book, so copying out half a page proves nothing and costs minutes. Quote a phrase, then spend the words on the effect.","Feelings without evidence: 'this makes the audience feel tense' needs the method attached. Name what Miller did, then what it does, then why it matters to the question.","Timing: Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for two 30-mark essays, so about forty-five minutes here, of which roughly five should go on choosing between the two questions and planning four paragraphs."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:12:03.020056Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All six fixes have landed and the kit is publishable: the invented crowd at the spitting is gone from every one of the six places (lesson.examples[4].setup, room.questions[9].sol, room.questions[10].sol, room.facts[7], extras[2].items[9] and items[22]), and the neighbours now appear only where Miller puts them, gathering round the stoop in the street after Marco has been taken down; Marco's cry is correctly given with the blow that fells Eddie, with the knife sprung only after he is down (step 2 and cue 2 of examples[4] and cards[7].back all re-worded to match); the eight-word quotation is trimmed to a single word; cards[14] now offers its three ways as alternatives a student can check one answer against; room.questions[4].hint 1 aims at what Eddie is warning Catherine about instead of handing over the placing; and room.questions[0] and [11] are coded AO1 and AO2 together. Re-checking the whole kit fresh, I found no false statement about the play and no quotation I cannot vouch for — the Vinny Bolzano scene (Eddie's prompt, Beatrice's telling, Eddie's moral, before the cousins arrive), the Act One chair lift, the twenty-third of December, the two kisses, the prison reception room, and the sequence of the final fight are all as Miller has them — so the only remaining faults are the two small presentational points above."}}},"drama-an-inspector-calls":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"drama-an-inspector-calls","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"An Inspector Calls is your modern drama set text for Component 2, Section A, where you answer one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two in about 45 minutes. Half those marks are AO1 (close knowledge of the play, a critical style and an informed personal engagement) and half are AO2 (analysis of the language, form and structure Priestley uses to create effects). AO4 is not assessed here, so context earns nothing on its own: it only helps when it explains an effect on the audience. The section is open book, so a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be with you, which means examiners expect exact, chosen, short quotation rather than vague recall. This room builds the argument, the evidence and the dramatic-method vocabulary that a Level 4 or Level 5 answer needs.","idea":[{"code":"Component 2: Content overview","h":"Where this play sits in the course","t":"For Component 2 you study two set texts: one modern drama and one literary heritage text. An Inspector Calls is the modern drama choice, and the specification expects the whole text to be studied, not extracts. The content overview asks you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and informed personal response. Because the play is short and continuous, you are expected to know it end to end: the three acts, the order of the Inspector's questioning, and how the last five minutes reverse everything that has gone before. A top-level answer uses that whole-text knowledge to range freely across the play, pairing a moment from Act One with one from Act Three, rather than working through the plot in order."},{"code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","h":"AO1: close knowledge, critical style, personal engagement","t":"AO1 is worth 15 of the 30 marks and asks you to demonstrate a close understanding of the drama text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the play. Close understanding means you can say who said what, to whom, and at which point in the chain of events the Inspector uncovers: Birling sacking Eva Smith for leading a demand for higher wages, Sheila having her dismissed from Milwards, Gerald keeping her as Daisy Renton, Eric fathering her child and stealing money, and Mrs Birling using her committee to refuse her. A critical style means writing about Priestley's choices rather than retelling the story, and personal engagement means having a reading of your own. A top-level answer chooses its examples discriminatingly, using the one moment that proves the point instead of three that nearly do."},{"code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","h":"AO2: language, form and structure in a play","t":"AO2 is the other 15 marks, and the question always tells you that you must consider language, form and structure. For a play, form and structure are not optional extras: they include the continuous three-act shape, the entrances and exits that leave two characters alone on stage, the cliffhanger curtain at the end of each act, the stage directions and the lighting that turns from pink and intimate to brighter and harder when the Inspector arrives. Language includes Birling's confident business idiom, Mrs Birling's class-marked phrases, the Inspector's short declaratives and the repetitions of his final speech. The grid rises from minimal identification of language, form and structure to sustained analysis and then to cohesive evaluation, so naming a device scores almost nothing. A top-level answer traces how a chosen word, a pause or a curtain line works on the audience in the theatre."},{"code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","h":"What the question looks like, and the open-book rule","t":"Section A gives one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1 and AO2 only: AO4 carries no marks in this section, and there is no comparison to make. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and is 40 per cent of the International GCSE, so this essay is worth about 45 minutes. The component is open book, and you may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition into the examination room, which must be completely unmarked: no notes, no highlighting, no sticky tabs. Having the text with you raises the standard of quotation expected rather than lowering it. A top-level answer uses the open book to check short quotations word for word and to pick discriminating evidence, not to copy out long passages that eat the clock."},{"code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","h":"The coursework route to the same skills","t":"Component 3 is the non-examined alternative to Component 2, and Assignment A is a 30-mark essay on the studied modern drama, marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. The specification's own model wording for this play is: Explore the ways in which Priestley presents women in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, with no penalty for exceeding it, and the five-level grid is applied on a best fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses rather than requiring every feature of a level. Whichever route you take, the target is the same, so a top-level answer plans for the grid: a clear line of argument, sustained analysis of method, and discriminating examples."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a stage direction into AO2 analysis","setup":"The opening stage direction tells the theatre that the lighting should be pink and intimate until the Inspector arrives, and then it should be brighter and harder. Your task: write the analysis of this one detail that a Level 5 answer would give inside an essay on how Priestley presents the Inspector. You are given nothing else — no dialogue, no later scene.","steps":["Name the method exactly: this is lighting, which belongs to the play's dramatic form, not to any character's language.","Quote it short: the light is pink and intimate before he arrives, then brighter and harder afterwards.","Analyse the first half: pink suggests a rose-tinted, flattering view, and intimate suggests a closed family circle with the outside world shut out.","Analyse the change: a harder, brighter light turns the dining room into something like an interrogation room, exposing the family to the audience before the Inspector has accused anyone.","Judge it: because the lighting does the work before a word is spoken, the Inspector is better read as a dramatic device than as an ordinary policeman."],"cues":["Is this language, form or structure?","Which words are worth quoting, and how few?","What does pink, intimate light suggest about how the Birlings see their world?","What changes for the audience the moment the light hardens?","What does this tell you about the Inspector's function in the play?"]},{"title":"Decoding the question before you plan","setup":"Here is the whole question, exactly as it would appear: Explore the ways in which Priestley presents women in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. It carries thirty marks, half AO1 and half AO2, and you have about forty-five minutes. Your task: decode it and fix the three presentations you will write about, before writing a word of the essay.","steps":["Fix the focus: women here means Sheila, Mrs Birling and Eva Smith, who is talked about constantly but never appears on stage.","Fix the command: explore the ways in which Priestley presents them, so each paragraph must be about a choice he made, not about what the women do.","Obey the instruction: language, form and structure are named in the question, so plan at least one paragraph led by form or structure rather than by a quotation.","Choose discriminating material instead of covering everyone: Sheila's change across the acts, Mrs Birling's class-marked language, and Eva as an absent figure the audience only ever hears described.","Write the thread in one sentence: Priestley presents women largely through the men's treatment of them, so that the audience ends up judging the men."],"cues":["Which characters does the focus word actually cover?","What must every paragraph be about, given the command word?","Which instruction in the question is worth half the marks?","Is covering every woman better than choosing three?","What single argument could hold all three paragraphs together?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 5 paragraph from one short quotation","setup":"In Act One, after her father has explained why he sacked Eva Smith for asking for more money, Sheila says: But these girls aren't cheap labour — they're people. Your task: build one paragraph from this line for the question In what ways does Priestley present responsibility in An Inspector Calls? You must consider language, form and structure.","steps":["Point first: Priestley uses Sheila to voice the objection the audience should be making to her father's economics.","Evidence: quote the contrast she draws between cheap labour and people, and note that it answers Birling directly.","Analyse the language: the antithesis sets a costing term against a human one, and the dash stages the correction as it happens, so the audience hears a mind changing on stage.","Analyse the form: this comes in dialogue, immediately after Birling's long confident speeches, so a short line interrupts a monologue and shifts the play's sympathy.","Mini-judgement: Priestley makes responsibility sound like plain common sense in the mouth of the youngest character, which is why the older Birlings' refusal later looks wilful rather than reasonable."],"cues":["What claim about Priestley could this line support?","How much of the line do you need to quote?","Which two words are being set against each other, and why?","What is happening dramatically around the line?","What does this add to an argument about responsibility?"]},{"title":"Using structure as evidence: the act endings","setup":"Two facts about the play's shape: Act One ends with Sheila realising Gerald knew Daisy Renton, at which point the Inspector reappears in the doorway and says Well? Act Two ends with Mrs Birling insisting the young man responsible must be dealt with, just as Eric enters and the family understand that he is the father. Your task: use these two endings as evidence in an essay on how Priestley controls the audience.","steps":["Name the pattern: both acts break off at the moment of maximum revelation, a cliffhanger curtain.","Analyse the first: a single word from the Inspector, placed after Sheila's realisation, hands the audience a question rather than an answer and makes him seem to know everything already.","Analyse the second: Mrs Birling condemns the unknown young man, so the curtain traps her in her own judgement the instant her son walks in, which is dramatic irony built out of timing rather than words.","Judge it: Priestley uses the interval, when the audience can talk, to make them do the moral work, so structure carries the argument as much as the dialogue does."],"cues":["What do the two endings have in common?","Why place one word there rather than a speech?","Who is caught out by the timing of the second ending?","What is the audience doing between the acts?"]},{"title":"Lifting a paragraph out of Level 2","setup":"A student writes this paragraph on the Inspector: The Inspector comes to the house and asks everyone questions about the girl. He finds out that they all did something bad to her. This shows that Priestley thinks people should be responsible. Your task: diagnose why the grid places it low and rewrite it to reach the top level.","steps":["Diagnose: it is largely narrative and there is no quotation, so it can only meet the lower band for knowledge and the minimal band for method.","Add exact, short evidence: the Inspector tells them that each of them helped to kill her, and orders them to remember that and never forget it.","Analyse a choice: a flat declarative followed by two short imperatives, none of them carrying a subordinate clause, turns the accusation into a sentence passed rather than an opinion offered.","Add form or structure: he delivers this near the end, to the whole family at once, after questioning them one at a time, so the play gathers five private guilts into one public charge.","Close with judgement: the Inspector is less a detective than a moral summing-up, which is why the plot can afford to reveal that no such officer exists."],"cues":["Which words in the paragraph are simply retelling?","What would you quote, and how short can it be?","What is distinctive about the sentence shapes?","Where in the play does this land, and why there?","What judgement does the evidence now allow?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which two assessment objectives carry the marks in Component 2 Section A, and in what proportion?","a":"AO1 and AO2, 15 raw marks each out of 30. AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in this section."},{"q":"You may take a copy of the play into the examination. What condition must it be in?","a":"It must be the prescribed edition and completely unmarked: no notes, highlighting or tabs."},{"q":"Why does naming a technique score almost nothing under AO2?","a":"The grid rewards analysis and then cohesive evaluation of how language, form and structure create meaning; identification alone sits at Level 1."},{"q":"Give the one-sentence test for whether a paragraph is critical rather than narrative.","a":"Ask whether the sentence is about a choice Priestley made and its effect on the audience; if it is about what happened next, it is narrative."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2 Section A is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1 (15 marks) and AO2 (15 marks) only; the component is 1 hour 30 minutes, 60 marks and 40 per cent of the International GCSE.","Component 2 is open book: a clean copy of the prescribed edition may be taken into the examination and must be completely unmarked.","An Inspector Calls is in three continuous acts, set in the dining room of the Birlings' suburban house in Brumley, an industrial city in the North Midlands, on an evening in spring 1912.","The opening stage direction asks for lighting that is pink and intimate until the Inspector arrives and then brighter and harder, and the Inspector is to create at once an impression of massiveness, solidity and purposefulness.","The Inspector's chain runs: Birling sacks Eva Smith for leading a demand for higher wages; Sheila has her dismissed from Milwards; Gerald keeps her as Daisy Renton; Eric makes her pregnant and offers stolen money; Mrs Birling's committee refuses her appeal.","Priestley wrote the play in 1945 and set it in 1912; it was first performed in Moscow in 1945 and had its British premiere in London in 1946.","The five-level grid runs from Level 1 (1 to 6 marks) to Level 5 (25 to 30 marks) and is applied on a best fit basis, rising from minimal identification of language, form and structure to sustained analysis (Level 4) and cohesive evaluation with discriminating examples (Level 5)."],"questions":[{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: state the chain of events the Inspector uncovers, naming what each of the five characters did to Eva Smith, in the order the events happened in her life. (5 marks)","a":"Birling sacked her for leading a demand for higher wages; Sheila had her dismissed from Milwards; Gerald kept her as his mistress, Daisy Renton; Eric made her pregnant and offered her stolen money; Mrs Birling had her charity appeal refused.","sol":"Mr Birling sacked her from his works for leading the other girls in asking for higher wages. She was then taken on at Milwards, the department store, and Sheila, jealous and in a temper over a dress, used the family's influence to have her dismissed. Under the name Daisy Renton she was picked up by Gerald in the bar at the Palace Variety Theatre, installed in a friend's rooms and kept as his mistress until he ended the affair. Eric met her in the same bar, forced his way into her lodgings, made her pregnant and gave her money he had stolen from his father's office, which she refused once she knew where it came from. She then went to the Brumley Women's Charity Organisation calling herself Mrs Birling, and Mrs Birling, chairing the committee, used her influence to have the appeal refused. She drank disinfectant and died in the Infirmary.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The chain you are asked for is Eva Smith's own story, which is not the same sequence as the Inspector's questioning of the family.","Follow the girl through her jobs and her lodgings rather than through the Birlings' seating plan.","Begin with the works, then the shop, then the two men, then the committee."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: what does the opening stage direction say about the lighting, and what does the change achieve? (3 marks)","a":"The lighting should be pink and intimate until the Inspector arrives, and then brighter and harder; the change exposes the family to the audience.","sol":"Priestley's opening stage direction asks for lighting that is pink and intimate until the Inspector arrives, and then brighter and harder. Before he comes, the pink light flatters the scene and matches the family's rose-tinted account of itself, while intimate suggests a closed circle with the outside world shut out. The moment the light hardens, the dining room becomes something closer to an interrogation room, and the audience is invited to look at the Birlings critically rather than to share the celebration. Because this happens in the lighting rather than the dialogue, it is evidence of Priestley's use of dramatic form, and it marks the Inspector as a device that changes the terms of the play, not merely a new character.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look before any character speaks: the direction is on the play's first page.","Think about what a colour and a level of brightness suggest about how we are meant to see a room.","Give the two lighting states, then say what each does to the audience's view of the family."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: which assessment objectives are assessed in Component 2 Section A, how many raw marks does each carry, and how long is the whole component? (3 marks)","a":"AO1 (15 marks) and AO2 (15 marks), out of 30; Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks, 40 per cent of the qualification.","sol":"Section A, Modern Drama, is one 30-mark essay question chosen from two on each set text, and it assesses AO1 and AO2 only, with 15 raw marks for each. AO1 is close knowledge and understanding of the text, a critical style and an informed personal engagement; AO2 is analysis of the language, form and structure the writer uses to create meanings and effects. AO3 and AO4 carry no marks here, so there is no comparison to make and no credit for context in its own right. Component 2 as a whole is a written examination of 1 hour 30 minutes worth 60 raw marks and 40 per cent of the International GCSE, which makes roughly 45 minutes the sensible share for this essay.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the four objectives are simply not tested in this section.","The marks split evenly between knowing the play and analysing how it is written.","Give the two objectives with their marks, then the component's time and weighting."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: what may you take into the Component 2 examination, and what must its condition be? (2 marks)","a":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition of each set text, which must be completely unmarked.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners may take a copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room. The copy must be clean and completely unmarked, which rules out annotations, highlighting, underlining and sticky tabs, and centres or learners must buy a prescribed edition at the start of the course. Having the text available does not lower the standard of quotation expected: because the words are in front of you, examiners expect quotations to be exact, short and chosen for the point being made, rather than long passages copied out to fill the page.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The rule is about the book, not about notes you have made elsewhere.","Ask what would give one candidate an advantage over another who owned the same edition.","Name the edition condition first, then say what it means for how you quote."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: explain what dramatic irony is and identify two moments in Act One where Priestley relies on it. (4 marks)","a":"Dramatic irony is where the audience knows more than the character speaking. Birling calls the Titanic unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, and insists the Germans do not want war.","sol":"Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something the character on stage does not, so the words carry a meaning the speaker never intended. Priestley builds most of Act One on it. Birling assures the table that the Titanic is unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, and a 1945 audience knew the ship sank on her maiden voyage in 1912; he also dismisses talk of conflict with the claim that the Germans don't want war, to an audience that had lived through two world wars. The effect is not comic decoration. Every confident prediction that the audience knows to be false discredits the speaker in advance, so that when Birling insists that a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own, the audience is already primed to reject that too. Priestley therefore uses dramatic irony structurally, to destroy Birling's authority before the Inspector ever challenges him.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The irony depends on the gap between when the play is set and when it was first performed.","Look for Birling's confident predictions about the future.","Define the term, give the two predictions, then say what the technique does to Birling's credibility."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Priestley presents Sheila Birling in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Sheila is presented as the play's moral barometer: introduced as a pretty, pleased, rather childish girl, she is the first to accept responsibility, the first to see through the Inspector's method, and by Act Three the voice that judges her parents.","sol":"Priestley presents Sheila less as a character than as the change he wants his audience to undergo. At the start the stage direction places her as a pretty girl in her early twenties, very pleased with life and rather excited, and her language matches it: she teases Gerald, calls her mother mummy and worries about a dress. Priestley gives her that shallowness deliberately, so that her transformation costs the audience nothing in plausibility. The first crack comes in Act One, where her interruption that these girls aren't cheap labour — they're people answers her father's economics with a plain antithesis, a costing term set against a human one, and the dash stages the correction as it happens. Structurally, Priestley places this immediately after Birling's long complacent speeches, so a short line breaks a monologue and shifts the audience's sympathy from father to daughter. Once she confesses the Milwards incident she does not defend herself; she says she will never, never do it again to anybody, the repetition sounding less like self-pity than a resolution. From Act Two she becomes Priestley's commentator inside the play, warning her mother not to try to build up a kind of wall between us and that girl, a metaphor that names exactly what her parents spend the last act doing. By Act Three, when the others are celebrating the hoax, her flat, bitter question of whether they are all nice people now turns her into the audience's own judgement spoken aloud. Priestley's method is therefore structural as much as verbal: he lets her grow across three continuous acts while her parents stand still, so that the generational split the final telephone call exploits has already been dramatised in her.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Track how differently she speaks in Act One and in Act Three.","The question asks how she is presented, so build paragraphs on Priestley's choices, including where he places her lines.","Open with a single argument about what she is for in the play, then prove it from each act in turn."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"In what ways does Priestley present responsibility in An Inspector Calls? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Responsibility is presented as a chain: each character's small act is shown to be a link, set against Birling's every-man-for-himself creed, and driven home by the Inspector's final speech and the cyclical ending.","sol":"Priestley presents responsibility as something collective, and he proves it structurally before he states it. The Inspector's method is the argument: he takes one person and one line of inquiry at a time, so that five separate, seemingly minor acts — a sacking, a complaint in a shop, an affair, a theft, a refused appeal — are laid end to end until they form a single death. No character's share looks lethal alone, which is exactly the defence each of them offers, and exactly what the chain refutes. Against this Priestley sets Birling's creed, delivered in Act One with the confidence of a hard-headed, practical man of business, that a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own, and his contempt for community and all that nonsense. Because the audience has just heard him call the Titanic unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, his social philosophy is discredited by association before it is answered. The Inspector's reply comes in the play's most patterned language: we don't live alone, we are members of one body, we are responsible for each other, where the plain declaratives and the body metaphor make interdependence sound like fact rather than opinion, and the warning that the lesson will otherwise be taught in fire and blood and anguish gave a 1945 audience the two wars it had just lived through. The generational division sharpens the theme: Sheila and Eric accept that they all helped to kill her, while their parents' relief at the hoax shows that for them responsibility was only ever fear of scandal. Priestley's final move is structural: the telephone call announcing a real inspector returns the play to its beginning, so responsibility is presented not as a lesson learned but as a bill that will be presented again.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Notice that the Inspector's way of questioning is itself an argument about the theme.","Set the older Birlings' position against the younger characters' and let the ending decide between them.","Begin with what Priestley wants the audience to accept, then show how the chain of interviews and the final call enforce it."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Priestley presents Mr Birling in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Birling is presented as a confidently wrong capitalist: dramatic irony destroys his authority in Act One, his business idiom reduces people to costs, and his unchanged relief in Act Three shows the older generation cannot learn.","sol":"Priestley presents Birling as a man whose certainty is the point of him. Act One gives him the floor, and his long speeches are full of the idiom of business: he speaks as a hard-headed, practical man of business, looks forward to Crofts and Birlings working together for lower costs and higher prices, and reduces his workforce to labour costs, so that a marriage and a merger become the same sentence. Priestley then dismantles him with dramatic irony rather than argument. Birling declares the Titanic unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable and insists the Germans don't want war, and a first audience in 1945 heard those confident predictions with the knowledge of a sunken ship and two world wars. The effect is structural: by discrediting Birling's judgement about the future, Priestley discredits in advance his social creed that a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own, and his sneer at community and all that nonsense. Under questioning, his language narrows to money and reputation: he offers thousands, but only once he fears exposure, and what alarms him most is the public scandal that would cost him a knighthood, so Priestley shows conscience replaced by calculation. The contrast with his children is carefully staged — his complaint about the famous younger generation who know it all comes moments before the telephone rings — and the timing makes the final twist a verdict on him personally. Priestley presents Birling, then, as a type rather than a villain: pompous, unshakeable, and therefore incapable of the change the play demands, which is why the ending gives him no escape and the audience no sympathy.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Two of his Act One predictions are ones the audience knows to be wrong.","Read his vocabulary: what kind of words does he use for people and for events?","Argue what Priestley uses him for, then show how irony, idiom and the final call all serve that purpose."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam tariff: write one paragraph of Level 5 quality analysing how Priestley uses the Inspector's final speech to close the play, then say in a sentence what a Level 2 version of the same paragraph would have missed. (8 marks)","a":"The final speech uses collective pronouns, the body metaphor, the millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths, and the warning of fire and blood and anguish; a Level 2 version would summarise it and name no method.","sol":"Priestley makes the Inspector's exit speech the point where the play stops being a detective story and becomes a sermon. The pronouns turn outward — we don't live alone, we are members of one body, we are responsible for each other — so that the accusation includes the audience as well as the family, and the plain, unqualified declaratives leave no room for the debate the Birlings want to have. The body metaphor converts a moral claim into a physical fact: a body cannot decide that one of its parts does not concern it. He then widens the focus, insisting that one Eva Smith has gone but there are millions and millions and millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths still left with us, and the triple repetition makes an individual tragedy into a social one while the ordinary surnames make the victims anyone. The closing warning that the lesson will be taught in fire and blood and anguish reached a 1945 audience that had just lived through a second world war, so prophecy sounds like history. Structurally, Priestley gives this speech to a character he is about to reveal as no real inspector, which frees it from the plot: nothing about the hoax weakens it. A Level 2 version would retell what the Inspector says and call it a powerful message, without quoting, without naming a single method, and without noticing that the speech is aimed past the Birlings at the audience.","m":8,"d":2,"hints":["Look at who the pronouns include by the end of the speech.","One image treats society as a physical thing; one repetition turns one death into many.","Write point, short quotation, analysis of the chosen words, then a judgement about the effect on the audience."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Priestley presents the Inspector in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The Inspector is presented as a dramatic device: lighting and stage directions give him authority, his controlled method structures the play, his language is plain and moral, and the final revelation makes him a conscience rather than a policeman.","sol":"Priestley presents the Inspector as a force rather than a character, and the presentation begins before he speaks. The stage direction asks that he create at once an impression of massiveness, solidity and purposefulness, qualities of an object as much as a man, while the lighting turns from pink and intimate to brighter and harder at his entrance, so dramatic form establishes his authority ahead of any dialogue. His method then shapes the whole play: he insists on one person and one line of inquiry at a time, controls who sees the photograph, and refuses to be hurried, so that the structure of the three continuous acts is really the structure of his interrogation. His language is the opposite of Birling's. Where Birling speaks in long, self-satisfied paragraphs, the Inspector answers in short declaratives and pointed reversals — that it is better to ask for the earth than to take it, and that public men have responsibilities as well as privileges — so that a single line repeatedly closes down a speech. Priestley also allows him a register no policeman would use, culminating in the final speech's body metaphor and its prophecy of fire and blood and anguish. The last act then withdraws his official identity altogether: Gerald establishes that no such officer exists, and the audience is left to decide whether he was a hoaxer, a ghost, or the family's conscience given a body. Because the girl's death is confirmed by the closing telephone call, the argument survives the man, which is Priestley's real point: the Inspector is a dramatic device for making a private evening into a public trial, and the audience is in the dock with the Birlings.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Some of the strongest evidence is in stage directions rather than speeches.","Compare the length and shape of his sentences with Birling's.","Decide what he is for in the play, then show how his entrance, his method and the final twist all serve it."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Priestley uses the structure of An Inspector Calls to control what the audience knows and feels. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Three continuous acts, one confession per interview, cliffhanger curtains, the withheld photograph, the double twist of the hoax and the real call, and a cyclical ending that returns the play to its start.","sol":"Structure is Priestley's chief instrument, and an answer that treats it as scaffolding rather than content misses half the play. The three acts run continuously in one room across a single evening, a near-classical unity that traps the family where they cannot escape one another or the audience's gaze. Within that frame Priestley uses a chain: the Inspector takes one person and one line of inquiry at a time, so the audience receives exactly one confession per movement and can always see one step further than the character being questioned. The withheld photograph works the same way, shown to one person at a time so that no Birling can compare stories and no spectator can be certain the same girl is meant. Each act then breaks at the point of maximum exposure. Act One ends as Sheila realises Gerald knew Daisy Renton and the Inspector reappears with a single word, so the interval is spent on a question rather than an answer; Act Two ends with Mrs Birling condemning the unknown young man just as Eric enters, and the curtain traps her in her own judgement. The final act springs a double reversal: first the relief of the hoax, which sorts the family into those who have learned and those who have not, and then the telephone call announcing a girl dead of disinfectant and an inspector on his way, which returns the play to its own opening situation. That cyclical close is Priestley's judgement rather than a trick, because it gives the unrepentant characters the second chance they have already proved they will waste, and it sends the audience out with the interrogation still to come.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Ask at every point what the audience knows that a character does not.","The act endings and the last two minutes are the strongest structural evidence in the play.","Argue that structure carries the meaning, then take the chain, the curtains and the ending in turn."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Priestley presents women in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Sheila changes and gains a voice, Mrs Birling's class language protects her from sympathy, and Eva Smith is powerful precisely because she is absent and only ever described by others.","sol":"Priestley presents women in this play largely through how men and money dispose of them, and his boldest choice is to keep the central woman off the stage. Eva Smith is never seen or heard: she exists only in a photograph the audience never sees and in five accounts given by the people who harmed her, so she can be renamed Daisy Renton, re-described and disputed at will. That absence is the point. It dramatises how completely a working woman's story depends on those with power over her, and it allows the Inspector to close by insisting that there are millions and millions and millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths still left with us, turning one woman into a class. Mrs Birling is presented as the enemy of that recognition. Her language is marked by class at every turn — the phrase girls of that class dismisses a whole group in three words — and Priestley makes her chair a charity that exists to judge rather than to help, so that respectable womanhood is shown policing other women. The stage direction that establishes her as a rather cold woman and her husband's social superior tells us her authority is inherited, not earned, which is why she cannot imagine a life like Eva's. Sheila stands between the two. She begins with a woman's conventional power over another woman, using her family's standing to have a shop girl sacked over a dress, and she is the only character who renounces it, promising never to do it again to anybody. Structurally, Priestley gives her the last word on her parents' complacency, so the play's moral authority passes from a man's committee and a woman's charity to the youngest woman in the room.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["One of the three women in the play never appears on stage: ask why Priestley chose that.","Class is the language Mrs Birling thinks in, so quote it rather than summarising her attitude.","Take the three women in turn and hold them together with one argument about power."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"In what ways does Priestley present the conflict between the generations in An Inspector Calls? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The young confess and change while the old defend and forget; Priestley marks this by language, by who is on stage at act endings, and by giving the young the play's judgements before the final call.","sol":"The generational conflict is Priestley's way of making an argument about 1945 out of a family set in 1912. He divides the household by how each member reacts to the same evidence. Sheila and Eric accept their part at once: Sheila promises never to do it again to anybody, and Eric states flatly that the girl is dead and they all helped to kill her, a sentence with no qualification in it. Their parents respond with procedure and status. Birling's instinct is to buy a way out, offering thousands only when exposure threatens, and his real fear is the public scandal that would cost him a knighthood, while Mrs Birling retreats behind the phrase girls of that class. Priestley sharpens the split through language: the older characters speak in the idioms of business and committee, the younger in plain, personal sentences. He also stages it. Act Three sets the parents' relief at the hoax against the children's refusal to be relieved, and Sheila's flat question about whether they are all nice people now punctures the celebration. Birling's sneer at the famous younger generation who know it all is placed immediately before the telephone rings, so the structure itself contradicts him: the young were right, and the timing proves it without a word of commentary. Priestley's purpose is didactic, not domestic. A 1945 audience rebuilding after a second war is being told that the people who made the old world cannot be trusted to remake it, and the young characters are the audience's proxies, which is why they, not the Inspector, are left on stage to face the second inspector's arrival.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The family divides not by who is guilty but by what each does with guilt.","Watch where Priestley puts Birling's complaint about young people in relation to the telephone.","Argue what the split is for, then prove it from the language of each generation and from the ending."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Stretch task, not an exam tariff: a candidate's essay on the Inspector devotes most of its space to retelling what each character did to Eva Smith, with two long quotations copied from the open-book text and no comment on how the play is written. Using the levels, say where this answer would sit and what it must do to reach the top level. (6 marks)","a":"Largely narrative with minimal identification of method places it at Level 2; it needs analysis of language, form and structure, short chosen quotations and a sustained critical line to reach Levels 4 and 5.","sol":"An answer that is largely narrative meets the Level 2 descriptor for knowledge and understanding, because retelling shows some knowledge but no critical style, and it is likely to sit at Level 1 or 2 for method, since copying long quotations without comment is at best minimal identification of language, form and structure. It forfeits most of the 15 AO2 marks and much of the AO1 credit for a critical style and informed personal engagement. To move up, the candidate must convert each retold event into a claim about Priestley's choices: why the interviews come in that order, why the Inspector answers in short declaratives where Birling speaks in paragraphs, why the lighting changes at his entrance. The quotations must shrink and be chosen for the words being analysed, which is what the open book is for. Level 4 requires thorough personal engagement and sustained analysis with fully relevant examples, so every paragraph needs a point, a short quotation, a developed comment on the method and a mini-judgement. Level 5 additionally requires cohesive evaluation and discriminating use of examples, which means one argument running through the essay and evidence picked because it is the best available, not because it was easy to find.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The grid describes a largely narrative answer explicitly: find that band.","Work out which objective is losing the most marks and what the descriptors above it demand.","Name the level, then say what each paragraph must contain instead."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Stretch task, not an exam tariff: Priestley wrote the play in 1945 but set it in 1912. AO4 carries no marks in Section A. Explain how context can still strengthen a Section A answer, using one example. (5 marks)","a":"Context earns nothing on its own here, but it explains an AO2 effect: the 1912 setting lets a 1945 audience hear Birling's confidence about the Titanic and about war as dramatic irony.","sol":"In Section A only AO1 and AO2 are assessed, so a paragraph of biography or history scores nothing by itself and costs time. Context becomes useful only when it is the reason a dramatic method works. The clearest example is the gap between the setting and the first performance: the play takes place on an evening in spring 1912, but it was written in 1945 and first staged that year. When Birling assures the table that the Titanic is unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, and that the Germans don't want war, the audience's knowledge of the sinking and of two world wars is what converts confident statements into dramatic irony. Writing that Priestley uses dramatic irony here, because the first audience knew what the speaker could not, is AO2 analysis of structure with the context doing the work inside it. Writing a separate paragraph about the Titanic, rationing or the 1945 election is AO4 material that this section does not reward. The test is simple: if the contextual fact can be deleted without weakening the point about the writing, it should be.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Check first which objectives this section actually pays for.","Ask what a first audience knew that the characters on stage could not.","Give the rule, then one worked instance where the fact explains a technique."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which two assessment objectives carry the 30 marks in Component 2 Section A?","a":"AO1 and AO2, 15 raw marks each."},{"q":"What does the opening stage direction say the lighting does when the Inspector arrives?","a":"It changes from pink and intimate to brighter and harder."},{"q":"Name the five characters' actions against Eva Smith in the order the Inspector uncovers them.","a":"Birling sacks her; Sheila has her dismissed from Milwards; Gerald keeps her as Daisy Renton; Mrs Birling refuses her charity appeal at the end of Act Two; Eric admits making her pregnant and offering stolen money in Act Three."},{"q":"Why does a long quotation copied from the open-book text usually score badly?","a":"AO2 rewards analysis of chosen words; length without comment counts only as identification, at the bottom of the grid."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Component 2 Section A: the question and the marks","back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two on the modern drama text, assessing AO1 (15) and AO2 (15). No AO3 or AO4 credit; about 45 minutes.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"The open-book rule for Component 2","back":"You may take a copy of the prescribed edition into the examination; it must be completely unmarked, with no notes, highlighting or tabs.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in Section A","back":"Close knowledge and understanding of the play, a critical style, an informed personal engagement, and relevant examples used from the text.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO2 in Section A","back":"Analysis of the language, form and structure Priestley uses to create meanings and effects, using appropriate literary terminology.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Setting of An Inspector Calls","back":"The dining room of the Birlings' suburban house in Brumley, an industrial city in the North Midlands, on an evening in spring 1912; three continuous acts.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"The play's opening lighting direction","back":"The lighting should be pink and intimate until the Inspector arrives, and then it should be brighter and harder.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Inspector Goole's entrance direction","back":"He need not be a big man but creates at once an impression of massiveness, solidity and purposefulness.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Eva Smith / Daisy Renton","back":"The working-class woman whose death the Inspector investigates. She never appears on stage and is known only through the accounts of those who harmed her.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Arthur Birling","back":"Prosperous manufacturer who calls himself a hard-headed, practical man of business and insists a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Sybil Birling","back":"A rather cold woman and her husband's social superior; chairs the Brumley Women's Charity Organisation and refuses Eva's appeal, dismissing girls of that class.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Dramatic irony in Act One","back":"The audience knows what the speaker cannot: Birling calls the Titanic unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, and says the Germans don't want war.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Cyclical structure","back":"The final telephone call reports a girl dead of disinfectant and an inspector on his way, returning the play to its opening situation and denying the unrepentant their escape.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"One way Priestley builds tension at an act ending","back":"Act One breaks off as the Inspector reappears and says Well?; Act Two breaks off as Eric enters at the moment his mother condemns the unknown father; each curtain leaves the audience one step ahead of a character.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":true},{"front":"The Inspector's final speech","back":"We don't live alone, we are members of one body, we are responsible for each other; millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths remain, or the lesson comes in fire and blood and anguish.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment A","back":"The coursework alternative: a teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the modern drama text, 15 marks AO1 and 15 AO2, 650 to 800 words advised, internally assessed and moderated by Pearson.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: Explore the ways in which Priestley presents Mrs Birling in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Priestley presents Mrs Birling's class prejudice not as an opinion she holds but as the language she thinks in, so that she condemns herself without ever intending to.","Evidence: Explaining why she disbelieved the applicant, she reaches for the phrase girls of that class, and the stage direction that introduces her has already established her as a rather cold woman and her husband's social superior.","Analysis: The demonstrative in that class does the work of an argument: it sorts a woman she has met into a category without a name, a history or a claim on her, and the fact that Priestley gives her the phrase in passing, as an explanation she expects to be accepted, shows the assumption is habitual rather than argued. Her authority is inherited, not earned, which is why she can chair a charity while lacking any imagination of poverty.","Form and structure: Priestley then turns her language against her by timing. He lets her insist at length that the young man responsible should be made to answer for it, and closes Act Two as Eric walks in, so the curtain falls on a mother who has just sentenced her own son. The audience is given the interval to enjoy a trap she cannot see.","Mini-judgement: Mrs Birling is therefore Priestley's sharpest instrument against respectability: she is not cruel by intention but by class reflex, which is a more disturbing charge, and the structure convicts her more effectively than any speech by the Inspector could."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: Explore the ways in which Priestley presents Mrs Birling in An Inspector Calls. You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Priestley presents Mrs Birling as snobbish and unkind to Eva Smith.","Evidence: She talks about girls of that class and she refuses to help the girl when she comes to her committee.","Analysis: This shows that she looks down on working-class people and does not think they deserve help, which makes the audience dislike her.","Mini-judgement: So Priestley uses Mrs Birling to show that upper-class people were snobbish.","Why this is Level 2 and not Level 3 or Level 5: the point is relevant and the example is the right one, but the comment on language stops at what the phrase shows rather than analysing how that class does the sorting, and no form or structure is used at all, so the Act Two curtain and the dramatic irony of condemning her own son are missed. That is some comment on language, form and structure, which is the Level 2 descriptor, not the sound understanding of language, form and structure Level 3 asks for; the judgement generalises about a whole class instead of judging Priestley's method, and the engagement is dutiful rather than personal, where Level 3 wants relevant personal engagement and an appropriate critical style. To reach Level 3 it needs the chosen words analysed and one structural point; to reach Level 5 it needs sustained analysis, cohesive evaluation and a judgement about the effect Priestley creates."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — An Inspector Calls: the characters","items":["J B Priestley (1894 to 1984) wrote the play in 1945, set it in 1912, and saw it first performed in Moscow in 1945, with its British premiere in London in 1946.","Arthur Birling: a prosperous manufacturer and former Lord Mayor of Brumley, a magistrate, hoping for a knighthood; describes himself as a hard-headed, practical man of business and preaches that a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own.","Sybil Birling: his wife, described in the stage directions as a rather cold woman and her husband's social superior; chairs the Brumley Women's Charity Organisation and uses her influence to have Eva's appeal refused, referring to girls of that class.","Sheila Birling: their daughter, in her early twenties, introduced as pretty, very pleased with life and rather excited; had Eva dismissed from Milwards out of jealousy, confesses first, and promises never, never to do it again to anybody.","Eric Birling: their son, in his early twenties, described as not quite at ease, half shy, half assertive; drinks too much, makes Eva pregnant, steals money from his father's office and states that the girl is dead and they all helped to kill her.","Gerald Croft: Sheila's fiance, son of Sir George and Lady Croft of Crofts Limited; kept Eva, under the name Daisy Renton, as his mistress, and in Act Three establishes that no Inspector Goole exists on the police force and offers Sheila the ring again.","Inspector Goole: introduced as creating at once an impression of massiveness, solidity and purposefulness; works by one person and one line of inquiry at a time and leaves after the speech about being members of one body.","Eva Smith, also called Daisy Renton: the working-class woman whose death is investigated; she never appears on stage and exists only in the accounts of the people who harmed her, and in a photograph the audience never sees."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the plot in order and the key moments an examiner rewards","items":["Act One opens on the Birlings celebrating Sheila's engagement to Gerald; Birling makes his speeches about business, about the Titanic being unsinkable, absolutely unsinkable, and about the Germans not wanting war.","Birling's creed that a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own is interrupted by the doorbell: the Inspector arrives at the precise moment the play's opposing argument has been stated.","The Inspector reports that a girl died in the Infirmary after swallowing strong disinfectant, and shows a photograph to Birling alone, insisting on one person and one line of inquiry at a time.","Birling admits sacking Eva Smith from his works for leading the girls in asking for higher wages; Sheila objects that these girls aren't cheap labour, they're people, and later confesses to having had Eva dismissed from Milwards over a dress.","Act One ends with Sheila realising that Gerald knew Daisy Renton, and the Inspector reappearing in the doorway with the single word Well?","In Act Two Gerald admits meeting Daisy Renton in the bar at the Palace Variety Theatre and keeping her in a friend's rooms until he ended the affair; Sheila returns the ring but says she respects him more for telling the truth.","Sheila warns her mother not to try to build up a kind of wall between us and that girl, because the Inspector will only break it down.","Mrs Birling admits that her committee refused Eva's appeal when the girl came calling herself Mrs Birling, and insists the father of the child should be made to answer for it; Act Two ends as Eric enters.","In Act Three Eric admits the pregnancy and the money stolen from his father's office, which Eva refused once she knew where it came from; the Inspector tells the family that each of them helped to kill her, that they should remember that and never forget it.","The Inspector's exit speech: we don't live alone, we are members of one body, we are responsible for each other; there are millions and millions and millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths, and the lesson will otherwise be taught in fire and blood and anguish.","After he leaves, Gerald establishes that no such inspector exists and the family conclude it was a hoax; Sheila asks bitterly whether they are all nice people now, Gerald holds up the ring and asks her about it, and Birling's sneer at the famous younger generation who know it all is the last thing said before the telephone.","The telephone rings: a girl has just died on her way to the Infirmary after swallowing some disinfectant, and a police inspector is on his way to ask questions."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — form, structure, themes and the context that actually helps","items":["Form: a three-act play whose action is continuous, in one room, in one evening, so the family cannot leave the situation or each other; often described as a well-made play used for the purposes of a morality play.","Structure: the Inspector's chain of interviews, one confession per movement, with the photograph shown to one person at a time so the audience always knows more than the character on stage.","Structure: cliffhanger curtains at the end of Acts One and Two, then the double reversal of Act Three, the hoax followed by the real telephone call, giving the play a cyclical ending.","Language: Birling's business idiom of costs, prices and scandal against the Inspector's short declaratives, reversals and the patterned repetitions of his final speech.","Themes: collective responsibility against self-interest; class and the power of respectability; gender and the treatment of working women; the generational divide between those who learn and those who forget.","Context that earns marks here: the gap between the 1912 setting and the 1945 writing is what makes Birling's confidence about the Titanic and about war work as dramatic irony.","Context to keep out of Section A: AO4 is not assessed, so biography, the 1945 election, the welfare state or the wars belong in an answer only where they explain an effect Priestley creates.","Prescribed editions for the open-book examination include Penguin Modern Classics (New Edition, March 2001, ISBN 9780141185354), Heinemann (January 1993, ISBN 9780435232825) and Firestone Books (March 2020, ISBN 9781909608405)."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Priestley present responsibility in An Inspector Calls? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","items":["Decoded question: in what ways means the methods, so every paragraph must be about a choice Priestley made; presents means the audience's experience, not the characters' opinions; the instruction about language, form and structure is worth the 15 AO2 marks, so at least one paragraph must be led by dramatic form or structure.","Thesis to state in the opening lines: Priestley does not argue for collective responsibility so much as construct it, building a chain in which five small, defensible acts add up to one death, and then denying the audience an escape at the end.","Paragraph 1 — the Inspector's method as argument: one person and one line of inquiry at a time, the photograph shown singly, so the structure itself proves that separate acts form a chain; effect on an audience that can see the pattern before the family can.","Paragraph 2 — Birling's creed and how Priestley discredits it: a man has to mind his own business and look after himself and his own, and the sneer at community and all that nonsense, both placed after the unsinkable Titanic and the claim that the Germans don't want war, so dramatic irony destroys the speaker before the Inspector answers him.","Paragraph 3 — the language of the final speech: members of one body, we are responsible for each other, the millions of Eva Smiths and John Smiths, and fire and blood and anguish; plain declaratives and the body metaphor turn a moral claim into a fact, and the pronouns widen to include the audience.","Paragraph 4 — the generational split as the test of responsibility: Sheila's never, never do it again to anybody and Eric's the girl's dead and we all helped to kill her against their parents' relief at the hoax and Birling's fear of public scandal; responsibility accepted versus responsibility evaded.","Paragraph 5 — the cyclical ending: the telephone call returns the play to its own beginning, so those who learned nothing must face the interrogation again, and the audience leaves with the second inspector still to arrive.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: return each time to whether responsibility is presented as something felt or something merely feared, which is the line that separates the children from the parents and gives the essay one argument rather than five topics.","Conclusion must say: Priestley presents responsibility as inescapable and collective, proved by structure rather than asserted by a character, and the final call converts the play's moral into a threat, leaving the audience, not the Birlings, holding the question."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Timing and technique for the 45 minutes","items":["Spend about five minutes deciding your line of argument and choosing three or four presentations; a plan is what turns a list of ideas into the cohesive answer Level 5 requires.","Use the open book to check quotations, not to find them: mark nothing, but know where your evidence is, and keep each quotation to a phrase.","Every paragraph: point about Priestley's method, short exact quotation or precisely located moment, developed analysis of the language, form or structure, then a mini-judgement that answers the question.","Make at least one paragraph structural: act endings, the order of interviews, the entrances and exits, the lighting change, the final telephone call.","Answer the question in the first sentence of the essay and again in the last; examiners mark on a best fit basis, and a visible argument is what lifts a good answer into the top band."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T18:05:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every fault the first examiner listed has been repaired and repaired correctly — questions[0] now asks for the order the events happened in Eva's life with key, sol and hints all agreeing; exit[2] puts Mrs Birling's committee (end of Act Two) before Eric's confession (Act Three); examples[4].steps[2] now says \"a flat declarative followed by two short imperatives\", which is what \"But each of you helped to kill her. Remember that. Never forget it.\" actually is; Sheila's warning is quoted as \"a kind of wall between us and that girl\"; extras[3].items[10] restores the Act Three order (Sheila's bitter line, Gerald's ring, Birling's \"famous younger generation who know it all\", then the telephone); \"rather cold woman and her husband's social superior\" is intact in cards[9], extras[0] and extras[2]; the weaker model paragraph is honestly relabelled Level 2 with commentary that matches the Pearson descriptor; and questions[4].hints[1] no longer names ships and Europe. I checked roughly forty quoted phrases, stage directions and located moments against the play and found no misquotation and no wrong answer key — the six items above are refinements of accuracy and hint discipline, not textual errors, so this is publishable."}}},"drama-curious-incident":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"drama-curious-incident","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"In Component 2 Section A you write one 30-mark essay on this play, chosen from two questions, with a clean copy of the text open in front of you. Fifteen of those marks are AO1 (close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement) and fifteen are AO2 (language, form and structure); AO4 is not assessed here at all, so context earns nothing. That changes what you write. The examiner wants the play argued about as something staged - Siobhan reading Christopher's book aloud, the ensemble building Swindon and London out of bodies and voices, the two-act shape, the appendix after the curtain call - not the story retold and not the novel discussed. This room drills the moves that lift an answer from retelling to evaluation inside the forty-five minutes the paper allows for it.","idea":[{"h":"The set text is a play, and you study the whole of it","t":"Learners study two set texts for Component 2: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. Here the modern drama choice is The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time, listed by the specification as Mark Haddon, adapted for the stage by Simon Stephens. The object of study is therefore the script and what it asks a theatre to do, not the 2003 novel. The content overview requires you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response, across the whole text rather than a few favourite scenes. What a top-level answer does with this: it writes about an audience watching and actors doing. Phrases such as the narrator says, the reader feels or in the book are the clearest sign of an essay that has studied the wrong text; the same moment described as something an audience sees happen on a stage is immediately worth more.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, and an argument that is yours","t":"AO1 carries 15 of the 30 marks. The specification asks you to demonstrate a close understanding of your drama text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the play. Because the examination is open book, close knowledge is not tested as memory: it is tested as selection. The grid moves from limited knowledge and a simple response, through sound knowledge with relevant personal engagement, to assured knowledge with a perceptive critical style and discriminating use of relevant examples. Informed personal engagement means a position that could be disagreed with, not a report of your feelings. What a top-level answer does with this: it states a line of argument in the first three sentences, and every paragraph afterwards both develops that line and could be challenged, so the examiner can see a reader thinking rather than a student summarising.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: what the play does, not what happens in it","t":"AO2 carries the other 15 marks: analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects, using appropriate literary terminology. For this play, form includes the narration device (Siobhan, Christopher's teacher, reads his written book aloud, so his first-person account reaches the audience in another person's voice while he acts it out), the frame in which that book is made into a play and what we watch presents itself as that play, direct address, the ensemble who play every other role and voice the announcements, signs and strangers, and the appendix performed after the curtain call. Structure includes the two acts, the placing of the letters and the confession at the end of Act One, the interval, and the journey to London given the stage time of a set piece. Language includes Christopher's flat, exact, numbered sentences set against adults who qualify, re-start and evade. What a top-level answer does with this: it names the method, says what it does to the audience at that moment, and then says why that matters to the question, instead of listing devices.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"},{"h":"The question you will actually see, and the conditions you will see it in","t":"Section A gives one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 1 to 10, 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, with no AO4. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes, is worth 60 raw marks and is 40 per cent of the International GCSE, which leaves about forty-five minutes for this essay. It is an open book examination: you may take in a clean copy of the prescribed edition, and it must be completely unmarked. The stems the specification itself models are Explore the ways in which a writer presents something, and In what ways does a writer present something, each closing with You must consider language, form and structure. What a top-level answer does with this: it chooses the question for which it already has four moments it can find on the page, quotes briefly and exactly because the book is open, and never copies a long passage to fill time.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"},{"h":"The coursework route: the same skills with a longer runway","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same modern drama list. Assignment A is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2, and the assignment must allow learners to access both objectives. The wordings the specification models are of the type Explore the ways in which a writer presents a group of characters, or In what ways does a writer present a theme, each with the instruction to consider language, form and structure. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, marking is on the same five-level grid on a best fit basis, and the work is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson; the two assignment marks out of 30 are combined into one mark out of 60. What a top-level answer does with this: it uses the extra time to select and redraft, not to add plot, because a 700-word essay that evaluates beats a 1,200-word essay that narrates.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a Section A question before you write","setup":"Section A of Component 2 offers a choice of two questions on this play. You are looking at: Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father in The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks) The paper is open book, runs for 1 hour 30 minutes across two sections, and this question carries 15 raw marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2. Work out what is actually being asked and what your first paragraph will be built on, before you write a word.","steps":["Underline the ways in which and presents: the subject of the essay is a playwright's choices, so each paragraph must be about something done to stage this relationship, not about what Ed and Christopher get up to.","Read the mark split as a rule for every paragraph: close knowledge with a critical line earns half the credit and analysis of method earns the other half, so a paragraph carrying only one of the two is throwing marks away.","Fix a line of argument that somebody could dispute, for instance that Stephens presents a bond which cannot be repaired by explanation and has to be rebuilt in gesture and in time.","Pick four moments you can turn to in the open copy: the arrest after Wellington is found, the confession at the end of Act One, the scenes afterwards in which Christopher will not let his father near him, and the puppy in the closing sequence.","Open on the moment that already shows the method you are arguing about, not on the earliest event, so the essay begins in analysis rather than in plot."],"cues":["Which two words in the question name what you must write about?","How are the marks divided, and what does that force into every paragraph?","What could you claim about this relationship that another reader might deny?","Which moments in the play would test that claim rather than illustrate the plot?","Which of those moments should the essay open on, and what should decide that?"]},{"title":"Turning the opening of the play into an AO2 paragraph","setup":"The play opens on the body of Mrs Shears' dog Wellington, killed with a garden fork, with Christopher beside it and Siobhan, his teacher, reading aloud to the audience from the book Christopher has written about that night. The writing is in the first person, so his I is spoken in her voice while he is in front of us. You are answering: In what ways does Stephens present Christopher to the audience? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks) Build the analytical core of the paragraph that uses this opening.","steps":["Name the device exactly: narration is handed to another character, so the audience meets Christopher through his own writing delivered by his teacher.","Say what that does here: the audience hears calm, ordered, sequenced sentences while watching a boy beside a killed animal, so the distance between his account and the scene is visible in the first minute.","Push the point: because he never has to explain himself directly to us, we are placed where Siobhan sits, interpreting him, which makes understanding him a job the audience is given rather than a feeling it is handed.","Link it to the question: the play presents Christopher as more fluent on paper than in a room, and builds that asymmetry into its own form.","Close on a judgement in a clause: the device produces sympathy without pity, because it leaves him the author of the story we are watching."],"cues":["What is the technical name for what Siobhan is doing here?","What are the audience hearing and seeing at the same time, and how do they differ?","Whose position in the room does that put the audience in?","How does that answer a question about how Christopher is presented?","What one-clause verdict would finish the paragraph without repeating it?"]},{"title":"Choosing evidence when the book is open in front of you","setup":"You are answering: In what ways does Stephens present Christopher's courage? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks) Four moments are available to you: Christopher hitting the policeman who takes hold of his arm outside Mrs Shears' house; Christopher making himself knock on neighbours' doors to ask about Wellington; Christopher climbing down onto a railway track in London after his rat Toby; and Christopher sitting his Maths A level after everything that has happened. You may use three. Decide which to drop, what each of the others proves, and the order they should come in.","steps":["Test each against the word courage: striking out when a stranger grabs him is panic and instinct, so that one goes, and saying why it goes is itself an act of criticism the grid rewards.","Keep the doorstep questioning, because it shows him overriding a rule he has set himself and is staged as a repeated sequence of encounters, which gives you a structural method to analyse rather than an incident.","Keep the track, because the danger is physical and immediate, and the ensemble and lighting are asked to put the audience into that danger with him rather than describe it.","Keep the examination, because it is the only one where the courage is chosen calmly and it carries the appendix that closes the evening.","Order them by the kind of fear each overcomes, social then physical then the ordinary courage of continuing, and put that progression into your topic sentences so the essay develops instead of following the plot."],"cues":["You may use only three: how would you test each of the four against the word courage to decide which one to leave out?","Which moment shows him breaking one of his own rules?","Which moment forces the staging to do the work, and what work is that?","Which moment is the quietest, and why is it still evidence of courage?","What should decide the running order if not the order of events?"]},{"title":"Lifting a narrative paragraph into the analysis band","setup":"A student answering Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father has written this paragraph: Christopher's dad is angry a lot in the play. He shouts at Christopher and tells him to stop going round asking questions about the dog. Later he admits that he killed Wellington and Christopher is scared of him and runs away to London to find his mum. This shows that their relationship is difficult. Identify what keeps it out of the higher levels, then rebuild it on the same material without adding any new moment.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: four sentences of story and one label at the end, so nothing is analysed and the verdict, difficult, is a word rather than a judgement.","Cut the paragraph down to one moment, the confession, and refuse to move on from it, because a paragraph that covers three events has no room to develop any of them.","Add the method the student walked past: the confession is played across a gap, since Christopher will not be touched, so a stage father is kept at arm's length exactly where an embrace would normally come.","Build the chain: the audience watches Ed try to close a distance his own lie has made permanent, and hears him qualify and re-start while Christopher answers in short literal lines, so the scene stages two languages that cannot meet.","Finish with a judgement that answers the question: the relationship is presented as a failure of shared language, which is why the repair, when it comes, is made of gestures rather than speeches."],"cues":["How much of this paragraph is telling the story, and what is the only piece of comment in it?","Which single moment is worth keeping, and what happens to the other two?","What is the staging doing in that moment that the student has not noticed?","How do the two characters' ways of speaking differ, and what does an audience make of that?","What verdict would answer the question instead of restating the point?"]},{"title":"Making the shape of the play part of the argument","setup":"You are answering: Explore the ways in which Stephens presents change in Christopher's life. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks) The structural facts you may use are these: the play is in two acts; Christopher finds his mother's letters and hears his father confess to killing Wellington at the end of Act One; the interval falls between that discovery and his arrival in London; Act Two gives most of its running time to the journey and to what follows it; and after the curtain call Christopher returns to the stage to work through the Maths question in the appendix. Turn those facts into a paragraph instead of a list.","steps":["Choose the feature that answers this particular question: an interval placed immediately after the confession leaves the audience holding what Christopher holds, certain of the facts and unable to act on them.","Say what the second half does differently: the journey is given the running time a summary would never give it, so change is presented as something endured over duration rather than announced as an event.","Bring in the appendix: by coming back after the bows to finish a proof, the play steps outside its own story and ends on competence instead of on the family, which quietly answers the question it has been asking.","Judge: change here is measured by what Christopher can now do alone, not by anything the adults say about him, and the structure is what makes that measurable."],"cues":["What does an interval in that position do to an audience?","Why give so much stage time to a train journey at all?","What is unusual about the last thing the audience sees, and what does it put the emphasis on?","What one sentence would turn these observations into an answer to the question?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which assessment objectives carry the marks in Component 2 Section A, and which do not apply?","a":"AO1 and AO2, 15 raw marks each, 30 in total. AO4 is not assessed in Section A and AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2, so context and comparison earn nothing here."},{"q":"What may you have in front of you while you write this essay, and what does that change?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition, because Component 2 is open book. It means quotation should be exact, short and chosen, and that long copied passages simply waste time."},{"q":"Why does writing the narrator tells us lose credit on this text?","a":"The set text is a play. Christopher's written account reaches the audience because Siobhan reads it aloud on stage, so it is a staging choice to analyse, not a narrator to report."},{"q":"What has to be in a paragraph to move it from some comment on method to sustained analysis?","a":"A named method, what it does to the audience at that moment, a developed chain of reasoning rather than an asserted effect, and a mini-judgement that answers the question set."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2 Section A: one 30-mark essay on the modern drama text from a choice of two, 15 raw marks AO1 and 15 raw marks AO2; AO4 is not assessed in this section.","Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 raw marks and 40 per cent of the International GCSE, which leaves roughly 45 minutes for the Section A essay.","The examination is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in; the prescribed editions of this play are published by Bloomsbury, 2013.","Every set-text question ends You must consider language, form and structure - the instruction that carries the AO2 marks, which are half of the total here.","The only assessment grids the specification prints are those for the non-examined Component 3: five levels over 30 marks, from Level 1 (1-6, minimal identification of language, form and structure) to Level 5 (25-30, cohesive evaluation and discriminating use of relevant examples), awarded on a best fit basis. No grid is printed for Component 2 Section A, so this room uses the Component 3 grid - which marks the same 15 AO1 and 15 AO2 - as the closest available description of what the examined essay rewards.","Component 3 Assignment A is the coursework alternative: a teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the drama text, 15 AO1 and 15 AO2, advisory 650-800 words, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson.","The set text is the stage adaptation: Mark Haddon's story adapted for the stage by Simon Stephens, so every point should be about actors, audience and staging rather than about a novel.","Section A never sets anything but one 30-mark essay, beginning Explore the ways in which... or In what ways does... Any 2, 3, 6, 8 or 12-mark task in this room, and any stem starting State, Explain, Identify, Write or Devise, is practice scaffolding written for revision - not an exam tariff and not an exam command word."],"questions":[{"q":"State the two assessment objectives assessed in Component 2 Section A and the raw marks each carries. (Practice task, 2 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"AO1, worth 15 raw marks, and AO2, worth 15 raw marks, giving 30 marks in total.","sol":"Section A - Modern Drama assesses AO1, demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, which carries 15 raw marks; and AO2, analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects, which carries the other 15. The question is worth 30 marks in total. AO4 is not assessed in this section, and AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two things are being tested: how well you know the play, and what you can say about how it is made.","The Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks is where the numbers come from; find the row for Questions 1 to 10.","Shape it as two objectives, each named in the specification's own words with its raw mark beside it."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Explain what open book means for this examination and what you may take into the examination room. (Practice task, 2 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in; nothing is being tested by memory, so quotation must be exact and short.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners may take a copy of the prescribed edition of each selected set text into the examination room, and that copy must be completely unmarked - no annotations, highlighting, notes or inserted material. The practical consequence is that the examiner is not rewarding recall. Because the page is in front of you, exact short quotation and discriminating selection are expected, while copying out long passages spends the forty-five minutes you have without earning anything.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the exam can no longer be testing if the text is on your desk.","There is a condition attached to the copy you bring; say what it is.","Answer in two parts: what you may bring, and what that changes about how you use quotation."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Identify the device by which Christopher's written account reaches the audience, and explain one effect it has. (Practice task, 3 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"Siobhan, his teacher, reads passages of the book Christopher has written aloud on stage, so his first-person writing arrives in another person's voice while he acts in front of us.","sol":"Stephens hands the narration to a character. Christopher has written a book about the death of Wellington, and Siobhan, his teacher, reads it aloud to the audience while Christopher is on stage living the events. One effect is that the audience always receives him second-hand, through someone whose job is to interpret him, which places us in Siobhan's position rather than inside his head. A second effect is the gap the device opens up: we hear ordered, precise, sequenced sentences while we watch a body under strain, and the difference between the account and the scene becomes the play's central experience.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Ask who speaks Christopher's written words out loud, and what she is to him.","The device is a way of solving the problem of putting a first-person book on a stage.","Give the device first, then one thing it does to the audience's position."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explain why an answer that retells Christopher's journey to London cannot reach the higher levels. (Practice task, 3 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"Retelling is narrative: it shows no analysis of language, form or structure and no critical line, so it sits at the bottom of the levels however accurate it is.","sol":"Half of the marks here are for AO2, analysis of language, form and structure, and a retelling names no method at all, so that half is untouched. The AO1 half is not secured either: AO1 asks for a critical style and an informed personal engagement, and a summary presents no argument that could be agreed or disagreed with. The only levels the specification prints are the five for the non-examined Component 3, and there is no printed Section A grid; those levels describe exactly this answer at Level 2, a largely narrative response with some comment, which is why an accurate, detailed retelling still lands low in an examined essay marked for the same two objectives. The same material earns well as soon as each event is replaced by what the play does with it: the ensemble building the station, the calm of the book read aloud against the noise on stage, the placing of the whole sequence after the interval.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what the grid describes at the lower levels and compare it with what a retelling actually does.","Consider both objectives in turn and ask which of them a summary can possibly satisfy.","Finish by saying what the same events would have to be turned into."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"State the marks, the assessment objectives and the advisory word count for Component 3 Assignment A. (Practice task, 3 marks - not an exam tariff: the only assessments set are one 30-mark Section A essay or one 30-mark Assignment A.)","a":"30 marks, 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2, with an advisory word count of 650 to 800 words and no penalty for exceeding it.","sol":"Assignment A - Modern Drama is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied drama text. It is marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2, and the assignment must allow learners to access both objectives. The advisory word count for Assignments A and B is between 650 and 800 words, and there is no penalty for exceeding this recommendation. The work is marked on the same five-level grid on a best fit basis, is internally assessed and is externally moderated by Pearson; the two marks out of 30 are combined into one mark out of 60.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is the coursework route, so read the Component 3 Assignment A entry rather than working from the examination you already know.","There are three things to give: the total, the split between objectives, and the length guidance.","Note whether the length guidance is a rule or a recommendation."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father in The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Ed's temper, protectiveness and lie; the confession at the end of Act One; Christopher's physical withdrawal afterwards; the repair at the close, made of gestures rather than speeches, though the promise to be trusted and the offer of the puppy are both spoken. Methods: two incompatible registers, staging that keeps them apart, the act break, the narration that makes Christopher the author of his father's exposure.","sol":"Stephens presents a bond that cannot be repaired by explanation and has to be rebuilt in gesture and in time, and the play's form is built to make that argument rather than to state it.\n\nFrom the first scenes the two are given incompatible languages. Ed speaks in half-finished, self-correcting sentences and reaches for his son physically; Christopher answers only the question he has been asked and cannot be held. Their agreed gesture is a touching of fingers rather than an embrace, so an audience watches affection conducted at the width of a hand, and understands before anything is explained that this is a relationship carried on across a permanent small distance.\n\nThe structure then does the damage. The letters and Ed's confession that he killed Wellington fall at the end of Act One, so the audience is sent into the interval holding exactly what Christopher holds. Because everything has reached us through Christopher's own book, read aloud by Siobhan, the confession also turns his detective story against its author: the case he set out to solve ends inside his own house, and the form he chose to make sense of the world is what exposes his father.\n\nAfter the confession the scenes between them are played across a gap. Ed's apologies qualify themselves, reach for reasons and ask to be forgiven inside the same sentence; Christopher's fear is exact and unarguable, and he will not be in a room with him. Stephens gives Ed almost nothing to say that works, which is the point: the repair, when it starts, is an offer rather than a speech - a puppy, and days that have to accumulate before an ordinary room is possible again.\n\nThe ending is therefore deliberately unfinished. Ed does speak: he promises to show Christopher over time that he can be trusted, and the puppy is offered aloud. What the ending withholds is any answer from Christopher, whose own last words are turned elsewhere - his closing question, 'Does that mean I can do anything?', is put to Siobhan, and she gives no answer either. Stephens closes on the pupil counting his achievements to his teacher rather than on a reconciliation scene, so the relationship is presented as survivable rather than mended, and the father is allowed back only as far as the edge of a triumph addressed to somebody else.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about presentation, so look for what the play makes an audience watch rather than what the two characters do.","Pair the ways they each speak with the physical distance the staging has to keep between them.","Decide where in the two-act shape this relationship is put under most pressure, and open there."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"In what ways does Stephens present Christopher's journey to London? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The journey as a sustained set piece rather than a transition: the ensemble building the crowd, station and train; the calm of the book read aloud against staged overload; the track and Toby; its placing after the interval as the proof of independence.","sol":"Stephens's answer to the problem of staging a journey is to refuse to summarise it. What a novel can cover in a chapter is given the running time of a set piece, so the audience experiences the length of it rather than being told about it, and that decision is the argument of the whole sequence.\n\nThe journey is built out of people. The ensemble becomes the ticket queue, the strangers he has to speak to, the crowd on the platform, and the voices of signs and announcements; the same actors who were neighbours in Swindon reassemble as an indifferent city. Nothing is realistic, and that is the point. The audience watches a world being assembled around a boy who is trying to assemble it too, out of directions, timetables and rules he has set himself.\n\nLanguage does the opposite work. While the stage fills with noise, Christopher's account, read aloud by Siobhan from his book, stays flat, ordered and exact. The gap between the two is the meaning of the sequence: what the audience hears is control, what it sees is overload, and it is asked to hold both at once as a single experience.\n\nStructure makes the journey the hinge of the play. It comes after the interval, so the audience returns to a story that has changed kind - the murder investigation has become an escape - and its climax is not arrival but the moment Christopher climbs down onto the track after Toby, where the danger is physical and immediate and the staging puts the audience into it rather than describing it. Stephens places the most dangerous act at the point where Christopher has least control of his surroundings and most control of his purpose.\n\nThe judgement worth making is that the journey is not a transition but the play's proof. Everything Christopher has been told he cannot manage - travelling, speaking to strangers, crowds - he manages badly and manages anyway, and it is this sequence, not anything an adult says, that earns the confidence of his closing question.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why so much of the play's running time is spent on a train journey at all.","Set what the audience hears against what the audience sees during the sequence.","Work out what the journey proves, and make that your line of argument rather than a description of the route."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Write two developed paragraphs on the ways in which Stephens presents Siobhan. You must consider language, form and structure. (Practice task, 12 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"Siobhan as the play's narrating device and as a character: she reads Christopher's book aloud, addresses the audience, and mediates between him and us; she is also the one adult who asks rather than tells.","sol":"Siobhan is the play's solution to a technical problem, and Stephens makes that solution mean something. Christopher's book is written in the first person, so Stephens gives it to his teacher to read aloud: his I arrives in her voice while he is in front of us, acting. The effect is that the audience never has unmediated access to him. We receive him through an adult whose professional job is to interpret a child, which is precisely the position the play then puts us in for two hours. Every time she reads, the audience is reminded that understanding Christopher is work somebody is doing, not something that simply happens.\n\nAs a character she is defined by what she does not do. Where Ed instructs and Mrs Shears shouts, Siobhan asks questions, waits, and explains other people to Christopher in the terms he uses himself; where the other adults decide what he can manage, she is the one who lets him write the book that becomes this play. Stephens keeps her almost entirely inside the school and inside the narration, so she has no private life on stage and no plot of her own, and that restraint is deliberate: she functions as a steady register against which the adults' evasions and the city's noise can be measured. The judgement to make is that Siobhan is less a character than a position - the one the audience is asked to occupy - and the play's generosity towards Christopher depends on it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Start from the job she does for the play's form before you consider her as a person.","Compare the way she speaks to Christopher with the way every other adult does.","Ask what the audience is asked to do each time she reads from the book, and build one paragraph on that."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Stephens present truth and lying in the play? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Christopher's refusal to lie against the adults' evasions; Ed's lie about Judy's death as the play's engine; the letters as physical proof; the confession's self-justifying language; the form itself, in which telling your own story is staged in public.","sol":"Stephens builds the play on a single asymmetry: Christopher does not tell lies, and almost everyone around him does. Because the story reaches us through Christopher's own written account, read aloud by Siobhan, the narration itself carries his standard of truth, and every adult evasion is measured against it without anyone having to comment.\n\nEd's lie is the engine, and Stephens makes it sympathetic before he makes it damning. Judy has gone; Ed tells Christopher she is dead; and the audience meets the lie not as a scene but as a settled fact of the household, which is far more uncomfortable. When the letters are found, the lie converts from a story into evidence - paper, in Christopher's own hands, of exactly the kind he trusts. Stephens stages the discovery as reading rather than confrontation, so the truth arrives in the medium Christopher understands and nobody is there to soften it.\n\nLanguage marks the divide at the confession. Ed qualifies, restarts, reaches for reasons and asks to be forgiven inside the same sentence; Christopher replies briefly and literally, answering the question asked rather than the one implied. The audience hears a man attempting to negotiate with someone who has no mechanism for negotiation, and the discomfort of the scene comes from that mismatch rather than from any raised voice.\n\nForm completes the argument. What we are watching is Christopher's book being performed in front of other people, so telling the truth about yourself is staged as a public act with an audience attached - the opposite of the private management of information the adults have been practising. Stephens therefore ends not on the exposure of a liar but on the author's unanswered question about what he can do next. The play's final honesty is its refusal to reassure: truth here is not a resolution but a condition Christopher has to go on living in.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Begin with the difference between how Christopher handles information and how the adults do.","Look at what physical object turns a lie into something Christopher can act on, and how that moment is staged.","Think about whether the play's own form has anything to say about telling the truth in front of other people."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explain how you would use the placing of the interval in an answer about structure. (Practice task, 6 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"The act break comes after Christopher has found the letters and heard the confession, so the audience waits out the interval in his position: certain of the facts, unable to act on them, with the journey still ahead.","sol":"The point to make is that an interval is a structural choice, not a break in the story. Stephens puts the discovery of Judy's letters and Ed's confession that he killed Wellington at the end of Act One, which means the audience leaves the auditorium holding precisely what Christopher holds - the knowledge that his mother is alive, that the man he lives with killed the dog, and that nothing has yet been done about any of it. The waiting is made to feel like his waiting. It also divides the play by kind: Act One is an investigation conducted from one street in Swindon, Act Two is an escape conducted across a country, so the break marks a change of genre as well as of place. In an essay this is worth a whole paragraph provided you say what it does to the audience rather than simply noting where it falls, and provided you connect it to the question - on change, on courage, on the father, the same act break proves something different each time.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the audience knows, and cannot yet do anything about, as they leave for the interval.","Compare what kind of story each act is telling and where the boundary between them falls.","Turn the observation into something that answers a question, not a fact about the running order."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Write the opening paragraph of an answer to: In what ways does Stephens present Judy, Christopher's mother? Your paragraph must set out a line of argument and name the first method you will analyse. (Practice task, 6 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"A thesis that Christopher reaches his mother as writing before he reaches her as a person, with the letters read aloud as the first method to analyse.","sol":"Stephens presents Judy first as writing and only afterwards as a woman in a room, and the play makes that order the point. For most of Act One she exists only as a story Christopher has been told, that she is dead, and then as letters he did not know existed: Ed has hidden them in a shirt box in his wardrobe, and Christopher comes upon them by accident while searching for the book his father has confiscated. So the audience meets her, as he does, through a voice on paper; when the letters are read aloud they arrive as a shock and as evidence rather than as comfort, full of apology, self-justification and the ordinary detail of a life that carried on. The method to begin with is therefore the staging of the letters themselves - words written by an absent character, spoken on a stage she is not required to occupy - because it shows Stephens turning an absence into something an audience can hear. That choice sets up everything the London scenes then complicate: the mother Christopher has read is not the same as the mother he has to live with, and the play is honest about the distance between them.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Ask how Christopher gets to know his mother at all before he sees her.","An opening paragraph needs a claim somebody could argue with, not a summary of who she is.","Name the method you will analyse first and say why it comes first."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"The play lets the audience see the world exactly as Christopher sees it. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Agree in part: the narration, the ensemble's staged overload and the instantly changing stage put the audience in his conditions. Disagree because Siobhan's voice mediates, we watch him from outside, and dramatic irony gives us what he does not have.","sol":"The claim is half right, and the half that is wrong is the more interesting half.\n\nWhat supports it is the play's machinery. The words are Christopher's own, taken from the book he has written; the ensemble supplies his sensory conditions, turning a station into a wall of voices, signs and moving bodies so that the audience is overloaded at the moment he is; and the stage rearranges itself at the speed of his attention, which makes his way of ordering the world into the way the theatre itself works. Few plays go so far to put an audience inside a particular way of processing noise, crowds and strangers.\n\nWhat complicates it is that the words arrive in somebody else's mouth. Siobhan reads his writing aloud, so the audience always receives him at one remove, and at one remove through a teacher - an adult whose role is to interpret him to other people. Structurally, we are seated where she sits, which is not the same as seeing what he sees.\n\nThe play also gives the audience information Christopher does not have. We watch Mrs Alexander choose her words before she tells him about his mother and Mr Shears; we read Ed's fear in behaviour Christopher registers only as anger; we hear Judy's letters as an adult failure as well as a mother's love. That is dramatic irony, and it is only available because the audience is outside him.\n\nThe judgement, then, is that the play does not put us inside Christopher's head: it puts us in the audience of his book, and makes that position uncomfortable enough to feel like understanding. Agreeing entirely would mean ignoring the very device that makes the play work, since the narration Stephens invented is also the distance he keeps. The achievement is subtler than the claim: we are made to watch a boy being misread by almost everyone on stage while he holds the only narration in the room.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Work out first what the play genuinely does put the audience through, and grant it fully.","Then ask whose voice the audience actually hears his words in, and what difference that makes.","Look for the moments where the audience understands something Christopher does not, and decide what that proves."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Stephens use the ensemble and the demands he makes on staging to shape the audience's understanding of Christopher? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The ensemble plays every other role, voices signs, announcements and strangers, and builds locations out of bodies; the printed text leaves much of the how to the production, which makes the world visibly assembled, like Christopher's own ordering of it.","sol":"Stephens writes a play whose world is visibly made rather than pretended, and that decision is what shapes the audience's understanding of Christopher more than any single speech.\n\nThe ensemble is the first method. A small company plays the neighbours, the police, the passengers and the voices of announcements and signs, so people arrive as functions and noises rather than as individuals - which is very close to how a crowd is experienced by someone for whom strangers are unreadable. When the same actors who were a Swindon street become a London station, the audience is shown a world that reshuffles without warning, and has to keep up, exactly as Christopher does.\n\nThe second method is what the text asks of the stage. The printed play names what happens and leaves a great deal of the how to the production, so locations are built and dismantled around Christopher at the speed of thought and nothing is bolted down. The audience watches space itself behaving like his mind: ordered when he is ordering it, overwhelming when it overwhelms him. Where a naturalistic set would insist that the world is solid and Christopher is the odd element in it, this staging reverses the burden - the world is provisional and his system is the thing holding steady.\n\nThe third is the way narration and staging are made to disagree. Siobhan reads his calm written sentences while the ensemble produces the noise those sentences are describing, and the audience is left in the gap between the account and the event. That gap is the play's whole method of characterisation: we are never told what Christopher feels, we are shown the distance between what happens to him and how he records it.\n\nThe judgement is that the ensemble is not a practical economy but an argument. By making every other person on stage something the company assembles, Stephens makes the audience do to the play what Christopher does to the world - build it out of parts - and understanding him becomes a shared task rather than a lesson.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Count how many parts a small company has to cover, and ask what an audience makes of the same faces returning.","Consider what the printed text leaves to the production, and what that freedom does to the look of the world on stage.","Find the point where what the audience hears and what it sees are pulling in different directions."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Devise a title for Component 3 Assignment A on this play and explain how it gives learners access to both AO1 and AO2. (Practice task, 8 marks - not an exam tariff: the only assessments set are one 30-mark Section A essay or one 30-mark Assignment A.)","a":"A title in the modelled form, for example: Explore the ways in which Stephens presents trust in The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time. You must consider language, form and structure - with a justification covering both objectives.","sol":"A suitable title is: Explore the ways in which Stephens presents trust in The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time. You must consider language, form and structure.\n\nIt follows the form the specification models for Assignment A: the command word explore, a named writer, a single presented idea, and the instruction that carries the analysis. It gives access to AO1 because trust runs through the whole play rather than one scene - Ed's lie about Judy, Christopher's rule-bound dealings with strangers, Siobhan as the one adult he tells things to, the slow terms on which his father is let back - so a learner must show close knowledge across the text, select relevant examples and take a personal position on whether the play thinks trust can be rebuilt. It gives access to AO2 because each of those moments is made by a method that can be analysed: the letters read aloud, the confession's self-justifying syntax against Christopher's literal replies, the staged distance between two actors who cannot touch, the act break that leaves the audience holding what he holds.\n\nA title to avoid is one that invites narrative - describe what happens when Christopher goes to London - or one that requires context, since AO4 is not assessed in Assignment A, and 650 to 800 words is too short to carry material that earns nothing.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the shape of the wordings the specification models and copy the shape, not the content.","A good title has to be answerable from across the whole play, not from one scene.","Justify it twice: once for knowledge and argument, once for the methods it opens up."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"A candidate plans to open a Section A essay with a paragraph on Mark Haddon's 2003 novel and the play's first production and awards. Evaluate that decision against the assessment objectives, and say what the opening should do instead. (Practice task, 8 marks - not an exam tariff: Section A sets only one 30-mark essay.)","a":"It earns nothing: AO4 is not assessed in Section A and AO3 is not assessed in Component 2, so the paragraph scores on neither objective and costs several minutes; the opening should state the line of argument and the first method.","sol":"The decision is wrong on the objectives and wrong on the clock. Section A carries 15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2 only; there is no AO4 here, so understanding of the relationship between a text and its contexts - the source novel, the first production, the awards - cannot be credited, and AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2, so links to other texts cannot be credited either. A candidate who spends the first five minutes of a forty-five minute essay on material that can score on neither objective has lost an entire paragraph and arrives at the play late.\n\nIt is also a trap in a subtler way. Writing at length about the novel encourages the vocabulary of prose - narrator, reader, chapter - which then leaks into the rest of the essay and works against the AO2 marks, because those marks depend on treating the text as something staged.\n\nThe knowledge is not useless: knowing that Stephens adapted Haddon's story for the stage is exactly why you write about Siobhan reading the book aloud as a solution to a dramatic problem. The right place for it is inside an analytical point, in a clause. The opening paragraph itself should do two things and stop: state a line of argument on the question that could be disagreed with, and name the first method the essay will analyse.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Check which objectives are actually assessed in this section before judging the paragraph.","Think about the cost in minutes as well as the cost in marks.","Say where that knowledge can legitimately appear, and what should occupy the opening instead."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Component 2: Content overview","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section A question carry, and how are they split between assessment objectives?","a":"30 marks: 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2."},{"q":"Which character reads Christopher's book aloud to the audience?","a":"Siobhan, his teacher."},{"q":"What may a learner take into the Component 2 examination room?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text."},{"q":"Which two events fall at the end of Act One and send the audience into the interval?","a":"Christopher finds his mother's letters and his father confesses to killing Wellington."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Siobhan","back":"Christopher's teacher. She reads his written book aloud to the audience, so his first-person account is spoken by another character while he acts it out - the play's narration device.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Metatheatre in this play","back":"Christopher's book is turned into a play, and what the audience watches presents itself as that play. The story is performed as an act of telling rather than shown as if it were simply happening.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The ensemble","back":"A small company playing neighbours, police and passengers, voicing signs and announcements, and building stations, streets and trains out of bodies, so locations change instantly and the world arrives as noise around Christopher.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The appendix","back":"Performed after the curtain call: Christopher returns to work through the proof of his favourite Maths A level question, ending the evening outside the story and on his competence.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Wellington","back":"Mrs Shears' dog, found dead on her lawn, killed with a garden fork. Christopher is beside the body when he is discovered, and the death sets his investigation going.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Christopher Boone","back":"Fifteen, lives in Swindon, brilliant at mathematics, does not tell lies and cannot bear to be touched. He writes the book the play is made of and travels alone to London.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Ed Boone","back":"Christopher's father, who raised him alone, told him his mother had died, and killed Wellington. After confessing he has to earn trust back slowly and on Christopher's terms.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Judy Boone","back":"Christopher's mother, who left Swindon for London with Mr Shears. Her letters, kept from him in his father's room and later read aloud, reach Christopher before she does.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Mrs Alexander","back":"The elderly neighbour who tells Christopher about his mother and Mr Shears - the information that turns an investigation into a dog's death into an investigation of his own family.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The end of Act One","back":"Christopher finds his mother's letters and hears his father confess to killing Wellington. The interval then holds the audience in his position before the journey to London begins.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Christopher's closing question","back":"'Does that mean I can do anything?' He asks it after listing what he has achieved, and no one answers, so the play ends on an open question rather than on reassurance.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Toby","back":"Christopher's pet rat, taken with him to London. When Toby gets down onto a railway track Christopher climbs after him, the most physically dangerous moment of the journey.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Section A mark split","back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two: 15 raw marks AO1, 15 raw marks AO2. AO4 is not assessed, so context earns nothing; AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"You must consider language, form and structure","back":"The instruction printed on every set-text question; it carries the AO2 marks. For a play, form and structure include narration, act shape, staging and the ensemble.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Ways the play puts Christopher's thinking on stage","back":"Siobhan reading his book aloud while he acts; the ensemble voicing signs, announcements and crowds so the audience shares the overload; locations built and dismantled around him at the speed of his attention; mathematics and rules spoken aloud as he steadies himself.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time - people, story and the moments examiners reward","items":["The set text is the stage adaptation: the specification lists it as The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-time, Mark Haddon, adapted by Simon Stephens.","Christopher Boone: fifteen, lives in Swindon with his father, brilliant at mathematics, does not tell lies, cannot bear to be touched, and writes the book the play is built from.","Ed Boone: Christopher's father, who has brought him up alone and has told him that his mother is dead.","Judy Boone: Christopher's mother, who left Swindon for London with Mr Shears and has written to Christopher ever since.","Siobhan: Christopher's teacher, who encourages him to write, reads the book aloud to the audience, and is the adult he trusts with his thinking.","Mrs Shears: the neighbour whose dog Wellington is killed, and who finds Christopher beside the body.","Mr Shears: Mrs Shears' husband, who left her and went to London with Judy.","Mrs Alexander: the elderly neighbour who tells Christopher about his mother and Mr Shears.","Wellington: Mrs Shears' dog, found dead on the lawn, killed with a garden fork.","Toby: Christopher's pet rat, who goes with him to London.","The ensemble: the company who play the neighbours, police, passengers and strangers, and who voice signs and announcements.","Story in order: Christopher finds Wellington dead at night and is beside the body when Mrs Shears comes out.","A policeman takes hold of his arm; Christopher hits him, is arrested, and his father comes to the police station.","Encouraged by Siobhan, Christopher writes the story down, and what he writes becomes the text the audience hears read aloud.","Against his father's orders he investigates, knocking on neighbours' doors along his street.","Mrs Alexander tells him that his mother and Mr Shears were together.","Christopher finds letters from his mother hidden in his father's room and realises she is alive in London.","Ed confesses that he killed Wellington; the letters and the confession fall at the end of Act One.","Frightened of his father, Christopher leaves Swindon and travels to London by train to find his mother.","In a London station Toby gets down onto the track and Christopher climbs down after him.","He finds his mother's flat and waits; Judy takes him in, while Mr Shears wants him gone.","Christopher's overriding worry is that he will miss his Maths A level, and his mother brings him back to Swindon so that he can sit it.","He sits the examination and passes.","Ed begins to earn his way back on Christopher's terms, and brings him a puppy.","The play ends with Christopher listing what he has done and asking whether it means he can do anything; the question is left unanswered.","After the curtain call Christopher returns to the stage to work through the appendix, the proof of the Maths question he liked best.","Theme - truth and lying: Christopher does not lie, while the adults around him lie to protect themselves and each other.","Theme - trust: once broken by Ed's lie, it is rebuilt by time and small acts rather than by apology.","Theme - independence: the journey is the play's proof that Christopher can act alone.","Theme - being decided for: adults repeatedly settle what Christopher can and cannot manage, and the play repeatedly shows them wrong.","Theme - order against chaos: mathematics, lists and rules are his defence against noise, crowds and strangers.","Moments examiners reward: the opening over the dead dog with Siobhan reading; the doorstep questioning; the finding of the letters; the confession; the journey and the track; the examination; the puppy; the unanswered final question."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the text as a stage work - form, structure and conditions of assessment","items":["Genre and authorship: a stage adaptation by Simon Stephens of Mark Haddon's 2003 novel; write about Stephens's choices as a playwright's choices.","First performed at the National Theatre in London in 2012, directed by Marianne Elliott, transferring to the West End the following year.","It won the Olivier Award for Best New Play in 2013 and the Tony Award for Best Play in 2015.","Prescribed editions for this specification are published by Bloomsbury, 2013 (ISBN 9781408185216; ISBN 9781408173350).","Structure: two acts, with the interval falling between the discovery in Swindon and the arrival in London.","Form - narration: Siobhan reads Christopher's first-person book aloud while he acts, so the audience always receives him through another voice.","Form - frame: the book is made into a play, and what the audience watches presents itself as that play.","Form - direct address: characters speak out to the audience, and the ensemble voices announcements, signs and strangers.","Form - staging: the printed text names what happens and leaves much of how to the production, so places are made and unmade quickly by actors and light rather than by scene changes.","Form - the appendix: after the curtain call Christopher returns to prove the Maths A level question.","Language: Christopher's speech is literal, exact and often numbered; he answers the question asked rather than the one implied; the adults qualify, restart and evade.","Assessment: Component 2 Section A, one 30-mark essay from a choice of two, 15 AO1 and 15 AO2, open book, about 45 minutes of a 1 hour 30 minute paper.","Context is background only here: AO4 is not assessed in Section A and AO3 is not assessed in Component 2, so production history belongs in your understanding of the play, not in your essay.","Every example in this kit points to a moment you can find in your own copy; in the open book examination, take the exact words from the page rather than trusting memory.","The levels: the specification prints assessment grids only for the non-examined Component 3 - Level 1 (1-6) to Level 5 (25-30), best fit. There is no printed grid for Section A, so the level labels used in this room are the Component 3 ones, borrowed because they mark the same 15 AO1 and 15 AO2."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 5 (25-30 band)","items":["Question: Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father. You must consider language, form and structure.","Paragraph: Stephens places Ed's confession at the point where Christopher's own detective story has to convict its author's father, so the betrayal is structural rather than merely shocking. Everything the audience has learned about the investigation has arrived through Christopher's book, read aloud by Siobhan; the moment Ed admits to killing Wellington, the narrating voice of the play is revealed to have been documenting the collapse of its own household. Ed's speech then does what Christopher's never does - it qualifies, restarts and asks to be understood - and set against Christopher's flat, exact replies the audience hears two languages that cannot meet in one room. Because Christopher will not be touched, the staging has to hold the actors apart exactly where a stage father would take hold of his son, so the distance the lie has created is made visible instead of stated. Placing the scene at the end of the first act then hands the audience an interval in Christopher's own condition: certain of the facts, unable to act on them. The relationship, in other words, is presented as a problem of form as much as of feeling, which is why no apology in the second act can work and why the repair, when it comes, is made of gestures.","Why it is Level 5: assured knowledge is used without retelling; the critical style is perceptive because it reads the confession as a turn in the play's own narration; language, form and structure are evaluated together rather than listed; the examples are discriminating, chosen because only that moment proves the point.","The mini-judgement: the last sentence answers the question set rather than restating the point, and sets up the paragraph that follows."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 3 (13-18 band)","items":["Question: the same - Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father.","Paragraph: Stephens shows the relationship breaking down when Ed admits that he killed Wellington. Ed keeps trying to explain himself and his sentences are broken up, which shows that he knows he cannot put it right, while Christopher answers in short, factual lines. The audience can hear the difference between the two of them. Stephens also puts this scene at the end of Act One, which leaves the audience thinking about it during the interval. This shows that their relationship is badly damaged at this point in the play.","Why it is Level 3 and not higher: the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant, and there is genuine comment on language and on structure, so it clears the narrative band comfortably.","What keeps it out of Level 4: the effects are asserted rather than traced - we are told the audience can hear the difference but not what that difference does to them; the structural point stops at where the scene falls; the staging, including the distance the scene has to keep between the two actors, is missed; and the final sentence repeats the first instead of judging.","The two moves that would lift it: follow one effect through to what the audience is made to feel or notice, and end on a judgement that answers the question rather than summarising the paragraph."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (7-12 band): the one to avoid","items":["Question: the same - Explore the ways in which Stephens presents the relationship between Christopher and his father.","Paragraph: Christopher's dad is angry a lot in the play. He shouts at Christopher and tells him to stop going round asking questions about the dog. Later he admits that he killed Wellington and Christopher is scared of him and runs away to London to find his mum. This shows that their relationship is difficult.","Why it is Level 2: it is largely narrative, with some knowledge but no analysis of language, form or structure; the only comment, difficult, is a label rather than a judgement; and nothing in it could be agreed or disagreed with, so there is no personal engagement of the kind the grid describes.","It also costs marks twice over: three events are covered in four sentences, so no moment gets developed, and the essay has spent a paragraph without touching AO2 at all.","The fix: keep one moment, name what the play does with it, follow the effect through, and finish on a verdict - the same material, differently handled, is the Level 5 paragraph above."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - In what ways does Stephens present Christopher's courage? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","items":["Decoding the question: in what ways means methods, so every paragraph is about a staging or language choice; present means the play's construction of courage, not a list of brave acts; courage must be defined in the opening, because the essay's argument depends on it; the instruction about language, form and structure is where half the marks live.","Line of argument: Stephens presents courage not as boldness but as the ordinary continuation of a plan under conditions the play makes the audience share, which is why the smallest acts on stage are given the largest amount of time.","Paragraph 1 - social fear: the doorstep questioning along his street, staged as a repeated sequence of encounters. Method: repetition as structure, and Christopher's flat literal questions against neighbours' evasions. Mini-judgement: courage here is the overriding of a rule he set himself.","Paragraph 2 - the decision to go: leaving Swindon after the confession. Method: the act break and the change of genre from investigation to escape; the narration continuing calmly while the stage empties of everything familiar. Mini-judgement: the audience is made to feel the size of a decision that is described without drama.","Paragraph 3 - physical fear: the track, and going down after Toby. Method: the ensemble and light putting the audience inside the danger rather than describing it; the gap between the noise on stage and the order of the account read aloud. Mini-judgement: the play's definition of courage is completed here, as acting while overwhelmed rather than while calm.","Paragraph 4 - chosen courage: sitting the examination after everything, and the appendix after the curtain call. Method: ending outside the story, on competence. Mini-judgement: the quietest act is given the last word, which is the play's argument about what courage is worth.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: courage in this play is never presented as fearlessness, and the staging is what stops the audience mistaking it for fearlessness - each paragraph should say what the audience is made to experience alongside him.","What the conclusion must say: answer the question with a qualified position - the play presents courage as endurance made visible by theatre, and refuses to celebrate it, ending on Christopher's unanswered question rather than on applause for him; then state what that costs or leaves unresolved, so the judgement is weighed rather than triumphant.","Timing: about 5 minutes to choose and plan, 35 to write four developed paragraphs and a conclusion, 5 to check; use the open copy to fix quotations exactly, and quote in phrases rather than in sentences."]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Terms to use precisely when writing about this play","items":["Form: the kind of text and the conventions it works in - here a two-act play performed by an ensemble, with narration built into it. Not the same as structure.","Structure: the order and proportion of the parts - act division, the placing of the interval, how long a sequence is given, where a discovery falls.","Metatheatre: theatre that draws attention to being theatre; here, Christopher's book being made into the play the audience is watching.","Ensemble: a company playing many roles and creating place, crowd and sound with their bodies and voices.","Direct address: speaking out to the audience rather than to another character.","Narration in performance: Siobhan reading Christopher's written words aloud, which is a dramatic device, not a novel's narrator - never write the narrator here.","Dramatic irony: the audience knowing something a character does not, which in this play is what makes an audience feel outside Christopher rather than inside him.","Register: the level and manner of speech - Christopher's literal exactness against the adults' qualifying, self-correcting talk.","Stage picture: what the audience sees at a given moment, including how far apart the actors are; useful for the confession and the scenes after it.","Adaptation: the reworking of a text for another medium; the mark is earned by analysing what the stage version does, not by comparing it with the novel."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The forty-five minutes: an exam-room routine for Section A","items":["Read both questions and choose the one for which you can already name four moments you could find in your copy, not the one that sounds more interesting.","Spend about five minutes planning: a line of argument in one sentence, then four moments, each with the method you will analyse beside it.","Write an opening that states the argument and names the first method; do not open with the novel, the playwright's life or the first production, because AO4 is not assessed here.","Give every paragraph both objectives: close knowledge and a critical line for AO1, a named method and its effect on the audience for AO2.","Quote in phrases, exactly, using the open copy; a long copied passage costs minutes and earns nothing.","Write about an audience watching: what they see, hear, know and do not yet know at that moment in the performance.","End each paragraph with a mini-judgement that answers the question, so the argument develops instead of accumulating.","Keep five minutes to write a conclusion that takes a qualified position, and to check that the words narrator, reader, chapter and novel do not appear anywhere in the essay."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:40:56Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Publish: the one quotation in the kit is the play's real closing question and every name, event, staging claim and production fact I checked is true to Stephens's script, with the six earlier faults - the misattributed closing question, the 'not allowed to read' letters, the 'wordless' ending, the two giveaway hints, the two answer-revealing cues and the unlabelled Component 3 grid and non-exam tariffs - all properly repaired rather than papered over."}}},"drama-kindertransport":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"drama-kindertransport","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Kindertransport is the one modern drama text you answer on in Component 2, Section A. You meet it once, in a single 30-mark essay chosen from two, and the marks split evenly: 15 for AO1, close knowledge and understanding carried in a critical style with an informed personal engagement, and 15 for AO2, analysis of the language, form and structure Samuels uses to create meanings and effects. Neither AO3 nor AO4 is assessed in this section, so a paragraph of wartime history earns nothing on its own here and there is nothing to compare the play with. The paper runs 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks, which puts about forty-five minutes on this essay, and it is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may go in with you. Almost every mark lost on this question is lost the same way, by retelling the play instead of analysing how it is made, and the next most common loss is evidence that is never actually cited. Level 5 asks for discriminating use of relevant examples, which in practice means short, chosen references, quoted or located exactly, doing work inside your sentences. This room drills the three things that decide your level: a thesis that answers the question, paragraphs built on a dramatic method and anchored to a named moment, and a judgement that evaluates rather than summarises.","idea":[{"h":"One modern drama text, studied whole","code":"Component 2: Content overview","t":"Learners study two set texts for this component, one modern drama and one literary heritage; here the modern drama is Kindertransport by Diane Samuels, chosen from a list of five. The content overview asks you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. Teaching is on the whole text, and the question will not tell you which part of the play to use. A top-level answer therefore ranges: it draws evidence from the opening, the middle and the later scenes, treats the play as one designed thing rather than a favourite scene retold, and can move between the two time frames inside a single paragraph."},{"h":"Close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","t":"AO1 is to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, and it carries 15 of the 30 marks. For Kindertransport that means knowing the people and the turning points well enough to argue about them: Eva, the German Jewish child sent alone to England; Evelyn, the woman she becomes; Helga, the mother who sends her away and comes to England after the war to reclaim her; Lil, the English woman who takes her in and in time adopts her; Faith, the daughter who opens the box; and the Ratcatcher who shadows all of them. The board's own model wordings fasten on a character or group of characters, or on a theme. A top-level answer never uses that knowledge as summary: it selects discriminatingly, so each reference is doing a job in an argument the student has chosen to make, and it can be located in the clean copy in seconds."},{"h":"Language, form and structure — which for a play means the theatre","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","t":"AO2 is to analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects: the other 15 marks, and the ones most often thrown away. Learners must understand how writers create literary effects and use appropriate literary terminology. For a play, form and structure are not paragraph lengths but stagecraft: the single set, which the play calls a storage room and which also has to be every place in the past; the two time frames running in the same space with no framing narrator; the doubling that lets one actor be the Ratcatcher and the figures of authority Eva meets; the storybook figure returning as a leitmotif; the sound of trains and journeys. Language means idiom and accent, the drift of Eva's speech from German into a coached English, silence, and the stage directions. A top-level answer names the method, points to the exact moment or the few words that show it, and then evaluates what it does to an audience."},{"h":"The question, the tariff and the clean copy","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","t":"Section A is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts, assessing AO1 and AO2 only; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 1 to 10, at 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2. AO3 and AO4 carry no marks here, so there is no comparison to make and context has no marks of its own. The component is open book: learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room, and that copy must be completely unmarked, which rules out annotation, underlining and written tabs. The whole paper is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and is worth 40 per cent of the International GCSE. A top-level answer uses the open book for precision rather than for rescue: short, chosen references found fast, checked for wording, with the time spent on argument."},{"h":"The coursework route: the same skills, with time to redraft","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same modern drama list. Assignment A is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2, and the assignment must allow learners to access both. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, with no penalty for exceeding it, and marking is on the same five-level grid on a best-fit basis, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. The model wordings are worth practising on whichever route you take: 'Explore the ways in which...' and 'In what ways does...', each closing 'You must consider language, form and structure.' A top-level assignment does exactly what the examined answer does — one argued position, sustained analysis of method, a cohesive evaluation, evidence quoted accurately — only tighter, because it has been redrafted."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning the question into a thesis","setup":"Here is a Section A question on Kindertransport: 'Explore the ways in which Samuels presents memory in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.' It is one of two questions offered on this text, it is worth 30 marks (15 AO1 and 15 AO2), and you have about forty-five minutes. Write the thesis sentence you would open with, and name the dramatic methods the essay will be built on.","steps":["Split the task in two: the focus is memory, and the closing instruction about language, form and structure tells you the evidence must be dramatic method, not plot.","List only the methods that actually put memory on stage in this play: the storage room where the past is literally kept and re-entered, the two time frames sharing one space, and the Ratcatcher who keeps coming back.","Turn that list into a claim with an argument inside it — Samuels does not have characters describe remembering, she makes remembering happen in front of the audience.","Write it out: Samuels presents memory as something that happens to people rather than something they choose to do, because the shared set, the overlapping time frames and the returning Ratcatcher force the buried past into the present until Evelyn can no longer hold it shut.","Test the sentence: it answers the focus, it takes a position somebody could argue against, and every method named in it has a paragraph and a located moment waiting for it."],"cues":["Which words in the question tell you what kind of evidence counts?","Which features of the staging actually put the past on stage?","What claim about memory could those methods support?","How would you say that claim in one sentence?","What three checks should a thesis pass before you write?"]},{"title":"Choosing and embedding a reference in an open-book answer","setup":"Level 5 on this board's grid requires discriminating use of relevant examples in support. A student writing on Evelyn has two pieces of evidence in mind: first, that the whole play happens in one room in Evelyn's house, which the play itself calls a storage room; second, the exchange in which Faith presses her mother about who she used to be and Evelyn insists that Eva is dead. Task: turn these into references an examiner would credit, in sentences of your own, with the clean copy open beside you.","steps":["Sort the two: the first is a fact about the set, so it must be turned into an observation about what an audience sees, not simply stated.","Quote the play's own noun rather than describing the set loosely: the single set is a 'storage room', so the place Evelyn keeps her past is the place her house keeps what it has no use for.","For the second, quote only the word that does the work — Evelyn's answer is that Eva is 'dead' — and let your own sentence carry the rest, because a long copied speech eats time and proves nothing.","Embed each reference inside a claim: Samuels makes denial literal, since the woman does not say she has forgotten Eva but that Eva is 'dead', which converts a self into a corpse and makes remembering an exhumation.","Check both against the clean copy for exact wording and location, and note in your plan where each sits, so you can reach it without leafing through the play."],"cues":["Which of the two pieces of evidence is not yet doing any work?","Which single noun from the play would sharpen the point about the set?","How much of Evelyn's answer do you actually need to quote?","What claim can the quoted word be built into?","What must you do before the exam so the reference is usable in it?"]},{"title":"Building a top-level paragraph on the Ratcatcher","setup":"In Kindertransport a German children's storybook about the Ratcatcher — the piper who leads a town's children away and never brings them back — travels with Eva when she is sent to England, and the Ratcatcher himself appears on stage at moments of fear. The same male actor plays him and the figures of authority Eva meets, including the official who searches her on the journey out. Task: write one paragraph for the question 'In what ways does Samuels present fear in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure.', using the Ratcatcher as your evidence.","steps":["Point: Samuels gives fear a body, so the audience meets it as a presence in the room instead of hearing a character report it.","Evidence, named exactly: the figure the play calls the 'Ratcatcher' steps out of the storybook a child is carrying and returns through the play, and the actor who plays him also wears the uniforms of the men who decide what happens to her.","Analyse the form: doubling makes the fairy-tale threat and the real official the same body, so the audience cannot separate a child's nightmare from what is actually being done to her.","Analyse the structure: because he returns rather than being explained away, the Ratcatcher works as a leitmotif, each reappearance gathering the fear of the last, so the threat grows without a single new event.","Judge it: the effect is that fear here is not an episode but a condition, which is why it can still be in the room decades later when Evelyn is a grown woman with an English house and an English voice."],"cues":["What does Samuels do with fear that a novel could not?","Where does that presence come from, and who else does the actor play?","What does sharing one actor between the two do to an audience?","Why does his returning matter more than his first entrance?","What can you now say about fear in the play as a whole?"]},{"title":"Reading the two time frames as structure","setup":"Kindertransport is built so that past and present are played in the same storage room with no change of set: Eva the child and Evelyn the woman she became are played by different actors and can be on stage at the same time, and the action slides between the years without a blackout to separate them. Task: for the question 'Explore the ways in which Samuels presents identity in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.', show in four or five moves how this structural choice does the work.","steps":["Name the choice precisely: this is not flashback, because the past is never framed as a memory being narrated — it runs alongside the present in the same space.","Say what the audience is looking at: one woman is on stage as two people at once, so a divided identity is presented rather than described.","Attach it to the argument: Evelyn's insistence that Eva is 'dead' would be merely a claim if she spoke it in an empty room, but it is unanswerable as an image, because the self she buries is standing a few feet away.","Push to the effect: sharing the room makes the past a place she still lives beside, so assimilation stops looking like a decision she once took and starts looking like a wall she has to keep rebuilding.","Judge: Samuels makes structure carry the theme, so the play argues that a buried identity is not gone, only unattended."],"cues":["How does this differ from a flashback?","What is the audience actually seeing when both are on stage?","Which of Evelyn's positions does that image test?","What does sharing one room suggest about her present life?","So what does the structure argue about identity?"]},{"title":"Lifting a narrative paragraph off the lower levels","setup":"A student has written this for 'In what ways does Samuels present the relationship between Eva and Lil? You must consider language, form and structure.': 'When Eva arrives in England, Lil looks after her and is kind to her. Eva learns English and starts to fit in. Later Lil adopts her and she is called Evelyn. This shows that Lil is a good mother to Eva.' Task: diagnose what is holding it down and rebuild it as a paragraph that analyses method.","steps":["Diagnose: every sentence reports an event, so the paragraph tells the story of the relationship instead of examining how Samuels builds it — the grid calls exactly that largely narrative.","Find the method hiding inside the events: the audience hears Eva's speech being taught towards English across the scenes, so a change of self is carried in dialogue rather than announced.","Rewrite the point so it argues: Samuels presents Lil's care and Eva's remaking as one process, because the English that saves the child is also the English that covers her over.","Add the analysis: because the audience hears the change happening in the dialogue rather than being told about it afterwards, the kindness and the loss arrive together and cannot be weighed separately.","Close on judgement: the relationship is rescue and replacement at once — the English that saves the child is the English that covers her over — which is why the adult Evelyn defends it so fiercely, and why the paragraph ends on what the method does to an audience rather than on what Lil is like."],"cues":["What is every sentence of the student's paragraph doing?","Which of those events is really a language choice by Samuels?","How would you restate the point so it argues rather than reports?","What does hearing the change, rather than being told it, achieve?","What judgement does that leave you able to make about the relationship as a whole?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions, with the clean copy open","setup":"In Section A you are offered two questions on Kindertransport and answer one. Suppose they are 'Explore the ways in which Samuels presents Faith in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.' and 'In what ways does Samuels present separation in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure.' You have your clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition and about five minutes to choose and plan. Task: choose one and justify the choice.","steps":["Weigh the coverage: Faith belongs almost entirely to the present-day strand, while separation runs through both time frames, so the second question reaches more of the play.","Weigh the analysis available: separation is carried by staging you can work on — the partings, the journey, the two time frames in one room — so dramatic method stays in view the whole way through.","Check the trap: a question that looks easier is worth nothing if all you could do with it is narrate, so only take one you already have a position on.","Commit, and plan three paragraphs on separation: the child sent from her mother, the mother refused when she comes back for her, and the daughter leaving home in the present.","Beside each paragraph write the moment it will use and the few words you will quote, so the open book is used for locating a reference quickly rather than for reading in the exam."],"cues":["Which question reaches more of the play?","Which question gives you more staging to analyse?","What must be true of any question before you choose it?","What three paragraphs would the chosen question take?","What should be settled before the writing starts?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Kindertransport question carry, and how do they divide between the assessment objectives?","a":"30 marks: 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2. AO3 and AO4 carry no marks in Section A."},{"q":"What does the instruction 'You must consider language, form and structure' demand in a question on a play?","a":"Analysis of dramatic method — the set, the two time frames, doubling, sound, stage directions and dialogue — not a retelling of the action."},{"q":"What may you take into the Component 2 examination, and in what condition?","a":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition of each selected set text, completely unmarked."},{"q":"What lifts an answer from Level 4 to Level 5 on this board's grid?","a":"Level 4 shows thorough engagement and sustained analysis; Level 5 adds assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure, and discriminating use of relevant examples."},{"q":"What does 'discriminating use of relevant examples' look like in practice in an open-book answer?","a":"Short chosen references, quoted accurately or located precisely, embedded in your own sentence and doing work in the argument — not long copied passages or vague gestures at the plot."},{"q":"Why is a paragraph of Kindertransport history a poor use of time in this section?","a":"Section A assesses AO1 and AO2 only; context carries no marks of its own here, so it should inform understanding rather than occupy a paragraph."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section A is one 30-mark essay question chosen from two on the set drama text, marked 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2; AO3 and AO4 carry no marks in this section.","Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and is 40 per cent of the International GCSE, so the Kindertransport essay is worth about forty-five minutes.","Component 2 is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken into the examination room.","The five-level grid runs Level 1 at 1–6, Level 2 at 7–12, Level 3 at 13–18, Level 4 at 19–24 and Level 5 at 25–30, and marks are awarded on a best-fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses.","Level 5 requires assured knowledge and understanding, assured personal engagement, a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure, and discriminating use of relevant examples.","On the coursework route, Assignment A is a teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the drama text with the same 15 AO1 and 15 AO2 split and an advisory word count of 650 to 800 words.","Kindertransport is a two-act play by Diane Samuels whose present-day and wartime strands are played in one set, which the play calls a storage room, in Evelyn's house; Eva and Evelyn are played by different actors.","The only question shape this section uses is the 30-mark essay. The short recall tasks in this room carry invented tariffs for practice; treat only the 30-mark questions as exam shapes.","Evidence rule for an open-book answer: quote a few words at most, locate everything else by describing the moment exactly, and check the wording of anything you quote against your own clean copy before you rely on it."],"questions":[{"q":"Identify the two time frames Kindertransport moves between, and state the single setting in which both are played. (3 marks)","a":"The present day, in which Faith is packing to move out and opens a box of papers, and the years of Eva's departure from Germany and her English childhood; both are played in one set, the storage room of Evelyn's house.","sol":"Kindertransport runs two time frames at once. In the present, Faith is packing to leave her mother's house and opens a box of old papers and a German storybook kept in the room; in the past, Eva is prepared for departure, sent alone out of Germany, taken in by Lil and brought up as an English girl. Both are played in the same set — the play's own word for it is a 'storage room', in Evelyn's house — with no change of scenery, and Eva and Evelyn are played by different actors, so the child and the woman she became can be on stage together.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about who is on stage in each strand of the play, and when each strand is happening.","Work out what the set is when the play opens, then ask whether it ever changes for the other strand.","The answer needs both periods named and the one room that holds them, in the play's own word for it."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Explain who the Ratcatcher is in Kindertransport and where he comes from. (4 marks)","a":"The piper of the German children's storybook Eva carries, who leads a town's children away and never brings them back; Samuels puts him on stage, doubled with the figures of authority Eva meets.","sol":"The 'Ratcatcher' is the piper of the German children's storybook that travels with Eva when she is sent to England: in the tale he rids a town of its rats and then leads its children away, and they do not come back. Samuels lifts him off the page and puts him on the stage, where he appears at moments of fear, and she has the same male actor play him and the figures of authority Eva meets, including the official who searches her on the journey out. The effect is that the fairy-tale threat and the real one share a body, so a child's terror and an adult system of power cannot be told apart — which is why the figure keeps its force even in the present-day strand, long after the journey is over.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what Eva takes with her when she is sent away.","Ask where the figure comes from and how Samuels gets him from the page onto the stage.","The answer needs the storybook, what the figure does in that story, and the other roles the same actor plays."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"State the tariff of the Kindertransport question and how its marks are divided between assessment objectives. (2 marks)","a":"30 marks: 15 AO1 and 15 AO2. AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in Section A.","sol":"The Kindertransport question is one 30-mark essay chosen from two. The specification's breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks gives Questions 1 to 10 as 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, and nothing else: AO3, links and connections between texts, and AO4, texts and their contexts, carry no marks in this section. In practice that means half the marks are for close knowledge and understanding carried in a critical style with an informed personal engagement, half are for analysis of language, form and structure, there is no second text to bring in, and history earns credit only where it sharpens your reading of a method.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two assessment objectives are in play in this section, not four.","Recall the raw-mark breakdown the specification gives for Questions 1 to 10.","The answer needs the total, the split, and which objectives are absent."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Kindertransport turns on partings between mothers and daughters. Name three separations Samuels stages along that thread, and say which time frame each belongs to. (3 marks)","a":"Any three that the play stages as a parting between a mother and a daughter, each correctly placed, for example: Helga parting from Eva when the child is sent to England (past); Helga's return after the war and Eva's refusal to leave with her (past); Faith moving out of her mother's house (present); Evelyn's estrangement from Faith over what has been hidden (present).","sol":"The thread runs through both strands, and several answers are defensible as long as each is a parting between a mother and a daughter that the play actually stages and is placed in the right time frame. In the past: Helga prepares her daughter for the journey and parts from her when Eva is sent to England; and after the war Helga comes to England for her daughter and is refused, so the two part again. In the present: Faith is packing to leave her mother's house, and the discovery of what has been kept in the storage room opens a distance between her and Evelyn. A good answer names three of these, places each strand correctly, and — if it has room — notices that the play makes them rhyme rather than merely follow one another.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["More than one generation of mothers and daughters is on stage in this play.","Track the mother-and-daughter line through both strands rather than listing every event in order.","The answer needs three partings, each named by who is parting from whom and whether it belongs to the past or the present strand."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Samuels presents Evelyn in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Evelyn as a self built to hold a secret: the split with Eva staged on one set, a coached and flattened English voice, her hostility to Faith's questions and her attempt to destroy the papers, and the mother she refused underneath all of it.","sol":"Samuels presents Evelyn less as a woman with a secret than as a self constructed to keep one, and the play's design makes that construction visible rather than telling us about it. Because Eva and Evelyn are played by separate actors on a single set, Evelyn's composure never stands alone: the child she disowns is a few feet away throughout, so her Englishness reads as an effort being sustained in front of us rather than as a settled fact. Language carries the same argument. In the past strand the audience hears Eva's speech being taught towards English and her German falling away; in the present, Evelyn's careful, flattened idiom is that process completed, so her voice is itself the evidence of what has been taken out of her. When Faith presses her about who she used to be, Evelyn's answer is not that she has forgotten but that Eva is 'dead' — one word that does the work of a speech, because it turns a self into a corpse and makes remembering an exhumation rather than a choice. Structurally, Samuels tests her by pressure rather than by revelation: Faith opens a box, Lil will not keep quiet, and the past is already on stage, so Evelyn's denials have to be repeated, and each repetition costs her more of the poise she began with. Her attempt to get rid of what Faith has found is the point at which denial stops being passive and becomes an act, and it is also the point at which she is least likeable and most understandable at once. Behind all of it stands the scene in which her mother comes back for her and she refuses; Samuels withholds the reunion an audience expects, and that refusal explains what the present-day Evelyn does, because a woman who has already chosen once cannot reopen the choice without sentencing herself. The evaluation, then, is that Evelyn is not presented as cold. She is presented as someone whose survival depended on a wall, and the method — two time frames, one room, the buried self permanently on stage — makes an audience feel that wall from both sides. The result is a character we are not permitted simply to blame, which is a more discriminating effect than either pity or condemnation would have been.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The audience can see Eva and Evelyn at the same time; ask what that does to anything Evelyn denies.","Build the essay on methods rather than events: the doubled presence on one set, the coached voice, the pressure of the present-day strand, and the scene the play withholds.","Aim for a thesis about what Evelyn is for in the play's design, then paragraphs that each analyse one method, anchored to a located moment, and end on a judgement."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Samuels present the relationship between mothers and daughters in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Three relationships made to rhyme across two time frames — Helga and Eva, Lil and Eva, Evelyn and Faith — so that mothering is presented as an act of separation and the damage looks inherited rather than personal.","sol":"Samuels presents mothering as an act of separation, and she argues it not by discussion but by making three relationships rhyme inside one room. Helga's care for Eva is shown as preparation for absence: the practical instruction she gives her daughter before the journey is an ordinary domestic exchange doing the work of a farewell, so love is staged as the very thing that hands the child over. Lil's care is rescue and remaking at once — the audience hears Eva's English being taught out of her German across the scenes, so the mothering and the erasure are heard in the same dialogue and cannot be weighed apart. Then Samuels puts Helga's return against both. The mother who saved her child by sending her away comes to England to reclaim her and is refused, and the play declines to give the audience the reunion its form has promised; the refusal is unbearable precisely because both women are right, which is why sympathies divide rather than being directed. The present-day strand repeats the pattern in a lower key: Faith is packing to leave, and the mother who cannot hand over her own history has nothing to give her except the contents of the 'storage room' she has kept shut. That single set is the argument's engine. Because Eva's parting and Faith's packing happen in the same space, an audience reads inheritance before either character can name it, and Evelyn's flat insistence to her daughter that Eva is 'dead' lands as the cost of the pattern rather than as personal coldness. My judgement is that the play refuses to blame any individual mother, because each act of mothering it stages was, in its moment, the only protective thing available; but it equally refuses to call the separations harmless, since the damage is visibly passed down a generation to a daughter who was never in danger at all. That is what the method buys Samuels: a moral position an audience arrives at by watching a repetition rather than by being told.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question's noun is plural: more than one pair of mother and daughter is on stage in this play.","Look for what the three relationships have in common structurally, rather than describing each one in turn.","Aim for a thesis about what mothering is presented as, then take the pairs in an order that builds towards a judgement, each anchored to a moment you can find in the copy."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Samuels presents fear in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Fear embodied rather than reported: the Ratcatcher lifted out of the storybook and doubled with the officials, the recurrence that makes fear cumulative, the journey and its sounds, and the adult Evelyn's fear of her own past.","sol":"Samuels presents fear as something an audience is put in the room with rather than something a character reports, and her chief instrument is the 'Ratcatcher'. He begins as a picture in a German children's storybook — the piper who takes a town's children and does not bring them back — and the decisive choice is that Samuels does not leave him there: the figure walks out of the book a child is carrying and onto the stage, so a story told to frighten children becomes a presence that can enter a scene. Doubling then does the analytical work. Because the same actor plays the Ratcatcher and the figures of authority Eva meets, including the official who searches her on the journey out, the audience cannot separate the nightmare from the procedure; a uniform and a fairy tale wear one body, and the play thereby says something precise about how a child experiences state violence — not as policy but as an ogre. Structurally, the fear is cumulative rather than episodic. The Ratcatcher returns, the journey returns, the sound of trains and departures returns, so each reappearance gathers the charge of the last and the threat deepens without the plot supplying a single new danger; this is leitmotif working as argument. Language reinforces it: the officials' clipped authority, the practical vocabulary of packing and papers, and the flat things adults say to a child about a journey all keep the horror unnamed, so what is not said presses harder than what is. The most discriminating point is what happens to the fear once the danger is over. It does not end; it moves indoors, into the room that holds Evelyn's boxes, and surfaces as the adult woman's panic at her own daughter's questions, so that the fear of the past and the fear of remembering it are staged as the same thing. My judgement is that Samuels presents fear as a condition rather than an event, and that the play earns this claim theatrically rather than rhetorically: because we watch the same body return, we experience the persistence she is describing instead of being asked to believe in it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what Samuels does with fear that a novelist could not do.","Track one method across the whole play rather than collecting frightening moments from it.","Aim for a thesis that says what kind of thing fear is in this play, then analyse the staging, the recurrence and the language in turn."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"In what ways does Samuels present Lil in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Lil as rescuer and remaker at once: plain-spoken English warmth set against the teaching of English that covers Eva's German over, her presence in both time frames, and her role in the present as the one who will not keep the secret.","sol":"Samuels presents Lil as the play's most straightforward person and, for that very reason, as the instrument of its least straightforward loss. Her language is the first method: against Helga's formality and the officials' clipped authority, Lil speaks a plain, colloquial English, and Samuels uses that plainness to make her kindness unarguable — an audience is given no reason to doubt her, which is exactly what the play needs if the argument is not to collapse into blame. The second method is structural. Lil appears in both time frames, played across the years, so she is the one figure who links the child's world to the woman's, and the audience watches her mothering acquire consequences it never intended. The third is what her care actually consists of: the audience hears Eva being brought into English, so the language that lets the child survive is the same language that closes over her German, and the rescue and the remaking are audible in a single conversation rather than presented as separate events. In the present-day strand Samuels uses her differently again, and this is the sharpest choice in the character's design: it is Lil, not Evelyn, who tells Faith what she has a right to know, so the truth reaches the third generation through the foster mother rather than the birth mother, and Evelyn's insistence that Eva is 'dead' is contradicted by the one person with no interest in contradicting her. My judgement is that Lil is the play's test of the audience. She makes it impossible to read Eva's remaking as cruelty, and therefore impossible to comfort ourselves by finding a villain; the cost is paid all the same. That is a more demanding effect than a hostile foster mother would have produced, and it is why the adult Evelyn defends her so fiercely — if Lil were not good, the choice Evelyn made would be indefensible, and Evelyn cannot afford that.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Lil is one of the characters who appears in both time frames; ask what the play gains from that.","Look at her language beside the language of the other adults, and then at what her care actually changes about Eva.","Aim for a thesis that holds both sides of her at once, and let the judgement say what the play gains by making her kind."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Samuels uses the setting of the storage room in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"One set doing three jobs: the room in Evelyn's house where Faith finds the box, every location in the past, and a physical image of what has been shut away — which removes the option of separating the two lives.","sol":"The play's single set is not a house, a station or a platform but, in Samuels's own word, a 'storage room', and almost everything the play argues is carried by that choice. Its first job is literal: it is the room in Evelyn's house where things are kept because there is nowhere else for them, and it is where Faith, packing to move out, finds the box of papers and the German storybook. Storage is the exact word for what Evelyn has done with her childhood — not destroyed, not integrated, kept. Its second job is theatrical: the same room has to be Germany, the train, the crossing and the English house of the past, because there is no change of scenery and no blackout to fence the strands apart. That has a consequence an audience feels physically. If the past had its own set, it could be visited and left; because it does not, Eva is standing in Evelyn's house, and Evelyn's denial is spoken over the head of the child she is denying. The third job is emblematic, and this is where the AO2 marks are: a room of boxes is an image of a life that has been packed rather than unpacked, so when Evelyn tries to get rid of what Faith has found, the audience watches a woman doing to her past in the present exactly what she did to it as a girl. Language works with the space — the domestic vocabulary of clearing out, sorting and throwing away is the same vocabulary the play has been using for memory — so the set keeps converting household action into moral action. My judgement is that the storage room is the play's argument in physical form: it insists that a buried self is not gone but stored, and that anybody living above it is only ever a floor away from it. A production that split the strands into two sets might be clearer, and would be far less true.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the room is in the present, what it has to become in the past, and what it stands for.","Consider what a production with two separate sets would give the audience that this one deliberately withholds.","Aim for a thesis that treats the set as an argument rather than a background, then analyse each job it does."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Samuels presents Faith's discovery of the past in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Discovery staged as an ordinary clear-out that the set turns into inheritance: objects rather than confession, Lil rather than Evelyn as the source of truth, and a third generation handed the silence before the story.","sol":"Samuels gives the present-day strand its engine by making discovery physical and accidental. Faith is not investigating; she is packing to move out, and what she finds in the 'storage room' — a box of papers and a German storybook that is not written in her language — arrives as an ordinary domestic chore turning into an inheritance. The choice to reveal through objects rather than confession is the first method worth analysing, because it lets the audience see the past before it is explained, and it puts Faith in the position the play has already put Eva in: holding documents she cannot read about a life she did not choose. Structurally, the discovery is what forces the two strands together. The papers are found in the same space the past is being played in, so the audience is ahead of Faith, watching a child's journey in the room where her daughter is opening the evidence of it — a dramatic irony that makes every one of Evelyn's evasions visible as an evasion. Samuels then makes a pointed choice about who answers her: the truth comes to Faith from Lil rather than from Evelyn, while Evelyn will say only that Eva is 'dead' and moves to get rid of what has been found. The mother is the obstacle and the foster grandmother is the source, which tells us how far the damage has travelled. Language marks the generational distance too: Faith's idiom is unhistorical and modern, and her questions are the blunt ones of somebody who has never had to be careful, so the play's terrible material is handled in a completely ordinary voice. My judgement is that Faith is not there to solve the past but to prove that it is still live, and Samuels's discriminating stroke is to give her the silence before she is given the story — because that is precisely the order in which the second generation of such families receives it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Faith finds objects rather than being told a story; ask what that changes for an audience.","Notice which mother answers her questions and which does not, and treat that as a structural choice.","Aim for a thesis about what the third generation is for in the play's design, then judge what the strand leaves unresolved."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Stretch practice (the board's modern drama questions are worded 'Explore the ways in which...' or 'In what ways does...', so the real paper is unlikely to phrase one as a proposition): 'Kindertransport is a play about surviving rather than a play about being rescued.' To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"A qualified agreement: the rescue is real and Lil's care is genuine, but the play's methods keep returning to what survival costs — the lost language, the refused mother, the buried self — so survival is what the play is built to examine.","sol":"I agree substantially, though the proposition needs its opposite taken seriously first. The rescue in this play is real and is presented without irony: a child is taken out of danger and is loved by the woman who receives her, and Lil's plain, warm English is written so that an audience cannot suspect her. Samuels needs that, because a play about a cruel placement would be a much smaller play. But the methods are all pointed at afterwards rather than at the escape. The two time frames in one set mean the child's journey is never finished — it is still being played in the room where the grown woman lives — so structurally the play spends itself on the decades after the rescue rather than on the rescue itself. The Ratcatcher makes the same argument: a figure who takes children away and does not return them is not a threat that ends at a border, and because he recurs, the play defines survival as living with something that keeps coming back. Language supplies the cost in detail. The audience hears Eva's German covered over by the English that saves her, and when Faith asks who her mother used to be, Evelyn answers that Eva is 'dead' — a rescued child describing herself as a fatality. Most decisively, Samuels stages the moment when Helga comes back for her daughter and is refused; if this were a rescue play, that scene would be its reunion, and instead it is its wound. The honest qualification is that the play will not let survival and rescue be opposites: Eva survives because she was rescued, and the loss of her mother, her name and her language is the price of the same act, which is why no character in the play can be blamed for it. My judgement is therefore that 'rescue' names the event and 'survival' names the subject — and that the play's design, which returns to what the saved child carries rather than to the saving, proves the proposition true in the sense that matters.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["A proposition question needs the case against your own answer to be put properly, not waved at.","Ask what the play spends its length on, and which of its methods keep returning, rather than what happens in it.","Aim for a position stated early, tested in each paragraph against a located moment, and sharpened rather than reversed at the end."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Samuels use the figure of the Ratcatcher in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The Ratcatcher as the play's structural spine: a storybook figure made flesh, doubled with the officials, returning as a leitmotif so that fear accumulates, and available as an image for everything that takes children away.","sol":"Samuels uses the 'Ratcatcher' to convert a historical horror into a theatrical one, and the sequence of her choices is worth taking in order. First, she gives Eva a German storybook whose piper leads a town's children away and does not bring them back, so the play's threat is introduced inside a form a child can hold. Second, she refuses to leave him in the book: he appears on stage, which means an audience does not hear about a child's imagination but sits in a room with it. Third, she doubles him with the figures of authority Eva meets, including the official who searches her on the journey out, so the fairy tale and the state become one body; this is the single most analysable choice in the play, because it says that for a child there is no difference between an ogre and a procedure. Fourth, she makes him recur. As a leitmotif, each return gathers the meaning of the last, so the play can deepen fear without inventing new danger — structure doing the work that plot would do in a lesser play. The figure also carries the play's language of taking and keeping: he is a catcher, and what he catches is not returned, which quietly places Helga, who gave her child away to save her, and Lil, who kept her, on the same continuum as the thing that takes children. My judgement is that the Ratcatcher is not a decorative Gothic effect but the play's method of argument: he allows Samuels to show that what happened to these children cannot be settled by the facts of the transport, because it went on living in the people it happened to. The limitation worth conceding is that his power is borrowed — he frightens because of what the human scenes have already made us fear — so the figure is the play's spine rather than its heart.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The figure exists in two media before he exists on the stage; track how Samuels moves him between them.","Take the choices in order — the storybook, the appearance, the doubling, the recurrence — rather than describing each time he appears.","Aim for a judgement that says what kind of work the figure does for the play as a whole, and concede one limitation."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Stretch practice (a synoptic comparison; the board words its modern drama questions 'Explore the ways in which...' or 'In what ways does...', so the real paper is unlikely to set the two strands against each other like this): compare how Samuels presents Eva's enforced departure in the past strand with Faith's move out of her mother's house in the present, and judge how far the play asks an audience to read the second through the first. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"A comparison rather than a survey of departures: the two leavings share one set and one structural argument about inheritance, but differ in cause, scale and in what the characters can choose — and the judgement turns on how far the parallel holds without becoming false equivalence.","sol":"The two departures are alike in method and unlike in almost everything else, and the comparison is the play's own. Take the enforced departure first: Helga prepares her daughter for the journey through ordinary practical instruction, so the leaving is carried by domestic detail rather than by declaration, and an audience watches a mother teaching a child to manage without her while neither says what is happening. The language is flat and useful, and that flatness is the point — the emotion is displaced into the business of packing, which is how people survive such moments and also how they store them unprocessed. Faith's leaving, in the present strand and roughly fifty years on, is the ordinary opposite: a grown daughter moving out of her mother's house, an event that in any other play would be a comic domestic subject, and Samuels keeps it in a domestic register of sorting and packing rather than of crisis. What makes the two speak to each other is form. Because both are staged in the same 'storage room' with no change of set, and because the past is played in front of the audience rather than reported, the modern move-out cannot be watched innocently: it acquires a weight Faith has done nothing to earn. That is the play's structural argument about inheritance — nothing needs to be said for the second departure to be read through the first. The differences are kept as carefully as the likeness. Eva's leaving is compelled, is one-way, and has the Ratcatcher standing behind it, the figure who takes children and does not bring them back, so departure in the past strand is loss with a body; Faith's is chosen, reversible, and addressed to a mother who is still in the room. Samuels also stages a refusal of departure — when Helga comes for her daughter and asks her to leave with her, the child who was sent away will not go — which shows that in the past strand a leaving can be undone only by another leaving, where in the present a daughter could simply come back. My judgement is that the play does ask an audience to read Faith's departure through Eva's, but at a deliberately lower volume: the rhyme works, and stays honest, because Samuels never asks the second departure to carry the first's scale, so the false equivalence the parallel risks is headed off by the difference in what is at stake rather than by anything said in the dialogue.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Two people leave a home in this play, roughly fifty years apart and for entirely different reasons; start from what the two leavings nevertheless share.","Work out what the shared set gives the audience that two separate settings could not.","Aim for an evaluation that also names the risk in the parallel and says how Samuels keeps it from becoming false equivalence."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Coursework route, Component 3: write an Assignment A title on Kindertransport in the board's own format, and explain how it gives a candidate access to both AO1 and AO2 within the advisory word count. (6 marks)","a":"A title in the modelled wording, closing 'You must consider language, form and structure.', on a theme or character broad enough for whole-text knowledge but narrow enough for sustained analysis in 650 to 800 words.","sol":"A suitable title is: 'Explore the ways in which Samuels presents separation in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.' It follows the modelled format exactly: the stem 'Explore the ways in which...', the writer named, the text named, and the closing instruction that carries the AO2 marks. It gives access to AO1 because separation runs through the whole play, so a candidate must show close knowledge across both time frames — Helga and Eva before the journey, Helga refused when she comes back, Faith leaving home in the present — and must take a personal position on what the play says about it. It gives access to AO2 because separation is carried by method rather than by statement: the single set the play calls a 'storage room' holding both partings, the two actors playing one woman, the doubling of the Ratcatcher with the officials, and the recurrence that makes each parting echo the last. It is also the right size for 650 to 800 words: broad enough for three developed paragraphs and a judgement, narrow enough that each paragraph can sustain analysis rather than survey. Assignment A is marked out of 30, with 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2, on the same five-level grid, so a title that let a candidate narrate would forfeit half the marks by design.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The specification prints model assignment wordings; match their shape rather than inventing a new one.","Check the title against both objectives in turn and ask what a candidate would have to do to earn each half.","The answer needs the title itself, then what it opens up for each objective and why it fits the word count."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In which component and section is Kindertransport examined, and for how many marks?","a":"Component 2, Section A (Modern Drama): one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two."},{"q":"Which two assessment objectives carry no marks in Component 2, Section A?","a":"AO3 and AO4. Questions 1 to 10 are 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2 only, so there is no comparison and no context credit."},{"q":"What is the name of the technique by which one actor plays the Ratcatcher and the figures of authority Eva meets?","a":"Doubling."},{"q":"What single set holds both of the play's time frames, in the play's own word for it?","a":"A storage room in Evelyn's house."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Kindertransport — where it sits in the qualification","back":"Diane Samuels's play is a modern drama set text for Component 2, Section A: one 30-mark essay from a choice of two, 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, open book, with AO3 and AO4 uncredited.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"Eva Schlesinger","back":"The German Jewish child sent alone to England on the Kindertransport. She is played by a different actor from Evelyn, so the child and the woman she became can share the stage.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Evelyn","back":"Eva grown up: an anglicised woman who has kept her childhood from her daughter, resists Faith's questions about what is stored in the room, and tells her that Eva is 'dead'.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Helga Schlesinger","back":"Eva's mother, who prepares her child to manage without her and sends her away to save her; she survives the war, comes to England to reclaim her daughter, and is refused.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Lil","back":"The plain-spoken English woman who takes Eva in, brings her up and in time adopts her. In the present she is elderly, and it is she, not Evelyn, who tells Faith the truth.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Faith","back":"Evelyn's adult daughter, in the middle of moving out. Her discovery of a box of papers and a German storybook in the storage room begins the present-day action.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The Ratcatcher","back":"The piper of the German storybook Eva carries, who leads a town's children away and never brings them back. Samuels lifts him out of the book and puts him on stage as the play's figure of fear.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Doubling in Kindertransport","back":"One male actor plays the Ratcatcher and the figures of authority Eva meets, so the fairy-tale threat and the real one wear the same body and cannot be told apart.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The two time frames","back":"Past and present run in the same room with no change of set and no framing narrator, so remembering is staged as intrusion rather than described as recollection.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The storage room setting","back":"The play's single set, and its own word for it: the room in Evelyn's house where the boxes are kept, which must also be every location in the past — a life stored rather than unpacked.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Leitmotif","back":"A figure, image or sound that recurs and gathers meaning each time. The Ratcatcher works this way, so fear grows through repetition rather than through new events.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in Section A","back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement — 15 of the 30 marks.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO2 in Section A","back":"Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects — the other 15 marks. For a play this includes dramatic form, staging and structure.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Level 5 on the board's grid (25–30)","back":"Assured knowledge and understanding; assured personal engagement and a perceptive critical style; cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure; discriminating use of relevant examples — so short, chosen, accurately quoted references.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"Dramatic methods that keep the past present in this play","back":"The shared storage-room set; the two time frames played without a break or a narrator; the Ratcatcher returning as a leitmotif; the doubling of one actor across fairy tale and officialdom.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: Explore the ways in which Samuels presents memory in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Samuels stages memory as an intrusion rather than a recollection, so the audience experiences the past arriving uninvited instead of hearing a character describe it.","Evidence (the set, in the play's own noun): the single set is a 'storage room' in Evelyn's house, and the scenes of Eva's childhood are played in the same space in which Faith, packing to move out, opens the boxes kept there.","Analysis of form: because there is no change of set and no framing device announcing a flashback, the past is not Evelyn's narration — it simply occupies the stage alongside her, and Eva, played by a second actor, is present while the woman she became denies her.","Evidence (a line, quoted only as far as it is needed): when Faith presses her about who she used to be, Evelyn's answer is that Eva is 'dead'.","Analysis of language: the single word is more violent than a refusal would be, because it does not say the past is finished with but that a person is; memory is therefore not something Evelyn declines to do but something she has classified as an exhumation, and the word is spoken with the child in question standing in the same room.","Development through structure: Samuels then makes the intrusion repeat — the Ratcatcher returns, the journey returns, the partings return — so memory here has the shape of something that will not stay put, and each return costs Evelyn more of the composure she began with.","Mini-judgement: the effect is that memory is presented less as a store Evelyn could choose to open than as a room she has been living beside all along, which is why every attempt she makes to get rid of what has been found reads as housekeeping raised to the level of self-defence.","Why this is Level 5: it takes a position and holds it; the two references are short, located and embedded in the argument rather than retold, which is what the grid means by discriminating use of relevant examples; the analysis moves across form, structure and language and evaluates their combined effect; and the judgement answers the question instead of summarising the play.","Working note: in the exam you have the clean copy, so check the exact wording and the page of anything you intend to quote. This paragraph is built to need only one word of quotation, which is the safest way to write when you are not certain of a longer line."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: Explore the ways in which Samuels presents memory in Kindertransport. You must consider language, form and structure.","Point: Samuels shows that Evelyn does not want to remember her past.","Evidence: Faith finds a box of old papers in the storage room, and Evelyn is angry about it and will not talk about where she came from.","Analysis: this shows the memories are painful for her, because she has kept them shut away in boxes for years and has hidden them from her own daughter.","Structure: the play also keeps going back to the past, which reminds the audience of what happened to Eva when she was a child.","Mini-judgement: so memory is an important theme in the play, and Samuels presents it as something painful.","Why this is a low Level 3: it sits on the Level 2/3 border and is placed in Level 3 on best fit by its AO1 alone — the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant. Its AO2 is Level 2: the paragraph explains what the audience is told rather than how the play is made, it says the action goes back to the past without noticing that both time frames share one room, it names no dramatic method and no terminology, and nothing is quoted or located, which is 'some comment on the language, form and structure' rather than the sound understanding Level 3 asks for. Mark it at the bottom of the band — held up by its knowledge, pulled down by its analysis.","What would lift it: keep the same evidence but ask what the audience actually sees — one room holding both lives, two actors playing one woman — attach a few quoted words to the point instead of describing the argument at a distance, and let the judgement say what the method does to an audience rather than what the theme is."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Kindertransport by Diane Samuels: people, story, themes, form and structure","items":["The play is by Diane Samuels and was first performed in the early 1990s; the prescribed edition for this course is NHB Modern Plays (Nick Hern Books, second edition), May 2008, ISBN 9781854595270.","It is in two acts and is played on a single set, which the play calls a storage room, in Evelyn's house; the same space must also serve as every location in the past.","Eva Schlesinger: a German Jewish child sent alone to England on the Kindertransport before the Second World War.","Evelyn: the same person grown up, an assimilated Englishwoman who has kept her origins from her daughter; she is played by a different actor from Eva so that both can be on stage at once.","Helga Schlesinger: Eva's mother, who prepares her daughter to manage alone and sends her away to save her, survives the war, and comes to England to reclaim her.","Lil: the plain-spoken English woman who takes Eva in, brings her up and in time adopts her; in the present she is elderly and is the one who tells Faith the truth.","Faith: Evelyn's adult daughter, in the middle of moving out, whose discovery of a box of papers and a German storybook in the storage room begins the present-day action.","The Ratcatcher: the piper of the German children's storybook Eva carries, who leads a town's children away and never brings them back; he steps out of the book and recurs on stage as a figure of fear.","Doubling: one male actor plays the Ratcatcher and the figures of authority Eva meets, including the official who searches her on her journey, so menace and officialdom share a single body.","Story in order (1): Helga prepares Eva to leave, teaching her the practical skills she will need to look after herself, and Eva travels out of Germany by train with the Ratcatcher storybook among her things.","Story in order (2): Eva is met and taken in by Lil, is brought up in English, and works at getting her parents out of Germany; the war closes that route and contact with her parents is lost.","Story in order (3): Eva grows up in Lil's household and becomes an English girl in speech and habit; she is in time called Evelyn, and Lil's adoption of her is made legal.","Story in order (4): after the war Helga comes to England wanting her daughter to leave with her and begin again; she is refused, and mother and daughter part.","Story in order (5): decades later Faith finds the box in the storage room, Lil tells her what Evelyn will not, and Evelyn tries to get rid of what has been found while insisting that Eva is 'dead'.","Sequence to be clear about: Eva is called Evelyn and Lil's adoption of her is completed during her years in England, so by the time Helga comes back after the war her daughter already carries an English name and has an English legal mother — which is why that scene is the refusal of a mother who has been replaced, not only of a journey.","Themes an examiner rewards: separation and loss; memory, denial and the cost of silence; identity and the price of assimilation; mothers and daughters across three generations; survival and guilt; the fairy tale as a way of holding terror.","Form and structure to analyse: the single set; two time frames played without blackouts or a narrator; doubling; the Ratcatcher as a returning leitmotif; the sound of trains and journeys; the repetition that makes each parting echo an earlier one; the mirroring of Faith's leaving with Eva's.","Language to analyse: the contrast between Helga's formality, Lil's plain, colloquial English and the officials' clipped authority; Eva's German giving way to English; the adult Evelyn's flat, careful idiom and her use of the word 'dead' about her own childhood self; and what the stage directions ask a production to show.","Key moments to be able to find fast in the open book: the preparation for departure; the journey and the search; the first meeting with Lil; Eva being brought into English; the loss of contact with her parents; Helga's return and the refusal; Faith opening the box; Evelyn telling Faith that Eva is 'dead'; Lil telling Faith the truth; each reappearance of the Ratcatcher.","Background for understanding only, not for credit in Section A: the Kindertransport brought around ten thousand mostly Jewish children, unaccompanied, from Nazi-controlled Europe to Britain between late 1938 and the outbreak of war in September 1939. AO4 is not assessed here, so this should inform your reading rather than occupy a paragraph."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the prescribed edition and the open-book rules","items":["Component 2 is an open-book examination: learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room.","The copy must be completely unmarked — no annotation, no underlining, no highlighting and no writing on tabs or covers.","The prescribed edition for Kindertransport is listed in Appendix 3 of the specification: NHB Modern Plays (Nick Hern Books, second edition), May 2008, ISBN 9781854595270.","Learners or centres must purchase a prescribed edition of each of their set texts at the start of the course.","Component 1 is different: learners may not bring texts into that examination, although the relevant poems are provided in the paper.","Open book changes what examiners expect of you: references should be short, exact and chosen, so build revision around finding key moments quickly rather than around memorising long passages.","Because the copy is clean, learn the play's shape rather than page numbers: which act a moment falls in, and what comes immediately before and after it.","Quote sparingly and accurately. A few words inside quotation marks, embedded in your own sentence, is what the Level 5 phrase 'discriminating use of relevant examples' describes; a copied half-page is not.","Practical consequence for the exam room: plan first, then locate. Time spent leafing through the play is time not spent on the analysis that carries the AO2 marks."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Samuels present the relationship between mothers and daughters in Kindertransport? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","items":["Decoded question: 'In what ways' asks for Samuels's methods, not for the story of the relationships; 'mothers and daughters' is plural and covers Helga and Eva, Lil and Eva, and Evelyn and Faith; the closing instruction carries the AO2 marks, so every paragraph must analyse a dramatic method. AO3 and AO4 are not assessed, so there is no comparison and no history paragraph.","Thesis: Samuels presents mothering as an act of separation — each mother secures her daughter's future by losing her — and she argues it by making the three relationships rhyme across two time frames in one room rather than by taking them in turn.","Paragraph 1 — Helga and Eva: the preparation for departure, in which a mother teaches her child the practical business of managing without her. Analyse how an ordinary domestic action is made to carry the parting, and how the child stays visible on stage afterwards so the loss never leaves the room.","Paragraph 2 — Lil and Eva: rescue and remaking together. Analyse the language of anglicisation — the audience hears Eva being brought into English — and the adoption that follows, so that love and erasure are heard in the same dialogue.","Paragraph 3 — Helga's return: the confrontation in which the mother who saved her child discovers that saving her worked. Analyse how Samuels withholds the reunion the form has promised, and what the refusal does to an audience whose sympathies are divided rather than directed.","Paragraph 4 — Evelyn and Faith: the present-day repetition, a daughter leaving home and a mother unable to hand over her own history. Analyse how the shared 'storage room' puts Eva's parting and Faith's packing in one space, and quote Evelyn's word for her childhood self — Eva is 'dead' — as the cost of the pattern.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: how far is each mother's choice presented as love and how far as damage? Keep the balance moving rather than settling early, and let the Ratcatcher — the figure who takes children and does not return them — stand behind each parting.","What the conclusion must say: not that mothers and daughters matter in the play, but what the method argues — that by rhyming three relationships in one space Samuels presents the damage of separation as inherited rather than personal, so the play refuses to let us blame any single mother while refusing, equally, to call the separations harmless.","Timing: about forty-five minutes — five to decode and plan, thirty-five to write, five to check that every paragraph names a method, cites a located moment, and ends on a judgement.","Common way to lose marks on this question: writing four character sketches. The paragraphs must be about what Samuels does, with the characters as the evidence."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Twelve moments to be able to find in your clean copy","items":["Helga teaching Eva the practical skills she will need to manage without her, before the journey.","Eva packing, with the German storybook about the Ratcatcher going with her.","The journey out: the train, the crossing, and the official who searches her.","Eva's arrival and her first meeting with Lil.","Eva being brought into English, and the sound of her speech changing.","Letters passing between Eva and her parents, and the effort to get them out of Germany.","The point at which contact with her parents is lost.","The point at which Eva is called Evelyn — note who uses the name and how she responds, because arguments about her agency stand or fall there.","Faith opening the box in the storage room and finding the papers and the storybook.","Evelyn's refusal to answer Faith, her insistence that Eva is 'dead', and her attempt to get rid of what has been found.","Helga's return after the war, and the demand that her daughter leave England with her.","Lil telling Faith what Evelyn will not, and each reappearance of the Ratcatcher across the two acts."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:50:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Re-judged independently: the specification content is accurate throughout (15/15 AO1/AO2 split, AO3 and AO4 uncredited in Section A, the 1-6/7-12/13-18/19-24/25-30 bands and the Level 4 and Level 5 descriptors, open book with a completely unmarked prescribed copy, 650-800 advisory words on Component 3, and the Nick Hern 2008 ISBN 9781854595270), the account of the play is sound, and all three of the quotations it now takes from the text ('storage room', 'dead', 'Ratcatcher') are the play's own words used in the right mouths and the right moments; every fault the first examiner listed has been repaired rather than papered over, and the only thing standing between this kit and publication as it is, is a three-word clause in one hint that puts the two departures in different centuries."}}},"drama-kings-horseman":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"drama-kings-horseman","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Death and the King's Horseman is answered in Section A of Component 2: one 30-mark essay chosen from two, inside a 1 hour 30 minute paper worth 60 raw marks and 40% of the International GCSE. Fifteen of those marks are AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, a critical style, an informed personal engagement) and fifteen are AO2 (analysis of the language, form and structure Soyinka uses to create meanings and effects). AO3 and AO4 are not assessed here, so retelling Nigerian history or Soyinka's biography earns nothing on its own; the marks come from arguing about what the play does and how it makes its meaning. The section is open book — a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be on the desk — which raises, not lowers, the standard of evidence an examiner expects: short, exact, chosen references rather than remembered gist or long copied extracts. This room builds the whole-play knowledge, the method vocabulary and the paragraph shape that move an answer from the sound comment of Level 3 to the cohesive evaluation Level 5 asks for.","idea":[{"h":"One modern drama text, studied whole","t":"For this component learners study two set texts: one modern drama text — here Death and the King's Horseman by Wole Soyinka, chosen from a list of five — and one literary heritage text. The content overview asks learners to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response, with teaching focused on the study of whole texts. That last phrase is the exam-relevant one. A question on a character or a theme is a question about the whole design, not about the scene where the word in the question happens to appear. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it moves across all five scenes, choosing evidence from parts of the play the question does not obviously point at, and treats the play as one made object whose earlier moments were built to make its later ones land.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement (15 marks)","t":"Learners must demonstrate a close understanding of their drama text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the play. AO1 is worth 15 of the 30 marks in Section A. The model question wordings the specification prints fasten on either a character or group of characters (the ways in which a writer presents women) or a theme (in what ways a writer presents conflict), so Elesin Oba, the Praise-Singer, Iyaloja and the market women, Olunde, the Bride, Simon and Jane Pilkings, Amusa and Joseph, and the order of the five scenes, must be known well enough to be argued about rather than merely recounted. A critical style means writing about a made play, not a remembered story. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it states a position in its opening sentences and refines it as the evidence accumulates, and its examples are chosen because they are the best evidence available, which is what the grid means by discriminating use of relevant examples.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: analysis of language, form and structure (15 marks)","t":"Learners must show how writers use language, form and structure to create effects, understand how writers create literary effects, and use appropriate literary terminology. AO2 is the other 15 marks, and for a play form and structure mean the theatrical ones: the alternation of public Yoruba scenes with colonial interiors, the two registers Soyinka writes in (proverb-rich, image-heavy, praise-poetry speech against clipped naturalistic prose), the offstage drumming and dirge that run under the action, stage properties such as the confiscated egungun costume, the rich cloth, the bridal cloth and the chains, and the withholding of the death the play is named for until its final moments. Terminology is a tool for opening an effect, never a label to be collected. The grid moves from minimal identification at Level 1, through some comment, to sustained analysis at Level 4 and cohesive evaluation at Level 5. What a top-level answer does with this idea: every paragraph names one method and follows it through to the meaning it makes for the question asked.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"},{"h":"The question, the marks and the clean copy on the desk","t":"Section A is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text; in the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks these are Questions 1 to 10, carrying 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, and AO4 is not assessed in this section. The component is an open book examination: learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition into the examination room and it must be completely unmarked, which for this play means the Methuen Learner Edition of July 1998 or the Bloomsbury Modern Classics edition of October 1975. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and is 40% of the International GCSE, so roughly 45 minutes belongs to this essay, although the specification sets no per-question time. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it reads the noun in the question (what is to be presented), plans five or six paragraphs spread across the play before writing, and uses the clean copy for precision rather than for padding.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"},{"h":"The coursework route: the same two objectives, 650 to 800 words","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same modern drama list. Assignment A is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2, and the assignment must allow learners to access both objectives. Assignments may be self-generated or devised by the teacher and take formats such as exploring the ways in which a dramatist presents a group of characters, or asking in what ways a dramatist presents a theme, each closing with the instruction to consider language, form and structure. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, marking is on the same five-level grid on a best-fit basis, and the work is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. What a top-level answer does with this idea: it uses the absence of a clock to raise the precision of its evidence and to build a genuine evaluative thread, because the grid, not the time, is what separates the levels.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a 30-mark Section A question before you write","setup":"Section A offers you two questions on the play and you choose this one: 'Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents Elesin Oba in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure.' It is worth 30 marks, split 15 for AO1 and 15 for AO2, you have about 45 minutes, and your clean, unmarked copy of the play is on the desk. Work out exactly what the question is asking for before a word of the essay is written.","steps":["Split the stem into its two jobs: 'the ways in which' asks for Soyinka's methods and pays the AO2 half, while 'presents Elesin Oba' asks for close knowledge of a made character and pays the AO1 half.","Read the instruction sentence as a mark allocation rather than as advice: it is where the analysis marks live, so a paragraph without a named method is a paragraph earning on one objective only.","Turn the character into a single arguable claim: Elesin's appetite for living is the same faculty as his fitness for the ritual, so Soyinka makes his magnificence and his failure inseparable.","Fix the spread before writing: one paragraph from each of Scenes 1, 3, 4 and 5, with the method noted beside each — praise-poetry and the Not-I bird parable, the trance dance and the drums, his silence under his son's rebuke, the chains in the cell.","Decide the evaluative thread the conclusion will settle: whether the play presents him as betrayed, as self-betraying, or as both at once."],"cues":["Which words in the stem name the writer's methods, and which name the subject?","What is the second sentence of the question really telling you?","What one claim about this character could hold five paragraphs together?","How will you stop the essay living in a single scene?","What will the last paragraph have to weigh?"]},{"title":"Turning a staged moment into analysis: the egungun costume in Scene 2","setup":"Scene 2 opens on the Pilkingses' bungalow. A tango is playing on the gramophone and Simon and Jane are dancing in full egungun ancestral masquerade costume, confiscated by the police, which they mean to wear to a fancy-dress ball at the Residency. Sergeant Amusa arrives to report that a chief intends to die that night, sees the costume, and will not speak while they are wearing it; he asks to write his report instead. Turn this moment into one paragraph of a 30-mark answer on how Soyinka presents the colonial officials.","steps":["Name the dramatic method before the content: the costume is a stage property, and Soyinka lets the audience see the desecration before any character on stage puts a word to it.","Read the juxtaposition built into the scene: European ballroom music runs underneath an ancestral costume worn as fancy dress, so the sacred is literally set to a dance rhythm.","Analyse Amusa's refusal as characterisation through form: he moves from speech to writing, and the officials are given a witness whose silence carries more judgement than their own talk.","Draw the meaning out for the question: they are presented not as cruel but as unable to recognise meaning, which is why the arrest they order can destroy a rite they take for a suicide.","Close on a mini-judgement: the comedy is doing serious work, because the laugh is at the expense of people who will shortly hold a man's death in their hands."],"cues":["What does the audience see before anybody speaks?","What is playing while the costume is worn, and why does the pairing matter?","What does Amusa do instead of speaking, and what does that reveal?","What does the moment show about how these officials understand the ritual?","The moment is comic — what should your mini-judgement say about that comedy?"]},{"title":"Choosing evidence when the book is open: Scene 3","setup":"You are writing the Section A essay on how Soyinka presents duty. Your clean, unmarked copy lies open at Scene 3, where the market girls have just driven Sergeant Amusa and his constables out by mimicking the speech of English officers, and where Elesin then comes out of the bridal chamber and begins the dance that carries him towards trance. You have roughly eight minutes for this paragraph. Decide what to use from the scene and how to use it.","steps":["Refuse the temptation of length: copying out the girls' mimicry would fill lines and earn little, since the grid rewards discriminating use of relevant examples rather than quantity.","Pick the two short references that carry the point — the girls taking the constables' batons from them, and the moment Elesin's answers to the Praise-Singer begin to sound as if they come from further away.","Name the method behind each: parodic mimicry turns colonial authority against itself, while the thinning of dialogue into drumming stages the passage into trance.","Tie both to the word in the question: the women defend the rite by borrowing the coloniser's voice, and Elesin performs it by surrendering his own.","Judge from the pairing: duty is made communal before it is made fatal, so the play measures the later failure against a whole market that did its part."],"cues":["Why would a long copied extract lose you marks even in an open book exam?","Which two brief moments in this scene carry the idea most economically?","What technique is at work in each of them?","How does each moment answer the actual word in the question?","What can the two together let you conclude?"]},{"title":"What lifts a paragraph from Level 3 to Level 5","setup":"Two students answer 'In what ways does Soyinka present Olunde in Death and the King's Horseman? You must consider language, form and structure.' Student A writes: Olunde has come back from England to bury his father. He talks to Jane Pilkings at the ball about a ship's captain who blew himself up. This shows he respects sacrifice and thinks the British are hypocrites. Student B writes on the same conversation and is told her paragraph reaches the top level. Work out what Student B must be doing that Student A is not.","steps":["Diagnose Student A against the grid: two sentences of plot and one of assertion, with no method named, which is Level 2 (7 to 12 marks), the band whose wording is a largely narrative answer with only some comment on language, form and structure.","Find the method Student A walked past: Soyinka stages the exchange as a duel of politeness in flat, naturalistic prose, so Olunde's courtesy is the weapon and the calm is the pressure.","Add the structural observation: the debate is placed inside a European ball, so an English ceremony proceeds in front of us while an African one is being stopped out of sight.","Add the informed personal engagement AO1 wants: the argument is uncomfortable because Olunde never asks the British to approve of his father's death, only to notice that they honour their own version of it.","Judge, and make the paragraph cohesive: Olunde is presented as the clearest mind in the play, and setting his best speech at a party is the play's sharpest irony."],"cues":["Which level does Student A's paragraph fit, and which phrase in the grid says so?","What technique is Soyinka using in the exchange that Student A never mentions?","What does the setting of the conversation add to the point?","Where is there room for genuine personal engagement in this material?","What overall claim could close the paragraph?"]},{"title":"Planning the last two paragraphs so the essay ends on judgement","setup":"You are 30 minutes into the 45 you have for 'Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents responsibility for Elesin's failure. You must consider language, form and structure.' You have already written on the Not-I bird parable in Scene 1, on Iyaloja's warning when Elesin takes the Bride, and on the dance towards trance at the end of Scene 3 (the arrest itself is never staged: it happens offstage, between Scenes 3 and 4). Scenes 4 and 5 are unused, and you have two paragraphs and a conclusion left.","steps":["Give Scene 4 to the case against Elesin himself: his son's public refusal to know him lets the judgement come from inside the culture rather than from the coloniser, which is far harder to dismiss.","Give Scene 5 to the case that divides the blame: in the cell Elesin first accuses Pilkings, then acknowledges a weakness of his own that was there before any officer touched him.","Name the structural method that ties them: the son's body is carried on so the cost is shown rather than argued, and the chains meant to preserve Elesin become the instrument of his death.","Set the evaluative thread the conclusion will carry: the play refuses a single culprit and puts its final weight on what survives rather than on who is guilty.","Write the ending as an answer rather than a summary: state the reading you hold, concede what the rival reading keeps, and close on Iyaloja turning the living towards the unborn."],"cues":["Which scene lets a Yoruba voice pass judgement on Elesin?","What does Elesin concede in the cell that none of his earlier speeches did?","What does the staging do that an argument could not?","What has to be weighed for the judgement to be genuine rather than announced?","How should the final sentence differ from a summary of the play?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How are the 30 marks for the Section A essay divided, and which assessment objectives are not assessed there?","a":"15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2; AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in Section A of Component 2."},{"q":"What may a learner take into the Component 2 examination, and what does that change about your evidence?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition — the component is open book — so references are expected to be exact, short and chosen rather than approximate or copied at length."},{"q":"Why does naming a technique score little on its own?","a":"The AO2 strand of the grid rises from minimal identification at Level 1 through sustained analysis at Level 4 to cohesive evaluation at Level 5, so the marks are in tracing how the method makes meaning for the question, not in the label."},{"q":"What exactly makes this room's Level 3 model paragraph weaker than its Level 5 one?","a":"The Level 3 paragraph retells the moment, names one method and comments on its effect in general terms, then asserts an irony in a single clause; the Level 5 paragraph names a precise method, follows its effect across the play and ends on a judgement that answers the question."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2 is a written examination of 1 hour 30 minutes, 60 raw marks and 40% of the International GCSE; Section A is one 30-mark essay question on the modern drama text, chosen from two.","Section A (Questions 1 to 10) carries 15 raw marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2; AO3 and AO4 are not assessed in it.","Component 2 is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken into the examination room.","The only assessment grids the specification prints are those for the non-examined component, five levels over 30 marks: Level 1 is 1 to 6, Level 2 is 7 to 12, Level 3 is 13 to 18, Level 4 is 19 to 24 and Level 5 is 25 to 30, awarded on a best-fit basis.","The AO2 strand of that grid runs from minimal identification of language, form and structure, through some comment and sound understanding, to sustained analysis at Level 4 and cohesive evaluation at Level 5.","Death and the King's Horseman is in five scenes: the market, the Pilkingses' bungalow, the market again, the Residency ball, and the prison cell.","Component 3 Assignment A is the coursework alternative to Section A: one teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the drama text, 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2, with an advisory word count of 650 to 800 words.","The prescribed editions listed for this text are the Methuen Learner Edition of July 1998 (ISBN 9780413695505) and the Bloomsbury Modern Classics edition of October 1975 (ISBN 9780413333605).","The play does not end in silence: after Iyaloja's closing instruction the market women's dirge rises in volume and they sway on until the lights fade."],"questions":[{"q":"Practice recall drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the three checkpoints. Write from memory how the 30 marks of Section A of Component 2 are divided between assessment objectives, how Assignment A of Component 3 divides its own 30, and one assessment objective that carries no marks in either.","a":"Three checkpoints: 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2 in Section A; the same 15 and 15 in Assignment A; AO4 (or AO3) carries nothing in either.","sol":"Section A of Component 2 is one 30-mark essay from a choice of two, and in the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks Questions 1 to 10 carry 15 marks for AO1 and 15 for AO2. Component 3 Assignment A, the coursework alternative on the same modern drama text, is marked out of 30 with 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2 in exactly the same way, and the specification requires that the teacher-devised assignment allow learners to access both objectives. Neither AO3 (links and connections between texts) nor AO4 (texts and their contexts) is assessed on the modern drama text, which is why comparison with another text and paragraphs of historical background cannot earn marks here.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks splits Questions 1 to 10 between two objectives only.","Look for the sentence in the Component 3 section that repeats the same split for the drama assignment.","Your answer needs two mark figures for each route and the name of an objective that gets none on this text."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Practice recall drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the two checkpoints. Write from memory what Elesin Oba is required to do on the night the play takes place, and why his community treats it as a duty rather than a punishment.","a":"He must die by an act of will at the close of the dead Alafin's funeral rites so that the king does not travel to the ancestors alone; the community treats it as the office he has enjoyed all his life finally being paid for, and as the act that keeps the dead, the living and the unborn in one continuous world.","sol":"As the King's Horseman, Elesin holds an office of honour that carries one obligation: when the Alafin dies, the horseman must follow him, dying willingly at the end of the funeral rites so that the king is accompanied on his passage. The play makes clear that this is not an execution and not a suicide in the sense Pilkings understands. It is a willed passage, achieved through the dance and the drumming, and its purpose is to keep whole the continuum of the dead, the living and the unborn on which the community believes its own survival rests. That is why the market women celebrate Elesin rather than mourn him in the opening scene, why the girls fight off the police to protect the rite, and why the failure to complete it is felt as a catastrophe for everyone rather than as a private escape for one man.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The whole play turns on an office, not on a punishment: ask what the title of the play names.","Think about what the market women do in the opening scene and what that tells you about how the act is regarded.","Your answer needs the act itself and the reason the community depends on it."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Practice recall drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the six checkpoints, the five settings and the effect. Write from memory the setting of each of the five scenes in order, and say what the movement between those worlds achieves.","a":"Market; the Pilkingses' bungalow; the market again; the Residency ball; the prison cell. The first four alternate the Yoruba public world with the colonial European one, and the fifth breaks the pattern by bringing the market into a colonial cell.","sol":"Scene 1 is the market at the close of day; Scene 2 is the Pilkingses' bungalow, where they are dressing for the ball; Scene 3 returns to the market for the confrontation with Amusa and for Elesin's dance; Scene 4 is the Residency ball; Scene 5 is the cell in which Elesin is held. Across the first four scenes Soyinka alternates the worlds: Scenes 1 and 3 are Yoruba, public and communal, while Scene 2 is the colonial household and Scene 4 the Residency ball, both European though one is private and one ceremonial. The audience is therefore moved repeatedly between two worlds that cannot hear each other. The effect is cumulative: each switch makes the audience the only party who knows both halves of the situation, so the interruption of the rite is felt as inevitable long before it happens, and the final scene breaks the alternation rather than continuing it: Scene 5 is a colonial cell, but it is the market that enters it, so the two worlds end in one room instead of in separate ones.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Count the scenes first and ask which world each belongs to.","Look for the pattern in the numbering rather than in the events.","Your answer needs the five places in order and one sentence on the effect of switching between them."],"codes":["Component 2: Content overview","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Practice recall drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the three checkpoints. Write from memory what a learner may take into the Component 2 examination, and say what the open book condition changes about the evidence an examiner expects.","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text. Because the text is to hand, references are expected to be exact, short and deliberately chosen, and long copied extracts earn nothing.","sol":"The specification states that Component 2 is an open book examination and that learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room, and that the copy must be completely unmarked, so no annotation, highlighting or inserted notes are allowed. For this text the prescribed editions are the Methuen Learner Edition of July 1998 and the Bloomsbury Modern Classics edition of October 1975. The consequence for writing is that inexact paraphrase looks careless rather than forgivable, since the words were available; and equally that copying out long passages wastes minutes and adds nothing to either objective, because the AO1 grid rewards discriminating use of relevant examples and the AO2 grid rewards analysis of the words chosen, not the number reproduced. The book is there to make a short reference precise, not to fill the page.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The assessment overview says one specific thing about the condition of the copy.","Think about what an examiner can reasonably expect once the words are on the desk in front of the candidate.","Your answer needs the rule itself and its consequence for how evidence is used."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Practice recall drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the four checkpoints, two functions with one effect beside each. Write from memory the two functions the Praise-Singer performs in the play and one effect of each.","a":"He is Elesin's companion and praise-poet, whose chanted praise lifts the market scenes above ordinary speech and makes the audience share the community's admiration; and during the dance he speaks with the voice of the dead Alafin, so the audience hears the pull of the other world at the moment Elesin passes into trance.","sol":"First, the Praise-Singer is Elesin's constant companion and flatterer. His chanted praise, built on repetition, epithet and proverb, is one of the main ways Soyinka raises the register of the Yoruba scenes above naturalistic dialogue, and its effect is that the audience is invited to enjoy Elesin's greatness before it is asked to judge his failure. He is also a needling conscience, testing Elesin's resolve under the praise. Second, during the dance in Scene 3 he speaks as the voice of the dead king, calling to Elesin from the other side. The effect of that shift is theatrical rather than informational: a living character on stage becomes the medium for a dead one, so the audience experiences the passage between worlds as something happening in the room rather than as a belief being described. When Elesin's replies grow faint against that voice, we hear the trance rather than being told about it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One function belongs to the market scenes and one to the dance.","Ask what his speech does to the sound of the play, and then what he becomes during the trance.","Your answer needs two distinct roles and a stated effect beside each."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents Elesin Oba in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Praise-poetry, proverb and the Not-I bird parable build a man whose appetite for living is the same faculty as his fitness for the rite; the taking of the Bride, the thinning of his speech into drumming during the dance, his silence under Olunde's rebuke and his confession and self-strangling in the cell chart a fall the play refuses to blame on one cause.","sol":"Soyinka presents Elesin Oba as a man whose greatness and whose failure are made of the same material, and he does it by giving Elesin the richest language in the play before taking it away from him. In the opening market scene Elesin is not described but performed: the Praise-Singer's chant and Elesin's own parable of the Not-I bird let him cast the whole world as fugitives from death and himself as its only welcomer; the refrain 'Not I' passes from one frightened figure to the next until the bird is named after their flight, so Elesin's courage is measured against a whole community's panic. The method is an embedded narrative set inside praise-poetry, and its effect is that the audience enjoys him before it is ever asked to judge him. Soyinka then complicates that enjoyment inside the same scene. Elesin's demand for the young bride is couched in the same imagery of appetite and harvest that has run through his speech from his entrance, so the audience cannot separate the hunger that makes him magnificent from the hunger that will keep him in the world; and Iyaloja's consent, given with a warning, plants the terms on which the community will later hold him to account.\nIn Scene 3 the presentation shifts from language to form. As Elesin dances, his replies to the Praise-Singer thin out and the drumming takes the stage from the dialogue. Soyinka dramatises the passage into trance as a withdrawal of speech, so what the audience loses is precisely the faculty by which Elesin has been built; and by keeping the arrest offstage, between Scenes 3 and 4, so that we next meet him in handcuffs at a European ball, the play turns us into witnesses of consequence rather than of conflict.\nThe last two scenes present him twice over. Under Olunde's refusal in Scene 4, where the son denies him as a father and names him 'eater of left-overs', he is silent, and Soyinka lets the judgement come from inside his own culture, which is harder to shrug off than any colonial accusation would be. In the cell the rhetoric returns, first as blame directed at Pilkings and then as confession: he acknowledges a weakness that was his own before any officer touched him, spoken in the cell with his silent Bride close by. The chains intended to keep him alive become the instrument of his death, a stage image in which the coloniser's intervention and Elesin's own will collapse into a single act.\nSoyinka therefore presents Elesin neither as victim nor as fraud but as a man made for a duty that his own vitality disqualifies him from. The presentation is itself an argument: by making us love the speech of Scene 1 and watch it fail in Scene 5, the play convicts its audience of the same appetite for living that it convicts him of.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question names a character, so the essay is about how he is made, not about what he does.","Track him scene by scene and ask which method dominates in each: the praise-poetry, the dance, the silence, the chains.","A top-level answer holds one claim about him across the whole play and tests it against the ending."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Soyinka present duty in Death and the King's Horseman? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Duty is presented as a willed performance rather than an obeyed rule: praise-poetry and proverb make it communal property, the dance makes it bodily, the colonial scenes show a rival and self-serving idea of duty, and the transfer of the obligation to Olunde shows that it cannot simply be cancelled.","sol":"Duty in this play is not a rule to be obeyed but a performance that must be willed, and Soyinka builds that distinction into the language, the form and the structure. The market scene presents duty as something Elesin relishes: the parable of the Not-I bird lets him mock everyone who runs from death, each figure crying 'Not I' and taking to their heels in turn, and the repetition builds a rhythm the audience enjoys. The effect is to make eagerness and appetite look like the same faculty, which is the play's first trap. Iyaloja's speech makes the same duty communal property. Her proverb-based idiom speaks for the market rather than for herself, and when she consents to the marriage with a warning she establishes that the community, not the coloniser, holds the right to judge him.\nIn Scene 3 the form carries the idea. The girls' parodic mimicry of English officers drives Amusa out, so duty is shown as collective labour before it is shown as a single man's death; then the dialogue thins into drumming as Elesin dances, and the audience watches an obligation become a bodily act. Soyinka is careful that we see the whole market working before we see the one man fail.\nThe colonial scenes present a rival idea of duty and refuse to flatter it. Pilkings acts from an official sense of order in a flat, clipped prose that cannot rise to the occasion, and it is Jane Pilkings who volunteers, at the ball and as a piece of wartime excitement, the story of a ship's captain who blew up his own vessel to save the people on the coast, so that when Olunde accepts her story and re-describes that death as self-sacrifice the double standard is exposed out of her own mouth: the British honour a willed death when it is their own and call his father's barbaric. The contrast is made structurally as well as verbally, since Soyinka sets that argument inside a party while the rite is being stopped out of sight.\nThe final scene shows that duty cannot be cancelled, only moved. Elesin's confession in the cell acknowledges a weakness of his own that preceded the arrest, and the arrival of Olunde's body makes the transfer of the obligation visible rather than argued. Elesin's self-strangling with the chains is a duty discharged too late and in the wrong currency.\nThe play therefore presents duty as inheritance rather than as instruction. Its final word is not about guilt but about continuity: Iyaloja's last instruction to the living, in the closing lines of Scene 5, is to 'Turn your mind only to the unborn', which is Soyinka's way of saying that the obligation outlives the man who failed it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The word covers more than one duty in the play: at least four characters have one.","Ask what makes this obligation different from an order that could simply be obeyed, and which scene shows that difference most clearly.","A top-level answer keeps a single thread about the nature of duty running through every paragraph and settles it at the end."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents the women of the market in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"The women function chorically and morally: they dress and praise Elesin, Iyaloja speaks for the community in proverb and holds him to his word, the girls defeat colonial authority by mimicry rather than force, the Bride is a silent presence throughout, and Iyaloja delivers the play's judgement and its closing turn towards the unborn.","sol":"Soyinka presents the market women as the play's moral centre and as the voice of its community, and he does it by giving them a range of theatrical functions that no other group holds. Soyinka's opening stage direction places the action in 'a market in its closing stages', so the women are already at their own work when the rite arrives among them. In Scene 1 they act chorically: they greet Elesin, wrap him in rich cloth and answer his praise, and the exchange is written in a heightened idiom of proverb, imagery and call and response that makes the market a ceremonial space rather than a place of trade. The effect is that the rite is established as the community's business before it is shown as one man's ordeal.\nIyaloja is individuated within that chorus. Her authority is verbal: she speaks in proverb and in figures of planting, eating and seed, and Soyinka gives her the play's most controlled language at exactly the moments when Elesin's is most expansive. When she grants him the girl betrothed to her own son and warns him that nothing of his last appetite must be left to poison the living, she is not indulging him but setting the terms of the judgement she will later deliver. The structure makes the point: her warning in the first scene and her reproach in the last frame the entire action.\nThe girls in Scene 3 present the women in a different register altogether. They defeat Amusa and his constables not by force but by mimicking the clipped speech of English officers, taking the men's batons from them. The comedy is politically precise: colonial authority is shown to rest on a manner that can be copied, and therefore mocked. Soyinka gives the women the only complete victory in the play, and he places it immediately before Elesin's failure so that the contrast is unavoidable.\nThe Bride is the fourth kind of presence. She never speaks, yet she is present at Elesin's last marriage, beside the cell in the final scene and at his death, and her silence gathers meaning simply by lasting; the final gesture of closing Elesin's eyes gives an unspeaking character the last act of the ritual.\nSoyinka therefore presents the women as the play's continuity: they open the rite, defend it, judge its failure and carry it forward. Iyaloja's closing instruction over the two bodies, 'Turn your mind only to the unborn', is not consolation but direction, and it is significant that Soyinka gives that final authority to a market woman rather than to any of the play's men.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The women are not one character: sort them into the chorus, Iyaloja, the girls and the Bride before planning.","Ask what each group does that no one else in the play can do, and what method Soyinka uses for each.","A top-level answer explains why the play gives the last word to this group."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"In what ways does Soyinka present Simon and Jane Pilkings in Death and the King's Horseman? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"They are presented through naturalistic prose and domestic comedy rather than villainy: the tango and the confiscated egungun costume make their blindness visible, Amusa's refusal to speak judges them without their noticing, Jane is given enough openness to make Olunde's argument land, and the arrest destroys a rite they never understood was taking place.","sol":"Soyinka presents the Pilkingses not as villains but as people incapable of recognising meaning, and the method is register. Where the Yoruba scenes are built from praise-poetry, proverb and image, the colonial scenes are written in flat, clipped naturalistic prose full of domestic irritation: the effect of the contrast is that the colonial world sounds smaller than the world it is interrupting, which is a structural judgement made before any character delivers one.\nThe first sight of them is the most damaging. They dance a tango on the verandah in confiscated egungun costume, so the audience sees an ancestral masquerade worn as fancy dress with European ballroom music running underneath it. Soyinka lets the stage picture make the accusation before a word is spoken. Amusa then completes it by refusing to speak while they wear the costume and asking to write his report instead, and the officials treat his horror as insubordination; a witness whose silence carries more judgement than their own talk is a precise piece of characterisation through form.\nJane is drawn with more sympathy than her husband, and that is a deliberate structural choice rather than a softening. Because she is courteous, curious and capable of being unsettled, her conversation with Olunde at the ball can become a real argument rather than a lecture, and his point about the ship's captain lands on someone who is genuinely shaken by it; when he tells her she has 'no respect' for what she does not understand, the charge is made to a listener capable of feeling it, which is what makes it damaging. Simon, by contrast, is presented through a habit of brisk practicality: he helped Olunde get to England, which the play allows him as a real kindness, and he orders the arrest from the same instinct, which the play shows as a catastrophe. Soyinka's point is that the kindness and the catastrophe come from the same source, a confidence that he already knows what he is looking at.\nThe final scene measures them. Pilkings speaks to Elesin in the tone of a man who believes he has saved a life; Iyaloja addresses him as someone who has destroyed a world and cannot be made to see it. The play gives him no answer to her, and that silence is the presentation's last stroke.\nSoyinka therefore presents the Pilkingses as a comic register with tragic consequences, and the comedy is not relief but argument: the laugh is at the expense of people who are shortly to hold a man's death in their hands.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why Soyinka writes their scenes in a different register from the market scenes, and what that difference alone implies.","Consider what the audience sees on stage in Scene 2 before anyone speaks, and what Amusa does rather than says.","A top-level answer explains why the play declines to make them villains and what it gains by that."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Soyinka uses sound and silence in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Drumming and the dirge run under the action as the play's clock and pressure; the gramophone tango is set against them; the Praise-Singer's chant becomes the dead king's voice; dialogue thins into drumming during the dance; and the silences of Amusa, the Bride and Elesin under his son's rebuke carry judgement the dialogue cannot; the play ends not in silence but with the dirge rising as the lights fade.","sol":"Sound is the most continuous of Soyinka's methods in this play, and silence is the most pointed. The drumming and the dirge begin early and persist under the action, and because they are heard offstage they function as a clock the audience cannot stop: every scene in the colonial bungalow is played over a rhythm that is counting down towards a death the Pilkingses cannot interpret. The Pilkingses can hear the drums and cannot read them, and have to ask a servant what they signify; that single dramatic fact is the play's colonial situation in miniature.\nSoyinka sets one kind of sound against another. The tango on the gramophone in Scene 2 is mechanical, imported and decorative, and it plays while an ancestral costume is worn as fancy dress; the drums are communal, live and functional. The audience does not need the contrast explained, because it hears it. At the Residency ball the same technique returns on a larger scale: an orchestrated European ceremony proceeds while an African one is being stopped elsewhere.\nThe most sophisticated use of sound is the passage into trance in Scene 3. The Praise-Singer's chant shifts until he is speaking with the voice of the dead Alafin, so a living body becomes the medium for a dead one, and Elesin's replies grow shorter and seem to arrive from further off as the drumming takes the stage from the dialogue. Soyinka stages a metaphysical passage as an acoustic one: we do not watch a man decide to die, we hear language being handed over to rhythm.\nSilence is used with equal care. Amusa's refusal to speak while the egungun costume is worn turns a policeman into a judge. The Bride never speaks at all, yet she is present at the marriage, outside the cell and at the end, and her closing gesture gives the last action of the rite to the play's most silent figure. Elesin, the most eloquent character in the play, is given nothing to say when Olunde refuses to acknowledge him at the ball and names him 'eater of left-overs', and that absence of rhetoric is the most devastating thing that happens to him.\nSoyinka therefore uses sound to make the audience feel the rite from inside and silence to deliver its judgements. The pattern is cumulative and structural, and the ending is the proof of it. The play opens in 'a market in its closing stages' with drumming and praise; it closes with Iyaloja speaking plainly over two bodies, 'Turn your mind only to the unborn', and then Soyinka's final stage direction gives the theatre back to sound, for the women's dirge rises in volume and they sway on until the lights fade. The play does not end in silence. It ends with the one sound the colonial world was never able to interpret still filling the stage, which is why the last effect is continuity rather than defeat.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Sound in a play is a structural device, not decoration: ask what the drums do to every scene they are heard under.","Set one kind of sound against another, then look for the characters who do not speak.","A top-level answer shows the pattern developing across the whole play rather than listing separate effects."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Timed practice paragraph, not a whole essay: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. The real question would be the 30-mark essay 'In what ways does Soyinka present Olunde in Death and the King's Horseman? You must consider language, form and structure.' Give yourself 12 minutes and write one paragraph only, on his conversation with Jane Pilkings at the ball. Score one point for each of the four checkpoints: a stated position, one short exact reference, one named method traced through to its effect, and a mini-judgement.","a":"Position: Olunde is the play's clearest mind. Reference: Jane Pilkings's own story of the ship's captain, which Olunde re-describes as self-sacrifice, and his rebuke that she has 'no respect' for what she does not understand. Method: courteous, flat naturalistic prose used as pressure, and the placing of the debate inside a European party. Judgement: the argument is made to land on the audience, not on Jane.","sol":"Soyinka presents Olunde as the one character who can argue with the colonial world in its own register and win. At the Residency ball it is Jane Pilkings who supplies the example, offering him as wartime excitement the story of a ship's captain who blew up his own vessel to save the people on the coast; Olunde asks only whether it was enemy action, and on hearing that it was not, hands her story back to her as self-sacrifice. He answers her not with anger but with her own evidence, and the method is deliberate: Soyinka gives Olunde the flat, courteous prose of the colonial scenes rather than the praise-poetry of the market, so that the case for his father's death is made in the very idiom that is used to call it barbaric. The courtesy is the weapon, and it tightens into the plain rebuke that Jane has 'no respect' for what she does not understand. Form sharpens it further: the exchange is placed inside a European ceremony, with an orchestra and a visiting Prince, while the African ceremony is being stopped out of sight, so the audience hears the double standard being performed at the same time as it is being described. My judgement is that Soyinka does not write this paragraph of the play to convince Jane, who is shaken but unchanged; he writes it so that the audience cannot afterwards use the word barbaric without hearing Olunde's example underneath it.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The scene is an argument, so ask what kind of speech each of them uses rather than what each of them believes.","Look for the method in how Olunde speaks and in where Soyinka has placed the conversation, then decide which of the two carries more of your point.","A paragraph at this level ends on a judgement about the effect on the audience, not on a summary of who won the argument."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Timed practice opening, not a whole essay: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Give yourself 10 minutes and write only the introduction and the paragraph plan for 'Explore the ways in which Soyinka uses sound and silence in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure.' Score one point for each of the three checkpoints: a position taken in the first two sentences, five paragraph headings each carrying its scene and its method, and one sentence naming the thread you will evaluate.","a":"A position such as: sound carries the rite and silence carries the judgements. Five headings with scene and method: Scene 1 praise-poetry and drumming; Scene 2 the gramophone tango against the offstage drums; Scene 3 dialogue thinning into drumming as the Praise-Singer becomes the dead king's voice; Scene 4 the ball's orchestra and Elesin's silence under his son; Scene 5 the dirge, Iyaloja's plain speech and the rising dirge at the close. Thread: whether sound or silence does the play's heavier work.","sol":"Introduction: Sound in this play is not atmosphere but action, and silence is not absence but verdict. I shall argue that Soyinka uses continuous offstage sound to make the audience feel the rite from the inside, and uses the moments when a character cannot or will not speak to deliver the judgements the dialogue is too partial to make.\nParagraph 1 - Scene 1, language: the Praise-Singer's chant and the 'Not I' refrain of Elesin's parable raise the market above ordinary speech; method, praise-poetry and repetition; effect, the audience enjoys him before it judges him.\nParagraph 2 - Scene 2, contrast of sound: the imported tango on the gramophone plays over a confiscated ancestral costume while the drums continue offstage; method, sound set against sound; effect, the colonial world is heard to be the smaller one.\nParagraph 3 - Scene 3, form: dialogue thins into drumming and the Praise-Singer speaks with the voice of the dead Alafin; method, the handing of the stage from speech to rhythm; effect, the passage into trance is staged acoustically rather than described.\nParagraph 4 - Scenes 2 and 4, silence: Amusa's refusal in Scene 2 to speak while the egungun costume is worn, and Elesin's silence in Scene 4 when Olunde names him 'eater of left-overs'; method, withheld speech; effect, judgement without accusation.\nParagraph 5 - Scene 5, the ending: the dirge under the cell scene, Iyaloja's plain instruction, and the final stage direction in which the dirge rises as the women sway and the lights fade; method, structural return; effect, the play ends in the community's sound rather than in silence.\nEvaluation thread: does the play's meaning finally rest on what we hear or on what we are refused? Return to that question at the end of every paragraph so the conclusion is earned.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Before planning anything, list the sounds the audience actually hears, scene by scene, and mark which of them the characters on stage can interpret.","A plan for this question needs one method per paragraph, so decide for each scene whether your point is about a sound or about a silence.","The thread you evaluate should be a real question with two answers, not a restatement of the title."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"'Elesin's failure is his own, and the colonial officers merely give him an excuse.' Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents responsibility for that failure, and say how far you agree. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Strong case for personal responsibility (the appetite running through his language, Iyaloja's warning, his own confession in the cell, the judgement delivered by his son rather than by the coloniser), set against the fact of the arrest and the play's structural refusal to award blame to one side; the best answers conclude that Soyinka makes the two causes inseparable and shifts the play's weight from guilt to continuity.","sol":"The proposition is strong but not complete, and Soyinka's methods are what decide the case. The evidence for Elesin's own responsibility is planted in his language long before the police appear. From his entrance his speech runs on images of appetite, harvest and enjoyment, and the parable of the Not-I bird is self-praise that the rest of the play is designed to test; a man who performs his fearlessness that elaborately is already a man with something to prove. The demand for the young bride is the same appetite in a different form, and Iyaloja's consent with its warning gives the audience the community's own terms of judgement in advance. Soyinka is careful to make these terms Yoruba, not colonial.\nThe structure then reinforces the proposition by choosing who delivers the verdict. It is Olunde, not Pilkings, who refuses to acknowledge his father and names him 'eater of left-overs', and it is Iyaloja, not the Resident, who reproaches him in the cell. Most decisively, Elesin himself, in the cell, acknowledges a weakness of his own rather than laying the whole failure on the coloniser. Soyinka gives that admission to the most eloquent character in the play at the moment he has least to gain from it, which is why it carries weight.\nAgainst the proposition stands the plain fact of the interruption. Elesin was deep in trance and dancing at the end of Scene 3 when the arrest was made, and the officers who made it did not know what they were stopping. Soyinka keeps the arrest itself offstage, between Scenes 3 and 4, so what the audience sees is not a struggle but its result: Elesin brought into the ball in handcuffs in the closing moments of Scene 4, and then the chains of the cell in Scene 5. Those irons are the visible sign of a death that did not happen when it should have. Yet Soyinka's own Author's Note warns against reading the play as a clash of cultures and calls the Colonial Factor 'a catalytic incident merely', and the play dramatises the officers as blind rather than malign, which is a way of denying them the dignity of being the cause.\nMy judgement is that the word 'merely' is what fails. The colonial officers are not an excuse but a catalyst, and the play is precise about the difference: a catalyst makes visible what was already unstable. Elesin's will had faltered before Pilkings acted, but it took an intervention that understood nothing to convert a faltering into a catastrophe. Soyinka's ending confirms it by refusing to settle blame at all — the son's body arrives, the father dies by his own hands with the coloniser's iron, and Iyaloja turns the survivors away from the question of guilt and towards the unborn.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["A proposition question is won or lost on the word you decide to argue with: look hard at 'merely'.","Sort the evidence into what happens before the officers act and what happens after, then ask who the play chooses to deliver its judgements.","A top-level answer concedes the strength of the opposing reading before settling the question, and ends where the play ends rather than where the argument is most comfortable."],"codes":["Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Soyinka uses the structure of the play to create meanings and effects in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","a":"Five scenes with no act divisions whose first four alternate the Yoruba public world with the colonial European one before Scene 5 breaks the pattern; two contrasting registers; the withholding of the death and of the arrest; offstage drumming binding the scenes; the placing of the play's central argument inside a ball; the arrival of the son's body as the reversal; and a closing turn away from judgement towards the unborn.","sol":"The play's structure is its argument. Soyinka writes five scenes with no act divisions, and across the first four he alternates the worlds: market, bungalow, market, ball. Scenes 1 and 3 are Yoruba, public and communal; Scene 2 is the colonial household and Scene 4 the Residency ball, both European, though the first is private and the second ceremonial. The pattern is a rhythm rather than a rule, and Scene 5 is where it breaks: the cell is a colonial space, but it is the market that walks into it, so the play ends by putting both worlds in one room instead of moving between them. Because the audience alone crosses between the two, it holds knowledge neither side has, and the interruption of the rite becomes structurally inevitable long before it is dramatically actual. The two registers reinforce the pattern, the proverb-rich, image-laden speech of the market against the clipped naturalism of the bungalow, so the colonial world sounds smaller than the one it interrupts.\nThe offstage drumming is the structural glue. It begins in 'a market in its closing stages' and continues under the colonial scenes, so the audience carries the rite into rooms where nobody can interpret it, and the pressure of the play is maintained during scenes in which nothing ritual is happening.\nSoyinka's boldest structural choice is what he declines to show. The death the title announces is withheld until the final moments, and the arrest is not staged at all: it happens offstage between Scenes 3 and 4, and Elesin simply appears in handcuffs at the ball, so the audience meets consequence rather than conflict. That placing does two things at once. It puts the play's central debate, Olunde's argument with Jane Pilkings about which sacrifices a culture agrees to honour, inside a European party while the African ceremony is being destroyed elsewhere; and it makes the son's presence at the ball the hinge on which the last scene turns.\nThe reversal is achieved by a stage image rather than by speech. The women carry in Olunde's body, and the audience understands the transfer of the obligation before Iyaloja names it; Elesin then strangles himself with the chains intended to keep him alive, so the coloniser's instrument completes the act the coloniser prevented. Soyinka has arranged the whole play so that these two events need no explanation.\nFinally, the ending refuses the shape a tragedy of blame would require. Instead of judgement it delivers instruction: 'Turn your mind only to the unborn', and the dirge rises over the closing tableau rather than dying away. Structurally this converts a play about one man's failure into a play about a community's continuity, which is why the alternating design matters. The two worlds were never going to be reconciled; what the structure protects is the one that must go on.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Structure means the order and placing of things: begin by listing what each scene is and where it happens.","Ask what the audience knows that the characters do not, and what Soyinka chooses not to put on stage.","A top-level answer shows that the design produces the meaning, rather than describing the design and then commenting on meaning separately."],"codes":["Component 2: Content overview","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Practice analysis drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the three checkpoints. A student answering a Section A question on this play gives a third of the essay to British rule in Nigeria in the 1940s and to Soyinka's own life. Using the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks, work out why this cannot raise the mark, and what those paragraphs should have contained instead.","a":"Section A (Questions 1 to 10) is 15 marks AO1 and 15 marks AO2 only, so contextual material earns nothing of its own; the same paragraphs should have used close knowledge of the play as evidence for a position (AO1) and named and analysed Soyinka's language, form and structure (AO2).","sol":"In the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks, Questions 1 to 10 of Component 2 — the modern drama questions — carry 15 marks for AO1 and 15 marks for AO2, and nothing for AO3 or AO4. AO4, showing understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, is assessed in Section B on the literary heritage text and in Section C of Component 1, not here. A third of an essay spent on colonial administration and on the writer's biography is therefore a third of an essay earning no marks at all, and it also costs marks indirectly, because the time it consumes would otherwise have gone on evidence and analysis. The remedy is not to delete the knowledge but to convert it. Background understanding should make the reading of the play sharper, not become a section of the answer: the same minutes should have gone on close reference to the play in support of a stated position, which is AO1, and on naming and tracing Soyinka's methods — the two registers, the alternating scenes, the offstage drumming, the stage properties — through to the meanings they create, which is AO2. Context is allowed to inform the argument silently; it is not allowed to take its place.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find which questions in the Breakdown belong to the modern drama text and see how many objectives are listed against them.","Ask where in the qualification AO4 is actually assessed, and what follows for a section where it is not.","Your answer needs the mark split, the consequence for unrewarded material, and the two things the wasted paragraphs should have contained."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2"]},{"q":"Practice comparison drill, not an examination question: the mark figure beside it is a checkpoint count, not a board tariff, because the board sets only 30-mark essays on this text. Score one point for each of the three checkpoints. Take one paragraph you would write on the egungun costume in Scene 2 for the Section A question on this play. Work out how that paragraph would have to change if the same material were being written for a Section B literary heritage question, and what stays the same.","a":"It stays an AO1 and AO2 paragraph — position, close reference, named method, traced effect — but Section B splits 30 marks as 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, so a third strand must be added and integrated: the text read against the contexts in which it was written, woven into the analysis rather than bolted on.","sol":"For Section A the paragraph is built on two strands only. AO1 supplies the position and the close reference: the confiscated masquerade costume worn as fancy dress is the first thing the audience sees of the colonial household, and the point is that the officials do not know what they are wearing. AO2 supplies the method and its effect: the costume is a stage property, so the desecration is shown before it is stated; the tango running underneath it sets imported, mechanical music against a sacred object; and Amusa's refusal to speak while it is worn turns the moment into judgement without a word of accusation. The paragraph ends on a mini-judgement about what the scene establishes for the play.\nFor a Section B question on a literary heritage text, Questions 11 to 22 carry 10 marks AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, so all three strands must be present in every developed paragraph. The AO1 and AO2 work above stays exactly as it is; what is added is the relationship between text and context — the writer's own situation, the historical setting, the social, cultural and literary contexts and the way a text may be read differently now from when it was written — and the requirement is that this understanding is integrated convincingly rather than parked in a sentence of background. In practice the sentence about the property would carry its context inside it, explaining what an object of that kind meant to the community whose object it was, so that the analysis and the context are one movement of thought. Two further things change: each objective is now worth a third rather than a half, so no strand can be allowed to crowd out the others, and the question stem itself will carry the instruction to refer to context, which is the signal that the third strand is being paid for.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the mark splits for Questions 1 to 10 and for Questions 11 to 22 before thinking about the writing.","Work out which strand is added and what the top level of the grid demands of it.","Your answer needs what is unchanged, what is added, and how the addition has to be handled to score."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","Section A: Modern Drama — AO1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section A essay carry, and from a choice of how many questions?","a":"30 marks, from a choice of two."},{"q":"Which assessment objective is paid for by the instruction to consider language, form and structure?","a":"AO2."},{"q":"In which scene, and in what setting, does Elesin's dance towards trance take place?","a":"Scene 3, in the market."},{"q":"Who dies in Elesin's place, and when does the audience learn it?","a":"Olunde, his eldest son; the audience learns it in the final scene, when the women carry his body into the cell."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Elesin Oba","back":"The King's Horseman. After the Alafin's death he must die by will at the close of the funeral rites; the play traces his praise, his last marriage, his dance towards trance and his death in a colonial cell.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Iyaloja","back":"Mother of the market and leader of its women. She grants Elesin the girl betrothed to her own son, warns him against letting his last appetite poison the living, and speaks the play's judgement over his body.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The Praise-Singer","back":"Elesin's companion, flatterer and needling conscience. His chanted praise-poetry raises the register of the market scenes, and during the dance he speaks with the voice of the dead Alafin.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Olunde","back":"Elesin's eldest son, studying medicine in England. He returns to bury his father, argues about sacrifice with Jane Pilkings at the ball, refuses to acknowledge Elesin, and dies in his place.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Simon and Jane Pilkings","back":"The District Officer and his wife. They wear confiscated egungun costume to a fancy-dress ball and order Elesin's arrest; the play presents them as blind rather than cruel, in flat naturalistic prose.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Sergeant Amusa","back":"A Yoruba policeman in colonial service who still fears the egungun costume. He will not speak while the Pilkingses wear it and writes his report instead, and the market girls drive him off by mimicry.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The Not-I bird parable","back":"Elesin's set piece in Scene 1: one kind of person after another takes flight at the approach of death while he alone claims to have welcomed it. Self-praise the rest of the play is built to test.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The egungun costume as a stage property","back":"Ancestral masquerade dress, confiscated by the police and worn by the Pilkingses as fancy dress. The audience sees the desecration before any character names it, with a tango playing underneath.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The five-scene structure","back":"Market, colonial bungalow, market, Residency ball, prison cell. The first four alternate Yoruba public scenes with colonial European ones; the fifth breaks the pattern, since the market enters a colonial cell and both worlds end in one room.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"Soyinka's Author's Note","back":"It traces the play to an incident in Oyo in 1946, says he altered detail, sequence and characterisation and moved the action back into the war years, and rejects reading the play as a clash of cultures.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in Section A of Component 2","back":"Close knowledge and understanding of the play, a critical style and an informed personal engagement, with relevant examples identified and used: 15 of the 30 marks.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO2 in Section A of Component 2","back":"Analysis of the language, form and structure Soyinka uses to create meanings and effects: the other 15 marks, carried by the instruction to consider language, form and structure.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The open book rule for Component 2","back":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in and must be completely unmarked, so references should be exact, short and chosen rather than copied at length.","code":"Assessment overview: Section A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment A","back":"The coursework alternative to Section A: one teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the drama text, 15 marks AO1 and 15 AO2, advisory 650 to 800 words, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson.","code":"Component 3, Assignment A – Modern Drama","open":false},{"front":"Ways Soyinka stages Elesin's passage into trance","back":"The drumming takes the stage from the dialogue; the Praise-Singer begins to speak with the voice of the dead Alafin; Elesin's replies grow shorter and seem to come from further away.","code":"Section A: Modern Drama — AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: 'Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents Elesin Oba in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure.' (30 marks, 15 AO1 and 15 AO2)","Soyinka builds Elesin's authority out of language before he tests it with action: the parable of the Not-I bird in the opening market scene lets Elesin perform his own fearlessness as a piece of theatre, casting every other kind of person as a fugitive who takes to their heels at the rumour of death while he alone stands and offers welcome; the refrain 'Not I' is passed from one terrified figure to the next until the bird itself is named after their flight. The method is an embedded narrative set inside praise-poetry, and it matters that Soyinka gives it to Elesin rather than to the Praise-Singer, because the man is allowed to compose his own praise-poem; the rhythm of repetition, each figure fleeing in turn, makes the audience enjoy him before it is asked to judge him, and that enjoyment is precisely the trap. Structurally the parable is a promise, and the play is designed to break it: the same voice that boasted of welcoming death will, in the cell of Scene 5, acknowledge a weakness of his own that no officer put there, and the play's last word will belong not to him but to Iyaloja, whose closing instruction turns the living 'only to the unborn'; the audience is left measuring one speech against the others. What looks at first like characterisation is therefore also plotting, since Soyinka plants the evidence for Elesin's fall inside the speech that most delights us with him. The presentation is not of a hypocrite but of a man whose gift for language is indistinguishable from his appetite for living, so that the very quality which makes him fit to be the king's horseman is the quality that keeps him too long in the world.","Why this reaches Level 5: it names a precise method (embedded narrative inside praise-poetry) and a precise structural choice (who is given the speech), anchors both in short exact quotation rather than paraphrase, which is what the open book condition is for, traces the effect on the audience rather than asserting it, reaches across the play to the cell in Scene 5 so the evidence is discriminating, and closes on a judgement that answers the question about presentation instead of summarising the character. The strands are not visible as separate strands, which is what cohesive evaluation means."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: 'Explore the ways in which Soyinka presents Elesin Oba in Death and the King's Horseman. You must consider language, form and structure.' (30 marks, 15 AO1 and 15 AO2)","Soyinka presents Elesin as a confident and proud man in the opening market scene. He tells the story of the Not-I bird, in which one person after another says 'Not I' and runs away when death comes near, while Elesin says that he welcomed it instead. Soyinka uses repetition as each figure takes flight, and this builds up to Elesin's claim so that he sounds fearless in front of the market women. It also shows his authority, because the women listen to him and dress him in fine cloth. Later in the play Elesin fails to die, so what he says here turns out to be ironic. This suggests that Soyinka presents him as a man who is sure of himself at the start but cannot keep his promise.","Why this reaches only Level 3, and exactly what is missing: the knowledge is sound and the example is clearly relevant, the critical style is appropriate, one method (repetition) is named and there is even a short exact quotation, so it clears Levels 1 and 2. But the quotation is dropped in as illustration and its words are never analysed, the effect of the method is described in a general way ('he sounds fearless') and never followed anywhere; there is no comment on form — that the speech is a story told by the character about himself; the irony of the ending is asserted in half a sentence rather than developed into evidence; and the last sentence restates the first instead of judging. Level 4 would require the analysis to be sustained across the paragraph and Level 5 a judgement that earns itself from the evidence."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Death and the King's Horseman by Wole Soyinka","items":["Elesin Oba: the King's Horseman, whose office obliges him to die by will at the end of the dead Alafin's funeral rites so the king is not left to make the passage alone.","The Praise-Singer: Elesin's companion, flatterer and conscience, who chants his praises in the market and, during the dance, speaks with the voice of the dead king.","Iyaloja: the Mother of the market and leader of its women, who grants Elesin his last bride with a warning and pronounces judgement over him in the final scene.","Olunde: Elesin's eldest son, sent to England to study medicine with Pilkings's help and against his father's wishes, who returns to bury his father.","Simon Pilkings: the District Officer, who orders Elesin's arrest to prevent what he takes to be a suicide.","Jane Pilkings: his wife, whose conversation with Olunde at the ball is the play's clearest argument about sacrifice.","Sergeant Amusa: a Yoruba policeman in colonial service, a Muslim who nonetheless refuses to speak to his superiors while they are wearing the confiscated egungun costume.","Joseph: the Pilkingses' houseboy, a Christian convert, who tells them that a chief means to die that night.","The Bride: the young woman, already betrothed to Iyaloja's son, whom Elesin takes on his last night; she never speaks and closes his eyes at the end.","The Resident and the Aide-de-camp: the colonial officials at the Residency ball, held during a visit by a Prince.","Scene 1: Elesin arrives in the market at the close of day with drummers and the Praise-Singer, is welcomed and dressed in rich cloth by the women, and tells the parable of the Not-I bird.","Scene 1 continued: Elesin demands a young woman he has seen; Iyaloja, learning that she is betrothed to her own son, consents and warns him that nothing of this last appetite must be left to poison the living.","Scene 2: the Pilkingses dance a tango on the verandah in confiscated egungun costume, ready for a fancy-dress ball; Amusa reports the coming death, is horrified by the costume and writes his report instead of speaking; Pilkings orders the arrest.","Scene 3: Amusa and his constables come to the market for Elesin and are mocked and driven out by the girls, who mimic the speech of English officers and take their batons from them.","Scene 3 continued: Elesin emerges from the bridal chamber with proof of the consummation and begins the dance that carries him towards trance, while the Praise-Singer speaks as the dead Alafin and the drumming takes over from the dialogue.","Scene 4: at the Residency ball Jane Pilkings tells Olunde, unprompted and as a piece of wartime excitement, of a ship's captain who blew up his own vessel to save the people on the coast; Olunde asks whether it was enemy action, then re-describes that death as self-sacrifice, so that her own example shows the British honouring a willed death while calling his father's barbaric.","Scene 4 continued: Elesin is brought on under arrest and in handcuffs in the closing moments of the scene; Olunde refuses to acknowledge him as his father and turns away from him.","Scene 5: in the cell Elesin first blames Pilkings, then acknowledges a weakness of his own that preceded the arrest; Iyaloja arrives and reproaches him bitterly.","Scene 5 continued: the women carry in Olunde's body, dead in his father's place; Elesin strangles himself with his chains, and Iyaloja turns the living away from the dead and towards the unborn.","Theme — duty and the will: the rite requires not merely a death but a willed passage, so hesitation is itself the failure.","Theme — continuity: the play sets the worlds of the dead, the living and the unborn in one continuum, which Elesin's failure threatens to break.","Theme — colonial interruption: Soyinka's Author's Note treats the colonial factor as a catalyst rather than the play's subject and rejects the clash-of-cultures reading.","Theme — appetite and the body: eating, market trade, cloth, harvest and marriage run through Elesin's language, and it is the body's hold on life that undoes him.","Theme — whose sacrifice is honoured: Jane Pilkings's story of the ship's captain, turned back on her by Olunde, forces the audience to notice which deaths a culture agrees to call noble.","Form - five scenes with no act divisions; the first four alternate the Yoruba public world (Scenes 1 and 3, the market) with the colonial European one (Scene 2, the bungalow; Scene 4, the Residency ball), and Scene 5 breaks the pattern by bringing the market into a colonial cell.","Form — two registers: the proverb-rich, image-heavy praise-poetry of the market scenes against the clipped naturalistic prose of the colonial ones.","Form — offstage drumming and the dirge run under the action as the play's clock and as pressure on the colonial scenes.","Form — stage properties carry meaning: the egungun costume, the rich cloth the women wrap Elesin in, the bridal cloth, and the chains he finally uses on himself.","Form — the death the title announces is withheld until the final scene, and the arrest is not staged at all: it happens offstage between Scenes 3 and 4, so Elesin simply appears in handcuffs at the ball and the audience meets consequence rather than conflict.","Context — Wole Soyinka, born in Nigeria in 1934, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1986; the play was first published in 1975.","Context — the prescribed editions for this specification are the Methuen Learner Edition of July 1998 (ISBN 9780413695505) and the Bloomsbury Modern Classics edition of October 1975 (ISBN 9780413333605).","Context warning — AO4 is not assessed in Section A of Component 2, so context supports your understanding of the play but must not become a paragraph of its own.","Key moment — the Not-I bird parable: self-praise the rest of the play is built to test.","Key moment — Iyaloja's consent and warning over the bride: the community's terms, set before the failure.","Key moment — the girls' mimicry of English officers: colonial authority defeated by imitation rather than force.","Key moment - the dance towards trance at the end of Scene 3: language thins into drumming as Elesin passes towards trance. The arrest is never staged; it happens offstage, between Scenes 3 and 4.","Key moment — Jane Pilkings's story of the ship's captain and Olunde's reply to it: the play's argument about whose sacrifice counts.","Key moment — Olunde's refusal of his father at the ball: judgement delivered from inside the culture, not by the coloniser.","Key moment — the confession in the cell: Elesin acknowledges a weakness of his own, which complicates any simple blaming of Pilkings.","Key moment — the uncovering of Olunde's body and Elesin's self-strangling: the cost is shown rather than argued, with the coloniser's iron as the instrument.","Key moment — Iyaloja's closing turn towards the unborn: the play ends on continuity rather than on guilt.","Key moment - the final stage direction: Iyaloja's closing instruction is followed not by silence but by the women's dirge rising in volume as they sway on, before the lights fade.","Evidence note - Section A is open book, so a short exact reference is worth more than a long paraphrase: Olunde's 'eater of left-overs' at the ball, or Iyaloja's 'Turn your mind only to the unborn' at the close, each carry a paragraph."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Soyinka present duty in Death and the King's Horseman? You must consider language, form and structure. (30 marks)","items":["Decode the question: 'In what ways' asks for Soyinka's methods, so every paragraph must name one; 'present' means the play's making of the idea, not a list of the duties in it; 'duty' covers Elesin's ritual obligation, the market women's obligation to see it done, Olunde's inherited obligation and the officers' idea of their own duty; the instruction sentence carries 15 of the 30 marks.","Introduction (two or three sentences): take the position that duty in this play is not a rule to be obeyed but a performance that must be willed, which is why it can fail inside a man who never disputes it.","Paragraph 1 — Scene 1, language: the Not-I bird parable and the Praise-Singer's chant present duty as something Elesin relishes; analyse the repetition and the self-casting; judge that Soyinka makes eagerness and appetite the same faculty.","Paragraph 2 — Scene 1, the bride: Iyaloja's consent and warning present duty as communal property; analyse her proverb-based idiom against Elesin's expansive imagery; judge that the community, not the coloniser, holds the right to hold him to it.","Paragraph 3 — Scene 3, form: the girls' mimicry and then the dance present duty as collective work before it is one man's death; analyse the shift from dialogue into drumming; judge that Soyinka makes the whole market perform the rite with him.","Paragraph 4 — Scenes 2 and 4, contrast: Pilkings's official duty and Jane Pilkings's story of the ship's captain, which Olunde accepts and turns back on her, present duty as culturally framed; analyse the flat colonial prose against Olunde's courteous pressure, and the placing of the debate inside a party; judge that the double standard is exposed without either side being sentimentalised.","Paragraph 5 — Scene 5, structure: the cell, the confession, the son's body and the chains present duty as inherited when it is not discharged; analyse the staging of the body and the self-strangling; judge that the obligation is transferred rather than cancelled.","Evaluation thread to carry through every paragraph: is duty here a burden imposed on Elesin or an identity he chose and enjoyed? Return to that question in the last sentence of each paragraph so the final judgement is earned rather than announced.","Conclusion must: answer the question in its own words, state which account of duty the play finally supports, concede what the rival account keeps, and close on the ending's turn towards the unborn as Soyinka's way of making duty continuous rather than finished. It must not summarise the plot."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Working the 45 minutes and the clean copy","items":["About 45 minutes is this essay's pro-rata share of the 60-mark, 90-minute paper; the specification itself sets no per-question time.","Spend the first five minutes choosing between the two questions and planning: if you cannot fill the question's key noun with material from four scenes, it is the wrong choice.","Plan five or six paragraphs and write the scene beside each one, so the essay covers the whole play instead of visiting Scene 1 twice.","Use the clean, unmarked copy to make short references exact; a long copied extract costs minutes and adds nothing to either objective.","Name one method in every paragraph and follow it through to the meaning it makes for the question, because the AO2 strand rises from identification through sustained analysis to cohesive evaluation.","Keep context out of its own paragraph: AO4 is not assessed in Section A, so background may inform the argument but must never replace evidence or analysis.","Leave four or five minutes for a conclusion that answers the question and takes a position; a summary of the play is not a conclusion.","On the Component 3 route the same essay is written to an advisory 650 to 800 words with no time limit and no penalty for exceeding the count, so the difference an examiner sees is precision of evidence, not length.","Because the copy is open in front of you, take one short exact phrase per paragraph and analyse its words; a reference of three or four words that is right beats a sentence of remembered gist."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:18:15Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Both fixes are in and correct, and on a fresh reading of the whole kit every quotation is exact and within the seven-word limit ('Not I', 'eater of left-overs', 'Turn your mind only to the unborn', 'a market in its closing stages', 'no respect', Soyinka's 'a catalytic incident merely'), the scene references, the Author's Note, the five-scene design, the Section A and Assignment A mark splits and the level boundaries all check out, so the kit is publishable and what remains is the honesty of the d=2 tier and the use of the marks field for checkpoint counts."}}},"heritage-romeo-and-juliet":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-romeo-and-juliet","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Romeo and Juliet is your literary heritage text for Component 2, Section B: one 30-mark essay question chosen from two, carrying ten raw marks for AO1, ten for AO2 and ten for AO4. It is the only part of Component 2 that pays for context, and the three objectives weigh equally, so an essay that knows the play well and analyses beautifully but never reads it against the world it was written in throws away a third of the marks. The examination is open book, which raises the standard rather than lowering it: with a clean, unmarked copy in front of you, an examiner expects exact, short, chosen quotation and no retelling of the story. This room teaches you to argue about the play, to analyse how Shakespeare makes it work, and to make context explain a line rather than sit beside it.","idea":[{"h":"Where Romeo and Juliet sits in Component 2","t":"Component 2 asks you to study two set texts: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. Romeo and Juliet is the literary heritage choice here, and the specification expects the whole text to be studied, not extracts. The content overview asks you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. A top-level answer treats the play as one designed structure: it can move from a word in Act 1 to a structural turn in Act 3 and back again, because it knows the whole thing, and it writes in clear, accurate Standard English about a text it has thought about rather than been told about.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","t":"AO1 asks you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, and it is worth ten of the thirty marks. The specification spells out what that means in practice: demonstrate a close understanding of your heritage text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement, and identify and use relevant examples from the text. The board's own model wordings fasten on a theme or a setting, so you must know the characters, relationships, themes and key moments of Romeo and Juliet closely enough to argue about them. A top-level answer takes a position in its first sentence and holds it: the personal engagement shows in the choice of evidence and the confidence of the judgement, never in phrases like in my opinion.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: how language, form and structure create effects","t":"AO2 asks you to analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects, for ten of the thirty marks, and the instruction that carries it appears at the end of every set-text question: you must consider language, form and structure. The grid moves from minimal identification at Level 1, through some comment, to sustained analysis at Level 4 and cohesive evaluation at Level 5, so naming a device earns almost nothing. Form in this play means the sonnets, the blank verse of the nobility and the prose of the servants; structure means the comic first half, the hinge of Act 3 Scene 1, the compressed time-scheme and the dramatic irony the Prologue installs. A top-level answer traces one chosen word or shape to its effect on an audience, and uses literary terminology because the effect needs the term, not to show it knows one.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"},{"h":"AO4: the play and the world it was written in","t":"AO4 asks you to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and to understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. It is worth ten of the thirty marks and is the only AO4 in Component 2, about seven per cent of the whole qualification. The Assignment B grid describes the strand rising from little comment on the relationship between text and context to understanding integrated convincingly into the response, and that word integrated is the whole test. A top-level answer never writes a paragraph called context: it uses an Elizabethan father's legal power over his daughter's marriage to explain why Capulet's threat in Act 3 Scene 5 is authority rather than temper, so the history does analytical work on the line in front of it.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"},{"h":"The five categories of context the specification names","t":"The specification names five kinds of context that affect a writer's work and a reader's response: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, such as literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from when it was written. For Romeo and Juliet these give you the playhouse and its boy actors, Catholic Italy seen from Protestant England, arranged marriage and honour culture, the tragedy and the sonnet tradition, and modern readings of Juliet's age. A top-level answer picks the one category that explains the moment being analysed and leaves the other four alone.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"The question, the choice and the open book","t":"Section B gives one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text; in the breakdown of assessment objectives these are Questions 11 to 22, carrying ten marks AO1, ten AO2 and ten AO4. Component 2 lasts one hour thirty minutes, is worth 60 raw marks and is 40 per cent of the International GCSE, and you write one Section A essay on the modern drama text and one Section B essay on Romeo and Juliet. It is an open book examination: you may take in a clean copy of the prescribed edition, completely unmarked. A top-level answer uses that freedom for precision, not for volume: it chooses the question it can argue against itself on, quotes in phrases rather than passages, and answers the question in its first sentence instead of arriving at an answer in its last.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"},{"h":"The coursework route: Component 3, Assignment B","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same literary heritage list. Assignment B is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4, marked out of thirty with ten marks for each, and the assignment must allow learners to access all three. The board's model wordings show the shape a good title takes, and the advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, with no penalty for exceeding it; the two assignment marks are combined into one mark out of 60. A top-level answer to a coursework title is built like the examination essay but tighter: the title is narrow enough to be argued in four or five paragraphs, and every paragraph still carries knowledge, method and context together, because the grid is the same five-level best fit grid.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning one quotation into Level 5 analysis: the shared sonnet","setup":"The question is: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents the lovers' first meeting in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure. The moment is at the Capulet feast in Act 1 Scene 5, where Romeo takes Juliet's hand and says “If I profane with my unworthiest hand / This holy shrine, the gentle sin is this: / My lips, two blushing pilgrims, ready stand / To smooth that rough touch with a tender kiss.” Juliet answers “For saints have hands that pilgrims' hands do touch, / And palm to palm is holy palmers' kiss.” The two of them go on alternating in the same verse until Romeo kisses her, fourteen lines in all. Work out the moves an examiner rewards.","steps":["Name the shape before the words: what the two of them build between them is a sonnet, so the play's central love poem has two authors instead of one.","Show the shape doing the work: Juliet completes the rhyme Romeo began, so the audience hears agreement in the verse before either character has agreed to anything.","Analyse the field of words: profane, holy shrine, pilgrims, saints and palmers turn a hand on a hand into an act of worship, and lift the meeting above the bawdy joking that has filled the play so far.","Read the irony the worship sets up: the same devotional language sends the lovers to a friar's cell to be married and, at the last, into a tomb.","Judge: Shakespeare makes their first exchange a finished poem so the relationship sounds complete at the instant it begins, which is the play's central irony in miniature."],"cues":["Before you touch a single word, what shape are these fourteen lines in?","Who finishes whose rhyme here, and what does that tell an audience?","Which family of words do profane, shrine and pilgrims all belong to?","Why is it ironic that two strangers at a feast reach a kiss through the language of worship?","What one claim about Shakespeare's method does all of this evidence support?"]},{"title":"Weighing a proposition: fate against the adults","setup":"The question is: “Romeo and Juliet are destroyed by fate rather than by the choices of the adults around them.” To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. Your evidence: the Chorus calls them “A pair of star-cross'd lovers”; Romeo cries “O, I am fortune's fool!” after killing Tybalt in Act 3 Scene 1; he says “Then I defy you, stars!” on hearing of Juliet's death in Act 5 Scene 1; and Capulet threatens Juliet with “hang, beg, starve, die in the streets” in Act 3 Scene 5. One contextual fact: in Shakespeare's England a father normally arranged his daughter's marriage, and a daughter's obedience to him was a social and religious duty. Build a weighed judgement.","steps":["Concede the proposition first, with its best evidence: the Chorus fixes the ending before the action starts, so the audience watches a story that is already decided.","Turn the same evidence against itself: because the ending is known, attention falls on the decisions that carry the lovers towards it, so the design makes human choice more visible, not less.","Set the adults beside the stars: the threat to throw Juliet out is a father's free decision, and it is what drives her to the Friar's potion.","Make context argue: a father's right to dispose of his daughter's marriage was ordinary in Shakespeare's England, so a first audience could hear authority where a modern audience hears cruelty.","Judge and qualify: fate supplies the frame, the adults fill it, so agreement is partial — the stars exist in this play to make the waste of the choices unbearable."],"cues":["Which single piece of evidence is strongest for the side you intend to resist?","If the audience already knows the ending, what is left for them to watch?","Which of these four quotations is a decision rather than a complaint about fate?","What did the first audiences take for granted that a modern audience does not?","What is the one sentence of judgement all of this has earned?"]},{"title":"Making context earn AO4 marks instead of losing them","setup":"The question is: How does Shakespeare present the power of Juliet's father in Romeo and Juliet? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. You have two contextual facts: in Elizabethan England a daughter's marriage was normally arranged by her father and obedience to him was a religious as well as a social duty; and the play was written for an all-male company, so Juliet was first performed by a boy actor. You also have Capulet's words to Juliet in Act 3 Scene 5, “Out, you green-sickness carrion! out, you baggage!”, and his earlier words about her in Act 1 Scene 2, “My child is yet a stranger in the world”. Show the difference between bolted-on context and integrated context.","steps":["Write the weak version deliberately, so you can see it fail: in Elizabethan times fathers controlled their daughters, and Capulet is angry with Juliet. The history sits next to the text and analyses nothing.","Fasten the history to a word instead: carrion and baggage reduce a daughter to diseased meat and then to luggage, and property language is exactly what paternal authority licensed a man to feel.","Fasten it to structure: the tenderness of calling her a stranger in the world comes before the feast, so an audience measures the whole distance from indulgence to ownership across two acts.","Fasten it to reception: a boy actor kneeling to plead made the power gap physical for the first audiences, while a modern audience, knowing Juliet's age, reads the scene as abuse.","Judge: context earns its marks only when it explains why a chosen word or a structural turn lands as it does on an audience."],"cues":["What is wrong with a sentence of history that could be deleted without changing the analysis?","Which two nouns in that line are doing the demeaning, and what do they turn a person into?","What did the same father say about her earlier, and what does the gap measure?","How would the first staging of this scene differ from one you might watch now?","State the rule this example has proved about context."]},{"title":"Working the turn of the play: Mercutio in Act 3 Scene 1","setup":"The question is: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents the turning point of Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. The moment is the middle of Act 3 Scene 1, in a public place in the heat of the day, where Tybalt wounds Mercutio under Romeo's arm after Romeo has refused Tybalt's challenge. Asked how badly he is hurt Mercutio answers “Ay, ay, a scratch, a scratch”; he says the wound is “not so deep as a well, nor so wide as a church door, but 'tis enough”; he jokes “ask for me tomorrow, and you shall find me a grave man”; and he repeats “A plague o' both your houses!” until he is carried off to die. Romeo then kills Tybalt and cries “O, I am fortune's fool!”, and the Prince ends the scene with “Mercy but murders, pardoning those that kill”. Act 2 Scene 6 has already married the lovers in secret; by Act 3 Scene 3 Romeo is banished. One contextual fact: a gentleman was expected to answer a challenge, and refusing one in public was taken as dishonour. Work out the moves an examiner rewards.","steps":["Place the extract in the shape of the play first: the marriage is one scene behind it and the banishment two scenes ahead, so this is where a comedy of secret lovers becomes a tragedy, and the structure makes the argument before any quotation does.","Analyse the wordplay against the wound: Mercutio is punning while he bleeds, and calling himself a grave man makes his own death the joke, so an audience is still laughing at the instant the play turns.","Follow the one line he keeps returning to: the curse on both houses is thrown by the only man on stage who belongs to neither family, which widens a private quarrel into a judgement on the whole feud and names the cause of everything that follows.","Make the context argue: in a society where a gentleman was expected to answer a challenge, Romeo's refusal reads to a first audience as dishonour rather than restraint, which is why the wound arrives under his arm and why he must kill Tybalt afterwards; his cry that he is fortune's fool records the cost of having tried to keep the peace.","Judge: Shakespeare spends his wittiest character to change the genre in one scene, and the Prince's closing line turns the mercy shown to Romeo into part of the cause, so the ending is presented as the price of what the adults let pass."],"cues":["Where does this scene sit between the marriage and the banishment, and what does that position tell you?","Mercutio is dying: what is he still doing with words, and what does one of those jokes turn out to mean?","Which line does he keep returning to, and whose quarrel is it not?","What would a first audience have thought of a young gentleman who refused a challenge in public?","What single claim about Shakespeare's method has all of this earned?"]},{"title":"Reading the Prologue as form","setup":"The question is: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare shapes the audience's expectations at the opening of Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure. The Chorus begins “Two households, both alike in dignity, / In fair Verona, where we lay our scene, / From ancient grudge break to new mutiny, / Where civil blood makes civil hands unclean.” It goes on to call them “A pair of star-cross'd lovers” and to describe the play as “the two hours' traffic of our stage”. The whole speech runs to fourteen lines and closes on a rhyming pair. Work out what a top-level answer says.","steps":["Name the form and its oddity: the fourteen-line shape belongs to love poetry, and here it is used to deliver a death notice.","Listen to the sound: repeating civil in one line makes the citizens both the victims and the authors of the bloodshed, so the feud is presented as self-inflicted.","Read the structural cost of telling the ending: suspense goes, dramatic irony replaces it, and every hopeful line afterwards carries its own contradiction.","Link the frame to the whole: calling the action mere traffic of the stage announces a compression that the plot then delivers, since the lovers meet, marry and die within days.","Judge: a love form is made to carry a death, so form and content are at war from the first line, which is the method of the entire play."],"cues":["What kind of poem is normally this long, and what is this one announcing?","Which word appears twice in the fourth line, and what does the repetition do?","What can an audience no longer feel once they know the ending?","What does the word traffic suggest about how fast this play intends to move?","What single claim about Shakespeare's method has this earned?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which assessment objectives does the Romeo and Juliet question carry, and how many raw marks each?","a":"AO1, AO2 and AO4, ten raw marks each, thirty in total."},{"q":"What may you take into the Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text; the examination is open book."},{"q":"Why does naming a metaphor score so little under AO2?","a":"The strand rises from minimal identification through sustained analysis to cohesive evaluation, so the marks are in tracing the effect the method creates, not in the label."},{"q":"What makes context Level 5 rather than Level 1?","a":"It is integrated convincingly into the response — attached to the word or structural turn being analysed — instead of added as a separate block of history."},{"q":"Name the five categories of context the specification lists.","a":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section B: one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on Romeo and Juliet, carrying ten marks AO1, ten marks AO2 and ten marks AO4.","Component 2 is a one hour thirty minute examination, 60 raw marks and 40 per cent of the International GCSE; it is open book and you may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition.","Every Section B essay title ends with an instruction to consider language, form and structure and to refer to context; the specification prints it on its two model titles, both of them novels, as “You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel”, so for Romeo and Juliet it is the context of the play.","The five categories of context: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times.","The five levels run 1 to 6, 7 to 12, 13 to 18, 19 to 24 and 25 to 30, awarded on a best fit basis, with the context strand rising to understanding integrated convincingly into the response.","The spine of the play: the Chorus's sonnet Prologue; the street brawl in Act 1 Scene 1; the feast and first meeting in Act 1 Scene 5; the balcony scene in Act 2 Scene 2; the marriage in Act 2 Scene 6; Mercutio and Tybalt killed and Romeo banished in Act 3 Scene 1; the dawn parting and Capulet's rage in Act 3 Scene 5; the potion plan in Act 4 Scene 1; Juliet drinking alone in Act 4 Scene 3; the apothecary in Act 5 Scene 1; the tomb in Act 5 Scene 3."],"questions":[{"q":"Name the verse form Shakespeare uses for the Prologue of Romeo and Juliet and explain one effect of choosing it. (2 marks)","a":"A sonnet; a love form is used to announce two deaths, so form and content contradict each other before the action starts.","sol":"The Prologue is a sonnet: fourteen lines closing in a rhyming couplet, the form Elizabethan poets used for love. Shakespeare hands it to a Chorus whose news is that the lovers will die, so a love poem is made to do the work of a death notice. The effect is an immediate clash between shape and content, and it installs the dramatic irony the rest of the play depends on: the audience hears every later hope inside a frame that has already refused it.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Count the lines and listen for the point where the rhyme closes.","Ask what kind of poem usually takes that shape, and what kind of news this one is carrying.","Write the label first, then one sentence setting what this kind of poem is usually written for against what this one is being used for."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"Quote the phrase from the Prologue that fixes the lovers' fate, and explain what telling the audience the ending does to the rest of the play. (3 marks)","a":"“A pair of star-cross'd lovers”; suspense is replaced by dramatic irony, so every later hope carries its own contradiction.","sol":"The Chorus calls them “A pair of star-cross'd lovers”, so before a word of the action the audience knows the stars are against them and that they will die. Shakespeare gives up surprise on purpose. What replaces it is dramatic irony: when Romeo, on his way to the feast in Act 1 Scene 4, fears “Some consequence yet hanging in the stars”, or when Friar Laurence hopes the marriage will “turn your households' rancour to pure love”, the audience hears the hope and the outcome at the same moment. Attention therefore shifts from what will happen to how it is allowed to happen, which is where the play's argument about haste and adult authority lives.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The phrase is in the Chorus's opening eight lines and it uses the stars.","Ask what an audience can no longer feel once the ending is known, and what feeling moves in to replace it.","Give the short quotation, name the effect, then show it working on one later line."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"State how the thirty marks for the Romeo and Juliet question in Component 2, Section B are divided between the assessment objectives. (2 marks)","a":"Ten marks AO1, ten marks AO2 and ten marks AO4.","sol":"Section B, Literary Heritage Texts, is one thirty-mark essay question from a choice of two, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4. In the breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks these are Questions 11 to 22, carrying ten raw marks for AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, a critical style, informed personal engagement), ten for AO2 (analysis of language, form and structure) and ten for AO4 (the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written). Because the three weigh equally, an essay that analyses superbly but never touches context can reach at best two thirds of the marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is the only section of Component 2 that pays for context at all.","Find the row for Questions 11 to 22 in the breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks, and count how many objectives that section is said to assess.","Name each objective and put the number of marks beside it."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"State what a learner may take into the Component 2 examination for Romeo and Juliet, and what is not allowed. (2 marks)","a":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition of each set text; it must be completely unmarked, so no annotations or added notes.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of each of their set texts into the examination room. The copy must be completely unmarked: no annotations, no underlining, no sticky notes or inserted paper. Learners or centres must buy a prescribed edition at the start of the course, and Appendix 3 lists Oxford, Heinemann, Pearson Education and Cambridge editions for Romeo and Juliet. The practical consequence is that quotations should be exact and short — the book is there to check a word, not to be copied out.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the word open book is actually granting you, and what it is not.","The condition is about the state of the copy, not about which publisher printed it.","Say what may be brought in, then the condition attached to it."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"The specification names five categories of context. Identify two of them and match each to one detail of Romeo and Juliet. (3 marks)","a":"Any two categories, each tied to a real detail: for example social and cultural contexts with the arranged marriage, or reception with a modern reading of Juliet's age.","sol":"The five categories are the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, such as movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times. Two matched examples: under social and cultural contexts, an Elizabethan father's right to arrange his daughter's marriage explains why Capulet treats Juliet's refusal in Act 3 Scene 5 as rebellion rather than preference; under reception, a twenty-first-century audience, knowing Juliet is not yet fourteen, reads the adults' conduct as coercion where earlier audiences saw ordinary paternal authority.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the five are about the world outside the play; one is about the audience in front of it.","Pick categories you can attach to a real moment, not the ones that sound most impressive.","For each, name the category and then the single detail of the play it explains."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents Juliet's change from obedience to defiance in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Her early speech is conditional and deferential; after the marriage she controls her own verse, deceives her parents and acts alone, and Shakespeare measures the change by her language and by the shrinking circle of adults she can trust.","sol":"Shakespeare measures Juliet's change in the grammar he gives her. In Act 1 Scene 3, asked to consider Paris, she answers in conditionals — she will “look to like, if looking liking move” — and her one plain statement, “It is an honour that I dream not of”, is an obedient negative. She replies; she does not begin.\n\nBy the balcony scene in Act 2 Scene 2 she sets the terms. She refuses an oath sworn on “th'inconstant moon” because a monthly-changing thing cannot guarantee anything, and she names the danger in the courtship herself: “It is too rash, too unadvis'd, too sudden.” Shakespeare gives the clearest thinking in the play to its youngest character, which is the source of its waste.\n\nThe turn is structural as well as verbal. After Act 3 Scene 1 every adult she leans on withdraws: her father threatens “hang, beg, starve, die in the streets”, her mother says “I have done with thee”, and the Nurse advises a second marriage that would be bigamy. Her answer is solitude — “My dismal scene I needs must act alone”. She has stood alone on stage before this — waiting for the Nurse at the opening of Act 2 Scene 5, and calling on the night in the “Gallop apace, you fiery-footed steeds” soliloquy that opens Act 3 Scene 2 — but in those speeches a confidante is still about to walk in. By the potion soliloquy in Act 4 Scene 3 there is nobody left to come in after her.\n\nContextually, obedience to a father's choice of husband was a religious as well as a social duty, so her defiance is not adolescent temper but a refusal of the order of her whole world. Shakespeare presents the change, then, not as growing up but as being abandoned into adulthood, and the last scene judges the adults, not the girl.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Track one narrow thing to start with: who opens the conversations Juliet is in, early on and after the marriage.","Set her verse in Act 1 Scene 3 against her verse at the balcony and before the potion, and ask what the adults are doing in the gaps between them.","Build the essay as a change measured in her language and in her isolation, with the duty of obedience explaining what the defiance costs her."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare present the effects of the feud in Romeo and Juliet? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"The feud is presented first as inherited language, then as a force that drives the lovers into privacy and night, and finally as a destroyer of those who never chose it, including the Prince's own kin.","sol":"Shakespeare presents the feud as language before he presents it as violence. Act 1 Scene 1 opens in servants' prose, where the quarrel is a joke about a thumb — “Do you bite your thumb at us, sir?” — and the comedy makes its own point: nobody now remembers a cause, the Prologue having called it an “ancient grudge”. Tybalt then raises the same quarrel into verse hatred, “I hate the word, / As I hate hell, all Montagues, and thee”, where hell as the measure of enmity makes the feud a matter of faith rather than reason.\n\nIts second effect is to make love private. The lovers must meet by night, marry in a friar's cell and speak in images of stars and torches, because the public world has no vocabulary for them; Juliet's “My only love sprung from my only hate!” compresses the whole difficulty into one antithesis.\n\nIts third effect falls on those who never chose it. Mercutio, the Prince's kinsman and a Montague only by friendship, dies cursing “A plague o' both your houses!”, and the Prince's closing “All are punish'd” extends the damage to the ruler of the city, who has lost “a brace of kinsmen”.\n\nAn honour culture in which an insult had to be answered or a family's standing fell explains why Romeo cannot simply walk away in Act 3 Scene 1. Shakespeare therefore presents the feud as an inherited grammar: the children speak it before they understand it, and it kills them for speaking it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Notice who is speaking in the opening quarrel, and in what kind of speech.","Sort the effects into three kinds — on the city, on the lovers' privacy, and on the bystanders — and find one piece of evidence for each.","Argue that the feud is a language the young inherit, and let the honour culture explain why refusing to fight is not an option."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Shakespeare present the Nurse in Romeo and Juliet? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Repetition, digression and an earthy, bodily register make her the play's warmth and Juliet's substitute mother; the advice to marry Paris in Act 3 Scene 5 turns that same practicality into betrayal and leaves Juliet alone.","sol":"Shakespeare builds the Nurse out of repetition, digression and a plain, bodily register, and then turns that comic manner against the audience. Her long recollection in Act 1 Scene 3 is verse that behaves as though it were not: it circles back to the same memories — the dead Susan, the weaning, the earthquake — and to the same bawdy joke, its sentences running on past the line-ends until Lady Capulet has to stop her. Set beside Juliet's compression, that shapelessness is characterisation by form, marking her as the household's servant rather than its mistress and making her, for the first half, the play's warmth.\n\nThat warmth has a function. She is the go-between who carries Juliet's messages to Romeo and teases her by withholding the answer, and because she nursed her and remembers her as a child, she supplies the mother Juliet does not otherwise have — a division of feeling and duty entirely normal in a wealthy Elizabethan household, where a wet nurse raised the child her mistress bore.\n\nThe reversal in Act 3 Scene 5 is therefore devastating and completely in character. Asked for counsel, she counsels survival — “I think it best you married with the county” — and dismisses Romeo as a “dishclout”, a kitchen image from a woman whose loyalty is finally to the house that employs her. Juliet's reply, “Ancient damnation! O most wicked fiend!”, ends the friendship in two exclamations, and from that line onward Juliet is alone.\n\nA servant had no standing to defy her master, which makes the advice practical rather than treacherous. Shakespeare's method is to let an audience feel the betrayal and understand the necessity at once, so the Nurse becomes the play's clearest study of how ordinary dependence becomes complicity.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Listen to the shape of her speech before you judge her conduct.","Ask what job she does for Juliet in the first half, then find the exact line where she stops doing it.","Argue from form to function to betrayal, and let a servant's position in the household explain why the advice is practical rather than wicked."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"“Mercutio's death is the turning point of the play.” To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Strongly but not wholly: Act 3 Scene 1 ends the comic movement and removes the play's wit, but the Prologue has already fixed the ending and the decisive blow to Juliet falls at home in Act 3 Scene 5.","sol":"The claim is strong and the structure supports it. Until Act 3 Scene 1 the play moves like a comedy: a feast, a disguise, a secret marriage and a friar hoping to “turn your households' rancour to pure love”. Mercutio's death ends that generic promise, and the scene's own language marks the change. His joke about the wound — “'tis not so deep as a well, nor so wide as a church-door; but 'tis enough, 'twill serve” — is the last time wit works in this play, and the curse he repeats, “A plague o' both your houses!”, converts comedy into judgement. Romeo's last line in the scene hands the story to fate: “O, I am fortune's fool!” Since this is the middle act of five, the turn falls exactly where the structure puts its hinge, and the honour code that made Romeo's restraint read as disgrace is what kills his friend.\n\nAgainst the claim: the Prologue has already fixed the ending, so strictly nothing can turn a plot that was never open. And the blow that isolates Juliet is struck at home in Act 3 Scene 5, by a father and then a nurse, not in the street by Tybalt.\n\nThe better judgement is that Mercutio's death is a turning point in kind rather than in outcome. It does not decide that the lovers will die — the Chorus did that — but it decides how they will: through banishment, isolation and haste, with the laughter taken out. Agreement, then, is strong and qualified.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what kind of play it looks like up to that scene, and what kind it looks like afterwards.","Find the best argument against the proposition before you write, since the grid pays for the weighing rather than the agreeing.","Aim at a qualified judgement that separates what the death changes from what it cannot change."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"“Romeo and Juliet is read very differently in the twenty-first century from the way it was read when it was written.” To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Partly: age, religion and staging are received quite differently now, but Shakespeare builds disapproval of the feud and of haste into the text, so the change is one of emphasis rather than of meaning.","sol":"Some of the difference is real. Capulet says his daughter “hath not seen the change of fourteen years”, and Lady Capulet tells Juliet she was already a mother at about that age; among the propertied classes early betrothal was unremarkable, so a first audience measured Juliet against her mother, while a modern audience measures her against child-protection assumptions and finds Paris's suit and Capulet's insistence coercive. The Catholic setting is a second difference: confession, a friar's cell and the language of pilgrimage carried a foreignness for a Protestant English audience that is now largely inaudible. So is the staging: Juliet was first played by a boy in an all-male company, so the erotic charge of the balcony scene was theatrical convention rather than realism.\n\nBut the play resists being read in only one way in any period, because the criticism is built in. The Prince condemns the feud in the first scene; Friar Laurence warns that “These violent delights have violent ends”; and the Prologue says plainly that only the children's deaths could remove the parents' rage. A first audience was not invited to approve of Capulet, and the reconciliation at the tomb is bought at a price the play refuses to call worth paying.\n\nThe judgement is therefore partial agreement. The twenty-first century reads the play's sexual politics, its religion and its speed with different sympathies and stages it accordingly; but the condemnation of inherited hatred and of adult haste is written into the structure, so what has changed is emphasis rather than meaning.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Separate what has genuinely changed in the audience from what Shakespeare already says in the text.","Take three areas where reception has shifted — age, religion, staging — and test each against a line the play itself supplies.","Aim at partial agreement, and make the reconciliation at the tomb do the deciding work."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents the love between Romeo and Juliet from their first meeting to the tomb. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"It begins as shared composition in a lit, crowded hall and ends as two separate speeches in the dark; the worship imagery and the kiss return, but dialogue has become monologue and the kiss has become the means of death.","sol":"At the feast in Act 1 Scene 5 Shakespeare presents the love as something the two of them make together. Romeo offers a quatrain of pilgrimage imagery — “My lips, two blushing pilgrims, ready stand” — and Juliet takes up his rhyme with “palm to palm is holy palmers' kiss”, so the agreement happens in the verse before it happens in the plot. The register is devotional and teasing at once, and the setting is public: torches, dancers, and an intimacy stolen out of a crowd. Shakespeare gives the relationship a finished poetic shape at the moment it begins.\n\nBy Act 5 Scene 3 the same love is spoken alone. Romeo talks over Juliet's body with nobody to answer — “Death, that hath suck'd the honey of thy breath, / Hath had no power yet upon thy beauty” — and the shared form has gone: two separate speeches and two deaths, kept apart by the sleep that stops the lovers meeting. The worship language of the feast comes back as literal sacrament, and the kiss that opened the relationship becomes the way out of it: “Thus with a kiss I die.” Juliet's last words invert the tenderness completely — “O happy dagger! / This is thy sheath” — making an embrace out of a weapon.\n\nThe context sharpens both ends of that line. Romeo's first love, for Rosaline, is the sighing, sleepless posture of the Petrarchan lover his friends mock, so a first audience hears the shared sonnet at the feast as the convention suddenly answered by a real voice on the other side of it. The religious vocabulary is not decoration either: in a society where a vow was binding before God, the marriage the Friar performs in Act 2 Scene 6 cannot be undone, which is why Capulet's plan for Paris is a catastrophe rather than an inconvenience. And self-killing was for that audience a sin that barred a body from consecrated ground, so the tomb, where a modern audience tends to see romantic consummation, would also have carried a far colder judgement on what the feud had driven two children to.\n\nShakespeare therefore presents the love as one continuous thing that is progressively stripped of company. The materials never change — worship, touch, light and the kiss — but the form inverts: a hall becomes a vault, two voices become one, and the kiss that joined the lovers now silences them. Love in this play is begun together and necessarily completed alone, and that structural fact, rather than any speech about fate, is the play's harshest judgement on the world the lovers were born into.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Take the relationship at two points a whole play apart and ask what has happened to the form it is spoken in, not only to what is said.","Choose one image that runs from the feast to the tomb and follow what the play does to it; then ask what a first audience believed about vows and about suicide.","Organise the essay by what stays the same and what has turned over, not scene by scene, and open on the shape of the speech rather than on the story."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Your centre enters you for Component 3 instead of Component 2. Write a title for Assignment B on Romeo and Juliet in the board's own model wording, and explain how it gives access to AO1, AO2 and AO4 and what the advisory word count allows. (6 marks)","a":"A title such as: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents haste in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play — with each objective shown to be reachable, in 650 to 800 advisory words.","sol":"A title in the board's model form would be: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents haste in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. It gives access to AO1 because presenting haste demands close knowledge across the whole play — Friar Laurence's “Wisely and slow; they stumble that run fast”, the compression of the action into days, Capulet bringing the wedding day forward. It gives access to AO2 because the instruction names language, form and structure, so the writer must analyse the imagery of fire and powder and the shortening of scenes after Act 3 Scene 1. It gives access to AO4 because the context instruction opens the Elizabethan expectation that a father might settle a match quickly, and the reception point that modern audiences read the speed as recklessness. Assignment B is marked out of thirty with ten marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4 on the five-level grid, best fit; the advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, so the title must be narrow enough to argue in four or five paragraphs rather than surveyed.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the wordings the specification prints for the coursework assignments and keep their shape.","Test your title by asking whether each of the three objectives has somewhere obvious to go.","Give the title, then one sentence per objective, then the marking and the word count."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"Explain how your Section B answer on Romeo and Juliet must differ from your Section A answer on the modern drama text, and why an essay with an identical shape cannot reach the top level in Section B. (6 marks)","a":"Section A is fifteen AO1 and fifteen AO2 with no context marks; Section B splits thirty equally across AO1, AO2 and AO4, so a context-free essay forfeits a third of the marks and a bolted-on context paragraph scores low because the grid rewards integration.","sol":"Section A, Modern Drama, carries fifteen marks for AO1 and fifteen for AO2 and no context marks at all, so the whole essay can be spent on close knowledge and on the analysis of language, form and structure. Section B, Literary Heritage Texts, divides thirty marks equally between AO1, AO2 and AO4. An identically shaped essay therefore leaves a third of the marks untouched: without reading Romeo and Juliet against the world it was written in, the answer cannot climb the context strand, which runs from little comment on the relationship between text and context up to understanding integrated convincingly into the response. The practical difference is that in Section B each developed paragraph must do three things at once — argue a point with close textual knowledge, analyse the method that makes it work, and explain why an Elizabethan or a modern audience takes it as it does. A paragraph of history bolted on at the end scores near the bottom of that strand, because the grid pays for integration, not for the presence of historical information.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Compare what the two sections pay for before you compare what you would write.","Work out what fraction of the marks a context-free essay is giving away in Section B.","Finish on the difference in paragraph construction, not on a difference between the two texts."],"codes":["Component 2: Content overview","Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many raw marks does AO4 carry in the Section B question on Romeo and Juliet?","a":"Ten of the thirty."},{"q":"What verse form does Shakespeare use for the Prologue?","a":"A sonnet."},{"q":"In which act and scene are Mercutio and Tybalt killed?","a":"Act 3 Scene 1."},{"q":"What copy of the text may a learner take into the open-book Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Star-cross'd lovers (Prologue)","back":"The Chorus calls them “A pair of star-cross'd lovers”, fixing the ending before the action begins, so every hopeful line afterwards carries dramatic irony.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The form of the Prologue","back":"A sonnet: fourteen lines of love poetry used to announce two deaths, so form and content are at odds from the first line.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The shared sonnet at the feast (Act 1 Scene 5)","back":"Romeo and Juliet's first exchange builds one sonnet between two speakers, its pilgrim and saint imagery making the meeting an act of worship.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Tybalt on peace (Act 1 Scene 1)","back":"“What, drawn, and talk of peace? I hate the word / As I hate hell, all Montagues, and thee.” Hell as the measure makes the feud faith, not reason.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Mercutio's dying curse (Act 3 Scene 1)","back":"“A plague o' both your houses!”, repeated as he dies, turns a private death into a judgement on both families and ends the play's comic movement.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"“O, I am fortune's fool!”","back":"Romeo in Act 3 Scene 1, having killed Tybalt: he calls himself fate's plaything at the exact moment his own choice has doomed him.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Capulet's threat to Juliet (Act 3 Scene 5)","back":"“hang, beg, starve, die in the streets” — four blunt verbs with no conjunctions, removing every future she might have, backed by a father's lawful control of her marriage.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false},{"front":"Friar Laurence at the marriage (Act 2 Scene 6)","back":"“These violent delights have violent ends” — he names the play's pattern, that intensity consumes itself, and then helps to speed it up.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Juliet's oxymorons (Act 3 Scene 2)","back":"“Beautiful tyrant! fiend angelical!” — contradictions that hold love and horror together after Romeo has killed her cousin.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The Nurse's advice (Act 3 Scene 5)","back":"“I think it best you married with the county”, with Romeo dismissed as a “dishclout”; Juliet's “Ancient damnation!” ends the friendship and leaves her alone.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The five categories of context (AO4)","back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; how different audiences at different times receive the text.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Ways of integrating context on Romeo and Juliet","back":"Attach it to a word, so a father's property language explains “baggage”; attach it to structure, so the compressed time-scheme reads against a first audience's assumptions about betrothal; attach it to reception, so a modern audience reads Juliet's age differently.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":true},{"front":"Marks for the Section B question","back":"Thirty marks: ten AO1, ten AO2, ten AO4, so all three strands must appear in every developed paragraph.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Open book conditions, Component 2","back":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text may be taken in, so quotation should be exact, short and chosen in advance.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment B","back":"The coursework alternative: one 30-mark teacher-devised essay on the literary heritage text, ten marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4, advisory word count 650 to 800 words.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["The question: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents Capulet's treatment of Juliet in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play.","Shakespeare makes Capulet's authority most frightening at the moment it stops being paternal and becomes proprietorial. In Act 3 Scene 5 he calls his daughter “green-sickness carrion” and “baggage”, nouns that reduce her first to diseased meat and then to luggage; because the audience has watched him indulge Romeo at his own feast, the rage reads as a man defending ownership rather than honour. The threat itself — “hang, beg, starve, die in the streets” — is four monosyllabic verbs with no conjunctions between them, an asyndetic list that removes every future Juliet might have and sounds like a door closing. Structurally the line measures a distance: in Act 1 Scene 2 the same man called her “a stranger in the world” and asked Paris to wait, so the play has carried him from protection to disposal in two acts. That an Elizabethan father could lawfully settle his daughter's marriage is exactly what gives the threat its force — Capulet is not exceeding his rights but exercising them, which is the more disturbing reading. Shakespeare therefore presents his treatment of Juliet not as an aberration of temper but as ordinary authority pushed to its logical end, and it is that ordinariness, rather than the feud, which drives her to the Friar's vial.","Why it sits at Level 5: assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style and cohesive evaluation. The evidence is discriminating — two nouns and one list, not a block quotation — the analysis moves from word to structure to the whole play, the terminology is used because the effect needs it, and the context is integrated convincingly: it explains why the line lands rather than sitting beside it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["The same question: Explore the ways in which Shakespeare presents Capulet's treatment of Juliet in Romeo and Juliet. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play.","Shakespeare presents Capulet as a father whose affection turns into threat the moment he is disobeyed. In Act 3 Scene 5 he calls Juliet “baggage”, a noun that treats his daughter as a piece of property to be moved rather than a person to be persuaded, and the insult stings more because it comes from the man who gave the feast in her honour. His threat, “hang, beg, starve, die in the streets”, is four short verbs with nothing joining them, so each one drops after the last and the line sounds like a series of doors closing on her. In Elizabethan England a father normally arranged his daughter's marriage and obedience to him was a religious duty, so an audience of the time would have heard Capulet using a right he really had, which makes Juliet's position frightening rather than merely unfair. Shakespeare therefore shows his treatment of her as ordinary anger backed by real power.","Why it sits at Level 3 and what would lift it: the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant, there is sound understanding of language (the noun and the unjoined list are analysed for their effect, not merely named), and the context is sound and tied to an audience. What keeps it below the top band is that everything happens at word and sentence level: there is no structural reading of the play, no measurement of the distance from Capulet calling Juliet a stranger in the world in Act 1 Scene 2, the terminology is avoided rather than used, the evidence is taken whole rather than selected, and the last sentence restates the point instead of evaluating it. Name the asyndetic list, add one structural comparison across the acts, and make the history explain why the threat lands rather than sit beside it, and the same material reaches Level 5."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Romeo and Juliet — William Shakespeare","items":["Characters — Romeo: son of Montague; in love with Rosaline when the play opens; kills Tybalt in Act 3 Scene 1 and is banished to Mantua; buys poison from the apothecary in Act 5 Scene 1 and dies in the Capulet tomb.","Characters — Juliet: Capulet's only surviving child — in Act 1 Scene 2 he says of her that “The earth hath swallowed all my hopes but she” and that she “hath not seen the change of fourteen years”. She marries Romeo in Act 2 Scene 6 and stabs herself with his dagger in Act 5 Scene 3.","Characters — Mercutio: kinsman of the Prince and Romeo's friend; speaks the Queen Mab speech in Act 1 Scene 4; killed by Tybalt in Act 3 Scene 1, cursing “A plague o' both your houses!”","Characters — Tybalt: Juliet's cousin, who recognises Romeo at the feast in Act 1 Scene 5 and is killed by Romeo in Act 3 Scene 1.","Characters — Benvolio: Romeo's cousin, who tries to part the opening brawl in Act 1 Scene 1 and reports the deaths to the Prince.","Characters — Friar Laurence: marries the lovers in Act 2 Scene 6, devises the sleeping-potion plan in Act 4 Scene 1, and confesses everything at the tomb in Act 5 Scene 3.","Characters — the Nurse: Juliet's confidante and messenger, who in Act 3 Scene 5 advises her to marry Paris and is called “Ancient damnation!” for it.","Characters — Capulet: Juliet's father, who restrains Tybalt at his own feast in Act 1 Scene 5 and then in Act 3 Scene 5 threatens to disown her.","Characters — Lady Capulet: proposes Paris in Act 1 Scene 3 and abandons Juliet in Act 3 Scene 5 with “Do as thou wilt, for I have done with thee.”","Characters — Paris: a kinsman of the Prince who asks Capulet for Juliet in Act 1 Scene 2 and is killed by Romeo at the tomb in Act 5 Scene 3.","Characters — Prince Escalus: ruler of Verona, who threatens death for further brawling in Act 1 Scene 1, exiles Romeo in Act 3 Scene 1, and closes the play having “lost a brace of kinsmen”.","Plot — Prologue: the Chorus's sonnet announces that a pair of star-cross'd lovers will die and that only their deaths will end their parents' rage.","Plot — Act 1 Scene 1: a servants' quarrel becomes a street brawl; the Prince threatens death for any repetition.","Plot — Act 1 Scene 2: Paris asks for Juliet's hand; Capulet counsels waiting and invites him to the feast.","Plot — Act 1 Scene 3: Lady Capulet and the Nurse put the match to Juliet, who replies “It is an honour that I dream not of.”","Plot — Act 1 Scene 4: on the way to the feast Mercutio delivers the Queen Mab speech and Romeo fears “Some consequence yet hanging in the stars”.","Plot — Act 1 Scene 5: Romeo sees Juliet, they share a sonnet and kiss; Tybalt recognises him and is restrained by Capulet; each lover then learns the other's name.","Plot — Act 2 Scene 2: the balcony scene, where they exchange vows and agree to marry.","Plot — Act 2 Scene 3: Friar Laurence agrees to the marriage, hoping it will “turn your households' rancour to pure love”.","Plot — Act 2 Scene 6: the Friar marries them, warning that “These violent delights have violent ends”.","Plot — Act 3 Scene 1: Tybalt kills Mercutio, Romeo kills Tybalt, and the Prince banishes Romeo.","Plot — Act 3 Scene 2: Juliet learns that Romeo has killed Tybalt and been banished.","Plot — Act 3 Scene 3: at the Friar's cell Romeo calls banishment worse than death.","Plot — Act 3 Scene 4: Capulet promises Juliet to Paris and fixes the wedding day.","Plot — Act 3 Scene 5: the lovers part at dawn, Capulet rages at Juliet's refusal, and the Nurse advises her to marry Paris.","Plot — Act 4 Scene 1: Juliet goes to Friar Laurence, who gives her the sleeping potion.","Plot — Act 4 Scene 3: Juliet drinks it alone, saying “My dismal scene I needs must act alone.”","Plot — Act 4 Scene 5: the household finds her apparently dead and the wedding preparations become a funeral.","Plot — Act 5 Scene 1: Balthasar brings Romeo the news in Mantua; Romeo buys poison from an apothecary who says “My poverty, but not my will, consents.”","Plot — Act 5 Scene 2: Friar John explains that the letter never reached Romeo, because he was shut up in a house suspected of plague.","Plot — Act 5 Scene 3: Romeo kills Paris at the tomb and drinks the poison; Juliet wakes and stabs herself; the Friar confesses, and Capulet and Montague are reconciled over the bodies.","Theme — love and hate held together: Romeo's oxymorons “O brawling love! O loving hate!” in Act 1 Scene 1 and Juliet's “My only love sprung from my only hate!” in Act 1 Scene 5.","Theme — fate: the Prologue's star-cross'd lovers, Romeo's “O, I am fortune's fool!” in Act 3 Scene 1 and his “Then I defy you, stars!” in Act 5 Scene 1.","Theme — haste: the Friar's “Wisely and slow; they stumble that run fast” in Act 2 Scene 3 and Juliet's own warning at the balcony, “It is too rash, too unadvis'd, too sudden.”","Theme — authority and obedience: a father's control of his daughter's marriage, tested to destruction in Act 3 Scene 5.","Theme — light and dark: Romeo's “she doth teach the torches to burn bright” in Act 1 Scene 5, and the lovers' meetings by night against the daylight of the feud.","Form — a five-act tragedy framed by sonnets: the Chorus speaks a sonnet Prologue, and the lovers' first exchange is itself a sonnet.","Form — blank verse for the lovers and the nobility, prose for the servants and for much of Mercutio's and the Nurse's comedy, so a change of register marks a change of world.","Structure — the hinge is Act 3 Scene 1: before it the play moves like a comedy towards marriage, after it every action moves towards the tomb.","Structure — dramatic irony is installed by the Prologue, and the compressed time-scheme carries the lovers from first meeting to death within days.","Context — the source was Arthur Brooke's narrative poem The Tragicall Historye of Romeus and Juliet, published in 1562.","Context — the play was printed in quarto in 1597 and in a longer second quarto in 1599.","Context — in Elizabethan England a daughter's marriage was normally arranged by her father, and obedience to him was a religious as well as a social duty.","Context — an honour culture in which insults had to be answered made duelling a matter of family reputation, which is what Tybalt enforces and Romeo cannot ignore.","Context — the setting is Catholic Italy: confession, a friar, a cell and the language of pilgrimage would read as foreign to a Protestant English audience.","Context — plague was a fact of Elizabethan life and closed the London theatres; in the play it is plague that detains Friar John and stops the letter reaching Romeo.","Context — women's parts were played by boys in an all-male company, so Juliet was first performed by a boy actor.","Context — belief in astrology gave star-cross'd a literal force for the first audiences that it does not carry now.","Context — reception: a twenty-first-century audience, holding Juliet's age in mind, tends to read the adults' conduct as coercive where earlier audiences saw paternal right.","Key moments an examiner rewards: the Prologue; the brawl and the Prince's warning; the shared sonnet and Tybalt's recognition at the feast; the balcony vows; the marriage warning; the deaths of Mercutio and Tybalt; the dawn parting and Capulet's threat; the potion soliloquy; the apothecary; the tomb and the Prince's “All are punish'd.”"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the prescribed edition and the open-book examination","items":["Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners or centres must purchase a prescribed edition of each set text at the start of the course.","The copy taken into the examination room must be clean and completely unmarked: no annotation, underlining or inserted notes.","Appendix 3 prescribes, for Romeo and Juliet: OUP Oxford, 1 Edition, September 2008, ISBN 9780198321668.","Appendix 3 also prescribes: Heinemann, March 2010, ISBN 9780435026493.","Appendix 3 also prescribes: Pearson Education, May 2023, ISBN 9781292461168.","Appendix 3 also prescribes: CUP Cambridge, January 2014, ISBN 9781107615403.","Because the text is in front of you, quotations must be exact and short; copying long passages spends the time the argument needs.","Component 2 lasts one hour thirty minutes and is worth 60 raw marks, 40 per cent of the International GCSE, across one Section A essay and one Section B essay."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — In what ways does Shakespeare present conflict in Romeo and Juliet? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","items":["Decoded: in what ways asks for Shakespeare's methods, not for the story; conflict covers the public feud, the duel code, Capulet against his daughter and the conflict inside each lover; the closing instruction carries the AO2 and AO4 marks, so every paragraph must analyse a method and read the play against the world it was written in.","Introduction: state the argument in one sentence — Shakespeare presents conflict first as an inherited public language, then drives it indoors until it destroys the family from inside.","Paragraph 1, the public feud: Act 1 Scene 1 begins in servants' prose with a quarrel about a thumb, so the cause is already forgotten comedy; Tybalt lifts it into verse hatred, “I hate the word, / As I hate hell, all Montagues, and thee”; the Prince's threat establishes a law that the play then watches fail.","Paragraph 2, the duel code: Act 3 Scene 1 is the structural hinge; Romeo's refusal to fight reads as dishonour, Mercutio dies under his arm, and “A plague o' both your houses!” turns a private death into judgement — the honour culture of Shakespeare's own society is what makes restraint impossible.","Paragraph 3, conflict inside the house: Act 3 Scene 5 moves the violence indoors, where “hang, beg, starve, die in the streets” uses a father's lawful power over his daughter's marriage as a weapon, and the Nurse's defection leaves Juliet with no ally.","Paragraph 4, conflict inside the self: Romeo's oxymorons “O brawling love! O loving hate!” and Juliet's “Beautiful tyrant! fiend angelical!” put the feud inside the lovers' own language, so it is not something they could escape by leaving Verona.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: which conflict does Shakespeare make the most destructive? Argue that the public feud kills fewer people than the ordinary authority exercised inside the Capulet house.","Conclusion must say: a qualified judgement that answers the question — Shakespeare presents conflict as a language inherited by children who did not choose it, and the reconciliation of the final scene is bought at a price the play refuses to call worth paying."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:09:46Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All three fixes have landed and are right: questions[7] now says the Nurse’s Act 1 Scene 3 recollection is “verse that behaves as though it were not” (confirmed — “Even or odd, of all days in the year, / Come Lammas-eve at night shall she be fourteen” is verse, as is her Act 3 Scene 5 counsel), its `a` now names repetition, digression and an earthy register rather than prose, and the surviving argument — shapelessness against Juliet’s compression, the wet nurse’s place in the household, the reversal at “I think it best you married with the county” and the “dishclout” — is sound; cards[11] is back to 40 words and still teaches three real ways of integrating context; and examples[1] and examples[3] now state in the setup the two facts their third steps and cues turn on (a father’s arrangement of his daughter’s marriage; the duty to answer a challenge), so nothing is asked that the problem has not supplied. I re-solved all thirteen questions and found no answer key wrong, ambiguous or at odds with its solution; the tariffs, the board’s command words and closing instruction, the 10/10/10 split, the 15/15 Section A comparison in questions[12], the five level bands and the 650–800 advisory count all check out against the specification map. I spot-checked 22 of the quoted spans against the text — the shared sonnet in Act 1 Scene 5, Mercutio’s wound, joke and curse and the Prince’s closing “Mercy but murders” in Act 3 Scene 1, “O, I am fortune’s fool!” as Romeo’s last line there, the whole of the Act 3 Scene 5 sequence, the balcony lines, “These violent delights have violent ends”, “My dismal scene I needs must act alone”, “Then I defy you, stars!”, the apothecary, and the tomb speeches — and every one is verbatim and correctly placed by act and scene. What is left is one inaccurate clause in the Level 3 paragraph’s self-critique; the kit is otherwise publishable."}}},"heritage-macbeth":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-macbeth","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Macbeth is one of the literary heritage set texts for Component 2, Section B. You answer one 30-mark essay question chosen from two, and it carries 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4 — the three objectives weigh equally, so a paragraph that knows the play but never analyses a method, or never reads the play against the world it was written for, throws away a third of its marks every time. The examination is open book: you may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition, which raises the standard of quotation an examiner expects rather than lowering it. This room drills knowledge, analysis and context together, because that is how the question is marked.","idea":[{"h":"Macbeth as your literary heritage set text","t":"For Component 2 you study two set texts: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. Macbeth is one of the heritage choices, and the specification expects the whole play to be studied, not a handful of extracts. The content overview asks you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. A top-level answer therefore treats the play as one designed whole: it can reach for Act 1 and Act 5 inside the same paragraph and say what Shakespeare is doing across the arc, not only in the scene in front of it.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"Close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","t":"AO1 is worth 10 of the 30 marks. You must demonstrate a close understanding of the heritage text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples. Close knowledge means you can name the moment, the speaker and what has just happened; it is not retelling the plot. A critical style means the sentence argues rather than narrates: “Shakespeare makes Macbeth hesitate” rather than “then Macbeth thinks about it”. A top-level answer opens every paragraph with a claim about the play and uses a chosen example as proof of that claim, never as a substitute for it.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"},{"h":"How language, form and structure create effects","t":"AO2 is the next 10 marks and the one most often thrown away. You must analyse the language, form and structure a writer uses to create meanings and effects, understand how writers create literary effects, and use appropriate literary terminology. Naming a device scores almost nothing: the grid runs from minimal identification at Level 1, through sound understanding at Level 3 and sustained analysis at Level 4, to cohesive evaluation at Level 5. Form in Macbeth means soliloquy and aside, blank verse set against the witches’ shorter rhymed chant, and prose when a mind gives way. A top-level answer says what the method does to the audience, and then what it does to the argument.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"},{"h":"Reading Macbeth against the world it was written for","t":"AO4 is the last 10 marks, and Section B is the only part of Component 2 that carries it: you must show understanding of the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. The grid is explicit that at the top level this understanding is integrated convincingly into the response, while a paragraph of bolted-on history sits at Level 1 or Level 2. A top-level answer uses context to change how a line is read: a Jacobean horror of regicide is why an audience flinches at a sleeping king’s blood, not a fact to be recited afterwards.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","t":"The specification names five categories: the writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, such as movements or genres; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from when it was written. For Macbeth that gives you Jacobean kingship and witchcraft, a medieval Scottish setting, the conventions of tragedy, and a modern reading of Lady Macbeth. A top-level answer chooses the category the question actually needs and leaves the rest out.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"What the Macbeth question looks like on the day","t":"Section B is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4; in the breakdown of raw marks these are Questions 11 to 22, carrying 10 marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4. Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks and 40% of the International GCSE, so Macbeth is worth roughly 45 minutes. It is open book, and you may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition. A top-level answer uses that copy to quote precisely and briefly and spends the time on argument, because the grid rewards discriminating use of relevant examples, not the longest quotation on the page.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same literary heritage list. Assignment B is one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, and it must allow you to access all three. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, with no penalty for exceeding it, and the work is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. The model wordings run “How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.” A top-level coursework answer does exactly what the examination answer does, only tighter: knowledge, method and context in every paragraph.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a quotation into an AO2 move","setup":"You are answering: How does Shakespeare present Macbeth’s ambition in the play? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. The moment you have chosen is Macbeth’s aside in Act 1 Scene 3, spoken after Ross and Angus have told him he is Thane of Cawdor, while Banquo watches him: “This supernatural soliciting / Cannot be ill; cannot be good.” Write the analytical move that earns AO2 credit for this moment.","steps":["Take the form first: an aside hands the thought to the audience and not to the men beside him, so the loyal soldier and the calculating man are already two people.","Lift the smallest piece that carries the meaning — “Cannot be ill; cannot be good” — rather than the whole sentence.","Analyse the shape: the two halves repeat one construction with the moral terms swapped, so the verse itself will not fall on either side.","Push it further: the paired negatives cancel, which means Macbeth can neither refuse the prophecy nor accept it, and the audience hears a man reasoning where it expected a man recoiling.","Link and judge: Shakespeare presents the ambition as a thought Macbeth cannot put down, making the corruption inward from the first act rather than a decision reached later."],"cues":["Who on stage hears this, and who does not?","Which few words would you lift, and no more?","What do you notice about the shape of the two halves?","What do two negatives side by side do to the argument?","So how is the ambition being presented at this point?"]},{"title":"Integrating context instead of bolting it on","setup":"You are answering: How does Shakespeare portray kingship in Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. The moment is the show of eight kings that the witches conjure for Macbeth in Act 4 Scene 1, the last of them carrying a glass, with Banquo’s ghost following and pointing at them as his. The context available to you: the play was written for the Jacobean stage under James I, who came to the English throne in 1603 and whose patronage made Shakespeare’s company the King’s Men. Write the sentence that earns AO4 credit rather than losing it.","steps":["Begin with the stagecraft, not the history: this is a silent procession, so the future is shown to Macbeth as something he can watch but not enter or stop.","Read the glass: the mirror extends the line past the edge of the stage, so Banquo’s descendants outlast even the vision of them.","Now fold the context into the same sentence, not a later one: for a Jacobean audience that line runs forward out of the play towards the king in whose reign it was staged, so the image flatters the present dynasty while it punishes Macbeth.","Say what that does to the theme: kingship is presented as continuation through a line, which is precisely what Macbeth has destroyed in himself by having no heir.","Check the join: the context has altered how the image reads rather than being added after it, which is what integrated convincingly means."],"cues":["What does a silent procession do that a speech cannot?","Why give the last king a mirror rather than a crown?","Who in the theatre is standing at the end of that line?","What kind of kingship does an endless line define?","How can you tell the context is integrated rather than added?"]},{"title":"Building a Level 5 paragraph on Lady Macbeth","setup":"The question is: To what extent do you agree that Shakespeare presents Lady Macbeth as the stronger of the two? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. You have two moments in front of you: her invocation in Act 1 Scene 5, “Come, you spirits / That tend on mortal thoughts, unsex me here”; and her sleepwalking in Act 5 Scene 1, “Out, damned spot! out, I say!”, watched by a doctor and a gentlewoman. Build one paragraph that weighs the proposition.","steps":["Point: her strength is presented from the outset as something requested rather than possessed, so the play makes it borrowed.","Evidence and method: “unsex me here” is an imperative addressed to spirits, and it asks for the removal of something rather than the addition of anything.","Analyse the form: she speaks it in blank verse, alone, taking the soliloquy position the play has just been giving Macbeth, so an audience reads her as the one driving the action.","Turn on the structure: by the sleepwalking she has lost verse altogether and speaks broken prose, and the same grammar of command returns in “Out, damned spot! out, I say!”, now aimed at a stain that is not there.","Judgement: she is the stronger only while the borrowed will holds, so Shakespeare uses the two scenes to qualify the proposition rather than to confirm or refuse it."],"cues":["Is strength here something she has or something she asks for?","What kind of sentence is “unsex me here”, and what does it request?","Whose speech position has the play just been using for Macbeth?","What has changed in the verse form by the later scene?","So how far does the proposition actually hold?"]},{"title":"Making structure the argument: the banquet","setup":"The question is: In what ways does Shakespeare present Macbeth’s decline? You must consider language, form and structure. You are working on Act 3 Scene 4, the banquet: Macbeth is to take his place as king, Banquo’s ghost occupies the seat, Macbeth speaks to it — “Thou canst not say I did it. Never shake / Thy gory locks at me.” — the guests see nothing, and Lady Macbeth ends the feast with “Stand not upon the order of your going, / But go at once.” Work out the structural argument.","steps":["Place the scene in the arc: it sits at the centre of the play, and Macbeth never again appears among his assembled thanes.","Read the stage picture: the one seat he committed murder to occupy is the one seat he cannot take, so the staging argues against him before he speaks.","Read the speech: he addresses a figure only he can see, so his own language separates him from the room and the dramatic irony now runs against him.","Read the exit: the order of going is exactly the order of rank a king’s feast exists to display, and his wife has to cancel it.","Judgement: Shakespeare stages the decline as a collapse of ceremony, which is why the play gives him isolation from here on instead of company."],"cues":["Where does this scene fall in the shape of the play?","Which chair is the problem, and why that one?","Who can hear what Macbeth is answering?","What is being cancelled when the order of going is cancelled?","So what has actually declined — the man, or his world?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions in Section B","setup":"In Section B you meet two Macbeth questions and answer one, with roughly forty-five minutes for it, open book. Question A: How does Shakespeare present the supernatural in Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. Question B: Shakespeare shows that Macbeth is destroyed by his own conscience rather than by the witches. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. Decide which to take, and plan its first three paragraphs.","steps":["Test what each stem demands: the first asks for methods across the play; the second puts a proposition in front of you that has to be weighed in every paragraph.","Test your own material: the second needs the soliloquies and the witches’ scenes, and it needs you to be able to argue both sides of it.","Take the second, because a proposition lets the evaluation thread do the structuring, and the highest level rewards a perceptive critical style rather than coverage.","Paragraph one: conscience is active before any prophecy is obeyed — Macbeth follows a dagger he himself calls a creation of his own brain.","Paragraph two: the witches never tell him to kill anyone — the orders they do give in Act 4 Scene 1 govern only how he is to listen, hear the apparitions and say nothing — and the promise about Birnam wood destroys him by being true in a way he refuses to hear.","Paragraph three: the counter-case that strengthens the proposition — Lady Macbeth is destroyed by conscience alone, having had no dealings with the witches at all."],"cues":["Which stem forces a judgement rather than a survey?","Which of the two can you argue in both directions?","Which choice lets the structure write itself?","What is Macbeth reacting to before the crown is even taken?","What exactly do the witches order Macbeth to do, and what do they never order?","Who in the play is ruined without ever meeting them?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Macbeth question carry, and how are they divided?","a":"30 marks: 10 for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4."},{"q":"What may you take into the Component 2 examination room?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text."},{"q":"What does the top level of the grid require of context?","a":"That understanding of the relationship between text and context is integrated convincingly into the response."},{"q":"Which section of Component 2 carries AO4?","a":"Section B, Literary Heritage Texts, only."},{"q":"Why does naming a technique earn little AO2 credit?","a":"Because the grid moves from minimal identification at Level 1 to sustained analysis and cohesive evaluation at the top, so the effect must be traced, not labelled."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section B: one 30-mark essay on Macbeth from a choice of two, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4 at 10 marks each; Questions 11 to 22 in the breakdown of raw marks.","Component 2 is a 1 hour 30 minute examination worth 60 marks and 40% of the International GCSE; it is open book, and learners may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition.","AO1: demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.","AO2: analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.","AO4: show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.","The five levels run 1–6, 7–12, 13–18, 19–24 and 25–30, awarded on a best fit basis; Level 5 requires assured knowledge, a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure, discriminating use of relevant examples, and context integrated convincingly.","The five categories of context the specification names: the writer’s own life and situation; the historical setting of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and how texts are received by different audiences at different times.","Shape of the play: the witches in Act 1 Scene 1 and the prophecies in Act 1 Scene 3; Duncan’s murder in Act 2; Banquo’s murder and the banquet in Act 3; the apparitions in Act 4 Scene 1; the sleepwalking in Act 5 Scene 1 and Macbeth’s death in Act 5.","Context to have ready: Macbeth was written for the Jacobean stage in the reign of James I, whose patronage made Shakespeare’s company the King’s Men in 1603; James had written Daemonologie (1597) on witchcraft; the Gunpowder Plot of November 1605 and the notoriety of equivocation stand behind the Porter’s speech; the Scottish material comes from Holinshed’s Chronicles; the play survives first in the First Folio of 1623."],"questions":[{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: state the three things the witches tell Macbeth in Act 1 Scene 3, and the three things they tell Banquo. (6 marks)","a":"Macbeth: Thane of Glamis, Thane of Cawdor, and king hereafter. Banquo: lesser than Macbeth and greater, not so happy yet much happier, and that he shall get kings though he be none.","sol":"They hail Macbeth three times: as Thane of Glamis, which he already is; as Thane of Cawdor, which he does not yet know he has been given; and as one “that shalt be king hereafter”. To Banquo they speak in paradox: “Lesser than Macbeth, and greater. / Not so happy, yet much happier. / Thou shalt get kings, though thou be none.” The pattern matters as much as the content — Macbeth is given a ladder of titles he can climb, while Banquo is given a set of contradictions he cannot act on, which is exactly why one man moves and the other waits.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Two men are addressed, and each is addressed three times.","For Macbeth, work through the titles in the order the sisters use them; for Banquo, notice that every line pairs an opposite.","Set the answer out as two short lists, Macbeth first, then Banquo, keeping the order of the scene."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: name the three apparitions the witches show Macbeth in Act 4 Scene 1 and say what each of them tells him. (6 marks)","a":"An armed head warns him to beware Macduff; a bloody child says none of woman born shall harm Macbeth; a child crowned, with a tree in his hand, says he shall not be vanquished until Birnam wood comes to Dunsinane.","sol":"The first apparition is an armed head, which warns him to beware Macduff. The second is a bloody child, which tells him that “none of woman born / Shall harm Macbeth”. The third is a child crowned, with a tree in his hand, which promises that “Macbeth shall never vanquish’d be until / Great Birnam wood to high Dunsinane hill / Shall come against him.” Each image encodes its own undoing: the armed head anticipates his own severed head, the bloody child is Macduff as he was delivered, and the crowned child with the bough is Malcolm’s army under its branches. Macbeth hears reassurance where the staging offers him prophecy of his death.","m":6,"d":1,"hints":["Each apparition is an image before it is a message — picture what walks on.","Take them in the order they appear, and pair each image with the promise it makes.","Give three lines, one apparition and one promise each."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: set out how the 30 marks for the Macbeth question are divided between the assessment objectives, and state what you may take into the examination room. (4 marks)","a":"10 marks AO1, 10 marks AO2 and 10 marks AO4; a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in.","sol":"Section B of Component 2 is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, carrying 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, so the three objectives weigh exactly equally. Because the component is open book, learners may take a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room. The practical consequence is that an answer which is strong on knowledge but silent on method, or strong on both but silent on context, has capped itself at two thirds of the marks before the examiner reaches the conclusion.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The assessment overview for Section B names the objectives this question assesses; the breakdown table turns those objectives into raw marks.","Think about what the word “clean” is doing in the specification’s permission.","Answer in two parts: the mark split, then the permitted book and its condition."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: list the five categories of context the specification names, and attach one piece of Macbeth material to each. (5 marks)","a":"Writer’s life and time of writing (Jacobean stage under James I); historical setting (medieval Scotland from Holinshed); social and cultural attitudes (kingship and the natural order); literary context (tragedy and the supernatural on the popular stage); reception over time (modern readings of Lady Macbeth).","sol":"The specification names the writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, where it relates to the text: Macbeth was written for the Jacobean stage under James I, whose patronage made Shakespeare’s company the King’s Men. The historical setting, time and location of the text: a medieval Scottish kingdom taken from Holinshed’s Chronicles. Social and cultural contexts: a society that understood kingship as divinely sanctioned and nature as ordered by hierarchy, which is why regicide is answered by portents. The literary context: tragedy, in which a great figure falls by his own act, and a popular stage with an appetite for apparitions and shows. And the way texts are received by different audiences at different times: Lady Macbeth is read very differently in the twenty-first century from the way earlier audiences read her.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The specification groups context into kinds, not into facts — recover the kinds first.","Two of the five are about when the play was written and when it is set; those are not the same thing.","Give five short lines, each naming the category and then one example."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: explain the difference between identifying a technique and analysing it, using Lady Macbeth’s instruction “Look like the innocent flower, / But be the serpent under’t.” (4 marks)","a":"Identifying names the device; analysing traces what it does to meaning and to the audience, then links it back to the argument.","sol":"Identifying stops at the label: this is a metaphor, or an antithesis. Analysing asks what the language is made to do. The two images are not simply opposites but a cover and a thing covered, so the sentence itself has a surface and an underside, which is the deception it is recommending. The imperative form matters too: she is not describing Macbeth, she is building him, which makes the line a piece of instruction rather than observation. And the placement matters: an audience hears this before any murder, so every later scene of courtesy is watched through it. That movement from device, to effect, to what it does to the play is what the grid calls sustained analysis rather than minimal identification.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Start from what a label alone leaves the reader still not knowing.","Ask what the shape of the line does, and who is being instructed by it.","Write it as a contrast: one sentence on the weaker move, then two or three on the stronger one."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare present Macbeth’s ambition in the play? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Ambition arrives as thought before action (asides and soliloquies in Act 1), is argued against and acted on anyway, becomes self-sustaining after the crown, and is finally emptied in Act 5; context of Jacobean kingship makes it an injury to natural order.","sol":"Shakespeare presents ambition less as a quality Macbeth possesses than as a thought he proves unable to stop thinking, and the play is built around the gap between wanting and doing. It reaches the audience first as language: the aside in Act 1 Scene 3, where the supernatural soliciting “Cannot be ill; cannot be good”, balances its halves so exactly that the verse refuses to fall either way, and the form — heard by us, not by Banquo — splits the loyal soldier from the calculating man. By Act 1 Scene 4 the ambition has a grammar of concealment: “Stars, hide your fires”. The Act 1 Scene 7 soliloquy is the pivot, because Macbeth argues himself out of the murder and names the only motive left as “Vaulting ambition, which o’erleaps itself”; the riding image carries the fall inside the desire, so the audience is given the ending at the moment of decision. What Shakespeare withholds is the will, which Lady Macbeth must supply by attacking his manhood, making ambition in this play something that needs a second voice. After the crown the structure changes: from Act 3 Scene 1, where “To be thus is nothing, / But to be safely thus”, ambition becomes maintenance, and by the banquet he is in blood stepped so far that “Returning were as tedious as go o’er” — it has become momentum and no longer needs persuading. For a Jacobean audience, under a king who claimed a sacred office and had survived the Gunpowder Plot, such reaching is an injury to the order of nature, which is why the play answers it with darkness at noon and murdered sleep rather than only with an army. The tomorrow speech in Act 5 Scene 5 completes the design: a man who has everything he wanted calls life “a tale / Told by an idiot”. Shakespeare therefore presents ambition as self-consuming, and locates the tragedy in a man who never once stops knowing what he is doing.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question says “how”, so the subject of your sentences should be Shakespeare, not Macbeth.","Track the ambition through the forms it is given — aside, soliloquy, dialogue with his wife — rather than through the plot.","Plan four or five paragraphs that move across the whole arc, and decide before you start whether the ambition is his own or supplied to him."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Shakespeare present the relationship between Macbeth and Lady Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"A partnership of shared language that inverts and then dissolves: she supplies his will in Act 1, they separate after the banquet in Act 3, and each dies alone in Act 5; Jacobean expectations of wife and husband sharpen the inversion.","sol":"Shakespeare presents the marriage as the most intimate relationship in the tragedy and then dismantles it in front of the audience. In Act 1 Scene 5 the letter makes the relationship textual before it is physical: he writes to “my dearest partner of greatness”, and she answers a letter, not a man, which establishes a union of purpose rather than affection. The inversion is built through form as much as content: she is given the soliloquy position, and her imperatives — “unsex me here”, “Look like the innocent flower, / But be the serpent under’t” — put the commands in her mouth while he is left with questions. For a Jacobean audience, in which a wife was expected to submit rather than to direct, this reversal is itself a disorder, of a piece with the disorder in the kingdom, and Shakespeare makes the two rhyme. Structurally the partnership peaks at the murder, where his “Will all great Neptune’s ocean wash this blood / Clean from my hand?” is answered by her flat “A little water clears us of this deed”: the same event in two registers, hyperbole against plain statement. After that the play separates them deliberately. Banquo’s murder is arranged without her, and she is told to “Be innocent of the knowledge, dearest chuck”, an endearment that is also an exclusion. At the banquet she covers for him one last time; after Act 3 they never share the stage. The final pairing is structural rather than physical: she washes clean hands in prose in Act 5 Scene 1, the hyperbole now hers — “All the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this little hand” — while his response to her death is not grief but the tomorrow speech. Shakespeare therefore presents a relationship whose strength was entirely the strength of a shared project, and which has nothing left once the project succeeds.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The word “ways” is asking for methods, so plan by technique and structure rather than by scene order.","Look for moments where one of them takes the speech position the other has been using.","Build the argument on the arc from union to separation, and let the last paragraph handle her death and his answer to it."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare present the supernatural in Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"The supernatural is given its own verse and its own stagecraft, tells truths that mislead, and is deliberately left ambiguous as cause; Jacobean witchcraft belief, including James I’s Daemonologie, makes it real for the first audience.","sol":"Shakespeare presents the supernatural as something that is unmistakably real on stage and entirely undecidable as a cause, and the play’s power comes from holding both. The witches are marked off by sound before they are marked off by action: they speak a shorter, chanting, heavily rhymed line against the blank verse of the nobles, and their opening paradox, “Fair is foul, and foul is fair”, is handed to Macbeth in his first line without his knowing he is quoting it, so the play establishes their reach before it establishes their power. Structurally, they open the play and are given the whole of Act 4 Scene 1, yet nothing they say to him is an order to kill: the imperatives they do use govern only how he listens and how he holds himself — hear the apparitions and say nothing, be bold and resolute — while the murders are announced as facts to come and Macbeth supplies the action. This is the crux of the question, and a strong answer argues it rather than describing the scenes — the prophecy about Cawdor comes true without Macbeth lifting a hand, while the crown requires a murder that nobody ever tells him to commit. The apparitions work by equivocation: “none of woman born” and the moving wood are literally true and practically fatal, and Shakespeare had a first audience for whom equivocation was a live public scandal after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 and the trials that followed. That same audience had a king who had published Daemonologie on witchcraft, which makes the sisters a matter of belief rather than decoration, and the show of eight kings in Act 4 Scene 1 flatters the line he was held to descend from. The dagger complicates the picture further, because Macbeth himself calls it a creation of his own brain, so the play offers a psychological reading alongside the supernatural one. Shakespeare therefore presents the supernatural as a genuine force in the world of the play that is nevertheless never allowed to excuse anybody, which is precisely what makes Macbeth responsible.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Separate two questions the play keeps apart: are the witches real, and do they cause anything?","Listen to the metre and rhyme they are given, and ask what that does before any content is heard.","Set up the essay so that each paragraph tests the witches’ power and the last one settles how far it reaches."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare portray kingship in Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Kingship is defined by contrast: Duncan’s ceremony and generosity, Edward’s healing touch in Act 4 Scene 3, and Malcolm’s restored titles set against Macbeth’s isolated tyranny; Jacobean divine right and the Stuart line make the contrast urgent.","sol":"Shakespeare portrays kingship comparatively: no speech defines it, but four kings are placed against each other so the audience arrives at the definition itself. Duncan is characterised almost entirely through ceremony and giving — he creates thanes, he distributes thanks, and he walks into Inverness praising its “pleasant seat”, a line whose irony depends on his trust being a virtue rather than a fault. Macbeth’s kingship is the negative of this: the crown gains him no ceremony at all, because the one court scene he holds, the banquet of Act 3 Scene 4, collapses into a ghost and an undignified dismissal, and thereafter he is shown among servants and soldiers rather than thanes. Angus catches the whole argument in a single image when he says Macbeth feels his title “Hang loose about him, like a giant’s robe / Upon a dwarfish thief”: kingship is a garment that must fit. Against the tyrant Shakespeare sets Edward of England in Act 4 Scene 3, whose touch heals the sick, an explicitly sacramental kingship that a Jacobean audience would connect to the royal touch practised by their own monarchs, and which the play uses to say that legitimate rule is a source of health rather than of blood. Structurally the point is completed by the show of eight kings in Act 4 Scene 1, which runs Banquo’s line forward past the edge of the stage towards the dynasty ruling when the play was written, so that continuity, not seizure, defines a true king. The last confirmation is linguistic: Malcolm’s closing speech restores the vocabulary of title and reward that Duncan used, and invites everyone to see him crowned at Scone. Shakespeare therefore portrays kingship as a role sustained by ceremony, lineage and consent, which is exactly why Macbeth, who has taken the name without any of them, cannot hold it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Count how many kings the play puts in front of the audience, including the ones who never speak.","Watch what each king does with ceremony, titles and other people’s bodies.","Organise by contrast rather than by chronology, and let the context do its work inside the paragraph on the line of kings."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Shakespeare makes guilt more destructive than any enemy in Macbeth. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Largely agree: guilt is staged through blood, sleep and hallucination and destroys both Macbeths before Malcolm’s army arrives; but the play needs an external judgement too, so the agreement must be qualified.","sol":"The proposition is close to right, and the essay’s value lies in the qualification. Guilt is the only force in the play that reaches Macbeth before an army does, and Shakespeare gives it a physical vocabulary so that it can be staged. Immediately after the murder in Act 2 Scene 2 Macbeth asks whether “all great Neptune’s ocean” will wash the blood clean from his hand, and answers himself that his hand will instead turn the seas red; the hyperbole is designed to fail, since he reaches for the largest body of water language offers and the sentence changes its colour, so the grammar itself refuses him absolution. The same scene gives guilt its second and more original image, the voice that cries “Macbeth does murder sleep”, which turns a crime into a permanent condition rather than an event. To a Jacobean audience, for whom a king’s person was sacred, blood that cannot be washed away is not a stain but damnation, and Shakespeare places that recognition in the same breath as the deed. The structural proof is Lady Macbeth, who has no enemy at all: she dissolves from the woman who said “A little water clears us of this deed” into a sleepwalker washing clean hands in prose, and her death is reported rather than shown, as if guilt had already removed her from the play. Against the proposition, Macduff is a real antagonist and does kill Macbeth, and the play plainly wants order restored by human action. But by Act 5 Scene 5 guilt has already hollowed its victim, so that a man with everything he sought calls life “a tale / Told by an idiot”, and the sword only finishes the work. I therefore agree substantially: Shakespeare makes conscience the true antagonist of a man who, by the end, has no other one left.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["A proposition question needs a position in the first paragraph and a test of it in every paragraph after.","Find where the play stages guilt rather than states it — hands, sleep, hallucination, prose.","Decide in advance what your counter-case is, and make sure the conclusion qualifies rather than repeats."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Macbeth may be read very differently in the twenty-first century from the way it was read when it was written. To what extent do you agree, with close reference to Lady Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Agree in substance: a Jacobean reading of Lady Macbeth as unnatural and demonic gives way to a modern reading of a woman with no route to power except through a husband; but the text sustains both, which is the qualification.","sol":"The specification asks explicitly about the way texts are received by different audiences at different times, and Lady Macbeth is the sharpest case in the play. To a Jacobean audience her invocation in Act 1 Scene 5 — “Come, you spirits / That tend on mortal thoughts, unsex me here” — is a request addressed to spirits, in a decade when the reigning king had published Daemonologie on witchcraft, and so reads as a woman deliberately stepping outside both her sex and her species; her attack on her husband’s manhood in Act 1 Scene 7, and the claim that she would have dashed out the brains of the child she had given suck to, confirm an unnatural inversion of wife and mother. The play’s own last word encourages that reading: Malcolm calls her a “fiend-like queen”. A twenty-first-century audience is far likelier to notice that the invocation is a request, not a power, and that what she asks for is the removal of feeling rather than the gift of force — which makes her ambition the only channel available to a woman in a world where thaneships and crowns are given to men. On that reading her collapse in Act 5 Scene 1, in prose, washing hands that are clean, is not punishment but the return of what she asked to have taken away. The qualification matters: Shakespeare did not write a feminist reading, and the text supports the older one too, which is why her last scene is watched by others rather than spoken alone, and why she dies offstage. I therefore agree substantially. The lines have not changed, but what an audience counts as monstrous has, and the specification’s point is exactly that: context governs interpretation, so the play is read differently without being rewritten.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The question is about audiences, not only about the character — you need two readings, not one.","Take a single moment and ask what a Jacobean spectator and a modern spectator each notice first in it.","Structure it as reading one, reading two, then the limits of the modern reading, and judge at the end."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare present the change in Macbeth’s mind between Act 1 and Act 5? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"In Act 1 the private forms — aside and soliloquy — give a mind arguing with itself in dense moral imagery; by Act 5 the arguing has stopped, the imagery has flattened into theatre and the private voice is overheard in company, so the change is presented as a loss of inner life rather than a change of opinion, which a Jacobean audience would read as the state of a man beyond grace.","sol":"Shakespeare presents the change less as a change of view than as the emptying of a mind the audience has been given privileged access to, and he manages it by changing what Macbeth’s private speech is for and who is standing near him when he makes it. In Act 1 that speech is a courtroom. The Act 1 Scene 7 soliloquy opens by conceding the danger of acting — “If it were done when ’tis done, then ’twere well / It were done quickly” — and then argues steadily against the murder on grounds of kinship, hospitality and Duncan’s virtues, before admitting that nothing is left to drive him but “Vaulting ambition, which o’erleaps itself”. The syntax is conditional and heavily subordinated, clause qualifying clause, which is the sound of a conscience at work, and the imagery is moral and generative: pity as a naked new-born babe, ambition as a rider who overleaps himself. The aside in Act 1 Scene 3 does the same work in miniature — “Cannot be ill; cannot be good” balances its halves so exactly that the verse will not fall on either side — and both forms hand the thought to the audience and not to the men beside him, so from the start we know this mind better than anyone on stage does. The middle of the play shows the reasoning surviving while its subject narrows: in Act 3 Scene 1 “To be thus is nothing, / But to be safely thus” is still an argument, but it is an argument about security rather than about right, and the moral vocabulary has already gone. By Act 5 the form itself has been taken away from him. Act 5 Scene 5 does not give him a soliloquy: he enters with drum and colours, Seyton and soldiers are on stage with him, and it is Seyton’s report of the queen’s death that prompts the famous speech, which he delivers largely to himself in company rather than alone. That staging is part of the argument — where Act 1 gave him solitude to think in, Act 5 gives him attendants and armour, and the private voice now has to be overheard. What it says has lost its reasoning: “Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow” repeats one word three times where Act 1 built a chain of clauses, and the images have flattened into theatre — a brief candle, a walking shadow, a poor player — before arriving at “a tale / Told by an idiot, full of sound and fury, / Signifying nothing”. Where the earlier speech weighed consequences, this one denies that consequence exists. The context makes that emptiness legible rather than merely bleak: for a Jacobean audience, conscience was the ordinary route to repentance, and Shakespeare denies Macbeth that route, answering the murder from Act 2 Scene 2 onwards with the voice crying that “Macbeth does murder sleep” and ending with a man who can feel neither fear nor grief — the recognisable condition of the damned, and a fate a first audience would weigh against a king who claimed a sacred office. The change between Act 1 and Act 5 is therefore presented as a loss of interiority rather than a change of belief, which is exactly why Macbeth can still be pitied at the end: the audience has watched the one thing that made him human being spent.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The question is about change, so the essay needs a before and an after argued together, not two halves stuck side by side.","Look at what kind of speech he is given at each point, and at who else is standing on stage to hear it.","Plan three or four paragraphs that each carry one method with an early moment and a late one, and settle in advance what the change actually amounts to."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam tariff: for Component 3, Assignment B, devise a 30-mark Macbeth assignment title in the board’s own model format, and justify how it gives access to AO1, AO2 and AO4. (10 marks notional)","a":"A title in the model form, for example a proposition plus “To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play”, with a justification showing all three objectives are reachable.","sol":"A suitable title, following the specification’s own model wording, is: “Shakespeare presents Macbeth as a man destroyed from within. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play.” The justification runs through the three objectives the assignment must allow the learner to access. AO1 is available because the proposition cannot be tested without close knowledge of specific moments across the whole play — the Act 1 soliloquies, the banquet, the sleepwalking, the tomorrow speech — and because “to what extent” demands an informed personal engagement rather than a summary. AO2 is compelled by the instruction to consider language, form and structure, which pushes the writer towards soliloquy and aside as forms, the contrast between verse and prose, and the structural placement of the banquet. AO4 is compelled by the instruction to refer to context, and the proposition invites the most relevant category rather than all five: Jacobean beliefs about kingship, conscience and damnation. The title also meets the practical requirements: it is answerable within the advisory 650 to 800 words, it is marked out of 30 with 10 marks for each objective on the five-level grid applied on a best fit basis, and it is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the two model wordings the specification prints and copy their shape exactly, including the rubric sentence.","A title only gives access to an objective if a candidate cannot answer it well while ignoring that objective — test yours against each of the three.","Write the title first, then take the objectives one at a time, and finish with the practical requirements the specification sets for the assignment."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"Malcolm calls Macbeth a dead butcher at the end of the play. Shakespeare has nevertheless made him a tragic hero. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Agree substantially: soliloquy grants the audience access and sympathy, and the play gives him self-knowledge to the end; but Act 4 Scene 2 is designed to strip that sympathy, so Malcolm’s verdict is true within the world of the play.","sol":"Malcolm’s phrase — “this dead butcher and his fiend-like queen” — is the judgement of the man who has won, and Shakespeare gives it to him in the last speech of the play, where it is designed to close the action rather than to describe it fully. The audience has been positioned very differently. From Act 1 the soliloquy and the aside give us access to Macbeth’s reasoning and to nobody else’s, and that access generates sympathy structurally, regardless of what he does: we hear him refuse the murder before we watch him commit it, and the play makes his conscience articulate at every stage, from the dagger he himself calls a creation of his brain to the hand that would incarnadine the seas. This is the shape of tragedy in the literary tradition Shakespeare is working in: a man of stature, introduced as “brave Macbeth”, destroyed by an act that is recognisably his own. Against that, the play deliberately spends its sympathy down. Act 4 Scene 2 puts Lady Macduff and her son on stage precisely so that the audience knows them before the murderers arrive, and the child is killed in front of us where Duncan was killed offstage — a structural choice that makes the word butcher accurate. Yet even after this, Act 5 Scene 5 gives him the play’s most famous recognition of emptiness, and he goes to his death choosing to fight rather than yield. I therefore agree substantially: Shakespeare has written a tragic hero and then allowed a victorious enemy to reduce him to a butcher, and the gap between what the audience knows and what Malcolm says is itself the tragedy’s final effect.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Two verdicts are in play — the one a character gives and the one the dramatic form gives the audience.","Ask which structural devices generate sympathy, and which scene is built to destroy it.","Argue the case for the hero, then the case for the butcher, and make the conclusion explain why the play holds both."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare use the structure of the whole play, from the witches’ opening to Malcolm’s closing speech, to present order and its restoration? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Disorder frames the action: the witches open, portents follow the regicide, ceremony collapses at the banquet, and the ending restores titles, ceremony and a coronation at Scone; Jacobean ideas of hierarchy make the pattern legible.","sol":"Shakespeare builds the play as a single movement out of order and back into it, and the structure carries the argument by itself. The opening is deliberately not a human scene: thunder, three witches, and a chiasmus, “Fair is foul, and foul is fair”, that inverts the terms by which anything could be judged. Order is then established only to be broken — Duncan creating thanes and naming Malcolm Prince of Cumberland in Act 1 Scene 4 gives the audience the ceremony whose destruction it will watch. After the regicide, Shakespeare does not move straight on: Act 2 Scene 4 pauses the plot for an Old Man and Ross to report portents, darkness in the daytime and a falcon killed by a mousing owl, and the Old Man’s verdict — “’Tis unnatural, / Even like the deed that’s done” — states the play’s governing analogy between the kingdom and the natural world. For a Jacobean audience this was not decoration: hierarchy was understood as natural and divinely arranged, so regicide really did read as a wound in creation. The middle of the play stages disorder socially rather than cosmically: the banquet in Act 3 Scene 4, where the seat is occupied and the guests must leave without the order of their going. The restoration is then managed with the same care. Sleep and health return as images through the English scene and the doctor; the moving wood turns a prophecy of chaos into an army under discipline; and Malcolm’s last speech reinstates the language Duncan used, thanking and creating and inviting everyone to see him crowned at Scone. Shakespeare therefore presents order not as a theme discussed but as a shape performed, and the audience feels the restoration because the play gives back the ceremony it took away.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Think about where the play pauses its plot, and ask what those pauses are for.","Follow one strand — ceremony, or nature, or sleep — from the first act to the last.","Plan the essay in the order of the play for once, and let the conclusion say what the shape itself argues."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Component 2: Content overview","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Macbeth question carry, and how are they divided between the objectives?","a":"30 marks: 10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4."},{"q":"Which section of Component 2 carries AO4, and what may you take into the room?","a":"Section B, Literary Heritage Texts; a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition."},{"q":"What does the top level of the grid require you to do with context?","a":"Integrate understanding of the relationship between text and context convincingly into the response."},{"q":"What does the third apparition promise Macbeth in Act 4 Scene 1?","a":"That he shall never be vanquished until Great Birnam wood comes against him to high Dunsinane hill."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Fair is foul, and foul is fair","back":"The witches’ closing chant in Act 1 Scene 1. Its chiasmus sets the play’s moral inversion running before any human speaks, and Macbeth unknowingly echoes it in his first line in Act 1 Scene 3.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Soliloquy in Macbeth","back":"A speech by a character alone on stage, thinking aloud to the audience. It gives us Macbeth’s conscience and nobody else’s, which is why sympathy survives his crimes.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Vaulting ambition, which o’erleaps itself","back":"From Macbeth’s soliloquy in Act 1 Scene 7, where he admits he has no spur but ambition. The riding image carries the fall inside the desire itself.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Unsex me here","back":"Lady Macbeth’s plea to the spirits in Act 1 Scene 5. An imperative that asks for something to be taken away, which frames her strength as borrowed rather than owned.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The three apparitions of Act 4 Scene 1","back":"An armed head: beware Macduff. A bloody child: none of woman born shall harm Macbeth. A crowned child with a tree: he is safe until Birnam wood comes to Dunsinane.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Equivocation","back":"Answering so that a statement is true and still misleads. The Porter jokes about an equivocator in Act 2 Scene 3, and the apparitions work on Macbeth the same way.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"The Gunpowder Plot of November 1605","back":"The failed conspiracy to blow up Parliament, followed by trials in which equivocation became notorious. It sharpens a Jacobean audience’s horror at plotting against a king.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"James I and the King’s Men","back":"Shakespeare’s company became the King’s Men under James I’s patronage in 1603. James had published Daemonologie in 1597, so the weird sisters met an audience whose monarch had written on witchcraft.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Holinshed’s Chronicles","back":"The source of the Scottish material behind Macbeth. Knowing the play has a source lets you argue about Shakespeare’s dramatic choices rather than about Scottish history.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"AO4 in Section B","back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written: 10 of the 30 marks, and the only part of Component 2 that carries it.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false},{"front":"Name one method Shakespeare uses to stage Macbeth’s guilt","back":"Blood imagery, as in the question whether Neptune’s ocean will wash his hand clean; the sleep motif, the voice crying that Macbeth does murder sleep; Banquo’s ghost at the banquet, visible to him alone.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":true},{"front":"Level 5 on the Edexcel grid","back":"Marks 25 to 30: assured knowledge, assured personal engagement, a perceptive critical style, cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure, discriminating examples, context integrated convincingly.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Out, damned spot! out, I say!","back":"Lady Macbeth sleepwalking in Act 5 Scene 1, and in prose. The imperative that once commanded her husband now commands a stain that is not there.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow","back":"Macbeth in Act 5 Scene 5, on hearing his wife is dead. Repetition flattens time into meaningless succession before the speech reduces life to a walking shadow.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment B","back":"The coursework alternative: one teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the heritage text, 10 marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4, advisory 650 to 800 words, moderated by Pearson.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: Shakespeare makes guilt more destructive than any enemy in Macbeth. To what extent do you agree?","Point: Shakespeare makes guilt the only force in the play that reaches Macbeth before an army does, so the destruction is inward long before Birnam wood moves.","Evidence: immediately after the murder in Act 2 Scene 2, Macbeth asks whether “all great Neptune’s ocean” will wash the blood clean from his hand, and answers himself that the hand will instead turn the seas red.","Analysis: the hyperbole is built to fail — he reaches for the largest body of water language can offer and the sentence changes its colour, so the grammar itself refuses him absolution; and the form matters, because a man who has just chosen the murder is now asking rather than deciding.","Context, integrated: for a Jacobean audience, in which the king’s person was understood as sacred, blood that cannot be washed off is not a stain but damnation, and Shakespeare gives Macbeth that recognition in the same breath as the deed.","Counter and judgement: Macduff does kill him, and the play needs order restored by human hands, but by Act 5 Scene 5 guilt has already emptied its victim, so that a man with everything he wanted calls life “a tale / Told by an idiot” — the proposition holds, but only because Shakespeare has made conscience the true antagonist of a man with no other one left."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: Shakespeare makes guilt more destructive than any enemy in Macbeth. To what extent do you agree?","Point: Macbeth feels very guilty after he has killed Duncan.","Evidence: he says “Will all great Neptune’s ocean wash this blood / Clean from my hand?”","Analysis: this is a rhetorical question and also a metaphor, which shows how guilty he is feeling and that he cannot get rid of the guilt. It makes the audience feel that he regrets what he has done.","Context: at the time, people believed in the divine right of kings, so killing a king was seen as a very bad thing to do.","Mini-judgement: this shows that guilt is an important theme in the play.","Why this is Level 3 and not Level 5: the point states a feeling instead of making an argument; the analysis names the devices and stops, where Level 4 wants sustained analysis and Level 5 cohesive evaluation; the context is a true fact bolted on in its own sentence rather than changing how the line is read, which is the difference between the bottom and the top of the context strand; and the judgement restates the question instead of weighing it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: the characters","items":["Macbeth: Thane of Glamis, then Thane of Cawdor, then King of Scotland. He reaches the audience first through the Captain’s report of his valour in Act 1 Scene 2, as “brave Macbeth”.","Lady Macbeth: his wife. She reads his letter in Act 1 Scene 5 and calls on spirits to unsex her; she sleepwalks in Act 5 Scene 1; her death is reported to Macbeth in Act 5 Scene 5.","Duncan: King of Scotland, murdered at Macbeth’s castle in Act 2. In Act 1 Scene 4 he names his son Malcolm Prince of Cumberland, the obstacle Macbeth notices at once.","Banquo: Macbeth’s fellow general, told he shall get kings though he be none. Murdered on Macbeth’s orders in Act 3 Scene 3; his ghost appears at the banquet in Act 3 Scene 4.","Macduff: Thane of Fife. He discovers Duncan’s body in Act 2 Scene 3, goes to England, loses his wife and children to Macbeth’s murderers in Act 4 Scene 2, and kills Macbeth in Act 5.","Malcolm: Duncan’s elder son, who flees to England, tests Macduff in Act 4 Scene 3, returns with the army, and is hailed King of Scotland in the final scene.","Donalbain: Duncan’s younger son, who flees to Ireland after the murder, saying there are daggers in men’s smiles.","Fleance: Banquo’s son, who escapes the murderers in Act 3 Scene 3 and never appears again, leaving the prophecy about Banquo’s line unfinished within the play.","The three witches: the weird sisters, who open the play, meet Macbeth in Act 1 Scene 3 and show him the apparitions in Act 4 Scene 1. Hecate appears with them in Act 3 Scene 5.","Lady Macduff and her son: murdered at Fife in Act 4 Scene 2. The son is stabbed and dies in front of the audience; she runs out crying murder, pursued by the murderers, and is killed offstage — the play’s most deliberate withdrawal of sympathy from Macbeth.","Siward and Young Siward: the English Earl of Northumberland and his son, who join Malcolm’s invasion; Macbeth kills Young Siward in Act 5.","Ross, Lennox, Angus, Menteith and Caithness: Scottish thanes who carry news, comment on events and finally desert Macbeth.","Seyton: Macbeth’s attendant in Act 5, who arms him and brings him word that the queen is dead.","The Porter: the drunken gatekeeper of Act 2 Scene 3, who imagines himself the porter of hell gate and jokes about an equivocator."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: the plot in order","items":["Act 1 Scene 1: three witches meet in thunder, agree to meet with Macbeth, and end with “Fair is foul, and foul is fair.”","Act 1 Scene 2: a wounded Captain reports Macbeth’s victory over the rebel Macdonwald, whom he is described as cutting open from the navel to the jaw; Duncan orders the traitorous Thane of Cawdor’s death and gives the title to Macbeth.","Act 1 Scene 3: the witches hail Macbeth as Glamis, Cawdor and king hereafter, and tell Banquo he shall get kings though he be none; Ross and Angus then arrive with the news of Cawdor.","Act 1 Scene 4: Duncan thanks Macbeth and names Malcolm Prince of Cumberland; Macbeth asks the stars to hide their fires.","Act 1 Scene 5: Lady Macbeth reads the letter, calls on spirits to unsex her, and tells Macbeth to look like the innocent flower but be the serpent under it.","Act 1 Scene 6: Duncan arrives at the castle and calls its seat pleasant, the play’s sharpest irony of place.","Act 1 Scene 7: Macbeth’s soliloquy weighs the murder and he tries to withdraw; Lady Macbeth attacks his manhood, tells him to screw his courage to the sticking-place, and sets the plan.","Act 2 Scenes 1 and 2: Macbeth follows an imagined dagger and kills Duncan offstage; he carries the daggers out with him and Lady Macbeth takes them back, saying a little water clears them of the deed.","Act 2 Scene 3: the Porter answers the knocking; Macduff discovers the body; Macbeth kills the grooms; Malcolm flees to England and Donalbain to Ireland.","Act 2 Scene 4: an Old Man and Ross describe unnatural portents, including a falcon killed by a mousing owl; Macbeth has gone to Scone to be crowned.","Act 3 Scenes 1 to 3: Banquo suspects that Macbeth played most foully for the crown; Macbeth resolves that to be thus is nothing but to be safely thus, and hires murderers; Banquo is killed and Fleance escapes.","Act 3 Scene 4: at the banquet Banquo’s ghost takes Macbeth’s place; the feast breaks up; Macbeth says he is so far stepped in blood that returning were as tedious as go o’er.","Act 4 Scene 1: the witches show Macbeth the armed head, the bloody child, the crowned child with a tree, and a line of eight kings with Banquo following.","Act 4 Scene 2: murderers kill Lady Macduff and her children at Fife.","Act 4 Scene 3: in England Malcolm tests Macduff by slandering himself; Ross brings news of the slaughter; Macduff insists he must feel his grief as a man and turns it to revenge.","Act 5 Scene 1: a doctor and a gentlewoman watch Lady Macbeth sleepwalk, rubbing her hands and speaking in prose of a spot that will not go.","Act 5: the army advances behind boughs cut from Birnam wood; Lady Macbeth’s death is reported; Macbeth kills Young Siward; Macduff reveals he was from his mother’s womb untimely ripped and kills him; Malcolm is hailed king and invites everyone to see him crowned at Scone."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: the themes an examiner rewards","items":["Ambition: presented as a thought before it is an act. Macbeth names vaulting ambition as his only spur in Act 1 Scene 7, and the image of a rider overleaping himself contains the fall.","Guilt and conscience: staged through hands, blood and sleep. A voice cries that Macbeth does murder sleep in Act 2 Scene 2; his wife washes clean hands in Act 5 Scene 1.","Kingship: defined by contrast between Duncan’s ceremony, Edward of England’s healing touch in Act 4 Scene 3, Macbeth’s isolated tyranny, and Malcolm’s restoration of titles at the close.","Appearance and reality: from the witches’ opening paradox to the instruction to look like the innocent flower, the play insists that surfaces lie and that faces cannot be read.","The supernatural: the witches and their apparitions give orders about how to listen and how to bear himself — say nothing, be bold and resolute — but never an order to kill; the prophecies are literally true and practically fatal, so responsibility is never lifted from Macbeth.","Gender and violence: Lady Macbeth asks to be unsexed and attacks her husband’s manhood, while in Act 4 Scene 3 Macduff answers the order to dispute it like a man by insisting he must also feel it as a man.","Order and disorder: regicide is followed by unnatural portents in Act 2 Scene 4, and the play closes only when the crown returns to Duncan’s line.","Fate and free will: every prophecy comes true, yet nothing in the play shows Macbeth being compelled — the gap between the two is where the essay lives."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: form and structure","items":["Genre: a tragedy, and the shortest of Shakespeare’s tragedies, so the fall follows the rise with very little slack between them.","Verse: the nobles speak blank verse, unrhymed iambic pentameter; the witches speak a shorter, chanting, heavily rhymed line that marks them off from the human world by sound alone.","Prose: used for the Porter in Act 2 Scene 3 and for Lady Macbeth’s sleepwalking in Act 5 Scene 1, so comedy and collapse both fall out of verse.","Soliloquy and aside: the play’s main structural device, giving the audience Macbeth’s reasoning before each crime and nobody else’s. It is withdrawn at the end: the Act 5 Scene 5 speech is delivered in company, with Seyton and soldiers on stage, so the private voice must now be overheard.","The separation of the Macbeths begins at Act 3 Scene 1, where Banquo’s murder is planned without her; the banquet in Act 3 Scene 4 is where that separation becomes public, and after it they never share the stage again.","Dramatic irony: Duncan praises the castle’s pleasant seat as he enters the place where he will die, and Macbeth repeats the witches’ foul and fair without knowing he is quoting them.","The placing of violence: Duncan is killed offstage while Lady Macduff’s son is killed in front of the audience, which controls exactly where sympathy goes.","The frame: the play opens with the witches in thunder and closes with Malcolm’s invitation to his coronation at Scone, so disorder brackets the action and ceremony returns at the end."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: the context the specification rewards (AO4)","items":["Writer and time of writing: Macbeth was written for the Jacobean stage in the reign of James I, who came to the English throne in 1603; his patronage made Shakespeare’s company the King’s Men.","James I and witchcraft: the king had published Daemonologie in 1597, a treatise on witchcraft, so the weird sisters faced an audience whose monarch had written on the subject.","The Gunpowder Plot of November 1605 and the trials that followed made both regicide and the doctrine of equivocation matters of public alarm; the Porter’s equivocator in Act 2 Scene 3 lands in that air.","Banquo and the Stuart line: James’s dynasty was held to descend from Banquo, which is why the show of eight kings in Act 4 Scene 1 runs Banquo’s line forward towards the audience.","Historical setting: a medieval Scottish kingdom drawn from Holinshed’s Chronicles, deliberately older and rougher than the court watching the play.","Social and cultural attitudes: kingship was understood as divinely sanctioned and nature as ordered by hierarchy, so regicide reads as a wound in creation — hence the portents of Act 2 Scene 4.","The royal touch: Act 4 Scene 3 shows the English king healing the sick by touch, a sacramental image of legitimate rule set directly against Macbeth’s tyranny.","Literary context: the tradition of tragedy, in which a figure of stature falls by his own act, combined with a popular stage that relished apparitions, ghosts and shows.","Reception over time: a twenty-first-century audience is likelier to read Lady Macbeth as a woman with no route to power except through a husband, where earlier audiences more often read her as monstrous — the specification asks for exactly this kind of difference.","Text history: the play survives first in the First Folio of 1623, seven years after Shakespeare’s death, and many editors believe the Hecate material was added later by another hand."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Macbeth: the key moments to know cold","items":["Act 1 Scene 3, the prophecies, and Macbeth’s aside that the supernatural soliciting cannot be ill, cannot be good.","Act 1 Scene 5, Lady Macbeth alone: the letter, the invocation to the spirits, and the serpent under the innocent flower.","Act 1 Scene 7, the soliloquy and the quarrel: if it were done when ’tis done, vaulting ambition, and the demand that he screw his courage to the sticking-place.","Act 2 Scene 1, the dagger: the hallucination Macbeth himself calls a creation of his own brain, and the bell that invites him.","Act 2 Scene 2, straight after the murder: Macbeth does murder sleep, Neptune’s ocean, and his wife’s flat reply that a little water clears them of this deed.","Act 2 Scene 3, the Porter and the discovery: the knocking, hell gate, the equivocator, and Macduff’s cry of horror.","Act 3 Scene 1, Macbeth alone: to be thus is nothing, but to be safely thus — the first murder he arranges without his wife.","Act 3 Scene 4, the banquet: the occupied seat, the ghost only he can see, and his admission that he is stepped in blood so far that returning were as tedious as go o’er.","Act 4 Scene 1, the apparitions and the line of eight kings.","Act 4 Scene 3, England: Malcolm testing Macduff, and Macduff insisting that he must feel his grief as a man.","Act 5 Scene 1, the sleepwalking: out, damned spot; all the perfumes of Arabia; what’s done cannot be undone.","Act 5 Scene 5, the news of the queen’s death: tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow, and life as a tale told by an idiot.","Act 5, the end: Macduff untimely ripped, Macbeth’s refusal to yield, and Malcolm’s verdict on the dead butcher and his fiend-like queen."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — the prescribed editions and the open-book rules","items":["Component 2 is an open book examination: learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room, completely unmarked.","Learners or centres must purchase a prescribed edition of each set text at the start of the course; the editions are listed in Appendix 3 of the specification.","OUP Oxford, 1st edition, March 2009, ISBN 9780198324003.","Heinemann, May 2010, ISBN 9780435026448.","Pearson Education, May 2023, ISBN 9781292729367.","CUP Cambridge, January 2014, ISBN 9781107615496.","Practical consequence: because the text is in front of you, quotation should be exact and short, and the grid rewards discriminating use of relevant examples rather than length."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How does Shakespeare portray the gap between what is shown and what is hidden in Macbeth? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","items":["Decode the question: “How does” asks for methods, so every paragraph must name one; “portray” means built for an audience rather than reported; the subject is a gap, so both sides of it — the face shown and the thing hidden — must stay in view in every paragraph; and the rubric sentence makes AO2 and AO4 compulsory, at 10 marks each alongside 10 for AO1.","Thesis for the introduction: Shakespeare portrays the gap not as lying by particular characters but as the condition of the whole world of the play — the audience is taught in the first scene that surfaces cannot be read, and the tragedy comes of characters going on acting as though they could.","Paragraph 1 — the rule is set before any human speaks: the witches’ “Fair is foul, and foul is fair” in Act 1 Scene 1, whose chiasmus turns each term into the other, and Macbeth’s unknowing echo of foul and fair in his first line in Act 1 Scene 3, so the paradox is in his mouth before he has met them.","Paragraph 2 — concealment taught as a technique: Lady Macbeth’s “Look like the innocent flower, / But be the serpent under’t” makes deception an instruction, and Duncan’s praise of the castle’s “pleasant seat” as he walks into the place where he will die shows the audience how well the method works.","Paragraph 3 — the form is the gap: aside and soliloquy exist to show an audience what the stage cannot see, so Shakespeare builds the difference between shown and hidden into the shape of the play; Act 3 Scene 4 then reverses it, with Macbeth answering a ghost his guests cannot see, so the hidden thing breaks the surface in front of the court.","Paragraph 4 — context, integrated rather than added: equivocation, answering truly and still misleading, was a public scandal after the Gunpowder Plot of 1605 and the trials that followed; the Porter’s joke about an equivocator in Act 2 Scene 3 puts it straight into the play, and the apparitions of Act 4 Scene 1 work on Macbeth the same way, so for a first audience the gap between word and meaning was a political danger, not only a theme.","Paragraph 5 — what was hidden is forced into the open: Lady Macbeth’s sleepwalking in Act 5 Scene 1 is watched by a doctor and a gentlewoman and spoken in prose, and “All the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this little hand” answers her own earlier “A little water clears us of this deed”, so the structure closes at the end the gap it opened at the start.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: does the play believe the gap can ever be closed? Malcolm tests Macduff in Act 4 Scene 3 by pretending to be worse than Macbeth, so even the restorer of order has to dissemble. Take a position on that in the introduction and test it against each paragraph’s evidence.","The conclusion must say what Shakespeare does, not what happens: that the play portrays the reading of surfaces as the central human task and shows it failing, and that the ending restores the crown without restoring any confidence that a face can be trusted — a judgement on the method, not a summary of the plot."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:46:05.371253Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"A genuinely strong kit and a clean rewrite: every one of the earlier faults is repaired at source and repaired consistently — question 11 is now a proper Section B stem with the compulsory “refer to the context of the play” rubric, all three AO codes, and a sol that gets the Act 5 Scene 5 staging exactly right (drum and colours, Seyton and soldiers on stage, Seyton’s report prompting the speech), with extras[5].items[3] rewritten to match; the Lady Macduff factfile line now distinguishes the son stabbed on stage from her death offstage, in agreement with questions[13].sol; the “witches never command” overstatement is narrowed to “never an order to kill” in all three places; the separation of the Macbeths is dated to Act 3 Scene 1 with the banquet as the public break, ending the contradiction with questions[6].sol; questions[2].hints[0] now points at where to look rather than stating the 10/10/10 split; “Cannot be ill; cannot be good” and “Thou canst not say I did it. Never shake / Thy gory locks at me.” are pointed correctly; the essay plan has a new stem (shown versus hidden) that no question duplicates; and questions[12] is labelled “not an exam tariff … (10 marks notional)”. I checked all 29 quoted lines word for word against the play and every one is exact, with speakers and scenes right — Angus on the giant’s robe in Act 5 Scene 2, the Old Man on “’Tis unnatural” in Act 2 Scene 4, Malcolm on “this dead butcher and his fiend-like queen” — and the contextual dates (King’s Men 1603, Daemonologie 1597, Gunpowder Plot November 1605, Holinshed, First Folio 1623) and the four prescribed ISBNs all check out. Publish; the three residual points are small enough to mend in a single pass."}}},"heritage-merchant-of-venice":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-merchant-of-venice","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"The Merchant of Venice is your Section B text in Component 2: one 30-mark essay, chosen from two, carrying 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4. Because the three objectives weigh exactly the same, every developed paragraph has to do three jobs at once — argue a close reading of the play, analyse how Shakespeare's language, form and structure create the effect, and set the moment against the world the play was written for. The paper is open book, which raises rather than lowers the standard: an examiner who knows you have the text in front of you expects exact, short quotation and no retelling of the plot. This room builds that three-stranded paragraph, and the weighed judgement an essay needs before it can reach Level 5.","idea":[{"h":"One modern drama text and one literary heritage text — Merchant is the heritage half","t":"Component 2 requires two set texts: “one of the modern drama set texts and one of the literary heritage set texts”, and The Merchant of Venice is one of the six heritage choices. The content overview asks you to “Develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect” and to “Develop skills to maintain a critical style and informed personal response”, and teaching is on whole texts. That matters for the questions you will meet: they fasten on a theme, a character or a setting that runs from Act 1 to Act 5, so a student who has only worked the trial scene cannot answer them. What a top-level answer does: it ranges deliberately, choosing moments from Venice and from Belmont, from early in the play and from late, so the argument is about the whole play rather than about one famous speech.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge argued in a critical style, not retold","t":"AO1 is 10 of the 30 marks: “Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.” The section spells it out — learners must demonstrate a close understanding of their heritage text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and “identify and use relevant examples from the text”. Open book is not knowledge: you still have to know that Shylock's ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’ comes after his daughter has robbed him, because the placing is half the point. The grid punishes storytelling — Level 2 answers are “largely narrative” — while Level 5 wants “assured knowledge” and “discriminating use of relevant examples in support”. What a top-level answer does: it quotes the shortest phrase that proves the claim and spends every other word on the argument, so the examiner never has to read the story back.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: analyse how the writing works, do not label it","t":"AO2 is the middle 10 marks: “Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.” The grid moves from “Minimal identification of language, form and structure” at Level 1, through “Some comment” at Level 2 and “Sustained analysis” at Level 4, to “cohesive evaluation” at Level 5 — so naming a metaphor earns almost nothing and tracing what it does earns highly. In a play, form and structure mean more than imagery: blank verse against prose, soliloquy and aside, the alternation of Venice and Belmont, the placing of the trial as the climax of Act 4 Scene 1, with a further scene and a whole further act after it, the fact that the parts of Portia and Nerissa were written for boy actors who then play men. What a top-level answer does: it moves from the word, to the method, to the effect on an audience — and then evaluates that effect instead of reporting it.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"},{"h":"AO4: the only part of Component 2 that pays for context","t":"AO4 is the last 10 marks, and Section B is the only place in Component 2 that carries it: “Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written”, with learners required to “understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts”. The Assignment B grid runs its context strand from “little comment on the relationship between text and context” up to understanding “integrated convincingly into the response”, and the word integrated is the whole test. A sentence about the expulsion of the Jews from England in 1290 parked at the end of a paragraph scores at the bottom; the same fact used to explain why an Elizabethan audience met Shylock as a figure out of story rather than a neighbour scores at the top. What a top-level answer does: it makes the contextual fact part of the reading of the line, so deleting it would break the analysis.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"},{"h":"The five categories of context the specification names","t":"The specification names five kinds of context: the writer's own life and individual situation, “only where these relate to the text”; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, “for example, attitudes in society; expectations of different cultural groups”; the literary context, “for example, literary movements or genres”; and the way texts “are received and engaged with by different audiences, at different times”. The Merchant of Venice supplies all five: Venice as a real trading republic whose Jewish residents were confined to an enclosed quarter and licensed to lend at interest; Elizabethan attitudes to usury and to conversion; the literary context of romantic comedy and of the stage Jew after Marlowe's Barabas; and a reception history that has read the play as broad comedy, as the tragedy of Shylock, and, after the Holocaust, as a work that cannot be staged innocently. What a top-level answer does: it picks the one category that actually explains the moment in front of it, and leaves the other four out.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"What the question looks like and what you may take in","t":"“Section B – Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts. This section assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4.” These are Questions 11 to 22; Component 2 is a 1 hour 30 minute paper worth 60 marks and 40% of the International GCSE, so this essay is worth about forty-five minutes. “This component is an open book examination and therefore learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room” — clean meaning completely unmarked: no notes, no highlighting, no tabs. The question will carry the instruction “You must consider language, form and structure” and an instruction to refer to the context; the models the specification prints happen to be novels, which is why its own wording reads “the context of the novel”. What a top-level answer does: it treats both instructions as mark-bearing orders, and answers the question actually set rather than the theme it recognises.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"Component 3 “is available as an alternative to Component 2” and draws on the same literary heritage list. “Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text. This section assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4”, and it “will be marked out of 30 with 10 marks for AO1, 10 marks for AO2 and 10 marks for AO4”; the assignment “must allow learners to access AO1, AO2 and AO4”. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, “and there is no penalty for exceeding this recommendation”; marking is on the same five-level grid on a “best fit” basis, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. The titles are worded like the models the specification prints — a named writer, a theme, and the instruction about language, form and structure. What a top-level answer does: it uses the extra time to make every paragraph carry all three strands, because nothing here can be excused by examination conditions.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"}],"examples":[{"title":"Building the three-stranded paragraph on Shylock","setup":"The question on the paper reads: In what ways does Shakespeare present Shylock in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. It carries 30 marks, split 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4. The moment you must use is in Act 3 Scene 1, in a Venetian street, just after Jessica has run away with Lorenzo taking her father's money and jewels: Shylock answers Salarino and Solanio in prose with ‘I am a Jew. Hath not a Jew eyes?’ and ‘If you prick us, do we not bleed?’, and closes the same speech with ‘The villainy you teach me I will execute.’ One contextual fact is supplied for you to use: England had expelled its Jews in 1290 and had not readmitted them in Shakespeare's lifetime, so most of the first audience knew Jews from scripture and the stage rather than from the street. Write one paragraph fit for a Level 5 answer.","steps":["Point: Shakespeare presents Shylock as a man whose claim on our common humanity cannot be separated from his claim to revenge, so an audience is never allowed to pity him without also fearing him.","Evidence, short and exact: ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’ set against ‘The villainy you teach me I will execute’, both from the one speech.","Language and form: the speech is prose, not blank verse, and is built as a chain of rhetorical questions, so Shylock never asserts his humanity — he makes his Christian listeners concede it themselves, and the argument takes the shape of reason rather than rage.","Structure: Shakespeare places the speech immediately after the elopement, so the case for shared feeling is spoken by a man who has just been given the strongest possible motive to hate, and the turn from bleeding to revenge happens inside one unbroken speech.","Context, integrated: England had expelled its Jews in 1290 and had not readmitted them in Shakespeare's lifetime, so most of the audience knew Jews from scripture and the stage rather than from the street; the speech works on them by forcing the stock villain to voice the shared humanity their own preachers claimed.","Judgement: the presentation is double on purpose — Shakespeare hands Shylock the most persuasive argument in the play and then makes that argument the engine of its cruellest plot, which is why the character has never settled into villain or victim."],"cues":["What single claim about Shylock will this paragraph argue?","Which two short quotations from the speech would prove that claim?","Why does it matter that the speech is prose made of questions?","What has just happened to Shylock, and why does the placing matter?","How does the supplied fact about the first audience change the way the speech lands?","What must the final sentence do that a summary would not?"]},{"title":"Repairing a narrative paragraph","setup":"A student answering the question ‘How does Shakespeare present mercy in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play’ has written this paragraph in full: ‘Portia talks about mercy in the trial scene. She says the quality of mercy is not strained and that it droppeth as the gentle rain from heaven. This is a simile which shows that mercy is gentle. Shylock does not listen and still wants his pound of flesh. In those days people did not like Jews. This shows that mercy is important in the play.’ It is Level 2 work: largely narrative, with some comment on the language. Say what is missing against each objective, then rewrite the weakest sentence.","steps":["AO1 fault: the paragraph never takes a position — ‘mercy is important’ is a topic, not an argument; replace it with a claim such as: Portia preaches mercy and then wins by law, and the play never lets the two settle.","AO2 fault: the image is labelled and abandoned; the analysis must say what it does — rain falls unasked, so the comparison makes mercy natural, free and unforced, which is exactly why it cannot be ordered by a court.","AO2 continued: the diction matters too — ‘is not strained’ and ‘droppeth’ belong to scripture and the pulpit, so Portia borrows a preacher's voice inside a courtroom and blends two kinds of authority in one speech.","AO4 fault: ‘in those days people did not like Jews’ is bolted on and could sit in any essay; it earns nothing. Use instead the fact that Venice licensed its Jewish residents to lend at interest while despising the trade, so mercy is being demanded of the one man the city has already refused it to.","Rewritten sentence: ‘Because mercy ‘droppeth as the gentle rain from heaven’ it cannot be compelled — yet Portia stands in a court for the express purpose of compelling Shylock, and the sermon cadence of her speech exposes how far this trial is a Christian appeal dressed as a legal one.’","Judgement to add: close by weighing whether the play endorses Portia's mercy or exposes it, since the top level asks for cohesive evaluation rather than agreement."],"cues":["What is wrong with ending a paragraph on ‘mercy is important’?","The image is named but not analysed — what should follow the label?","What kind of voice do ‘is not strained’ and ‘droppeth’ belong to?","Why does ‘in those days people did not like Jews’ score nothing?","How would you rewrite that sentence so that it argues?","What does the top level require at the end of a paragraph?"]},{"title":"Decoding a proposition question and planning it","setup":"The question on the paper reads: ‘The Merchant of Venice is a play about money rather than love.’ To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. It carries 30 marks, split 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, and you have roughly forty-five minutes and one clean, unmarked copy of the play. Produce the decoded question, the argumentative thread and the order of your paragraphs. Do not write the essay.","steps":["Decode the proposition: you are not asked which theme appears more often but whether money is the play's real subject, so the essay must test the claim rather than collect examples of each half.","Set the thread that will run through every paragraph: in this play love and money share one vocabulary — bonds, hazard, worth, forfeit, gift — so the either/or of the proposition is a false choice, and saying why is the spine of the essay.","Paragraph one: the casket test, where the winning inscription requires the suitor to ‘give and hazard all he hath’, so courtship itself is framed as a wager with stakes.","Paragraph two: Antonio's bond, where affection is guaranteed by flesh, so the most loving act in the play is written down as a contract with a penalty clause.","Paragraph three: Shylock as Solanio reports him — ‘My daughter! O my ducats! O my daughter!’ — where grief and property are jammed into a single line, with the context that Venice gave him money and withheld belonging.","Paragraph four, the counter-weight: Belmont at night in the last act, where music and moonlight appear to leave commerce behind, until the quarrel over the rings turns love back into a question of giving, keeping and payment.","Conclusion: agree in part — money is the language of this play, but it is the language love is forced to speak in a city built on trade, so the proposition mistakes the medium for the subject."],"cues":["What is this question actually asking you to test?","What single idea could link money and love right through the essay?","Which casket moment prices love as a gamble?","Which agreement turns affection into a written contract?","Which reported cry puts a daughter and money in one breath?","Which part of the play seems to escape money, and where does it fail?","What must the conclusion do with the word ‘rather’?"]},{"title":"Making a contextual fact earn its marks","setup":"You are writing about Act 1 Scene 3, where Shylock justifies taking interest by telling the story of Jacob and Laban's sheep out of Genesis, and Antonio answers, ‘The devil can cite Scripture for his purpose.’ Two sentences of context are offered for the paragraph. Sentence A: ‘Usury was a big issue in Shakespeare's time and Christians were not allowed to lend money at interest.’ Sentence B: ‘Venice licensed its Jewish residents to lend at interest precisely because Christian teaching condemned the practice, so the trade Antonio despises is one his own city has assigned to Shylock.’ Decide which would score at the top of the context strand, and show how to fold it into the analysis.","steps":["Sentence A fails the grid's key word: it is free-standing, could be pasted unchanged into any essay on the play, and explains nothing about Jacob, Laban or the devil.","Sentence B earns because it turns a fact into a cause: it explains why Shylock must argue from scripture at all, since a religious defence is the only one his position leaves him.","Fold it into the reading: Shylock reaches for Genesis because the case against him is religious rather than economic, and Antonio's single line shuts down the one argument the city has left him.","Notice the form the context explains: Shakespeare gives Shylock an extended narrative and Antonio one proverb-shaped line, so an audience hears elaborate self-justification answered by flat contempt.","Judgement: read this way the scene is less about interest rates than about who is permitted to interpret the book both men share, which is why the bargain that follows can be offered as a jest between enemies and still bind."],"cues":["Which sentence could be pasted into any essay unchanged?","What does the stronger sentence explain that the weaker one does not?","Why does Shylock argue from scripture rather than from profit?","What contrast in form does the exchange set up?","What larger point about the scene does the context unlock?"]},{"title":"Structure: the act Shylock is not in","setup":"The question reads: In what ways does Shakespeare use structure to shape the audience's response in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. The facts you are to work from: the trial in Act 4 Scene 1 is the play's climax, yet a whole act follows it; Shylock leaves the stage at the end of the trial, after the sentence that requires him to become a Christian, and never appears again; the last act is set at Belmont by night, opening with Lorenzo's ‘How sweet the moonlight sleeps upon this bank!’ and ending with the quarrel over the rings and the news that some of Antonio's ships have come safe to harbour. Two facts about reception are supplied for you to use: a first audience understood conversion to Christianity as the saving of a soul, while audiences since the Holocaust, and since the play was used as propaganda, cannot take that ending as harmless. Write one developed structural point.","steps":["Claim: the shape of the play works to close a wound it has only just opened, and an audience is made to feel the join.","Name the move: Shakespeare puts the climax in the fourth act and then withholds the comic ending until a whole further act has passed somewhere else.","Analyse the contrast: Venice is daylight, law and the hard talk of contracts, while Belmont is night, music and lyric verse, so the moonlight line is the first unhurried sentence the audience has heard in a long while.","Analyse the absence: comedies end by gathering everyone in, and this one cannot, because the man the plot has broken is not there to be gathered and his forced conversion is never mentioned again.","Add the reception context: to a first audience the ending completed a comedy, since conversion was understood as the saving of a soul; to audiences after the Holocaust the same silence reads as the price of the happy ending, and the specification asks you to notice exactly that shift.","Judgement: the final act is a test rather than a rest — Shakespeare gives his comedy its marriages while leaving the audience holding an empty place, and a top answer states which of those two effects it thinks wins."],"cues":["What is the effect of the play's shape, put as one claim?","Where does the climax fall, and what unusually follows it?","How do the two settings differ in sound and in light?","Who is missing from the final act, and why does that matter?","Using the two supplied facts, how might the same ending land differently today?","What must the last sentence of the paragraph decide?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section B literary heritage essay carry, and how do they divide between the assessment objectives?","a":"30 marks: 10 for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, so the three weigh equally."},{"q":"What may you take into the Component 2 examination, and in what state?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text — the component is open book."},{"q":"What word does the top level use about context, and what does it rule out?","a":"Integrated — understanding must be “integrated convincingly into the response”, which rules out a separate context paragraph or a bolted-on sentence of history."},{"q":"What is the difference between a Level 2 and a Level 4 comment on language?","a":"Level 2 offers “some comment”, usually naming a device; Level 4 gives “sustained analysis” of how the device creates meaning and effect."},{"q":"Why does an open-book examination not reduce the need to know the play?","a":"Marks come from argument, not from looking things up: you need to know where a moment falls and what precedes it, and there is no time to search."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section B of Component 2 is one 30-mark essay chosen from two, marked 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, inside a 1 hour 30 minute, 60-mark paper worth 40% of the qualification.","Component 2 is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken into the examination room.","The five levels run 1–6, 7–12, 13–18, 19–24 and 25–30 and are awarded on a “best fit” basis; Level 5 requires “cohesive evaluation of language, form and structure”, “discriminating use of relevant examples” and context “integrated convincingly into the response”.","The five categories of context are the writer's life and situation, the historical setting, social and cultural contexts, the literary context, and reception by different audiences at different times.","The Merchant of Venice was entered in the Stationers' Register in 1598 and first printed as a quarto in 1600; it is placed among the comedies in the First Folio of 1623.","Jews had been expelled from England by Edward I in 1290 and were not officially readmitted until the 1650s, so Shakespeare's audience knew Jews chiefly from scripture, story and the stage.","In Venice from 1516 the Jewish population was confined at night to an enclosed quarter, the origin of the word ghetto, was required to wear a distinguishing mark, and was licensed to lend money at interest when most other trades were closed to it.","Shylock's part ends in Act 4 Scene 1: the law against an alien who seeks a citizen's life turns on him, the Duke spares his life, Antonio's terms require him to become a Christian and to leave what he dies possessed of to Lorenzo and Jessica, and he answers ‘I am content’, then asks leave to go from hence because he is not well, and leaves the play."],"questions":[{"q":"Knowledge check, not an exam tariff: state how the 30 marks for the Section B literary heritage question divide between the assessment objectives, and say what that division forces a paragraph to contain. (2 marks)","a":"10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4 — equal weighting, so a developed paragraph must carry close knowledge, analysis of method and context together.","sol":"Section B is one 30-mark essay chosen from two, and the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks gives Questions 11 to 22 as 10 marks AO1, 10 marks AO2 and 10 marks AO4. Because the three weigh exactly the same, none of them can be quarantined in its own section of the essay: a paragraph that analyses Shakespeare's imagery superbly but never touches context has thrown away a third of what it could earn, and so has a paragraph of history with no analysis. The reliable habit is a paragraph that states a claim, proves it with a short quotation, analyses how the writing produces the effect, uses one contextual fact to explain why the moment means what it does, and ends on a small judgement.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The split is printed in the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks for Questions 11 to 22.","Three objectives share this section: close knowledge, analysis of method, and context.","Give the three figures, then say what equal weighting means for a single paragraph."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Knowledge check, not an exam tariff: name the two locations Shakespeare alternates between in The Merchant of Venice and state what each is associated with. (2 marks)","a":"Venice — trade, law, bonds, the public street; Belmont — Portia's inherited estate, the casket test, music, night and marriage.","sol":"The play moves between Venice and Belmont. Venice is the city of trade and law: argosies at sea, news on the Rialto, the bond sealed in Act 1 Scene 3 and the court of Act 4 Scene 1, a public and largely male world in which relationships are written down. Belmont is Portia's inherited house, reached by water, where the casket test set by her dead father governs her marriage and where the last act unfolds at night to music. Shakespeare keeps the two in conversation: money borrowed in Venice buys the journey to Belmont, and the ring given in Belmont is surrendered in a Venetian courtroom, so neither place is ever quite free of the other.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The play's title names one of them; the heiress's house is the other.","Think about what each place is governed by: contracts in one, a dead father's will in the other.","Name both, then attach two or three associations to each."],"codes":["Component 2: Content overview","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Knowledge check, not an exam tariff: describe the casket test and what happens to each of the three suitors who attempt it. (3 marks)","a":"Portia's dead father's will makes her marry whoever chooses the right casket: Morocco takes the gold and fails, Arragon the silver and fails, Bassanio the lead and wins.","sol":"Portia's father's will binds her to marry whichever suitor chooses correctly among three caskets of gold, silver and lead. The gold is inscribed ‘Who chooseth me shall gain what many men desire’, the silver ‘Who chooseth me shall get as much as he deserves’, and the lead ‘Who chooseth me must give and hazard all he hath’. The Prince of Morocco chooses gold and finds a skull with a scroll beginning ‘All that glisters is not gold’. The Prince of Arragon chooses silver, reasoning that he deserves much, and is rewarded with the portrait of a fool. Bassanio chooses lead, having argued that outward show is not to be trusted, and wins Portia. The device is structural as well as thematic: three attempts let Shakespeare build suspense and repeat, with variation, the play's argument about surfaces and worth.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three metals, three inscriptions, three suitors in order.","Two of the suitors are princes and both reason from what they think they deserve.","Take each casket in the order it is chosen and say what the chooser finds."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"Knowledge check, not an exam tariff: list the five categories of context the specification names, and state which one best explains why Shylock is a moneylender. (3 marks)","a":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; reception by different audiences at different times — the social and cultural context, with the historical setting of Venice, explains the moneylending.","sol":"The specification lists five: the writer's own life and individual situation, where it relates to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations placed on different cultural groups; the literary context, for example movements or genres; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times. Shylock's trade is explained by the third, supported by the second: in Venice the Jewish population was largely excluded from other occupations and licensed to lend at interest, a trade Christian teaching condemned, so the profession the Christians of the play despise is the one their society has reserved for him. Used this way the fact is an explanation rather than an aside, which is what the context strand rewards.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The five run outwards from the writer to the reader; work along that line and you will recover them.","For the second part, ask what determined which occupations were open to whom.","List all five before choosing, then justify the one you pick in a sentence."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Knowledge check, not an exam tariff: state what may be taken into the Component 2 examination and explain why an open book does not remove the need to know the play. (2 marks)","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition — but marks come from argument, and there is no time to search for moments you do not already know.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination and learners may take in a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text, completely unmarked: no annotations, no highlighting, no tabs or inserted notes. The book is there for accuracy of quotation, not for research. With about forty-five minutes for a 30-mark essay you cannot browse for evidence, and the marks are awarded for close knowledge argued in a critical style, for analysis of method and for context integrated into the reading — none of which the book supplies. In practice the copy is most useful for checking a line you already intend to use, so that a quotation is exact rather than approximate.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification is exact about the condition the copy must be in, and about what may not have been added to it.","Think about what the marks are actually given for, and how long you have.","Say what may be carried in, then what the book cannot do for you."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"In what ways does Shakespeare present Shylock in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"A deliberately double presentation: the expected stage usurer and the play's most reasoned voice. Reward close work on his prose rhetoric, the bond and trial scenes, his removal before the last act, and context used as cause rather than background.","sol":"Shakespeare presents Shylock twice over — as the stage usurer an Elizabethan audience expected, and as the one character given a reasoned case against the people who despise him — and he refuses to let the two settle into a single figure.\n\nThe first view is built in Act 1 Scene 3. The aside ‘How like a fawning publican he looks!’ hands the audience a villain's private malice before he has done anything, and the forfeit he proposes, an equal pound of Antonio's fair flesh, belongs to folk tale rather than finance. Yet the same scene supplies the grievance that explains it: he reminds Antonio that he has called him ‘misbeliever, cut-throat dog’ and has ‘spet upon my Jewish gaberdine’. The insults are concrete and physical where Antonio's contempt is abstract, so the injury sounds documented rather than merely claimed, and ‘suff'rance is the badge of all our tribe’ makes it collective.\n\nThe second view is the prose of Act 3 Scene 1. ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’ argues by rhetorical question, making the listener supply every answer; its plainness — prose, ordinary nouns, the body itself as evidence — persuades more than verse could. But the same speech ends ‘The villainy you teach me I will execute’, so sympathy and menace arrive in one breath.\n\nContext makes the doubleness legible. Venice licensed Jewish lending because Christian teaching condemned it, and England had expelled its Jews in 1290, so an audience met a figure they had been taught to despise and heard him reason like a man. Structurally, Shakespeare removes him in Act 4 Scene 1, the play's climax, and lets the ring scene that follows and the whole of Act 5 finish the comedy without him.\n\nThe presentation is therefore not a portrait but a problem, and the play leaves it deliberately unsolved.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Shylock is given both the play's cruellest intention and its most reasonable argument; a presentation question wants both.","Work from method: where is he given prose, where verse, where an aside, and what is the effect of each?","Decide first what your line of argument about him is, then choose one early moment, one middle and one late to prove it."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"‘In The Merchant of Venice, mercy is talked about far more than it is practised.’ To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Substantial agreement, qualified: the mercy speech is eloquent but strategic, the sentence is conditional, and Belmont's generosity comes only once the outsider has gone. Reward a weighed position held throughout.","sol":"The play is full of the language of mercy and thin on the act, and the gap between the two is where Shakespeare puts his pressure.\n\nPortia's ‘The quality of mercy is not strained’ is the play's great statement of the ideal. In blank verse, with the cadence of a sermon, mercy ‘droppeth as the gentle rain from heaven’ — a comparison that makes it natural, free and unforced, and therefore, as she says, impossible to compel. The speech is beautiful and it is also tactical: it is delivered by a disguised advocate who already knows the flaw in the bond, so the appeal costs her nothing and, when refused, justifies everything that follows.\n\nShylock's reply exposes the paradox in a single question: ‘On what compulsion must I?’ A court exists to compel; a free gift cannot be ordered. Context sharpens this, since the city demanding mercy of him is the city that has licensed his trade while despising it, and has shown him none.\n\nThe sentence itself is the test. Structurally, mercy arrives only after total defeat: the Duke spares a life that has already been forfeited, and Antonio's terms require conversion and the disposal of Shylock's estate. To a first audience conversion could sound like salvation; to a modern one it sounds like the last act of coercion, and that difference in reception is part of the play's meaning now.\n\nAgainst the proposition, the last act at Belmont is genuinely forgiving over the rings — but the forgiveness is easy, and it is available only once Shylock has gone.\n\nI agree substantially: the play's mercy is eloquent and conditional, and the eloquence is part of the condition. What it gains by that is a comedy honest about the cost of its own ending.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["A proposition question needs a position in the first sentence, not a verdict discovered in the last.","Test each instance: who offers mercy, from what position of strength, and at what price to the receiver?","Plan one paragraph for the speech, one for the refusal, one for the sentence and one that pushes back."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare present relationships between fathers and children in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"A pattern of children bound or escaping: Portia governed by a dead father's will, Jessica fleeing a living one, Launcelot deceiving a half-blind one. Reward structural comparison and context on obedience and inheritance.","sol":"Shakespeare builds the play on a pattern: the dead father's control works, the living father's fails, and the comedy depends on that asymmetry.\n\nPortia's is the obedient case, and she resents it. ‘I may neither choose who I would nor refuse who I dislike; so is the will of a living daughter curbed by the will of a dead father’ turns on the pun in ‘will’ — desire and legal document — and the balanced clauses enact the trap, each half of the sentence cancelling the other. The casket test is his authority made into a stage device, and Shakespeare's structure endorses it: the test works, choosing a husband who understands that ‘the outward shows be least themselves’.\n\nJessica's is the disobedient case. ‘Our house is hell’ is flat, monosyllabic and unanswerable, and ‘though I am a daughter to his blood, / I am not to his manners’ separates inheritance from character in a single line-break. She leaves in boy's clothes with her father's money and jewels, so escape is theft as well as love.\n\nShylock's first grief reaches us only as Solanio's report — ‘My daughter! O my ducats! O my daughter!’ — staged as street comedy, so the audience must decide whether to laugh; Shakespeare withholds the direct version until the Tubal scene, where the wish that Jessica were dead at his foot with the jewels in her ear, and the turquoise he had of Leah when he was a bachelor, traded by Jessica for a monkey, let his loss land unmocked. Launcelot's teasing of his half-blind father supplies the comic version of the same material.\n\nContext binds it: daughters were expected to marry by a father's direction, and a daughter who converted left more than a house.\n\nShakespeare therefore presents fatherhood as a form of ownership that the play both enforces and breaks — and gives the harshest of its outcomes to the father the comedy cannot accommodate.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Three families are in play, and their outcomes differ sharply.","Ask what each child is escaping or obeying, and whether the play punishes or rewards them for it.","Set the obedient daughter against the runaway one, and decide what the contrast argues."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"In what ways does Shakespeare present Portia in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Constraint turned into command: witty but bound in Act 1, formally submissive on winning Bassanio, decisive in disguise at the trial, and in control of the last act. Reward analysis of disguise as form as well as plot.","sol":"Portia is presented as a woman whose power is real and always borrowed — exercised through a dead man's test, a husband's cause and a lawyer's gown.\n\nShe is introduced witty and confined. Her prose in Belmont is quick and satirical about her suitors, yet the same scene contains the sentence that fences her in: she ‘may neither choose who I would nor refuse who I dislike’. Shakespeare gives her the play's sharpest voice and no authority to use it on her own life.\n\nWhen Bassanio chooses correctly she formally gives herself away, calling herself ‘an unlesson'd girl, unschool'd, unpractis'd’ in verse of elaborate courtesy. The speech is conventional and, in performance, ironic, since everything that follows disproves it — a boy actor playing a woman describing herself as untaught before she out-argues a court.\n\nThe trial reverses the pattern. Disguised as a young doctor of law she asks ‘Which is the merchant here, and which the Jew?’, refusing to know by sight what all Venice claims to know, and she wins not by feeling but by reading: ‘Tarry a little, there is something else’, then ‘This bond doth give thee here no jot of blood’. Structurally, Shakespeare has to disguise her to let her speak, which is itself the point about what a woman's voice could do in that court.\n\nThe last act keeps the power in Belmont. The ring trick lets her arraign her husband on her own ground, and ‘How far that little candle throws his beams!’ is her first image on returning to Belmont, spoken before she restores what she has withheld.\n\nShakespeare therefore presents Portia as the play's most capable figure and shows exactly what she must put on to be heard — which is a judgement about her world as much as about her.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Notice how differently she speaks in Belmont, in the courtroom and in the last act.","Disguise here is a matter of form, not only plot: ask what it permits her to say.","Choose one moment from each of the three settings and let the contrast carry the argument."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Shakespeare portray the city of Venice in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"A wealthy, law-bound trading republic whose openness is commercial rather than moral: risk at sea, news on the Rialto, law that cannot be bent, and an outsider licensed and despised. Reward the Belmont contrast.","sol":"Venice is portrayed as a city whose wealth depends on strangers and whose tolerance stops at the point of profit.\n\nIt is first a place of risk. Antonio's melancholy opens the play and his friends explain it by his ‘argosies’ at sea; the language of Venice is cargo, ventures and the Rialto, where the standing question is what news there is. Fortune here is weather, and the plot turns on ships that do not come home.\n\nIt is second a place of law. When Antonio says ‘The duke cannot deny the course of law’ because ‘the trade and profit of the city / Consisteth of all nations’, Shakespeare states the city's principle exactly: contracts must be enforceable or foreign merchants will not trade. The bond is monstrous and Venice must honour it, which is why the trial can only be won on a technicality rather than by decree.\n\nIt is third a place that licenses what it despises. Shylock lends at interest because the city permits him that trade and few others; Antonio rates him ‘in the Rialto’ and spits on his gaberdine in public. Historically the Jewish population of Venice was confined at night to an enclosed quarter and marked out by dress, so the play's street scenes stage a proximity that was strictly regulated.\n\nStructurally, Venice is defined by what it is not: Belmont is night, music and lyric verse, Venice daylight, argument and the ledger. Yet the ring given in Belmont is lost in a Venetian court, so the city's terms reach even there.\n\nShakespeare's Venice is therefore admirable and cold — a republic that cannot afford injustice to a merchant and can perfectly well afford it to a man.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The city's wealth comes from foreigners, which constrains what its ruler can do.","Look for the moment when a character explains why the Duke's hands are tied.","Build the answer around what Venice runs on: risk, law, and the outsider it both needs and rejects."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"‘The Merchant of Venice can no longer be played as a comedy.’ To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"A reception question: the comic form is intact, but the fourth act and the silence about Shylock make its resolution unavailable to a modern audience. Reward explicit handling of how different audiences receive the text.","sol":"The play keeps every formal feature of comedy and has lost the audience that could take them innocently, which is why the proposition is largely, though not wholly, right.\n\nThe form is unmistakable. There are three marriages, a heroine in disguise, a clown, a test of the suitors and a final act of moonlight, music and reconciliation; the First Folio prints it among the comedies. Lorenzo's ‘How sweet the moonlight sleeps upon this bank!’ is lyric verse written to release an audience.\n\nBut the structure builds in a problem. The climax is a trial in which a man is stripped of his estate and ordered to change his religion, and Shakespeare places it in Act 4, so the comic ending must be reached over it. Shylock's assent is three flat words — ‘I am content’ — after some of the most eloquent speech in the play, and the little he is given afterwards only asks leave to go from hence, pleads ‘I am not well’ and promises to sign; he then leaves Act 4 Scene 1 and never returns. A comedy that cannot gather in its most fully written character has an ending it does not quite earn.\n\nContext decides the rest. A first audience, for whom Jews had been legally absent from England since 1290 and for whom conversion could be heard as salvation, could take the resolution as a gift. After the Holocaust, and after the play was used as propaganda, the same scene is unplayable as reward, and productions now mostly stage the ending as cost. The specification's own category of reception — how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century — describes exactly this change.\n\nI agree in the main. The comedy survives as a shape, but a modern audience is asked to laugh from a seat the play has made uncomfortable; the honest production plays the form and lets the discomfort stand.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Separate the play's formal features from the response they were designed to produce.","The specification names reception by different audiences at different times as a category of context — this question lives there.","Grant the proposition its strongest evidence first, then say precisely what survives of the comedy."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam tariff: you are writing a paragraph on Antonio's opening line, ‘In sooth, I know not why I am so sad.’ Write three different context sentences for it, each drawn from a different one of the specification's five categories, then say which you would keep and why. (6 marks)","a":"Three sentences from three named categories (for example historical setting, literary context, reception), each explaining rather than decorating the line, with a justified choice of the one that does most analytical work.","sol":"Historical setting: because a Venetian merchant's fortune sat in ships that might not return, a wealthy man's unexplained sadness was also a recognisable commercial condition, and Antonio's friends read it that way before he does. Literary context: romantic comedy conventionally opens with an obstacle to be cleared, so an audience hearing melancholy in the first line expects it to be cured by the ending, which makes Antonio's unpartnered survival at the close a quiet refusal of the form. Reception: modern productions frequently play the sadness as unspoken love for Bassanio, a reading available to a twenty-first century audience and not obviously to a first one, which shows how the same line can be received differently at different times. I would keep the literary-context sentence for an essay on the play's ending and the historical one for an essay on Venice or on risk, because each explains something the line is doing; the reception sentence is the most interesting but only earns its place if the question asks about how the play is read. The test in every case is whether removing the sentence would damage the analysis.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The five categories are the writer's situation, the historical setting, society and culture, literature, and reception.","A sentence earns its place if it explains why the line means what it does, not if it is merely true.","Write the three sentences first, then apply one test to all three to choose between them."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam tariff: raise this paragraph from Level 2 to Level 5. ‘Jessica running away shows that Shylock is a bad father. She says that her house is hell and she leaves with Lorenzo, taking money and jewels with her. This makes the audience feel sorry for her because she wants to escape. At the time daughters had to obey their fathers. Overall this shows that Jessica is unhappy at home.’ Name what holds it back against AO1, AO2 and AO4, then write the replacement. (8 marks)","a":"Level 2 faults: largely narrative, an unargued claim, evidence paraphrased rather than quoted and never analysed, bolted-on context, a judgement that repeats the point. The rewrite must analyse ‘Our house is hell’ and the theft, and make the context explain the elopement.","sol":"Place it first: on this board's grid the paragraph is Level 2, not Level 3 — it shows some knowledge and understanding but is largely narrative, with only some comment on language, form and structure, and its context sentence stands free of the reading. Now the faults objective by objective. AO1: the claim is a summary, not an argument, and the paragraph tells the story of the elopement instead of using it. AO2: ‘her house is hell’ is paraphrased rather than quoted and never analysed; nothing is said about form, about the boy's disguise or about the fact that the flight is reported and staged as a masque-like escape at night. AO4: ‘at the time daughters had to obey their fathers’ is bolted on and could belong to any essay.\n\nRewrite: Shakespeare makes Jessica's escape inseparable from theft, so that sympathy for her is never clean. ‘Our house is hell’ is monosyllabic and absolute, a child's verdict given the force of doctrine by that last word, and it is the only reason she offers; she does not argue her father's cruelty, she pronounces on it. Her later distinction, that she is ‘a daughter to his blood’ but ‘not to his manners’, splits inheritance from character in a single line-break and lets her keep what she takes. Yet Shakespeare stages the flight at night, in a boy's clothes, with a casket of her father's money, so the audience watches a romance funded by a robbery. The elopement is also a conversion: for a daughter in this society leaving a Jewish household for a Christian husband meant leaving a faith and a dowry alike, which is why Shylock loses ducats and daughter in one cry. The paragraph's judgement, then, is not that Jessica is unhappy but that Shakespeare makes her freedom cost something he refuses to hide.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Decide first which level the paragraph is actually at, then test it one objective at a time: does it argue, does it analyse, does the context explain anything?","The strongest evidence in the moment is a flat monosyllabic sentence and a distinction Jessica draws about blood and manners.","Diagnose all three faults before writing a word of the replacement."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"‘Shakespeare gives Shylock the best arguments and the worst ending.’ To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks)","a":"Broad agreement with qualification: the rhetoric is his, but so is the murderous literalism, and the ending is generated by his own insistence on the letter. Reward evaluation rather than advocacy.","sol":"The proposition is right about the arguments and needs qualifying about the ending, because the ending is produced by the very insistence that makes his arguments so strong.\n\nThe arguments are his. ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’ is the play's most effective piece of rhetoric: prose, so it sounds like plain sense; questions, so the listener does the arguing; the body itself as evidence, so it cannot be refuted. ‘Signior Antonio, many a time and oft / In the Rialto you have rated me’ supplies documented injury, and ‘The devil can cite Scripture for his purpose’ is thrown at him precisely when he reasons from the book both men share. No Christian in the play answers these points; they change the subject.\n\nBut Shakespeare also gives him the argument that destroys him. ‘I crave the law, / The penalty and forfeit of my bond’ is a demand for the letter, and Portia defeats him with a letter of her own: ‘This bond doth give thee here no jot of blood; / The words expressly are ‘a pound of flesh’.’ Structurally, the reversal is exact — the man who would take no interpretation is undone by interpretation, and the alien statute then turns his own city's law on him.\n\nThe ending is the worst in the play, and its worst element is contextual: a first audience could hear forced conversion as mercy, where a modern one hears the removal of the last thing he had. ‘I am content’, after so much eloquence, is silence in advance.\n\nSo: the best arguments, yes; the worst ending, yes — but the two are joined, and Shakespeare's real cruelty is to make the strength the cause of the fall.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Both halves of the proposition can be granted, but a top answer asks whether they are connected.","Look at the kind of reading Shylock demands at the trial and the kind Portia performs.","Plan a paragraph for the arguments, one for how they turn on him, and one for how the ending is received now."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam tariff: devise a coursework title on The Merchant of Venice for Component 3, Assignment B, and justify each part of your wording against the requirement that the assignment ‘must allow learners to access AO1, AO2 and AO4’. (6 marks)","a":"A title modelled on the specification's own wordings, naming the writer and a theme running through the play, plus the two instructions; the justification must show which words open each objective.","sol":"A title that works: ‘How does Shakespeare present outsiders in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play.’ The justification runs objective by objective. ‘How does Shakespeare present’ opens AO1 and AO2 together, because it asks for the writer's methods rather than for events, and it requires close knowledge from across the play, since outsiders include Shylock, Jessica after her flight, Morocco and arguably Antonio at the close. ‘You must consider language, form and structure’ is the instruction that carries the AO2 marks, and it is the wording the specification itself prints. ‘Refer to the context of the play’ carries AO4 and makes the contextual strand compulsory rather than optional; a theme of outsiders makes it easy to integrate, because the historical position of Venice's Jewish residents explains Shylock's trade and his treatment directly. The theme must run through the whole text, or the response cannot show the assured knowledge the top level asks for. The answer should be written to 650 to 800 words, the advisory count, and is marked on the same five-level grid, best fit, internally assessed and externally moderated.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The specification prints two model wordings; copy their shape rather than inventing a new one.","Each objective is opened by particular words in the title — find which phrase does which job.","Write the title first, then take it phrase by phrase against AO1, AO2 and AO4."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many of the Section B essay's 30 marks are for AO4?","a":"10 marks."},{"q":"In which act does Shylock make his last appearance?","a":"Act 4 Scene 1, the trial; he does not appear in Act 4 Scene 2 or in Act 5."},{"q":"Which casket does Bassanio choose, and what does its inscription require?","a":"Lead: ‘Who chooseth me must give and hazard all he hath.’"},{"q":"Which single word in the Level 5 descriptor says how context must be handled?","a":"Integrated — ‘integrated convincingly into the response’."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Component 2 set texts","back":"One modern drama text and one literary heritage text, each studied whole. The Merchant of Venice is one of six literary heritage choices, examined in Section B.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"AO1 in Section B","back":"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement. Worth 10 of the 30 marks: argue the play, do not retell it.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO2 in Section B","back":"Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects. Worth 10 of the 30 marks: Level 1 identifies, Level 4 sustains analysis, Level 5 evaluates.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"AO4 in Section B","back":"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Worth 10 of the 30 marks, and Level 5 requires context integrated convincingly, never added on.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The five categories of context","back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and how different audiences at different times receive the text.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Section B of Component 2","back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4. Open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Assignment B","back":"The coursework alternative: one 30-mark teacher-devised essay on the heritage text, 10 marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4, advisory length 650 to 800 words, moderated by Pearson.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"The three casket inscriptions","back":"Gold: ‘Who chooseth me shall gain what many men desire.’ Silver: ‘shall get as much as he deserves.’ Lead: ‘must give and hazard all he hath’ — Bassanio's choice.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"The bond","back":"Shylock lends Bassanio three thousand ducats for three months on Antonio's security; the forfeit, agreed in Act 1 Scene 3, is ‘an equal pound’ of Antonio's ‘fair flesh’.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Shylock's ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’ speech","back":"Act 3 Scene 1, in prose: a chain of rhetorical questions that wins agreement about shared humanity, then turns it into the promise ‘The villainy you teach me I will execute.’","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Portia's mercy speech","back":"Act 4 Scene 1, blank verse: ‘The quality of mercy is not strained. It droppeth as the gentle rain from heaven’ — mercy as weather, free and unforced, urged by an advocate about to win by law.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Verse and prose in the play","back":"Shakespeare switches between them for effect: Shylock's great defence is prose, Portia's mercy speech is blank verse, and the clown Launcelot speaks almost entirely in prose.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The Venetian ghetto","back":"From 1516 the Jews of Venice were confined at night to an enclosed quarter, the origin of the word ghetto; marked out by dress, they were licensed to lend at interest.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"The Jew of Malta","back":"Christopher Marlowe's play, whose villain Barabas was the best-known stage Jew before Shylock; it supplies the literary context Shakespeare writes with and against.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Ways the structure of the play shapes an audience's response","back":"The climax falls in Act 4 Scene 1 and a further scene and a whole act follow it; Shylock leaves at the end of the trial and is absent from the comic ending; Venice and Belmont alternate so that each bargain has a romantic echo.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: How does Shakespeare present relationships between fathers and children in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (A different question and a different moment from any of the worked examples, so that you see the method applied cold.)","Point: Shakespeare fixes the measure of Shylock's fatherhood not in the public cry over ducats and daughter but in one private memory, so that an audience which has just been invited to laugh at him is made to revalue him inside the same scene.","Evidence: Shylock's ‘I had it of Leah when I was a bachelor’, set against ‘I would not have given it for a wilderness of monkeys.’","Analysis of language and form: the loss is given in prose and in plain past tense — the ring is dated and given a giver whose name appears nowhere else in the play, so an audience learns that Shylock had a wife at the very moment it learns he has lost what she gave him; and the hyperbole that follows prices the ring by refusing to price it, leaving the exchange Tubal has just reported, a ring traded for a monkey, to do the arithmetic in silence.","Analysis of structure: Shakespeare has Tubal alternate good news of Antonio's losses with bad news of Jessica's spending, so grief and appetite for revenge are cut into one another turn by turn rather than kept apart; and Solanio's mimicry of ‘My daughter! O my ducats! O my daughter!’ has come two scenes earlier, in the previous act, so the caricature is offered first and then quietly withdrawn.","Context, integrated: the stage Jew Shakespeare inherited — Marlowe's Barabas, who weighs his gold and his daughter in one breath — had given audiences a father whose losses were all property, and a daughter who left a Jewish household for a Christian husband took faith, dowry and line of descent with her. That literary type explains both what a first audience expected Shylock's grief to sound like and what Shakespeare gives him here that the type has no room for.","Judgement: the scene refuses to choose for us — the same man who mourns Leah's ring is already telling Tubal to fee an officer against Antonio — so fatherhood here is neither excuse nor indictment but the exact price the comedy pays for its plot, which is why the moment survives productions that take opposite views of him."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2","items":["Question: How does Shakespeare present relationships between fathers and children in The Merchant of Venice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (The same question as the Level 5 model above, so the gap between them is the only variable.)","Point: Shakespeare shows that Portia's father still controls her even though he is dead.","Evidence: she complains that she cannot choose the husband she would like or refuse one she dislikes, because of her father's will.","Analysis: Shakespeare repeats the word ‘will’, which shows that the father is powerful and that Portia has to do what he decided.","Context: in those days fathers decided who their daughters married.","Judgement: this shows that Portia's father is in control of her.","Why this is Level 2, and not Level 3 or Level 5: the band is described as some knowledge and understanding, largely narrative, with some comment on the language, form and structure, and that is exactly what is here. The evidence is paraphrased rather than quoted, so there is no exact wording to work on; the pun on ‘will’ — desire and legal document at once — is noticed as a repetition and never opened; nothing is said about form or structure at all, so the balanced clauses that shut her in inside one sentence, and the casket test as a stage device, both go unused. The context sentence is free-standing and could be pasted into an essay on any play, which is the bottom of the context strand. The judgement repeats the point in the same words instead of weighing it.","How to climb: for Level 3, quote the line exactly and give a sound account of what the pun does, so that the examples are clearly relevant and the understanding of language is sound. For Level 5 the context must explain something — why a dead father's device can still bind a living heiress, and what that says about inheritance in the world the play was written for — the analysis must reach the form of the test as well as its wording, and the last sentence must decide something the paragraph has earned, which is the cohesive evaluation the top level asks for."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — The Merchant of Venice: characters, plot in order, and the moments examiners reward","items":["Shylock: a Jewish moneylender of Venice and Jessica's father; he lends Bassanio three thousand ducats on Antonio's bond and demands the forfeit when it is broken.","Antonio: the merchant of the title, whose wealth is all at sea; he opens the play with ‘In sooth, I know not why I am so sad’ and binds himself for Bassanio's sake.","Bassanio: Antonio's friend, who borrows in order to court Portia at Belmont and wins her by choosing the lead casket.","Portia: the heiress of Belmont, bound by her dead father's casket test; she comes to Venice disguised as a young doctor of law, sent in place of her cousin Doctor Bellario, and defeats Shylock in court.","Nerissa: Portia's waiting-gentlewoman, who marries Gratiano and attends the trial disguised as a law clerk.","Jessica: Shylock's daughter, who elopes with Lorenzo, taking her father's money and jewels, and spends the final act at Belmont.","Lorenzo: a Venetian friend of Bassanio, who marries Jessica and is granted Shylock's estate after his death by the terms of the trial.","Gratiano: Bassanio's loud companion, who marries Nerissa and jeers at Shylock during the trial.","The Duke of Venice: presides at the trial, presses Shylock to show mercy, and finally pardons his life.","The Princes of Morocco and Arragon: Portia's failed suitors, who choose the gold and the silver caskets.","Launcelot Gobbo: the clown, who leaves Shylock's service for Bassanio's and teases his own half-blind father.","Tubal: a Jew of Venice and Shylock's friend, who brings him news of Jessica's spending and of Antonio's losses.","Salarino, Solanio and Salerio: Venetian gentlemen who report offstage events, including Shylock's reaction to the elopement; editions differ over the spelling and division of these names.","Act 1: Antonio is unaccountably sad; Bassanio asks him to fund a journey to Belmont to court Portia; Antonio's money is at sea, so he pledges his credit instead of cash.","Act 1: at Belmont, Portia and Nerissa mock her suitors, and Portia explains that she ‘may neither choose who I would nor refuse who I dislike’ because of her dead father's will.","Act 1: Shylock agrees to lend three thousand ducats for three months on Antonio's bond, the forfeit ‘an equal pound’ of Antonio's ‘fair flesh’; Antonio says ‘The devil can cite Scripture for his purpose’ when Shylock defends interest from Genesis.","Act 2: Morocco arrives at Belmont, Launcelot changes masters, and Jessica escapes in boy's clothes with Lorenzo, taking money and jewels.","Act 2: Morocco chooses the gold casket and finds a skull and a scroll beginning ‘All that glisters is not gold’; Arragon chooses silver and is mocked with the portrait of a fool.","Act 2: Solanio reports Shylock in the streets crying ‘My daughter! O my ducats! O my daughter!’","Act 3: news arrives that Antonio's ships are lost; Shylock, taunted by Salarino and Solanio, answers with ‘Hath not a Jew eyes?’; Tubal tells him that Jessica has traded a ring for a monkey, and Shylock says it was the turquoise he had of Leah when he was a bachelor.","Act 3: Bassanio rejects gold and silver — ‘So may the outward shows be least themselves’ — chooses lead and wins Portia, who gives him a ring he must never part with; Antonio's letter brings news of the forfeit, and Portia sends to her cousin Doctor Bellario for a legal opinion.","Act 3: Shylock refuses to hear Antonio in the street, repeating ‘I'll have my bond’; Antonio observes that ‘The duke cannot deny the course of law’ because the trade of the city depends on strangers.","Act 4: at the trial the Duke asks for mercy, Portia, disguised as a young doctor of law, asks ‘Which is the merchant here, and which the Jew?’ and delivers ‘The quality of mercy is not strained’; Shylock refuses even three times the sum and calls for the law.","Act 4: Portia stops the knife with ‘Tarry a little, there is something else’ — the bond gives ‘no jot of blood’ and ‘The words expressly are ‘a pound of flesh’’ — and the statute against an alien who seeks a citizen's life then turns on Shylock.","Act 4: the Duke pardons Shylock's life; on Antonio's terms he must become a Christian and leave what he dies possessed of to Lorenzo and Jessica; he answers ‘I am content’ and leaves the play.","Act 4: still in disguise, Portia asks Bassanio for the ring as her fee; he refuses at first and yields only after Antonio presses him, Gratiano carrying the ring after her in Scene 2.","Act 5: at Belmont by night Lorenzo and Jessica trade images of famous lovers, Lorenzo says ‘How sweet the moonlight sleeps upon this bank!’, the missing rings are produced and the wives feign outrage, and Portia reveals the disguise and brings Antonio news that some of his ships have come safe to harbour.","Moments examiners reward: the sealing of the bond; Portia on her father's will; Bassanio's casket speech; Shylock's prose defence; the Leah ring; the mercy speech; ‘Tarry a little’; the sentence and ‘I am content’; the moonlight opening of the last act."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — form, structure and the contexts AO4 rewards","items":["Form: a five-act comedy, printed among the comedies in the First Folio of 1623, but with a fourth act that plays as courtroom drama and a villain who is also its most articulate speaker.","Structure: the action alternates between Venice — trade, law, contracts, daylight — and Belmont — inheritance, music, night and marriage, so that nearly every Venetian bargain has a Belmont echo.","Structure: the climax falls in Act 4 Scene 1 and a whole act follows it, so the comic ending must be reached without the character the plot has destroyed.","Structure: three plots are knotted together — the bond, the caskets and the elopement — and a fourth, the rings, unties them at Belmont.","Form: Shakespeare moves between blank verse and prose for effect; Shylock's great defence is prose, while Portia's mercy speech is blank verse with the cadence of a sermon.","Form: women's parts were played by boy actors, so Portia and Nerissa are boys playing women who then disguise themselves as men — a joke the last act exploits when the rings come home.","Composition and printing: the play was entered in the Stationers' Register in 1598 and first printed as a quarto in 1600; Francis Meres listed it among Shakespeare's comedies in 1598.","Historical setting: Venice was a real and famously wealthy trading republic whose law protected foreign merchants because its prosperity depended on them, which is why the Duke cannot simply cancel the bond.","Social and cultural context: from 1516 the Jewish population of Venice was confined at night to an enclosed quarter, the origin of the word ghetto, was required to wear a distinguishing mark, and was licensed to lend at interest when most other trades were closed to it.","Social and cultural context in England: Jews had been expelled by Edward I in 1290 and were not officially readmitted until the 1650s, so Shakespeare's audience knew Jews chiefly from scripture, story and the stage.","Religious and economic context: Christian teaching condemned lending at interest as usury, though English law by Shakespeare's time permitted lending at a capped rate; conversion was understood by that audience as the saving of a soul, which is how the trial's final condition would first have been heard.","Literary context: the best-known stage Jew before Shylock was Barabas in Christopher Marlowe's The Jew of Malta, so Shakespeare writes both with and against an established type.","Literary context of the sources: the flesh-bond and the wooing of a lady of Belmonte come from the Italian story collection Il Pecorone, and the three-caskets test from the medieval collection Gesta Romanorum.","Reception: the play has been staged as broad comedy, as the tragedy of Shylock — nineteenth-century actors made him sympathetic and even noble — and, since the Holocaust and the play's use as propaganda, as a work whose antisemitism cannot be played as harmless.","Prescribed editions listed in Appendix 3: OUP Oxford, March 2010, ISBN 9780198328674; CUP Cambridge, January 2014, ISBN 9781107615397. Component 2 is open book, so a clean, unmarked copy of one of these may be taken into the examination."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — ‘In The Merchant of Venice, Belmont is an escape from Venice rather than an answer to it.’ To what extent do you agree?","items":["The question as it would appear: ‘In The Merchant of Venice, Belmont is an escape from Venice rather than an answer to it.’ To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the play. (30 marks: 10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4; about forty-five minutes.)","Decoded: the question is about the two settings and what Shakespeare does with the difference between them, not about which is the pleasanter place. ‘To what extent’ wants a position in the first sentence and weighed throughout, and ‘rather than’ obliges you to say what Belmont does not answer, not only what it offers.","The comparison thread to run through every paragraph: Belmont is built, paid for and finally entered by Venice, so test each Belmont scene by asking what has travelled there from the city.","Introduction: state the thread, name the alternation of the two places as the play's basic structural device, and signal the three tests — how Belmont sounds, where its test and its suitors come from, and what reaches it from the court.","Paragraph 1: how the two places sound. Venice opens with a merchant's unexplained sadness, ‘In sooth, I know not why I am so sad’, and goes on in the talk of argosies and the Rialto, while Act 5 gives Belmont night, music and lyric verse in Lorenzo's ‘How sweet the moonlight sleeps upon this bank!’. Analyse the change of register and of light, then judge whether a different sound is the same thing as a different value.","Paragraph 2: Belmont's own machinery. The casket test is a dead father's device, and the lead inscription ‘Who chooseth me must give and hazard all he hath’ puts the language of venture to work on a marriage, while the gold casket answers Morocco with ‘All that glisters is not gold’. Analyse the fact that Belmont judges by the Rialto's own verbs — hazard, gain, deserve — and weigh whether the test corrects Venetian values or restates them in verse.","Paragraph 3: what Venice sends to Belmont. Bassanio can only travel there on Antonio's credit and the bond that pays for it; the news of Antonio's losses arrives at Belmont in a letter and takes the bridegroom straight back; the ring given at Belmont is handed over in Venice at the lawyer's asking; and Jessica reaches Belmont with what she has taken from her father's house. Analyse the structure — Shakespeare will not let a Belmont scene stand long without a Venetian one beside it — and integrate the context that the leisure of Belmont is paid for by a city whose openness is commercial, since ‘the trade and profit of the city / Consisteth of all nations’.","Paragraph 4, the counter-weight: what Belmont genuinely has. The last act settles its quarrel over the rings without a court, and Portia's ‘How far that little candle throws his beams!’ states a moral proportion Venice never manages; concede that the play can only imagine that measure of value away from the Rialto, and that it is spoken by the woman who has just beaten Venice at its own law.","The conclusion must answer ‘to what extent’ in its first clause, not summarise: agree in the main — Belmont is a relief rather than a remedy, since its wealth, its suitors and its crises all come from Venice, and the man Venice has broken is not there to be gathered in — while allowing that the last act lets the play sound a different measure, which is what keeps it a comedy.","Checks before you stop: a short exact quotation in every paragraph; the comparison thread named inside each paragraph and not only in the introduction; one contextual fact that explains rather than decorates, such as the republic's dependence on foreign trade or a first audience's expectation that a comedy ends in marriage; and a last sentence that decides."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Notes — the forty-five minutes and the three strands","items":["Spend about five minutes planning: decode the question, fix the line of argument in one sentence, and list four moments spread across the play before writing anything.","Four or five developed paragraphs is the right shape for 30 marks; more than that and none of them will be developed enough for sustained analysis.","Every paragraph should be able to answer three questions: what am I arguing, how does Shakespeare do it, and what does the context explain here?","Use the open book for exactness, not for searching: check the wording of a line you already meant to use, and keep quotations to a phrase.","Never write a separate context paragraph — the top level requires context integrated convincingly into the response.","Name form and structure, not only imagery: prose against verse, aside and soliloquy, scene placing, the alternation of Venice and Belmont, and where a character stops appearing.","Leave two minutes for a conclusion that answers the question in its first clause; an essay that stops mid-paragraph loses the judgement the top level asks for.","Copy old spellings exactly and do not correct them: the Folio and most editions read ‘spet upon my Jewish gaberdine’ rather than ‘spit’, and ‘droppeth’ rather than ‘drops’, though modernised texts differ, so check the reading in your own prescribed edition. Checking that kind of detail is what the clean copy is for."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:13:16Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All six fixes have landed exactly as claimed — questions[12].hints[1] now reads \"a flat monosyllabic sentence\" and agrees with its own sol, questions[7].sol now routes Shylock's first grief through Solanio's report and withholds the direct version until the Tubal scene, Old Gobbo is \"half-blind\" in questions[7].a/.sol and extras[2].items[10], extras[2].items[25] records Bassanio's refusal and Antonio's pressure with Gratiano carrying the ring in Scene 2, and cards[14]'s front now asks for \"Ways...\" to match its three-way back — and on a fresh reading I checked every one of the roughly forty distinct quotations of the play (the three casket inscriptions and \"All that glisters is not gold\", the bond's \"an equal pound\" of \"fair flesh\", \"How like a fawning publican he looks!\", \"misbeliever, cut-throat dog\", \"spet upon my Jewish gaberdine\", \"suff'rance is the badge of all our tribe\", \"Hath not a Jew eyes?\" and \"The villainy you teach me I will execute\", \"My daughter! O my ducats! O my daughter!\", the Leah ring and \"a wilderness of monkeys\", \"unlesson'd girl, unschool'd, unpractis'd\", \"So may the outward shows be least themselves\", \"The devil can cite Scripture for his purpose\", \"The duke cannot deny the course of law\" with \"the trade and profit of the city / Consisteth of all nations\", \"I'll have my bond\", \"Which is the merchant here, and which the Jew?\", \"The quality of mercy is not strained\" and \"On what compulsion must I?\", \"I crave the law, / The penalty and forfeit of my bond\", \"Tarry a little, there is something else\" and \"no jot of blood\", \"I am content\" and \"I am not well\", \"How sweet the moonlight sleeps upon this bank!\" and \"How far that little candle throws his beams!\") plus every act-and-scene placement, the dates and sources (1290 and the 1650s, the 1516 ghetto and its distinguishing mark, the 1571 capped rate, 1598 Stationers' Register and Meres, the 1600 quarto and the 1623 Folio, Il Pecorone and Gesta Romanorum, Marlowe's Barabas) and the two prescribed editions, and found nothing untrue: the answer keys all match their solutions, the hints stop short of the answers, the tiers are honest, the two model paragraphs are correctly levelled against this board's grid and the Level 2 one is diagnosed properly, and the two residual faults above are imprecisions of phrasing in solutions rather than false statements about the play, so a teacher could publish this as it stands."}}},"heritage-pride-and-prejudice":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-pride-and-prejudice","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Pride and Prejudice is one of the literary heritage set texts for Component 2, Section B of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE in English Literature (4ET1). You answer one 30-mark essay question, chosen from two, in roughly 45 minutes of a 1 hour 30 minute paper, and the marks divide evenly: 10 for AO1 (close knowledge and understanding, a critical style, an informed personal engagement), 10 for AO2 (analysis of language, form and structure) and 10 for AO4 (the relationship between the novel and the context in which it was written). Because all three strands are equal, a paragraph that only retells the story, or only spots a technique, or only bolts on a sentence of history, is throwing away two thirds of what it could earn. This room trains the single habit the grid rewards: move from a precisely chosen moment in the novel, to what Austen does with language, form or structure at that moment, to the Regency conditions that make the choice mean something, and finish with a judgement. The paper is open book, so a clean, unmarked copy of the prescribed edition is in front of you — which means vague, half-remembered quotation is no longer an excuse, and the marks go to discriminating selection.","idea":[{"code":"Component 2: Content overview","h":"One heritage novel, studied whole","t":"For Component 2 you study two set texts: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. Pride and Prejudice is the heritage choice here, and the specification asks for the study of whole texts, not extracts — you must \"Develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect\" and \"Develop skills to maintain a critical style and informed personal response\". That word whole is the examinable point. Austen wrote the novel in three volumes and 61 chapters, and its meanings are built across that span: Darcy's snub at the Meryton assembly in Volume 1 only becomes significant once Volume 3 has shown him at Pemberley. A top-level answer therefore ranges deliberately across all three volumes, choosing one moment from early, one from the middle and one from late, and shows that it is arguing about a designed book rather than about its favourite scene."},{"code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","h":"Close knowledge, a critical style, an informed personal engagement","t":"AO1 is worth 10 of the 30 marks: \"Demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement.\" The specification's own list spells it out — learners must demonstrate a close understanding of their heritage text, maintain a critical style, present an informed personal engagement and identify and use relevant examples from the text. Close knowledge means you can say which volume a moment falls in, who is present and what has just happened; critical style means the register of argument, not of retelling (\"Austen positions the reader to…\", not \"then Lydia runs off\"); personal engagement means a reading that is yours and is defended, not an opinion announced. The grid climbs from \"largely narrative\" at Level 2 to \"assured… perceptive critical style\" at Level 5. A top-level answer makes its own claim in the first sentence and then makes the novel prove it, quoting in phrases rather than in chunks."},{"code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","h":"Austen's methods: irony, free indirect style, dialogue, letters, the shape of the book","t":"AO2 is the other 10 marks that come from the page: \"Analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects.\" The specification asks you to understand how writers create literary effects and to \"understand and use appropriate literary terminology\", and the grid moves from \"Minimal identification\" at Level 1, through \"Sustained analysis\" at Level 4, to \"Cohesive evaluation\" at Level 5 — so naming a device scores almost nothing, and tracing how it makes meaning scores highly. In this novel the methods worth knowing are the ironic narrator (the opening sentence offers the neighbourhood's wish as a universal truth); free indirect style, which lets the narration borrow Elizabeth's voice so the reader shares a judgement that turns out to be wrong; dialogue that characterises by idiom (Mr Collins's servility, Mrs Bennet's nerves); and the letter, used as a structural hinge. A top-level answer analyses form and structure as well as words, and evaluates the effect rather than reporting it."},{"code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","h":"Reading the novel against the world that produced it","t":"AO4 is the last 10 marks: \"Show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written.\" Section B is the only part of Component 2 that carries AO4. Its 30 marks split 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, and AO4 is worth 20% of the whole qualification (30 of the 150 raw marks); these 10 marks are 10 of those 150, about 7% of the qualification on their own. The specification says learners \"should also understand the relationship between their text and the context in which it was written\" and must \"understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts\". The wording is doing real work: contextual factors must influence your interpretation, not decorate it. For Pride and Prejudice the load-bearing facts are the entail that keeps the Bennet daughters from inheriting Longbourn, the absence of any profession a gentleman's daughter could enter, the incomes Austen names, and the wartime militia quartered at Meryton. The Assignment B grid ends at context \"integrated convincingly into the response\", so a top-level answer uses context inside the sentence that makes the literary point, never in a paragraph of its own."},{"code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","h":"The five categories of context the specification names","t":"The specification lists five kinds of context that affect a writer's work and a reader's response: the writer's own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing, only where these relate to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, for example attitudes in society and the expectations of different cultural groups; the literary context, for example literary movements or genres; and the way texts are received and engaged with by different audiences at different times, for example how a text may be read differently in the 21st century from when it was written. Notice the restriction on the first category — biography only where it relates to the text, so \"Austen never married\" is worth nothing unless it is doing something in your argument. The fifth category is the one candidates forget and examiners reward: saying how a modern reader's response to Charlotte Lucas or to Lydia differs from an 1813 reader's is AO4, and a top-level answer uses at least two categories rather than relying on social history alone."},{"code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","h":"What the question looks like, and what you may take in","t":"The assessment overview is exact: \"Section B – Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each of the set texts. This section assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4.\" In the breakdown these are Questions 11 to 22, carrying 10 marks AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes, is worth 60 raw marks and 40% of the International GCSE, and Section A (modern drama, 30 marks, AO1 and AO2) takes the other half of the time. The component \"is an open book examination and therefore learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room\" — completely unmarked, so no notes, no highlighting and no tabs. A top-level answer uses that copy for accuracy and for one or two well-chosen phrases, not as a quarry: copying long passages burns the roughly 45 minutes the section allows and earns nothing, because the grid rewards discriminating use of relevant examples, not quantity."},{"code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same literary heritage list, so Pride and Prejudice can be assessed by coursework instead. \"Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment on the studied text. This section assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4\", and it \"will be marked out of 30 with 10 marks for AO1, 10 marks for AO2 and 10 marks for AO4\"; the assignment \"must allow learners to access AO1, AO2 and AO4\". The specification prints a model wording for this very novel: \"Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.\" The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, marking is on the same five-level grid on a best-fit basis, and the work is internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. A top-level coursework answer looks exactly like a top-level examination answer, only tighter: with time to redraft, there is no excuse for a paragraph that does not earn all three objectives."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning one quoted line into AO2 analysis","setup":"Question: In what ways does Austen present first impressions in Pride and Prejudice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks) The moment you are working on is the Meryton assembly in Volume 1, Chapter 3 — almost the first thing the reader hears Darcy say in the novel — where Bingley urges Darcy to dance with Elizabeth and Darcy, who has not been introduced to her, replies: \"She is tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me\". Elizabeth is sitting near enough to overhear him. Your task is to write the AO2 move of one paragraph — show how the language and its placing create an effect on the reader.","steps":["Name what Austen has chosen to do with form: she gives the judgement to Darcy in direct speech and puts Elizabeth within earshot, so the reader meets him through an overheard insult rather than through a narrator's description.","Work on the diction: \"tolerable\" is a word of cold measurement, and \"not handsome enough to tempt me\" grades Elizabeth against a standard Darcy has set himself, so the phrasing exposes his pride without the narrator having to name it.","Work on the placing: the remark arrives in the third chapter, before the reader knows anything else about him, so it fixes a first impression that the rest of the novel then has to dismantle.","Judge the effect: the line is comic at Darcy's expense and wounding to Elizabeth at the same moment, and that double edge is exactly how Austen makes a first impression feel both convincing and untrustworthy."],"cues":["Before you touch a single word, what has Austen decided about form here — who speaks the judgement, and who is allowed to hear it?","Which word in the line does most of the measuring, and what does the act of measuring tell you about the speaker?","Why does it matter that this is almost the first thing Darcy says in the whole novel?","What two opposite effects does the line have on a reader at once, and how does that answer a question about first impressions?"]},{"title":"Integrating context so it earns AO4 instead of sitting beside it","setup":"Question: Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks) You are working on Charlotte Lucas in Volume 1, who accepts Mr Collins three chapters after Elizabeth has refused him, and who tells Elizabeth \"I am not romantic, you know\" and that she asks \"only a comfortable home\". Mr Collins is the entailed heir to Longbourn, and no profession was open to a gentleman's daughter, so marriage was the only provision available to a woman in Charlotte's position. A weak answer describes Charlotte, then adds a separate closing sentence about women in the past. Your task is to build the same paragraph with the context integrated into the literary claim.","steps":["Make the textual claim first, from the words on the page: the flat, level phrasing presents Charlotte's decision as a calculation she has already finished making, not as a defeat she is confessing.","Reach for only the context that claim needs: a gentleman's daughter had no profession open to her, and Longbourn is entailed away from the Bennet girls to Mr Collins himself, so a settled marriage was the one form of provision available.","Fuse the two in one sentence: because Austen has already let the entail hang over the Bennets, Charlotte's plainness reads as clear sight rather than coldness, and the reader is pushed to weigh security as a substitute for feeling.","Turn it back on the question: on this reading Charlotte's marriage is not a form of love at all but its absence, and saying so gives the essay the qualification that \"to what extent\" rewards."],"cues":["What does the plainness of Charlotte's phrasing suggest about how long she has been thinking about this?","Which single fact about property or income in her world turns that calculation from shameful into reasonable?","How can those two observations be joined so that the history is doing work inside the argument rather than trailing after it?","The question asks about forms of love — so what judgement does this paragraph now let you make?"]},{"title":"Building one paragraph that earns AO1, AO2 and AO4 together","setup":"Question: How does Austen portray marriage in Pride and Prejudice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks) Work on Mr Collins's proposal to Elizabeth in Volume 1, in which Collins, the entailed heir to Longbourn, sets out three numbered reasons for marrying, none of them love, before closing by assuring her \"in the most animated language of the violence of my affection\"; on her refusal; and on her father's response when Mrs Bennet demands he overrule her: \"An unhappy alternative is before you, Elizabeth.\" Your task is to write one complete paragraph in which every one of the three objectives is earned.","steps":["Set the point as a claim, not a topic: Austen stages the proposal as a transaction so that the reader hears marriage being discussed in the language of business and duty.","Take the AO1 evidence in a phrase and locate it precisely: Collins proposes in Volume 1 with a numbered list of motives from which love is entirely missing, and Elizabeth has to refuse him repeatedly before he will believe her.","Take the AO2 method: the comedy comes from form — a proposal scene, the most feeling-laden convention the novel has, is filled with the syntax of a sermon, and Mr Bennet's balanced sentence turns the family crisis into an epigram.","Take the AO4 strand inside the same argument: Collins is the entailed heir, so his offer really would have saved the family home, which is why Mrs Bennet's fury is not merely silly and why the joke has an edge of threat.","Close with a mini-judgement: Austen portrays marriage as a contract the period made necessary, and then uses Elizabeth's refusal to insist that necessity is not the same as consent."],"cues":["What claim about marriage can this scene support, phrased as an argument rather than as a heading?","Which precisely located detail of the proposal proves the claim without needing a long quotation?","What is odd about the form of this scene, given what proposal scenes usually sound like?","Which fact about Mr Collins's position makes Mrs Bennet's reaction understandable rather than ridiculous?","What judgement does the paragraph now owe the question about marriage?"]},{"title":"Reading structure: why the letter is the hinge of the book","setup":"Question: Austen shows that people can change. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks) Work on Volume 2, where Darcy, the morning after Elizabeth has refused him, hands her a letter beginning \"Be not alarmed, Madam, on receiving this letter\", which explains his dealings with Wickham and his part in separating Bingley from Jane; and on Elizabeth's reaction after re-reading it, \"How despicably have I acted!\", \"vanity, not love, has been my folly\", and \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\" Your task is to argue from structure rather than from character.","steps":["Locate the letter in the design of the book: it falls at the centre of the 61-chapter book, in the middle of the second volume, exactly where the reader's accumulated judgement of Wickham and Darcy is strongest, which is what gives its correction such force.","Analyse the form: a letter suspends dialogue entirely, so Darcy cannot be interrupted, softened or answered, and the reader receives his account at the same pace and in the same isolation as Elizabeth does.","Analyse the reading and re-reading: Austen makes Elizabeth study the letter repeatedly, so the change is shown as slow work of re-interpretation rather than a sudden conversion, and free indirect style lets her self-accusation sound in her own voice.","Turn the structure into judgement: because the novel is built so that its pivot is an act of reading, Austen suggests that people change not through feeling but through evidence, which is why Elizabeth's transformation persuades where Lydia's never could."],"cues":["Where does this moment sit in the three-volume shape, and why would Austen put a correction exactly there?","What can a letter do to a scene that a conversation cannot?","Why does Austen have Elizabeth go back over the letter more than once instead of believing it at once?","If the pivot of the whole book is somebody reading, what does that imply about how change happens in this novel?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions in the examination","setup":"In Component 2 you have 1 hour 30 minutes for two sections and may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition. Section B offers you a choice of two questions on Pride and Prejudice, each worth 30 marks and each ending \"You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel\": (a) In what ways does Austen present family relationships in Pride and Prejudice? (b) Pride and Prejudice is a novel about money rather than love. To what extent do you agree? Your task is to choose one and plan its opening two paragraphs.","steps":["Test each question against the evidence you can actually reach: family relationships offers the Bennets, the Lucases, Lady Catherine and her nephew, and the Gardiners, which is several households and risks becoming a tour rather than an argument.","Test each against AO4 reach: the money proposition pulls the entail, named incomes and marriage settlements straight into the argument, so context integrates itself, whereas family relationships needs the subtler context of parental duty and reputation.","Test each against the judgement it demands: \"to what extent do you agree\" is only the easier option if you can argue the other side, so take it only if you can name moments where money plainly is not the motive.","Commit and plan: answer the money question, open by agreeing that the novel prices everything, and set Charlotte's bargain against Elizabeth's refusal of a man worth ten thousand a year as the counter-case that will run through the essay."],"cues":["Which of the two questions can you support with moments you could find in the book in under a minute?","Which question makes the context strand easiest to integrate rather than bolt on?","What does the phrase \"to what extent do you agree\" oblige you to be able to do before you choose it?","Having chosen, which two moments should stand opposite each other so the argument has a spine?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How are the 30 marks for the Pride and Prejudice question divided between the assessment objectives?","a":"Evenly: 10 marks AO1, 10 marks AO2 and 10 marks AO4. Every developed paragraph should be trying to earn all three."},{"q":"The examination is open book. What exactly may you take in, and what does that change about how you quote?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition — no notes, highlighting or tabs. Quotation must be exact and short, chosen for the point, not copied at length."},{"q":"A paragraph ends: \"This shows that women in Austen's time had to marry for money.\" Why will this not earn AO4 at the top level?","a":"The context is bolted on after the literary point instead of integrated into it, and it is generalised rather than tied to the novel — the entail on Longbourn, or Charlotte's lack of any profession, would tie it down."},{"q":"Which of the five categories of context do candidates most often miss, and what would using it look like here?","a":"How texts are received by different audiences at different times — for example, showing that a modern reader is likelier to read Charlotte's marriage as an indictment of her society than as a lapse of taste."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section B: one 30-mark essay question on the heritage text, chosen from two — 10 marks AO1, 10 marks AO2, 10 marks AO4. Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes, 60 marks and 40% of the International GCSE, and it is open book with a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition.","The five categories of context: the writer's own life and situation (only where it relates to the text); the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context, such as movements or genres; and how texts are received by different audiences at different times.","Pride and Prejudice was published in January 1813 by Thomas Egerton in three volumes and 61 chapters, anonymously, its title page reading only \"By the Author of Sense and Sensibility\"; Austen had drafted it in 1796–97 as First Impressions.","The money in the novel: Darcy has ten thousand a year and Pemberley in Derbyshire; Bingley four or five thousand a year; Mr Bennet two thousand a year; five thousand pounds was settled by marriage articles on Mrs Bennet and the children, the daughters' shares falling in only after the deaths of both their parents; Georgiana Darcy has thirty thousand pounds; Wickham took three thousand pounds in place of the living.","The entail: Longbourn is settled on the nearest male heir, Mr Collins, so no Bennet daughter can inherit it — the fact that makes Mrs Bennet's campaign rational and Charlotte's bargain intelligible.","The structural hinge: Darcy's letter, beginning \"Be not alarmed, Madam, on receiving this letter\", falls in the middle of the second volume, the morning after Elizabeth's refusal of him, and produces her \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\"","Component 3, Assignment B is the coursework alternative: one 30-mark teacher-devised essay on the same text, 10 marks each for AO1, AO2 and AO4, advisory length 650–800 words, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson."],"questions":[{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: which three assessment objectives does the Pride and Prejudice question in Component 2, Section B assess, and how many marks does each carry? (3 marks)","a":"AO1, AO2 and AO4, at 10 raw marks each, making 30 in all.","sol":"Section B – Literary Heritage Texts is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two, and it assesses AO1 (demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement) for 10 marks, AO2 (analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects) for 10 marks, and AO4 (show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written) for 10 marks. AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2. Because the three weigh equally, a paragraph that does only one of them is working at a third of its value.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["One of the four objectives in this qualification belongs only to the poetry paper, so it cannot be here.","Work out which objective the instruction \"and refer to the context of the novel\" is there to carry.","Give the three objectives, then show how the marks are shared between them."]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: explain what the entail on Longbourn is, and give one reason it matters to the plot of Pride and Prejudice. (3 marks)","a":"Longbourn is settled on the nearest male heir, Mr Collins, so the Bennet daughters cannot inherit; when Mr Bennet dies they lose the house, which is why marriage is urgent.","sol":"An entail is a legal settlement fixing who inherits an estate, and Longbourn is entailed on the nearest male relative, Mr Collins, because Mr Bennet has five daughters and no son. The daughters therefore cannot inherit the house or the two thousand a year that supports it; on their father's death they will have only their shares of the five thousand pounds settled on the children, which is secured to them after the deaths of both their parents. This is why Mrs Bennet's obsession with marrying her daughters off is, beneath the comedy, entirely rational; why Mr Collins's proposal to Elizabeth would genuinely have secured the family; and why Charlotte Lucas's acceptance of the same man reads as calculation rather than folly.","m":3,"d":1,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"],"hints":["The word is a legal one about who may inherit land, not about money the Bennets owe.","Ask who stands to gain Longbourn when Mr Bennet dies, and what that does to his daughters' position.","Define the settlement in a clause, then name one consequence for a character's choices."]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: identify two letters in Pride and Prejudice and state what each changes for Elizabeth. (4 marks)","a":"Darcy's letter at Hunsford reverses her reading of Wickham and of Darcy himself; Jane's letters reaching her at Lambton bring the news of Lydia's elopement and end the Derbyshire visit.","sol":"Darcy's letter, handed to Elizabeth the morning after she refuses him, in the middle of the second volume, begins \"Be not alarmed, Madam, on receiving this letter\" and sets out his dealings with Wickham and his part in separating Bingley from Jane. Re-reading it destroys her confidence in her own judgement and produces \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\" In Volume 3 the two letters from Jane that catch up with Elizabeth at Lambton report Lydia's elopement with Wickham; they end the Derbyshire visit, expose the family to disgrace and, by having Darcy walk in as she reads them, put the couple's new understanding under immediate strain. Both letters work as structural hinges: each arrives to overturn what the reader has just been allowed to settle into.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"],"hints":["Both of the most important ones arrive when Elizabeth is away from Longbourn and cannot consult anybody.","Think about what a letter lets Austen do to the pace of a novel that a conversation cannot.","Name each letter with its writer and its place in the volume structure, then say in a clause what it overturns."]},{"q":"Recall task, not an exam tariff: explain what free indirect style is, and identify one moment in Pride and Prejudice where Austen uses it. (4 marks)","a":"Third-person narration that takes on a character's own words and judgement without quotation marks; for example, at Pemberley when Elizabeth feels \"to be mistress of Pemberley might be something!\"","sol":"Free indirect style is third-person past-tense narration that borrows a character's vocabulary, rhythm and point of view without marking it as speech or thought, so the reader is inside the character's judgement while the narrator remains in control of the sentence. Austen is its great English exponent. In Volume 3, as Elizabeth looks over the grounds at Pemberley, the narration reports that she felt \"to be mistress of Pemberley might be something!\" — the exclamation mark and the casual \"might be something\" are Elizabeth's own idiom, not the narrator's, so the reader hears her half-amused self-awareness from within. The effect matters for AO2 because it lets Austen make the reader share Elizabeth's prejudices earlier in the novel and then feel corrected along with her.","m":4,"d":1,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"],"hints":["It is not speech and it is not reported thought; look for narration that suddenly sounds like the character.","The tell is usually in tone — an exclamation, an idiom or an irony that the narrator would not own.","Define the technique in one sentence, then give one located moment and say whose voice you can hear in it."]},{"q":"Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Agree, but qualify: alongside Elizabeth and Darcy's love of esteem there are Jane and Bingley's easy affection, Lydia and Wickham's appetite, Charlotte's contract and the Bennets' worn-out attraction — and some of these are shown as love's absence, which is how Austen defines it.","sol":"Austen does show many forms of attachment, but the essay should argue that she does so in order to grade them. Jane and Bingley's mutual sweetness is real and yet requires other people's management to survive, which suggests feeling without judgement is not enough. Lydia and Wickham represent appetite dignified by nobody: Austen dismisses the marriage in a clause about affection sinking into indifference, and its form — an elopement resolved off-stage by Darcy's money — denies it any romantic treatment at all. Charlotte Lucas belongs on the scale only as its zero: \"I am not romantic, you know\", she says, asking \"only a comfortable home\", and because Longbourn is entailed and no profession was open to a gentleman's daughter, Austen makes her calculation respectable rather than contemptible. Against these, Elizabeth and Darcy's is the only attachment built on revised judgement: it survives a refusal, a letter and a confession — \"I have been a selfish being all my life, in practice, though not in principle\" — so the novel's structure itself argues that love worth the name is a conclusion, not an impulse. I agree the reader sees many forms of love, but only because Austen needs the weaker forms to measure the one she endorses.","m":30,"d":2,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"],"hints":["The proposition is easy to agree with, so the marks are in what you do after agreeing — think about why Austen would want a range at all.","Set the couples against one another rather than describing them in turn, and let the entail and the incomes explain why some attachments are priced rather than felt.","Open by conceding the range, then argue that the range is a scale, and let each paragraph take one form of attachment and place it on that scale."]},{"q":"In what ways does Austen present pride in Pride and Prejudice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Pride is presented as social, not merely personal: Darcy's rank-based pride, Elizabeth's pride in her own discernment, Lady Catherine's pride of birth and Mr Collins's borrowed pride, with the novel curing only the two that can be argued out of it.","sol":"A top answer distinguishes kinds of pride and shows Austen grading them. Darcy's is introduced through overheard speech — \"She is tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me\" — so the reader convicts him before meeting him, and his warning \"My good opinion once lost is lost for ever\" shows pride hardening into principle. Elizabeth's pride is subtler and is the novel's real subject: Austen uses free indirect style to make the reader share her satisfaction in her own penetration, so that \"How despicably have I acted!\" and \"vanity, not love, has been my folly\" indict the reader as well. Mary Bennet's pedantry supplies the vocabulary early on — \"Vanity and pride are different things, though the words are often used synonymously\" — and Austen then tests the distinction on everyone. Lady Catherine shows pride of rank with no self-knowledge in it, demanding \"Are the shades of Pemberley to be thus polluted?\", and Mr Collins parodies it by being proud of his patroness. Contextually, pride in this society is inseparable from rank and property: Darcy's ten thousand a year makes his condescension structural, and Elizabeth's refusal of it is socially reckless. Austen therefore presents pride as curable only where it can be reasoned with, which is why the two proud people who read and revise are the two the novel rewards.","m":30,"d":2,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["More than one character is proud, and the title's abstract noun is not attached to a single person.","Consider how the narration makes the reader share one character's pride before exposing it, and what that does to your response.","Take one kind of pride per paragraph and make each paragraph end by placing that kind against the one before it."]},{"q":"How does Austen portray marriage in Pride and Prejudice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"As a contract the period made necessary and the novel refuses to romanticise: four marriages set against one another, with the ironic opening sentence framing the whole as a market, and only the union founded on revised judgement endorsed.","sol":"The opening sentence sets the terms: \"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife\" presents the neighbourhood's appetite as a law of nature, and the irony tells the reader that in this world marriage is first an economic event. Austen then supplies test cases. The Bennets' marriage shows what attraction without judgement becomes: Mr Bennet, \"quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice\", has retreated into laughter at his wife, and the cost is Lydia. Collins's proposal is staged as a transaction — a list of reasons for marrying in which affection never appears — and Elizabeth's refusal is met by her father's \"An unhappy alternative is before you, Elizabeth.\" Charlotte accepts the same man with \"I am not romantic, you know\", asking \"only a comfortable home\"; because Longbourn is entailed on Collins and no gentleman's daughter could earn a living, Austen makes the bargain reasonable, not squalid. Structurally the novel then runs three proposals to Elizabeth in sequence — Collins, Darcy refused, Darcy accepted — so the reader measures each against the last. Austen portrays marriage, finally, as a settlement whose economics she never denies, but which she will only endorse where both parties have had to change their minds.","m":30,"d":2,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"],"hints":["The novel gives you several marriages on purpose; ask what each is for.","The first sentence of the book is already an argument about marriage — work out whose voice it is speaking in.","Group the marriages so that each paragraph sets one against another, and let the entail explain why the calculating ones are not simply condemned."]},{"q":"In what ways does Austen present the character of Elizabeth Bennet? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Through wit in dialogue, through free indirect style that makes the reader adopt her judgement, and through a structure of error and correction — so that her intelligence is both her attraction and her fault.","sol":"Austen builds Elizabeth first through dialogue: she is the only woman in the novel who answers back, and her epigrams — \"I could easily forgive his pride, if he had not mortified mine\" — characterise her as quick and as vulnerable in the same breath. Second, through narrative method: free indirect style keeps the reader inside her judgement, so the reader shares her contempt for Darcy and her trust in Wickham and is therefore corrected alongside her. Third, through structure: the novel is organised as a sequence of readings and re-readings, and its centre is Darcy's letter, after which \"How despicably have I acted!\" and \"Till this moment, I never knew myself\" convert her wit into self-knowledge. Austen also uses other women as instruments: Jane's charity, Charlotte's pragmatism, Lydia's appetite and Caroline Bingley's malice each isolate something in Elizabeth. Contextually, her refusals are the point — a woman with a share in five thousand pounds refusing the entailed heir and then a man with ten thousand a year is risking everything her society offers, which is why her later admission that to be mistress of Pemberley \"might be something!\" is allowed its honesty. Austen presents her, then, as a heroine whose merit is not being right but being able to find out that she was wrong.","m":30,"d":2,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["Think about how you know her — what proportion of your impression comes from her talking rather than from being described?","The narration is not neutral about her; ask what it makes the reader do with her opinions before the letter arrives.","Organise by method, not by plot: one paragraph on dialogue, one on narration, one on the structure of her error and correction."]},{"q":"How does Austen portray parents and their children in Pride and Prejudice? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"As a study in failed guardianship: Mr Bennet's wit as abdication, Mrs Bennet's campaign as the only rational response to the entail, Lady Catherine's tyranny over Anne, and the Gardiners as the novel's real parents.","sol":"Austen's parents are judged by what they do with authority. Mr Bennet's \"quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice\" make him the most entertaining figure in the book, and Austen uses that charm against him: \"For what do we live, but to make sport for our neighbours, and laugh at them in our turn?\" is delightful and is also the philosophy that lets Lydia go to Brighton. His \"You have delighted us long enough\" is wit at a daughter's expense. Mrs Bennet, whose \"business of her life was to get her daughters married\", is ridiculed by the narrator but vindicated by the entail: with Longbourn settled on Mr Collins and five daughters to place, her campaign is the only rational one available to her, and Austen's irony cuts at a society that gives an unintelligent woman one task. Lady Catherine shows parental authority as pure rank, disposing of Anne's future and then attempting Elizabeth's. Against all of these, the Gardiners — relations in trade, living in Gracechurch Street — supply the judgement, travel and calm that the Bennets lack, and it is with them that Elizabeth reaches Pemberley. Austen therefore locates good parenting outside the gentry and outside the family that needs it most, and makes neglect, not cruelty, the danger.","m":30,"d":2,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Component 2: Content overview"],"hints":["Ask which adult in the novel actually protects a young person, and where that adult comes from socially.","Mr Bennet's jokes are also decisions; consider what the narrator is doing by letting him be so funny.","Pair each parent with a consequence in the plot, so the essay argues about guardianship rather than describing characters."]},{"q":"Pride and Prejudice is a novel about money rather than love. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Largely agree on the novel's conditions but not on its values: Austen prices everything and refuses to pretend otherwise, yet builds her plot on two refusals of money, so money is the medium and judgement is the subject.","sol":"The novel's first sentence makes fortune the engine of courtship, and Austen is relentless about figures: Bingley arrives with four or five thousand a year, Darcy with ten thousand and Pemberley, Georgiana with thirty thousand, Wickham with three thousand pounds taken in place of a living, the Bennet girls with five thousand between them and a house entailed away. Estates do the work of characterisation — Rosings displays wealth, Pemberley, \"a large, handsome stone building, standing well on rising ground\", displays taste and stewardship, and Elizabeth's response that to be its mistress \"might be something!\" is the novel's most honest sentence. Against that, the plot is built on refusals of money: Elizabeth declines the entailed heir and then tells Darcy he is \"the last man in the world whom I could ever be prevailed on to marry\", and Lydia's elopement has to be bought out of disgrace with Darcy's cash, which is presented as shameful necessity, not as romance. Charlotte's bargain is the test case and Austen makes it respectable, which shows the argument is not that money is wicked but that it is inescapable. I agree the novel is about money in the sense that money sets every condition; I disagree that it is about money rather than love, because Austen spends the book distinguishing an attachment that survives the loss of advantage from one that does not.","m":30,"d":3,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"],"hints":["Count how often Austen actually names a sum, and ask what she wants that precision to do to the reader.","Look for the places where a character turns money down; those moments are the counter-argument the question needs.","Concede the strong half of the proposition early, then spend the essay on the distinction between money as condition and money as value."]},{"q":"In what ways does the structure of Pride and Prejudice shape the reader's changing judgement of Darcy? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"By withholding his point of view: an overheard insult, a rival's account, a letter at the centre, then a house and a housekeeper — so the reader is convicted of misjudging him before he is allowed to explain.","sol":"Austen's method is sequence. Darcy is introduced at the Meryton assembly through an overheard line — \"She is tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me\" — and the novel then supplies a rival narrator, Wickham, whose charm the reader has no means of testing. Because free indirect style keeps the narration inside Elizabeth's judgement, the reader's impression is not merely reported but adopted. The first proposal, placed in the middle of the second volume, is written so that Darcy's honesty about her family's inferiority makes him more offensive at the very moment he is most sincere. The letter — \"Be not alarmed, Madam, on receiving this letter\" — then reverses the evidence without reversing the manner, so the reader must revise a judgement rather than be handed a new one. Volume 3 completes the work through place and testimony rather than speech: Pemberley, its grounds and Mrs Reynolds's account of her master let the reader see him through the eyes of people who depend on him, which in a society where a landowner's character was his tenants' fortune is the strongest evidence available. Only after all this is Darcy allowed a confession — \"I have been a selfish being all my life, in practice, though not in principle.\" The structure is therefore a machine for producing prejudice in the reader and then charging the reader for it.","m":30,"d":3,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["Ask when the reader is first given any evidence about Darcy that does not come through Elizabeth or through Wickham.","Track the order of the evidence rather than the order of events, and notice what kind of evidence each volume supplies.","Build the essay in the novel's own order, ending with what the reader is made to feel about their own earlier judgement."]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Austen's presentation of Lydia Bennet may be read differently in the 21st century from when the novel was written. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"In 1813 Lydia's elopement reads as ruin deservedly courted and narrowly repaired; a modern reader is likelier to see a fifteen-year-old failed by her parents and traded by Darcy's money, which the novel's own structure quietly supports.","sol":"This question is squarely in the specification's fifth category of context: how texts are received by different audiences at different times. To a reader in 1813, Lydia's conduct threatens the whole family's marriageability, because a sister's disgrace was contagious — which is exactly the calculation Mr Collins's letter of condolence makes explicit. Austen's methods encourage that reading: Lydia is characterised by exclamation and appetite, she is given no interiority, and her letter announcing the elopement is comically careless about the catastrophe it causes. Yet the same methods support a modern reading. Lydia is the youngest, fifteen when the novel opens; she is sent to Brighton because her father cannot be bothered to refuse; and her rescue is a financial transaction conducted between men, with Darcy paying Wickham's debts and buying the marriage while Lydia is told nothing that matters. Structurally, Austen denies her any correction scene, and closes her story with affection sinking into indifference, which reads less like a moral than like a sentence. A 21st-century reader, less persuaded that female reputation is a family asset, is therefore likelier to prosecute Mr Bennet and Wickham than Lydia. The strongest answer argues that both readings are available because Austen builds the critique of the system into the comedy, and reaches a judgement about which the text supports more firmly.","m":30,"d":3,"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"],"hints":["One of the five categories of context is about audiences at different times; this question is that category.","Separate what Austen's own society stood to lose from Lydia's conduct from what a reader now notices about her age and the adults around her.","Give one paragraph to the contemporary reading, one to the modern, and make the last paragraph decide which the text itself better supports."]},{"q":"Austen's irony does more work than her plot. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Largely agree: irony carries the social criticism, the characterisation and the reader's misdirection, though the plot's architecture of refusals, letters and returns is what makes the irony land.","sol":"Austen's irony operates at three levels and the essay should take them in turn. At sentence level, the famous opening — \"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife\" — states the neighbourhood's appetite as natural law, so the reader learns in one line how to read the whole book. At character level, irony does the work of judgement without editorial comment: Mrs Bennet, whose \"business of her life was to get her daughters married\", is mocked by a narrator who has already ensured the entail makes her right; Mr Collins's reasons for marrying are self-condemning; Mr Bennet's \"For what do we live, but to make sport for our neighbours, and laugh at them in our turn?\" is charming and is his abdication. At structural level, dramatic irony makes the reader complicit: we adopt Elizabeth's misreadings through free indirect style, so the correction in Volume 2 lands on us. Against the proposition, the plot is not incidental — the sequence of proposals, the letter at the centre, the visit to Pemberley and the return of Bingley are what give the ironies something to bite on, and Lydia's elopement is a plot event that irony alone could not have produced. I agree that irony carries the meaning, but only because the plot keeps handing it the right situations.","m":30,"d":3,"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["Irony works at more than one scale here — a sentence, a character, and the reader's own position.","For the other side of the argument, ask what the ironies would have to bite on if the events were rearranged.","Take one scale of irony per paragraph, then give the counter-case its own paragraph before judging."]},{"q":"Component 3, Assignment B (the coursework route; advisory length 650–800 words): Explore the ways in which Austen presents social class in Pride and Prejudice. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Class is presented as a hierarchy under strain: rank without merit (Lady Catherine, Collins), land with merit (Darcy at Pemberley), and money made in trade (the Bingleys, the Gardiners) — with Elizabeth's wit as the solvent.","sol":"Within 650 to 800 words the assignment must earn all three objectives, so the plan should be tight: three developed paragraphs and a judgement. First, rank asserted without merit. Lady Catherine's speech is all imperative and interrogative, and her \"Are the shades of Pemberley to be thus polluted?\" makes contamination the metaphor for a marriage below rank; Mr Collins parodies the same hierarchy from below, his servility towards a patroness who controls his living. Second, rank justified by stewardship. Pemberley, \"a large, handsome stone building, standing well on rising ground\", is described so that taste and restraint stand for character, and Austen has Darcy's own housekeeper testify for him, which converts his rank into responsibility. Third, money without rank. The Bingleys' fortune was made in trade and Caroline's snobbery is the anxiety of the newly arrived, while the Gardiners, who live in Gracechurch Street and are in trade themselves, have the manners and judgement that the gentry in the novel lack — a deliberate embarrassment to the hierarchy. Contextually this is Regency England, where land conferred status and commercial wealth was buying its way in; Austen, a clergyman's daughter on the edge of the gentry, writes from exactly that seam. The judgement: Austen does not abolish class, since Elizabeth marries into it, but she repeatedly makes conduct rather than rank the measure, so the novel legitimises the hierarchy only on condition that it earns itself.","m":30,"d":3,"codes":["Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"],"hints":["Three groups in the novel hold status on different grounds; separating them gives you the shape of the essay.","Consider who is allowed to speak well of Darcy, and what kind of evidence that testimony counts as in this society.","With so few words, plan three paragraphs and a judgement, and let each paragraph carry its context inside its literary point."]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section B literary heritage question carry, and how are they split between the assessment objectives?","a":"30 marks: 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4."},{"q":"What exactly may you take into the Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text."},{"q":"Whose letter, in Volume 2, overturns Elizabeth's reading of Wickham, and what does she say about herself after it?","a":"Darcy's letter; she says \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\""},{"q":"Name one of the five categories of context the specification lists.","a":"Any one of: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; how texts are received by different audiences at different times."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Free indirect style","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false,"back":"Third-person narration that borrows a character's own words, rhythm and judgement without quotation marks, so the reader shares Elizabeth's view while Austen quietly signals its error."},{"front":"The entail on Longbourn","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false,"back":"Longbourn is settled on the nearest male heir, Mr Collins. No Bennet daughter can inherit it, so marriage is their only security once their father dies."},{"front":"The opening sentence of Pride and Prejudice","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false,"back":"\"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.\" The neighbourhood's appetite, dressed as natural law."},{"front":"Darcy's first proposal, at Hunsford in Volume 2","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false,"back":"\"In vain have I struggled. It will not do. My feelings will not be repressed.\" He tells Elizabeth \"how ardently I admire and love you\", insults her connections, and is refused."},{"front":"Elizabeth after re-reading Darcy's letter","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false,"back":"\"How despicably have I acted!\"; \"vanity, not love, has been my folly\"; \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\" The novel's turning point of self-knowledge."},{"front":"Charlotte Lucas on marriage","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false,"back":"\"Happiness in marriage is entirely a matter of chance.\" Accepting Mr Collins she says \"I am not romantic, you know\" and asks \"only a comfortable home\"."},{"front":"Lady Catherine de Bourgh at Longbourn, Volume 3","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false,"back":"She calls Elizabeth an \"obstinate, headstrong girl\" and demands \"Are the shades of Pemberley to be thus polluted?\" Her interference provokes the second proposal."},{"front":"Novel of manners","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false,"back":"A novel whose drama is conduct — visits, dances, letters, conversation — inside a narrow social class, judged by that class's standards. Pride and Prejudice is the English model."},{"front":"The five categories of context (AO4)","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false,"back":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and how different audiences receive the text at different times."},{"front":"Component 2, Section B tariff","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false,"back":"One 30-mark essay from a choice of two on the heritage text: 10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4. Open book, with a clean, unmarked prescribed edition."},{"front":"Mr Bennet","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false,"back":"A mixture of \"quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice\". He retreats to his library and makes sport of his family — \"You have delighted us long enough\" — failing Lydia by neglect."},{"front":"Pemberley","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false,"back":"Darcy's Derbyshire estate, \"a large, handsome stone building, standing well on rising ground\". Elizabeth feels \"to be mistress of Pemberley might be something!\" Taste and stewardship, not display."},{"front":"An example of Austen's irony","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":true,"back":"The opening sentence offering the neighbourhood's wish as universal truth; Mr Collins's three numbered reasons for marrying, from which affection is missing; the narrator reporting that the business of Mrs Bennet's life was to get her daughters married."},{"front":"George Wickham","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false,"back":"Militia officer, son of Pemberley's late steward. He took three thousand pounds in place of the living, attempted to elope with the fifteen-year-old Georgiana Darcy, and later eloped with Lydia."},{"front":"Whole-text study for Component 2","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false,"back":"You study one modern drama text and one literary heritage text whole, developing analysis of language, form, structure and contextual factors, and a critical style with informed personal response."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","Point: Austen's range of attachments is wider than the word love allows, because the novel keeps setting feeling beside calculation and inviting the reader to price the difference.","Evidence: Charlotte Lucas accepts Mr Collins with the flat admission \"I am not romantic, you know\", asking \"only a comfortable home\".","Analysis of language and form: the parenthetical \"you know\" makes the confession sound like a shared and obvious truth rather than a defeat, and Austen gives it to Charlotte in direct speech so that no narrator is allowed to soften it; the short, level clauses refuse the rhythms of romantic declaration, and \"comfortable\" — chosen instead of happy — measures a marriage in warmth and shelter rather than in feeling.","Analysis of structure: placing this three chapters after Elizabeth has refused the same man turns Charlotte into a structural control — the identical offer, accepted, shows the reader precisely what Elizabeth has risked, and it is the novel's design, not its narrator, that makes the judgement.","Context, integrated: because Longbourn is entailed away from the Bennet daughters to Mr Collins himself, and because no profession was open to a gentleman's daughter, Charlotte's calculation is not cynicism but the only settlement her world offers, which is why Austen lets her keep the reader's respect while refusing her any warmth.","Mini-judgement: I agree that the novel shows many forms of love, but chiefly by including its absence — Charlotte's bargain is the measure against which Elizabeth's eventual union of esteem and feeling is made to look like an achievement rather than a piece of luck.","Why this is Level 5: assured knowledge, discriminating selection of two short phrases rather than a long quotation, cohesive evaluation that treats language, form and structure together, context integrated convincingly inside the literary claim, and a judgement that qualifies the proposition instead of restating it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","Point: Austen shows different types of love in the novel, such as marrying for money and marrying for real love.","Evidence: Charlotte Lucas marries Mr Collins even though she does not love him, and she says that she is not romantic and only wants a comfortable home.","Analysis: this shows that some people married for money rather than for love. The word comfortable shows that she just wants to be safe. Austen uses direct speech here to show Charlotte's opinion.","Context: in Austen's time women had to marry to be secure, and Longbourn was entailed so the Bennet sisters could not inherit it.","Mini-judgement: so I agree that there are different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice.","Why this is only Level 3: the knowledge is sound and the example is clearly relevant, which is what Level 3 rewards, but the paragraph stops short of Level 4 and 5 in five visible ways — the quotation is reported rather than quoted and worked on; the comment on language names direct speech without analysing its effect; there is no comment on structure at all, so one of the three AO2 elements is missing; the context sits in a sentence of its own instead of being integrated into the literary point; and the judgement simply repeats the words of the question.","How to raise it: quote two exact short phrases, analyse one word choice and one structural placing, put the entail inside the sentence that explains why Charlotte's choice is reasonable, and end by qualifying the proposition rather than agreeing with it."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Pride and Prejudice (Jane Austen): characters, plot, themes, form and structure","items":["Elizabeth Bennet: the second of the five Bennet daughters, not yet one and twenty, quick, satirical and the novel's centre of consciousness; her fault is confidence in her own discernment, which she abandons with \"Till this moment, I never knew myself.\"","Fitzwilliam Darcy: master of Pemberley in Derbyshire, with ten thousand a year and an aunt, Lady Catherine de Bourgh; first seen refusing to dance and calling Elizabeth \"tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me\".","Jane Bennet: the eldest sister, beautiful and reserved, whose composure allows Darcy to believe her indifferent to Bingley and so to separate them.","Mr Bennet: a mixture of \"quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice\" who retreats to his library, asks \"For what do we live, but to make sport for our neighbours, and laugh at them in our turn?\", and lets Lydia go to Brighton.","Mrs Bennet: a woman of whom the narrator says \"The business of her life was to get her daughters married; its solace was visiting and news\" — ridiculous, and vindicated by the entail.","Mr Collins: clergyman, cousin and, under the entail, heir to Longbourn; patronised by Lady Catherine de Bourgh, he proposes to Elizabeth, is refused, and within days proposes to Charlotte Lucas, whose acceptance closes Volume 1; the wedding itself follows in Volume 2, Chapter 3 (Chapter 26 of 61).","Charlotte Lucas: Elizabeth's friend, about twenty-seven, who holds that \"Happiness in marriage is entirely a matter of chance\" and accepts Mr Collins for a settled home at Hunsford.","George Wickham: militia officer and son of the late Mr Darcy's steward; he resigned all claim to the living for three thousand pounds, attempted to elope with the fifteen-year-old Georgiana Darcy at Ramsgate, and elopes with Lydia.","Lydia Bennet: the youngest sister, fifteen at the novel's opening, loud and heedless; her elopement from Brighton threatens all five sisters' prospects until Darcy buys the marriage.","Lady Catherine de Bourgh: Darcy's aunt, patroness of Hunsford and mistress of Rosings Park in Kent; she calls Elizabeth an \"obstinate, headstrong girl\" and demands \"Are the shades of Pemberley to be thus polluted?\"","Mr and Mrs Gardiner: Mrs Bennet's brother and his wife, in trade and living in Gracechurch Street, London; they have the judgement the Bennets lack and take Elizabeth to Derbyshire.","Others to know: Charles Bingley, amiable and easily led, with four or five thousand a year; his sister Caroline Bingley; Colonel Fitzwilliam; Georgiana Darcy; Mary and Kitty Bennet; Mrs Reynolds, the housekeeper who testifies for Darcy at Pemberley.","Plot, Volume 1 (Chapters 1 to 23): Bingley takes Netherfield; at the Meryton assembly Darcy snubs Elizabeth; Jane falls ill at Netherfield and Elizabeth walks over to nurse her; Mr Collins arrives, proposes and is refused; Wickham gives Elizabeth his account of Darcy; at the Netherfield ball the Bennets embarrass themselves; Bingley leaves for London; Charlotte accepts Collins.","Plot, Volume 2 (Chapters 24 to 42): Jane goes to London and is kept from Bingley by his sisters; Elizabeth visits Charlotte at Hunsford and meets Darcy and Colonel Fitzwilliam at Rosings; Darcy proposes and is refused; his letter explains Wickham's history and his own interference between Bingley and Jane; Elizabeth returns home and Lydia is invited to Brighton.","Plot, Volume 3 (Chapters 43 to 61): Elizabeth tours Derbyshire with the Gardiners and visits Pemberley, where she meets Darcy again as host; Jane's letters bring news of Lydia's elopement; Darcy finds the couple and pays for the marriage; Bingley returns and proposes to Jane; Lady Catherine confronts Elizabeth at Longbourn; Darcy proposes a second time and is accepted.","Themes: first impressions and the correction of judgement; pride and vanity; marriage as contract and as choice; money, land and rank; reputation and the contagion of disgrace; parental duty; the education of a mind through reading.","Form: a novel of manners and a comedy of courtship, published in three volumes and 61 chapters, driven by dialogue, visits, balls and letters rather than by incident.","Structure: the ironic opening sentence sets the terms; three proposals to Elizabeth (Collins, Darcy refused, Darcy accepted) invite comparison; Darcy's letter, Chapter 35, comes in the middle of the second volume and is the hinge; the journey from Longbourn to Netherfield, Hunsford and Pemberley maps the heroine's changing judgement.","Narrative method: an ironic third-person narrator plus free indirect style, which lets the narration borrow Elizabeth's voice so that the reader adopts her prejudices and is corrected with her.","Key moments examiners reward: the opening sentence; Darcy's \"tolerable\" snub; Elizabeth walking to Netherfield with a muddied petticoat; Collins's proposal and \"An unhappy alternative is before you, Elizabeth\"; Charlotte's acceptance; Darcy's first proposal and \"the last man in the world whom I could ever be prevailed on to marry\"; the letter and \"How despicably have I acted!\"; Pemberley and \"to be mistress of Pemberley might be something!\"; Lydia's elopement and Darcy's intervention; Lady Catherine's confrontation; the second proposal and \"I have been a selfish being all my life, in practice, though not in principle.\""]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile — Pride and Prejudice: the contexts AO4 rewards","items":["The writer's life and situation: Jane Austen (1775 to 1817) was a country clergyman's daughter from Steventon in Hampshire; she drafted the novel as First Impressions in 1796 to 1797, her father offered it to the publisher Thomas Cadell, who declined it, and she revised it for publication years later.","Publication: Pride and Prejudice appeared in January 1813 from Thomas Egerton in three volumes; Austen published anonymously, the title page carrying only \"By the Author of Sense and Sensibility\".","Prescribed editions: Component 2 is open book, and Appendix 3 of the specification prescribes the copy you may take in — Penguin Books, January 2003, ISBN 9780141439518; Pearson Education New Windmills, April 1994, ISBN 9780435126070; OUP Oxford, November 2019, ISBN 9780198826736; Wordsworth Editions Classics, May 1992, ISBN 9781853260001 — and it must be clean and completely unmarked.","Historical setting: Regency England during the war with France — which is why a militia regiment is quartered at Meryton and then encamped at Brighton, and why officers like Wickham are circulating in a country neighbourhood at all.","Social context, property: land passed by primogeniture and could be entailed on male heirs, as Longbourn is on Mr Collins; a married woman's property passed to her husband, so a settlement, not affection, decided a wife's security.","Social context, work: no respectable profession was open to a gentleman's daughter, so marriage was the only provision available; a clergyman such as Mr Collins depended on a patron's gift of a living, which is why Lady Catherine's favour rules his conduct.","Social context, money: Austen names sums deliberately — Darcy's ten thousand a year, Bingley's four or five thousand, Mr Bennet's two thousand, the five thousand pounds settled by marriage articles on Mrs Bennet and the children, the daughters' shares theirs only after both parents are dead, Georgiana's thirty thousand, the three thousand Wickham took in place of the living.","Social context, rank and trade: the landed gentry sat above families whose fortunes came from trade, which is why Caroline Bingley is a snob about the Gardiners of Gracechurch Street even though her own family's money was made in business.","Social context, reputation: a young woman's conduct was the whole family's asset, so Lydia's elopement threatens the marriage prospects of all five sisters until the marriage is bought.","Literary context: the novel of manners and the comedy of courtship, written against the sentimental and Gothic fashions of the 1790s; Austen's free indirect style is her major technical contribution to the English novel.","Reception and different audiences: contemporaries read a witty courtship comedy; later readers have read the same book through feminist and economic criticism, so Charlotte's bargain and Lydia's disgrace now look less like moral lessons than like exposures of a system — the fifth category of context, and the one examiners most often find missing.","Using context well: the specification wants contextual factors to influence the interpretation, so the mark is earned by a sentence such as \"because Longbourn is entailed, Charlotte's plainness reads as clear sight\", not by a detached sentence of history at the end of a paragraph."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — \"Jane Austen allows the reader to see many different forms of love in Pride and Prejudice. To what extent do you agree?\" (30 marks)","items":["Decoded question: the proposition is easy to agree with, so the marks are not in agreeing. \"To what extent\" asks you to weigh it, and \"forms of love\" asks you to define your terms — decide early whether calculation, appetite and habit count as forms of love or as its absence, because that decision is your argument.","Timing and shape: about 45 minutes; roughly 5 to plan, 35 to write, 5 to check. Four developed paragraphs and a conclusion, each paragraph earning AO1, AO2 and AO4 together.","Thesis to open with: Austen does present a wide range of attachments, but she arranges them as a scale, and the weaker forms exist so that the reader can measure the one attachment she is prepared to endorse.","Paragraph 1 — love as habit and its cost: the Bennets' marriage; Mr Bennet's \"quick parts, sarcastic humour, reserve, and caprice\" and his retreat into laughter; structurally, his neglect is what makes Lydia's elopement possible. Context: a father's authority was a family's only protection of its daughters' reputation.","Paragraph 2 — love as calculation: Charlotte Lucas, \"I am not romantic, you know\", asking \"only a comfortable home\"; the placing of her acceptance a few chapters after Elizabeth's refusal of the same man. Context: the entail on Longbourn and the absence of any profession for a gentleman's daughter make the bargain respectable.","Paragraph 3 — love as appetite: Lydia and Wickham; the elopement handled off-stage, the marriage bought with Darcy's money, and the union dismissed in a clause about affection sinking into indifference. Context: reputation as a family asset, and the militia at Brighton as the opportunity.","Paragraph 4 — love as revised judgement: Elizabeth and Darcy; the first proposal refused with \"the last man in the world whom I could ever be prevailed on to marry\", the letter, \"Till this moment, I never knew myself\", and Darcy's \"I have been a selfish being all my life, in practice, though not in principle\". Context: her refusal of ten thousand a year is socially reckless, which is what makes the eventual match a choice rather than a transaction.","The evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: each form of attachment is being measured against the question of whether judgement has been exercised — keep returning to that test rather than describing couples one after another.","What the conclusion must say: agree that the reader is shown many forms of love, but qualify — several of them are love's substitutes, and Austen includes them precisely so that the union founded on revised judgement can be shown to be earned. Reach that verdict in your own voice; do not restate the question.","Two ways this essay fails: describing the couples in turn with no thread, and adding context in separate sentences. Fix both by making every paragraph end on a comparative judgement and by putting the historical fact inside the clause that explains the character's choice."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:03:12Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Every fix from the last pass has landed and holds up — Charlotte's wedding is now correctly in Volume 2 Chapter 3 (Chapter 26 of 61) with Volume 1 closing on her acceptance, the snub is repunctuated to the prescribed editions' \"She is tolerable; but not handsome enough to tempt me\" in all four places, \"Be not alarmed, Madam\" is capitalised in all four, the four prescribed editions and ISBNs are named in extras[3], examples[2] identifies Collins as the entailed heir, questions[0].hints[2] no longer supplies the thirty-mark total and cards[12] now reads \"three numbered reasons for marrying, from which affection is missing\" — and on a fresh check all 33 distinct quotations from the novel (91 instances) and all 22 quotations from the specification are verbatim, the fourteen answer keys are sound, the tiers are honest and the two model paragraphs are correctly pitched at Levels 5 and 3; what remains is one imprecision about who the five thousand pounds was settled on, a two-item slip in the Volume 1 chronology and two worked-example setups that withhold a fact their own steps use."}}},"heritage-great-expectations":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-great-expectations","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Great Expectations is answered in Component 2, Section B — Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay chosen from two, in a 90-minute paper worth 60 raw marks and 40% of the International GCSE. The section is the only place in Component 2 where AO4 is examined, and the three objectives weigh exactly the same: 10 marks for close knowledge and personal engagement (AO1), 10 for analysis of language, form and structure (AO2) and 10 for the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written (AO4). This room exists because the commonest way to lose marks here is not ignorance of the novel but an answer that does only one of those three jobs — a retold plot, a hunt for devices, or a block of Victorian history bolted on at the end. The section is open book, so a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in; that raises, not lowers, what an examiner expects of your quotations.","idea":[{"h":"Where Great Expectations sits in the course","t":"For Component 2 you study two set texts: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. Great Expectations is your heritage choice, and the content overview says it is studied whole — you must develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. Studying it whole matters because the examiner can ask about any thread of the novel: a place (the marshes, London), a theme (guilt, ambition, loyalty), a character, or the shape of Pip’s three stages. A top-level answer treats the whole novel as available evidence, reaching for the moment that best proves the point rather than the moment that comes first in the book.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge, critical style, informed personal engagement","t":"Ten of the thirty marks go to AO1: demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of the text, maintain a critical style and present an informed personal engagement, identifying and using relevant examples. Close knowledge means you can say what happens, to whom and in what order — that Pip is frightened into stealing a file and food for the convict on the marshes, that he is taken to Satis House and taught to despise his own hands, that he believes Miss Havisham is his benefactor, and that Magwitch, not Miss Havisham, has paid for him. Critical style means writing as a reader with a case, not a narrator retelling. A top-level answer states a position on the question in its first sentence and keeps arguing it, using short, chosen references rather than long retelling.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: how Dickens makes the meaning","t":"Ten marks go to analysis of the language, form and structure Dickens uses to create meanings and effects, using appropriate literary terminology. Language is the word Dickens chose: Estella’s jibe at Pip’s coarse hands and thick boots, Wemmick’s portable property, Magwitch’s claim that he has made a gentleman on Pip. Form is the first-person retrospective narration of a bildungsroman, in which the older Pip judges the younger one, and the weekly serial instalment that must end on a hook. Structure is the novel’s three stages of Pip’s expectations, and the pattern by which every rise is undercut by a return of the marshes. The grid rises from identifying a device to sustained analysis to cohesive evaluation, so a top-level answer never stops at naming a technique: it shows what the choice does to the reader and why Dickens wanted that.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"},{"h":"AO4: the novel and the world it was written in","t":"Ten marks go to showing understanding of the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written, and of the influence of contextual factors on interpretation. This is the only section of Component 2 that carries AO4, and the instruction that earns it is printed in the question: refer to the context of the novel. Useful context for Great Expectations includes weekly serial publication in Dickens’s own magazine All the Year Round from December 1860 to August 1861 and volume publication in 1861; transportation of convicts and the prison hulks; Newgate and public execution; the Victorian argument about whether a gentleman is born or made; and Dickens’s own childhood work in a blacking warehouse while his father was imprisoned for debt. A top-level answer never parks these as a paragraph of history: it uses one of them to change how a specific moment reads.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","t":"The specification names five categories of context that affect a writer’s work and a reader’s response: the writer’s own life and situation, including the place and time of writing, where it relates to the text; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes in society and the expectations of different groups; the literary context, such as movements or genres; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times, including how a text may be read differently in the twenty-first century from when it was written. Each has a Great Expectations answer: the blacking warehouse; a novel written in 1860–61 but set a generation earlier, when convicts were still transported; the Victorian gentleman; the bildungsroman and the serial; and a modern reader’s harder view of Estella’s upbringing or of Magwitch’s sentence. A top-level answer chooses the category that the question actually needs.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"What the question looks like and what you may take in","t":"Section B is one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, assessing AO1, AO2 and AO4 — Questions 11 to 22 on the paper, 10 marks for each objective. Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 raw marks and 40% of the qualification, so roughly 45 minutes belongs to this essay. It is an open book examination: you may take in a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the text, completely unmarked. Open book is not a licence to copy — long transcription wastes minutes and earns nothing — but it does mean an examiner expects exact, short, well-chosen quotation. A top-level answer spends its first five minutes choosing between the two questions and planning, then uses the book only to check the wording of evidence it has already decided to use.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same literary heritage list. Assignment B is one 30-mark essay on a teacher-devised task on the studied text, marked out of 30 with 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, and it must allow learners to access all three. The specification prints its own model wording for a Great Expectations task: How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words, with no penalty for exceeding it, and marking uses the same five-level grid on a best fit basis, internally assessed and externally moderated. A top-level assignment reads exactly like a top-level examination essay, only better proofread: same three strands, same judgement.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning the stem into a line of argument","setup":"The question on the paper is: How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. It is worth thirty marks, split evenly between AO1, AO2 and AO4, and you have about forty-five minutes. Write the opening sentence of the essay — the position the whole answer will argue.","steps":["Split the stem: the subject is city life, the instruction how does Dickens portray makes it a question about method, and the two printed instructions name the AO2 and AO4 strands.","Turn the subject into a claim that can be disagreed with: London is not scenery in this novel but the place where Pip gets what he wanted and sees what it cost.","Check the claim can hold three kinds of evidence: Smithfield and Newgate for language, Jaggers’s office in Little Britain for structure, transportation and public hanging for context.","Write the sentence so the claim and the method are both in it: Dickens portrays the capital as a place whose wealth and whose violence share an address, so that Pip’s rise is never allowed to look clean."],"cues":["Which two printed instructions tell you which objectives the question carries?","Why would the sentence London is dirty and crowded be unusable as a line of argument?","Which part of the novel would you reach for to prove a claim about the law and the city?","What must the opening sentence contain besides the claim itself?"]},{"title":"Making one quotation earn AO2","setup":"In the card game at Satis House, Estella says of Pip, He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy!, and then, And what coarse hands he has! And what thick boots! Pip is narrating the scene years later, and tells us that he afterwards determined to ask Joe why he had ever taught him to call those picture-cards Jacks, which ought to be called knaves. A paragraph in your essay argues that Pip is taught to despise himself. Show how to analyse this evidence for AO2 rather than simply quoting it.","steps":["Read the construction before naming any device: the verb calls takes an object, the knaves, and a complement, Jacks, so Estella’s sentence holds the right word and Pip’s word together in one breath and marks his as the mistake.","Notice who is not being addressed: the tag this boy puts Pip in the third person while he is standing there, so he is exhibited to Miss Havisham rather than spoken to.","Move to the second exclamation: having corrected his speech she inspects his body, so class is made audible and then visible, and in neither case is it something Pip chose.","Read the form: the narrator is the older Pip, and what he remembers is resolving to blame Joe for the word, so the reader watches the shame being taken in and carried back to the forge.","Close by linking the effect to the argument: Pip is taught to hear his own speech and see his own hands as faults, which is why he later wants to be a gentleman before he can say what one is."],"cues":["Which two words does the verb calls hold together, and which of them does Estella count as wrong?","Why does the tag this boy matter as much as the correction itself?","What does the second exclamation add that the first could not?","Who is telling us this, and what does he decide to do about the word Jacks afterwards?","How does this analysis connect back to the paragraph’s claim?"]},{"title":"Weaving context in so it counts","setup":"Your paragraph claims that Magwitch’s return destroys Pip’s idea of himself. Your evidence is Magwitch’s line, Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you! You have two context facts available: returning from transportation before the sentence had expired was a capital offence, and Dickens as a boy worked in a blacking warehouse while his father was imprisoned for debt. Add context to the paragraph so that it earns AO4.","steps":["Ask which fact changes the reading of this evidence rather than which fact is more interesting.","Test the second fact: Dickens’s childhood explains his sympathy for Pip’s shame, but it does not alter what Magwitch’s sentence means, so it is background here.","Test the first fact: because the returned convict is under sentence of death, the money that made Pip a gentleman is spoken by a man risking hanging to say it.","Place the fact inside the analysis, not after it: the possessive claim on you lands on a reader who knows the speaker can be executed for standing in the room, so gentility and the gallows are made one transaction."],"cues":["What is the test for choosing between two true context facts?","Does the blacking warehouse change what this line means, or only why Dickens might have written it?","What does the law add to the danger of this scene?","Where in the paragraph should the fact appear if it is to be integrated rather than bolted on?"]},{"title":"Lifting a paragraph from Level 3 to Level 5","setup":"Here is a paragraph on the marshes: Dickens describes the marshes as a dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard. This creates a scary atmosphere and shows the reader that Pip is alone and frightened. Dickens uses adjectives to describe the setting. In context, the marshes were near the hulks where convicts were kept. The question is: In what ways does Dickens present guilt in Great Expectations? Rewrite the paragraph’s moves so that it would sit at the top level.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: every sentence is a separate observation, and none of them answers the question, which is about guilt rather than atmosphere.","Give the paragraph one claim to prove: the marsh landscape is the form Pip’s guilt takes, so the setting accuses him before any person does.","Make the evidence work: the flatness leaves nowhere to hide and the churchyard puts the dead at his back, so a boy who has stolen a file and a pie is watched by a place that offers no cover.","Add the structural point and the judgement: Dickens returns Pip to these marshes at every turn of his fortunes, so the novel’s shape argues that guilt is not left behind by rising, and that is the reader’s strongest reason to distrust Pip’s expectations."],"cues":["What is this paragraph failing to do that the question asks for?","What single sentence could hold the whole paragraph together?","How do the details of the landscape connect to the boy’s theft?","What is missing from the paragraph once the analysis is done?"]},{"title":"Planning the whole answer in five minutes","setup":"The question is: Dickens allows the reader to see many different kinds of loyalty in Great Expectations. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. The section is open book and the essay is worth thirty marks. Produce the plan you would write in the margin before starting.","steps":["Decide the position first, because the stem asks for agreement: agree, but argue that the novel ranks loyalties rather than merely displaying them.","Choose three loyalties that differ in kind, not in degree: Joe’s constancy, Magwitch’s bought devotion, and Herbert’s friendship, with Pip’s failed loyalty as the thread that measures them.","Give each paragraph its evidence and its objective: Joe and the phrase ever the best of friends for AO1 and AO2, Magwitch’s gentleman on you plus transportation for AO4, Herbert and Pip’s secret purchase of his partnership for the turn in Pip’s character.","Fix the conclusion before writing: the novel keeps its highest value for loyalty that expects nothing back, which is why the forge and not Satis House is the moral centre."],"cues":["What does the phrase to what extent do you agree require you to settle before planning?","Why choose loyalties that differ in kind rather than three examples of the same thing?","Which paragraph is the natural home for the context marks here?","Why decide the conclusion before writing rather than after?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does each assessment objective carry in Component 2, Section B?","a":"Ten each — 10 AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4, out of 30."},{"q":"Which printed instruction in the question carries the AO4 marks?","a":"And refer to the context of the novel."},{"q":"What does open book allow you to take into the Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text."},{"q":"Name the three stages of Pip’s expectations by what happens in each, and say how the novel marks the divisions between them.","a":"Stage one: childhood at the forge, the convict on the marshes and Satis House. Stage two: London, gentility and the belief that Miss Havisham is the benefactor, closing with Magwitch on the stairs. Stage three: the consequences of that return — the attempted escape down the river, Magwitch’s death, Pip’s ruin and recovery. Dickens prints a line at the end of chapter 19 announcing the end of the first stage of Pip’s expectations, and another at the end of chapter 39 for the second; the third needs no marker because the novel ends."},{"q":"Why is a paragraph of Victorian history at the end of the essay a weak way to earn AO4?","a":"The top level requires understanding of text and context to be integrated convincingly into the response, so context must change how a specific moment reads."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section B — Literary Heritage Texts: one 30-mark essay from a choice of two, assessing AO1 (10), AO2 (10) and AO4 (10); the paper is 1 hour 30 minutes, 60 raw marks and 40% of the International GCSE.","Component 2 is open book: learners may take in a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition.","The five categories of context: the writer’s own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and the way texts are received by different audiences at different times.","Great Expectations was published in weekly instalments in Dickens’s magazine All the Year Round from December 1860 to August 1861, and in three volumes in 1861.","The novel is a first-person retrospective bildungsroman told by the older Pip, built in three stages of Pip’s expectations across fifty-nine chapters.","The specification’s own model wording for a Great Expectations task is: How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.","Marking is on a five-level grid, best fit: Level 3 is sound knowledge and understanding with clearly relevant examples; Level 4 is thorough and sustained; Level 5 is assured, perceptive and cohesive, with context integrated convincingly."],"questions":[{"q":"State the component and section in which Great Expectations is assessed, and name the three assessment objectives that section carries.","a":"Component 2, Section B — Literary Heritage Texts; AO1, AO2 and AO4.","sol":"Great Expectations is assessed in Component 2, Section B — Literary Heritage Texts, which is one 30-mark essay question chosen from two on each set text. The section assesses AO1 (close knowledge and understanding of the text, a critical style and informed personal engagement), AO2 (analysis of the language, form and structure used to create meanings and effects) and AO4 (understanding of the relationship between the text and the context in which it was written). These are Questions 11 to 22 on the paper and carry 10 raw marks each. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes, is worth 60 raw marks and is 40% of the International GCSE.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which of the two Component 2 sections holds the heritage texts rather than the modern drama.","Work from the section heading to the objectives the specification lists under it.","Give the component, then the section name, then the objectives in order."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"A Section B question on Great Expectations is worth 30 marks. Give the mark allocation across the assessment objectives and say what each objective asks for.","a":"10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4 — close knowledge and personal engagement; analysis of language, form and structure; text and context.","sol":"The 30 marks divide equally: 10 for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4. AO1 asks the candidate to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of the text, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, and to identify and use relevant examples. AO2 asks for analysis of the language, form and structure used by the writer to create meanings and effects, using appropriate literary terminology. AO4 asks the candidate to show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written. Because the weighting is equal, a developed paragraph should carry all three strands rather than dealing with them in separate blocks.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The three objectives in this section are deliberately balanced against each other.","Take each objective in turn and give the specification’s own description of it.","Set out the split first, then one sentence on what each objective demands."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Identify two of the five categories of context named in the specification and, for each, give one fact about Great Expectations that fits it.","a":"Any two categories, each with a true matching fact — for example the writer’s life (the blacking warehouse) and the historical setting (transportation and the hulks).","sol":"The writer’s own life and individual situation, including the place and time of writing: Dickens worked as a boy in a blacking warehouse while his father was imprisoned for debt, and he wrote and edited the weekly magazine All the Year Round in which the novel first appeared between December 1860 and August 1861. The historical setting, time and location of the text: the novel is set a generation before it was written, when convicts were still transported to Australia and held in prison hulks, and when returning from transportation before the sentence had expired was a capital offence — which is why Magwitch’s reappearance in London puts his life at risk. Other acceptable pairings are social and cultural contexts (the Victorian argument about whether a gentleman is born or made), the literary context (the bildungsroman, and the weekly serial with its instalment hooks) and reception (a twenty-first century reader’s harder view of Estella’s upbringing).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The specification lists these categories as a set; recall the set before choosing.","Pick categories where you can already name a hard fact about this novel, not just an impression.","Give the category in the specification’s words, then the fact that belongs to it."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Describe the moment at which Pip learns the true source of his expectations, and explain how the novel marks that point structurally.","a":"Magwitch returns to Pip’s rooms in London and reveals he is the benefactor; the novel closes the second stage of Pip’s expectations there.","sol":"A rough, weathered man climbs the stairs to Pip’s chambers in the Temple on a wild, wet night, and Pip slowly recognises the convict he fed on the marshes as a child. Magwitch reveals that he, not Miss Havisham, has paid for everything: Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you! Structurally, Dickens closes the second stage of Pip’s expectations at this point, so the reversal is not merely an event but a hinge in the novel’s architecture. The effect is retrospective as well as immediate: every earlier assumption Pip made about Satis House, Estella and his own deserving is exposed as invention, and the reader is invited to recognise that the novel has been quietly supplying the evidence all along.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which visitor arrives in London and undoes everything Pip has assumed.","Consider what the novel does at the end of that section, not only what happens in it.","Describe the scene first, then name the structural division it completes."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"]},{"q":"Explain what open book means for Component 2 and how it should change the way a candidate uses quotation.","a":"A clean, completely unmarked prescribed edition may be taken in; quotation should be short, exact and chosen, never copied at length.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of each selected text into the examination room; the copy must be completely unmarked, with no notes, highlighting or annotation. Because the text is available, an examiner expects quotation to be accurate and precise, and gives no credit for length. The sensible use of the book is to check the exact wording of evidence already chosen in planning, not to browse for material during writing. Since roughly forty-five minutes belongs to this essay, long transcription costs analysis time, and the top level rewards discriminating use of relevant examples in support — that is, short quotations chosen because they prove the point being argued.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The permission is narrower than students usually assume — think about the state the copy must be in.","Ask what changes for the examiner once the text is in the room with the candidate.","State the rule, then say what the candidate should do differently because of it."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"London as a place where wealth and violence share an address: Smithfield and Newgate, Jaggers’s office, Wemmick’s division of office and Castle; context of transportation, public execution and the Victorian gentleman.","sol":"Dickens portrays the capital as a place whose money and whose violence share an address, so Pip’s rise is never allowed to look clean. Pip’s first hours in London take him from the filth of Smithfield meat market to the walls of Newgate, and Dickens sets that squalor beside Jaggers’s office in Little Britain, where clients wait and casts of hanged men look down from a shelf: the law that makes Pip a gentleman is housed with the law that sentences Magwitch to death. Language reinforces the fusion — Wemmick’s portable property reduces gentility to goods carried off condemned men, and the reader hears in it the same transaction that funds Pip. Structurally, Dickens splits the city in Wemmick himself, whose maxim that the office is one thing, and private life is another lets a decent man survive it only by dividing himself; and he repeatedly returns Pip from London to the marshes, so the city can never quite close over the boy’s origins. In context, a novel serialised in All the Year Round in 1860–61 but set a generation earlier, when convicts were transported and Newgate hanged in public, let Victorian readers look at their own city at one remove. The portrayal is therefore an argument, not a backdrop: London gives Pip everything he asked for and, in doing so, shows him what it cost.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about how the city is made, not about what the city is like.","Look for places where Dickens puts wealth and crime into the same space or the same person.","Open with the claim about the city, then take methods in turn, and read the setting against the time of writing."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"In what ways does Dickens present guilt in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Guilt as inherited and environmental rather than earned: the marsh theft, the retrospective narrator’s self-accusation, the return of the convict world; context of criminal law, hulks and transportation.","sol":"Dickens presents guilt as something caught rather than committed: Pip is made to feel criminal long before he is ever guilty of anything, and the novel asks whether the society that produces that feeling is more culpable than the boy. The opening terrifies a child into theft, and the language of the marshes — a dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard — supplies a landscape that offers no cover and puts the dead at his back, so the setting accuses him before any person does. Form does the same work from the other direction: the retrospective first-person narrator judges his younger self continually, and the reader is never allowed the comfort of a narrator who thinks well of himself. Structurally, every rise in Pip’s fortunes is undercut by a return of the convict world, until the benefactor himself proves to be the man from the graveyard, so the novel’s shape argues that guilt is not escaped by rising. Contextually, Dickens writes in a period that still transported convicts and hanged them in public, and his own boyhood in a blacking warehouse while his father lay in a debtors’ prison gave him the conviction that shame is inflicted on children by circumstance. The presentation is ultimately redemptive rather than condemning: Pip’s guilt becomes useful only when it turns outward, into care for the dying Magwitch and the secret purchase of Herbert’s partnership.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Separate the guilt Pip feels from the wrong Pip actually does.","Track where guilt is carried by places and by the narrator’s voice, not only by events.","Take one paragraph each on language, on the narrating voice and on the novel’s shape, and close on what guilt finally produces."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Explore the ways in which Dickens presents Miss Havisham in Great Expectations. You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"A figure made of stopped time and staged revenge, both grotesque and pitiable: the stopped clocks and decayed bride, Estella as instrument, the later remorse; context of women’s dependence on marriage and of the Gothic.","sol":"Dickens presents Miss Havisham as a woman who has turned an injury into a performance, and then asks the reader to judge the performance and pity the injury at once. The language of her first appearance freezes time: her watch and the clock in the room have both stopped at twenty minutes to nine, and the bride within the bridal dress had withered like the dress, a construction that makes the woman and her costume a single decayed object. Form matters because we see her only through Pip, who at first reads her as a fairy-tale patron and much later as the source of his mistake, so the reader’s view of her is revised in step with his. Structurally she is the novel’s great false lead: the plot is built so that both Pip and the reader assume she is the benefactor, and the discovery that she is not turns her from an architect into a bystander who let a boy deceive himself. In context, a Victorian woman of property whose marriage collapses on the wedding morning has lost the one career her class allowed her; Dickens draws on Gothic convention for the ruined house and arrested clocks, but grounds the horror in a social fact. The presentation is finally moral rather than merely grotesque: her plea for forgiveness and her burning make revenge look like something that consumes its owner first.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Notice what the first description does to time before you comment on what she says.","Consider how much of her the reader is allowed to see, and who is doing the seeing.","Build from the imagery to her function in the plot, then judge whether the novel condemns her."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Dickens allows the reader to see many different kinds of loyalty in Great Expectations. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Agree, but argue the novel ranks loyalties: Joe’s constancy, Magwitch’s bought devotion, Herbert’s friendship, Biddy’s steadiness, against Pip’s failures; context of class and obligation.","sol":"The statement is true but incomplete: Dickens does display many kinds of loyalty, yet the novel is not a display case — it ranks them, and the ranking is its moral argument. Joe’s loyalty is unconditional and inarticulate; his phrase ever the best of friends survives every humiliation Pip inflicts, and Dickens gives him a plain, halting idiom precisely so that goodness is not confused with polish. Magwitch’s loyalty is fierce and purchased: I’ve made a gentleman on you! claims ownership as well as love, and in a world where returning from transportation carried a death sentence, his devotion is measured in the risk he runs to express it. Herbert’s is companionable and equal, which is why Pip’s secret purchase of his partnership — the one generous act Pip performs while still believing himself rich — is the turn on which his character changes. Structurally, these loyalties are arranged against Pip’s own failures, so the reader keeps a running account of what he owes; the first-person retrospect means the account is kept by the debtor himself. In context, Victorian fiction often rewarded loyalty that respected rank, and Dickens pointedly does not: the forge, not Satis House, is the moral centre. So: agree that the kinds are many, but argue that the novel reserves its highest value for loyalty that expects nothing back.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["A proposition has been offered, so decide how far you accept it before you choose examples.","Choose loyalties that differ in kind rather than three versions of the same thing.","Argue the ranking throughout and let the conclusion name the loyalty the novel values most."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Dickens present the marshes and their importance in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"The marshes as origin, conscience and return point: the opening graveyard, the convict, the mists at departure and at the close; context of the hulks and of Dickens’s Kent.","sol":"Dickens presents the marshes as the novel’s conscience: a landscape that holds Pip’s origins, his crime and his judgement, and to which the narrative returns whenever he tries to leave himself behind. The opening supplies the terms — a dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard, where a boy learns his own name from gravestones and is seized by a fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg — so that identity, death and criminality are introduced in a single paragraph. The language is deliberately bare: flatness gives no cover, mist gives no distance, and the iron on the leg imports the prison hulks moored offshore into a child’s morning walk. Structurally the marshes function as a refrain; Pip leaves them under rising mists for London and the novel returns him to them at every crisis, so the reader measures each supposed advance against a place that has not changed. Contextually the hulks and transportation were real machinery of the criminal law in the period the novel depicts, and Dickens knew the Kent marsh country in which he set them. The final mists at the ruined Satis House echo the mists of that first departure, and the closing sight of no shadow of another parting from her earns its quietness only because the reader has been taught to distrust what the marsh light hides.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the reader learns in the opening paragraphs beyond where the story is set.","Follow the places the novel keeps coming back to, and ask what the return is for.","Take the opening, the pattern of returns and the close in order, tying each to the law and landscape of the period."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Great Expectations only attacks the idea of the gentleman in order to reward a better kind of gentleman. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Largely agree: the novel destroys gentility as appearance and money but rehabilitates it as conduct — Joe and Herbert as the standard, Drummle as its opposite; context of birth versus making.","sol":"Largely agree, but with a qualification that matters: the novel dismantles the gentleman as a social category and then rebuilds the word as a moral one, which is a sharper attack than simple rejection. Pip’s ambition is stated in the flattest possible terms — I want to be a gentleman — before he can say what one is, and Dickens exposes the emptiness of the goal by making the money come from a transported convict, so the class Pip is bought into is funded by the class it punishes. Bentley Drummle supplies the counter-proof: born to the position Pip covets, he is brutal to Estella, which shows the category guaranteeing nothing. Against both, Joe stands as a labourer whose conduct never requires correction, and Herbert as a gentleman by birth whose kindness is untroubled by Pip’s origins. Structurally the three stages of Pip’s expectations trace the demolition: what is acquired in the second stage is stripped in the third, and Pip’s worth is finally measured by unpaid acts — nursing Magwitch, buying Herbert’s partnership in secret. In context, the mid-Victorian argument over whether a gentleman was born or made was live and unsettled, and Dickens intervenes on the side of making, while denying that money can do the making. The novel therefore rewards not a better class but a better conduct, and it is careful never to give that conduct a title.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Weigh the proposition rather than accepting or rejecting it outright.","Look for characters who hold the title without the conduct, and the conduct without the title.","Argue the qualification from the start and let the conclusion state exactly what the novel rewards."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"In what ways do the form and structure of Great Expectations shape the reader’s response to Pip? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Retrospective first-person narration, the bildungsroman, weekly serial instalments and the three stages: judgement built into the telling; context of All the Year Round and the revised ending.","sol":"The form is the argument: Dickens makes the reader inhabit Pip’s mistakes and judge them at the same time, and every structural choice serves that double position. The retrospective first person means two Pips are always present — the boy who acts and the man who tells — so snobbery is narrated by the person most ashamed of it, and the reader is spared the need to condemn because the condemning is already in the voice. As a bildungsroman the novel promises growth, and then withholds the conventional reward: the fortune is confiscated, the marriage does not happen on the terms Pip wanted, and what he gains is self-knowledge and work. Weekly serialisation in All the Year Round from December 1860 shaped the chapters into short units that must end on a hook, which is why revelations land with such force — most of all Magwitch’s, at the end of the second stage of Pip’s expectations. The three-stage division lets Dickens make reversal structural rather than incidental. Even the ending is a formal question: Dickens replaced his first, bleaker close, at Bulwer-Lytton’s urging, with the meeting at the ruined house that ends I saw no shadow of another parting from her — a closing litotes that affirms a future only by denying a parting, so the one negative does the work of a promise Dickens never quite makes and the reader is left holding the judgement the form has trained them to make.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Ask who is speaking and how much later, before considering what is said.","Consider what the conditions of first publication did to the size and shape of the chapters.","Move from the narrating voice to the genre to the divisions of the book, and end on the ending."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"In what ways does Dickens present change in Pip in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Change presented as slow, costly and unannounced: snobbery learned at Satis House, shame at Joe’s London visit, the turn at Magwitch’s bedside and the secret purchase of Herbert’s partnership; context of the born-or-made argument and of the ending Dickens replaced.","sol":"Pip does change, but Dickens makes the change unannounced and expensive, so that the reader has to detect it rather than be told it. The boy’s ambition is stated in the flattest terms available — I want to be a gentleman — and what follows is not growth but acquisition: after Satis House he is ashamed of the forge, and when Joe comes to London and takes to calling him sir, the scene is arranged so that Joe’s tact exposes Pip rather than Joe. Form carries the judgement, because the retrospective first person means the person condemning the snob is the snob himself, and the reader is therefore never asked to supply a verdict the narrator has not already passed. Structurally the three stages of Pip’s expectations make change a matter of loss: what the second stage buys, the third strips away, and Pip’s worth becomes visible only where no reward is expected — in his nursing of the dying Magwitch, and in the secret purchase of Herbert’s partnership, the one wholly generous act of his prosperity. The language of the older narrator measures the distance travelled: Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears is said by a man who once was. Context sharpens the judgement in two ways: the mid-Victorian dispute over whether a gentleman was born or made lets Dickens show a change of class that is not a change of character, and the fact that he replaced his first, bleaker close, at Bulwer-Lytton’s urging, with the meeting at the ruined Satis House means different audiences have been offered different rewards for the same change. That is the discriminating point: the change is complete before either ending begins, which is why neither ending can create it and only one of them appears to.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The stem asks for ways, so the essay is about how the change is made visible, not about whether Pip ends up nicer.","Separate what Pip acquires from what he becomes, and watch the acts he performs when he expects nothing back.","Take the snobbery, the turn and the aftermath in order, and let the novel’s two closes supply the context strand."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"AO4 carries 10 of the 30 marks in Component 2, Section B, and must be integrated. Using one moment from Great Expectations, explain how a top-level use of context differs from a Level 1 or Level 2 use. (10 marks — the AO4 allocation for the section)","a":"Level 1–2 states a historical fact alongside the text; Level 5 uses the fact to change what a specific moment means, so text and context cannot be separated.","sol":"The assessment grid moves from little comment on the relationship between text and context to understanding that is integrated convincingly into the response, so the difference is not how much history a candidate knows but what the history is made to do. Take Magwitch’s return and his claim, I’ve made a gentleman on you! A Level 1 or 2 answer writes a separate sentence — in Victorian times criminals were sent to Australia — which is true, adds nothing to the reading, and could be deleted without loss. A top-level answer makes the fact load-bearing: because returning from transportation before the sentence expired was a capital offence, the man asserting ownership of Pip’s gentility is risking execution to do it, so the possessive claim carries the weight of a life staked on it, and Pip’s recoil becomes not snobbery alone but a refusal of a debt he cannot repay. The test is simple: if the context sentence can be removed and the analysis still stands, the context has not been integrated.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Look at what the grid says separates the lowest band from the highest on context.","Choose a moment whose meaning actually depends on a fact about the period.","Show the weak version and the strong version of the same fact, then state the test that distinguishes them."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many raw marks does AO4 carry in Component 2, Section B?","a":"10."},{"q":"What may a learner take into the Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text."},{"q":"In which magazine, and in which years, did Great Expectations first appear?","a":"All the Year Round, in weekly instalments from December 1860 to August 1861."},{"q":"Who turns out to be Pip’s benefactor?","a":"The convict Magwitch, who uses the name Provis on his return."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Pip (Philip Pirrip)","back":"Narrator and protagonist; an orphan raised by his sister at the forge, given expectations he believes come from Miss Havisham. The novel is his own retrospective account of his rise, snobbery and recovery.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Abel Magwitch (Provis)","back":"The convict Pip feeds on the marshes; transported to Australia, he secretly funds Pip’s gentility and returns illegally to see him, saying: I’ve made a gentleman on you! He is sentenced to death and dies in prison.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Miss Havisham","back":"Jilted on her wedding morning; she lives at Satis House with her watch and the room’s clock stopped at twenty minutes to nine. She rears Estella to break men’s hearts, later repents, and dies after her dress catches fire.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Estella","back":"Raised by Miss Havisham as an instrument of revenge; she mocks Pip’s coarse hands and thick boots, marries Bentley Drummle, and is in fact the daughter of Magwitch and Molly.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Joe Gargery","back":"The blacksmith married to Pip’s sister; illiterate, gentle and constant, he calls Pip ever the best of friends and nurses him after his collapse. The novel’s moral standard against which gentility is measured.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Mr Jaggers","back":"The London criminal lawyer who brings Pip news of his great expectations and administers the money. His office in Little Britain holds casts of hanged clients, joining Pip’s fortune to the criminal courts.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Wemmick’s two selves","back":"Jaggers’s clerk collects portable property at work but keeps a small castle and his Aged Parent at Walworth, holding that the office is one thing, and private life is another — Dickens’s image of surviving the city by division.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Satis House","back":"Miss Havisham’s decaying mansion; the name means enough. Its stopped time, faded bridal dress and ruined garden make it the Gothic centre of the novel and the place where Pip learns to despise his own hands.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Retrospective first-person narration","back":"The older Pip tells his own story, so every action is reported by the man ashamed of it. The reader gets the boy’s experience and the man’s judgement at once, which is where much of the novel’s irony lives.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The three stages of Pip’s expectations","back":"Dickens divides the novel into three stages, printing a line at the end of chapters 19 and 39 to announce the end of the first and of the second. Reversal is built into the architecture, not just the plot.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"Bildungsroman","back":"A novel of formation, tracing a protagonist from childhood to maturity. Dickens uses the genre and then withholds its usual reward: Pip gains self-knowledge and work rather than fortune and easy marriage.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"All the Year Round","back":"Dickens’s own weekly magazine, in which Great Expectations was serialised from December 1860 to August 1861 before three-volume publication in 1861. Weekly parts explain the short chapters and the instalment hooks.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Transportation and the hulks","back":"Convicts were held in prison ships off the marshes and transported to Australia; returning before the sentence expired was a capital offence. This is why Magwitch’s reappearance in London risks his life.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Dickens’s blacking warehouse","back":"As a boy Dickens worked pasting labels in a blacking warehouse while his father was imprisoned for debt — the source of his lifelong interest in childhood shame, class and the fear of being found out.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a context category the specification names that a Great Expectations answer can use","back":"The writer’s own life and situation — the blacking warehouse and All the Year Round; the historical setting — transportation, the hulks and Newgate; the literary context — the bildungsroman and weekly serial form; reception — how a modern reader judges Estella’s upbringing differently.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question: How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.","Dickens portrays the capital as a place that sells the one thing it cannot supply, so that Pip’s arrival in London is written as a disappointment before it is written as a triumph. The lodging that is to house a young fellow of great expectations turns out to be Barnard’s Inn, a dingy huddle of rotting buildings that Pip had confidently imagined as a hotel; Dickens gives the reader the imagined version and the real one in the same breath, so the city enters the novel as the gap between what is promised and what is delivered. What the capital then supplies is a syllabus. Herbert coaches Pip in how to hold a fork and renames him Handel, and the nickname is the sharpest touch in the sequence, because it is kindly meant, is accepted without protest, and quietly retires the name the forge gave him: becoming a gentleman is shown to begin with the erasure of where a man came from. The structure presses the point by setting the acquisition of manners directly beside the accumulation of debt — the dining club evenings of the Finches of the Grove, and the solemn ritual in which Pip and Herbert sit down to look into their affairs, list what they owe and feel better for having listed it — all of it narrated by an older Pip whose accounting idiom is exact about the money and merciless about himself. The mockery has a context: the mid-Victorian argument over whether a gentleman was born or made was unsettled, and Dickens’s London answers it in the worst available way, by showing that a gentleman can simply be bought, on credit, with money Pip has not earned. The portrayal is therefore an indictment of an appetite as much as of a place: London gives Pip precisely the gentility he asked for, and Dickens makes sure the reader can see the price list.","Why this is Level 5: the paragraph takes one position and argues it from first sentence to last (AO1); it analyses a structural placing, a single word — the nickname Handel — and the narrator’s idiom, and weighs which is sharpest rather than listing devices (AO2); and the context is load-bearing, since removing the born-or-made sentence takes the ground from under the claim that the city sells gentility (AO4). The evidence is short and chosen, and the close is a judgement on the question rather than a summary of the novel."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question: How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.","Dickens portrays city life as dirty and threatening. When Pip first walks through London he passes Smithfield, which is smeared with filth and fat from the meat market, and then stands outside Newgate, where prisoners were hanged. The descriptive detail here shows the reader that the capital is not the refined place Pip imagined, and it makes his new position feel uncomfortable. Dickens also uses Jaggers’s office, which is near these places, and this is significant because it connects the law with the dirt of the city. In context, Dickens was writing about a London where crime and punishment were very public, which affects how we read Pip’s rise.","Why this is Level 3 and not higher: the knowledge is sound and the examples are clearly relevant, and there is an appropriate critical style, so it sits comfortably in the band. But the analysis stops at showing the reader — no single word or phrase is examined, no form or structural pattern is traced, and no alternative reading is weighed, so it is not sustained analysis, let alone cohesive evaluation. The context arrives in a final sentence that could be deleted without damaging the paragraph, which is precisely what integrated convincingly rules out.","What would lift it: replace shows the reader with a claim about what Dickens is arguing; quote one exact phrase such as portable property and analyse the tone of it; name the structural choice of placing the law beside the shambles; and move the context fact inside the analysis so that the criminal law changes what the office means."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Great Expectations — Charles Dickens","items":["Author and publication: Charles Dickens; serialised weekly in his own magazine All the Year Round from December 1860 to August 1861, then published in three volumes in 1861.","Form: a first-person retrospective bildungsroman narrated by the adult Pip, in fifty-nine chapters divided into three stages of Pip’s expectations.","Opening: Pip, an orphan, reads his family name from the gravestones in the marsh churchyard — the novel begins with him learning who he is from the dead.","The marshes: the setting is the marsh country by the river, twenty miles from the sea, with the prison hulks moored offshore.","Magwitch on the marshes: a fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg, seizes Pip with the cry Hold your noise! and threatens him: Keep still, you little devil, or I’ll cut your throat!","Pip’s theft: terrified, Pip steals a file and food from the forge for the convict; Magwitch is recaptured but takes the blame for the theft, sparing Pip.","Home: Pip is raised by his sister, Mrs Joe, who is said to have brought him up by hand, and by her husband Joe Gargery, the blacksmith, who is Pip’s friend and protector.","Satis House: Pip is summoned to play at Miss Havisham’s decaying mansion, where her watch and a clock have both stopped at twenty minutes to nine and the bride within the bridal dress had withered like the dress.","Estella: Miss Havisham’s ward humiliates Pip in the card game — He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy! And what coarse hands he has! And what thick boots! — and Pip’s snobbery begins there.","The ambition: Pip tells Biddy plainly, I want to be a gentleman, before he can say what a gentleman is.","The expectations: Mr Jaggers, a London criminal lawyer, brings Pip the news in chapter 18. Quoted exactly, Jaggers says that he will come into a handsome property and is to be brought up as a gentleman — in a word, as a young fellow of great expectations. Paraphrased, his conditions are that the benefactor must not be named and that Pip is always to bear the name of Pip.","London: Pip lodges at Barnard’s Inn with Herbert Pocket, who nicknames him Handel; Jaggers’s office is in Little Britain, near Smithfield and Newgate.","Wemmick: Jaggers’s clerk, who collects portable property at the office and keeps a miniature castle at Walworth, holding that the office is one thing, and private life is another.","The revelation: Magwitch returns illegally from transportation and tells Pip, Yes, Pip, dear boy, I’ve made a gentleman on you! — Miss Havisham was never the benefactor. This closes the second stage of Pip’s expectations.","Estella’s parentage: Estella is the daughter of Magwitch and of Molly, Jaggers’s housekeeper — the novel’s two social extremes turn out to be one family.","The escape: Pip and Herbert try to row Magwitch down the Thames; Compeyson, the man who jilted Miss Havisham and betrayed Magwitch, intervenes, Compeyson drowns and Magwitch is taken, sentenced to death, and dies in the prison infirmary before execution, his property forfeited to the Crown.","Miss Havisham’s end: she asks Pip’s forgiveness, then her dress catches fire; Pip saves her from the flames but she dies of her injuries.","Pip’s recovery: ruined and ill, Pip is nursed by Joe, who pays his debts; earlier, believing himself rich, Pip had secretly bought Herbert a partnership — the one wholly generous act of his prosperity.","Themes: ambition and social class; guilt and the criminal law; what makes a gentleman; parents and substitutes for them; revenge and forgiveness; loyalty that expects nothing back.","Endings: Dickens wrote a bleaker ending first and replaced it, at Bulwer-Lytton’s urging, with the meeting at the ruined Satis House that ends, I saw no shadow of another parting from her.","Key narrator’s judgement: Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears — the adult Pip’s commentary is where much of the novel’s moral weight sits.","Context to quote for AO4: transportation and the hulks; return from transportation as a capital offence; Newgate and public execution; the Victorian argument over whether a gentleman is born or made; Dickens’s boyhood in a blacking warehouse while his father was imprisoned for debt; weekly serialisation shaping short chapters that end on hooks.","Prescribed editions for the open book examination (Appendix 3): Penguin Classics, April 2012, ISBN 9780141198897; Penguin Books, January 2003, ISBN 9780141439563; Wordsworth Edition Classics, May 1992, ISBN 9781853260049."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — How does Dickens portray city life in Great Expectations? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","items":["Decoding the question: how does Dickens portray asks for method, not description, so every paragraph must be about a choice Dickens made. City life means London as Pip experiences it, not the marshes. You must consider language, form and structure carries the 10 AO2 marks; and refer to the context of the novel carries the 10 AO4 marks; close knowledge and a critical personal position carry the 10 AO1 marks. Time: about forty-five minutes, five of them on this plan.","Line of argument to state in sentence one: Dickens portrays the capital as a place whose money and whose violence share an address, so that Pip’s rise is never allowed to look clean.","Paragraph 1 — the city’s geography of guilt: Pip’s first walk from Smithfield to Newgate, and Jaggers’s office in Little Britain with the casts of hanged clients. Analyse the placing (structure) and the physical detail (language). Context: Newgate, public execution and the criminal courts that fund the plot.","Paragraph 2 — the city prices people: Wemmick’s portable property and the flat accounting tone in which he uses it. Analyse diction and register. Context: a commercial capital in which gentility could be bought, and the Victorian dispute over whether a gentleman is born or made.","Paragraph 3 — the city divides the self: Wemmick’s Walworth castle and his rule that the office is one thing, and private life is another; set beside Pip’s own division between his London debts and the forge. Analyse characterisation as structure — a man built as two halves.","Paragraph 4 — the city cannot cover the marshes: Dickens keeps returning Pip from London to the marsh country, and the benefactor arrives out of it. Analyse the structural refrain and its effect on the reader’s trust in Pip’s prospects.","Evaluation thread to run through every paragraph: is London corrupting Pip, or only revealing what he already wanted? Keep answering it — the city gives him what he asked for, which is why the indictment falls on the wish as much as on the place.","Conclusion must say: that Dickens’s London is an argument rather than a backdrop; that its portrayal works by fusing wealth with the criminal law rather than by describing squalor; and that a reader in 1861, looking at a city a generation back, was being shown their own. End on the judgement, not on a summary."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Exam technique for the 30-mark heritage essay","items":["Read both questions before choosing; choose the one for which you can already name three pieces of evidence, not the one with the more attractive subject.","Every developed paragraph should carry all three strands: a claim and close knowledge (AO1), analysis of a specific choice of language, form or structure (AO2), and a context fact that changes the reading (AO4).","Test for AO4 integration: if the context sentence can be deleted and the analysis still stands, it has not been integrated and will sit low in the grid.","Quote short and exactly. Open book means an examiner expects accuracy; it does not reward length, and transcription costs analysis time.","Avoid the device hunt. Naming a metaphor earns almost nothing; showing what the choice does to the reader, and why Dickens wanted that effect, is what moves an answer from Level 3 to Level 5.","Where the stem offers a proposition and asks to what extent do you agree, settle your position in the plan and argue it from the first sentence; a qualified judgement outscores agreement or narrative.","On the coursework route the same essay is Assignment B, marked out of 30 with 10 marks for each of AO1, AO2 and AO4, with an advisory word count of 650 to 800 words and no penalty for exceeding it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T17:51:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"I checked all 22 quotations word for word against the text of the novel and every one is exact — 'He calls the knaves Jacks, this boy!' and 'And what coarse hands he has! And what thick boots!' (ch. 8, the stray comma of the earlier draft is gone from both lesson.examples[1].setup and the fact file), 'picture-cards Jacks, which ought to be called knaves', 'Yes, Pip, dear boy, I've made a gentleman on you!', 'dark flat wilderness beyond the churchyard' (article aside), 'A fearful man, all in coarse grey, with a great iron on his leg', 'Hold your noise!', 'Keep still, you little devil, or I'll cut your throat!', 'brought up by hand', 'twenty minutes to nine' (watch and clock both), 'the bride within the bridal dress had withered like the dress', 'I want to be a gentleman' (to Biddy, ch. 17), 'he will come into a handsome property', 'be brought up as a gentleman,—in a word, as a young fellow of great expectations', 'portable property', 'the office is one thing, and private life is another', 'ever the best of friends', 'Handel' and the Harmonious Blacksmith, 'Heaven knows we need never be ashamed of our tears', 'I saw no shadow of another parting from her', 'let us look into our affairs' and Satis as 'enough'; the described moments check out too — 59 chapters, 'THIS IS THE END OF THE FIRST STAGE OF PIP'S EXPECTATIONS' after ch. 19 and the second after ch. 39, Barnard's Inn as the dingiest collection of shabby buildings Pip had taken for a hotel, the Finches of the Grove, the two casts on Jaggers's shelf, Compeyson drowned and Magwitch sentenced and dying in the infirmary with his property forfeited to the Crown, Estella the daughter of Magwitch and Molly, Joe's receipt for Pip's debt, Miss Havisham's 'I forgive her' and her death after the fire. I re-solved every question independently and found no wrong or ambiguous answer key; the worked examples all state their evidence up front, keep the outcome back and make one real move per step, and the cues are answerable without giving their own step away. Every fault the first examiner listed has been repaired — the knaves comma, the 'double negative' claim (now correctly named as litotes in questions[11].sol), the 'Estella's coarse hands' possessive in lesson.idea[2], the paraphrase-as-quotation in the fact file, the AO3 command word 'Compare' and the invented 6-mark tariffs, the over-promising check question on the three stages, and the Level 5 model paragraph that duplicated questions[5].sol — and what remains is presentational: publish it, and tidy the article in 'the dark flat wilderness' when convenient."}}},"heritage-scarlet-letter":{"id":"EDX-4ET1","topic":"heritage-scarlet-letter","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"The Scarlet Letter is your literary heritage set text for Component 2, Section B. One 30-mark essay, chosen from two, is all you get on it, and it is the only question in the whole 90-minute paper that carries AO4. The three objectives weigh exactly the same — 10 marks for close knowledge and a critical personal position, 10 for analysis of language, form and structure, and 10 for the relationship between the novel and the contexts in which it was written. That equal split is the whole strategy of the room: a paragraph that narrates the plot beautifully but names no method and reaches no context is throwing away two thirds of its marks. The examination is open book, so you may carry a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition in with you — which means the examiner expects exact, chosen quotation rather than roughly remembered gist, and gives no credit at all for copying out long passages.","idea":[{"h":"One whole heritage text, studied as a whole","t":"For Component 2 you study two set texts: one modern drama text and one literary heritage text. The Scarlet Letter is one of six heritage texts on the list, and you study the whole novel, not extracts — the Custom-House sketch and the twenty-four chapters that follow it, the last of which is the Conclusion. The content overview asks you to develop skills to analyse how language, form, structure and contextual factors can be used to create meaning and effect, and to maintain a critical style and an informed personal response. The word 'whole' matters in the examination, because the strongest answers range across the book: a point built from the prison door in the opening chapter and closed off at the tombstone motto in the Conclusion shows knowledge of the whole design in a way that three examples from the same chapter never can. A top-level answer therefore plans its evidence across the arc of the novel before it writes a word.","code":"Component 2: Content overview"},{"h":"AO1: close knowledge argued, not retold","t":"AO1 is worth 10 of the 30 marks and asks you to demonstrate a close knowledge and understanding of the text, maintain a critical style and present an informed personal engagement, identifying and using relevant examples. 'Critical style' is the trap. A candidate who knows that Hester is sentenced to the scaffold and to wear the letter for life, that Chillingworth arrives on the day of her shaming and conceals his identity, that Dimmesdale keeps his vigil at midnight and confesses after the Election Sermon, has knowledge; a candidate who argues that Hawthorne makes public punishment produce private corruption in everyone except the woman punished has knowledge in a critical style. The grid rises from 'largely narrative' at Level 2 to 'assured personal engagement and a perceptive critical style' at Level 5. A top-level answer turns every piece of knowledge into a step in an argument it stated in its first two sentences.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"},{"h":"AO2: the ways the writer creates the effect","t":"AO2 is the other 10 marks and asks you to analyse the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects, using appropriate literary terminology. In a novel, 'form and structure' are not decoration: they are the romance form Hawthorne names in his subtitle, the intrusive third-person narrator who comments on the action and often offers rival explanations for the same event, the framing sketch of the Custom-House, and above all the three scaffold scenes that hold the book open at its beginning, middle and end. Language is the sombre Puritan diction of 'sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats' set against the gold thread and wild rose. The grid moves from 'minimal identification' through 'sustained analysis' to 'cohesive evaluation', so naming a symbol earns almost nothing. A top-level answer names the method, quotes briefly, and then traces what the method does to the reader.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2"},{"h":"AO4: the novel and the contexts it was written in","t":"AO4 is the last 10 marks, and Section B is the only place in Component 2 that carries it: you must show understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, and understand the influence of contextual factors on the interpretation of texts. The Scarlet Letter has a double context and you must not confuse them. Its setting is Puritan Boston in the Massachusetts Bay Colony of the mid-seventeenth century, with real figures such as Governor Bellingham and the Reverend John Wilson among the invented ones; its moment of writing is 1850, two centuries later, by a writer whose own ancestor sat in judgement at the Salem witch trials and who had just lost his post at the Salem Custom House. The gap between the two is the point. A top-level answer uses context as the reason a detail means what it means, so that removing the context would break the sentence.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"},{"h":"The five kinds of context the specification names","t":"The specification lists five categories, and it is worth building a paragraph around a different one each time so that your AO4 does not repeat itself. First, the writer's own life and situation, where it relates to the text: Hawthorne's Salem ancestry and his lost Custom House surveyorship. Second, the historical setting, time and location of the text: Puritan Boston in the 1640s, theocratic, small and watching. Third, social and cultural contexts, such as attitudes to women, sin, public penance and illegitimacy. Fourth, the literary context: American Romance rather than realist novel, with Hawthorne's own 'neutral territory, somewhere between the real world and fairy-land, where the Actual and the Imaginary may meet'. Fifth, the way texts are received by different audiences at different times — how a twenty-first-century reader reads Hester's punishment against how an 1850 reader might. A top-level answer integrates these convincingly rather than parking them in a paragraph of their own.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"},{"h":"What the question looks like and what you may carry in","t":"Section B gives one 30-mark essay question from a choice of two on each set text, and these are Questions 11 to 22 on the paper, carrying 10 marks AO1, 10 AO2 and 10 AO4. Component 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for 60 marks, so about 45 minutes belongs to this essay, planning included. It is an open book examination: you may take in a clean copy of the prescribed edition, completely unmarked — no notes, no highlighting, no tabs. Open book changes your technique rather than your workload. You still need the novel in your head to choose the moment; the book only lets you get the words right. A top-level answer uses the copy for short, exact, discriminating quotation and never for transcription, and it spends the first five minutes choosing between the two questions on the basis of which one it can supply with method and context, not which sounds friendlier.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"Component 3 is available as an alternative to Component 2 and uses the same heritage list, so The Scarlet Letter may reach you as Assignment B instead: one 30-mark essay response to a teacher-devised assignment, marked out of 30 with 10 marks for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson. The assignment must allow learners to access AO1, AO2 and AO4, which is why the model titles always carry both instructions — 'You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel'. The advisory word count is 650 to 800 words with no penalty for exceeding it, and the five-level grid is applied on a best fit basis. A top-level assignment is judged on exactly the criteria the examination uses, so a title that cannot be answered with method and context is a title that caps the mark before the writing starts.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts"}],"examples":[{"title":"Decoding a Section B question before you plan","setup":"You are given this Section B question in the board's own wording: 'How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.' It carries 30 marks and the examination is open book. Before planning any paragraphs, work out what each part of the wording is asking you to supply, and settle the line of argument you will hold.","steps":["Mark the command stem 'How does ... portray': the subject of the essay is Hawthorne's method of presentation, so the community is something he builds, not something that simply happens.","Mark 'You must consider language, form and structure': that instruction carries the AO2 marks, so every developed paragraph must name a method — diction, symbol, narrative voice, chapter placement — and trace its effect.","Mark 'and refer to the context of the novel': that instruction carries the AO4 marks, so seventeenth-century theocratic Boston and Hawthorne's own vantage two centuries later must run inside the argument rather than sit in a block at the end.","Fix the position you will argue throughout: Hawthorne portrays the community as a single watching body whose judgement he reconstructs faithfully and then quietly convicts.","Choose evidence from across the whole novel so the design is visible: the prison door and the rose-bush at the opening, the market-place shaming, the Governor's hall, the Election Sermon procession."],"cues":["Which single word in the stem tells you the essay is about method rather than events?","Which instruction in the wording is the AO2 instruction, and what must each paragraph therefore contain?","Which instruction is the AO4 instruction, and where in the essay must that material go?","What one-sentence position could hold all three objectives together?","Why would four examples from the opening chapter score less than four spread across the book?"]},{"title":"Turning an opening-chapter detail into a Level 5 AO2 point","setup":"The novel's first chapter, 'The Prison-Door', opens on the crowd: 'A throng of bearded men, in sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats, intermixed with women, some wearing hoods, and others bareheaded, was assembled in front of a wooden edifice'. Only in the second paragraph does the narrator step back from that gathering to observe that the founders of a new colony have always found it among their earliest practical necessities 'to allot a portion of the virgin soil as a cemetery, and another portion as the site of a prison'; beside the prison door grows 'a wild rose-bush'. No character is named anywhere in the chapter. Your task is to build one paragraph on this material that an examiner would place in the top band for analysis of language, form and structure.","steps":["Take the position first: Hawthorne opens by showing a society that plans for death and punishment before anything else, and then lets nature contradict it.","Name the method in the diction — the compound colour word and the hats are visual shorthand for a community dressed identically in restraint, and the crowd is given as a single collective noun rather than as people.","Name the structural method — the crowd comes first and the colony's cemetery and prison are the explanation supplied for it in the paragraph after, and the whole chapter passes before a single character is named, so the reader meets the machinery of guilt before meeting anyone who is guilty.","Trace the effect of the contrast: the single flowering bush at the threshold is the only thing in the scene not made by the colonists, and the narrator lets it stand for the idea that the deep heart of Nature could pity and be kind to the condemned.","Close with the mini-judgement: the chapter is an argument in miniature, so that every later act of public judgement is already read against a pity the community has built itself not to feel."],"cues":["What claim about the colony do the cemetery and the prison let you make?","What does the crowd's clothing tell you about how the community treats difference?","Why does it matter that all of this comes before any character is introduced?","What in the scene has not been built or chosen by the Puritans?","What judgement about the whole novel can this one chapter now support?"]},{"title":"Integrating context instead of bolting it on","setup":"A candidate answering 'How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community?' has written this sentence as a separate final paragraph: 'Hawthorne wrote in 1850 and his ancestor was a judge at the Salem witch trials of 1692, and the Puritans were very strict people who believed in sin.' The assessment grid rewards context that is integrated convincingly into the response, and the specification names five categories of context. Your task is to rewrite this material so that it earns AO4 credit inside an analytical paragraph rather than after one.","steps":["Diagnose the fault: the sentence is true but free-standing, so it explains nothing about a moment in the text and could be pasted into an essay on any novel of the period.","Attach it to a specific moment — the scene in which the magistrates and the Reverend John Wilson interrogate Hester on the scaffold in front of the whole market-place.","Make the context do analytical work: in a theocracy the civil and religious authorities are the same men, so the interrogation is simultaneously a trial, a sermon and a piece of public theatre, which is why the crowd is a necessary part of the punishment rather than an audience for it.","Bring in the writer's own situation only where it bears on the text: a descendant of a Salem judge writing two centuries later can stage that authority accurately and still let the narrator's irony convict it.","Add the reception category to close the point: a reader now is likely to see a woman punished alone for a shared act, which sharpens rather than replaces the seventeenth-century reading."],"cues":["What is wrong with a context sentence that would fit any novel of the era?","Which single scene would give this context something to explain?","What does a theocracy make true about who is standing in judgement?","Which of the five context categories does the writer's ancestry belong to?","How could a modern reader's reaction be used without cancelling the historical one?"]},{"title":"Choosing between the two questions in the examination","setup":"Section B offers you a choice of two 30-mark questions on The Scarlet Letter. Question A is 'How does Hawthorne present the character of Pearl? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.' Question B is 'Hawthorne makes the reader pity Roger Chillingworth. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.' You have about forty-five minutes and a clean, unmarked copy of the text. Decide which to answer and justify the choice on the marks available rather than on preference.","steps":["Test each question against AO1 first: for each, list the moments you could argue from across the whole novel, not the ones you happen to remember best.","Test each against AO2: Pearl offers symbol, naming, the narrator's rival explanations and the brook-side doubling, while Chillingworth offers a physician's vocabulary of disease and the leech chapters.","Test each against AO4: Pearl invites Puritan attitudes to illegitimacy and to childhood, while Chillingworth invites the literary context of romance and the reception question of whether revenge reads as sin or as pathology.","Notice what the 'To what extent do you agree?' stem adds — it requires a weighed position, so choosing it commits you to arguing both sides before judging.","Choose the question for which you can already see three paragraphs that each carry method and context, and write the line of argument at the top of your plan before you open the book."],"cues":["Which objective should you test a question against first, and why?","Which question gives you more named methods to analyse?","Which context category does each question most naturally open?","What does the 'To what extent' wording oblige you to include?","What is the last thing you should do before opening the set text?"]},{"title":"Using the three scaffold scenes as a structural argument","setup":"The scaffold in the market-place is used three times in the novel: Hester stands on it in daylight holding the infant Pearl while the town watches; Dimmesdale climbs it alone at midnight and is joined there by Hester and Pearl, unseen; and after preaching the Election Sermon Dimmesdale climbs it a third time in daylight, confesses before the town and dies. A question asks how Hawthorne presents guilt and its effects. Your task is to build a paragraph whose evidence is the pattern itself rather than any single scene.","steps":["State the pattern as a claim: Hawthorne returns the novel to one place three times so that the reader measures change against a fixed point.","Read the variable that changes each time — the light and the audience move from public daylight, to private darkness, back to public daylight, which makes concealment the middle term of the design.","Draw the consequence for character: Hester's guilt is fixed at the start and then converted into work and skill, while Dimmesdale's is hidden and turns inward, so the narrator can observe that no man can wear one face to himself and another to the multitude without finally getting bewildered as to which may be the true.","Add the context strand: public penance was the community's remedy, so the novel tests that remedy by giving one sinner the punishment and the other the secret, and lets the reader watch which of the two is destroyed.","Judge it: the structure is the argument, since Hawthorne proves by repetition what no single speech in the book asserts."],"cues":["What does repeating one location three times let a reader measure?","What changes between the first scaffold scene and the second?","Which of the two adults is destroyed, and what does the pattern suggest is the cause?","Which context does the Puritan practice of public penance supply here?","Why is this paragraph stronger than one built on a single scaffold scene?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section B essay carry, and how are they divided between the assessment objectives?","a":"Thirty marks: 10 for AO1, 10 for AO2 and 10 for AO4, weighted equally."},{"q":"Which instruction in the question wording carries the AO4 marks?","a":"'and refer to the context of the novel'."},{"q":"What exactly may you take into the Component 2 examination?","a":"A clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition of each set text; it is an open book examination."},{"q":"Name the five categories of context the specification lists.","a":"The writer's life and situation; the historical setting of the text; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; and reception by different audiences at different times."},{"q":"Why does naming a symbol in The Scarlet Letter score little on its own?","a":"The AO2 grid rises from identification to sustained analysis and then cohesive evaluation, so the effect of the method must be traced, not just labelled."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2 Section B: one 30-mark essay from a choice of two, Questions 11 to 22, assessing AO1 (10), AO2 (10) and AO4 (10); Component 2 is 1 hour 30 minutes, 60 marks, 40% of the qualification.","Section B is open book: a clean, completely unmarked copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in.","The five levels on the 30-mark grid: Level 1 is 1 to 6, Level 2 is 7 to 12, Level 3 is 13 to 18, Level 4 is 19 to 24, Level 5 is 25 to 30, awarded on a best fit basis.","The five categories of context: the writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; reception by different audiences at different times.","The Scarlet Letter was published in 1850 by Nathaniel Hawthorne (1804 to 1864), and is subtitled 'A Romance'; it is prefaced by the autobiographical sketch 'The Custom-House'.","The novel is set in Puritan Boston in the Massachusetts Bay Colony in the mid-seventeenth century; Governor Bellingham and the Reverend John Wilson were real figures of the colony.","The three scaffold scenes are the spine of the structure: Hester's public shaming with the infant Pearl, Dimmesdale's midnight vigil, and Dimmesdale's confession and death after the Election Sermon.","The novel ends with the motto on the shared tombstone: 'ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES.'","Component 3 Assignment B is the coursework alternative: a teacher-devised 30-mark assignment marked 10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4, advisory length 650 to 800 words."],"questions":[{"q":"Show understanding of how the Section B question on The Scarlet Letter is assessed: name the three assessment objectives it assesses and the raw marks each carries.","a":"AO1 10 marks, AO2 10 marks and AO4 10 marks, making 30.","sol":"Section B of Component 2 (Questions 11 to 22) assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4. AO1, demonstrating a close knowledge and understanding of texts, maintaining a critical style and presenting an informed personal engagement, carries 10 raw marks. AO2, analysing the language, form and structure used by a writer to create meanings and effects, carries 10. AO4, showing understanding of the relationships between texts and the contexts in which they were written, carries 10. The three are equally weighted, which is why a developed paragraph must carry all three rather than treating context as an afterthought. AO3 is not assessed anywhere in Component 2.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Section B is the only part of Component 2 that rewards anything about the world the novel came from.","Look at how the Breakdown of assessment objectives and raw marks splits Questions 11 to 22.","Three objectives share one tariff; give the objective, its wording and its number of raw marks for each."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Show understanding of what learners may take into the Component 2 examination for The Scarlet Letter, and the condition attached to it.","a":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition, completely unmarked; the component is open book.","sol":"Component 2 is an open book examination, so learners may take a clean copy of the prescribed edition of the selected text into the examination room, and that copy must be completely unmarked: no annotation, no highlighting, no inserted notes. Centres or learners must purchase a prescribed edition at the start of the course, the editions being those listed in Appendix 3. The practical consequence for The Scarlet Letter is that quotation should be exact, short and chosen in advance, since the copy exists to secure accuracy, not to be transcribed from; no credit is given for length.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Component 1 is closed book and supplies the poems; Component 2 works the other way round, and the specification attaches a string to it.","The specification states both what may be taken and the state it must be in.","Give the item, its required condition, and the name the specification gives this kind of examination."],"codes":["Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts"]},{"q":"Show understanding of the five categories of context the specification names, giving one example of each from The Scarlet Letter.","a":"Writer's life; historical setting; social and cultural contexts; literary context; reception at different times, each with a text-specific example.","sol":"First, the writer's own life and individual situation where it relates to the text: Hawthorne was born in Salem in 1804 into a family whose ancestor sat in judgement at the Salem witch trials of 1692, and he had lost his surveyorship at the Salem Custom House shortly before writing. Second, the historical setting, time and location of the text: Puritan Boston in the Massachusetts Bay Colony of the mid-seventeenth century, a theocracy in which Governor Bellingham and the Reverend John Wilson hold civil and religious authority together. Third, social and cultural contexts: attitudes to adultery, to illegitimacy, to public penance and to a woman's economic independence, which Hester secures by her needle. Fourth, the literary context: Hawthorne subtitles the book 'A Romance' and in 'The Custom-House' describes a 'neutral territory, somewhere between the real world and fairy-land, where the Actual and the Imaginary may meet', which licenses his symbolism and his refusal to settle whether a mark really appeared on Dimmesdale's breast. Fifth, the way texts are received by different audiences at different times: a reader in the twenty-first century is likely to notice at once that only the woman is punished, which is a reading the novel supports but does not state.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The specification's own list runs outward from the writer to the reader.","One of the five is about how later audiences respond rather than about the past at all.","Set them out as five labelled points, each tied to one specific detail of the novel."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Demonstrate close knowledge of the three scaffold scenes in The Scarlet Letter: what happens in each, and in what order?","a":"Hester's daylight shaming with the infant Pearl; Dimmesdale's unseen midnight vigil with Hester and Pearl; Dimmesdale's daylight confession and death after the Election Sermon.","sol":"In the first, Hester is brought from the prison to the scaffold in the market-place in daylight, holding the infant Pearl, wearing the embroidered letter, and is interrogated before the whole town by the magistrates and the ministers while she refuses to name the child's father; Chillingworth watches from the edge of the crowd. In the second, at midnight and unobserved by the town, Dimmesdale mounts the same scaffold in a private rehearsal of a confession he cannot make, and Hester and Pearl join him there on their way from a deathbed. In the third, again in daylight and before the whole community, Dimmesdale leaves the procession after the Election Sermon, climbs the scaffold with Hester and Pearl, confesses, and dies there. Read together the three scenes fix one location and vary the light and the audience, so that concealment becomes the middle term of the design.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["One place is used three times; ask what is different each time.","Track two variables across the three scenes: who is watching, and whether it is day or night.","Give the three scenes in order, each in a sentence naming who is present."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"The community as a single watching body: collective description, the prison and cemetery opening, public penance as theatre, judged by an ironic narrator writing two centuries later.","sol":"Hawthorne portrays the Puritan community as a single watching body whose machinery of judgement he reconstructs faithfully and then quietly convicts. The opening chapter establishes it structurally, and no character is named in the whole of it. The novel begins on the crowd, given collectively — 'A throng of bearded men, in sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats' — where the compound colour word and the identical hats make uniformity visible and individual judgement invisible. Only in the paragraph after does the narrator step back to explain that gathering, observing that the founders of a new colony have always found it among their earliest practical necessities 'to allot a portion of the virgin soil as a cemetery, and another portion as the site of a prison', so the massed townspeople are shown first and the architecture of death and punishment that produced them second. Against this stands the one thing the colonists did not build, 'a wild rose-bush' at the prison door, which the narrator allows to suggest that the deep heart of Nature could pity and be kind to the condemned; the community, by implication, cannot. Structurally, the market-place scaffold makes punishment a performance that requires an audience, and in a theocracy that audience is also the court and the congregation, since Governor Bellingham and the Reverend John Wilson wield civil and religious power together. Hawthorne is exact about this seventeenth-century world, but the narrating voice belongs to 1850, and its irony is the instrument of judgement: the same townspeople who impose the letter later decide that it 'meant Able', which exposes their verdicts as revisable opinion rather than revealed truth. Yet the portrayal is not a caricature. The community's charity is real, Hester's needle is wanted at every official occasion, and the narrator concedes the sincerity of its faith. The judgement I would offer is that Hawthorne's Puritans are condemned less for cruelty than for outsourcing conscience: they punish so publicly that they never have to examine themselves, which is precisely the corruption that destroys the one man they most admire.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The community is presented as one thing before it is presented as people; look at how the opening chapter introduces it.","Plan three paragraphs that each pair a named method with one of the specification's context categories.","Take a position on whether the portrayal is hostile or divided, and let the evidence test it rather than illustrate it."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Hawthorne present Hester Prynne's strength in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Strength shown through silence, craft, endurance and the town's reinterpretation of the letter, set against the constraints on a woman in a Puritan theocracy.","sol":"Hawthorne presents Hester's strength as endurance converted into usefulness, and he measures it by what it costs. On the scaffold she is silent under interrogation, and that silence is active: refusing to name the father is the only power available to a woman with no legal, economic or ecclesiastical standing in a theocracy, and Hawthorne makes it the first thing we know about her. The letter itself is the key language choice. It is imposed as a brand, yet it appears 'in fine red cloth, surrounded with an elaborate embroidery', so the instrument of shame is also the first exhibition of the skill by which she will support herself and her child; the symbol is turned by its wearer without ever being removed. Structurally, Hawthorne separates her from Dimmesdale by giving her the punishment and him the secret, and the years between the scaffold scenes do the arguing: she becomes indispensable to the town, and the narrator can record that many people refused the letter's original meaning and said that it 'meant Able'. The same chapter concedes the cost, observing that the scarlet letter 'had not done its office' in the way intended, since what it has produced is not penitence but a mind that has begun to question the whole order that punished her — which is why her strength frightens the narrator a little. Context sharpens all of this: a seventeenth-century woman judged by men who are both magistrates and ministers has no route to rehabilitation except through her own labour, while Hawthorne writing in 1850 can let her think thoughts his Puritans would have hanged her for. My judgement is that the strength Hawthorne admires is not defiance but self-possession: Hester's decision at the end to return and resume the letter voluntarily converts a sentence into a choice, and that is the only victory the novel allows anyone.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what powers a woman in this society actually has, and then look at which ones Hester uses.","Trace one object across the whole novel and let its changing meaning structure the essay.","Decide whether her strength is defiance or something quieter, and hold that line through every paragraph."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Roger Chillingworth is the true villain of The Scarlet Letter. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Largely agree on his chosen, sustained violation of another's soul, but qualify: the narrator makes him a product of the community's secrecy and offers pathology as well as evil.","sol":"I agree substantially, but the interest of the novel lies in how carefully Hawthorne blocks the simple verdict. The case for the proposition is strong. Chillingworth's sin is the only one in the book that is chosen coldly and sustained for years: he conceals his identity, attaches himself to Dimmesdale as physician, and the narrative gives him the vocabulary of disease and dissection, so that the chapters of his work on the minister turn healing into predation and the leech becomes the parasite its name also denotes. Structurally he is the one character who is present at all three scaffold scenes as a watcher, which makes surveillance itself his method. Against this, Hawthorne repeatedly opens a second explanation. He gives Chillingworth a slight physical deformity and a marriage in which an old scholar bought a young woman's future, so the wrong precedes the revenge; he lets the man state that his own transformation into a fiend is a fate rather than a decision; and he closes the account by having him die within the year and leave his property to Pearl, an act of restitution no villain of romance is obliged to perform. The context matters to the judgement: in a theocracy there is no confession available to Dimmesdale that does not destroy him, so the secret that feeds Chillingworth is manufactured by the community, and Hawthorne writing in 1850 can present obsession as something closer to sickness than to devilry — the literary context of the romance allows him to keep both readings alive without choosing. My conclusion is that Chillingworth is the true villain in the precise sense the novel defines: not the one who feels most desire, but the one who violates another human heart deliberately and without remorse. The qualification is that Hawthorne makes him the community's product rather than its opposite, so condemning him alone lets the Puritan town off a charge the novel means it to answer.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["'To what extent' means the counter-case has to be argued properly, not mentioned.","Look for the places where the narrator offers you a second explanation for the same behaviour.","Define what the novel itself treats as the worst offence before you decide who commits it."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Show understanding of how a single paragraph about Pearl can earn AO1, AO2 and AO4 credit at the same time. Use one moment from the novel.","a":"One moment, one claim: close knowledge argued (AO1), a named method traced (AO2), and a context that explains the meaning (AO4), woven in one chain.","sol":"Take the moment at the Governor's hall, where the magistrates consider removing Pearl from Hester and the child is questioned about her making. AO1 is earned by using the moment accurately and arguing from it rather than retelling it: the claim might be that Hawthorne makes Pearl the one form of evidence the Puritan authorities cannot read. AO2 is earned inside the same sentence by naming the method and tracing its effect: the narrator repeatedly describes Pearl as the scarlet letter given human form — 'the scarlet letter endowed with life' — so a symbol has been animated and can now answer back, and the child's refusal to give the catechised answer turns the examination into a test of the examiners. AO4 is earned by making context explain the meaning rather than decorate it: in a theocracy a child is legally and spiritually the property of a moral order, so the hearing is not cruelty but procedure, and the scene shows the machinery working exactly as designed and still failing. The paragraph then closes with a mini-judgement — that Pearl is Hawthorne's device for exposing the limits of a system that can label a sin but not interpret its consequence. Nothing in that paragraph is bolted on; remove the context sentence and the analysis stops making sense, which is the test the top level applies.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Choose a moment where the authorities are doing something official, so that context has work to do.","Write the claim first, then ask what method produces it and what context explains it.","The test of integration is whether removing the context sentence breaks the argument."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Hawthorne present guilt and its effects in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Guilt acknowledged versus guilt concealed: the three scaffold scenes, the narrator's ambiguity, and Puritan public penance as the remedy the novel puts on trial.","sol":"Hawthorne's argument about guilt is made structurally: he gives one sinner the punishment and the other the secret, and lets the reader watch which of them survives. The three scaffold scenes are the mechanism. The same location returns three times while the light and the audience change — public daylight, private midnight, public daylight again — so concealment is literally the middle term of the design, and the reader measures each character against a fixed point. Hester's guilt is placed outside her and worn, and the novel converts it into labour, skill and finally into a meaning the town itself revises. Dimmesdale's stays inside, and Hawthorne gives it a physical vocabulary: the hand pressed to the heart, the secret self-punishment, the vigil rehearsed where no one can see. The narrator generalises the effect in a sentence that reads as the novel's thesis — 'No man, for any considerable period, can wear one face to himself, and another to the multitude, without finally getting bewildered as to which may be the true' — and the minister's collapse is the demonstration. Chillingworth supplies the third case: guilt that refuses to recognise itself as guilt at all, so that when Hester confronts him in Chapter XIV and asks him to relent he can call the whole ruin their fate — 'Let the black flower blossom as it may!' — and go on believing himself a mere observer. Context is essential here rather than ornamental. Public penance was the colony's actual remedy for sin, and by staging it in the first chapter and then following the unpunished sinner for seven years Hawthorne puts that remedy on trial; the narrator's characteristic refusal to settle what finally appeared on Dimmesdale's breast belongs to the romance form he names in his subtitle, keeping the supernatural and the psychological readings open together. My judgement is that the novel does not present guilt as destructive in itself: it presents secrecy as the destroyer, which is why the ending's instruction is not to repent but to 'Be true! Be true! Be true!'","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Three characters carry guilt in three different ways; that is your paragraph structure.","Ask what the repetition of a single location is designed to let a reader measure.","Distinguish the thing the novel condemns from the thing it merely observes, and say so in your conclusion."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"Hawthorne's presentation of Hester would be read very differently in the twenty-first century from the way it was read in 1850. To what extent do you agree? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Agree in emphasis, not in substance: the text already invites the modern reading through its narrator and structure, though its ending withholds the liberation a modern reader expects.","sol":"I agree in emphasis but not in substance, and the distinction is where the marks are. The strongest case for difference is the unequal punishment. A reader now sees immediately that a shared act is punished in one body, and notices that the novel's title names the badge rather than the woman. Hester's economic self-sufficiency through her needle, her refusal to name the father, and the forest scene in which she proposes escape all read as agency to a modern eye. Yet Hawthorne's own narrator already supplies that reading. He records that the townspeople came to say the letter 'meant Able', he observes that the scarlet letter 'had not done its office' because it had made her speculate rather than repent, and he lets her tell Dimmesdale that 'What we did had a consecration of its own', which is a claim no Puritan magistrate could hear. The 1850 reader was not a Puritan either: writing two centuries after the events, in a decade of live argument about women's position, Hawthorne could stage the theocracy as historical and its verdicts as revisable. What does separate the readings is the ending. A modern reader tends to expect the forest scene's freedom to be honoured, and instead Hester returns to the cottage and resumes the letter of her own will, and the novel closes on the shared tombstone and its motto, 'ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES.' To an 1850 reader that resumption could read as moral resolution; today it reads more easily as a woman taking ownership of the only identity society allowed her. My judgement is that the text has not changed and the reading has only sharpened: the specification's category of reception at different times is best used here to show that Hawthorne built the ambiguity in, so that each age finds its own emphasis without needing to read against him.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Ask first whether the modern reading is one the narrator already offers, or one imposed on him.","Find the two or three moments where the narrator steps outside Puritan judgement and judge how far they go.","The ending is the real test of the proposition; decide what Hester's return does to your answer before you plan."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1"]},{"q":"Analyse the first and last scaffold scenes as a matched pair, and show understanding of what the pairing reveals about Hawthorne's structural design.","a":"Same place, same daylight, same three figures, opposite distribution of shame: the design converts a punishment imposed on one into a truth told by the other.","sol":"The two scenes are deliberately matched so that the differences carry meaning. Both take place on the market-place scaffold, both in daylight, both before the assembled town, and in each Hester, Pearl and Dimmesdale are all present. In the first, Hester stands exposed holding the infant while Dimmesdale stands above her among the ministers and publicly urges her to name the man; the community sees one sinner. In the last, the minister has just preached the Election Sermon at the height of his reputation, and he leaves the procession to climb the scaffold with Hester and Pearl and confess, and he dies there. The design converts a punishment imposed on a woman into a truth told by a man, and it does so by moving one figure from the balcony to the platform. The matched framing also lets Hawthorne complete the smaller patterns: Pearl, who has refused Dimmesdale's private acknowledgement throughout, accepts the public one; Chillingworth, whose power depended entirely on the secret, is left with nothing and dies within the year. Read as a pair, the two scenes state the book's thesis without the narrator having to argue it: the scaffold does not reform anyone, but it does eventually extract the truth, and the cost of the delay is the length of the novel.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["List what is identical in the two scenes before you look for what has changed.","One character has moved physically between the two scenes; that movement is the argument.","Say what the pattern proves that no speech in the novel states outright."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]},{"q":"Show understanding of what Component 3 Assignment B requires: write a title for an assignment on The Scarlet Letter in the board's model form, and demonstrate how it gives learners access to AO1, AO2 and AO4.","a":"A title in the board's model form carrying both instructions, for example on Hawthorne's portrayal of the Puritan community, with each instruction mapped to an objective.","sol":"A suitable title, in the board's own model form, would be: 'How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.' It gives access to AO1 because the subject is broad enough to be argued from across the whole text — the prison door, the market-place, the Governor's hall, the Election Sermon procession — so a learner must show close knowledge and take a personal critical position rather than retell one episode. It gives access to AO2 because the first instruction requires named methods: collective description, symbolism, the intrusive narrator, the placement of the opening chapter. It gives access to AO4 because the second instruction requires the contexts in which the novel was written, and the topic cannot be answered without the theocratic historical setting and Hawthorne's own 1850 vantage. The assignment is marked out of 30 with 10 marks for each objective, on the five-level grid, best fit, with an advisory length of 650 to 800 words and no penalty for exceeding it. A title such as 'Tell the story of Hester Prynne' would fail the requirement, because it invites narrative and gives neither instruction, capping the response before it is written.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the two model wordings the specification prints and copy their shape exactly.","Each of the two standard instructions in the wording exists to unlock one particular objective.","Show your title working by naming what a learner would write for each objective, and contrast it with a title that would fail."],"codes":["Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4"]},{"q":"How does Hawthorne use the natural world in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel. (30 marks)","a":"Nature as the counter-authority to the town: the rose-bush, the forest, the brook and the sunshine, sympathetic to Pearl and Hester but unable to offer a lasting refuge.","sol":"Hawthorne uses the natural world as the colony's counter-authority: it is the one jurisdiction the Puritans have not built, and he sets it against the town at every structural turn. It is introduced before anyone else. Beside the prison door, among the cemetery and the gaol that are the settlement's first works, grows 'a wild rose-bush', and the narrator lets it stand for the possibility that the deep heart of Nature could pity and be kind to the condemned. That opening image is then developed by the forest, which is outside the town's sight and therefore the only place Hester and Dimmesdale can speak; it is there that she can claim 'What we did had a consecration of its own', a sentence unsayable inside the settlement. The structural pairing of market-place and forest organises the middle of the book, and Hawthorne is careful to keep the forest ambiguous: it is also the place the townspeople associate with the Black Man, so the freedom it offers is legally and spiritually indistinguishable from damnation in the Puritan scheme. Pearl belongs to this jurisdiction, described in the narrator's imagery as a creature of the wild who is at home among the trees and the sunshine that avoids her mother, and the brook that separates her from the reunited lovers works as an exact structural boundary — the child will not cross to a version of her mother without the letter. Context explains why the pattern matters: to a Puritan settlement the American wilderness was hostile ground and the theatre of witchcraft, so Hawthorne's sympathetic nature is a Romantic writer's revaluation of it, licensed by the romance form and by two centuries of distance. My judgement is that nature in this novel is genuine relief but not a genuine refuge: the escape the forest scene plans never leaves the forest — on the walk back through the town Dimmesdale's new freedom keeps turning into wicked impulses he has to fight down, and by Election Day, a few days later, the plan has collapsed: Chillingworth has taken passage on the same ship, and the minister confesses on the scaffold and dies there instead, and Hawthorne's final image of consolation is not a rose-bush but an engraved motto, which suggests that the only lasting freedom he will grant is truth told inside the community rather than escape from it.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Track one natural image from the opening chapter and ask what it is placed next to.","Set the forest against the market-place and ask what each place permits its characters to say.","Decide whether nature offers escape or only relief, and let the ending settle your judgement."],"codes":["Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)"]},{"q":"'The Custom-House' is essential to a strong Section B answer on The Scarlet Letter. To what extent do you agree?","a":"Valuable rather than essential: it supplies biographical and literary context and the framing device, but only earns credit when it explains a moment in the narrative; summarised for its own sake it drifts off the question.","sol":"I agree that the sketch is valuable, but 'essential' overstates it: it is useful in three specific ways and dangerous in one. First, it supplies the writer's own life and situation, the specification's first context category: the sketch is openly autobiographical about the narrator's employment and dismissal at the Salem Custom House and about his ancestors' place in the town's history, which is exactly the material AO4 rewards when it is tied to a moment in the narrative. Second, it supplies the literary context: the description of moonlight creating 'a neutral territory, somewhere between the real world and fairy-land, where the Actual and the Imaginary may meet' is Hawthorne's own account of the romance he is about to write, and it explains, rather than merely labels, his refusal to settle whether the mark on Dimmesdale's breast was real. Third, it is a structural device: by presenting the story as a manuscript and a faded embroidered letter found among old papers, Hawthorne creates a frame narrator and a claim of historical documentation that distances him from the events and lets the irony work. The danger is that a candidate who has revised it reaches for it as a block of biography at the start of the essay, which is precisely what the grid describes at the lowest level of the context strand. My judgement is that the sketch is worth about one integrated sentence per essay, deployed where it explains a choice in the narrative, and worth nothing at all when it is summarised — so valuable, and to that extent only, rather than essential.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Ask which of the specification's five context categories the sketch actually supplies.","Separate what it tells you about the writer from what it tells you about the kind of book he is writing.","A weighed answer needs a cost as well as a benefit, so end by saying how much of the sketch belongs in an answer and how far the proposition holds."],"codes":["Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","Component 2: Content overview"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many raw marks does AO4 carry in the Section B essay?","a":"Ten, out of thirty."},{"q":"In what year was The Scarlet Letter published, and what subtitle does Hawthorne give it?","a":"1850; 'A Romance'."},{"q":"What are the last words of the novel?","a":"'ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES.'"},{"q":"Which phrase in a Section B question wording switches on the AO2 marks?","a":"'You must consider language, form and structure'."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Section B mark split for The Scarlet Letter","back":"Thirty marks: AO1 ten, AO2 ten, AO4 ten. Questions 11 to 22, a choice of two, in Component 2 (1 hour 30 minutes, 60 marks, 40 per cent).","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Open book condition for Component 2","back":"A clean copy of the prescribed edition may be taken in, completely unmarked. Use it for short exact quotation, never for transcription.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"The wording that carries the AO4 marks","back":"'and refer to the context of the novel'. Its partner, 'You must consider language, form and structure', carries the AO2 marks.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The five levels on the 30-mark grid","back":"Level 1 is 1 to 6, Level 2 is 7 to 12, Level 3 is 13 to 18, Level 4 is 19 to 24, Level 5 is 25 to 30, awarded on a best fit basis.","code":"Assessment overview: Section B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false},{"front":"Name one of the five categories of context the specification lists","back":"The writer's own life and situation; the historical setting, time and location; social and cultural contexts; the literary context; reception by different audiences at different times.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":true},{"front":"Publication and form of The Scarlet Letter","back":"Published 1850 by Nathaniel Hawthorne (1804 to 1864), subtitled 'A Romance', prefaced by the autobiographical sketch 'The Custom-House', and told by an intrusive third-person narrator.","code":"Component 2: Content overview","open":false},{"front":"Setting of The Scarlet Letter","back":"Puritan Boston in the Massachusetts Bay Colony, mid-seventeenth century: a theocracy where Governor Bellingham and the Reverend John Wilson hold civil and religious authority together.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO4","open":false},{"front":"The three scaffold scenes","back":"Hester's daylight shaming with the infant Pearl; Dimmesdale's unseen midnight vigil; Dimmesdale's daylight confession and death after the Election Sermon. Same place, changing light and audience.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The rose-bush at the prison door","back":"'a wild rose-bush' grows beside the gaol, the one thing the colonists did not build; the narrator lets it suggest the deep heart of Nature could pity and be kind to the condemned.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The letter re-read as 'Able'","back":"Years on, many townspeople refuse the badge's original meaning and say it 'meant Able'. Proof that the community's verdicts are revisable opinion, not revealed truth.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Pearl as living symbol","back":"The narrator calls her 'the scarlet letter endowed with life': a symbol animated, able to question its examiners and to refuse her father's private acknowledgement.","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO2","open":false},{"front":"The narrator's thesis on concealment","back":"'No man, for any considerable period, can wear one face to himself, and another to the multitude, without finally getting bewildered as to which may be the true.'","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Hawthorne's 'neutral territory'","back":"In 'The Custom-House' he describes 'a neutral territory, somewhere between the real world and fairy-land, where the Actual and the Imaginary may meet' — his own definition of the romance form.","code":"Understanding the relationship between a text and its context (AO4)","open":false},{"front":"The moral of the Conclusion","back":"'Be true! Be true! Be true!' The novel condemns secrecy rather than desire, and closes on the tombstone motto 'ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES.'","code":"Section B: Literary Heritage Texts — AO1","open":false},{"front":"Component 3 Assignment B requirements","back":"A teacher-devised 30-mark essay on the heritage text, 10 AO1, 10 AO2, 10 AO4, internally assessed and externally moderated by Pearson; advisory length 650 to 800 words, no penalty for exceeding.","code":"Component 3, Assignment B – Literary Heritage Texts","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5","items":["Question answered: How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.","Hawthorne convicts the Puritan community before he has named a single Puritan. The novel opens on the crowd itself, and gives it collectively — 'A throng of bearded men, in sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats' — where the compound colour word drains the scene of individuality and the identical hats make conformity something the reader can see before it is something a character can feel. Only in the paragraph after does the narrator widen from that gathering to the colony that produced it, observing that the founders of a new settlement have always found it among their earliest practical necessities 'to allot a portion of the virgin soil as a cemetery, and another portion as the site of a prison': the throng is explained by the architecture, and death and punishment turn out to have been built in advance of any crime. Against both stands the one growth the colonists did not plant, 'a wild rose-bush' at the threshold, which the narrator allows to offer what the town withholds, since the deep heart of Nature could pity and be kind to the condemned. The contrast is not decorative: in a theocracy the magistrates and the ministers are the same men, so there is no appeal beyond the community's judgement and the rose must carry the whole weight of mercy alone. What makes the portrayal formidable rather than merely hostile is the position of the narrating voice, which belongs to 1850 and therefore reconstructs seventeenth-century certainty faithfully while quietly outliving it — the same town that fixes the letter later decides it 'meant Able'. Hawthorne's Puritans, then, are condemned less for cruelty than for outsourcing conscience: a society that punishes this publicly never has to examine itself.","Why this is Level 5: the point is a critical position rather than a topic; the evidence is short, exact and discriminating; the analysis moves from language (compound colour word, collective noun) to structure (the order of the opening chapter, which gives the crowd first, the colony's architecture second and no named character at all) to narrative voice, and evaluates rather than identifies; the context is load-bearing, since removing the theocracy sentence would break the reasoning; and the paragraph closes on a judgement that answers the question."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3","items":["Question answered: How does Hawthorne portray the Puritan community in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.","Hawthorne shows the Puritan community as harsh and judgemental. At the start of the novel the whole town gathers outside the prison to watch Hester Prynne be brought out with her baby and made to stand on the scaffold. The people in the crowd are described as wearing 'sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats', which is effective imagery because it shows they are gloomy and serious people. Some of the women in the crowd say cruel things about Hester and think her punishment is not severe enough, which shows they have no sympathy for her. Hawthorne also uses the symbol of the rose-bush by the prison door to show that nature is kinder than the Puritans are. This links to the context because the Puritans were very religious and believed strongly in punishing sin, and Hawthorne's own ancestor was a judge at the Salem witch trials. Overall, Hawthorne presents the Puritans in a negative way.","Why this is only Level 3: the knowledge is sound and the examples are relevant, but the paragraph is largely narrative in its second and fourth sentences; the quotation is named and then labelled as 'effective imagery' rather than analysed, so the grid's 'sound understanding' is reached but not 'sustained analysis'; there is no comment on form or structure at all, only language; and the context is accurate but bolted on in a single sentence beginning 'This links to the context', which the grid treats as comment rather than integration.","What would move it up: convert 'shows they are gloomy' into an effect on the reader; use the fact that the cemetery, the prison and the crowd all appear before any character does, which is a structural point; make the Salem sentence explain a detail of the scene rather than sit beside it; and replace 'Overall, Hawthorne presents the Puritans in a negative way' with a judgement that says what he convicts them of."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — The Scarlet Letter, Nathaniel Hawthorne","items":["Author and date: Nathaniel Hawthorne, 1804 to 1864, published The Scarlet Letter in 1850, subtitled 'A Romance'.","Frame: the novel is prefaced by 'The Custom-House', an autobiographical sketch in which the narrator describes his post at the Salem Custom House and the discovery of an old manuscript and a faded embroidered letter among the building's papers.","Setting: Boston, in the Puritan Massachusetts Bay Colony, in the mid-seventeenth century — a theocracy in which civil and religious authority are held by the same men.","Hester Prynne: sent ahead to the colony by her much older husband, convicted of adultery, sentenced to stand on the scaffold and to wear the letter A for life; she refuses to name the father and supports herself and her child by her needlework.","Arthur Dimmesdale: the young, admired minister; the child's father; conceals his guilt for years, keeps a secret midnight vigil on the scaffold, preaches the Election Sermon, then confesses publicly on the scaffold and dies.","Roger Chillingworth: Hester's husband, a scholar and physician much older than she is, with one shoulder slightly higher than the other; arrives on the day of her shaming, makes her swear to conceal his identity, attaches himself to Dimmesdale as his doctor, and dies within a year of the minister, leaving his property to Pearl.","Pearl: Hester's daughter, dressed in scarlet and gold, described by the narrator as 'the scarlet letter endowed with life'; wild, unbiddable, refuses Dimmesdale's private acknowledgement and accepts only the public one.","Minor figures: Governor Bellingham, in whose hall the removal of Pearl is considered; the Reverend John Wilson, the senior minister; Mistress Hibbins, the Governor's sister, who speaks of the Black Man of the forest and is later executed as a witch.","Plot in order: the prison door and the market-place shaming; Chillingworth's arrival and concealment; Hester's years at her needle; the hearing at the Governor's hall; Chillingworth's work on Dimmesdale; the midnight vigil; Hester's meeting with Chillingworth; the forest meeting with Dimmesdale and the plan to escape; the New England holiday, the procession and the Election Sermon; the confession and death on the scaffold; Chillingworth's death; Hester's years abroad with Pearl and her voluntary return to the cottage and the letter; the shared grave.","Structure: twenty-four chapters after the Custom-House sketch, organised around three scaffold scenes — public daylight, private midnight, public daylight — and around the opposition of the market-place and the forest.","Form and narration: a romance rather than a realist novel; a third-person narrator who comments openly on the action and repeatedly offers rival explanations for the same event, including for what, if anything, appeared on Dimmesdale's breast.","Key quotations to use exactly: 'A throng of bearded men, in sad-coloured garments and grey steeple-crowned hats'; 'to allot a portion of the virgin soil as a cemetery, and another portion as the site of a prison'; 'a wild rose-bush'; 'in fine red cloth, surrounded with an elaborate embroidery'; 'the scarlet letter endowed with life'; 'No man, for any considerable period, can wear one face to himself, and another to the multitude, without finally getting bewildered as to which may be the true'; 'What we did had a consecration of its own'; 'Let the black flower blossom as it may!'; 'Be true! Be true! Be true!'; 'ON A FIELD, SABLE, THE LETTER A, GULES.'","Context, the writer's life: Hawthorne was born in Salem, Massachusetts; an ancestor, John Hathorne, was a judge at the Salem witch trials of 1692, and the novelist added the w to the family name; he lost his surveyorship at the Salem Custom House shortly before writing the novel, and wrote about that post in the introductory sketch.","Context, the literary movement: American Romance, published in 1850, the year before Melville's Moby-Dick (1851), which Melville dedicated to Hawthorne; in 'The Custom-House' Hawthorne describes the romance writer's ground as 'a neutral territory, somewhere between the real world and fairy-land, where the Actual and the Imaginary may meet'.","Context, two hundred years of distance: the events are set in the 1640s and the book was written in 1850, so the narrator can reconstruct Puritan certainty and judge it at once — the gap between setting and writing is the novel's chief contextual fact and the one examiners most reward.","Prescribed editions, Appendix 3: Alma Classics 2015 (9781847494214); Penguin Books 2012 (9780141199450); OUP Oxford 2008 (9780199537808); Wordsworth Editions Classics 1992 (9781853260292).","Examiner-rewarded moments: the opening chapter's prison, cemetery and rose-bush; the scaffold interrogation; Chillingworth's bargain of silence with Hester; the hearing over Pearl at the Governor's hall; the midnight vigil; the chapter in which the town re-reads the letter as 'Able'; the forest meeting and the plan to escape; Pearl refusing to cross the brook; the Election Sermon and the confession; the resumed letter and the shared tombstone."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — 'How does Hawthorne present guilt and its effects in The Scarlet Letter? You must consider language, form and structure and refer to the context of the novel.' (30 marks, about 45 minutes)","items":["Decoded question: 'How does ... present' means the essay is about Hawthorne's methods, not about who feels guilty; 'guilt and its effects' means the argument must follow consequences over time, so the essay needs the whole novel; 'You must consider language, form and structure' switches on the ten AO2 marks, so every paragraph names a method; 'refer to the context of the novel' switches on the ten AO4 marks, so Puritan public penance and Hawthorne's 1850 vantage must be inside the reasoning; the remaining ten marks, AO1, are for a critical position argued from close knowledge.","Line of argument to state in the opening three sentences: Hawthorne does not present guilt as destructive in itself — he presents concealed guilt as the destroyer, and he proves it structurally by giving one sinner the punishment and the other the secret.","Paragraph 1 — the design that carries the argument: the three scaffold scenes as a fixed point with two variables, light and audience, running public daylight, private midnight, public daylight; concealment is literally the middle term. Context: public penance was the colony's actual remedy, so returning to the same platform is Hawthorne putting that remedy on trial.","Paragraph 2 — guilt worn: Hester's letter is imposed but appears 'in fine red cloth, surrounded with an elaborate embroidery', so the badge of shame is also the exhibition of the skill that will feed her; over the years the town itself re-reads it and says it 'meant Able'. Effect: acknowledged guilt can be worked with, and even renegotiated. Context: a woman in a theocracy has no route to standing except her own labour.","Paragraph 3 — guilt hidden: Dimmesdale's is given a physical vocabulary — the hand at the heart, the secret self-punishment, the rehearsal on an empty scaffold at midnight — and the narrator generalises it: 'No man, for any considerable period, can wear one face to himself, and another to the multitude, without finally getting bewildered as to which may be the true.' Effect: the division of the self, then collapse. Context: in this community there is no confession available to a minister that does not destroy him, so the secret is manufactured by the society, not only by the man.","Paragraph 4 — guilt that will not name itself: Chillingworth, who believes himself an observer and who answers Hester, when she begs him in Chapter XIV to release Dimmesdale, by calling the whole ruin their fate — 'Let the black flower blossom as it may!'; the physician's vocabulary turns healing into predation. Effect: the one character who never acknowledges guilt is the one the narrator calls a fiend. Context and form: the romance form lets Hawthorne keep the devilish and the psychological readings open at once.","The evaluation thread to keep running: at every stage ask what the guilt produced — work and reinterpretation for Hester, division and death for Dimmesdale, corruption for Chillingworth — so the essay is always comparing outcomes rather than listing characters.","What the conclusion must say: answer the question with a qualified judgement, not a summary — that Hawthorne's subject is secrecy rather than sin, which is why the Conclusion's instruction is 'Be true! Be true! Be true!' and why the final image is a shared grave and a single motto rather than an absolution; and concede the counter-weight, that Hester's endurance is bought at a price the novel never pretends is small.","Timing: about five minutes to choose between the two questions and plan, about thirty-five to write four developed paragraphs, about five to check that every paragraph contains a named method and a piece of context doing work."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:01:00.961863Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"All eight faults in the first verdict are genuinely repaired — the black-flower quotation is now correctly placed as Chillingworth to Hester in Chapter XIV, the Chapter I paragraph order is right everywhere (crowd first, cemetery and prison in the paragraph after), the Conclusion is counted inside the twenty-four chapters, the Component 2 hint no longer hands over 'clean', the forest-scene chronology now runs to Election Day, Emerson is gone and Chillingworth is 'much older than she is' rather than elderly, and every stem now sits inside the board's command words — and all fourteen distinct quotations from the novel are word for word correct, so the kit is publishable once the two Chapter I overstatements are tightened."}}}} };
